insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('bouillabaisse','en','Bouillabaisse','Marseille, France','Born in the cauldrons of Marseille fishermen, bouillabaisse turns unsold rockfish into a saffron broth of rare depth. The soup is served first with rouille and croutons, the fish following as a second course.',ARRAY['Sweat the sliced onion and fennel in olive oil without colouring.','Add tomatoes, garlic, orange zest and saffron, cover with water and simmer for 30 minutes.','Pass the broth through a food mill to extract every bit of flavour, then adjust the seasoning.','Poach the fish in it from firmest to most delicate, 8 to 12 minutes in all.','Serve the broth piping hot over garlic-rubbed croutons, with the fish and potatoes on the side.']::text[]),
('cassoulet','en','Cassoulet','Castelnaudary, France','This stew of white beans and confit meats bakes for hours in the earthenware cassole that gave it its name. The golden crust, broken and folded back in seven times, is the heart of its legend.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then simmer them for 1 hour with carrot, onion and thyme.','Brown the sausages and pork belly in duck fat, then set aside.','Layer beans and meats in the cassole and pour in the cooking broth to just cover.','Scatter with breadcrumbs and bake for 2 hours at 150°C.','Break the crust with a spoon several times during baking, and serve straight from the dish.']::text[]),
('pizza-napoletana','en','Neapolitan pizza','Naples, Italy','Three ingredients and nowhere to hide: San Marzano tomato, buffalo mozzarella, basil. The long-fermented dough puffs in 90 seconds in a 450°C wood oven to give that leopard-spotted rim.',ARRAY['Mix flour, water, yeast and salt, then knead for 15 minutes until smooth.','Let rise 2 hours at room temperature, then 24 hours in the fridge.','Divide into 250 g balls and rest 4 hours before stretching by hand.','Crush the tomatoes with a fork, season, and spread in a spiral over the base.','Add drained mozzarella and a drizzle of oil, bake as hot as your oven goes; basil on the way out.']::text[]),
('risotto-milanese','en','Risotto alla milanese','Milan, Italy','A brilliantly yellow risotto stained with saffron, whose colour legend credits to a glazier at the Duomo. Everything hangs on the final gesture: off the heat, cold butter and parmesan are beaten in for creaminess.',ARRAY['Sweat the finely chopped shallot in a little butter with the marrow.','Toast the rice for 2 minutes, deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.','Add hot broth a ladle at a time, stirring, for 16 to 18 minutes.','Stir in the saffron, steeped in a little broth, halfway through cooking.','Off the heat, beat in cold butter and parmesan, rest 1 minute and serve it wavy.']::text[]),
('paella-valenciana','en','Paella valenciana','Valencia, Spain','True Valencian paella contains no seafood: chicken, rabbit, flat beans and garrofó, cooked over vine cuttings. The treasure lies at the bottom — the crisp socarrat you scrape up with a spoon.',ARRAY['Brown the chicken and rabbit slowly in oil around the edge of the pan.','Add the beans, then grated tomato and paprika off the heat so it does not burn.','Cover with water, add the saffron and let this broth cook for 20 minutes.','Pour the rice in a cross, spread it once and never stir again.','Cook 18 minutes, lowering the heat, flash the flame 1 minute for the socarrat, rest 5 minutes.']::text[]),
('gazpacho','en','Andalusian gazpacho','Seville, Spain','A cold soup born of necessity: stale bread, olive oil and garden vegetables blended to face 40°C Andalusian summers. Properly emulsified, it turns velvety and almost pink.',ARRAY['Soak the bread in a little water for 10 minutes.','Blend tomatoes, peeled cucumber, pepper and garlic to a smooth liquid.','Add the squeezed bread and the vinegar, then blend again.','Pour in the olive oil in a thin stream with the motor running until the colour pales.','Pass through a fine sieve, season and chill at least 3 hours; serve ice cold.']::text[]),
('bacalhau-a-bras','en','Bacalhau à Brás','Lisbon, Portugal','A Bairro Alto tavern dish where shredded salt cod meets matchstick potatoes and barely-set eggs. The secret is to kill the heat before the eggs truly set, keeping everything silky.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod for 24 hours, changing the water, then shred it by hand.','Cut the potatoes into fine matchsticks and fry until golden.','Melt onion and garlic in olive oil, add the cod and let them mingle for 5 minutes.','Fold in the potatoes, pour over the beaten eggs and stir for 30 seconds off the heat.','Serve at once, scattered with parsley and black olives.']::text[]),
('francesinha','en','Francesinha','Porto, Portugal','The Portuguese croque-monsieur taken to excess: ham, sausage and steak between two slices of bread, drowned in melted cheese and a spicy beer sauce. Invented in the 1950s to warm up Porto nights.',ARRAY['Make the sauce: cook a roux, add beer, tomato paste and piri-piri, reduce for 15 minutes.','Pan-fry the steaks and sausages, and toast the bread.','Stack bread, ham, split sausage, steak, then close with the second slice.','Cover completely with cheese slices and grill until molten.','Pour over the hot sauce, crown with a fried egg and serve with chips.']::text[]),
('wiener-schnitzel','en','Wiener Schnitzel','Vienna, Austria','A veal escalope beaten as thin as a leaf, breaded and fried in clarified butter until the crumb puffs away from the meat. Lemon is compulsory, sauce forbidden.',ARRAY['Pound the escalopes between two sheets of film to 4 mm thick.','Season, then coat in flour, beaten egg and breadcrumbs without pressing.','Heat a generous amount of clarified butter to 170°C.','Fry 2 minutes per side, swirling the pan so the coating puffs up.','Drain on kitchen paper and serve at once with a lemon wedge.']::text[]),
('pierogi','en','Pierogi ruskie','Kraków, Poland','Dumplings stuffed with potato, farmer''s cheese and fried onion, poached and sometimes finished in butter. Every Polish family guards its own way of crimping the edges.',ARRAY['Knead flour, warm water, egg and salt into a supple dough; rest 30 minutes.','Mash the cooked potatoes with the cheese and one fried onion; season generously.','Roll the dough thin, cut 8 cm discs and place a walnut of filling on each.','Crimp the edges carefully and poach for 3 minutes in salted simmering water.','Fry in butter with the remaining onion and serve with sour cream.']::text[]),
('moussaka','en','Moussaka','Athens, Greece','Layers of roasted aubergine and cinnamon-scented lamb under a béchamel baked to gold. The modern version, devised by chef Tselementes in the 1920s, became the national dish.',ARRAY['Salt the aubergine slices, drain 30 minutes, then roast them in the oven with olive oil.','Brown the lamb with onion and garlic, add tomato, wine and cinnamon, reduce for 25 minutes.','Make a thick béchamel and stir in the cheese and an egg yolk off the heat.','Build the dish: aubergine, meat, aubergine, then blanket with béchamel.','Bake 45 minutes at 180°C and rest 20 minutes before cutting.']::text[]),
('fish-and-chips','en','Fish and chips','London, United Kingdom','A cod fillet in a beer batter so light it shatters, with thick-cut chips and minted mushy peas. Malt vinegar is part of the ritual.',ARRAY['Cut the potatoes into thick chips and blanch them for 8 minutes at 140°C.','Mix the flours, baking powder and ice-cold beer just before frying: keep the batter lumpy.','Dry the fillets, dust with flour, dip in the batter and fry at 180°C for 6 to 7 minutes.','Return the chips to 190°C oil until deep golden.','Crush the peas with the mint and serve everything with salt and malt vinegar.']::text[]),
('kottbullar','en','Köttbullar','Stockholm, Sweden','Small beef and pork meatballs bound with milk-soaked bread, served with a creamy gravy, mash and lingonberry jam. The tart berry cuts straight through the richness.',ARRAY['Soak the breadcrumbs in the milk and soften the chopped onion in butter.','Mix the meats, egg, cooled onion and allspice until smooth and sticky.','Roll walnut-sized balls with damp hands.','Brown them in butter, shaking the pan so they stay round, then set aside.','Deglaze with broth, add the cream, thicken for 5 minutes and return the meatballs.']::text[]),
('borscht','en','Borscht','Kyiv, Ukraine','A crimson soup of beetroot and cabbage, tangy and deep, defended in a different version by every Ukrainian village. The spoonful of smetana melting in draws pink swirls.',ARRAY['Simmer the meat, bay and whole onion for 1½ hours to make a broth, then shred the meat.','Fry grated carrot, onion and beetroot with tomato paste and vinegar, which locks in the colour.','Add potatoes and cabbage to the broth and cook for 12 minutes.','Stir in the beetroot mixture and simmer only 5 minutes to keep the bright red.','Add crushed garlic off the heat, rest overnight and serve with smetana and dill.']::text[]),
('fondue-moitie','en','Moitié-moitié fondue','Fribourg, Switzerland','Half gruyère, half vacherin fribourgeois, melted in white wine with a hint of garlic and kirsch. You stir in figures of eight, never circles, and whoever loses their bread buys the next round.',ARRAY['Rub the pot with the cut garlic clove, then pour in the white wine.','Add the grated cheeses over low heat, stirring in figures of eight with a wooden spoon.','Slake the cornflour in the kirsch and stir it in as soon as the mixture is smooth.','Season with pepper and nutmeg and let it barely simmer for 2 minutes, never boiling.','Set the pot on the burner and dip large cubes of bread.']::text[]),
('gulyas','en','Goulash','Budapest, Hungary','Originally the soup of the puszta cattle herders, cooked in a cauldron over an open fire. Paprika, always added off the heat so it never turns bitter, gives this beef and potato broth its glow.',ARRAY['Melt the sliced onions in lard for 15 minutes until golden.','Take off the heat, add the paprika and stir at once so it cannot catch.','Add the cubed beef, caraway and garlic, cover with water and simmer for 1½ hours.','Add peppers, tomatoes and potatoes and cook 30 minutes more.','Adjust the salt and let it rest: goulash is better the next day.']::text[]),
('sushi-edomae','en','Edomae sushi','Tokyo, Japan','In the 19th century sushi was street food, sold by the piece around Edo bay. Everything rests on the shari: warm vinegared rice whose grains must stay distinct under the fingers.',ARRAY['Rinse the rice until the water runs clear, cook it and let it stand covered for 10 minutes.','Warm vinegar, sugar and salt, pour over the hot rice and cut the grains apart while fanning.','Trim the fish and slice even 8 g pieces against the grain.','Shape 15 g rice pillows in a damp palm, never compressing them.','Add a dab of wasabi, lay on the fish, press gently and serve immediately.']::text[]),
('ramen-tonkotsu','en','Tonkotsu ramen','Fukuoka, Japan','Kyushu''s pork bone broth boils hard for twelve hours until it turns white and creamy. Poured over thin straight noodles, it is crowned with chashu and a marinated egg with a molten yolk.',ARRAY['Blanch the bones for 10 minutes, rinse, then boil hard for 8 hours, topping up the water.','Roll and tie the pork belly and braise it 2 hours in soy, mirin, garlic and ginger.','Boil the eggs for 6½ minutes, peel and marinate overnight in the braising liquid.','Cook the noodles for barely 90 seconds and shake them dry.','Pour the scalding broth over the noodles, add chashu, half an egg, spring onion and nori.']::text[]),
('peking-duck','en','Peking duck','Beijing, China','Served at the Ming court from the 15th century, the duck is scalded, glazed with malt and air-dried before roasting. The skin, crisp as glass, is eaten first, rolled in a thin pancake.',ARRAY['Separate the skin from the flesh with air or a spoon handle, without piercing it.','Scald the duck, brush with the maltose-vinegar glaze and dry 24 hours in the fridge.','Roast 30 minutes at 200°C, then 50 minutes at 160°C, breast up.','Cut the skin into diamonds, then slice the meat thinly.','Serve with warm pancakes, hoisin sauce, cucumber and spring onion batons.']::text[]),
('mapo-tofu','en','Mapo tofu','Chengdu, China','Sichuan''s emblematic dish, credited to the pockmarked wife of a 19th-century innkeeper. Sichuan pepper delivers the famous málà: a gentle burn doubled with an electric numbness.',ARRAY['Cut the tofu into 2 cm cubes and soak them 5 minutes in hot salted water.','Fry the pork until crisp, then add doubanjiang and fermented black beans.','Add garlic, ginger and broth, then slide the tofu cubes in gently.','Simmer 5 minutes without stirring, just shaking the wok.','Thicken with the cornflour slurry in three additions, drizzle chilli oil and dust with ground Sichuan pepper.']::text[]),
('har-gow','en','Har gow & dim sum','Guangzhou, China','The queen of Cantonese tea houses: a whole prawn in a translucent wrapper that must have exactly thirteen pleats. Yum cha is a morning affair of bamboo baskets and endlessly refilled teapots.',ARRAY['Pour the boiling water onto the starches all at once and work quickly into a smooth ball.','Roughly chop half the prawns, keep the rest whole and mix with the other filling ingredients.','Flatten very thin discs with an oiled cleaver blade.','Fill and fold with at least ten tight pleats along one side.','Steam 6 to 8 minutes over high heat in a lined bamboo basket.']::text[]),
('kimchi-jjigae','en','Kimchi jjigae','Seoul, South Korea','The stew you make when the kimchi has turned too sour to eat raw — which is exactly when it gives its best. It reaches the table still bubbling in its stone pot.',ARRAY['Fry the sliced pork belly in sesame oil until the fat renders.','Add the cut kimchi and its juice and stir-fry 5 minutes to concentrate the sourness.','Stir in gochujang, gochugaru, garlic and sugar, then pour in the broth.','Let it boil uncovered for 20 minutes.','Add sliced tofu and spring onion, cook 5 more minutes and serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('pad-thai','en','Pad thai','Bangkok, Thailand','Promoted as a national dish in the 1930s to cut rice consumption, pad thai balances four tastes: sour tamarind, palm sugar, salty fish sauce and chilli. The wok must be scorching and the serving immediate.',ARRAY['Soak the noodles 30 minutes in warm water: they must stay firm.','Stir tamarind, palm sugar and fish sauce together until fully dissolved.','Sear tofu, dried shrimp and fresh prawns in a very hot wok.','Add noodles and sauce, toss 2 minutes, push aside and scramble the eggs.','Finish with bean sprouts and garlic chives; serve with peanuts, chilli and lime.']::text[]),
('pho-bo','en','Phở bò','Hanoi, Vietnam','A crystal-clear broth of oxtail and beef bones perfumed with charred spices: star anise, cinnamon, black cardamom. In Hanoi it is drunk from six in the morning, perched on a plastic stool.',ARRAY['Blanch the bones for 10 minutes and rinse them well for a clear broth.','Char the whole onion and ginger over a flame until blackened.','Simmer bones, onion, ginger and toasted spices for 4 hours, skimming, never boiling.','Season with fish sauce and strain the broth twice.','Blanch the noodles, lay thin slices of raw beef on top and pour over the scalding broth; herbs on the side.']::text[]),
('nasi-goreng','en','Nasi goreng','Jakarta, Indonesia','Born from a refusal to waste yesterday''s rice, this fried rice owes its amber colour to kecap manis, a thick sweet soy sauce. It is crowned with a lace-edged fried egg and prawn crackers.',ARRAY['Pound shallots, garlic, chillies and shrimp paste into a smooth paste.','Fry it in hot oil until fragrant, about 2 minutes.','Add the chicken and prawns and sear them quickly.','Fold in the cold rice, breaking it up with the back of the spatula, then the kecap manis.','Stir-fry over maximum heat for 3 minutes and serve with a fried egg and cucumber.']::text[]),
('penang-laksa','en','Assam laksa','Penang, Malaysia','A noodle soup of flaked mackerel made tart with tamarind and torch ginger flower. Unlike the coconut version, Penang''s laksa is sharp, hot and strangely refreshing.',ARRAY['Poach the mackerel 10 minutes, keep the cooking water and flake the flesh, removing every bone.','Blend chillies, lemongrass, galangal and shrimp paste into a rempah paste.','Fry it, then add the fish water, tamarind and torch ginger; simmer 30 minutes.','Return the flaked fish, sweeten and salt until the sour-salty balance sings.','Serve over thick noodles with pineapple, cucumber, red onion and mint.']::text[]),
('butter-chicken','en','Butter chicken','Delhi, India','Invented in Delhi in the 1950s to rescue leftover tandoori chicken by simmering it in a buttery tomato sauce. The cream is never bland: dried fenugreek lifts everything at the end.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken 4 hours in yoghurt, garlic, ginger and spices.','Grill it in a very hot oven until the edges just char.','Reduce the tomato purée with the butter for 20 minutes, until it darkens.','Blend the sauce perfectly smooth, then add cream and sugar.','Return the chicken, simmer 10 minutes and crush the dried fenugreek between your fingers as you serve.']::text[]),
('masala-dosa','en','Masala dosa','Bengaluru, India','A fermented rice and lentil crêpe, spread so thin it turns to crisp lace, wrapped around turmeric potato. Natural fermentation gives it its characteristic tang.',ARRAY['Soak rice and lentils separately for 6 hours with the fenugreek, then grind to a smooth batter.','Ferment 12 hours somewhere warm, until doubled and pleasantly sour.','Make the filling: mustard seeds and curry leaves popped in ghee, onion, turmeric and crushed potato.','Pour a ladle of batter onto a very hot griddle and spread it in a spiral with the ladle''s back.','Drizzle with ghee, place the filling in the centre, fold and serve with sambar and coconut chutney.']::text[]),
('hyderabadi-biryani','en','Hyderabadi biryani','Hyderabad, India','Kacchi biryani cooks raw marinated meat and half-boiled rice together, sealed under a lid of dough. The trapped steam — the dum — does everything: the lid is lifted only at the table.',ARRAY['Marinate the lamb 3 hours in yoghurt, fried onions, herbs, chillies and spices.','Boil the basmati for only 4 minutes in heavily salted, perfumed water.','Lay the raw marinated meat in a heavy pot and cover with the half-cooked rice.','Pour over saffron steeped in warm milk and melted ghee, then seal the lid with dough.','Cook 15 minutes over high heat, then 40 minutes on the lowest; fold together only at the table.']::text[]),
('khachapuri','en','Adjarian khachapuri','Tbilisi, Georgia','A boat-shaped bread filled with molten cheese, into which an egg is cracked straight from the oven. You stir the yolk and butter into the cheese, then tear off the edges to scoop.',ARRAY['Knead flour, warm milk, yeast, sugar and salt; leave to rise for 1 hour.','Mix the grated cheeses, keeping a little liquid so they stay supple.','Roll each ball into an oval, fill, and roll the edges inward, pinching the ends.','Bake 12 minutes at 230°C, until golden and the cheese bubbles.','Make a well, crack in an egg, add butter and return to the oven for 2 minutes.']::text[]),
('plov','en','Samarkand plov','Samarkand, Uzbekistan','Cooked by men in a giant kazan, for weddings and for ordinary days alike. The rice is never stirred in: it cooks above the yellow carrots and lamb, drinking up the zirvak below.',ARRAY['Rinse the rice seven times, then soak it in warm salted water.','Melt the fat in the kazan, brown the large chunks of meat, then the onions.','Add the thickly cut carrots and the cumin, stirring as little as possible.','Cover with water, add chickpeas and whole garlic heads, simmer 40 minutes: this is the zirvak.','Spread the drained rice on top, add water to 2 cm above, cook uncovered then 20 minutes covered off the heat.']::text[]),
('iskender-kebab','en','İskender kebab','Bursa, Türkiye','Invented in Bursa in 1867 by İskender Efendi, who thought of turning the spit upright. Slices of lamb rest on toasted bread under tomato sauce, finished with sizzling browned butter at the table.',ARRAY['Slice the lamb thinly and marinate 2 hours with grated onion, oregano and paprika.','Sear it in a very hot pan in small batches so it caramelises.','Reduce the tomato purée with a little butter until it coats a spoon.','Cut the breads into diamonds and toast them in the pan.','Plate bread, meat, tomato sauce and yoghurt, then pour the sizzling browned butter over at the table.']::text[]),
('hummus-beiruti','en','Beiruti hummus','Beirut, Lebanon','A chickpea purée of absolute silkiness, where the tahini matters as much as the lemon. The Lebanese secret is a pinch of bicarbonate in the cooking water, which blows the skins off.',ARRAY['Soak the chickpeas overnight with half the bicarbonate.','Cook them 1 hour with the rest, until they crush between two fingers.','Blend the garlic with the lemon juice, infuse 10 minutes, then strain.','Blend the hot chickpeas with tahini, lemon and ice water for at least 4 minutes.','Spread with a swirl, pour on olive oil and dust with sumac and cumin.']::text[]),
('tagine-agneau','en','Lamb tagine with prunes','Fez, Morocco','The dish of Fez celebrations, where sweet and savoury meet without cancelling each other out. The tagine''s conical lid returns the steam to the base: the meat cooks in its own juices, almost without water.',ARRAY['Brown the lamb with grated onions, butter, saffron and ginger.','Half cover with water, add the cinnamon and simmer, covered, for 2 hours.','Plump the prunes in a little of the cooking liquid with the honey and cinnamon.','Fry the almonds until golden, then toast the sesame seeds dry.','Coat the meat in its reduced juices, arrange the prunes, orange blossom water, almonds and sesame.']::text[]),
('couscous','en','Couscous','Tunis, Tunisia','Hand-rolled semolina steamed over the broth itself, three separate times, so it stays light and loose. On Fridays the whole Maghreb gathers around the same dish.',ARRAY['Brown the meat and onion, add tomato paste and harissa, then cover with water.','Moisten the semolina, roll it between your palms and let it swell for 10 minutes.','Steam it a first time for 20 minutes over the broth.','Spread it out, sprinkle with water, fluff with a fork and repeat twice more.','Add the vegetables to the broth according to their cooking times; serve the semolina in a dome, broth on the side.']::text[]),
('koshari','en','Koshari','Cairo, Egypt','Egypt''s street dish par excellence: rice, lentils, macaroni and chickpeas stacked up, drenched in vinegary tomato sauce and crowned with fried onions. A pile of starches that should not work, and works perfectly.',ARRAY['Slice the onions very thinly and fry them slowly until deep brown and crisp.','Cook lentils, rice and macaroni separately, then mix rice and lentils in the onion-scented oil.','Make the sauce: garlic, cumin, tomato purée and vinegar, reduced for 15 minutes.','Make a sharp daqqa with vinegar, garlic and chilli.','Stack rice-lentils, macaroni and chickpeas, pour over the sauce and blanket with fried onions.']::text[]),
('doro-wat','en','Doro wat','Addis Ababa, Ethiopia','The stew of Ethiopian feast days, built on a mountain of onions cooked dry for a full hour before any fat goes in. Berbere gives it its dark red colour and lingering heat.',ARRAY['Rub the chicken pieces with lemon and salt and leave for 30 minutes.','Cook the chopped onions dry in a heavy pot for 45 minutes, stirring often.','Add the spiced butter, garlic, ginger and berbere and cook 10 minutes.','Add the chicken, barely any liquid, and simmer covered for 1 hour.','Slip scored hard-boiled eggs into the sauce 10 minutes before the end; serve on injera.']::text[]),
('jollof-rice','en','Jollof rice','Lagos, Nigeria','The red rice that has fuelled a joyful rivalry between Nigeria, Ghana and Senegal for decades. The real secret is the lightly scorched layer at the bottom of the pot, which perfumes the whole dish.',ARRAY['Blend tomatoes, peppers, chillies and one onion into a smooth purée.','Fry the tomato paste in hot oil for 5 minutes until it darkens.','Pour in the purée and reduce for 20 minutes: it must lose its raw taste.','Add broth, spices and rinsed rice, cover with baking paper then the lid.','Cook 25 minutes on the lowest heat without ever lifting the lid, then fluff with a fork.']::text[]),
('thieboudienne','en','Thieboudienne','Dakar, Senegal','Senegal''s national dish, inscribed by UNESCO: rice cooked in fish broth with vegetables and a herb stuffing called rof. It is served in one big communal dish, each person eating from the section in front of them.',ARRAY['Pound parsley, garlic, chilli and salt into rof, then stuff it into slits cut in the fish.','Fry the fish steaks, set aside, then fry the onion and tomato paste.','Add water, dried fish and vegetables; cook 25 minutes, lifting each out when tender.','Return the fish for 10 minutes, remove it, then pour the broken rice into the broth.','Cook the rice covered for 25 minutes, let the base caramelise, and serve with fish and vegetables.']::text[]),
('bobotie','en','Bobotie','Cape Town, South Africa','A baked dish of spiced mince with dried fruit under a savoury egg custard studded with bay leaves. It comes from Cape Malay cooking and tells five centuries of exchange between Asia and southern Africa.',ARRAY['Soak the bread in the milk and squeeze it out, keeping the milk.','Soften the onions, add curry powder and turmeric, then the meat.','Mix in the bread, apricot jam, vinegar, raisins and almonds; season generously.','Press into a dish, beat the eggs with the reserved milk and pour over the meat.','Stand the bay leaves upright and bake 40 minutes at 180°C, until just set.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('nyama-choma','en','Nyama choma','Nairobi, Kenya','Goat meat grilled slowly over charcoal with no marinade: salt and smoke are enough. It is chopped small on a board and eaten with the fingers, with kachumbari and ugali.',ARRAY['Salt the large pieces of meat generously and let them come to room temperature for 1 hour.','Prepare gentle embers and grill away from direct flame for 1½ hours, turning often.','Baste with salted lemon water throughout so it never dries out.','Make the kachumbari: tomato, red onion, chilli and coriander with lemon.','Make ugali by raining maize flour into boiling water and working it until it pulls from the pan.']::text[]),
('tacos-al-pastor','en','Tacos al pastor','Mexico City, Mexico','Lebanese shawarma reinvented with pork and chilli by migrants who reached Puebla in the 1920s. The trompo turns before the flame and the taquero flicks the meat — and a piece of pineapple — into the tortilla.',ARRAY['Rehydrate the chillies for 20 minutes, then blend with achiote, vinegar, garlic and spices.','Slice the pork thinly and marinate at least 3 hours in this red paste.','Stack the slices alternating with pineapple rings, then roast 1 hour at 200°C.','Shave the roasted meat thinly and sear it on a scorching griddle.','Serve on warm corn tortillas with onion, coriander, grilled pineapple and lime.']::text[]),
('mole-poblano','en','Mole poblano','Puebla, Mexico','An almost black sauce bringing together more than twenty ingredients, four dried chillies and a whisper of chocolate among them. Legend has a Puebla nun improvising it for a visiting archbishop.',ARRAY['Toast the chillies dry without scorching them, seed them and soak 30 minutes.','Separately fry almonds, peanuts, raisins, sesame, tortilla and spices in the lard.','Blend everything with the tomatoes and some broth, in batches, to a very smooth paste.','Fry the paste in lard for 20 minutes, stirring constantly, then loosen with broth.','Add the chocolate, simmer 1 hour and serve over poached chicken with toasted sesame.']::text[]),
('ropa-vieja','en','Ropa vieja','Havana, Cuba','"Old clothes": the beef, shredded after hours of cooking, looks like torn rags. The sofrito of peppers, cumin and olives makes it the Cuban Sunday dish par excellence.',ARRAY['Simmer the beef 2 hours in an aromatic broth until it falls apart.','Shred it with a fork along the grain and keep the broth.','Make the sofrito: onion, peppers and garlic melted slowly in olive oil.','Add tomato, wine, cumin, oregano and bay and reduce for 15 minutes.','Stir in the shredded beef and a little broth, simmer 30 minutes, add the olives and serve with rice.']::text[]),
('jerk-chicken','en','Jerk chicken','Kingston, Jamaica','A technique inherited from the Maroons, who smoked meat over pimento wood to preserve it in the mountains. Scotch bonnet and allspice make a marinade that is fiery and deeply aromatic at once.',ARRAY['Blend all the aromatics into a thick marinade, dosing the chilli according to your courage.','Score the chicken to the bone and massage the marinade in; rest 12 hours.','Set up an indirect fire with soaked wood chips for smoke.','Cook the chicken skin side up, covered, for 45 minutes.','Finish over the coals for 10 minutes to caramelise and serve with rice and peas.']::text[]),
('gumbo','en','Gumbo','New Orleans, United States','The creole dish where Africa, France and the Choctaw meet: a roux cooked to the colour of chocolate, okra, and the holy trinity of onion, celery and pepper. It is served over a mound of rice.',ARRAY['Make the roux: flour and oil over medium heat, stirring nonstop for 30 to 40 minutes to a chocolate colour.','Throw in the chopped trinity all at once: it stops the roux cooking.','Add the sausage, garlic and spices, then gradually whisk in the hot broth.','Simmer uncovered for 1 hour, add the okra and cook 20 minutes more.','Add the prawns 5 minutes before the end and serve over white rice.']::text[]),
('clam-chowder','en','New England clam chowder','Boston, United States','A thick soup of clams, potatoes and smoked bacon that has warmed New England ports since the 18th century. In Boston, adding tomato is still considered a provocation.',ARRAY['Steam the clams open in a little water, strain the juice carefully and chop the meat.','Render the diced bacon, then soften the onions and celery in the fat.','Dust with the flour, pour in the clam juice and let it thicken.','Add the diced potatoes, thyme and bay and cook for 15 minutes.','Add milk, cream and chopped clams, heat without boiling and serve with crackers.']::text[]),
('poutine','en','Poutine','Montreal, Canada','Chips, cheese curds and brown gravy: born in rural Quebec snack bars in the late 1950s, poutine became a national emblem. The curds must squeak against your teeth, or it is not poutine.',ARRAY['Cut the potatoes into thick sticks and rinse them in cold water.','Make a blond roux with butter and flour, pour in the broths and thicken for 10 minutes.','Sharpen the gravy with vinegar, Worcestershire sauce and pepper.','Fry the chips first at 150°C, then again at 190°C until deep golden.','Pile up the hot chips, scatter the curds and pour over the boiling gravy so they just start to melt.']::text[]),
('gallo-pinto','en','Gallo pinto','San José, Costa Rica','The Costa Rican breakfast: yesterday''s rice and black beans fried together until the rice turns speckled — hence "spotted rooster". Lizano sauce is non-negotiable.',ARRAY['Fry finely chopped onion, pepper and garlic until soft.','Add the black beans with a little of their cooking liquid.','Fold in the cold rice and the Lizano sauce, mixing without crushing the grains.','Fry 5 minutes so the rice colours and the base catches slightly.','Serve with a fried egg, fried plantain and fresh coriander.']::text[]),
('feijoada','en','Feijoada','Rio de Janeiro, Brazil','The black bean and pork stew Brazil eats on Wednesdays and Saturdays, never alone and never quickly. Farofa, rice, sautéed greens and orange slices to lighten it are compulsory.',ARRAY['Desalt the dried beef for 12 hours, changing the water several times.','Cook the soaked black beans with the bay leaves for 1 hour.','Add the meats from longest to shortest cooking time and simmer for 2 hours.','Take out a ladle of beans, mash it with fried garlic and stir it back in to thicken.','Serve with rice, shredded sautéed greens, buttery farofa and orange wedges.']::text[]),
('moqueca-baiana','en','Moqueca baiana','Salvador, Brazil','A fish stew with coconut milk and red palm oil, cooked in a black Bahian clay pot. Dendê oil, a West African legacy, gives it its deep orange colour and unmistakable perfume.',ARRAY['Marinate the fish 20 minutes in lime juice, garlic and salt.','Layer in the pot: onion, tomato, peppers, then the fish.','Pour over the coconut milk and dendê oil, never stirring.','Cover and cook 20 minutes over medium heat, simply shaking the pot.','Add the prawns and coriander 5 minutes before the end; serve with rice and pirão.']::text[]),
('asado-argentino','en','Argentine asado','Buenos Aires, Argentina','Far more than a barbecue: a Sunday ritual where the asador alone rules the embers for three hours. The meat cooks slowly, far from the flame, salted with coarse salt and served with sharp chimichurri.',ARRAY['Light the fire an hour ahead: you cook over embers, never over flame.','Salt the ribs with coarse salt and lay them bone side down, 20 cm above the coals.','Cook 2 hours without touching them, feeding in fresh embers regularly.','Make the chimichurri: parsley, garlic, oregano, vinegar, oil and chilli, rested for 1 hour.','Turn the meat only once, for 30 minutes, then carve and serve with the chimichurri.']::text[]),
('ceviche','en','Ceviche','Lima, Peru','Raw fish "cooked" by lime acidity in a matter of minutes — Lima''s cooks now say never more than five. The juice left behind, the leche de tigre, is drunk down in one go.',ARRAY['Cut very fresh fish into even 2 cm cubes and keep them cold.','Squeeze the limes without crushing the peel, which would turn the juice bitter.','Blend some juice with a little fish, celery, garlic and chilli: that is the leche de tigre.','Mix fish, salt, leche de tigre and finely sliced red onion; wait 3 minutes.','Serve immediately with cooked sweet potato, corn and chopped coriander.']::text[]),
('arepas','en','Arepas reina pepiada','Caracas, Venezuela','A griddled cake of precooked maize, split open and stuffed like a pocket. The reina pepiada, created in 1955 in honour of a Miss Venezuela, combines shredded chicken and crushed avocado.',ARRAY['Mix maize flour, warm water and salt, then rest 5 minutes so the dough hydrates.','Shape 1.5 cm thick cakes, smoothing the edges between your palms.','Sear 4 minutes per side in a pan, then finish 10 minutes in the oven: they should sound hollow.','Shred the poached chicken and mix with crushed avocado, mayonnaise, onion and lime.','Split the arepas while hot and stuff them generously.']::text[]),
('pastel-de-choclo','en','Pastel de choclo','Santiago, Chile','A hash of beef, onion, olives and hard-boiled egg under a purée of fresh corn scented with basil. Dusted with sugar, it caramelises in the oven and arrives still bubbling.',ARRAY['Cook the chopped onions slowly, add the beef, cumin and paprika: this is the pino.','Blend fresh corn with milk and basil, then thicken the purée over low heat for 15 minutes.','Divide the pino between individual dishes and add chicken, olives, raisins and hard-boiled egg.','Cover with the corn purée and smooth the surface.','Dust with sugar and bake 30 minutes at 200°C, until the top caramelises.']::text[]),
('bandeja-paisa','en','Bandeja paisa','Medellín, Colombia','An outsized platter descended from the food of Antioquia''s muleteers, who walked mountain trails all day. Beans, rice, minced beef, chicharrón, egg, plantain, avocado and arepa all arrive at once.',ARRAY['Cook the soaked red beans for 2 hours with a piece of green plantain, which thickens them.','Make a hogao: tomatoes and onions slowly melted, to be stirred into the beans.','Score the pork belly and cook it covered, then uncovered, until the rind blisters.','Fry the minced beef with cumin, grill the chorizos and fry the ripe plantains.','Arrange everything side by side on a big platter with the fried egg, avocado and arepa.']::text[]),
('pavlova','en','Pavlova','Auckland, New Zealand','A meringue disc, crisp outside and marshmallow within, created for the ballerina Anna Pavlova during her 1926 tour. New Zealand and Australia still argue over who invented it.',ARRAY['Whip room-temperature whites, adding the sugar in a very slow stream.','Beat for 10 minutes: the meringue must be stiff enough to stay put in an upturned bowl.','Fold in the cornflour, vinegar and vanilla with a spatula.','Shape a 22 cm disc with a slight hollow in the centre and bake 90 minutes at 120°C.','Cool in the switched-off oven with the door ajar, then top with whipped cream and fruit.']::text[]),
('meat-pie','en','Aussie meat pie','Melbourne, Australia','The pie eaten standing up at Australian stadiums, in one hand, with a squirt of tomato sauce. Shortcrust below, puff pastry above, and a beef filling thick enough never to run.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into small dice and sear it in batches for a proper crust.','Add the onion, dust with flour, then add broth, tomato paste and Worcestershire.','Simmer for 1 hour to a very thick gravy, then cool completely.','Line the tins with shortcrust, fill with the cold meat and cover with puff pastry.','Seal the edges, brush with egg and bake 25 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('kokoda','en','Kokoda','Suva, Fiji','The Pacific''s ceviche: fish marinated in lime then rested in fresh coconut milk, which rounds off the acidity. It is traditionally served ice cold in half a coconut shell.',ARRAY['Cut very fresh white fish into 1.5 cm cubes.','Cover with lime juice and marinate 20 minutes in the fridge, until it turns opaque.','Drain the fish, discarding half the juice, which is too sharp.','Mix with coconut milk, chopped tomato, cucumber, onion and chilli.','Chill 15 minutes and serve very cold, scattered with spring onion.']::text[]),
('poke','en','Ahi poke','Honolulu, Hawaii','Long before it became a global trend, poke was Hawaiian fishermen''s snack: chunks of tuna, sea salt, seaweed and kukui nuts. The word simply means "to slice".',ARRAY['Choose sashimi-grade tuna and cut it into 2 cm cubes.','Rehydrate the wakame for 10 minutes and chop it roughly.','Mix soy sauce, sesame oil, chilli and sea salt.','Dress the tuna gently with the sliced onions and seaweed; rest 15 minutes in the fridge.','Serve over warm vinegared rice with avocado and toasted sesame seeds.']::text[]),
('choucroute-garnie','en','Choucroute garnie','Strasbourg, France','Cabbage fermented in salt — a technique that reached Alsace from China via central Europe — simmered in riesling with five or six different cured meats. A brasserie dish meant to be shared, never plated alone.',ARRAY['Rinse the sauerkraut in cold water and squeeze it hard to remove excess acidity.','Soften the onions in lard, then add the sauerkraut, juniper and bay.','Pour in the riesling, bury the bacon and smoked loin, cover and simmer 2 hours very gently.','Add the peeled potatoes 40 minutes before the end.','Poach the sausages separately in barely simmering water for 10 minutes — never let them boil.']::text[]),
('boeuf-bourguignon','en','Beef bourguignon','Dijon, France','A peasant stew made famous by Burgundy''s red wine: the beef marinates overnight, then melts over three hours. The garnish of bacon, baby onions and mushrooms is always cooked separately.',ARRAY['Marinate the beef overnight in red wine with carrot, onion, thyme and bay.','Drain and dry the pieces thoroughly, then sear them in small batches.','Dust with flour, add tomato paste, the strained marinade and the broth.','Cover and cook 3 hours at 150°C, until the meat yields to a fork.','Separately fry the bacon, baby onions and mushrooms, and fold them in at the end.']::text[]),
('galette-sarrasin','en','Buckwheat galette','Rennes, France','Buckwheat, grown on Brittany''s poor soils since the 15th century, gives a gluten-free batter spread with a rozell across a scorching bilig. The complète is filled with egg, ham and emmental.',ARRAY['Mix buckwheat flour, salt and one egg, then whisk in the water little by little.','Rest the batter at least 2 hours: it must stay fluid and smooth.','Heat a buttered crêpe pan hard and spread a ladle of batter into a very thin disc.','After 1 minute flip the galette, crack an egg in the centre and scatter ham and cheese.','Fold the four edges into a square and serve piping hot, with dry cider.']::text[]),
('quiche-lorraine','en','Quiche lorraine','Nancy, France','A true quiche lorraine holds neither cheese nor onion: pastry, bacon and a migaine of cream, eggs and nutmeg. The word comes from the Alsatian Küchen, and the recipe is recorded from the 16th century.',ARRAY['Line a tin with the pastry, prick the base and chill for 30 minutes.','Fry the bacon dry until browned and drain it on kitchen paper.','Beat eggs, cream and milk, and season with salt, pepper and grated nutmeg.','Spread the bacon over the pastry and pour the custard on top.','Bake 35 to 40 minutes at 180°C: the centre should still wobble slightly when it comes out.']::text[]),
('ratatouille','en','Ratatouille niçoise','Nice, France','The secret of ratatouille is one simple rule: every vegetable is cooked separately, at its own pace, before being brought together. Nothing should collapse into mush — each mouthful must stay identifiable.',ARRAY['Cut every vegetable into even 2 cm cubes, keeping them apart.','Fry the aubergine alone in olive oil until golden, then set aside.','Repeat with the courgettes, then the peppers, then the onions.','Cook the tomatoes down with garlic, thyme and bay for 20 minutes.','Bring everything together, simmer uncovered for 15 minutes and add basil off the heat.']::text[]),
('tartiflette','en','Tartiflette','Annecy, France','Invented in the 1980s by the reblochon producers'' union to sell more cheese, on the model of an old Savoyard gratin, the péla. The history is recent; the comfort is not diminished.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in their skins for 20 minutes, cool slightly and cut into thick slices.','Fry the bacon and onions, deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.','Rub a dish with garlic and layer potatoes with the bacon and onion mixture.','Add the cream, then lay the reblochon halved, rind facing up.','Bake 25 minutes at 200°C until the cheese bubbles and browns.']::text[]),
('carbonade-flamande','en','Flemish carbonade','Lille, France','The stew of the north, where brown beer replaces wine and a slice of gingerbread spread with mustard, melted into the sauce, brings the sweet-and-sour edge. It is better reheated the next day.',ARRAY['Sear the large cubes of beef in butter, in small batches, then set aside.','Slowly brown the sliced onions in the same pot.','Return the meat, dust with flour, pour in the beer and the vinegar.','Spread the gingerbread with mustard and float it on top, mustard side down.','Cover and simmer 3 hours over very low heat; the sauce thickens by itself.']::text[]),
('piperade','en','Piperade','Bayonne, France','Sweet peppers, tomatoes and Espelette chilli slowly melted, then bound with egg at the last moment. In the colours of the Basque flag, it traditionally comes with a pan-fried slice of Bayonne ham.',ARRAY['Slice the peppers and melt them for 20 minutes with the onions in olive oil.','Add the peeled, chopped tomatoes, garlic, sugar and Espelette chilli.','Cook down uncovered for 20 minutes: the piperade must be unctuous, never watery.','Sear the slices of Bayonne ham for 30 seconds a side and set them aside.','Pour the beaten eggs into the hot piperade and stir off the heat until creamy.']::text[]),
('aligot','en','Aligot','Laguiole, France','Mashed potato beaten with fresh Aubrac tomme until it stretches into long ribbons. Monks served it to pilgrims on the way to Santiago; the arm movement has not changed since.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in salted water, then push them through a ricer — never a blender.','Return the mash to low heat with butter, cream and crushed garlic.','Add the fresh tomme, thinly sliced, in three additions.','Work with a spatula in wide figures of eight, lifting the mass, for 8 to 10 minutes.','Stop the moment the aligot pulls into a continuous ribbon and serve immediately.']::text[]),
('cannele','en','Bordeaux cannelés','Bordeaux, France','An almost burnt crust around a tender rum-and-vanilla custard heart: the whole paradox of the cannelé lies in that contrast. The batter must rest 24 to 48 hours before being poured into copper moulds.',ARRAY['Bring the milk to a bare simmer with the butter and split vanilla pod, then infuse.','Mix flour and sugar, fold in eggs and yolks without whisking, to avoid incorporating air.','Pour in the warm milk in a thin stream, add the rum, strain and rest 24 hours in the fridge.','Coat the copper moulds with the butter-and-beeswax mix, warmed then turned upside down.','Fill three-quarters full and bake 15 minutes at 240°C, then 50 minutes at 180°C; unmould hot.']::text[]),
('blanquette-veau','en','Blanquette de veau','Paris, France','A "white" stew: the meat is never browned but poached in an aromatic broth, which then becomes a sauce enriched with egg yolk and cream. All its finesse comes from that discipline.',ARRAY['Cover the meat with cold water, bring to a bare simmer and skim carefully for 10 minutes.','Add carrots, leeks, clove-studded onion and herbs; poach 1½ hours without ever boiling.','Separately cook the mushrooms and baby onions in a little butter and lemon.','Make a white roux, whisk in the strained broth and cook for 15 minutes.','Off the heat, bind with cream and yolks, add a dash of lemon and bring everything together.']::text[]),
('kouign-amann','en','Kouign-amann','Douarnenez, France','"Butter cake" in Breton: a bread dough laminated with unreasonable amounts of salted butter and sugar, caramelising in the oven. Created around 1860 by a Douarnenez baker who had run short of flour.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water, yeast and salt into a supple dough and let it rise for 1 hour.','Roll out into a rectangle, lay the slab of butter in the centre and fold like an envelope.','Give a single turn, dust generously with sugar, and repeat three times, resting 30 minutes between turns.','Cut and place in buttered, sugared moulds and prove for 30 minutes.','Bake 40 minutes at 200°C and unmould at once, before the caramel sets.']::text[]),
('caldo-verde','en','Caldo verde','Braga, Portugal','The soup of the Minho, now served at every Portuguese festivity: a potato purée enriched with olive oil, into which hair-thin shredded collard is dropped at the last moment. One slice of chouriço is enough to perfume it.',ARRAY['Cook the potatoes, onion and garlic in salted water for 25 minutes.','Blend everything with half the olive oil into a smooth, fluid soup.','Roll the collard leaves and shred them as finely as you possibly can.','Bring the soup back to the boil, throw in the greens and count exactly 5 minutes.','Add pan-fried chouriço slices and a thread of raw olive oil to each bowl.']::text[]),
('pasteis-de-nata','en','Pastéis de nata','Belém, Lisbon, Portugal','Born in the Jerónimos monastery, where monks used up the yolks left after starching their habits with the whites. Baked at ferocious heat, they emerge blistered black, the custard still trembling.',ARRAY['Bring the sugar and water to 105°C with the cinnamon and lemon peel: that is the syrup.','Slake the flour in a little cold milk, then whisk in the rest of the milk, boiling.','Pour in the warm syrup in a thin stream, cool, then add the yolks and strain.','Roll the puff pastry into a tight log, cut discs and press them into the moulds with your thumb.','Fill three-quarters full and bake 12 minutes at 280°C, until the tops blister black.']::text[]),
('cozido-a-portuguesa','en','Cozido à portuguesa','Coimbra, Portugal','The Portuguese boiled dinner: beef, pork, chicken, four kinds of sausage and a dozen vegetables cooked together, then arranged separately. The resulting broth cooks the rice served alongside.',ARRAY['Soak the salted meats overnight, then start them in cold water.','Add the beef, simmer 1 hour while skimming, then the pork and chicken.','Add the sausages only at the end, so they do not burst.','Cook the vegetables separately in a ladle of broth, each according to its own timing.','Cook the rice in the strained broth and arrange meats, sausages and vegetables on one large platter.']::text[]),
('arroz-de-marisco','en','Arroz de marisco','Figueira da Foz, Portugal','Unlike paella, this Portuguese rice is meant to be malandrinho — loose and almost soupy, served in an earthenware pot. A stock made from prawn heads gives the dish all its depth.',ARRAY['Peel the prawns and boil the heads and shells for 20 minutes to make a strong stock.','Open the clams and mussels in white wine, strain their liquor and add it to the stock.','Make a refogado of onion, garlic, pepper and tomato in olive oil.','Add the rice, coat it, then pour in the hot stock — three parts liquid to one of rice.','Cook 15 minutes, add the shellfish, piri-piri and coriander; serve at once, very loose.']::text[]),
('sardinhas-assadas','en','Grilled sardines','Setúbal, Portugal','Every June the whole of Portugal smells of grilled sardines: cooked over charcoal with nothing but coarse salt, eaten on a slice of country bread that catches the fat.',ARRAY['Salt the whole ungutted sardines generously and let them stand for 1 hour.','Prepare hot embers and lay the sardines on a well-heated grill.','Grill 4 to 6 minutes a side, never moving them before the skin releases by itself.','Char the peppers, peel them and dress with oil, garlic and vinegar for the salada de pimentos.','Serve with boiled potatoes and bread, laying the sardines on the slices.']::text[]),
('alheira','en','Alheira de Mirandela','Mirandela, Portugal','A sausage born of subterfuge: Jews forcibly converted in the 16th century made it without pork, from bread and poultry, so it would look as though they ate it. It became an emblem of Trás-os-Montes.',ARRAY['Prick the alheiras lightly so they do not burst while cooking.','Fry them in hot oil for 6 to 8 minutes, turning, until the skin crisps.','Cut the potatoes into batons and fry them in two stages.','Sauté blanched greens with garlic and olive oil.','Finish with a fried egg on top of the alheira, yolk still runny.']::text[]),
('leitao-bairrada','en','Leitão da Bairrada','Mealhada, Portugal','A suckling pig roasted in a wood oven, rubbed with a paste of garlic, lard and pepper slipped under the skin. All of Bairrada judges a roaster on one thing alone: the crackle of the crackling.',ARRAY['Pound garlic, lard, peppercorns, bay and salt into a thick paste.','Lift the skin by hand and massage the paste inside and under it; rest 12 hours.','Spit the pig, baste with white wine and roast 2½ hours at 200°C.','Brush the crackling every 30 minutes with a cloth dipped in the fat mixture.','Carve with a blade and serve with potato crisps, bread and a sparkling wine from Bairrada.']::text[]),
('acorda-alentejana','en','Açorda alentejana','Évora, Portugal','The soup of Alentejo shepherds: nothing but a mortar of garlic and coriander, olive oil, boiling water poured over stale bread, and a poached egg. Proof that a great dish can cost nothing at all.',ARRAY['Pound the garlic with coarse salt and coriander stalks into a green paste.','Loosen the paste with olive oil in the bottom of a large tureen.','Poach the eggs in simmering vinegared water for 3 minutes and set aside.','Pour the boiling poaching water over the garlic paste, whisking.','Add the sliced stale bread, let it swell for 2 minutes, then top with the eggs and chopped coriander.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('bibimbap','en','Bibimbap','Jeonju, South Korea','"Mixed rice": five colours of vegetables laid in a circle on the rice, each seasoned separately, then scrambled together with one stroke of the spoon. In a scorching stone bowl the base forms a golden crust.',ARRAY['Season each vegetable separately: blanched or sautéed, then sesame oil, garlic and salt.','Marinate the sliced beef 20 minutes in soy, sugar, garlic and sesame, then sear it.','Heat an oiled stone bowl hard and press the hot rice into it.','Arrange the vegetables in wedges around the bowl, the meat in the middle.','Crown with a raw yolk, add gochujang and mix vigorously at the table.']::text[]),
('adobo','en','Adobo','Manila, Philippines','Long before the Spanish arrived, Filipinos preserved meat in vinegar; the colonists merely gave the method a name. Vinegar, soy sauce, garlic and bay: four ingredients and no embellishment.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken and pork for 1 hour in soy, vinegar, garlic and pepper.','Bring to the boil without stirring, then simmer uncovered 10 minutes so the vinegar''s edge evaporates.','Add the bay and water, cover and simmer 40 minutes.','Lift out the meat and reduce the sauce with the sugar until it coats a spoon.','Brown the pieces in their own fat, return them to the sauce and serve over rice.']::text[]),
('kottu-roti','en','Kottu roti','Colombo, Sri Lanka','You hear it before you see it: two metal blades chopping godamba roti on a hot plate, in a rhythm each stall makes its own. It is the evening dish, born from the leftovers of lunch.',ARRAY['Cut the rotis into thin strips and the cooked chicken into small pieces.','Stir-fry onion, leek, carrot, chillies and curry leaves over fierce heat.','Push the vegetables aside and scramble the eggs in the cleared space.','Add the chicken, spices and soy sauce and toss for 1 minute.','Throw in the roti strips and chop everything with the spatula, striking the plate, until evenly mixed.']::text[]),
('cevapi','en','Ćevapi','Sarajevo, Bosnia and Herzegovina','Little rolls of minced meat grilled over charcoal, served ten at a time inside a somun flatbread still puffed with steam, with raw onion and kajmak. An Ottoman legacy turned Balkan identity.',ARRAY['Mix the meats with crushed garlic, bicarbonate, salt and sparkling water.','Work the mass for 10 minutes until sticky, then rest it 12 hours in the fridge.','Shape rolls 8 cm long and about as thick as a finger.','Grill over hot coals 3 to 4 minutes a side, never pressing them.','Split the bread, fill with ten ćevapi, finely sliced raw onion and a spoonful of kajmak.']::text[]),
('cepelinai','en','Cepelinai','Vilnius, Lithuania','Dumplings of grated potato stuffed with meat, whose elongated shape earned them the name "zeppelins" in the 1920s. The bacon and sour cream sauce is not negotiable.',ARRAY['Finely grate three-quarters of the raw potatoes and wring them in a cloth to extract the juice.','Let the juice settle, recover the starch at the bottom and add it to the pulp, along with the remaining quarter, boiled and mashed.','Season the minced pork with a softened onion, marjoram, salt and pepper.','Shape balls of dough in a damp palm, fill them and close into a spindle.','Poach 25 minutes in gently simmering salted water and top with rendered bacon and sour cream.']::text[]),
('shakshuka','en','Shakshuka','Tel Aviv, Israel','Brought from North Africa by Tunisian and Libyan Jews, this pan of peppers and tomatoes with eggs cracked into it became the late breakfast of the whole Levant. Bread replaces cutlery.',ARRAY['Soften the onion and sliced peppers in olive oil for 15 minutes.','Add garlic, cumin, paprika and harissa and warm for 1 minute to release the aromas.','Pour in the chopped tomatoes and reduce for 15 minutes to a thick sauce.','Make wells with a spoon, crack the eggs into them and salt the yolks.','Cover and cook 6 to 8 minutes: whites set, yolks running; scatter with feta and coriander.']::text[]),
('romazava','en','Romazava','Antananarivo, Madagascar','Madagascar''s national dish: a broth of mixed meats in which brèdes mafana melt — leaves that leave a faint tingling numbness on the tongue. It is served with rice, morning and evening.',ARRAY['Sear the large cubes of meat in oil with the onion, garlic and grated ginger.','Add the chopped tomatoes and let them melt for 10 minutes.','Cover with water and simmer for 1 hour, until the meat is tender.','Add the washed brèdes and the spinach and cook 10 more minutes, uncovered.','Adjust the seasoning and serve the broth and meats over hot white rice.']::text[]),
('waakye','en','Waakye','Accra, Ghana','Rice and beans cooked together with red sorghum leaves, which give the dish its unmistakable brown colour. Sold in leaves at daybreak, it is served with whatever the vendor has on the stall.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then cook them 40 minutes with the sorghum leaves and bicarbonate.','Remove the leaves: the broth should be a deep reddish brown.','Add the rinsed rice to this broth, salt, cover and cook 20 minutes on low heat.','Make the shito: chillies, onion and tomatoes blended and fried long in oil.','Serve with hard-boiled eggs, gari, fried plantain and a ladle of sauce.']::text[]),
('mofongo','en','Mofongo','San Juan, Puerto Rico','A direct descendant of West African fufu adapted to plantain: fried chunks are pounded in a wooden pilón with garlic, salt and pork fat. Served as a dome, often hollowed and filled with seafood.',ARRAY['Peel the green plantains and cut them into 3 cm lengths.','Fry them 6 minutes at 175°C: tender inside but not browned.','Pound the garlic with salt and olive oil in a wooden mortar.','Crush the hot plantain with this paste and the crisp cracklings, adding a little broth.','Press the mixture into a bowl, turn out as a dome and serve with a squeeze of lime.']::text[]),
('chivito','en','Chivito','Montevideo, Uruguay','Born of a misunderstanding in 1946: an Argentine customer asked for goat, the cook had none and improvised with beef fillet. The resulting sandwich became Uruguay''s national dish.',ARRAY['Flatten the fillet slices to 1 cm and salt them at the last moment.','Grill the bacon until it snaps, then the steaks 1 minute a side on a scorching griddle.','Lay the cheese on the meat while still on the heat so it barely melts.','Fry the eggs and toast the buttered buns.','Build: mayonnaise, lettuce, tomato, cheesy steak, ham, bacon, egg, olives; close and serve with chips.']::text[]),
('carbonara','en','Carbonara','Rome, Italy','No cream ever goes into a true carbonara: only guanciale, pecorino, egg yolks and black pepper. The sauce sets off the heat, using only the warmth of the pasta, in a motion that forgives no hesitation.',ARRAY['Cut the guanciale into strips and render it dry until crisp.','Whisk the yolks, whole egg, grated pecorino and plenty of cracked pepper.','Cook the spaghetti 1 minute short of the package time, very al dente.','Off the heat, toss the pasta with the guanciale and a little pasta water.','Pour in the egg mixture, stirring fast and constantly, so it turns creamy and never scrambles.']::text[]),
('ossobuco-milanese','en','Ossobuco alla milanese','Milan, Italy','A veal shank braised until the marrow slips free on its own, served with gremolata — lemon zest, garlic and parsley — that cuts through the richness with one stroke of freshness.',ARRAY['Dust the tied shanks with flour and brown them all over in butter.','Set aside, then soften onion, carrot and celery in the same pot.','Return the meat, deglaze with white wine, add tomato, broth and bay.','Cover and simmer 2 hours over very low heat, turning once.','Chop lemon zest, garlic and parsley for the gremolata and scatter it over each plate at serving.']::text[]),
('trofie-al-pesto','en','Trofie al pesto genovese','Genoa, Italy','Prà basil, grown on the slopes above the sea, crushed in a mortar with pine nuts and parmesan so the leaf is never warmed. Green beans and potatoes, boiled in the pasta water, are always added.',ARRAY['Pound basil, garlic and a pinch of coarse salt in a mortar to a fine paste.','Add the pine nuts, then the grated cheeses, still pounding gently.','Work in the olive oil in a thin stream, never whisking, to keep the vivid green.','Roll the trofie by hand from flour and water, twisting each small piece.','Cook pasta, diced potato and beans together, drain and toss with the pesto off the heat.']::text[]),
('ribollita','en','Ribollita','Florence, Italy','"Reboiled": a peasant soup of cannellini beans, Tuscan black kale and stale bread, better the next day when reheated in a pan until a crust forms on the bottom.',ARRAY['Cook the soaked beans for 1 hour, then blend half to thicken the broth.','Soften onion, carrot and celery in olive oil for 15 minutes.','Add black kale, cabbage and tomato, moisten with the bean broth and cook 30 minutes.','Stir in the remaining beans and slices of stale bread, let them swell for 15 minutes.','Reheat the next day in a pan with a drizzle of oil, until a golden crust forms.']::text[]),
('orecchiette-cime-rapa','en','Orecchiette alle cime di rapa','Bari, Italy','Small ear-shaped pasta, hollowed with the thumb, that catches the sauce in its little cup. Slightly bitter turnip tops are melted with anchovy and chilli until they almost dissolve into the sauce.',ARRAY['Knead semolina, flour and water into a firm dough, rest 30 minutes.','Roll thin ropes, cut small discs and hollow each with the thumb on the worktop.','Melt garlic and anchovies in the oil until they dissolve, add the chilli.','Cook the orecchiette with the turnip tops in the same boiling water, 10 minutes.','Drain pasta and greens together and toss them in the pan with the garlic and anchovy.']::text[]),
('arancini','en','Arancini','Palermo, Italy','Balls of saffron rice, breaded and fried, named for the little orange they resemble. Originally an Arab-Norman festive dish, stuffed with meat, peas and melting mozzarella.',ARRAY['Cook the saffron rice like a risotto without constant stirring, then let it cool completely.','Make a small ragù of minced beef, tomato, onion and peas, well reduced.','Shape a hollow ball of rice in your palm, fill with ragù and a cube of mozzarella, then close.','Coat each ball in flour, beaten egg, then breadcrumbs.','Fry at 170°C until golden and crisp; drain and serve hot.']::text[]),
('tortellini-in-brodo','en','Tortellini in brodo','Bologna, Italy','Tiny pasta stuffed with pork, mortadella and parmesan, folded into the shape of a navel — local legend has them invented by an innkeeper who peeked at a goddess''s through a keyhole.',ARRAY['Make a broth with beef, chicken, carrot, celery and onion, simmer 3 hours, skimming.','Knead flour and eggs into a smooth dough, rest 30 minutes under a cloth.','Finely mince roast pork, mortadella and ham, bind with parmesan, egg and nutmeg.','Roll the dough paper-thin, cut 4 cm squares, fill and fold into a triangle then a navel around your finger.','Poach the tortellini for 3 minutes straight in the strained, boiling broth and serve at once.']::text[]),
('brasato-barolo','en','Brasato al Barolo','Alba, Italy','A beef roast marinated overnight in a whole bottle of Barolo, then braised until the wine, reduced and blended, becomes an almost black sauce. Creamy polenta is its only legitimate escort.',ARRAY['Marinate the tied roast overnight in the wine with vegetables and spices.','Drain the meat, pat it dry and brown it all over in butter.','Add the marinade vegetables, then the strained wine; cover and braise 3 hours over very low heat.','Remove the meat, blend the sauce until smooth and coating, adjust the seasoning.','Slice the roast, coat with sauce and serve with a creamy polenta cooked in broth.']::text[]),
('cacio-e-pepe','en','Cacio e pepe','Rome, Italy','Two ingredients, pecorino and black pepper, and yet one of the hardest dishes in Italian cooking: the cheese must turn creamy in the pasta water without ever clumping into stringy lumps.',ARRAY['Toast the cracked pepper dry for 1 minute to wake its oils.','Cook the pasta in lightly salted water, saving plenty of pasta water.','Grate the pecorino very finely and loosen it with a little warm water into a smooth cream.','Drain the pasta very al dente, toss it with the pepper off the heat.','Fold in the pecorino cream, stirring vigorously, adjusting water to the desired silky texture.']::text[]),
('focaccia-di-recco','en','Focaccia di Recco','Recco, Italy','Nothing like the soft, spongy focaccia: here two sheets of dough, stretched almost transparent, enclose crescenza, a fresh cheese that melts into a creamy pocket in a very hot oven.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water, oil and salt into a supple dough, rest 1 hour under an oiled cloth.','Divide in two and stretch each piece by hand on a floured surface until almost transparent.','Dot the first sheet with knobs of crescenza cheese.','Cover with the second sheet, pinch the edges and pierce a few holes to let steam escape.','Bake 10 to 12 minutes at 250°C until large golden patches appear.']::text[]),
('sarde-in-saor','en','Sarde in saor','Venice, Italy','Invented so Venetian sailors could carry fish that would keep at sea: fried sardines marinated under layers of sweet vinegared onions, with raisins and pine nuts. Best after two days'' rest.',ARRAY['Gut and scale the sardines, dust with flour and fry until golden.','Slice the onions finely and melt them slowly in oil until translucent.','Deglaze with vinegar, add the bay and let it reduce slightly.','Soak the raisins in a little warm water and drain.','Layer sardines, onions, raisins and pine nuts in a dish; cover and marinate 2 days in the fridge before serving at room temperature.']::text[]),
('pizza-margherita','en','Pizza margherita','Naples, Italy','A dough risen three times, stretched by hand and never touched by a rolling pin, baked ninety seconds in a wood oven at 450°C. Tomato, mozzarella and basil are said to echo the colours of the Italian flag.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water, yeast and salt for 10 minutes into a smooth, elastic dough.','Let it rise 2 hours at room temperature, divide into balls and let rise a further 4 hours.','Stretch each ball by hand into a thin disc, keeping a thick rim.','Top with crushed tomato, torn drained mozzarella, a drizzle of oil and salt.','Bake in the hottest oven possible until the rim is puffed and charred in spots, add fresh basil straight after.']::text[]),
('tortilla-espanola','en','Spanish tortilla','Madrid, Spain','Nothing but potatoes confited in olive oil, onion and eggs — and yet two irreconcilable camps: with or without onion. The real argument is over the centre, which must stay runny.',ARRAY['Slice the potatoes thinly and confit them 20 minutes in oil over low heat with the onion.','Drain, keeping the oil, and lightly crush a few slices.','Beat the eggs, season, and fold in the still-warm potatoes; rest 10 minutes.','Pour into a pan with a little reserved oil, cook 3 minutes on high heat then covered 2 minutes on low.','Flip the tortilla using a plate and cook 2 more minutes: the centre should stay creamy.']::text[]),
('fabada-asturiana','en','Asturian fabada','Oviedo, Spain','A stew of fabes — large white Asturian beans — cooked with chorizo, morcilla and smoked bacon, its saffron broth thick enough to coat a spoon. It is never stirred, only shaken in the pot.',ARRAY['Soak the fabes overnight, then start them in cold water without salt.','Add onion, garlic and bay, bring to the barest simmer and skim often.','Add the whole cuts of meat and cook 2 hours over the lowest heat, shaking the pot from time to time.','Take a little hot broth to steep the saffron, then return it to the pot.','Rest 20 minutes off the heat before serving, the sliced meats laid over the stew.']::text[]),
('pulpo-a-la-gallega','en','Galician-style octopus','Santiago de Compostela, Spain','Served on a wooden board, snipped with scissors and dusted with smoked paprika and coarse salt. The pulpeiras'' secret: dip the octopus three times into boiling water before letting it cook, so it never toughens.',ARRAY['Bring a large pot of unsalted water to the boil.','Hold the octopus by the head and dip it three times for a few seconds into the boiling water before submerging it fully.','Cook 40 to 50 minutes depending on size, until a fork sinks in without resistance.','Cook the potatoes in the same water, then slice them thickly on the board.','Snip the octopus into rounds with scissors, arrange over the potatoes, drizzle with olive oil and dust with both paprikas and coarse salt.']::text[]),
('cochinillo-segoviano','en','Segovia-style suckling pig','Segovia, Spain','A three-week-old suckling pig, roasted in a wood oven until the skin cracks like glass. In the tradition of Segovia''s mesones, it is symbolically carved with the edge of a plate, never a knife.',ARRAY['Rub the whole suckling pig with lard and salt, inside and out.','Lay it on a bed of bay and garlic in an earthenware dish, add water and wine.','Roast 1½ hours at 160°C, skin down, basting regularly with the juices.','Carefully turn skin up and continue 30 minutes at 220°C until it cracks.','Rest 10 minutes and carve with a plate, pressing the back so the skin audibly cracks.']::text[]),
('cocido-madrileno','en','Madrid-style cocido','Madrid, Spain','A chickpea stew served in three successive vuelcos — courses: first the noodle soup, then the vegetables, finally the meats. A winter lunch that occupies an entire Sunday afternoon.',ARRAY['Soak the chickpeas overnight in salted water, tied in gauze to keep them whole.','Start beef, chicken, marrow bone and bacon in cold water, skim, then add the chickpeas.','Simmer 2 hours, adding chorizo, carrot and potato 40 minutes before the end.','Cook the cabbage separately and sauté it with garlic.','Serve first the broth with vermicelli, then the vegetables and chickpeas, finally the sliced meats.']::text[]),
('pisto-manchego','en','Pisto manchego','Toledo, Spain','La Mancha''s ratatouille, simmered slowly to an almost jammy texture rather than crisp. Traditionally crowned with a fried egg, so the yolk mingles into the melting vegetables.',ARRAY['Melt onion, peppers and garlic in olive oil for 15 minutes over low heat.','Add the diced courgettes and continue for 10 minutes.','Stir in the peeled, chopped tomatoes, sugar and salt.','Simmer uncovered for 25 minutes, stirring little, until jammy.','Serve hot, each plate crowned with a fried egg, yolk still runny.']::text[]),
('migas-extremenas','en','Migas extremeñas','Cáceres, Spain','The stale bread of Extremadura''s shepherds, crumbled, dampened overnight then fried in pork fat until it turns into small dry golden pearls. Served with grapes or chorizo, depending on the day''s mood.',ARRAY['Crumble the stale bread very finely, dampen it with salted water and cover with a damp cloth overnight.','Fry the diced pork belly and chorizo in lard, set aside.','Brown the whole unpeeled garlic cloves in the same fat, add the paprika off the heat.','Add the crumbled bread and fry over high heat for 15 minutes, stirring constantly with a spatula to dry it out.','Return the meats and garlic, serve piping hot with a bunch of fresh grapes.']::text[]),
('calcots-romesco','en','Calçots with romesco','Valls, Spain','Large sweet Catalan onions, grilled over vine cuttings until fully blackened, peeled with the fingers and dipped in romesco. The calçotada is eaten outdoors, a bib tied round the neck.',ARRAY['Grill the whole calçots over hot embers until the skin is fully blackened.','Wrap them in newspaper for 10 minutes to finish steaming in their own heat.','Blend roasted peppers, tomato, almonds, hazelnuts, garlic, soaked ñora peppers and fried bread into a paste.','Whip in the olive oil like a mayonnaise, season with vinegar and salt.','Peel the calçots with your fingers, pulling off the blackened skin, and dip them into the romesco.']::text[]),
('marmitako','en','Marmitako','San Sebastián, Spain','The stew Basque sailors made on board with freshly caught tuna and the ship''s stores: potatoes broken by hand to release their starch and thicken the broth without flour.',ARRAY['Melt onion, peppers and garlic in olive oil for 15 minutes.','Add the chopped tomato and paprika off the heat, let it cook down for 10 minutes.','Break the potatoes with a knife tip rather than cutting them, so they release their starch.','Add them with the wine and broth, cook 20 minutes covered until the broth thickens.','Add the tuna in large cubes, turn off the heat and let it poach 5 minutes in the residual warmth.']::text[]),
('salmorejo-cordobes','en','Salmorejo cordobés','Córdoba, Spain','Thicker and richer than its cousin gazpacho: far more bread, far more olive oil, blended until as smooth as cream. Served cold, crowned with hard-boiled egg and serrano ham.',ARRAY['Blend the raw tomatoes with the garlic until juiced smooth.','Add the crumbled stale bread and let it soak up the juice for 10 minutes.','Blend at length, at least 5 minutes, pouring in the olive oil in a thin stream.','Add the sherry vinegar and salt, blend again to a perfectly smooth texture.','Chill 2 hours and serve very cold, crowned with crumbled hard-boiled egg and diced serrano ham.']::text[]),
('empanada-gallega','en','Galician empanada','Vigo, Spain','A large yeasted pie filled with a slow-cooked jam of tuna or cod and peppers, cooked until almost sweet. It is eaten cold, cut into squares, straight from a picnic basket.',ARRAY['Knead flour, yeast, oil, water and salt into a supple dough, let it rise 1½ hours.','Melt the onions and peppers very slowly in oil, at least 45 minutes, until caramelised.','Add tomato and paprika, cook down 15 more minutes, then fold in the drained tuna.','Roll out half the dough, fill, cover with the rest and seal the edges by rolling them.','Brush with beaten egg and bake 30 to 35 minutes at 200°C until deep golden on top.']::text[]),
('churros-con-chocolate','en','Churros con chocolate','Madrid, Spain','Choux pastry piped into long ridged sticks, fried crisp outside and tender inside, dunked in a chocolate as thick as a happily failed custard. The breakfast of the morning after a party.',ARRAY['Bring salted water to the boil, tip in the flour all at once and beat vigorously off the heat until the dough pulls from the sides.','Cool 5 minutes, then fill a piping bag fitted with a ridged nozzle.','Fry long sticks at 180°C, snipping them with scissors over the oil, until golden.','Drain and roll while still hot in sugar.','Melt the chocolate in the milk with the cornflour, stirring until thick, and serve piping hot for dunking.']::text[]),
('baklava','en','Baklava','Gaziantep, Türkiye','Forty sheets of filo, each brushed with melted butter, layered over crushed pistachios, then drenched in a hot syrup poured over the cold pastry, so it stays crisp.',ARRAY['Roughly chop the pistachios with a knife, not a blender, to keep some crunch.','Butter a dish, layer eight sheets of filo, buttering each, then spread half the pistachios.','Repeat with eight more sheets, the rest of the pistachios, then finish with eight last buttered sheets.','Cut into diamonds before baking and bake 35 minutes at 180°C until golden on top.','Make a syrup of sugar, water, lemon and honey, and pour it hot over the cold baklava; rest 4 hours.']::text[]),
('mochi-daifuku','en','Daifuku mochi','Kyoto, Japan','A glutinous rice dough, steamed then pounded until elastic, wrapped around a ball of sweetened red bean paste. The work is done with starch-dusted, damp hands, or everything sticks.',ARRAY['Roll the red bean paste into twelve small balls and chill.','Mix glutinous rice flour, sugar and water, steam 8 minutes, stirring halfway through.','Turn the cooked dough onto a surface heavily dusted with starch, while still scalding hot.','Divide into twelve pieces with damp, starch-dusted hands, never letting it cool.','Flatten each piece, enclose a ball of bean paste and pinch closed to seal.']::text[]),
('schwarzwaelder-kirschtorte','en','Black Forest cake','Triberg, Germany','Layers of chocolate sponge soaked in kirsch, built up with whipped cream and morello cherries, all covered in dark chocolate shavings. The name is said to come from the region''s black, white and red traditional dress.',ARRAY['Bake a chocolate sponge, let it cool, then slice it into three discs.','Stew the morello cherries with a little sugar, drain them, keeping the juice.','Soak each disc with the cherry juice mixed with kirsch.','Whip the cream with icing sugar, layering discs, cream and cherries.','Cover entirely with cream, decorate with dark chocolate shavings and whole cherries.']::text[]),
('sachertorte','en','Sachertorte','Vienna, Austria','Invented in 1832 by a sixteen-year-old apprentice for a prince disappointed with his dessert, this chocolate sponge is split, filled with apricot jam and coated in a glaze so smooth it must catch the light.',ARRAY['Melt the chocolate, mix it with softened butter and half the sugar.','Add the yolks one by one, then the sifted flour.','Whip the whites with the rest of the sugar to stiff peaks and fold in gently.','Bake 45 minutes at 170°C, cool, then split the cake and coat with warm apricot jam, including the top.','Make a glaze of icing sugar and water heated with chocolate, pour it on in one go and smooth without going over it twice.']::text[]),
('tres-leches','en','Tres leches cake','León, Nicaragua','A sponge deliberately dry and porous, pricked all over, so it soaks up a mix of whole milk, condensed milk and evaporated milk without falling apart. Served chilled, with a light meringue or whipped cream.',ARRAY['Separate the eggs, whip the whites to stiff peaks with half the sugar.','Fold in the yolks and remaining sugar, then the sifted flour and baking powder, lifting gently.','Bake 25 minutes at 180°C in a rectangular tin, cool, then prick the whole surface with a fork.','Mix the three milks and pour them slowly over the cold cake, in stages, until fully absorbed.','Chill 3 hours, cover with whipped cream and dust with cinnamon before serving.']::text[]),
('gulab-jamun','en','Gulab jamun','Agra, India','Small balls of milk powder fried slowly over low heat to a deep brown, then submerged in a cardamom and rose water syrup. They double in size as they soak, without ever falling apart.',ARRAY['Mix milk powder, flour and baking powder, work in the ghee then enough milk to form a soft dough without kneading.','Roll perfectly smooth balls, with no cracks, about the size of a walnut.','Fry over steady low heat, swirling the oil, until an even deep brown, 10 minutes.','Make a syrup of sugar, water and cardamom, barely brought to the boil, scented with rose water.','Drop the hot balls into the warm syrup and let them swell for at least 2 hours before serving.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('basbousa','en','Basbousa','Alexandria, Egypt','A semolina and yoghurt cake, cut into diamonds before it even goes in the oven, each piece crowned with an almond. Cold syrup poured over the scalding cake creates its moist, orange-blossom-scented contrast.',ARRAY['Mix semolina, yoghurt, sugar, melted butter and baking powder into a smooth batter.','Spread in a buttered dish, cut into diamonds immediately and place an almond on each.','Bake 30 minutes at 180°C until golden on top.','Make a syrup of sugar, water, lemon and orange blossom water, let it cool completely.','Pour the cold syrup over the cake straight from the oven, let it soak in before serving.']::text[]),
('stroopwafel','en','Stroopwafel','Gouda, Netherlands','Two thin waffles cooked in an iron, split while still hot and filled with a thick brown-sugar syrup. It is placed atop a cup of hot coffee so the steam just softens the syrup.',ARRAY['Knead flour, yeast, melted butter, egg and milk into a supple dough, let rise 1 hour.','Shape balls and cook 2 minutes in a very hot round waffle iron with a shallow pattern.','While still hot, split each waffle into two thin discs with a knife.','Mix brown sugar, butter, syrup and cinnamon heated to a syrupy texture.','Spread one disc with hot syrup and close at once with the other, pressing the edges.']::text[]),
('gaufres-liege','en','Liège waffle','Liège, Belgium','A brioche-like dough, risen twice, into which pearl sugar is folded — it does not melt but caramelises into small crunchy pockets against the hot iron. Eaten plain, by hand, in the street.',ARRAY['Knead flour, yeast, eggs, milk and salt into a supple dough, let rise 1 hour.','Work in the softened butter little by little while kneading, until absorbed.','Let it rise a second time for 30 minutes, then fold in the pearl sugar without melting it.','Shape oval portions and rest them for 15 minutes.','Cook 3 minutes per waffle in a very hot iron, until the sugar caramelises on the surface.']::text[]),
('pastel-de-nata-macaense','en','Macanese egg tart','Macau, China','A direct descendant of the Portuguese pastel de nata, adapted by a British confectioner in the 1990s: the top is not spotted black but scorched in wide caramel patches, in the style of a crème brûlée.',ARRAY['Roll the puff pastry into a tight log, cut discs and press them into moulds with your thumb.','Warm cream, milk and sugar without boiling, let cool slightly.','Whisk the yolks with the condensed milk, pour the warm mixture in a thin stream over it and strain.','Fill the pastry cases three-quarters full.','Bake 15 minutes at 250°C until wide caramelised patches appear, almost scorched.']::text[]),
('sangria','en','Sangria','Barcelona, Spain','A young red wine in which summer fruit has macerated for hours, topped at the last moment with a splash of soda to keep its fizz. The golden rule: never use a wine you would not drink on its own.',ARRAY['Cut the oranges, lemon and apple into thin wedges, unpeeled.','Mix them with the wine, sugar, cinnamon and brandy in a large pitcher.','Cover and let macerate in the fridge at least 4 hours, ideally overnight.','Remove the cinnamon stick and taste to adjust the sugar.','Add the soda and ice just before serving, to keep the fizz.']::text[]),
('caipirinha','en','Caipirinha','Paraty, Brazil','The whole lime, cut into wedges and muddled with sugar to release the bitter oils from its peel, is the one true signature of this sugarcane spirit. It is always served muddled in its own glass, never strained.',ARRAY['Wash the lime and cut it into eight wedges, removing the bitter white tips.','Put the wedges and sugar in the bottom of the glass.','Muddle vigorously for 30 seconds, until the sugar soaks up the juice and essential oils.','Fill the glass with crushed ice.','Pour in the cachaça and stir well with a spoon to distribute the sugar.']::text[]),
('mojito','en','Mojito','Havana, Cuba','The mint is gently clapped between the palms rather than crushed, to release its scent without bringing out its green bitterness. It is the drink a little too readily attributed to a certain writer of Old Havana.',ARRAY['Clap the mint leaves between your palms to wake them, without crushing them.','Cut the lime into wedges, muddle lightly with the sugar in the bottom of the glass.','Add the mint and muddle just a little, only enough to bruise it.','Fill with crushed ice, pour in the white rum and stir.','Top up with sparkling water, stir once more and garnish with a mint sprig.']::text[]),
('horchata-mexicana','en','Mexican horchata','Puebla, Mexico','Rice soaked overnight then blended with cinnamon and vanilla, finely strained to keep only the white, fragrant milk. A dairy-free, alcohol-free drink, sold ice-cold from large glass jars.',ARRAY['Soak the rice with the cinnamon stick in the water overnight at room temperature.','Blend at length the rice, soaking water and vanilla until milky white.','Strain through a fine cloth, pressing well to extract all the rice milk.','Sweeten and lengthen with the milk, taste and adjust texture with a little water.','Chill at least 2 hours and serve ice-cold, dusted with cinnamon.']::text[]),
('gluehwein','en','Glühwein','Nuremberg, Germany','The red wine of German Christmas markets, warmed but never boiled with cinnamon, cloves and orange zest. Boiling it would drive off the alcohol, true, but also every last bit of its aroma.',ARRAY['Peel the orange zest in wide ribbons, avoiding the bitter white pith.','Pour the wine into a pan with the zest, cinnamon, cloves, star anise and sugar.','Heat very gently for 15 minutes, never letting it simmer or boil.','Taste and adjust the sugar depending on the wine used.','Strain out the spices and serve piping hot in heatproof glasses, with a cinnamon stick.']::text[]),
('chai-masala','en','Chai masala','Mumbai, India','Black tea boiled with ginger, cardamom, cinnamon and clove, then whisked with milk to raise a light froth. Every Indian family keeps its own balance of spices, jealously guarded.',ARRAY['Roughly crush ginger, cardamom, cinnamon, cloves and pepper in a mortar.','Bring the water to the boil with these spices, let it steep hard for 5 minutes, covered.','Add the black tea, boil for 2 minutes.','Pour in the milk and sugar, bring back to the boil three times, watching that it does not overflow.','Strain while whisking vigorously to raise a froth, and serve very hot.']::text[]),
('sauerbraten','en','Sauerbraten','Cologne, Germany','A roast marinated for up to five days in vinegar, red wine and spices, to tenderise a cut once too tough to roast plain. The sauce is traditionally thickened with crumbled gingerbread, giving it its near-black colour.',ARRAY['Marinate the roast 3 to 5 days in the fridge in vinegar, wine, water, sliced vegetables and spices, turning it daily.','Drain the meat, keeping the marinade, pat dry and brown it all over in butter.','Add the marinade vegetables, pour in the strained liquid and braise 2h30 covered over low heat.','Remove the meat, blend the sauce with the gingerbread crumbs until it thickens.','Add the raisins, adjust the sweet-sour balance with sugar, slice the roast and coat with hot sauce.']::text[]),
('currywurst','en','Currywurst','Berlin, Germany','Born in 1949 from a Berlin woman bartering with British soldiers for curry powder and ketchup, this grilled sausage sliced into rounds and drenched in spiced tomato sauce became the emblem of Berlin street food, eaten standing, no cutlery.',ARRAY['Sweat the sliced onion in the oil until translucent.','Add tomato, ketchup, sugar, vinegar and paprika, simmer 15 minutes into a thick sauce.','Grill the sausages in a pan or on a barbecue until the skin cracks and browns.','Slice them into thick rounds on a plate or cardboard tray.','Ladle generously with hot sauce and dust with extra curry powder before serving with fries.']::text[]),
('stamppot','en','Stamppot','Amsterdam, Netherlands','"Mashed pot": potatoes crushed with kale or endive, served with a smoked sausage and a well of brown pan juices at the centre. A simple winter dish, made to warm without ever tiring the palate.',ARRAY['Peel and chunk the potatoes, boil in salted water for 20 minutes.','Add the shredded kale to the potato water 5 minutes before it finishes.','Poach the smoked sausage separately in barely simmering water for 15 minutes.','Drain potatoes and kale, mash them with butter, milk and nutmeg.','Serve the mash with a well pressed in the centre, topped with mustard and slices of hot smoked sausage.']::text[]),
('moules-frites','en','Moules-frites','Brussels, Belgium','Mussels opened in minutes over a steam of white wine, celery and shallot, served in their own pot with twice-fried chips — true Belgian frying, never the other way round.',ARRAY['Scrub and debeard the mussels, discard any that stay open.','Sweat the sliced shallot and celery in butter, moisten with white wine.','Tip the mussels into the boiling liquid, cover and shake the pot for 5 minutes until they all open.','Cut the potatoes into batons, fry once at 150°C then drain.','Fry the chips again at 190°C until golden and crisp, serve with the mussels and chopped parsley.']::text[]),
('farikal','en','Fårikål','Oslo, Norway','Norway''s national dish, chosen by popular vote in 1972: mutton and cabbage layered in a single pot with whole peppercorns and nothing else, simmered until the meat falls from the bone.',ARRAY['Cut the mutton into large bone-in pieces and the cabbage into wide wedges.','Layer meat and cabbage in a large pot, salting and peppering each layer generously.','Dust a little flour between the layers to thicken the sauce slightly.','Pour in the water, cover and let it barely simmer for 2h30 without stirring.','Check the meat falls off with a fork, serve with boiled potatoes.']::text[]),
('smorrebrod','en','Smørrebrød','Copenhagen, Denmark','A single slice of dark rye bread, buttered to the edges then topped with one single garnish — pickled herring, egg and shrimp, or roast beef — never stacked, always eaten with knife and fork.',ARRAY['Butter each slice of rye bread right to the edges, leaving no dry patch.','Hard-boil the eggs for 9 minutes, cool and slice into rounds.','For the herring version, arrange the pickled fillets on the butter, scatter with red onion and dill.','For the shrimp version, bind them with mayonnaise and mound on top of the egg slices.','Garnish each open sandwich with sliced radish and a sprig of fresh dill, serve at once, chilled.']::text[]),
('karjalanpiirakka','en','Karjalanpiirakka','Karelia, Finland','Thin rye crusts shaped into little boats, filled with a milk-and-rice porridge and baked in a very hot oven until the edges blister. Served with munavoi, a butter mashed with hard-boiled egg, a breakfast staple.',ARRAY['Cook the rice in the milk over very low heat for 40 minutes, stirring often, into a thick porridge.','Knead rye flour, flour, water and salt into a firm dough, rest 20 minutes.','Roll the dough paper-thin, cut ovals and thin the edges further.','Fill the centre with rice porridge, fold the edges in, pinching to form an open little boat.','Bake 15 minutes at 250°C, brush with melted butter on coming out and serve warm with munavoi.']::text[]),
('plokkfiskur','en','Plokkfiskur','Reykjavik, Iceland','A dish born to waste nothing: leftover poached cod or haddock, roughly flaked into a light béchamel with potatoes, served with dark rye bread traditionally steamed by volcanic ground heat.',ARRAY['Poach the cod for 8 minutes in barely simmering, lightly salted water, drain and roughly flake.','Boil the potatoes, drain and dice.','Sweat the sliced onion in butter, dust with flour and whisk in the milk for a light béchamel.','Add the curry powder, flaked fish and potatoes, fold gently without crushing.','Warm through gently for 5 minutes without boiling, serve with dark rye bread and a little butter.']::text[]),
('irish-stew','en','Irish stew','Dublin, Ireland','A mutton or lamb stew so simple it traditionally holds only meat, potatoes, onions and water — not even a carrot originally, its thickness coming purely from the potatoes breaking down as they cook.',ARRAY['Cut the lamb into large chunks, peel the potatoes, onions and carrots.','Layer meat, onions, carrots and half the potatoes in a pot.','Cover with the water, add the bay leaves, cover and let it barely simmer for 1h30 over very low heat.','Add the remaining potatoes cut into large chunks and cook a further 30 minutes, until they break down slightly and thicken the broth.','Adjust the seasoning, scatter with chopped parsley and serve piping hot in deep bowls.']::text[]),
('svickova','en','Svíčková na smetaně','Prague, Czechia','A beef sirloin marinated then braised on a bed of root vegetables — carrot, celery, parsnip — blended into a creamy, tangy sauce, served with knedlíky bread dumplings and a spoonful of cranberry preserve.',ARRAY['Marinate the beef overnight with the sliced roots, vinegar, bay and spices.','Brown the meat larded with bacon, then add the marinade vegetables and braise 2 hours covered.','Remove the meat, blend the vegetable sauce with the cream until smooth and velvety.','Make the knedlíky dough with flour, yeast, egg and milk, shape into logs and poach 20 minutes in boiling water.','Slice the beef, coat with sauce, serve with sliced knedlíky and a spoonful of cranberry preserve.']::text[]),
('sarmale','en','Sarmale','Bucharest, Romania','Whole fermented cabbage leaves, rolled around minced pork, rice and dill, simmered for hours in an earthenware pot until the cabbage turns meltingly soft. A Christmas staple, even better reheated the next day.',ARRAY['Gently peel the leaves off the fermented cabbage, trim any overly thick ribs.','Mix minced pork, raw rice, chopped onion and chopped dill, season with salt and pepper.','Place a spoonful of filling at the edge of each leaf and roll tightly, folding in the sides.','Line the bottom of a pot with bacon lardons and shredded cabbage, pack in the rolls tightly, cover with tomato and water to the top.','Simmer covered for 2 hours over very low heat, adding water if needed, until the cabbage is meltingly soft.']::text[]),
('banitsa','en','Banitsa','Sofia, Bulgaria','Filo sheets crumpled by hand, never stacked flat, layered with a mix of sirene white cheese and beaten eggs. On New Year''s Eve, a small folded paper predicting the year''s fortune is hidden inside.',ARRAY['Crumble the sirene cheese and mash it with a fork with three beaten eggs.','Butter each filo sheet generously, crumple it carelessly into a loose heap.','Arrange the crumpled sheets in circles in a buttered round tin, filling in with cheese as you go.','Whisk the remaining two eggs with the yoghurt and baking soda, pour over the top.','Bake 35 minutes at 180°C until puffed and well browned on top, let cool slightly before cutting.']::text[]),
('pasticada','en','Pašticada','Split, Croatia','A beef roast larded with bacon and carrot, marinated two days in vinegar and wine before braising with prunes and cloves into an almost sweet sauce. A Dalmatian wedding dish, never cooked for an ordinary day.',ARRAY['Lard the roast with sticks of bacon and carrot using a sharp knife.','Marinate it 2 days in the fridge in the wine, vinegar, onion and cloves.','Drain and brown the meat all over, then moisten with the strained marinade and tomato.','Braise covered for 3 hours over very low heat, adding the prunes halfway through.','Remove the meat, reduce the sauce and blend it with the prunes, slice the roast and coat generously.']::text[]),
('karadjordjeva-snicla','en','Karađorđeva šnicla','Belgrade, Serbia','A pork escalope pounded flat then rolled around kajmak, a fermented cream cheese, before being breaded and fried. Created in the 1950s to honour a national hero, shaped like a roll to echo the medal he wore.',ARRAY['Pound thin pork escalopes with a mallet to 3 mm thickness.','Place a spoonful of kajmak at the centre of each escalope and roll tightly, closing the ends with a toothpick.','Coat each roll in flour, beaten egg then breadcrumbs, pressing well to adhere.','Repeat the breading a second time for a thicker, crunchier crust.','Fry at 170°C until the roll is golden, let it rest a minute before slicing.']::text[]),
('fenkata','en','Fenkata (Maltese rabbit)','Valletta, Malta','Rabbit, the king of Maltese tables, first fried with garlic then simmered in red wine until the meat falls off the bone. Traditionally served in two courses: the sauce over pasta first, the meat afterwards.',ARRAY['Cut the rabbit into pieces, marinate it 1 hour in the wine with crushed garlic and bay.','Drain the meat and brown it in olive oil in a large pot.','Add the sliced onion, sweat, then moisten with the marinade and tomato.','Cover and simmer 1h30 over low heat until the meat falls off the bone.','Cook the spaghetti, serve it first coated with the sauce, then the rabbit as a second course, scattered with parsley.']::text[]),
('judd-mat-gaardebounen','en','Judd mat Gaardebounen','Luxembourg City, Luxembourg','Luxembourg''s national dish: smoked pork collar, desalted then simmered until tender, served in thick slices with broad beans and a roux sauce bound with the cooking broth.',ARRAY['Desalt the smoked collar for 12 hours in cold water, changing the water once.','Poach it 2 hours in fresh water with onion, carrot and bay, at a gentle simmer.','Shell the broad beans and cook them separately with a sprig of savory until tender.','Make a roux with butter and flour, moisten with a little of the cooking broth, whisking to a coating consistency.','Slice the meat, serve with the beans and coat with the hot sauce.']::text[]),
('souvla','en','Souvla','Nicosia, Cyprus','Large chunks of pork or lamb threaded onto a long spit and hand-turned over the coals for hours, in a Sunday ritual that gathers the whole family around the charcoal.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into large 5 cm cubes, marinate 2 hours with oil, lemon juice, oregano and bay.','Thread the cubes onto a long spit, interspersing bay leaves.','Turn the spit over moderate coals for 3 to 4 hours, basting regularly with marinade.','Twenty minutes before the end, add slices of halloumi on a grill alongside to char.','Serve the meat pulled from the spit with the grilled halloumi, fresh lemon and warm pita bread.']::text[]),
('potica','en','Potica','Ljubljana, Slovenia','A very thin risen dough, stretched on a clean cloth then rolled tightly around a filling of ground walnuts, honey and cinnamon. Dozens of regional variants exist, each Slovenian family keeping its own.',ARRAY['Knead flour, yeast, eggs, milk and melted butter into a supple dough, let rise 1h30.','Stretch the dough as thin as possible on a large floured cloth, almost transparent.','Blend the walnuts with honey, sugar, cinnamon and raisins for the filling.','Spread the filling over the whole dough, roll tightly using the cloth into a long log.','Place in a ring in a tin, let rise 30 minutes then bake 45 minutes at 170°C.']::text[]),
('bryndzove-halusky','en','Bryndzové halušky','Bratislava, Slovakia','Slovakia''s national dish: small dumplings of raw grated potato, poached then smothered in bryndza, a creamy fermented sheep cheese, crowned with crisp fried bacon bits.',ARRAY['Finely grate the raw potatoes, drain lightly without pressing too hard.','Mix with the flour, egg and salt into a thick, sticky dough.','Drop small dumplings from a spoon or a holed strainer straight into salted boiling water.','Drain the dumplings as soon as they float to the surface, about 3 minutes.','Mash the bryndza with a little milk, mix it hot with the dumplings, crown with crisp bacon bits.']::text[]),
('ghormeh-sabzi','en','Ghormeh sabzi','Tehran, Iran','Widely considered Iran''s national dish: a mountain of fresh herbs — parsley, coriander, fenugreek, chives — slowly fried then simmered with meat and red beans, soured with sun-dried black limes.',ARRAY['Finely chop all the fresh herbs and fry them in oil for 20 minutes until they darken and lose their water.','Brown the sliced onion and cubed beef with the turmeric in a pot.','Add the fried herbs, the drained kidney beans and cover with water.','Pierce the dried black limes and add them whole, simmer 2 hours over low heat.','Lightly crush the limes in the sauce to release their tang, adjust salt and serve with rice.']::text[]),
('masgouf','en','Masgouf','Baghdad, Iraq','A Tigris carp split down the back, splayed open on wooden stakes and grilled standing upright, leaning toward the wood fire for hours. A dish over two thousand years old, mentioned on Sumerian tablets.',ARRAY['Butterfly the fish by splitting along the back without detaching the belly, and flatten it.','Brush the inside with olive oil mixed with turmeric, tamarind and salt.','Fix the opened fish onto two wooden stakes planted in the ground, flesh facing the fire.','Grill it standing at a distance from the embers for 45 minutes to 1 hour, basting regularly with oil.','Top with chopped tomato, onion and parsley, serve with lemon wedges.']::text[]),
('kabsa','en','Kabsa','Riyadh, Saudi Arabia','Basmati rice cooked in the chicken''s own juices, scented with a spice blend unique to each family — cardamom, cinnamon, clove, dried lomi — served on one huge communal platter at the centre of the table, shared straight from the rice.',ARRAY['Brown the chicken, whole or in pieces, in oil with the sliced onion.','Add crushed tomato, carrot, whole spices and pierced dried limes, cover with water and simmer 40 minutes.','Remove the chicken, strain and reserve the fragrant broth.','Fry the rinsed basmati rice for 2 minutes, moisten with the strained broth and cook covered for 18 minutes without lifting the lid.','Grill or fry the chicken briefly to crisp the skin, serve over the rice scattered with raisins and toasted almonds.']::text[]),
('khorovats','en','Khorovats','Yerevan, Armenia','Armenian barbecue, marinated for hours in wine or onion juice before grilling over fruit-wood embers. Its success is judged by how much juice runs when the meat is sliced — never dry.',ARRAY['Cut the meat into large cubes, blend two onions into juice and mix with the wine and bay.','Marinate the meat at least 3 hours, ideally overnight in the fridge.','Thread the cubes onto wide flat skewers, alternating with the remaining onion wedges.','Grill over moderate embers 25 to 30 minutes, turning regularly, until the juices run clear.','Grill whole tomatoes, peppers and aubergines alongside, peel and mash them into a rough sauce to accompany.']::text[]),
('piti','en','Piti','Baku, Azerbaijan','A lamb and chickpea stew simmered and served in its own individual clay pot, with saffron and prunes. The broth is drunk first, then the meat is mashed with a fork inside the pot for the second course.',ARRAY['Soak the chickpeas overnight, drain.','Divide lamb, chickpeas, onion and tomato among small individual clay pots.','Cover with water, cover each pot and simmer 2 hours in a low oven.','Add potatoes, prunes and saffron dissolved in water for the last 30 minutes.','Pour off the broth into a bowl to drink first, then mash the rest with a fork in the pot and dust with sumac.']::text[]),
('mansaf','en','Mansaf','Amman, Jordan','Jordan''s national dish, served on one huge communal tray of flatbread and rice, whole lamb drowned in jameed, a fermented sheep yoghurt dried into hard balls and reconstituted into sauce. Traditionally eaten standing, right hand only.',ARRAY['Soak the dried jameed balls for several hours in warm water until fully softened.','Poach the lamb with onion and cardamom for 1h30 until tender, reserve the broth.','Blend the softened jameed with a little broth until smooth, heat gently without boiling, stirring constantly.','Cook the rice in the rest of the fragrant broth until tender.','Spread the flatbread on a large tray, cover with rice then lamb, coat with the jameed sauce and scatter with almonds and pine nuts toasted in butter.']::text[]),
('saltah','en','Saltah','Sana''a, Yemen','Widely seen as Yemen''s national dish: a spiced meat stew topped with houlba, a whipped fenugreek foam beaten until firm, served bubbling hot in a scorching volcanic stone pot.',ARRAY['Soak the fenugreek seeds 6 hours in water, drain then blend with a little fresh water into a paste, whisk until it foams and triples in volume.','Fry onion, garlic and diced meat until browned.','Add crushed tomato, cumin, chilli and diced potato, moisten and simmer 30 minutes into a thick stew.','Blend some fresh coriander and chilli with a little water for a spicy green relish, on the side.','Pour the scalding stew into preheated stone bowls, crown with fenugreek foam and a drizzle of the green relish, serve with flatbread.']::text[]),
('nihari','en','Nihari','Karachi, Pakistan','A beef shank stew simmered all night over very low heat, born in Mughal court kitchens to be served at breakfast after dawn prayer. The golden fat that rises to the surface, the nihari itself, is stirred back into the sauce with one spoonful.',ARRAY['Fry the sliced onion in ghee until deep golden, set aside half for garnish.','Add the ginger-garlic paste, beef shin and nihari spices, brown the meat all over.','Cover generously with water, bring to the boil then simmer covered over very low heat for 5 to 6 hours, until the meat shreds easily.','Mix the flour with a little cold water, stir it into the stew whisking to thicken the sauce slightly.','Serve piping hot, topped with fried onions, julienned ginger, fresh chilli, coriander and lemon wedges.']::text[]),
('beshbarmak','en','Beshbarmak','Almaty, Kazakhstan','"Five fingers": the dish of the steppes traditionally eaten without cutlery, wide flat noodles topped with boiled, shredded horse or mutton meat, with the broth sipped separately from a bowl called sorpa.',ARRAY['Poach the mutton with one onion, the bay and pepper for 2 hours at a gentle simmer, until tender.','Knead flour, egg, water and salt into a firm dough, rest 30 minutes.','Roll the dough thin and cut large irregular diamonds.','Poach the diamonds of dough for a few minutes in the strained meat broth, drain.','Slice the remaining onions and soften them in a little broth, plate the noodles topped with onions and shredded meat, serve the broth on the side scattered with dill.']::text[]),
('ilish-bhapa','en','Ilish bhapa','Dhaka, Bangladesh','Hilsa, the most prized fish of the Ganges delta, marinated in a raw mustard and green chilli paste then steamed wrapped in a banana leaf, never touching direct heat that would dry it out.',ARRAY['Soak the mustard seeds 30 minutes in warm water, blend with half the green chillies into a fine paste.','Mix the mustard paste with turmeric, yoghurt, mustard oil and salt.','Coat the pieces of hilsa with this paste, marinate 20 minutes.','Wrap the marinated fish and the remaining whole chillies in a banana leaf passed briefly over a flame to soften it.','Steam 15 to 20 minutes in the closed parcel, serve straight from the opened leaf.']::text[]),
('momo','en','Momo','Kathmandu, Nepal','Small dumplings pleated by hand into a crown, filled with pork or vegetables and ginger, steamed in stacked bamboo baskets. Always served with a tomato-sesame dipping sauce called achar, as important as the filling itself.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water and salt into a firm, smooth dough, rest 30 minutes under a cloth.','Mix minced pork, finely shredded cabbage, chopped onion, ginger, garlic and coriander, bind with soy sauce.','Roll the dough paper-thin, cut discs and fill each with a spoonful of filling.','Pleat the edges, pinching all around to close into a crown, leaving a small hole at the top.','Steam 12 minutes in stacked bamboo baskets, serve with a spicy tomato-sesame sauce blended separately.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('mohinga','en','Mohinga','Yangon, Myanmar','Widely regarded as Myanmar''s national dish: a catfish broth thickened with chickpea flour and banana stem, poured over rice vermicelli, eaten in the street at dawn, even before the rice breakfast.',ARRAY['Poach the catfish with lemongrass and ginger for 20 minutes, drain reserving the broth, flake the flesh.','Fry the sliced onion and garlic in oil until browned, add to the reserved broth.','Mix the chickpea flour with a little cold water, whisk into the broth to thicken slightly, simmer 20 minutes with the fish sauce.','Hard-boil the eggs for 9 minutes, shell and halve them.','Divide the cooked rice vermicelli among bowls, pour over the hot broth, top with flaked fish, hard-boiled egg, coriander, lime and crushed chilli.']::text[]),
('fish-amok','en','Fish amok','Phnom Penh, Cambodia','A coconut-milk fish mousse with kroeung curry paste, steamed in a small folded banana-leaf cup until it sets into the texture of a light, fragrant custard.',ARRAY['Blend lemongrass, galangal, garlic, shallot and chilli into a fine curry paste.','Fry the paste dry for a few minutes until fragrant.','Blend the raw fish with the curry paste, coconut milk, egg and fish sauce until a smooth mousse forms.','Shape cups from the banana leaves, pour in the mixture, garnish with shredded kaffir lime leaves.','Steam 20 minutes until the mousse is set and slightly puffed.']::text[]),
('larb','en','Larb','Vientiane, Laos','Laos''s national dish: minced meat quickly seared then dressed raw with lime, chilli and mint, bound with ground toasted sticky rice that gives the warm salad its unique crunch.',ARRAY['Toast the raw sticky rice dry in a pan until deep golden, then grind it coarsely in a mortar.','Fry the minced meat dry in a very hot pan until fully cooked, breaking it up with a spoon.','Off the heat, season immediately with lime juice, fish sauce and crushed chilli.','Add sliced shallot, chopped mint and coriander, toss gently.','Fold in the toasted sticky rice just before serving, present on fresh lettuce leaves.']::text[]),
('hainanese-chicken-rice','en','Hainanese chicken rice','Singapore','A whole chicken poached at low temperature then plunged into ice water to firm the skin, served sliced over rice cooked in the chicken''s own fat and broth, with three mandatory sauces: ginger, chilli and sweet soy.',ARRAY['Rub the chicken with salt, stuff with crushed ginger and garlic, poach 40 minutes in barely simmering water, never boiling.','Plunge it immediately into ice water for 10 minutes to firm and make the skin translucent.','Fry rinsed rice with a little chicken fat, ginger and garlic, moisten with the cooking broth and cook like plain rice.','Blend chilli, remaining ginger and garlic for a hot sauce, mix soy with a little broth for a sweet-salty sauce.','Slice the chicken, serve over the rice with cucumber, coriander and the three sauces on the side.']::text[]),
('taiwanese-beef-noodle-soup','en','Taiwanese beef noodle soup','Taipei, Taiwan','A broth simmered for hours with fermented black bean paste, star anise and doubanjiang chilli bean, in which beef shank braises until meltingly tender. Every district of Taipei claims its own recipe as a family secret.',ARRAY['Blanch the beef and tendon 5 minutes in boiling water, rinse to remove the scum.','Fry ginger, garlic and doubanjiang in a little oil until the oil turns red.','Add the beef, black bean paste, star anise, tomato, soy and rice wine, cover generously with water.','Simmer covered for 2h30 over low heat until the meat is meltingly tender.','Cook the noodles and bok choy separately, plate in bowls, ladle over the scalding broth and garnish with chopped scallion.']::text[]),
('buuz','en','Buuz','Ulaanbaatar, Mongolia','Steamed dumplings of mutton coarsely chopped by knife, never minced, pleated with about twenty folds around a small hole left deliberately to vent steam — the emblematic dish of Mongolian lunar new year, Tsagaan Sar.',ARRAY['Chop the mutton coarsely by knife with the onion, more coarsely than for an ordinary filling.','Mix with crushed garlic, cumin and salt, without overworking the filling.','Knead flour, water and salt into a firm dough, rest 20 minutes, roll out and cut discs.','Fill each disc and pleat the edges into about twenty tight folds, leaving a tiny hole at the top.','Steam 12 to 15 minutes in stacked baskets, serve piping hot, eaten by hand.']::text[]),
('ema-datshi','en','Ema datshi','Thimphu, Bhutan','Bhutan''s national dish, where chilli is not a condiment but the main ingredient: whole large green chillies simmered in melted local datshi cheese until forming a thick, fiery sauce.',ARRAY['Slit the green chillies lengthwise without seeding them, cut into large chunks.','Fry onion and garlic in butter until translucent.','Add the chillies and crushed tomato, cook 5 minutes over high heat.','Crumble the datshi cheese over the top, moisten with a little milk and let it melt over low heat, stirring.','Simmer 10 minutes until the sauce is thick and creamy, serve with red rice.']::text[]),
('rendang','en','Rendang','Padang, Indonesia','A Minangkabau beef stew simmered four hours in coconut milk and a spice paste, until all the liquid evaporates and caramelises into dry, near-black meat that then keeps for weeks without refrigeration.',ARRAY['Blend rehydrated dried chillies, shallot, garlic, ginger and galangal into a fine paste.','Dry-toast the grated coconut until brown and fragrant, set aside.','Fry the spice paste in a little oil until fragrant, add the cubed beef and brown it.','Pour in the coconut milk, add lemongrass, kaffir lime leaves and tamarind, simmer uncovered 3 to 4 hours, stirring regularly.','Continue cooking until the liquid has almost fully evaporated and the sauce caramelises around the meat, fold in the toasted coconut at the end.']::text[]),
('xiaolongbao','en','Xiaolongbao','Shanghai, China','Steamed dumplings that hide an entire soup inside: a pork broth set into jelly in the cold, sealed inside the filling, which turns liquid again on cooking. A minimum of twenty pleats at the top is required for a fold judged well made.',ARRAY['Boil the pork skin 3 hours in water until a gelatinous broth forms, strain and chill until it sets into firm jelly.','Cut the jelly into small dice, mix with the minced pork, ginger, scallion, soy, rice wine and sesame oil.','Knead flour and water into a smooth dough, rest 30 minutes, roll paper-thin and cut discs.','Fill each disc with filling and jelly cubes, pleat the edges into at least twenty tight folds at the top.','Steam 8 minutes in lined bamboo baskets, serve immediately with rice vinegar and shredded ginger.']::text[]),
('chongqing-hotpot','en','Chongqing hotpot','Chongqing, China','A pot of scarlet broth made with beef tallow, dried chillies and Sichuan pepper, kept bubbling at the centre of the table where everyone takes turns dipping meat, vegetables and tofu with their own chopsticks.',ARRAY['Melt the beef tallow in a large pot, add the doubanjiang and fry until the oil turns red.','Add dried chillies, Sichuan pepper, ginger and garlic, let infuse 10 minutes over low heat.','Moisten with water or broth, bring to the boil and keep the pot simmering at the centre of the table on a burner.','Prepare individual dipping bowls of sesame oil and crushed garlic to cool each bite.','Everyone takes turns dipping thin beef slices, tofu, napa cabbage and enoki mushrooms for a few seconds in the bubbling broth before dipping into their sauce.']::text[]),
('naengmyeon','en','Naengmyeon','Pyongyang, North Korea','Chewy buckwheat noodles served ice-cold in a clarified beef and kimchi broth, chilled for hours. Born in Pyongyang to endure harsh winters, the soup is traditionally eaten cold even in the depths of winter outside.',ARRAY['Poach the beef brisket 2 hours in water until tender, strain and chill the broth overnight.','Skim the fat off the cold broth from the surface.','Mix the skimmed broth with kimchi juice, rice vinegar and mustard, adjust seasoning.','Cook the buckwheat noodles al dente, rinse immediately in ice water to stop cooking and firm the texture.','Plate the noodles in chilled bowls, cover with the very cold broth, top with sliced beef, hard-boiled egg, cucumber, pear and thinly sliced radish.']::text[]),
('brik','en','Brik','Tunis, Tunisia','A malsouka pastry sheet so thin it shatters like glass, folded into a triangle around a whole raw egg, tuna and capers, deep-fried just long enough for the yolk to stay runny at the first bite.',ARRAY['Lay a sheet of malsouka pastry on the worktop, place flaked tuna, sliced onion, capers and parsley in the centre.','Crack a whole egg into the middle of the filling without beating it.','Fold the pastry into a triangle enclosing the egg, working fast so it does not run before cooking.','Slide gently into hot oil and fry 2 minutes per side, never flipping more than once to keep the yolk runny.','Drain on paper towel and serve immediately piping hot, with a lemon wedge.']::text[]),
('bazin','en','Bazin','Tripoli, Libya','A compact ball of barley flour beaten for a long time with a wooden paddle until smooth and firm, set as a dome at the centre of a meat and hard-boiled egg stew, from which pieces are torn off by hand.',ARRAY['Pour the barley flour into salted boiling water in a stream, beating constantly with a wooden paddle.','Keep beating 20 minutes over low heat until you get a smooth, firm, lump-free ball.','Brown the lamb pieces with onion, garlic and turmeric, add the tomato and cover with water.','Simmer 45 minutes until the meat is tender, add the shelled hard-boiled eggs at the end.','Shape the bazin into a smooth dome at the centre of a plate, surround with the stew and eggs, serve tearing off pieces by hand.']::text[]),
('ful-medames','en','Ful medames','Khartoum, Sudan','Brown fava beans simmered all night over very low heat in a conical copper pot itself called "ful", roughly mashed with a fork with olive oil, lemon and cumin. Sudan''s quintessential breakfast, scooped up with flatbread.',ARRAY['Soak the brown fava beans overnight in cold water.','Cook them covered for 1h30 over very low heat in fresh water, until very tender.','Roughly mash them with a fork in their cooking water, without reducing to a smooth purée.','Season with crushed garlic, lemon juice, olive oil and cumin, mix.','Serve warm in a deep dish, topped with diced tomato and onion, with flatbread for scooping.']::text[]),
('moambe-chicken','en','Moambe chicken','Kinshasa, DR Congo','Chicken simmered in palm nut pulp, moambe, whose orange colour and rich flavour make it the national dish on both banks of the Congo River. Served with cassava fufu and pounded cassava leaves, pondu.',ARRAY['Brown the chicken pieces in a pot, set aside.','Fry sliced onion, garlic and ginger, add crushed tomato and chilli.','Return the chicken, stir in the palm nut cream and cover with water.','Simmer covered 45 minutes until the sauce thickens and the palm oil rises to the surface.','Make the fufu by pouring cassava flour into boiling water in a stream, beating vigorously into a smooth ball, serve with the chicken.']::text[]),
('ndole','en','Ndolé','Douala, Cameroon','Cameroon''s national dish: ndolé leaves, a slightly bitter amaranth variety, boiled then simmered with pounded peanuts, meat and dried shrimp, into a thick, almost brown sauce.',ARRAY['Boil the ndolé leaves twice, changing the water to remove bitterness, drain and chop them.','Dry-roast the peanuts then blend with a little water into a thick paste.','Brown the beef cubes with onion, garlic, ginger and chilli.','Add the peanut paste, dried shrimp and crushed crayfish, moisten and simmer 30 minutes, stirring often to prevent sticking.','Stir in the chopped ndolé leaves, cook a further 15 minutes into a thick sauce, serve with plantain or rice.']::text[]),
('attieke','en','Attiéké with fish','Abidjan, Ivory Coast','Fermented cassava semolina steamed into light grains, almost a plant-based couscous, served with a whole fish grilled over charcoal and a tomato-onion sauce spiked with kpakpo shito chilli.',ARRAY['Moisten the cassava semolina with a little water, fluff it with a fork.','Steam it 15 minutes in a couscoussier, fluffing halfway through to avoid lumps.','Rub the whole fish with salt and lemon, grill it over charcoal 8 minutes per side.','Blend tomato, onion, chilli and garlic into a fresh sauce, season with olive oil and salt.','Serve the warm attiéké with the whole grilled fish and the tomato sauce on the side.']::text[]),
('maafe','en','Maafe (mafé)','Bamako, Mali','A beef stew simmered in a peanut paste sauce, thickened until nearly creamy, with cabbage, carrot and sweet potato. Born among the Bambara and Malinké peoples, now found across West Africa in countless variations.',ARRAY['Brown the cubed beef with sliced onion and garlic in oil.','Add crushed tomato, cover with water and simmer 30 minutes.','Loosen the peanut butter with a little hot broth, stir it into the stew.','Add cabbage, carrot, sweet potato and a whole chilli, cook a further 30 minutes over low heat, stirring regularly to prevent sticking.','Adjust seasoning once the sauce is thick and creamy, serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('sadza','en','Sadza ne nyama','Harare, Zimbabwe','A cornmeal porridge beaten until it forms a firm, smooth ball, the country''s staple food, served with stewed meat and bitter leafy greens called muriwo. Eaten with the fingers, pinching a small spoon shape out of the hot ball.',ARRAY['Mix a third of the cornmeal with a little cold water, pour into boiling water whisking.','Cook 5 minutes into a thick porridge, then stir in the rest of the flour, beating vigorously with a wooden paddle.','Keep beating and cooking over low heat for 15 minutes until the ball is smooth, firm and pulls away from the sides.','Simmer the beef pieces with onion and tomato until tender, sauté the leafy greens with a little peanut butter.','Serve the sadza as a ball with the meat and greens on the side, eat with the fingers.']::text[]),
('piri-piri-chicken','en','Piri-piri chicken','Maputo, Mozambique','A butterflied chicken marinated in a sauce of African bird''s-eye chilli, garlic and lemon, born from the crossing of Portuguese settlers and local spices. Grilled over charcoal then basted one last time with fiery sauce before serving.',ARRAY['Butterfly the whole chicken by partially removing the backbone, so it cooks flat.','Blend chillies, garlic, lemon juice, olive oil, paprika and bay into a hot sauce.','Rub the chicken with sauce, working it under the skin, marinate at least 2 hours in the fridge.','Grill over charcoal 35 minutes over moderate heat, skin down first, turning regularly.','Baste one last time with hot piri-piri sauce off the grill, rest 5 minutes before carving.']::text[]),
('zanzibar-pilau','en','Zanzibar pilau','Zanzibar, Tanzania','Rice scented with pilau masala — cinnamon, cardamom, cumin, clove — dry-toasted before the meat is added, inherited from the Omani trade routes that made Zanzibar a spice crossroads.',ARRAY['Dry-toast cinnamon, cardamom, cumin and cloves for 2 minutes until fragrant, grind coarsely.','Fry the sliced onion in oil until deeply golden and caramelised, set half aside.','Brown the beef with garlic, ginger and the toasted spices, add crushed tomato.','Add the rinsed rice, moisten with hot broth and cook covered 18 minutes without lifting the lid.','Scatter the reserved caramelised onion over just before serving.']::text[]),
('isombe','en','Isombe','Kigali, Rwanda','Cassava leaves pounded at length in a mortar into a fine paste, simmered with palm oil, local eggplant and sometimes small dried fish, served with sweet potato or fufu.',ARRAY['Pound the cassava leaves at length in a mortar into a fine, smooth paste.','Boil them 20 minutes in water, drain.','Fry onion and garlic in palm oil, add the diced eggplant and crumbled dried fish.','Stir in the cassava paste and peanut butter, simmer 15 minutes, stirring.','Boil or steam the sweet potatoes, serve alongside the isombe.']::text[]),
('seswaa','en','Seswaa','Gaborone, Botswana','Botswana''s festive dish: beef boiled in salted water for hours with no other seasoning, until so tender it falls apart on its own when pounded in a mortar, served over sorghum bogobe porridge.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into large chunks, place in a large pot with the onion and salted water.','Bring to the boil then simmer covered 2h30 to 3 hours, until the meat is extremely tender.','Drain the meat, reserving a little broth, let it cool slightly.','Pound it in a mortar or shred with a fork into fine fibres, moisten with a little of the reserved broth.','Make the bogobe by cooking sorghum flour in boiling water, stirring into a thick porridge, serve with the shredded beef.']::text[]),
('calulu','en','Calulu','Luanda, Angola','Angola''s national dish, simmering dried and fresh fish together with sweet potato or cassava leaves in palm oil, inherited from enslaved cooks who had to use every available part of the fish.',ARRAY['Soak the dried fish 30 minutes in warm water to desalt, drain.','Fry onion and garlic in palm oil, add crushed tomato and chilli.','Add both fish, moisten and simmer 20 minutes over low heat.','Stir in sweet potato leaves, diced eggplant and okra, cook a further 15 minutes.','Add the sweet potato in chunks, simmer another 15 minutes until tender, serve with rice or fufu.']::text[]),
('rolex','en','Rolex','Kampala, Uganda','A vegetable omelette rolled inside a warm chapati, born at Kampala''s university food stalls where vendors called out "roll eggs" to rushing customers — a pun that became the name of Uganda''s most popular street food.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water and salt into a supple dough, rest 20 minutes, roll into thin discs.','Cook each chapati dry in a hot pan until it puffs in places and browns lightly.','Beat the eggs with finely chopped tomato, onion and cabbage.','Pour the mixture into the oiled pan and cook into a thin omelette, without flipping.','Lay the hot chapati onto the omelette still in the pan, press lightly then roll everything together tightly before slicing.']::text[]),
('pepian','en','Pepián','Guatemala City, Guatemala','A Maya-Quiché national dish declared cultural heritage of Guatemala, whose thick sauce owes its character to toasted pumpkin and sesame seeds ground fine, blended with dried chillies charred until slightly blackened.',ARRAY['Poach the chicken in salted water for 30 minutes, reserve the broth.','Dry-toast pumpkin seeds, sesame and dried chillies until fragrant.','Char-grill tomato, tomatillo, onion and garlic dry in a pan until spotted black in places.','Blend everything with a little broth into a thick, slightly grainy sauce.','Add the chicken, potatoes and green beans to the sauce, simmer 20 minutes and serve with rice.']::text[]),
('baleada','en','Baleada','Tegucigalpa, Honduras','A thick flour tortilla, folded over mashed red beans, crumbled cheese and thick cream — Honduras''s simplest street meal, sold at every hour from improvised stalls.',ARRAY['Knead flour, baking powder, salt, oil and water into a supple dough, rest 15 minutes.','Shape balls and roll into thick discs, cook dry in a hot pan 2 minutes per side.','Heat the cooked red beans and roughly mash into a thick purée.','Spread each hot tortilla with the bean purée.','Top with crumbled cheese and thick cream, fold in half and serve hot.']::text[]),
('pupusas','en','Pupusas','San Salvador, El Salvador','El Salvador''s national dish: a thick corn masa cake, filled with cheese, beans or chicharron before cooking on a griddle, always served with curtido, a lightly tangy fermented cabbage slaw.',ARRAY['Mix finely shredded cabbage, grated carrot, vinegar, oregano and salt, let ferment at least 2 hours for the curtido.','Knead the corn masa with water and salt into a supple dough that doesn''t stick to your hands.','Shape balls, hollow a well in the centre and fill with crumbled cheese, beans or chopped chicharron.','Close by pinching and gently flatten into a thick cake without bursting the filling.','Cook on a lightly oiled hot griddle 4 minutes per side, serve piping hot with the curtido and a tomato sauce.']::text[]),
('sancocho-panameno','en','Panamanian sancocho','Panama City, Panama','Panama''s national dish, a chicken broth scented with culantro, a long-leafed coriander far more pungent than the common kind, in which ñame roots simmer until naturally thickening the soup.',ARRAY['Place the chicken pieces in a large pot with onion, garlic and culantro, cover with water.','Bring to the boil, skim, then simmer 45 minutes over low heat.','Add the peeled yam in large chunks and the corn pieces.','Continue cooking 30 minutes until the yam breaks down slightly and thickens the broth.','Adjust the seasoning, add oregano and chopped scallion at the end, serve piping hot with white rice.']::text[]),
('mangu','en','Mangú','Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic','A purée of boiled green plantains, mashed with the cooking water until smooth and glossy, crowned with vinegared red onions cooked just enough to soften without melting away. The base of every Dominican breakfast, called "the three hits".',ARRAY['Peel the green plantains and cut into chunks, cook 20 minutes in salted water until tender.','Drain, reserving a little cooking water, mash with a potato masher adding the reserved water and butter to a smooth consistency.','Slice the red onion, fry it with the vinegar for 5 minutes until just softened.','Fry slices of salami and eggs to accompany.','Serve the plantain mash crowned with vinegared onions, with fresh cheese, salami and fried egg on the side.']::text[]),
('griot','en','Griot','Port-au-Prince, Haiti','Pork cubes marinated for hours in bitter orange juice and spices, boiled then fried twice until almost lacquered outside, served with pikliz, a relish of cabbage and scotch bonnet chilli pickled in vinegar.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into large cubes, marinate at least 3 hours with bitter orange juice, crushed garlic, onion, cloves and salt.','Drain and boil the meat 30 minutes in its strained marinade liquid until tender.','Make the pikliz by mixing finely shredded cabbage and carrot, scotch bonnet chilli and vinegar, marinate at least 2 hours.','Drain the boiled pork and fry it in two batches for 8 minutes in very hot oil until golden and crisp.','Serve piping hot with the pikliz and djon-djon or white rice.']::text[]),
('salteñas','en','Salteñas','La Paz, Bolivia','A sweet-savoury pastry filled with a beef and potato stew bound with gelatine before baking, so it turns back into scalding broth inside the oven. Eaten standing in the morning, never pierced before the first bite.',ARRAY['Fry onion and diced beef with paprika and sugar until browned.','Add potato in small dice and broth, simmer 25 minutes.','Dissolve the gelatine in the hot stew broth, cool then chill until set into firm jelly.','Knead flour, melted butter, egg and warm water into a supple dough, rest 30 minutes.','Roll the dough, fill with diced jellied stew, a pea, an olive and a raisin, close into a twisted turnover and bake 25 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('encebollado','en','Encebollado','Guayaquil, Ecuador','An albacore tuna and cassava soup drowned under a mountain of lime-marinated red onions, famed as a Sunday-morning hangover cure across Guayaquil, eaten with plantain chips.',ARRAY['Cook the peeled cassava in salted water 25 minutes until tender, reserve the broth.','Poach the tuna in this same broth for 10 minutes, roughly flake it.','Fry tomato and cumin in a little oil, add to the broth with the cassava and flaked tuna.','Very finely slice the red onion, marinate it in lime juice and mustard for at least 20 minutes.','Serve the scalding soup crowned generously with marinated onions and coriander, with plantain chips on the side.']::text[]),
('sopa-paraguaya','en','Sopa paraguaya','Asunción, Paraguay','Despite its name, this is not a soup but a savoury cornmeal cake with melted onion and cheese, born from a kitchen accident at President Carlos Antonio López''s court in the 19th century, when a too-thick porridge was baked by mistake.',ARRAY['Melt the sliced onions in butter and oil until translucent, without browning.','Mix the cornmeal with the milk, let it swell for 10 minutes.','Stir in the beaten eggs, crumbled cheese and melted onions.','Pour into a buttered tin, the batter should be pourable like a thick cake batter.','Bake 40 minutes at 180°C until golden on top and a knife comes out clean, let cool slightly before cutting into squares.']::text[]),
('doubles','en','Doubles','Port of Spain, Trinidad','Two small fried, turmeric-leavened flatbreads stacked around a spiced chickpea curry, hence the name: "doubles". Trinidad and Tobago''s most popular street breakfast, sold from carts from dawn.',ARRAY['Knead flour, yeast, turmeric, cumin, water and salt into a supple dough, let rise 2 hours.','Cook the soaked chickpeas until tender, mash part of them to thicken.','Season the chickpea curry with crushed garlic, minced scotch bonnet and coriander.','Divide the risen dough into small balls, flatten each into a thin disc and fry 1 minute per side in hot oil until they puff.','Stack two fried discs, fill generously with chickpea curry and fold closed, serve warm.']::text[]),
('conch-salad','en','Conch salad','Nassau, Bahamas','Conch, a large firm-fleshed shellfish, pounded then diced small and "cooked" only by lime acidity, prepared and served in minutes right on the docks of the fish markets.',ARRAY['Pound the conch flesh with a mallet to tenderise, rinse well.','Cut into very small even dice.','Mix with tomato, bell pepper, onion and scotch bonnet, all diced small.','Season generously with lime juice and orange juice, salt.','Marinate 15 minutes chilled, stirring once, serve very cold scattered with coriander.']::text[]),
('texas-brisket','en','Texas brisket','Austin, Texas, United States','A whole beef brisket smoked twelve hours over oak wood, seasoned with nothing but a rub of salt and cracked black pepper, until a black crust called bark forms around meat smoked pink to the core.',ARRAY['Trim the brisket leaving a thin fat cap, rub generously with salt and cracked pepper all over.','Rest at room temperature 1 hour before placing in a smoker preheated to 110°C.','Smoke over oak wood 10 to 12 hours, keeping a steady temperature, never opening the smoker needlessly.','Wrap the meat in butcher paper once the bark is well formed, continue cooking until a probe slides in with no resistance, about 93°C internal.','Rest wrapped at least 1 hour before slicing thin against the grain, serve with white bread, pickles and barbecue sauce.']::text[]),
('mumu','en','Mumu','Port Moresby, Papua New Guinea','A pit dug in the earth, lined with stones heated over a wood fire for hours, where pork, sweet potato and leafy greens wrapped in banana leaves are stacked, covered with earth to steam-cook all afternoon.',ARRAY['Dig a pit and heat volcanic stones in a large wood fire for 2 hours.','Arrange the burning stones at the bottom of the pit, cover with a layer of banana leaves.','Wrap the pork, sweet potato and cassava in separate banana-leaf parcels, salt them.','Stack the parcels on the stones, drizzle with coconut milk, cover with leaves then earth to seal tightly.','Let it steam-cook 2h30 without opening, dig it up and serve straight from the opened leaves.']::text[]),
('palusami','en','Palusami','Apia, Samoa','Tender young taro leaves layered into small parcels around fresh coconut cream and sometimes onion and corned beef, tied and steam-cooked until the leaves melt into a texture close to spinach.',ARRAY['Wash the young taro leaves carefully, stack five in a star shape to form a wide base.','Place a little chopped onion and crumbled corned beef in the centre.','Fold the leaves closed into a tight parcel, tie with a leaf strip or kitchen string.','Arrange the parcels in a pot, cover generously with coconut milk and salt.','Cook covered over low heat 45 minutes to 1 hour until the leaves are meltingly soft, serve hot.']::text[]),
('lap-lap','en','Lap lap','Port Vila, Vanuatu','Vanuatu''s national dish: a grated yam, cassava or taro batter, drowned in coconut milk and topped with chicken or fish, cooked for hours in a leaf-wrapped stone oven until it becomes a dense, melting loaf.',ARRAY['Peel and finely grate the yam until you get a moist batter.','Mix it with half the coconut milk to a thick cake-batter consistency.','Spread the batter over a wide bed of overlapping banana leaves, in an even layer.','Arrange the chicken pieces and leafy greens on top, drizzle with the rest of the coconut milk.','Fold the leaves to wrap everything well, steam-cook over hot stones or in a very low oven for 2 hours, serve cut into portions.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('mulgipuder','en','Mulgipuder','Tartu, Estonia','A peasant mash of barley and potato boiled together until they merge, crowned with grilled pork bits and their melted fat drizzled on scalding hot just before serving.',ARRAY['Soak the barley 2 hours, drain.','Cook barley and peeled potatoes together in salted water 35 minutes until fully tender.','Mash everything with a potato masher without aiming for smoothness, the texture should stay rustic.','Cut the bacon into small lardons, fry them dry with the sliced onion until nicely crisp.','Serve the mash topped with the bacon bits and their cooking fat poured on scalding hot.']::text[]),
('pelekie-zirni','en','Pelēkie zirņi (grey peas)','Riga, Latvia','Grey peas, an heirloom variety grown for centuries around the Baltic, simmered until tender then mixed with rendered pork lardons and caramelised onion. Latvia''s quintessential Christmas dish, served with fermented milk.',ARRAY['Soak the grey peas overnight, drain.','Cook covered 45 minutes in lightly salted water until tender but holding their shape.','Dice the bacon small, render it in a pan until it crisps up.','Add the sliced onion and let it caramelise slowly in the bacon fat.','Mix the drained peas with the bacon and caramelised onion, serve hot with fermented milk on the side.']::text[]),
('tave-kosi','en','Tavë kosi','Tirana, Albania','Albania''s national dish: browned lamb drowned under a yoghurt and egg sauce that puffs up in the oven like a soufflé, then settles into a golden, faintly tangy crust.',ARRAY['Brown the lamb pieces in butter with crushed garlic.','Add the rice and a little water, simmer 30 minutes until the meat is tender.','Spread the meat and rice in a buttered baking dish.','Whisk the yoghurt with the eggs and flour until smooth, pour over the meat.','Bake 30 minutes at 190°C until puffed and golden on top, let rest 10 minutes before serving.']::text[]),
('tavce-gravce','en','Tavče gravče','Skopje, North Macedonia','North Macedonia''s national dish: white beans simmered then finished in the oven in a clay dish with smoked paprika and dried mint, until a thin skin forms on top.',ARRAY['Soak the white beans overnight, drain.','Cook 1 hour in fresh water until nearly tender.','Fry onion and pepper in oil, add flour and smoked paprika, moisten with tomato.','Mix the drained beans into this sauce, pour into a clay dish.','Bake uncovered 30 minutes at 200°C, scatter with dried mint at the end until a golden skin forms on top.']::text[]),
('kacamak','en','Kačamak','Cetinje, Montenegro','A cornmeal and potato porridge beaten with a wooden paddle until elastic, drowned in melting fresh cheese and clotted cream, a shepherd''s dish from the Montenegrin mountains.',ARRAY['Cook the peeled, chunked potatoes in salted water until tender, roughly mash them in their water.','Pour the cornmeal into the mashed potatoes in a stream, beating constantly with a wooden paddle.','Continue cooking and beating for 15 minutes over low heat until an elastic dough forms that pulls from the sides.','Fold in the crumbled fresh cheese and half the cream, mix until they melt into the hot porridge.','Serve immediately, topped with the rest of the cream and a little melted butter.']::text[]),
('draniki','en','Draniki','Minsk, Belarus','Pancakes of raw grated potato, pressed to remove excess water then fried until the edges turn lacy and crisp, served with well-chilled sour cream.',ARRAY['Finely grate the potatoes and onion, press firmly to remove excess liquid.','Mix with the egg, flour and salt into a thick batter.','Heat a generous layer of oil in a wide pan.','Drop flattened spoonfuls of batter, fry 3 minutes per side until the edges are lacy and golden.','Drain on paper towel, serve piping hot with well-chilled sour cream.']::text[]),
('placinta','en','Plăcintă with cheese','Chișinău, Moldova','A dough stretched by hand into an almost transparent circle, rolled spiral-fashion around fresh cheese and dill before being flattened and pan-fried dry until it blisters into golden pockets.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water, egg and salt into a supple dough, rest 30 minutes under a cloth.','Divide into balls and stretch each by hand on an oiled surface until nearly transparent.','Mash the fresh cheese with chopped dill, spread over the whole stretched dough.','Roll into a tight log then coil the log onto itself into a spiral, gently flatten with a rolling pin.','Cook in a dry pan 4 minutes per side over medium heat, brush with melted butter on coming out.']::text[]),
('flija','en','Flija','Pristina, Kosovo','Up to twenty thin layers of batter, each brushed with cream and baked separately under a metal dome called a sač covered in embers, stacked one on another into a cake striped like a tree trunk.',ARRAY['Make a liquid batter with flour, water and salt, thin like crêpe batter.','Pour a thin layer into a large buttered round dish, bake in a very hot oven 5 minutes until lightly coloured.','Brush with cream, pour a new thin layer of batter on top without mixing the layers.','Repeat fifteen to twenty times, alternating baking and brushing with cream between each layer.','Finish with a pass under the grill to brown the top, let cool slightly then cut into striped triangular portions.']::text[]),
('machboos','en','Machboos','Dubai, United Arab Emirates','Basmati rice scented with the Gulf''s own bezar spice blend, cooked in meat juices and dried black lime, inherited from the sea routes linking Emirati ports to India and East Africa.',ARRAY['Brown the chicken with sliced onion and garlic in oil.','Add crushed tomato, whole spices and pierced black limes, cover with water and simmer 40 minutes.','Remove the chicken, reserve the strained fragrant broth.','Fry the rinsed rice for 2 minutes, moisten with the hot broth and cook covered 18 minutes without lifting the lid.','Grill the chicken briefly to crisp the skin, serve over the rice scattered with golden raisins.']::text[]),
('harees','en','Harees','Doha, Qatar','Cracked wheat and meat simmered for hours then beaten at length into a perfectly smooth, velvety porridge, served during Ramadan and at weddings across the Gulf.',ARRAY['Soak the cracked wheat 2 hours in water, drain.','Place wheat and chicken in a large pot, cover generously with water and bring to the boil.','Simmer covered 2h30 over very low heat until the chicken falls apart completely.','Remove the bones, beat vigorously with a whisk or mixer until a smooth, even porridge forms.','Serve piping hot, topped with melted butter and dusted with cinnamon.']::text[]),
('shuwa','en','Shuwa','Muscat, Oman','A whole lamb marinated for several days in a spice and vinegar paste, wrapped in banana leaves then buried in an ember-heated pit for twenty-four hours, until meltingly tender enough to shred on its own.',ARRAY['Blend garlic, vinegar and all the spices into a thick paste.','Rub the whole lamb generously with this paste, working it into knife slits, marinate 2 days in the fridge.','Wrap the meat in several layers of banana leaves, tie securely.','Steam-cook it 8 to 10 hours in a very low oven or an ember pit, until extremely tender.','Unwrap and shred the meat with a fork, serve with basmati rice.']::text[]),
('jireesh','en','Jireesh','Kuwait City, Kuwait','Coarsely cracked wheat simmered with chicken and spices until it thickens into a rustic porridge, coarser than the neighbouring harees, traditionally eaten on cool Kuwaiti winter nights.',ARRAY['Soak the cracked wheat 1 hour, drain.','Brown the sliced onion and chicken in butter.','Add the wheat, cinnamon, cardamom and dried limes, cover with water and bring to the boil.','Simmer covered 1h30 over low heat, stirring regularly to prevent sticking.','Lightly mash with a spoon at the end to thicken, serve hot.']::text[]),
('kibbeh','en','Kibbeh','Damascus, Syria','A shell of fine bulgur and raw meat pounded into a smooth paste, hand-shaped into a hollow torpedo, stuffed with spiced minced meat and pine nuts then fried to a deep, crisp brown.',ARRAY['Soak the fine bulgur 20 minutes in cold water, squeeze out firmly.','Blend half the minced meat with the bulgur, one onion and the spices until a smooth, pliable paste forms.','Fry the remaining onion with the rest of the meat and pine nuts for the filling, let cool.','Shape the paste into small hollow torpedoes with wet fingers, fill with the filling and pinch the ends closed.','Fry at 170°C until deep brown and crisp, drain and serve hot.']::text[]),
('musakhan','en','Musakhan','Nablus, Palestine','Roast chicken set over taboun bread soaked in olive oil and a mountain of onions slow-cooked with sumac for hours, until they turn into an almost jammy, tangy purple mass.',ARRAY['Rub the chicken with olive oil, sumac and spices, roast 45 minutes in a 200°C oven.','Finely slice a large quantity of onions.','Slow-cook them in olive oil over very low heat for 45 minutes, until soft and lightly caramelised.','Add the sumac to the confited onions at the end, mix.','Spread the taboun bread on a platter, cover generously with sumac onions, top with the roast chicken and scatter with pine nuts toasted in butter.']::text[]),
('muhammar','en','Muhammar','Manama, Bahrain','Basmati rice sweetened with date syrup and saffron, an unexpected contrast served alongside salty grilled fish, in a Bahraini culinary tradition where sweet and savoury answer each other on the same plate.',ARRAY['Cook the rinsed basmati rice in salted water until nearly tender, drain.','Heat the date syrup with butter and cardamom until fluid.','Pour the syrup over the drained rice, gently mix to coat without crushing it.','Cover and steam-cook 15 minutes over very low heat until fully absorbed.','Salt and grill the whole fish with lemon, serve alongside the sweetened rice.']::text[]),
('lagman','en','Lagman','Bishkek, Kyrgyzstan','Hand-pulled noodles, stretched and slapped on the worktop to the right thickness, topped with a cumin-spiced beef and vegetable stew, inherited from the Silk Road routes that crossed Central Asia.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water and salt into a very supple dough, rest 1 hour under oil.','Stretch the dough into long ropes, pull and slap them against the worktop to thin them into noodles.','Brown the beef strips with onion and garlic in oil.','Add pepper, carrot and tomato in strips, cumin and soy, moisten and simmer 25 minutes.','Cook the noodles in boiling water 3 minutes, drain and top with the hot beef stew.']::text[]),
('qurutob','en','Qurutob','Dushanbe, Tajikistan','Tajikistan''s national dish: fatir bread torn into pieces, drowned under a dried yoghurt reconstituted into sauce, with raw tomato and onion on top. Traditionally eaten by hand from one large communal dish.',ARRAY['Dissolve the dried yoghurt balls in warm water until a smooth, tangy sauce forms.','Tear the fatir bread into irregular pieces directly into a large dish.','Pour the yoghurt sauce over the torn bread, mix gently to soak it well.','Finely slice tomato and onion, arrange on top.','Drizzle with olive oil, scatter with coriander and serve immediately at room temperature.']::text[]),
('gutap','en','Gutap','Ashgabat, Turkmenistan','A half-moon pastry filled with pumpkin or wild leafy greens, cooked dry in a cast-iron pan without a drop of oil, until the dough puffs into small crisp pockets.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water and salt into a supple dough, rest 30 minutes.','Grate the raw pumpkin, mix with sliced onion, cumin and salt.','Roll the dough thin, cut discs and fill each with the pumpkin filling.','Fold into a half-moon and pinch the edges firmly to seal well.','Cook dry in a hot pan 4 minutes per side, covered, brush with melted butter on coming out.']::text[]),
('kabuli-palaw','en','Kabuli palaw','Kabul, Afghanistan','Afghanistan''s national dish: basmati rice cooked in lamb broth, coloured with caramelised sugar and crowned with fine carrot juliennes and raisins sautéed until lightly caramelised.',ARRAY['Brown the lamb with sliced onion, cover with water and simmer 1 hour until tender, reserve the broth.','Cut the carrots into fine julienne, sauté with the sugar until lightly caramelised, add the raisins at the end.','Fry the rinsed rice in a little oil, moisten with the lamb broth scented with cardamom and cumin.','Cook covered 18 minutes over low heat without lifting the lid.','Plate the rice, arrange the lamb on top and crown with the caramelised carrots and raisins.']::text[]),
('mas-huni','en','Mas huni','Malé, Maldives','The Maldivian national breakfast: hand-flaked smoked dried tuna, mixed raw with freshly grated coconut, red onion and chilli, eaten wrapped in roshi, a thin flatbread.',ARRAY['Finely flake the smoked dried tuna by hand, removing any bones.','Freshly grate the coconut.','Very finely slice the red onion and chilli.','Mix the flaked tuna, grated coconut, onion and chilli, drizzle with lime juice and salt.','Serve at room temperature with warm roshi to wrap each bite.']::text[]),
('ambuyat','en','Ambuyat','Bandar Seri Begawan, Brunei','Brunei''s national dish, a viscous, almost flavourless sago starch paste, wound onto forked bamboo sticks called candas and dipped into a tangy tamarind and chilli sauce.',ARRAY['Loosen the sago starch with a little cold water to form a smooth paste.','Bring the rest of the water to the boil, pour in the sago paste in a stream, stirring constantly.','Keep stirring over low heat until the mixture turns translucent and viscous, like thick glue.','Blend tamarind, chilli, sugar and dried shrimp with a little water for the dipping sauce.','Wind the ambuyat around forked sticks and dip each mouthful into the sauce before swallowing it whole, without chewing.']::text[]),
('ikan-sabuko','en','Grilled ikan sabuko','Dili, East Timor','A whole mackerel brushed with a paste of chilli, tamarind and lemongrass, grilled over wood embers until the skin cracks, eaten with rice and pounded cassava leaves, an everyday dish along the shores of Dili.',ARRAY['Gut and scale the mackerel, score the skin two or three times on each side.','Blend chilli, tamarind, lemongrass and garlic into a thick paste.','Brush the fish generously with this paste, including in the cuts, marinate 30 minutes.','Grill over wood embers 5 to 6 minutes per side until the skin cracks.','Boil then pound the cassava leaves, serve with the grilled fish, rice and a lime wedge.']::text[]),
('solomon-cassava-fish','en','Fish in coconut cream with cassava','Honiara, Solomon Islands','White fish barely simmered in fresh, hand-pressed coconut cream, served with boiled cassava and sweet potato, the everyday meal of most households across the Solomon Islands.',ARRAY['Peel and chunk the cassava and sweet potato, boil 20 minutes until tender.','Cut the fish into large pieces, salt lightly.','Warm the coconut milk to a bare simmer with the sliced onion.','Add the fish and poach gently for 8 minutes, never letting it boil hard.','Serve the fish in coconut milk with the cassava, sweet potato and a squeeze of lime, alongside boiled leafy greens.']::text[]),
('lu-pulu','en','Lu pulu','Nuku''alofa, Tonga','Corned beef or stewed beef wrapped in several layers of taro leaves with coconut milk, tied and steam-cooked until the thick leaves melt into a texture close to wilted spinach.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into pieces, mix with the crumbled corned beef and sliced onion.','Stack four to five taro leaves in a star shape to form a wide base.','Place the meat filling in the centre, fold the leaves into a tight parcel, tie.','Arrange the parcels in a pot, cover generously with coconut milk.','Cook covered 1h30 to 2 hours over low heat until the leaves are meltingly soft, serve hot with rice or boiled taro.']::text[]),
('bougna','en','Bougna','Nouméa, New Caledonia','The Kanak festive dish: fish, chicken or lobster wrapped with yam, sweet potato and plantain in banana leaves, drenched in coconut milk and cooked for hours in an earthen pit oven heated with volcanic stones.',ARRAY['Peel and chunk the yam, sweet potato and plantain.','Layer several banana leaves to form a wide, sturdy base.','Arrange the fish and vegetables in the centre, drench generously with coconut milk, salt.','Fold the leaves to wrap everything into one large parcel, tie securely.','Steam-cook 2 hours over hot stones or in a very low oven, unwrap and serve straight from the leaves.']::text[]),
('dambou','en','Dambou','Niamey, Niger','Steamed millet semolina mixed with pounded cabbage or spinach leaves, scented with peanut oil and dried chilli, a Nigerien family dish varying with whatever greens are found at the day''s market.',ARRAY['Moisten the millet semolina and fluff it by hand, steam it 20 minutes in a couscoussier.','Boil the cabbage and spinach 10 minutes, drain and roughly pound in a mortar.','Fry onion and tomato in peanut oil with the chilli.','Mix the pounded greens into the tomato-onion sauce.','Fold the cooked semolina into the greens, fluff with a fork, reheat 5 minutes over steam and serve hot.']::text[]),
('riz-gras','en','Riz gras','Ouagadougou, Burkina Faso','Rice cooked directly in an oil-rich tomato sauce with meat and vegetables, which gives it its orange colour and its name: "fatty rice". Burkina Faso''s quintessential festive dish, made in huge batches for big occasions.',ARRAY['Brown the beef pieces with onion and garlic in oil.','Add crushed tomato, bay, cover with water and simmer 40 minutes until tender.','Add carrot and cabbage in chunks, cook a further 10 minutes.','Remove the meat and vegetables, cook the rinsed rice directly in the remaining tomato sauce and its oil.','Once the rice is cooked and coloured, return the meat and vegetables, warm through together and serve.']::text[]),
('soupe-kandia','en','Soupe kandia','Conakry, Guinea','An okra sauce pounded until stringy and viscous, simmered with smoked fish and palm oil until it thickens into an almost sticky texture, poured over hot white rice.',ARRAY['Slice the okra into thin rounds, pound half of it in a mortar into a stringy paste.','Fry onion, garlic and tomato in palm oil.','Add the whole and pounded okra, the flaked smoked fish and chilli, moisten with water.','Simmer 30 minutes over low heat, stirring regularly, until the sauce is thick and viscous.','Serve over a bed of hot white rice.']::text[]),
('cassava-leaf-stew','en','Cassava leaf stew','Freetown, Sierra Leone','Cassava leaves pounded at length in a mortar into a fine paste, simmered for hours with smoked fish, meat and palm oil until nearly black and deeply fragrant.',ARRAY['Pound the cassava leaves at length in a mortar into a fine, smooth paste.','Boil the beef pieces 30 minutes until tender.','Add the cassava paste, flaked smoked fish, onion, chilli and pounded crayfish.','Pour in the palm oil and simmer 45 minutes over low heat, stirring often, until the mixture darkens and thickens.','Serve generously over hot white rice.']::text[]),
('palava-sauce','en','Palava sauce','Monrovia, Liberia','Finely chopped sweet potato or amaranth leaves, simmered with fish, smoked meat and peanut butter into a thick green sauce, whose name is said to come from the animated palavers exchanged while cooking it.',ARRAY['Finely chop the sweet potato leaves.','Boil the beef pieces 25 minutes until tender.','Add the flaked smoked fish, onion, chilli and chopped leaves.','Loosen the peanut butter with a little hot broth, stir into the sauce with the palm oil.','Simmer 25 minutes over low heat, stirring, until a thick green sauce forms, serve over rice.']::text[]),
('amiwo','en','Amiwo','Cotonou, Benin','Cornmeal batter beaten until smooth and firm, tinted red by a very spicy tomato sauce, served with grilled fish or chicken at Cotonou''s street stalls.',ARRAY['Make a well-spiced tomato sauce with onion, chilli and palm oil, let it reduce 20 minutes.','Mix a third of the cornmeal with a little cold water, pour into boiling water whisking.','Stir in the rest of the flour, beating vigorously with a wooden paddle.','Add half the tomato sauce directly into the corn batter while still beating, until a smooth, firm red dough forms.','Grill the whole fish, serve the amiwo with the fish and the rest of the tomato sauce.']::text[]),
('ablo','en','Ablo with pepper sauce','Lomé, Togo','Small fermented rice cakes steamed in banana leaves until soft and faintly tangy, sold in the evening on Lomé''s streets with a fiery pepper-tomato sauce.',ARRAY['Mix rice flour, water, yeast and sugar into a semi-liquid batter, let ferment at room temperature 4 to 6 hours.','Cut squares of banana leaf, pour a ladle of fermented batter into the centre of each.','Fold into small closed parcels and steam 20 minutes until the cakes are firm.','Blend tomato, chilli and onion into a fresh, fiery sauce, salt.','Serve the cakes warm, unwrapped, with the hot sauce.']::text[]),
('domoda','en','Domoda','Banjul, Gambia','A beef stew simmered in a thick peanut paste and tomato sauce, with cabbage and sweet potato, Gambia''s national dish, often compared to a mafé but always more concentrated in peanut.',ARRAY['Brown the beef cubes with onion and garlic in oil.','Add crushed tomato, cover with water and simmer 30 minutes.','Loosen the peanut butter with a little hot broth, stir into the stew.','Add cabbage, sweet potato and a whole chilli, cook a further 30 minutes over low heat, stirring regularly.','Adjust seasoning once the sauce is thick and creamy, serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('mechoui','en','Mauritanian méchoui','Nouakchott, Mauritania','A whole lamb basted with butter and spices, slowly turned over wood embers for hours until the skin becomes crackling and golden, a dish of hospitality offered on great family occasions.',ARRAY['Blend melted butter, crushed garlic, cumin, paprika, lemon juice and salt into a thick marinade.','Rub the whole lamb generously with this marinade, including inside, let rest 2 hours.','Skewer the lamb onto a long, sturdy metal or wooden rod.','Turn slowly over moderate embers 3 to 4 hours, basting regularly with the remaining marinade.','Check the meat pulls easily off the bone before carving and serving with bread and a fresh salad.']::text[]),
('gozo','en','Gozo with peanut sauce','Bangui, Central African Republic','A cassava paste beaten until smooth and elastic, the Central African staple, served with a thick peanut and leafy greens sauce, torn into small balls for dipping.',ARRAY['Pour the cassava flour into the boiling water in a stream, beating constantly with a wooden paddle until a smooth ball forms.','Fry onion, tomato and chilli in palm oil.','Loosen the peanut butter with a little hot water, add to the tomato sauce.','Stir in the shredded spinach, simmer 15 minutes until thickened.','Serve the gozo as a ball alongside the peanut sauce, tearing off pieces by hand to dip.']::text[]),
('poulet-nyembwe','en','Poulet nyembwe','Libreville, Gabon','Gabon''s national dish: chicken simmered in a palm nut cream extracted and pounded by hand, whose deep orange colour and richness echo the Congolese moambe, spiked with more chilli.',ARRAY['Brown the chicken pieces in a pot, set aside.','Fry sliced onion, garlic and ginger, add crushed tomato and chilli.','Return the chicken, stir in the palm nut cream and bay, cover with water.','Simmer covered 45 minutes until the sauce thickens and the oil rises to the surface.','Serve with white rice or boiled cassava.']::text[]),
('saka-saka','en','Saka-saka with chikwangue','Brazzaville, Congo','Finely pounded cassava leaves, simmered in palm oil with eggplant and smoked fish, served with chikwangue, a fermented cassava loaf wrapped and cooked in leaves, with a sticky, faintly tangy texture.',ARRAY['Make the chikwangue by fermenting grated cassava for 2 days, then wrapping it in banana leaves and boiling 2 hours.','Finely pound the cassava leaves, boil 20 minutes, drain.','Fry onion, garlic and chilli in palm oil, add the diced eggplant.','Stir in the pounded cassava leaves and flaked smoked fish, simmer 25 minutes over low heat.','Serve the saka-saka hot with slices of warm chikwangue.']::text[]),
('pepesoup','en','Pepper soup','Malabo, Equatorial Guinea','A clear fish broth with fiery chilli and locally bitter tree bark, reputed to cure colds and rough mornings after, served scalding hot in small bowls along the Gulf of Guinea.',ARRAY['Cut the fish into pieces, rinse well.','Bring the water to the boil with sliced onion, garlic and ginger.','Add the fish and the chilli, whole or crushed depending on the desired heat.','Simmer 20 minutes over low heat until the fish is cooked and the broth fragrant.','Add the chopped aromatic leaves at the end, serve scalding hot in small individual bowls.']::text[]),
('skoudehkaris','en','Skoudehkaris','Djibouti City, Djibouti','Djibouti''s national dish: rice cooked in the lamb''s own juices, coloured with turmeric and cardamom, inherited from the trading crossroads between the Arabian peninsula, India and the Horn of Africa.',ARRAY['Brown the lamb pieces with onion and garlic in oil.','Add crushed tomato, turmeric, cardamom and cinnamon, cover with water and simmer 40 minutes.','Remove the meat, reserve the fragrant broth.','Fry the rinsed rice for 2 minutes, moisten with the hot broth and cook covered 18 minutes.','Return the meat onto the rice, warm through together a few minutes and serve hot.']::text[]),
('zigni','en','Zigni','Asmara, Eritrea','A beef stew simmered in berbere, a blend of chillies and toasted spices unique to the Horn of Africa, served on injera, the large spongy flatbread made of fermented teff, which doubles as cutlery.',ARRAY['Make the injera by mixing teff flour and water, let ferment 2 days then cook into large thin flatbreads in a hot dry pan.','Slowly melt down the sliced onions dry in a pot until they reduce and brown, 20 minutes.','Add the butter, garlic, ginger and berbere, fry 5 minutes until the oil turns red.','Add the cubed beef and tomato, moisten and simmer 45 minutes over low heat until tender.','Serve the stew on the injera, tearing off pieces of bread to scoop up the meat.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('bariis-iskukaris','en','Bariis iskukaris','Mogadishu, Somalia','Long basmati rice scented with the Somali xawaash spice blend, tinted with a thread of saffron, often served with a sliced banana on the side, in a sweet-savoury pairing typical of the Horn of Africa.',ARRAY['Brown the chicken with onion and garlic in oil.','Dust with xawaash, moisten and simmer 30 minutes until tender.','Remove the chicken, reserve the strained fragrant broth.','Fry the rinsed rice, moisten with the hot saffron broth, cook covered 18 minutes.','Serve the rice with the chicken, raisins and slices of fresh banana on the side.']::text[]),
('ibiharage','en','Ibiharage','Bujumbura, Burundi','Red beans simmered at length in red palm oil with onion and tomato, until forming an almost creamy thick sauce, the everyday staple of most Burundian households, served with boiled plantain.',ARRAY['Soak the red beans overnight, drain.','Cook 1 hour in fresh water until tender.','Fry onion, garlic and tomato in palm oil.','Add the drained beans to the sauce, simmer 20 minutes, lightly mashing some to thicken.','Boil the peeled plantains, serve alongside the beans.']::text[]),
('nsima-chambo','en','Nsima with chambo','Lake Malawi, Malawi','A ball of cornmeal beaten until firm, served with chambo, a tilapia caught in Lake Malawi and fried whole until the skin cracks, a fish so prized it appears on the country''s banknotes.',ARRAY['Mix a third of the cornmeal with cold water, pour into boiling water whisking.','Stir in the rest of the flour, beating vigorously with a wooden paddle until a smooth, firm ball forms.','Gut and scale the tilapia, salt and fry whole in hot oil 5 minutes per side until the skin cracks.','Fry tomato and onion for a fresh sauce to serve alongside.','Serve the nsima as a ball with the fried chambo, the tomato sauce and a lemon wedge.']::text[]),
('ifisashi','en','Ifisashi','Lusaka, Zambia','Local leafy greens simmered in a thick pounded peanut sauce, served with nshima, the same beaten cornmeal ball that accompanies almost every Zambian meal, whatever the day.',ARRAY['Dry-roast the peanuts then coarsely pound them in a mortar.','Fry onion and tomato, add the pounded peanuts and a little water to form a sauce.','Add the chopped leafy greens, simmer 15 minutes until tender.','Make the nshima by beating the cornmeal into boiling water until a smooth, firm ball forms.','Serve the nshima with the hot ifisashi on the side.']::text[]),
('kapana','en','Kapana','Windhoek, Namibia','Beef grilled hot on a scorching plate right in the heart of Katutura market, sliced in front of the customer and dipped into a ground chilli-and-spice mix, eaten standing with no plate.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into wide, thin strips.','Heat a cast-iron plate or very wide pan over high heat.','Grill the beef strips 2 to 3 minutes per side, turning often to sear all sides well.','Blend chilli, paprika, garlic and salt into a dry mix for dipping.','Slice the grilled meat into small pieces in front of the guests, serve with the spice mix and a lemon wedge.']::text[]),
('motoho','en','Motoho','Maseru, Lesotho','A sorghum porridge fermented for a few days before cooking, which gives it a natural tang, eaten by the spoonful or poured into a cup like a thick Basotho breakfast drink.',ARRAY['Mix the sorghum flour with a little warm water, let ferment at room temperature 2 to 3 days in a jar covered with a cloth.','Bring the rest of the water to the boil.','Pour the fermented flour in a stream into the boiling water, stirring constantly.','Cook 15 minutes over low heat, whisking regularly, until a fluid, faintly tangy porridge forms.','Sweeten lightly, salt and serve warm, by the spoonful or poured into a cup.']::text[]),
('sidvudvu','en','Sidvudvu','Mbabane, Eswatini','Pumpkin boiled then mashed directly into the cornmeal it cooks alongside, forming a sweet, orange porridge, a comforting dish of the Swazi kingdom.',ARRAY['Peel and chunk the pumpkin, boil 20 minutes until fully tender.','Mash the pumpkin directly in its cooking water.','Add the cornmeal in a stream, beating constantly with a wooden paddle.','Continue cooking 10 minutes over low heat until a thick, even porridge forms.','Sweeten lightly, add the butter and salt, serve hot.']::text[]),
('kari-pwason','en','Kari pwason','Victoria, Seychelles','A fish curry with coconut milk and massalé, the Creole spice blend unique to the Seychelles, simmered with fresh tamarind for a tangy edge, served with rice and a breadfruit salad.',ARRAY['Fry onion, garlic, ginger and curry leaves in oil.','Add the massalé and toast briefly until fragrant.','Add tomato and tamarind, moisten with the coconut milk and simmer 15 minutes.','Add the fish cut into pieces, let it barely simmer 10 minutes without boiling hard so it doesn''t break apart.','Boil the breadfruit in chunks, serve the curry with rice and the breadfruit.']::text[]),
('dholl-puri','en','Dholl puri','Port Louis, Mauritius','A thin flatbread filled with ground, cooked yellow split peas, rolled barely thicker than a crêpe, served folded with a curry and chilli sauce. Mauritius''s most eaten Indian heritage food, sold on every street corner.',ARRAY['Soak the split peas 2 hours, cook until tender then drain and let dry.','Grind the cooked split peas with cumin and turmeric into a fine powder.','Knead flour, water and salt into a supple dough, rest 20 minutes, divide into balls.','Flatten each ball, fill with split pea powder, close and roll out again very thin.','Cook on a lightly oiled hot griddle 2 minutes per side, serve folded with a garlic chilli sauce.']::text[]),
('langouste-vanille','en','Vanilla lobster','Moroni, Comoros','Lobster from the Comorian lagoons flambéed then simmered in a coconut cream sauce infused with local vanilla pods, the archipelago being one of the world''s top producers of this spice.',ARRAY['Split the lobsters in half lengthwise, lightly shell the tail.','Sear them in butter 2 minutes per side, flambé with rum.','Remove the lobsters, sweat onion and garlic in the same butter.','Add the coconut milk and scraped vanilla seeds, let reduce 10 minutes into a velvety sauce.','Return the lobsters to the sauce, warm through 3 minutes, serve with a squeeze of lime.']::text[]),
('cachupa','en','Cachupa','Praia, Cape Verde','Cape Verde''s national dish, cracked corn and beans simmered for hours with pork and vegetables until nearly a purée, even better reheated and pan-fried the next day, then called cachupa refogada.',ARRAY['Soak the cracked corn and white beans overnight, drain.','Brown the pork and smoked sausage with onion, garlic and tomato.','Add the drained corn and beans, cover generously with water and simmer 2h30 over low heat.','Add cabbage and sweet potato in chunks, cook a further 30 minutes until everything is very tender.','Serve hot, or set aside and pan-fry the next day for the refogada version.']::text[]),
('muzongue','en','Muzongue','São Tomé, São Tomé and Príncipe','A fish soup spiced with local red chilli and palm oil, thickened with grated green plantain, a São Toméan festive dish made for big family gatherings by the ocean.',ARRAY['Cut the fish into pieces, salt lightly.','Fry onion, garlic and tomato in palm oil.','Finely grate the raw green plantains, add to the sauce with water to thicken.','Add the fish and chilli, simmer 20 minutes over low heat until the fish is cooked and the soup has thickened.','Serve scalding hot with a squeeze of lime.']::text[]),
('belize-rice-beans','en','Rice and beans with stewed chicken','Belize City, Belize','Belize''s quintessential Sunday meal: rice cooked in coconut milk and the soaking water of red beans, served with chicken slow-simmered in a recado sauce, a deep-red achiote condiment.',ARRAY['Soak the red beans overnight, drain reserving the soaking water.','Rub the chicken with achiote paste, garlic and salt, brown then simmer 40 minutes with onion and pepper.','Cook the beans in their soaking water until tender.','Add the rinsed rice and coconut milk to the cooked beans, cook covered 18 minutes.','Serve the rice and beans with the stewed chicken and its sauce.']::text[]),
('oil-down','en','Oil down','St George''s, Grenada','Grenada''s national dish: breadfruit, vegetables and salted meat layered and simmered in coconut milk until the liquid fully evaporates and the coconut oil rises to the surface, hence the name.',ARRAY['Desalt the salted pork 2 hours in cold water, drain and cut into pieces.','Peel and cut the breadfruit into large wedges.','Layer in a large pot: pork, sausage, onion, then breadfruit, salting each layer.','Mix the coconut milk with turmeric, pour over the layers, add the whole chilli.','Cover and simmer 45 minutes without stirring until the liquid nearly fully evaporates, add the leafy greens for the last 10 minutes.']::text[]),
('cou-cou-flying-fish','en','Cou-cou with flying fish','Bridgetown, Barbados','Barbados''s national dish: a cornmeal and okra mash beaten with a wooden spoon until smooth, served with flying fish poached in a Creole tomato sauce, a fish that even appears on the country''s coins.',ARRAY['Slice the okra into rounds, boil 10 minutes in salted water, drain reserving the water.','Pour the cornmeal into the boiling okra water in a stream, beating constantly with a wooden spoon.','Fold in the cooked okra, keep beating 10 minutes until a smooth, supple mash forms, add the butter.','Marinate the flying fish with lime and thyme, poach 15 minutes in a tomato and onion sauce.','Serve the cou-cou as a dome with the fish and its sauce on the side.']::text[]),
('green-fig-saltfish','en','Green fig and saltfish','Castries, Saint Lucia','Saint Lucia''s national dish: green bananas, called "figs" in the English Caribbean, boiled, flaked and mixed with desalted saltfish sautéed with tomato, onion and chilli.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod 24 hours, changing the water several times, then poach 10 minutes and flake.','Boil the unpeeled green bananas 20 minutes until tender, peel and slice.','Fry onion, pepper, garlic and chilli in oil.','Add crushed tomato and flaked cod, simmer 10 minutes.','Gently mix with the sliced bananas, scatter with scallion and serve hot.']::text[]),
('fungee-pepperpot','en','Fungee and pepperpot','St John''s, Antigua and Barbuda','Antigua''s national dish: a firm cornmeal and okra ball called fungee, served with a thick stew of leafy greens, okra and salted meat called pepperpot, as old as the settlement of the eastern Caribbean.',ARRAY['Desalt the salted pork 2 hours, drain and cut into pieces.','Simmer it with pumpkin, spinach, onion and chilli in water for 40 minutes until everything is tender and the sauce thick.','Slice the okra into rounds, boil in a little water, drain reserving the water.','Pour the cornmeal into the boiling okra water in a stream, beat with a paddle, fold in the cooked okra until a firm ball forms.','Serve the fungee as a ball with the hot pepperpot on the side.']::text[]),
('dominica-bouyon','en','Dominican bouyon','Roseau, Dominica','A hearty broth of local roots — yam, dasheen, green banana — and salted meat, thickened with flour dumplings, made every Sunday in nearly every household on the island of a thousand rivers.',ARRAY['Desalt the salted pork 2 hours, drain and cut into pieces.','Simmer it with onion and thyme in water for 30 minutes.','Peel and chunk the yam, dasheen and green banana, add to the broth and cook a further 20 minutes.','Knead flour and water into a firm dough, shape small elongated dumplings, add to the broth 10 minutes before the end.','Adjust seasoning, scatter with scallion and serve scalding hot in large bowls.']::text[]),
('pom','en','Pom','Paramaribo, Suriname','A bake of shredded chicken and grated pomtajer root, tied to Surinamese-Jewish heritage with a bitter orange sauce, oven-baked until golden on top, an unmissable dish for big family celebrations.',ARRAY['Poach the chicken 30 minutes until tender, shred it, reserving the broth.','Finely grate the raw pomtajer root.','Fry onion and garlic, add tomato and bitter orange juice, let reduce 10 minutes.','Mix shredded chicken, grated pomtajer and sauce, pour into a buttered baking dish, dust with breadcrumbs.','Bake 45 minutes at 180°C until the top is well browned and crisp.']::text[]),
('guyana-pepperpot','en','Guyanese pepperpot','Georgetown, Guyana','Guyana''s national Christmas dish, a beef and pig tail stew simmered for hours in cassareep, a black boiled bitter cassava syrup that acts as a natural preservative, letting the pot be reboiled daily for weeks.',ARRAY['Desalt the pig tail 2 hours, drain.','Place beef and pig tail in a large pot with onion, cinnamon and cloves.','Add the cassareep and cover with water, bring to the boil then simmer covered 2h30 over low heat.','Add the whole scotch bonnet pepper without piercing it to only lightly flavour, and the sugar.','Continue cooking until the sauce is black and thick and the meat is meltingly tender, serve with bread.']::text[]),
('appam-stew','en','Appam and stew','Kochi, Kerala, India','A fermented rice and coconut milk pancake, thin and crisp at the edges but thick and spongy in the middle, cooked in a curved pan called an appachatti, served with a mild coconut milk chicken curry, without excessive chilli.',ARRAY['Soak the rice 6 hours, blend with a little coconut milk into a smooth batter.','Add yeast and sugar, let ferment at room temperature overnight until the batter doubles and foams.','Pour a ladle of batter into a hot appachatti, swirl to spread thin edges and leave the centre thick, cover 2 minutes.','Fry onion, garlic, ginger and spices, add the chicken and brown it.','Moisten with the rest of the coconut milk, simmer 25 minutes until tender, serve with the warm appams.']::text[]),
('sarson-da-saag','en','Sarson da saag with makki di roti','Amritsar, Punjab, India','Mustard leaves simmered for hours then pounded into a velvety green purée with clarified butter, paired with a thick cornmeal flatbread, a Punjabi winter speciality eaten with a knob of butter melting at the centre.',ARRAY['Boil mustard leaves and spinach with ginger, garlic and chilli for 40 minutes until very tender.','Drain, reserving a little water, pound or roughly blend into a thick purée.','Fry the sliced onion in ghee until browned, stir into the green purée, simmer 10 minutes.','Knead the cornmeal with warm water into a supple dough, shape thick discs by hand.','Cook the roti dry in a hot pan, brush with ghee, serve with the saag topped with a knob of butter.']::text[]),
('macher-jhol','en','Macher jhol','Kolkata, West Bengal, India','A light, watery freshwater fish curry, scented with panch phoron, a five-whole-seed spice blend unique to Bengal, with potato and turmeric, eaten daily with rice by millions of Bengali households.',ARRAY['Rub the fish with turmeric and salt, lightly fry in mustard oil, set aside.','Heat a little oil, add the panch phoron and let it crackle a few seconds.','Add potato in chunks and tomato, fry then moisten with water.','Simmer 15 minutes until the potato is nearly tender, add the split green chillies.','Return the fish to the broth, let it barely simmer 8 minutes without boiling hard, scatter with coriander and serve with rice.']::text[]),
('dal-baati-churma','en','Dal baati churma','Jaipur, Rajasthan, India','Balls of hard wheat dough baked then dipped in melted ghee, served with a mixed lentil dal and a sweet churma of pounded wheat with sugar and cardamom. Three textures, three temperatures, one Rajasthani platter.',ARRAY['Knead whole wheat flour, melted ghee and water into a firm dough, shape into thick balls.','Bake the balls 30 minutes at 180°C until firm and cracked, turning halfway.','Cook both lentils together with turmeric until tender, fry onion, tomato and cumin then mix into the dal.','Crumble a third of the baked balls, pound with sugar, cardamom and melted ghee for the churma.','Dip the remaining balls in melted ghee, serve the trio of dal, baati and churma on one platter.']::text[]),
('dapanji','en','Dapanji (big plate chicken)','Ürümqi, Xinjiang, China','Chicken pieces simmered with potato and peppers in a sauce both spicy and lightly sweet, poured still steaming over wide hand-pulled fresh noodles, a Uyghur speciality of the Silk Road.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water and salt into a supple dough, rest 1 hour under oil, stretch into wide flat noodles.','Fry the chicken pieces with garlic, dried chillies, Sichuan pepper and star anise in oil.','Add potato in large chunks, soy and sugar, moisten and simmer 30 minutes.','Add the pepper strips for the last 10 minutes of cooking.','Cook the noodles in boiling water 3 minutes, drain and pour the chicken and its scalding sauce over the top.']::text[]),
('yangzhou-fried-rice','en','Yangzhou fried rice','Yangzhou, Jiangsu, China','Day-old rice, wok-fried grain by grain over high heat with ham, shrimp and peas, every grain meant to stay separate and coated in golden egg, never sticky. A classic born in the imperial kitchens of the Sui dynasty.',ARRAY['Use rice cooked the day before and well chilled, fluff with a fork to separate the grains.','Beat the eggs, scramble them quickly in the very hot wok, set aside in small pieces.','Fry shrimp and diced ham in oil until just cooked.','Add the rice, stir-fry over high heat, stirring constantly to separate the grains well, add peas and egg.','Season with soy sauce and sesame oil, scatter with chopped scallion and serve immediately, piping hot.']::text[]),
('guilin-rice-noodles','en','Guilin rice noodles','Guilin, Guangxi, China','Fresh rice noodles drowned in a pork and bone broth simmered for hours with spices unique to each stall, topped with marinated pork and peanuts, an unmissable breakfast dish in the karst streets of Guilin.',ARRAY['Boil the pork bones 5 minutes, rinse to remove the scum.','Return to cook with star anise and cinnamon in plenty of water, simmer 3 hours over very low heat.','Marinate and roast pork slices until well fragrant, slice thinly.','Warm the fresh rice noodles in boiling water a few seconds, drain and place in a bowl.','Pour the hot strained broth over, top with marinated pork, peanuts, pickled beans, scallion and a drizzle of chilli oil.']::text[]),
('guobaorou','en','Guobaorou','Harbin, Heilongjiang, China','Thin pork slices coated in starch and fried twice until extra crisp, then quickly tossed in a translucent sweet-sour sauce, a northeastern Chinese speciality invented to please Russian diplomats in the early last century.',ARRAY['Slice the pork thin, flatten lightly.','Mix starch, egg and a little water into a thick batter, coat each slice.','Fry once for 2 minutes at 160°C, drain, then fry a second time at 190°C until very crisp.','Make a sauce by heating sugar, rice vinegar, soy and garlic until syrupy and translucent.','Pour the scalding sauce over the fried pork just before serving, tossing quickly to coat without softening it, scatter with coriander.']::text[]),
('banh-mi','en','Bánh mì','Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam','A baguette inherited from French colonisation, with light crumb and thin crust, split and filled with pâté, grilled pork, vinegar-pickled vegetables and fresh coriander — the most successful meeting of French bakery and Vietnamese cuisine.',ARRAY['Cut carrot and daikon radish into fine julienne, marinate 30 minutes in vinegar and sugar.','Marinate the thinly sliced pork in soy sauce, grill 3 minutes per side.','Split the baguette lengthwise without cutting all the way through, spread with pâté and mayonnaise.','Fill with grilled pork, drained pickled vegetables and cucumber slices.','Finish with a few sprigs of fresh coriander and serve at once, crisp.']::text[]),
('bun-cha','en','Bún chả','Hanoi, Vietnam','Marinated pork patties grilled over charcoal until caramelised, dipped along with cold rice vermicelli and fresh herbs into a warm sweet-salty-tangy nuoc cham bowl, an emblematic lunchtime dish of Hanoi''s sidewalks.',ARRAY['Mix minced pork with a little fish sauce, sugar and crushed garlic, shape into small flat patties.','Grill the patties over charcoal embers 4 minutes per side until caramelised.','Make the nuoc cham by mixing fish sauce, sugar, lime juice, warm water, minced garlic and chilli.','Cut the carrot into fine julienne, add it lightly marinated to the nuoc cham.','Serve the grilled patties floating in the warm nuoc cham, with cold rice vermicelli and fresh herbs on the side for dipping.']::text[]),
('tom-yum-goong','en','Tom yum goong','Bangkok, Thailand','A shrimp soup that is at once fiery, sour and fragrant, where lemongrass, galangal and kaffir lime leaves infuse a clear broth spiked with chilli and lime juice at the very last moment, to preserve its zing.',ARRAY['Shell the shrimp, reserving the heads and shells.','Fry the shells with lemongrass, galangal and kaffir lime leaves in a little oil, moisten with water and let infuse 15 minutes, strain.','Bring the strained broth to the boil, add mushrooms and tomato, cook 5 minutes.','Add the shelled shrimp, cook 3 minutes until they turn pink.','Off the heat, season with fish sauce, lime juice and crushed chilli, scatter with coriander and serve immediately.']::text[]),
('som-tam','en','Som tam','Nakhon Ratchasima, Thailand','Grated green papaya pounded in a mortar with garlic, chilli, cherry tomato and long beans, dressed with fish sauce, lime and palm sugar, the clatter of the pestle against the wooden mortar setting the rhythm of Isan''s streets.',ARRAY['Shred the green papaya into fine strips.','Pound garlic and chilli in a mortar, add the cut long beans and pound lightly to soften them.','Add the shredded papaya and halved cherry tomatoes, pound and mix without turning to purée.','Season with fish sauce, palm sugar and lime juice, pound again to blend the flavours well.','Scatter with crushed peanuts and dried shrimp, serve immediately, well chilled.']::text[]),
('manti','en','Mantı','Kayseri, Turkey','Tiny dumplings stuffed with minced beef, so small that tradition holds you should fit forty on a single spoon, drowned under garlic yoghurt and a sizzling paprika oil poured at the table.',ARRAY['Knead flour, egg, water and salt into a very firm dough, rest 30 minutes.','Mix the minced beef with grated onion pressed to extract its juice, salt.','Roll the dough extremely thin, cut tiny 2 cm squares.','Fill each square with a dab of filling, pinch the four corners together to close.','Poach the mantı 10 minutes in boiling water, drain, top with garlic yoghurt and sizzling paprika butter poured hot at the table.']::text[]),
('lahmacun','en','Lahmacun','Şanlıurfa, Turkey','Dough stretched paper-thin, spread edge to edge with a raw paste of minced meat, tomato, pepper and spices, baked just a few minutes in a very hot oven, rolled around parsley and lemon to be eaten by hand.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water, yeast and salt into a supple dough, let rise 1 hour.','Blend raw minced meat, tomato, pepper, onion, garlic and paprika into a fine, even paste.','Divide the bread dough into balls, stretch each as thin as possible into an almost transparent disc.','Spread a thin layer of the meat paste to the edges of each disc.','Bake 6 to 8 minutes in a very hot 250°C oven, roll around fresh parsley and a squeeze of lemon before serving.']::text[]),
('gado-gado','en','Gado-gado','Jakarta, Indonesia','A composed salad of cooked and raw vegetables, tofu, tempeh and hard-boiled egg, drowned under a thick peanut sauce scented with kecap manis, Indonesia''s sweet soy syrup. Its name literally means "mix-mix".',ARRAY['Separately cook cabbage, green beans, bean sprouts and potatoes by steaming until tender but still crisp.','Fry the tofu and tempeh in golden dice, hard-boil the eggs.','Dry-roast the peanuts, blend with garlic, chilli, kecap manis, lime juice and a little water into a thick sauce.','Arrange all the vegetables, tofu, tempeh, eggs and raw cucumber on a large platter.','Coat generously with the peanut sauce and serve at room temperature.']::text[]),
('sate-ayam','en','Satay ayam','Madura, Indonesia','Small cubes of marinated chicken threaded onto bamboo skewers, grilled over coconut shell embers until lightly caramelised, served with a thick peanut sauce and compressed rice, a speciality of Madura island famed for its street vendors.',ARRAY['Cut the chicken into small cubes, marinate with soy, crushed garlic and ground coriander for 30 minutes.','Thread the cubes onto bamboo skewers pre-soaked in water.','Grill over moderate embers 8 to 10 minutes, turning regularly, until lightly caramelised.','Dry-roast the peanuts, blend with kecap manis, chilli, lime juice and a little water for the sauce.','Serve the skewers coated with peanut sauce, with compressed rice cubes.']::text[]),
('jambalaya','en','Jambalaya','New Orleans, Louisiana, USA','Rice simmered in a broth coloured by the Creole holy trinity — onion, celery, green pepper — with smoked andouille sausage and shrimp, heir to Spanish paellas and African pilafs brought together by centuries of Louisianan mixing.',ARRAY['Fry the sliced smoked sausage until it releases its fat, set aside.','In the same fat, sweat chopped onion, celery and green pepper until tender.','Add garlic, tomato, cayenne and bay leaf, fry 2 minutes, return the sausage.','Add the rice, moisten with chicken broth, cover and cook 20 minutes over low heat without stirring.','Add the shrimp for the last 5 minutes, mix gently and serve piping hot.']::text[]),
('philly-cheesesteak','en','Philly cheesesteak','Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, USA','Thinly sliced beef griddled on a flat-top, mingled with softened onions and drowned in melted cheese, piled into a soft roll — a sandwich born in the 1930s whose version every local defends for their own neighbourhood.',ARRAY['Freeze the beef slightly to ease slicing, cut into very thin strips.','Melt the sliced onion in butter on a hot griddle until browned.','Add the beef, grill it quickly, chopping it with a spatula as it cooks.','Scatter the cheese over the hot meat, let it melt as you fold everything together.','Fill the split hoagie roll with this steaming mixture and serve immediately.']::text[]),
('nashville-hot-chicken','en','Nashville hot chicken','Nashville, Tennessee, USA','Fried chicken with an extra-crisp coating, brushed straight from the fryer with a scorching paste of cayenne pepper and hot fat, served on a slice of white bread with pickles to soak up the fire.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken pieces in buttermilk at least 4 hours, ideally overnight.','Drain, dredge in flour seasoned with salt and pepper, pressing well to adhere.','Fry at 175°C for 15 to 18 minutes until deep golden and crisp, turning halfway.','Mix cayenne, brown sugar, paprika and a little scalding frying oil into a thick paste.','Generously brush the still-hot chicken with this paste, serve on white bread with pickles.']::text[]),
('kalua-pig-laulau','en','Kalua pig and laulau','Honolulu, Hawaii, USA','A whole pig buried for hours in an underground imu oven heated with volcanic stones, emerging smoky, salty and falling apart, served alongside laulau — pork and fish wrapped in steamed taro leaves — the centrepiece of the luau feast.',ARRAY['Rub the pork shoulder generously with sea salt and liquid smoke to mimic the imu flavour.','Wrap in banana leaves, bake at a low 190°C for 5 hours until it falls apart.','For the laulau, wrap pieces of pork and fish in several taro leaves, then in a banana leaf.','Steam the laulau parcels 3 hours until the taro leaves are meltingly soft and no longer irritating.','Shred the pork with a fork, serve with the laulau parcels opened at the table.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('new-mexico-green-chile-stew','en','New Mexico green chile stew','Santa Fe, New Mexico, USA','A pork and potato stew carried by Hatch green chiles, roasted until blackened then peeled by hand, whose smoky, earthy flavour sets New Mexican cooking apart from the rest of the American Southwest.',ARRAY['Roast the green chiles over an open flame or in the oven until the skin is fully blackened.','Seal them in a plastic bag 10 minutes then peel, seed and chop.','Brown the cubed pork with onion, garlic and cumin in a pot.','Add the chopped chiles, diced potato and broth, bring to the boil.','Simmer 45 minutes covered until the pork is tender, season with oregano and serve with tortillas.']::text[]),
('cochinita-pibil','en','Cochinita pibil','Mérida, Yucatán, Mexico','Pork marinated in achiote that dyes it deep red, wrapped in banana leaves and originally cooked in a buried pit oven called a pib, inherited from the Maya, emerging tender and fragrant with bitter citrus.',ARRAY['Dilute the achiote paste in sour orange juice and crushed garlic into a smooth marinade.','Coat the pork generously with this marinade, let rest at least 4 hours or overnight.','Wrap the marinated pork in banana leaves passed over a flame to soften them.','Bake the parcel 4 hours at 150°C until the meat shreds easily.','Shred the pork, serve with red onion pickled in vinegar and habanero, with tortillas.']::text[]),
('mole-negro','en','Mole negro','Oaxaca, Mexico','An almost-black sauce blending chilhuacle chiles roasted to bitterness, chocolate, spices and dried fruit, reduced for hours into a complex paste that coats the chicken — the most elaborate of Oaxaca''s seven moles.',ARRAY['Dry-roast the dried chiles until nearly black and brittle, soak in hot water.','Likewise roast almonds, raisins, sesame and a torn tortilla until browned.','Blend the drained chiles, roasted dried fruit, tomato and cinnamon with a little broth into a smooth paste.','Fry the paste in a pot 20 minutes, stirring constantly to prevent sticking, add the chocolate and remaining broth.','Simmer the chicken in this sauce 40 minutes until tender and the sauce is thick and glossy.']::text[]),
('huachinango-veracruzana','en','Huachinango a la veracruzana','Veracruz, Mexico','A whole red snapper braised in a tomato sauce scattered with green olives, capers and sliced jalapeño peppers, a direct legacy of Spanish, Arab and Caribbean influences converging in Mexico''s first colonial port.',ARRAY['Sweat chopped onion and garlic in olive oil until translucent.','Add crushed tomato and bay leaf, simmer 15 minutes until reduced.','Add green olives, capers and sliced jalapeño, simmer another 5 minutes.','Place the whole fish in the sauce, cover and cook 15 minutes, basting regularly.','Check the flesh flakes easily with a fork and serve the fish coated in its sauce.']::text[]),
('feijao-tropeiro','en','Feijão tropeiro','Belo Horizonte, Minas Gerais, Brazil','Drained black beans then fried with cassava flour, bacon, sausage, collard greens and scrambled egg, a hearty dish born from the tropeiro mule drivers who crossed Minas Gerais with provisions that kept for weeks.',ARRAY['Boil the black beans until tender, drain reserving a little of their water.','Fry bacon and diced sausage until crisp, add onion and garlic.','Add the drained beans, mix over high heat a few minutes.','Gradually stir in the cassava flour, stirring to coat without lumps.','Add the shredded collard greens and separately scrambled egg, mix everything together and serve hot.']::text[]),
('acaraje','en','Acarajé','Salvador, Bahia, Brazil','Fritters of shelled black-eyed peas, ground and fried in red dendê palm oil until forming a golden crust, split open and filled with vatapá and dried shrimp, sold by white-robed baianas on Salvador''s streets.',ARRAY['Soak the black-eyed peas overnight, rub between your hands to remove the skins.','Blend the shelled peas with onion and a little water into a smooth, airy paste.','Beat the paste vigorously with a spoon for several minutes to incorporate air.','Shape oval fritters with a spoon, fry in boiling dendê oil until golden.','Split each hot acarajé open, fill with vatapá and dried shrimp, serve immediately.']::text[]),
('tacaca','en','Tacacá','Manaus, Amazonas, Brazil','A bright yellow broth of tucupi, bitter cassava juice fermented and boiled to remove its toxicity, thickened with jambu leaves that tingle and numb the tongue, garnished with dried shrimp, served scalding in cuias gourds hollowed from an Amazonian squash.',ARRAY['Boil the tucupi with crushed garlic for 30 minutes to fully purge its residual toxicity.','Separately blanch the jambu leaves in boiling water 5 minutes.','Dissolve a little cassava starch in cold water, stir into the boiling tucupi to lightly thicken it.','Rehydrate the dried shrimp in a little hot water.','Serve the scalding tucupi in a deep bowl with jambu leaves and dried shrimp arranged on top.']::text[]),
('lomo-saltado','en','Lomo saltado','Lima, Peru','Beef quickly stir-fried in a wok with red onion and tomato, splashed with soy sauce, served with both fries and rice on the same plate — the most emblematic chifa dish born from the meeting of Cantonese immigrants and Andean cooking.',ARRAY['Cut the potatoes into fries, fry until golden and crisp, set aside.','Cut the beef into thick strips, sear very quickly in a scorching wok.','Add red onion and tomato in wedges, stir-fry another 2 minutes over maximum heat.','Deglaze with soy sauce and vinegar, shake the wok to coat well without releasing too much juice.','Add the fries at the end of cooking so they stay crisp, scatter with coriander and serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('anticuchos','en','Anticuchos','Cusco, Peru','Skewers of beef heart marinated in aji panca chili paste, cumin and vinegar, grilled over embers until just caramelised outside while staying tender within, an Incan tradition kept alive on Peruvian street braziers.',ARRAY['Trim the beef heart, removing sinew and membrane, cut into even cubes.','Mix aji panca paste, vinegar, cumin and crushed garlic into a thick marinade.','Coat the heart cubes with this marinade, let rest at least 4 hours chilled.','Thread the cubes onto wooden skewers, brush with a little oil.','Grill over hot embers 3 to 4 minutes per side, basting with marinade, serve immediately piping hot.']::text[]),
('empanadas-saltenas','en','Empanadas salteñas','Salta, Argentina','Small pastry turnovers folded into a tight braided edge, stuffed with knife-chopped beef, hard-boiled egg, olive and cumin, baked in a wood oven, a speciality of northwestern Argentina where every province jealously claims its own recipe.',ARRAY['Knead flour, melted butter, warm water and salt into a supple dough, rest 30 minutes.','Fry the very finely chopped onion with cumin and paprika until soft, add the knife-chopped beef.','Moisten with a little broth to keep the filling juicy, let cool then stir in chopped hard-boiled egg and olives.','Roll out the dough, cut circles, fill with the mixture and close, pinching the edges into a tight braid.','Bake 20 minutes at 200°C until golden, serve hot.']::text[]),
('ajiaco-bogotano','en','Ajiaco bogotano','Bogotá, Colombia','A chicken soup thickened by three varieties of Andean potato that break down at different speeds, scented with guasca, a local herb found nowhere else, served with sour cream, capers and half a corn cob.',ARRAY['Poach the whole chicken in the broth with onion and garlic 40 minutes until tender, shred the meat and reserve the broth.','Peel the three potato varieties, cut into pieces of different sizes according to how readily each breaks down.','Cook the potatoes in the broth 25 minutes, stirring, some dissolving to thicken the soup.','Add the shredded chicken and the corn cobs cut into chunks, simmer 10 minutes.','Add the guasca at the end of cooking, serve with sour cream, capers and a piece of corn per bowl.']::text[]),
('civet-de-sanglier-corse','en','Corsican wild boar civet','Ajaccio, Corsica, France','Wild boar hunted in the maquis, marinated several days in red wine and myrtle, simmered for hours until the sauce naturally thickens with blood, a winter hunting dish served with local chestnut polenta.',ARRAY['Cut the boar into large chunks, marinate 48 hours in red wine with myrtle berries, juniper, onion, carrot and bay leaf.','Drain the meat, reserving the marinade, brown it vigorously in a pot.','Add the vegetables from the marinade, moisten with the strained wine, simmer covered 2h30 over very low heat.','Check the sauce has naturally thickened and the meat pulls apart easily, adjust seasoning.','Make the chestnut polenta by stirring the flour into boiling salted water, serve the civet coated in its sauce with the polenta.']::text[]),
('poulet-vallee-auge','en','Poulet vallée d''Auge','Pont-l''Évêque, Normandy, France','A farmhouse chicken flambéed in calvados then simmered in a thick fresh cream sauce with softened apples, an emblematic dish of the Pays d''Auge where apple orchards, dairy cows and stills shape the landscape.',ARRAY['Brown the chicken pieces in butter until nicely coloured.','Warm the calvados, pour over the chicken and flambé off the heat.','Add the sliced onion and dry cider, simmer covered 30 minutes.','Meanwhile, fry the apple wedges in a little butter until tender and golden.','Stir the cream into the cooking juices, let thicken a few minutes, add the apples and serve coated in sauce.']::text[]),
('culurgiones','en','Culurgiones','Ogliastra, Sardinia, Italy','Hand-sealed ravioli pinched into a braided wheat-ear pattern, filled with mashed potato, pecorino and wild mint, whose precise folding distinguishes each village of Sardinia''s Ogliastra region.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water and a pinch of salt into a firm, smooth dough, rest 30 minutes under a cloth.','Mash the cooked potato with grated pecorino, chopped mint, garlic and olive oil into a smooth filling.','Roll the dough thin, cut circles, place a spoonful of filling in the centre of each.','Close each disc by pinching the edges in small successive folds to form the characteristic braided pattern.','Poach the culurgiones in salted boiling water a few minutes until they float, serve coated in tomato sauce.']::text[]),
('nduja-pasta','en','Pasta with ''nduja','Spilinga, Calabria, Italy','A fermented pork and Calabrian chilli sausage, so soft it spreads like a paste, melted into a tomato sauce until it dyes the pasta a burning red, a speciality of Spilinga now exported worldwide.',ARRAY['Heat the olive oil with crushed garlic until fragrant.','Add the crumbled ''nduja, let it melt over low heat until it dyes the oil red.','Pour in the tomato sauce, simmer 15 minutes over low heat.','Cook the pasta al dente in well-salted water, drain reserving a little cooking water.','Toss the pasta with the sauce, adding a little cooking water if needed, scatter with pecorino and parsley.']::text[]),
('canederli','en','Canederli','Trento, Trentino, Italy','Large dumplings of stale bread, smoked bacon and cheese bound with egg, poached then served floating in a clear beef broth or simply buttered, an alpine speciality betraying the Austrian influence on Trentino-Alto Adige.',ARRAY['Cut the stale bread into small dice, soak in warm milk 15 minutes.','Fry the diced bacon until crisp, add it to the soaked bread.','Stir in beaten eggs, flour and chopped parsley, mix into a homogeneous but not overly compact mass.','Shape large dumplings with wet hands, let rest 15 minutes so they hold together.','Poach the canederli in simmering beef broth 15 minutes, serve floating in the broth or drained with melted butter.']::text[]),
('goya-champuru','en','Gōyā champurū','Naha, Okinawa, Japan','A stir-fry of bitter melon, firm tofu and pork belly, bound with beaten egg, whose name champurū means "mixture" in the Okinawan dialect — an everyday dish of the island famed for its residents'' exceptional longevity.',ARRAY['Cut the bitter melon in half, deseed and slice thinly, salt to draw out bitterness 10 minutes then rinse.','Press the firm tofu, cut into cubes, dry-fry until golden and set aside.','Fry the thinly sliced pork belly until crisp.','Add the drained bitter melon, stir-fry 3 minutes, return the golden tofu, season with soy sauce.','Pour the beaten egg over the top, let it set without stirring too much, scatter with bonito flakes and serve.']::text[]),
('takoyaki','en','Takoyaki','Osaka, Japan','Small dashi-based batter balls, cooked in a plate with hemispherical moulds with a piece of octopus hidden inside, flipped with a precise pick motion, topped with sweet-savoury sauce and mayonnaise.',ARRAY['Mix flour, dashi and beaten eggs into a smooth, liquid batter, rest 20 minutes.','Cut the octopus into small cubes, chop the scallion and pickled ginger.','Generously oil the heated takoyaki plate, pour the batter into each hemispherical mould until slightly overflowing.','Drop a piece of octopus, scallion and ginger into each well before the batter fully sets.','Flip each ball halfway through with a fine pick to form a perfect sphere, top with sauce, mayonnaise and bonito flakes.']::text[]),
('jingisukan','en','Jingisukan','Sapporo, Hokkaido, Japan','Lamb and mutton grilled on a domed plate shaped like a Mongolian helmet, surrounded by vegetables cooking in the fat that drips to the edges, a convivial Hokkaido dish named after Genghis Khan.',ARRAY['Prepare the marinade by blending soy sauce, mirin, garlic and grated apple.','Marinate thinly sliced lamb in half the sauce for 30 minutes.','Heat the domed plate, arrange the cut vegetables around the lower rim where fat will flow.','Grill the marinated lamb on the domed centre of the plate a few minutes per side.','Mix the grilled lamb with the vegetables cooked in the drippings, serve with the remaining sauce for dipping.']::text[]),
('cataplana-de-marisco','en','Cataplana de marisco','Portimão, Algarve, Portugal','A stew of clams, shrimp and chouriço simmered in a shell-shaped copper vessel that clamps shut, the steam cooking everything sealed inside, a culinary symbol of the Algarve born from exchanges with North Africa.',ARRAY['Soak the clams in salted water 1 hour to purge their sand.','Fry sliced onion, garlic and pepper in the base of the open cataplana.','Add crushed tomato and sliced chouriço, simmer 10 minutes.','Place clams and shrimp on top, splash with white wine, seal the cataplana shut.','Cook 12 minutes over high heat without opening, open at the table and scatter with coriander.']::text[]),
('espetada-madeirense','en','Madeiran espetada','Funchal, Madeira, Portugal','Large cubes of beef marinated in garlic and bay, threaded onto a fresh bay branch whittled to a point, hung above the table on a wrought-iron stand to be pulled straight onto the plate.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into large even cubes of about 4 cm.','Marinate with crushed garlic, crumbled bay leaf, salt and white wine for 4 hours.','Thread the cubes onto fresh bay branches whittled to a point.','Grill over hot embers, turning regularly, 10 to 12 minutes depending on desired doneness.','Hang the skewers above the table on a stand, serve with corn bread.']::text[]),
('bolo-do-caco','en','Bolo do caco','Câmara de Lobos, Madeira, Portugal','A round flatbread made with sweet potato, cooked not in an oven but directly on a hot volcanic stone called a caco, split still steaming and spread with garlic butter on the streets of Madeira.',ARRAY['Cook and mash the sweet potato into a fine purée, let cool.','Knead flour, sweet potato purée, yeast, salt and warm water into a supple dough, let rise 1 hour.','Divide into balls, flatten each into a thick disc about 2 cm.','Cook on a hot stone or dry cast-iron pan, 5 minutes per side.','Mix melted butter, crushed garlic and parsley, spread generously on the still-hot bread split in two.']::text[]),
('cozido-das-furnas','en','Cozido das Furnas','Furnas, São Miguel, Azores, Portugal','A pot of meats and vegetables sealed in a large cloth-wrapped cauldron, buried in the steaming volcanic ground of the Furnas crater and cooked for six hours by the island''s geothermal heat alone.',ARRAY['Arrange all the meats and cured pork at the bottom of a large sturdy cloth cauldron.','Add cabbage cut into wedges, carrot, potato and sweet potato on top.','Seal the cauldron shut and bury it in a hole dug into the hot fumarole ground.','Let cook this way for 6 hours by the natural geothermal steam alone.','Dig up the cauldron, open it at the table and serve the tender meats and vegetables in their concentrated juices.']::text[]),
('tripas-a-moda-do-porto','en','Tripe Porto-style','Porto, Portugal','A stew of tripe, white beans and mixed meats, the dish that earned Porto''s residents their nickname tripeiros after they legendarily gave away all their meat to the ships of Prince Henry the Navigator.',ARRAY['Soak the white beans overnight, cook separately until tender.','Clean the tripe thoroughly, blanch then cook 2 hours in water with carrot and onion.','Fry chorizo and pork belly, add the chicken pieces and brown.','Combine drained tripe, meats, cooked beans and cumin in one pot, simmer 45 minutes.','Serve with white rice, a hearty dish traditionally shared around a large table.']::text[]),
('migas-alentejanas','en','Migas alentejanas','Beja, Alentejo, Portugal','Stale bread crumbled and pressed into a compact mass in pork fat, forming a golden crust with a soft interior, served in thick slices with grilled pork chops, a peasant dish of the Alentejo.',ARRAY['Crumble the stale bread very finely, sprinkle with warm salted water to slightly moisten.','Melt the pork fat in a large pan with crushed garlic and bay leaf.','Add the crumbled bread, press into a compact mass, cook over low heat, pressing regularly until a golden crust forms.','Separately grill the pork chops marinated in paprika.','Cut the migas into thick slices, serve with the grilled chops.']::text[]),
('posta-mirandesa','en','Posta mirandesa','Miranda do Douro, Trás-os-Montes, Portugal','A thick slice of Mirandesa veal, a local breed raised free-range on the border plateaus, simply grilled in olive oil and garlic to let the quality of the meat speak for itself.',ARRAY['Take the meat out of the fridge 1 hour before cooking so it reaches room temperature.','Heat a cast-iron plate over high heat with a little olive oil.','Sear the thick veal slices 3 minutes per side for a medium-rare doneness.','Drizzle with hot garlic and bay-infused olive oil at the end of cooking.','Serve with whole sautéed potatoes and coarse salt.']::text[]),
('rojoes-a-moda-do-minho','en','Rojões Minho-style','Barcelos, Minho, Portugal','Pork cubes marinated overnight in white wine, garlic and paprika, fried in their own fat until crisp outside and tender inside, served with pork tripe and chestnuts.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into cubes, marinate overnight with white wine, crushed garlic, paprika and bay leaf.','Drain the cubes reserving the marinade, brown them in their own fat over medium heat.','Add a little marinade during cooking to deglaze and flavour, let reduce.','Cook the chestnuts separately in water until tender.','Serve the browned pork with the chestnuts and fresh orange wedges to cut through the fat.']::text[]),
('chanfana-da-beira','en','Chanfana da Beira','Miranda do Corvo, Beira, Portugal','A young goat simmered for hours in thick, tannic red wine until the meat falls off the bone by itself, traditionally cooked in a black earthenware pot placed in a wood-fired bread oven.',ARRAY['Cut the young goat into large pieces, rub with crushed garlic and paprika.','Arrange in an earthenware pot with onion, bay leaf and pork fat.','Cover entirely with tannic red wine, close the pot.','Bake at a low 190°C for 4 hours in a wood-fired oven if possible.','Check the meat falls off the bone easily and the sauce has reduced, serve with corn bread.']::text[]),
('bifana','en','Bifana','Vendas Novas, Alentejo, Portugal','A thin pork slice marinated in white wine, garlic and chilli, simmered in its juices until extremely tender, tucked into a small roll fresh from the oven, a popular sandwich devoured in two bites.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into thin slices, marinate at least 4 hours in white wine, garlic, bay leaf, paprika and chilli.','Cook the marinated slices over medium heat in a wide pan with their marinade.','Simmer uncovered until the sauce reduces and coats the meat, about 15 minutes.','Warm the small rolls in the oven a few minutes.','Fill each roll with pork slices coated in sauce and a dab of mustard.']::text[]),
('ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato','en','Amêijoas à bulhão pato','Lisbon, Portugal','Clams opened in minutes in a broth of garlic, fresh coriander and white wine, a recipe named after a 19th-century poet known for his gourmandise, eaten straight with the fingers and bread for mopping up.',ARRAY['Purge the clams in salted water at least 1 hour.','Heat the olive oil with sliced garlic and chilli until fragrant without colouring.','Add the drained clams and white wine, cover.','Cook over high heat 5 minutes, shaking the pan, until all the clams have opened.','Scatter generously with chopped fresh coriander, drizzle with a squeeze of lemon and serve with bread.']::text[]),
('arroz-de-pato','en','Arroz de pato','Aveiro, Portugal','Rice cooked in shredded duck broth, layered with chouriço and pork rind in an earthenware dish then baked until the top caramelises into a crackling crust, a quintessential Sunday dish.',ARRAY['Poach the whole duck with onion, carrot and bay leaf 1 hour until tender, shred the meat and reserve the broth.','Cook the rice in the strained duck broth to two-thirds done.','Layer half the rice in an earthenware dish, cover with shredded duck and sliced chouriço.','Cover with the rest of the rice, arrange the rind on top, moisten with a little broth.','Bake 20 minutes at 220°C until the top is golden and crackling, serve with orange zest.']::text[]),
('feijoada-a-transmontana','en','Feijoada Trás-os-Montes style','Chaves, Trás-os-Montes, Portugal','A hearty stew of red beans and multiple smoked cured meats from Trás-os-Montes — chouriço, alheira, pig''s ear and trotter — simmered for hours so the smoky flavours fully permeate the beans.',ARRAY['Soak the red beans overnight, drain.','Desalt the pig''s ear and tail by blanching in boiling water.','Combine beans, salted meats, chorizo, onion, carrot and bay leaf in a large pot, cover with water.','Simmer covered 2 hours over low heat until the beans and meats are tender.','Adjust seasoning, let rest 10 minutes before serving piping hot.']::text[]),
('polvo-a-lagareiro','en','Octopus à lagareiro','Sesimbra, Portugal','A whole octopus boiled until tender then roasted, tentacles splayed like a star, drowned in a generous flood of virgin olive oil after cooking, surrounded by hand-crushed roasted potatoes.',ARRAY['Freeze then thaw the octopus to tenderise it, boil 40 minutes with bay leaf until tender.','Roast whole potatoes in their skins until tender.','Place the whole boiled octopus in an oven dish, surround with the potatoes lightly crushed by hand.','Drizzle generously with olive oil and scatter crushed garlic, bake 15 minutes at 220°C.','Scatter with fresh parsley and serve immediately, well oiled.']::text[]),
('sopa-da-pedra','en','Stone soup','Almeirim, Ribatejo, Portugal','A hearty soup of red beans, cured meats and vegetables, born from a legend of a beggar convincing a village to cook a soup from a plain stone by adding, one ingredient at a time, everything needed for a real feast.',ARRAY['Soak the red beans overnight, cook halfway.','Add chorizo and desalted pig''s ear, simmer 40 minutes until tender.','Add diced potato and shredded cabbage, cook another 20 minutes.','Adjust seasoning with crushed garlic, let reduce to a thick consistency.','Traditionally place a smooth clean stone at the bottom of the bowl as a nod to the legend, scatter with coriander and serve scalding.']::text[]),
('queijo-serra-broa','en','Serra cheese and broa','Seia, Serra da Estrela, Portugal','A creamy sheep''s milk cheese with a runny heart, curdled with thistle flower according to an age-old method, served by simply slicing off the top to dip a spoon in, paired with dense corn bread called broa.',ARRAY['Knead cornmeal, wheat flour, yeast, salt and warm water into a dense, sticky dough.','Let the dough rise 1 hour under a cloth in a warm spot.','Shape into a large round loaf, bake 40 minutes at 200°C until the crust is thick and dark.','Let the sheep''s cheese rest at room temperature 30 minutes so its texture softens.','Cut off the top of the cheese like a lid, serve with thick slices of still-warm broa.']::text[]),
('toucinho-do-ceu','en','Toucinho do céu','Guimarães, Portugal','A dense convent cake of ground almonds and egg yolks, whose name means "bacon from heaven" despite containing no trace of it, a legacy of nuns who used the whites to starch their headdresses.',ARRAY['Make a thick syrup with sugar and water, boil 8 minutes until it threads.','Finely blend the almonds, stir into the hot syrup off the heat, let cool slightly.','Beat the egg yolks, gradually whisk into the warm almond paste.','Add lemon zest, pour into a buttered mould.','Bake in a water bath 35 minutes at 170°C until barely set in the centre, let cool before unmoulding.']::text[]),
('favas-a-portuguesa','en','Portuguese-style broad beans','Santarém, Ribatejo, Portugal','Fresh spring broad beans simmered with chouriço, coriander and a poached egg dropped in at the last moment, a rustic Ribatejo dish celebrating the arrival of early spring produce after winter.',ARRAY['Shell the fresh broad beans, sweat sliced onion and garlic in olive oil.','Add the sliced chorizo, fry 3 minutes.','Add the beans, moisten with a little water, simmer covered 20 minutes until tender.','Poach the eggs separately in simmering vinegared water.','Serve the beans scattered with fresh coriander, a poached egg placed on each portion.']::text[]),
('confit-de-canard','en','Duck confit','Sarlat-la-Canéda, Périgord, France','Duck legs salted then slowly cooked submerged in their own fat until extremely tender, an ancestral preservation technique from the Périgord that became a classic with golden, crackling skin.',ARRAY['Rub the duck legs generously with coarse salt, crushed garlic and thyme, let rest 24 hours chilled.','Rinse and thoroughly dry the legs to remove excess salt.','Fully submerge them in melted duck fat with the bay leaf.','Cook at a very low temperature for 3 hours, the fat should never simmer hard.','Crisp the skin in a pan before serving with potatoes sautéed in duck fat.']::text[]),
('baeckeoffe','en','Baeckeoffe','Colmar, Alsace, France','Three meats marinated in Riesling with potato and onion, layered in a terrine sealed with a rope of dough, which Alsatian farmwives carried to the baker''s oven on Mondays, the day of washing.',ARRAY['Cut the three meats into large chunks, marinate overnight in Riesling with onion, bay leaf and juniper.','Peel and thinly slice the potatoes.','Layer in a terrine: potatoes, marinated meat, potatoes, ending with potatoes.','Pour the marinade over the top, seal the lid with a rope of flour-and-water dough.','Bake 3 hours at 160°C without opening, break the dough seal at the table to release the aromas.']::text[]),
('biscuits-roses-de-reims','en','Reims pink biscuits','Reims, Champagne, France','A dry biscuit tinted a delicate pink, born in the 17th century from Champagne bakers seeking to use their ovens'' residual heat, today traditionally dipped into a glass of champagne.',ARRAY['Separate the whites from the yolks, whisk the whites to stiff peaks with half the sugar.','Beat the yolks with the remaining sugar and vanilla until pale, add a few drops of pink colouring.','Gently fold in the flour then the whisked whites, without deflating the batter.','Pipe finger shapes onto a tray, dust generously with icing sugar.','Bake 12 minutes at 180°C, let dry outside the oven before serving with a glass of champagne.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('tarte-tatin','en','Tarte Tatin','Lamotte-Beuvron, Sologne, France','A caramelised apple tart baked upside down then flipped at the last moment, born from a famous mishap by the Tatin sisters who forgot the pastry and hastily added it over the already-cooking fruit.',ARRAY['Knead a shortcrust pastry with flour, butter, egg and a little water, let rest 30 minutes chilled.','Melt the sugar dry in an oven-safe pan until a golden caramel.','Add the butter to the caramel, arrange the peeled apples cut into tight wedges on top.','Cook the apples in the caramel over low heat 15 minutes, cover with the rolled pastry, tucking in the edges.','Bake 30 minutes at 200°C, let cool 10 minutes then flip onto a plate, serve with fresh cream.']::text[]),
('poulet-au-vin-jaune','en','Chicken with vin jaune and morels','Arbois, Jura, France','A farmhouse chicken simmered in Jura''s vin jaune, an oxidative wine with walnut notes aged six years under a yeast veil, paired with rehydrated dried morels and a velvety cream — the most prestigious pairing of the Jura.',ARRAY['Soak the dried morels in a little warm water 30 minutes, strain and reserve the liquid.','Brown the chicken pieces in butter, set aside.','Sweat the sliced shallot, deglaze with vin jaune, let reduce by half.','Return the chicken, add the morels and their strained liquid, simmer covered 35 minutes.','Stir in the cream at the end of cooking, let thicken slightly and serve piping hot.']::text[]),
('salade-lyonnaise','en','Lyonnaise salad','Lyon, France','A bitter frisée salad topped with crispy lardons and a poached egg whose runny yolk mingles with a warm vinegar dressing, a hearty Lyonnaise bouchon dish despite its light appearance.',ARRAY['Wash and thoroughly dry the frisée, place it in a large salad bowl.','Fry the lardons dry until crisp, reserve the rendered fat.','Rub toasted bread croutons with garlic.','Deglaze the hot bacon fat with vinegar and mustard for the warm dressing, pour over the frisée.','Poach the eggs, place one on each plate with lardons and croutons, serve immediately.']::text[]),
('soupe-au-pistou','en','Soupe au pistou','Toulon, Provence, France','A soup of summer vegetables and white and red beans, crowned just before serving with a spoonful of pistou — basil, garlic and parmesan pounded with olive oil — Provençal cousin of Genoese pesto.',ARRAY['Cook the white and red beans separately until tender.','Bring a large volume of water to the boil, add diced potato and zucchini, cook 15 minutes.','Add crushed tomato, cut green beans, cooked beans and pasta, cook another 15 minutes.','Pound basil, garlic and parmesan in a mortar, gradually work in the olive oil until a smooth paste forms.','Serve the soup scalding, a spoonful of pistou placed in the centre of each bowl just before eating.']::text[]),
('potjevleesch','en','Potjevleesch','Dunkirk, Flanders, France','A cold terrine of four meats — rabbit, chicken, pork and veal — simmered separately then set together in a white vinegar jelly, a Flemish speciality served sliced with hot fries.',ARRAY['Cut the four meats into large chunks, arrange in a large pot with onion and bay leaf.','Cover with water and white vinegar, simmer covered 2h30 over low heat until the meats are very tender.','Drain the meats reserving the broth, arrange in a terrine.','Reduce the broth, dissolve the gelatine in it, pour over the meats in the terrine.','Let set in the fridge at least 12 hours, unmould and serve sliced cold with fries.']::text[]),
('brandade-de-morue','en','Brandade de morue','Nîmes, Occitanie, France','Desalted flaked salt cod whipped with olive oil like a mayonnaise, until forming a white, velvety mousse, a legacy of exchanges between northern fishermen and Mediterranean olive-oil producers.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod 24 hours, changing the water several times.','Poach the cod in simmering water 10 minutes, drain and flake, removing skin and bones.','Mash the flaked cod with garlic, whip in olive oil gradually as you would a mayonnaise.','Add warm milk gradually to soften the texture, season with nutmeg.','Serve warm with toasted bread croutons, as a starter or light dish.']::text[]),
('kig-ha-farz','en','Kig ha farz','Brest, Brittany, France','A Breton pot-au-feu where salted beef and vegetables simmer, alongside a buckwheat far pudding cooked wrapped in a cloth bag which, once unmoulded and crumbled, is drizzled with browned butter and gwell, local fermented milk.',ARRAY['Cook the salted beef and bacon in a large volume of water 2 hours until tender.','Add cabbage in wedges and carrot, simmer another 40 minutes.','Prepare the far by mixing buckwheat flour, eggs, milk and raisins into a liquid batter.','Pour the batter into a clean cloth bag loosely tied, plunge it into the broth to cook 1 hour.','Unmould the far, crumble it with a fork, drizzle with melted butter and serve with the meat and vegetables.']::text[]),
('axoa-espelette','en','Axoa from Espelette','Espelette, Basque Country, France','Veal coarsely chopped by knife, simmered with green peppers and the famous Espelette pepper that hangs in garlands along the village façades throughout autumn, a deeply coloured and fragrant Basque dish.',ARRAY['Chop the veal coarsely by knife into small irregular pieces.','Sweat the onion and sliced green peppers until tender.','Add the veal, brown it over high heat a few minutes.','Add crushed tomato, garlic, Espelette pepper and white wine, simmer covered 30 minutes.','Adjust the chilli seasoning to taste, serve with fried Espelette peppers on the side.']::text[]),
('fiadone','en','Fiadone','Corte, Corsica, France','A soft cake made with brocciu, a fresh Corsican sheep''s or goat''s milk cheese, scented with lemon zest, with a texture between flan and cheesecake, served warm or cold at the end of an island meal.',ARRAY['Mash the brocciu with a fork until smooth.','Beat the whole eggs with the sugar until pale and doubled in volume.','Fold the mashed brocciu, lemon zest and brandy into the mixture.','Pour into a buttered mould, bake 35 minutes at 180°C until golden and barely set in the centre.','Let cool before unmoulding, serve warm or chilled depending on the season.']::text[]),
('potee-auvergnate','en','Auvergne potée','Clermont-Ferrand, Auvergne, France','A country pot-au-feu combining salt pork, sausage and green cabbage with root vegetables from the Massif Central, simmered for hours in a large cast-iron pot once hung on the hearth''s chain.',ARRAY['Desalt the salt pork several hours, changing the water.','Cook it in a large volume of water 1 hour with bay leaf.','Add carrot, turnip and leek, simmer another 45 minutes.','Add cabbage in wedges, potato and sausage, cook 30 more minutes until everything is tender.','Serve the meat and vegetables drained in large soup plates, with a little broth and mustard on the side.']::text[]),
('diots-au-vin-blanc','en','Diots in white wine','Chambéry, Savoie, France','Small Savoyard pork sausages simmered in Apremont white wine with onion and bay leaf, until the skin soaks up the wine, traditionally served with creamy polenta or potatoes.',ARRAY['Lightly prick the sausages with a fork to prevent them bursting.','Brown them in a pot with the sliced onion.','Moisten with white wine, add the bay leaf, simmer covered 30 minutes over low heat.','Meanwhile, prepare a creamy polenta by stirring cornmeal into boiling salted water, stir in butter and grated Comté.','Serve the diots coated in their cooking juices over a bed of melting polenta.']::text[]),
('entrecote-bordelaise','en','Bordelaise-style entrecôte','Bordeaux, France','An entrecôte traditionally grilled over vine cuttings for a subtle smoky aroma, coated in a sauce reduced with Bordeaux red wine, shallot and beef marrow, rich and full-bodied.',ARRAY['Grill the entrecôte over hot embers, ideally vine cuttings, to the desired doneness.','Let the meat rest under foil a few minutes.','Sweat the finely chopped shallot in a little butter.','Deglaze with red wine, reduce by two thirds, add the beef broth and diced marrow, let melt.','Finish the sauce off the heat with cold butter, coat the sliced entrecôte and serve immediately.']::text[]),
('tripes-mode-de-caen','en','Tripe Caen-style','Caen, Normandy, France','Beef tripe simmered for eight hours in cider and calvados with veal foot for gelatine, in an earthenware pot sealed with dough, a Norman recipe once praised all the way to Louis XIV''s table.',ARRAY['Clean the tripe thoroughly, cut into even squares.','Arrange tripe and cut veal foot in a large earthenware pot with carrot, onion, leek and bay leaf.','Moisten with cider and calvados, seal hermetically with a rope of flour-and-water dough.','Bake at a very low 90°C for 8 hours without opening.','Break the dough seal at the table, skim the surface slightly and serve scalding in soup plates.']::text[]),
('pot-au-feu','en','Pot-au-feu','Paris, France','A clear beef broth simmered for hours with marrow bone, carrot, leek and turnip, served in two successive courses — first the broth with vermicelli, then the meat and vegetables — a pillar of the French table for centuries.',ARRAY['Plunge the beef and marrow bone into a large pot of cold water, slowly bring to the boil, skimming.','Stud the onion with cloves, add with carrot, leek and turnip, simmer covered 3 hours.','Check the meat''s tenderness with a fork, it should pull apart easily.','Strain part of the broth, cook the vermicelli in it for the first course.','Serve the meat and vegetables as a second course with coarse salt, pickles and mustard.']::text[]),
('rougail-saucisse','en','Rougail saucisse','Saint-Denis, Réunion, France','Smoked sausages simmered in a tomato sauce with ginger, garlic and chilli, the most-eaten everyday dish of Réunion, served with white rice and grains — lentils or red beans.',ARRAY['Blanch the smoked sausages 5 minutes in boiling water to remove excess fat, drain.','Fry the sliced onion, garlic and ginger until fragrant.','Add crushed tomato, thyme and chilli, simmer 10 minutes into a thick sauce.','Add the blanched sausages cut into chunks, simmer covered 25 minutes.','Cook rice and lentils separately, serve the rougail with both on the side.']::text[]),
('colombo-de-poulet','en','Colombo de poulet','Fort-de-France, Martinique, France','A chicken curry with colombo spice blend, an Indian mix brought by 19th-century Tamil indentured workers, simmered with potato, eggplant and a splash of lime juice, a Creole dish that became emblematic of Martinique.',ARRAY['Rub the chicken pieces with colombo spice, marinate 1 hour.','Fry the sliced onion and garlic, add the marinated chicken, brown.','Add potato and eggplant in chunks, moisten with water and coconut milk.','Add the whole scotch bonnet without piercing it to flavour without too much heat, simmer 30 minutes.','Remove the chilli, drizzle with a splash of lime juice and serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('accras-de-morue','en','Accras de morue','Pointe-à-Pitre, Guadeloupe, France','Small fritters of desalted, flaked salt cod in a batter spiked with chilli and scallion, fried until an irregular golden crust forms, an unmissable appetiser at every Caribbean family gathering.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod 24 hours, changing the water, flake finely without cooking.','Prepare a fritter batter with flour, yeast, egg and warm water, let rise 30 minutes.','Fold in the flaked cod, chopped scallion, chilli and garlic.','Shape small balls with a spoon, slide directly into hot oil at 180°C.','Fry until golden and puffed, drain on paper towel and serve hot as an appetiser.']::text[]),
('beef-stroganoff','en','Beef Stroganoff','St. Petersburg, Russia','Thin strips of beef quickly seared and coated in a sauce of smetana cream and mustard, a dish created in the 19th century for Count Stroganov''s table, simple enough to prepare that it became a worldwide classic.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into thin, even strips.','Fry sliced onion and mushrooms in butter until golden, set aside.','Sear the beef strips over very high heat just a few seconds per side.','Return the onion and mushrooms, add smetana cream, mustard and a little broth, mix off the strong heat.','Warm through without boiling to avoid curdling the cream, serve immediately with sautéed potatoes.']::text[]),
('olivier-salad','en','Olivier salad','Moscow, Russia','A salad of potato, carrot, peas, pickles and diced meat bound with mayonnaise, created by a Belgian chef in Moscow whose exact recipe remained secret, now the essential dish of Russian New Year.',ARRAY['Cook potato, carrot and eggs separately in water until tender, let cool.','Cut potato, carrot, hard-boiled eggs, pickles and meat into small, even dice.','Combine all the dice in a large bowl with the peas.','Add the mayonnaise and a dab of mustard, mix gently to coat well without crushing.','Chill at least 2 hours before serving, ideally overnight so the flavours meld.']::text[]),
('pelmeni-sibirskie','en','Siberian pelmeni','Irkutsk, Siberia, Russia','Small dumplings of mixed beef and pork, hand-pinched then frozen in large batches outdoors in the Siberian cold, a winter reserve poached as needed through the long frozen months.',ARRAY['Knead flour, egg, water and salt into a firm dough, rest 30 minutes under a cloth.','Mix minced beef and pork with grated onion, salt and pepper into a smooth filling.','Roll the dough thin, cut circles, fill and fold, pinching the edges firmly into a small ear shape.','Freeze the uncooked pelmeni on a floured tray if preparing a reserve.','Poach in salted boiling water 8 minutes until they float, serve with smetana cream and dill.']::text[]),
('syrniki','en','Syrniki','Suzdal, Golden Ring, Russia','Small golden patties of tvorog, Russia''s granular fresh cheese similar to cottage cheese, mixed with egg and a little flour, served warm with smetana cream and berry jam for breakfast.',ARRAY['Mash the tvorog with a fork to remove large lumps.','Stir in egg, sugar, vanilla and most of the flour into a supple but firm batter.','Shape small round patties, lightly dust in the remaining flour.','Brown them in butter over medium heat, 3 minutes per side until a golden crust forms.','Serve warm with smetana cream and berry jam on the side.']::text[]),
('blini-ikra','en','Blini with caviar','Veliky Novgorod, Russia','Small thick yeast-leavened buckwheat pancakes, soft and slightly tangy, topped with smetana cream and salmon or sturgeon roe, a festive dish that traditionally accompanied Maslenitsa celebrations.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in a little warm milk, let foam 10 minutes.','Mix buckwheat and wheat flours, stir in the yeast mixture, remaining warm milk and beaten eggs into a smooth batter.','Let the batter rise 45 minutes in a warm spot until doubled.','Cook small thick pancakes in a buttered pan, 2 minutes per side until golden.','Serve warm topped with smetana cream and a spoonful of salmon roe on each blini.']::text[]),
('okroshka','en','Okroshka','Yaroslavl, Golden Ring, Russia','A cold summer soup of raw vegetables, hard-boiled egg and diced meat, drowned in slightly sparkling fermented kvass or kefir, served icy to cool down during the rare hot days of the short Russian summer.',ARRAY['Cut cucumber, hard-boiled eggs, meat and radish into small, very even dice.','Finely chop the scallion and dill.','Combine all the cut ingredients in a large bowl, mix.','Pour the cold kvass over the top until generously covered, add a spoonful of diluted mustard.','Add a spoonful of smetana cream to each individual bowl, serve immediately, well chilled.']::text[]),
('shchi','en','Shchi','Vladimir, Golden Ring, Russia','A soup of fresh or fermented cabbage simmered with pork and root vegetables, the oldest everyday dish of the Russian table, of which an old saying claims that it and kasha, porridge, are our food.',ARRAY['Cook the pork pieces in a large volume of water 1 hour until tender, skim regularly.','Add sliced onion and carrot, simmer 15 minutes.','Add fermented cabbage and diced potato, cook another 25 minutes.','Season with bay leaf, adjust salt according to the cabbage''s acidity.','Serve scalding with a spoonful of smetana cream and fresh chopped dill on each bowl.']::text[]),
('ukha','en','Ukha','Astrakhan, Volga Delta, Russia','A clear, limpid fish soup, never thickened or clouded, traditionally made with several different fish including Volga delta sturgeon, finished with a touch of vodka just before serving.',ARRAY['Poach the whole fish in water with whole onion and bay leaf 20 minutes, skim carefully to keep the broth clear.','Remove the fish, flake the flesh removing skin and bones, strain the broth.','Reheat the strained broth, add potato and carrot in chunks, cook 15 minutes.','Return the flaked fish to the broth, warm through without boiling.','Add a splash of vodka just before serving, scatter with fresh dill.']::text[]),
('kulebyaka','en','Kulebyaka','Pskov, Russia','A long, golden pastry pie enclosing several distinct layers of fish, rice, hard-boiled egg and mushrooms, each separated by a thin pancake to keep moisture from soaking the puff pastry.',ARRAY['Cook the rice until tender, hard-boil the eggs, fry sliced mushrooms and onion.','Prepare thin pancakes with flour, egg, milk and a little melted butter.','Roll out the puff pastry into a large rectangle, place a pancake then a layer of rice in the centre.','Layer salmon, pancake, mushrooms, sliced hard-boiled egg and dill, separating each layer with a pancake.','Close the pastry into a long golden pie, bake 35 minutes at 200°C until well browned, let rest before slicing.']::text[]),
('solyanka','en','Solyanka','Tula, Russia','A spicy, tangy soup mixing several types of smoked meats and cured sausage, olives, capers and pickled cucumbers, with contrasting flavours intentionally meant as a reviving remedy after an overly wet evening.',ARRAY['Fry sliced onion in a little bacon fat.','Add the various meats cut into strips and the tomato paste, fry 5 minutes.','Moisten with water or broth, add sliced pickles, olives and capers, simmer 25 minutes.','Adjust the tang with a little pickle brine if needed.','Serve scalding with a lemon slice and a spoonful of smetana cream on each bowl.']::text[]),
('chak-chak','en','Chak-chak','Kazan, Tatarstan, Russia','Small pieces of fried dough browned then bound together in warm honey, shaped into a decorative pyramid, a Tatar festive cake invariably presented at weddings and births.',ARRAY['Knead flour, eggs, sugar and a pinch of salt into a firm dough, let rest 20 minutes.','Roll the dough thin, cut into thin strips then small irregular pea-sized pieces.','Fry the pieces in small batches in hot oil at 180°C until golden and puffed.','Warm the honey until fluid without letting it caramelise.','Mix the drained fried pieces into the warm honey, immediately shape into a pyramid on a platter, let harden before serving.']::text[]),
('azu-po-tatarski','en','Azu Tatar-style','Naberezhnye Chelny, Tatarstan, Russia','Strips of beef simmered with pickled cucumbers and potato in a spiced tomato sauce, a Tatar speciality where the tang of the pickles cuts through the richness of the long-cooked meat.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into thick strips, brown vigorously in a pot.','Add sliced onion, fry until translucent.','Add tomato paste and broth, simmer covered 40 minutes until tender.','Add potato in wedges and pickles in strips, cook another 20 minutes.','Add the crushed garlic right at the end of cooking, adjust seasoning and serve scalding.']::text[]),
('pirozhki','en','Pirozhki','Rostov-on-Don, Russia','Small yeasted dough turnovers filled with meat, cabbage or potato, fried or baked, once hawked at train stations and platforms, the quintessential travel snack across all of Russia.',ARRAY['Knead flour, yeast, warm milk, egg and salt into a supple dough, let rise 1 hour.','Fry minced beef or shredded cabbage with onion for the filling of your choice, let cool slightly.','Divide the dough into balls, flatten each into a disc, fill with filling.','Close into a turnover, pinching the edges well to seal.','Fry in hot oil until golden or bake 20 minutes at 190°C, serve hot.']::text[]),
('kholodets','en','Kholodets','Nizhny Novgorod, Russia','A cold pork meat jelly simmered so long that the collagen from trotters and shanks naturally turns into aspic, with no added gelatine, served sliced with horseradish or sharp mustard.',ARRAY['Soak the pig''s trotters and shanks a few hours, brush thoroughly.','Cook in a large volume of water at a very gentle simmer 6 hours with onion, carrot and bay leaf, never boiling hard.','Skim regularly, salt at the end of cooking.','Shred the cooked meat, arrange with crushed garlic at the bottom of moulds or a dish.','Strain the collagen-rich broth, pour over the meat, let set in the fridge at least 8 hours, unmould and serve with horseradish.']::text[]),
('shashlyk-kubanskiy','en','Kuban shashlyk','Krasnodar, Kuban, Russia','Pork cubes marinated overnight in onion, vinegar and spices, grilled on wide flat skewers over fruitwood embers, a festive tradition of Russia''s fertile south near the Caucasus.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into large even cubes.','Mix with onion sliced into thick rounds, vinegar, bay leaf, pepper and crushed coriander.','Let marinate overnight chilled, stirring occasionally.','Thread the cubes onto wide flat skewers, alternating with rounds of marinated onion.','Grill over hot embers 12 to 15 minutes, turning regularly, serve with the grilled onions.']::text[]),
('pozy-buryatskie','en','Buryat pozy','Ulan-Ude, Buryatia, Russia','Large Mongol-Buryat steamed dumplings closed into a pleated crown with exactly twenty-five folds, filled with fatty meat and onion, from which the scalding juice is first sipped through a small hole before biting in.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water and salt into a firm dough, let rest 30 minutes.','Mix coarsely minced beef and pork with very finely chopped onion and a little water to make the filling juicy.','Roll out the dough, cut fairly large circles, fill generously with filling.','Close into a pleated crown, pinching the edges all around, leaving a small hole at the top.','Steam 18 minutes, let cool 2 minutes, sip the juice through the hole before eating the rest.']::text[]),
('stroganina','en','Stroganina','Yakutsk, Yakutia, Russia','A freshwater fish frozen solid by Yakutia''s extreme cold, shaved into paper-thin curls with a knife right at the table, dipped in a mix of salt and pepper, eaten at once before it thaws.',ARRAY['Use a freshwater fish frozen entirely solid, never previously thawed.','Hold the frozen fish firmly wrapped in a cloth for grip.','Shave very thin curls using a well-sharpened knife, directly over a cold plate.','Mix fine salt and freshly ground black pepper in a small dish.','Dip each curl of frozen fish into the salt and pepper just before eating, while still hard and cold.']::text[]),
('medovik','en','Medovik','Smolensk, Russia','A layer cake of thin honey-dough sheets stacked with velvety smetana cream, left to rest overnight so the layers soften as they absorb the cream, born from a pastry chef trying to please a tsarina who hated honey.',ARRAY['Melt honey, butter and sugar in a bain-marie, add the baking soda off the heat until it foams.','Stir in the beaten eggs then gradually the flour into a supple, still-warm dough.','Divide into 8 balls, roll each very thin, bake each disc 4 minutes at 200°C until golden.','Whip the smetana cream with icing sugar until velvety.','Stack the pastry discs, spreading cream between each, chill overnight before serving sliced.']::text[]),
('pashka','en','Pashka','Kostroma, Golden Ring, Russia','A cold dessert of tvorog, butter and candied fruit pressed into a pyramidal wooden mould marked with the letters XB, an Orthodox Easter symbol blessed at church before being served in thin wedges on Easter morning.',ARRAY['Pass the tvorog through a fine sieve for a perfectly smooth texture.','Beat the softened butter with sugar until creamy, add the egg yolks one at a time.','Mix with the sieved tvorog, smetana cream and vanilla.','Fold in candied fruit and crushed almonds.','Line a pyramidal mould with clean cloth, fill and press firmly, refrigerate under weight 24 hours before unmoulding.']::text[]),
('rassolnik','en','Rassolnik','Perm, Urals, Russia','A tangy soup with pickled cucumbers and pearl barley, whose pickle-brine acidity added at the end of cooking cuts through the tenderness of the pork or beef kidneys traditionally used.',ARRAY['Soak the kidneys in milk 1 hour to remove their strong smell, rinse and dice.','Cook the pearl barley separately until tender.','Fry the kidneys, onion and carrot, moisten with water, simmer 25 minutes.','Add potato, diced pickles and cooked barley, cook another 15 minutes.','Add pickle brine at the end of cooking to taste, scatter with dill and serve scalding.']::text[]),
('vareniki-vishnya','en','Cherry vareniki','Voronezh, Russia','Large sweet dumplings filled with pitted cherries macerated in sugar, poached then served warm drowned in smetana cream, a summer dessert that tints the dough a light pink during cooking.',ARRAY['Knead flour, egg, water and a pinch of salt into a supple dough, let rest 30 minutes.','Pit the cherries, mix with sugar, let macerate 20 minutes to release their juice.','Roll the dough thin, cut fairly wide circles.','Fill each circle with a few drained cherries, close by firmly pinching the edges into a half-moon.','Poach in boiling water 6 minutes until they float, drain and serve with melted butter and smetana cream.']::text[]),
('gurievskaya-kasha','en','Gurievskaya kasha','Saratov, Volga region, Russia','A sweet semolina porridge built up in successive layers with the creamy skin that forms on the surface of oven-warmed milk, scattered with dried fruit and nuts, an imperial 19th-century dessert named after a gourmet finance minister.',ARRAY['Pour the milk into a wide oven dish, bake at low temperature until a creamy skin forms on the surface, gently lift it off and repeat several times.','Cook the semolina in the remaining sweetened milk until thickened, flavour with vanilla.','Arrange a layer of semolina in a dish, cover with reserved milk skins and crushed dried fruit.','Repeat the layers alternating semolina, milk skins and dried fruit until used up.','Drizzle with warm honey, pass briefly under the grill to lightly caramelise the top, serve warm.']::text[]),
('lobster-roll','en','Lobster roll','Portland, Maine, USA','Freshly shelled lobster meat, barely bound with mayonnaise or simply melted butter depending on the school of thought, piled into a hot-dog bun toasted on the sides, an emblematic summer sandwich of Maine.',ARRAY['Cook the lobster in salted boiling water 10 minutes, let cool then shell.','Cut the meat into large even chunks.','Gently mix with mayonnaise, finely chopped celery and a squeeze of lemon.','Toast the buttered buns on both cut sides until golden.','Generously fill each bun with lobster meat, scatter with chives and serve immediately.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('buffalo-wings','en','Buffalo wings','Buffalo, New York State, USA','Chicken wings fried plain then drowned in a scorching butter and hot sauce, born in 1964 at a Buffalo bar to use up an unexpected delivery, served with celery sticks and blue cheese dip to temper the heat.',ARRAY['Thoroughly dry the chicken wings, fry in hot oil 12 minutes until well browned and crisp.','Melt the butter, mix it into the hot sauce while still warm to emulsify.','Drain the fried wings, plunge them into the scorching sauce, tossing to coat well.','Prepare the blue cheese dip by mixing crumbled blue cheese, sour cream and a little vinegar.','Serve the wings immediately with celery sticks and the blue cheese dip on the side.']::text[]),
('deep-dish-pizza','en','Deep-dish pizza','Chicago, Illinois, USA','A thick pizza baked in a high-sided pan like a pie, layered upside down with cheese under the tomato sauce to keep it from burning during the 35 minutes of baking needed, a speciality born in Chicago in the 1940s.',ARRAY['Knead flour, cornmeal, yeast, olive oil and warm water into a supple dough, let rise 1 hour.','Roll the thick dough into a well-oiled high-sided pan, bringing it up the sides.','Arrange sliced mozzarella directly on the dough.','Add cooked crumbled sausage, then the thick tomato sauce seasoned with oregano on top.','Bake 30 to 35 minutes at 220°C until golden crust, scatter with parmesan and let rest 10 minutes before cutting.']::text[]),
('chicago-hot-dog','en','Chicago hot dog','Chicago, Illinois, USA','A poached all-beef sausage nestled in a poppy-seed bun, topped with a precise and untouchable list of seven condiments — mustard, onion, bright green relish, tomato, pickle, sport pepper and celery salt — never ketchup.',ARRAY['Poach the beef sausages in simmering water 6 minutes without boiling.','Steam the poppy-seed buns to soften slightly.','Place each sausage in a bun, drizzle mustard in a zigzag.','Top in order with finely chopped onion, green relish, tomato wedges, a whole pickle spear.','Finish with a pinch of celery salt and a sport pepper, serve immediately, never adding ketchup.']::text[]),
('crab-cakes-maryland','en','Maryland crab cakes','Baltimore, Maryland, USA','Patties of Chesapeake Bay blue crab meat, barely bound with breadcrumbs and mayonnaise to let the crab dominate, pan-fried until golden, seasoned with the Old Bay spice blend emblematic of Maryland.',ARRAY['Carefully check the crab meat to remove any shell fragments.','Gently mix with breadcrumbs, mayonnaise, egg, mustard and Old Bay seasoning, without overworking to keep large lumps.','Shape thick patties by hand, chill 30 minutes so they hold together.','Brown the patties in butter over medium heat, 4 minutes per side.','Scatter with fresh parsley and serve with a lemon wedge and tartar sauce.']::text[]),
('shrimp-and-grits','en','Shrimp and grits','Charleston, South Carolina, USA','A creamy cheese-and-butter corn porridge, topped with shrimp sautéed with bacon and Cajun spices, a Lowcountry fishermen''s dish elevated in a few decades to national gastronomic speciality.',ARRAY['Bring the milk to a boil, whisk in the grits in a stream to avoid lumps.','Cook over low heat 20 minutes, stirring regularly, until creamy, stir in cheddar cheese and butter.','Fry the diced bacon until crisp, set aside.','Sauté the shrimp seasoned with Cajun spice and garlic in the bacon fat until pink, 4 minutes.','Serve the shrimp and bacon over the creamy grits, scatter with chopped scallion.']::text[]),
('kentucky-hot-brown','en','Kentucky Hot Brown','Louisville, Kentucky, USA','An open turkey sandwich drowned in cheesy mornay sauce, broiled with crispy bacon slices and tomato, invented in the 1920s by a Louisville hotel chef to feed hungry dancers after midnight.',ARRAY['Make a roux with butter and flour, moisten with hot milk, whisking until a smooth thick sauce forms.','Stir in the grated gruyère off the heat until melted, season.','Place a slice of bread in an individual dish, cover with thick turkey slices.','Coat generously with mornay sauce, add tomato slices and grated parmesan on top.','Broil a few minutes until golden and bubbling, garnish with crispy bacon and serve immediately, piping hot.']::text[]),
('chicken-and-waffles','en','Chicken and waffles','Harlem, New York, USA','Extra-crispy fried chicken set directly on a soft waffle, drizzled with maple syrup for a sweet-savoury contrast, a dish born in Harlem''s jazz clubs where people ate between late dinner and breakfast.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken in buttermilk at least 4 hours.','Drain, dredge in flour seasoned with cayenne, fry at 175°C 15 minutes until golden and crisp.','Prepare the waffle batter by mixing flour, eggs, milk and melted butter.','Cook the waffles in a hot waffle iron until golden and crisp.','Place the fried chicken on the warm waffles, drizzle generously with maple syrup and serve immediately.']::text[]),
('new-york-cheesecake','en','New York cheesecake','New York, USA','A dense, smooth cheesecake made with cream cheese, baked in a water bath on a thin crumbled biscuit crust, whose tall, compact texture radically contrasts with lighter European versions.',ARRAY['Blitz the biscuits into fine crumbs, mix with melted butter and press into the base of a springform pan.','Beat the cream cheese until smooth, gradually add the sugar.','Add the eggs one at a time, beating minimally to avoid incorporating too much air, then the cream, vanilla and lemon zest.','Pour over the crust, bake in a water bath 55 minutes at 160°C until barely wobbly in the centre.','Let cool in the turned-off oven with the door ajar, then chill at least 6 hours before unmoulding.']::text[]),
('pastrami-on-rye','en','Pastrami on rye','Manhattan, New York, USA','Beef brisket cured in a spiced brine then long-smoked and steamed, sliced thick and piled high between two slices of rye bread, a legacy of Eastern European Jewish delicatessens.',ARRAY['Rub the brisket with coarse salt, crushed garlic and spices, cure chilled 5 days, turning daily.','Rinse thoroughly, coat with a thick crust of cracked pepper and coriander.','Cold-smoke several hours then hot-smoke until a black crust forms.','Steam 3 hours until extremely tender.','Slice very thin against the grain, pile generously between two slices of rye bread with mustard, serve with pickles.']::text[]),
('cuban-sandwich-miami','en','Miami Cuban sandwich','Miami, Florida, USA','Roast pork, ham, Swiss cheese, pickle and mustard pressed in Cuban bread until the cheese melts and the crust turns crisp, a sandwich forged by the Cuban communities of Ybor City and Miami.',ARRAY['Roast or reheat the shredded or sliced pork.','Split the Cuban bread in two, spread mustard on both sides.','Fill with pork, ham, sliced Swiss cheese and pickle strips.','Butter the outside of the sandwich, press in a hot plancha or panini press.','Cook 5 minutes while pressing until the cheese melts and the bread is crisp and flattened, cut diagonally and serve hot.']::text[]),
('key-lime-pie','en','Key lime pie','Key West, Florida, USA','A tangy pie made with the juice of small key limes from the Florida Keys, mixed with sweetened condensed milk to thicken without complex cooking, on a Graham cracker crust, a typical dessert of the tropical archipelago.',ARRAY['Blitz the Graham crackers into fine crumbs, mix with melted butter and press into a pie pan.','Bake the crust 10 minutes at 180°C, let cool.','Whisk the sweetened condensed milk with the egg yolks and key lime juice until thick and smooth.','Pour onto the cooled crust, bake 15 minutes at 160°C until barely set.','Let cool then chill at least 3 hours, serve with whipped cream.']::text[]),
('sonoran-hot-dog','en','Sonoran hot dog','Tucson, Arizona, USA','A sausage wrapped in bacon and grilled until crisp, nestled in a soft bolillo-style bun and covered with pinto beans, onion, tomato and a creamy jalapeño sauce, a speciality born on the Mexican border.',ARRAY['Wrap each sausage in a slice of bacon in a tight spiral.','Grill over medium heat, turning regularly, until the bacon is crisp, about 10 minutes.','Warm the pinto beans, roughly mashed.','Blend jalapeño and mayonnaise for the creamy spicy sauce.','Fill the warm bun with beans, bacon-wrapped sausage, diced onion and tomato, finish with a drizzle of jalapeño sauce.']::text[]),
('fish-tacos-baja','en','Baja-style fish tacos','San Diego, California, USA','White fish battered in beer then fried crisp, rolled in a warm corn tortilla with finely shredded cabbage and a creamy spicy sauce, a dish born from cross-border fishing between San Diego and Mexican Baja California.',ARRAY['Mix flour and cold beer into a light, foamy batter.','Cut the fish into strips, dip in the beer batter.','Fry in hot oil at 180°C until golden and crisp, drain on paper towel.','Blend mayonnaise and chipotle for the creamy spicy sauce.','Fill the warm tortillas with shredded cabbage, fried fish and chipotle sauce, drizzle with a splash of lime.']::text[]),
('cioppino','en','Cioppino','San Francisco, California, USA','A stew of assorted fish and shellfish in a tomato and white wine broth, born from Fisherman''s Wharf''s Italian immigrant fishermen who pooled the day''s unsold catch into one big shared pot.',ARRAY['Sweat sliced onion and garlic in olive oil until translucent.','Add crushed tomato and white wine, simmer 15 minutes into a fragrant base.','Add the fish broth, bring to a gentle simmer.','Add the fish in chunks first, then crab, shrimp, mussels and clams according to their cooking time.','Simmer 12 minutes until all the shellfish have opened, serve scalding with crusty bread.']::text[]),
('wisconsin-cheese-curds','en','Wisconsin fried cheese curds','Madison, Wisconsin, USA','Small squeaky curds of fresh cheese, battered then fried until forming a golden crust that traps a molten interior, a Wisconsin county fair speciality from America''s leading dairy state.',ARRAY['Take the cheese curds out of the fridge 20 minutes before breading so they reach room temperature.','Prepare three plates: flour, egg beaten with milk, breadcrumbs seasoned with paprika.','Dredge each curd in flour then egg then breadcrumbs, pressing well to adhere.','Fry in hot oil at 190°C in small batches, only 1 to 2 minutes until golden without letting the cheese fully melt.','Drain on paper towel and serve immediately, piping hot, the cheese should still squeak.']::text[]),
('minnesota-hotdish','en','Minnesota hotdish','Minneapolis, Minnesota, USA','A comforting bake of ground beef, green beans and canned mushroom soup, topped with crispy onion crisps, a church-potluck dish typical of the Scandinavian-American culture of the Midwest.',ARRAY['Fry the ground beef with sliced onion until well browned.','Arrange the cooked beef at the bottom of a large oven dish.','Cover with green beans then mushroom soup spread evenly.','Add potato in thin rounds or mashed on top, scatter with grated cheese.','Bake 30 minutes at 190°C, crown with crispy onion crisps for the last 10 minutes.']::text[]),
('detroit-coney-dog','en','Detroit coney dog','Detroit, Michigan, USA','A sausage drowned under bean-free beef chili, mustard and finely chopped raw onion, born from a century-old rivalry between two neighbouring downtown Detroit restaurants run by Greek and Macedonian immigrants.',ARRAY['Fry the beef very finely until almost powder-like, without letting it crisp.','Add tomato paste, cumin and chili powder, moisten lightly and simmer 20 minutes into a thick, bean-free chili.','Poach or grill the sausages until hot.','Place each sausage in a bun, cover generously with hot beef chili.','Finish with a squiggle of mustard and finely chopped raw onion, serve immediately with a fork.']::text[]),
('beignets-nola','en','Beignets, Café du Monde style','New Orleans, Louisiana, USA','Squares of yeasted dough fried until puffing into golden pillows, buried under a thick snow of powdered sugar, a French dessert turned New Orleans institution, traditionally eaten with chicory coffee and milk.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm milk, let foam 10 minutes.','Knead with flour, egg, sugar, melted butter and salt into a supple dough, let rise 1 hour.','Roll the dough 1 cm thick, cut into even squares about 8 cm.','Fry in hot oil at 180°C until well puffed and golden on both sides.','Drain and immediately cover with a thick layer of powdered sugar, serve hot.']::text[]),
('shoofly-pie','en','Shoofly pie','Lancaster, Pennsylvania, USA','An extremely sweet molasses pie topped with a flour-and-butter crumble, a legacy of Pennsylvania''s Amish and Mennonite communities, whose name allegedly comes from the flies that had to be shooed from the pie cooling on the windowsill.',ARRAY['Mix flour, brown sugar and cold butter with fingertips into a coarse crumble.','Dissolve the molasses, baking soda and egg in hot water until smooth.','Line a pie pan with shortcrust pastry, pour half the crumble into the bottom.','Pour the molasses mixture over the top, cover with the remaining crumble.','Bake 35 minutes at 190°C until the centre barely wobbles, let cool before serving.']::text[]),
('peach-cobbler-georgia','en','Georgia peach cobbler','Atlanta, Georgia, USA','Ripe, juicy peaches from the state nicknamed the Peach State, simmered with sugar and cinnamon under a biscuit dough that puffs while baking into an irregular golden lid, a late-summer dessert served warm with a scoop of vanilla ice cream.',ARRAY['Peel and slice the peaches, mix with half the sugar and cinnamon, let macerate 15 minutes.','Arrange the peaches and their juice in a buttered baking dish.','Mix flour, remaining sugar, baking powder, melted butter, milk and vanilla into a thick biscuit batter.','Pour the batter over the peaches without spreading it completely, leaving some fruit exposed.','Bake 40 minutes at 190°C until golden and bubbling, serve warm with a scoop of vanilla ice cream.']::text[]),
('king-crab-alaska','en','Alaska king crab','Anchorage, Alaska, USA','Enormous king crab legs from Alaska''s icy waters, already precooked at sea on factory ships right after capture, simply reheated by steaming and served with melted butter, a catch among the most dangerous in the world.',ARRAY['Take the already precooked king crab legs out of the freezer to thaw slightly.','Heat a large pot of water with a steamer basket.','Steam the legs 8 to 10 minutes until heated through.','Melt the butter with crushed garlic and a little lemon juice.','Crack the shells with a nutcracker, serve with the warm garlic butter and lemon wedges.']::text[]),
('tourtiere','en','Tourtière','Quebec City, Canada','A pie of minced pork spiced with cinnamon and clove, enclosed in a golden shortcrust pastry, an essential Christmas Eve dish in Quebec, passed down through generations since New France.',ARRAY['Fry the minced pork with onion and garlic until well browned.','Add grated potato, cinnamon and clove, moisten with broth.','Simmer covered 25 minutes until thickened and the potato has melted into the filling.','Line a pie pan with shortcrust pastry, fill with cooled filling, cover with a second pastry disc, sealing the edges.','Bake 40 minutes at 190°C until golden, serve hot with homemade fruit ketchup.']::text[]),
('soupe-aux-pois-quebec','en','Quebec pea soup','Trois-Rivières, Quebec, Canada','A thick soup of split yellow peas simmered for hours with salt pork, so hearty it earned French Canadians the mocking nickname "pea-soupers" from their English-speaking neighbours.',ARRAY['Soak the split peas overnight, drain.','Cook the salt pork in a large volume of water 30 minutes, skim.','Add split peas, onion studded with cloves, carrot and bay leaf.','Simmer covered 2h30 over low heat, stirring occasionally, until the peas break down into a thick soup.','Remove the onion and bay leaf, shred the pork into the soup, serve scalding with homestyle bread.']::text[]),
('donair-halifax','en','Halifax donair','Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada','Spiced beef in the style of Greek kebab, thinly sliced and rolled in pita bread, but topped with a unique sweet sauce of condensed milk, vinegar and garlic — a local invention so beloved it became the city''s official dish.',ARRAY['Mix the ground beef with garlic powder, oregano and spices, press firmly into a loaf pan to form a compact meatloaf.','Bake 1 hour at 180°C until quite firm, let cool then slice very thin.','Fry the slices in a pan or under the grill until crispy at the edges.','Prepare the sauce by mixing condensed milk, white vinegar and crushed garlic until thick and smooth.','Fill the warm pita with meat, tomato and onion, coat generously with the sweet sauce.']::text[]),
('pei-mussels','en','Prince Edward Island mussels','Charlottetown, Prince Edward Island, Canada','Blue mussels raised on suspended ropes in the island''s cold, clean waters, opened in minutes with white wine steam, shallot and cream, the island alone producing the majority of Canada''s farmed mussels.',ARRAY['Scrape and debeard the mussels thoroughly, discard any that remain open.','Sweat sliced shallot and garlic in butter until translucent.','Add the white wine, bring to a boil.','Add the mussels, cover and cook 6 minutes, shaking the pan, until all have opened.','Add the cream, let thicken 1 minute, scatter with parsley and serve immediately with crusty bread.']::text[]),
('jiggs-dinner','en','Jiggs dinner','St. John''s, Newfoundland, Canada','Salt beef boiled with cabbage, turnip, carrot and potato, traditionally served on Sundays alongside a pease pudding cooked separately in a cloth bag, Newfoundland''s most emblematic Sunday meal.',ARRAY['Desalt the salt beef overnight, changing the water several times.','Soak the split peas separately overnight, tie them into a cloth bag.','Cook the beef in a large volume of water with bay leaf 2 hours, plunge the pea bag into the same broth.','Add turnip and carrot, cook 30 minutes, then cabbage and potato for the last 20 minutes.','Drain the pea bag, mash into a purée, serve the sliced beef with the vegetables and pea purée on the side.']::text[]),
('fish-and-brewis','en','Fish and brewis','Twillingate, Newfoundland, Canada','Salt cod and hard bread soaked separately overnight then combined and topped with scrunchions, sizzling fried pork fat cracklings, a Newfoundland fisherman''s subsistence dish turned into a Sunday staple.',ARRAY['Soak the salt cod and hard bread separately in cold water overnight, changing the water once.','Poach the soaked cod 15 minutes until tender, drain and flake.','Cook the soaked bread in simmering water 10 minutes until softened, drain.','Fry the diced pork fat until crisp with sliced onion, reserve the scrunchions and their fat.','Mix cod and bread in a dish, drizzle with the hot pork fat and crispy scrunchions, serve immediately.']::text[]),
('peameal-bacon-sandwich','en','Peameal bacon sandwich','Toronto, Ontario, Canada','A thick slice of boneless pork loin brined then rolled in yellow cornmeal, pan-fried until golden, served in a soft roll — a sandwich born at Toronto''s St. Lawrence Market, sold at dawn to market workers.',ARRAY['Brine the pork loin in salt, sugar and water 24 hours chilled.','Rinse and dry, cut into thick slices about 2 cm.','Roll each slice in yellow cornmeal, pressing well to adhere.','Brown the slices in butter over medium heat, 4 minutes per side until well cooked.','Fill a soft mustard-spread roll with one or two slices, serve hot.']::text[]),
('butter-tarts','en','Butter tarts','Muskoka, Ontario, Canada','Small tartlets filled with brown sugar, butter and maple syrup filling that bubbles while baking until gooey at the centre and lightly crisp on top, a national debate over raisins that has never found consensus.',ARRAY['Line small tartlet moulds with shortcrust pastry cut into circles.','Scatter a few raisins in the bottom of each shell for those who like them.','Melt the butter with brown sugar and maple syrup until smooth, let cool slightly.','Stir in the beaten eggs and vanilla, pour the filling two-thirds up each shell.','Bake 15 to 18 minutes at 200°C until the filling bubbles and the edges are golden, let cool before unmoulding.']::text[]),
('saskatoon-berry-pie','en','Saskatoon berry pie','Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, Canada','A pie filled with purple berries from the Canadian prairies, similar to blueberries but with a flavour closer to almond, wild-picked along the riverbanks that gave the city its name, between two buttery shortcrust layers.',ARRAY['Mix the berries with sugar, flour, lemon juice and a pinch of cinnamon.','Line a pie pan with shortcrust pastry, pour in the berry filling.','Scatter with small pieces of butter.','Cover with a second pastry disc, pinch the edges and cut a few slits to let steam escape.','Bake 40 to 45 minutes at 200°C until golden and bubbling, let cool before slicing.']::text[]),
('perogies-canadian','en','Canadian perogies','Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada','Large potato and cheddar dumplings inherited from the Prairie''s Ukrainian immigrants, poached then browned in butter with caramelised onions, served with a spoonful of ice-cold sour cream.',ARRAY['Knead flour, egg, part of the sour cream and warm water into a supple dough, let rest 30 minutes.','Cook and mash the potato, mix while still hot with grated cheddar until melted.','Roll the dough thin, cut circles, fill and close into a half-moon, pinching the edges well.','Poach the perogies in salted boiling water 5 minutes until they float.','Brown the poached perogies in butter with the caramelised onion, serve with the remaining sour cream.']::text[]),
('alberta-beef-steak','en','Alberta beef steak','Calgary, Alberta, Canada','A thick steak from grass-fed cattle raised on Alberta''s vast prairies, simply grilled with salt and pepper to let the meat''s quality speak for itself, a regional pride celebrated every year at the Calgary Stampede.',ARRAY['Take the steak out of the fridge 45 minutes before cooking so it reaches room temperature.','Season generously with coarse salt and cracked pepper on both sides.','Heat a cast-iron pan over very high heat until smoking.','Sear the steak 3 to 4 minutes per side depending on thickness, basting with garlic and thyme butter at the end of cooking.','Let rest 5 minutes under foil before slicing and serving.']::text[]),
('nanaimo-bars','en','Nanaimo bars','Nanaimo, British Columbia, Canada','A cold, no-bake three-layer square, with a crumbled coconut biscuit base, pale yellow buttercream custard, and a crackling dark chocolate topping, a mining town''s dessert that became a national emblem.',ARRAY['Blitz the biscuits into crumbs, mix with coconut, cocoa and melted butter, add the beaten egg and cook 3 minutes in a bain-marie, stirring.','Press this mixture into a paper-lined square pan, chill 20 minutes.','Beat butter, icing sugar and custard powder until light, spread over the cooled base.','Chill another 20 minutes until firm.','Melt the dark chocolate, pour over the custard layer, chill until hardened before cutting into small squares.']::text[]),
('bc-spot-prawns','en','BC spot prawns','Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada','Large spot prawns caught in the cold Pacific waters, available for only a few weeks each spring, simply grilled whole in their shells to preserve their sweet, delicate flesh.',ARRAY['Leave the prawns whole with their shells on, do not peel.','Mix olive oil, crushed garlic and sliced chilli, brush generously over the prawns.','Heat a grill or griddle over high heat.','Grill the prawns 2 minutes per side until the shell turns pink and the flesh turns opaque.','Drizzle with a squeeze of lemon, scatter with parsley and serve immediately, to be shelled with fingers.']::text[]),
('arctic-char-grilled','en','Grilled Arctic char','Iqaluit, Nunavut, Canada','A fish from the icy waters of the Canadian Arctic, a salmon cousin with delicate pink-orange flesh, caught by Inuit communities and simply grilled whole over a wood fire with salt, letting its exceptional freshness speak for itself.',ARRAY['Gut and rinse the whole fish, dry thoroughly.','Salt generously inside and out, tuck a few sprigs of dill and lemon rounds into the cavity.','Heat a grill or wood embers until hot but stable.','Grill the whole fish 6 to 7 minutes per side depending on thickness, until the flesh flakes easily.','Drizzle with melted butter and a squeeze of lemon just before serving.']::text[]),
('bannock','en','Bannock','Yellowknife, Northwest Territories, Canada','A traditional unleavened flatbread of the Indigenous peoples of northern Canada, cooked in a pan or on a hot stone, sometimes even wrapped around a stick and cooked over a campfire, an essential staple of bush cooking.',ARRAY['Mix flour, baking powder and salt in a large bowl.','Gradually add water, mixing until a supple but non-sticky dough forms.','Knead briefly, let rest 10 minutes under a cloth.','Flatten the dough into one or several thick discs about 2 cm.','Cook in an oiled pan over medium heat, 8 minutes per side until golden and cooked through.']::text[]),
('montreal-smoked-meat','en','Montreal smoked meat','Montreal, Quebec, Canada','A beef brisket cured in spices then smoked and steamed, less sweet than its New York pastrami cousin, hand-sliced in front of the customer and piled high between two slices of rye bread with mustard.',ARRAY['Rub the beef brisket with a mix of coarse salt, crushed garlic and spices, cure chilled 10 days, turning regularly.','Rinse thoroughly, coat with a generous crust of cracked pepper and coriander.','Cold-smoke then hot-smoke for several hours until a black crust forms.','Steam 4 hours until extremely tender.','Hand-slice in front of guests, pile generously between two slices of rye bread spread with mustard.']::text[]),
('beavertails','en','BeaverTails','Ottawa, Ontario, Canada','A yeasted dough hand-stretched into a flat oval shape resembling a beaver''s tail, fried until golden and crackling, covered in cinnamon sugar, sold hot to skaters on the frozen Rideau Canal every winter.',ARRAY['Knead flour, yeast, warm milk, egg, sugar and a pinch of salt into a supple dough, let rise 1 hour.','Divide the dough into balls, hand-stretch each into a flat, elongated oval shape.','Fry in hot oil at 180°C 1 minute per side until golden and crackling.','Drain quickly on paper towel.','Brush with melted butter and dust generously with cinnamon sugar, serve immediately, piping hot.']::text[]),
('tire-erable','en','Maple taffy on snow','Saint-Hyacinthe, Quebec, Canada','Pure maple syrup heated until it thickens into a concentrated syrup, poured in long streams directly onto fresh snow where it sets instantly, rolled around a wooden stick and eaten still warm-cold right at the sugar shack.',ARRAY['Pour the pure maple syrup into a large heavy-bottomed pan.','Heat over medium heat until reaching 112°C on a candy thermometer, never stirring.','Prepare a large tray of clean, freshly fallen snow, or crushed ice as a substitute.','Immediately pour long streams of hot syrup onto the packed snow.','Quickly roll each set stream around a wooden stick before it hardens too much, eat right away.']::text[]),
('bagna-cauda','en','Bagna càuda','Alba, Piedmont, Italy','A hot bath of garlic, anchovy and oil set in the middle of the table, where everyone dips raw vegetables. The dish of the Piedmont grape harvest: eaten slowly, in company, on an autumn evening.',ARRAY['Peel the garlic, remove the green germ and poach it gently in the milk for 30 minutes: this takes away its bite.','Drain the garlic and crush it with a fork together with the rinsed anchovy fillets.','Pour in the olive oil, add the butter, and melt over very low heat for 30 minutes, never letting it bubble.','The sauce is ready when smooth and pale gold; keep it warm over a table burner.','Serve with raw vegetables cut into batons, cardoon and Jerusalem artichoke first among them.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('vitello-tonnato','en','Vitello tonnato','Turin, Piedmont, Italy','Poached veal served cold in near-transparent slices under a sauce of tuna and capers. The pairing sounds improbable until the first mouthful: it has opened Piedmontese Sunday lunches for two centuries.',ARRAY['Tie the veal and poach it for 1½ hours in a broth of white wine, carrot, celery, onion and bay, never letting it boil.','Let it cool completely in its broth: this is what keeps it tender.','Whisk a firm mayonnaise with the yolks, oil and lemon juice.','Blend the drained tuna, anchovies and half the capers, fold into the mayonnaise and loosen with a little broth.','Slice the veal very thinly, coat with the sauce, scatter the remaining capers and chill for two hours before serving.']::text[]),
('pizzoccheri','en','Valtellina pizzoccheri','Teglio, Lombardy, Italy','Buckwheat tagliatelle boiled with cabbage and potato, then drowned in melted casera cheese and garlic butter. The Lombard mountains on a plate, with no concession whatsoever to lightness.',ARRAY['Knead both flours with the warm water, rest for 30 minutes, then roll out and cut strips 1 cm by 7.','Boil the diced potatoes for 5 minutes in plenty of salted water, then add the shredded cabbage.','Five minutes later drop in the pizzoccheri: they take about 8 minutes.','Meanwhile turn the garlic and sage golden in the butter without letting it burn.','Drain, layer pasta, vegetables and cheeses in the dish, pour over the sizzling butter and serve at once.']::text[]),
('casoncelli','en','Bergamo casoncelli','Bergamo, Lombardy, Italy','Sweet-wrapper-shaped ravioli whose filling mixes meat, amaretti and raisins — a survival of medieval cooking, where sweet and savoury shared a plate without embarrassment. Served with butter, pancetta and sage.',ARRAY['Knead the flour and eggs into a firm dough, wrap and rest for an hour.','Brown the minced beef and sausage, let cool, then mix with the crushed amaretti, raisins, grated pear, breadcrumbs and parmesan.','Roll the dough very thin, place hazelnut-sized mounds of filling every 5 cm, fold over and pinch both ends into sweet-wrapper shapes.','Crisp the pancetta in the butter with the sage.','Cook the casoncelli for 4 minutes in barely simmering water, drain and spoon over the pancetta butter.']::text[]),
('tortelli-di-zucca','en','Pumpkin tortelli','Mantua, Lombardy, Italy','Roast pumpkin meets Cremona mostarda and amaretti: sweet, mustardy and bitter all at once. Served on Mantuan tables on Christmas Eve since the Renaissance.',ARRAY['Roast the pumpkin in chunks for 45 minutes at 180°C: it should dry out as much as cook.','Mash the flesh with the powdered amaretti, chopped mostarda, parmesan and nutmeg; rest overnight in the fridge.','Knead flour and eggs, rest for an hour, then roll into very thin sheets.','Form 6 cm squares around a spoonful of filling and seal the edges carefully.','Cook for 4 minutes, drain and serve with sage butter and grated parmesan.']::text[]),
('polenta-concia','en','Polenta concia','Aosta, Aosta Valley, Italy','Here the polenta is only a pretext for the fontina. Stirred for a full hour, then loaded with cheese and butter until it pulls into threads. The dish of Aosta Valley mountain refuges.',ARRAY['Bring the salted water to the boil and rain in the cornmeal, whisking constantly.','Turn the heat to its lowest and stir with a wooden spoon for 50 minutes: the polenta should pull away from the sides.','Slice the fontina and fold it in off the heat, a handful at a time.','Add the butter in pieces and the parmesan, stirring until the mass is glossy and stringy.','Serve scalding hot, straight from the pot, with a turn of the pepper mill.']::text[]),
('risi-e-bisi','en','Risi e bisi','Venice, Veneto, Italy','Neither quite soup nor quite risotto: in Venice they say it must run off the spoon. It was served to the Doge on 25 April, Saint Mark''s day, with the first peas of the lagoon.',ARRAY['Pod the peas and boil the pods in the broth for 20 minutes, then strain: that is where the flavour lives.','Soften the onion and pancetta in half the butter.','Add the peas and parsley, moisten with a ladle of broth and stew for 10 minutes.','Pour in the rice, cover with boiling broth and cook for 15 minutes without stirring much: this is not a risotto.','Off the heat, beat in the remaining butter and the parmesan. It should stay loose enough to pour.']::text[]),
('baccala-alla-vicentina','en','Baccalà alla vicentina','Vicenza, Veneto, Italy','Norwegian stockfish stewed for four and a half hours in milk and oil, never once stirred. The brotherhood that guards the recipe insists on this stillness: one spoonful too many and the flesh falls apart.',ARRAY['Soak the stockfish for three days, changing the water morning and evening, then cut into pieces without removing the skin.','Soften the sliced onions very slowly in the oil, add the crushed anchovies and the parsley.','Flour the fish pieces and pack them tightly into a casserole, alternating with onion and parmesan.','Cover with milk and oil, bring to a bare simmer and cook uncovered for 4½ hours, never stirring.','Serve warm with a thick polenta: the dish is better still reheated the next day.']::text[]),
('tiramisu','en','Tiramisù','Treviso, Veneto, Italy','Six ingredients, no cooking, a 1960s invention that became the most copied Italian dessert in the world. The Treviso rule: no whipped cream, no alcohol, no gelatine — only eggs, mascarpone and strong coffee.',ARRAY['Make the coffee and let it cool completely: while hot it would soak the biscuits through.','Whisk the yolks with the sugar to the ribbon stage, then fold in the mascarpone with a spatula.','Whip the whites to firm peaks and fold them into the cream, lifting rather than stirring.','Dip each biscuit for one second per side in the coffee — no longer — and line the base of the dish.','Alternate two layers of biscuits and cream, smooth the top, chill for 6 hours and dust with cocoa just before serving.']::text[]),
('frico','en','Frico','Udine, Friuli, Italy','A cake of montasio and potato fried until it forms a lace-like crust. Born of thrift: Friulian shepherds melted down the rinds rather than throw them away.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel and crush them roughly with a fork.','Soften the sliced onion in the butter without colouring it.','Grate the montasio — one third young, two thirds aged — and mix it with the potato and onion.','Tip the mixture into a hot pan, press it down and let it set for 12 minutes over medium heat without touching it.','Flip it using a plate and brown the second side for 10 minutes. Serve cut into wedges, crust upwards.']::text[]),
('farinata-genovese','en','Farinata','Genoa, Liguria, Italy','A chickpea flatbread baked in a copper pan in a wood oven, crisp at the edge and creamy at the centre. Three ingredients and a long wait: the batter must rest five hours to lose its bitterness.',ARRAY['Whisk the chickpea flour into the water until lump-free, and salt it.','Rest for 5 hours at room temperature, skimming off the foam that rises: that foam carries the bitterness.','Stir in half the oil; the batter should stay very liquid.','Oil a wide pan generously, pour in a layer barely 5 mm deep and scatter over the rosemary.','Bake at 250°C with top heat for 12 to 15 minutes: the surface should be blistered brown. Pepper it and cut into uneven pieces.']::text[]),
('passatelli','en','Passatelli in brodo','Forlì, Romagna, Italy','Vermicelli of stale bread, parmesan and egg pushed through a perforated plate and cooked for two minutes in broth. Romagna''s cooking of leftovers raised to its highest point.',ARRAY['Mix the fine breadcrumbs, grated parmesan, eggs, nutmeg and lemon zest.','Work by hand to a compact, dry dough that no longer sticks; rest it for 30 minutes.','Bring the broth to a bare simmer — it should be fragrant, it is half the dish.','Push the dough through a coarse potato ricer directly over the broth.','The passatelli rise in 2 minutes: serve immediately, with extra parmesan on the side.']::text[]),
('piadina-romagnola','en','Piadina romagnola','Rimini, Romagna, Italy','An unleavened flatbread cooked on a terracotta griddle, folded over squacquerone cheese and rocket. Pascoli called it ''the national bread of the Romagnoli''; seafront kiosks still sell it at all hours.',ARRAY['Rub the lard into the flour with your fingertips, then add the salt and bicarbonate.','Pour in the warm water little by little and knead for 10 minutes to a supple dough; rest for 30 minutes under a cloth.','Divide into six balls and roll them into 3 mm discs.','Cook on a very hot dry griddle, 2 minutes a side, pricking the bubbles with a fork.','Fill while hot with the soft cheese, ham and rocket, then fold in half.']::text[]),
('pappa-al-pomodoro','en','Pappa al pomodoro','Siena, Tuscany, Italy','Stale unsalted Tuscan bread melted into tomato and olive oil until it becomes a thick porridge. A poor man''s dish turned regional emblem: eaten lukewarm, never piping hot.',ARRAY['Turn the crushed garlic golden in half the oil, then remove it.','Add the peeled and chopped tomatoes, salt, and reduce for 20 minutes.','Cut the stale bread into thick slices and drop them into the pan with the hot broth.','Crush with a wooden spoon and cook 20 minutes more: the bread should vanish into the sauce.','Off the heat, add the torn basil and the remaining raw oil. Serve lukewarm, never scalding.']::text[]),
('bistecca-alla-fiorentina','en','Bistecca alla fiorentina','Florence, Tuscany, Italy','A five-centimetre-thick Chianina T-bone seared over embers and served rare — asking for it well done is, in Florence, a breach of taste. Salt, pepper, raw oil: nothing else.',ARRAY['Take the meat out of the fridge two hours ahead: a bistecca cold at the centre will never cook properly.','Build a fierce fire and let the embers turn white with ash.','Lay the steak on the grill and do not touch it for 5 minutes, then turn it for 5 minutes more.','Stand it upright on the bone for 5 minutes to sear the rim, never piercing the meat.','Salt, pepper, pour over raw oil and rest for 5 minutes before carving along the bone.']::text[]),
('olive-ascolane','en','Olive ascolane','Ascoli Piceno, Marche, Italy','Large green olives stoned in a spiral, stuffed with three meats, breaded and fried. A work of patience invented in the 19th century by cooks in noble households to use up their leftovers.',ARRAY['Stone the olives by turning the knife around the pit so as to keep one continuous ribbon.','Brown the meats with the diced vegetables, deglaze with white wine and cook for 30 minutes.','Chop everything finely, bind with an egg, the parmesan and the nutmeg.','Re-form each olive around a hazelnut of stuffing, then coat in flour, beaten egg and breadcrumbs.','Fried for 3 minutes at 175°C they should be golden and crisp. Serve scalding hot.']::text[]),
('strangozzi-al-tartufo','en','Strangozzi with black truffle','Norcia, Umbria, Italy','Water-and-flour pasta, no egg, rolled by hand and served under a shower of raw grated Norcia black truffle. The sauce is three things: oil, garlic, anchovy — anything more would mask the scent.',ARRAY['Knead the flour and salted water for 15 minutes to a very firm dough; rest for an hour.','Roll out to 2 mm, flour it, roll the sheet on itself and cut thick strips, unrolling them at once.','Warm the oil gently with the whole garlic cloves and the anchovies until the latter dissolve; discard the garlic.','Grate two thirds of the truffle into the warm oil off the heat: it must never cook.','Cook the pasta for 3 minutes, toss with the sauce and a ladle of cooking water, and grate the rest of the truffle at the table.']::text[]),
('amatriciana','en','Bucatini all’amatriciana','Amatrice, Lazio, Italy','Guanciale, tomato, pecorino: the trinity of Amatrice, an Apennine village whose recipe conquered Rome. Guanciale is not pancetta, and the distinction is no quibble — the cheek gives that melting fat.',ARRAY['Cut the guanciale into thick lardons and render it over low heat, with no added fat, until golden.','Lift it out and keep it crisp; deglaze the pan with white wine.','Add the crushed tomatoes and chilli, salt lightly and reduce for 15 minutes.','Cook the bucatini very al dente and drain, keeping a ladle of the water.','Combine pasta, sauce and guanciale off the heat, then beat in the grated pecorino, stirring briskly.']::text[]),
('saltimbocca-alla-romana','en','Saltimbocca alla romana','Rome, Lazio, Italy','''Jump in the mouth'': a veal escalope, a sage leaf, a slice of raw ham, ten minutes in a pan. The speed is part of the recipe — overcooked, the veal toughens and the dish loses its point.',ARRAY['Flatten the escalopes between two sheets of paper to 4 mm.','Lay two sage leaves and a slice of ham on each, fixing them with a cocktail stick.','Flour very lightly, on the veal side only.','Sear ham-side down for 1 minute in foaming butter, then turn for 2 minutes.','Keep warm, deglaze with white wine, reduce by half and pour this buttery juice over the saltimbocca.']::text[]),
('arrosticini','en','Arrosticini','Pescara, Abruzzo, Italy','One-centimetre cubes of mutton threaded onto wooden skewers and grilled over a long narrow brazier, the ''canala''. Eaten by hand, by the dozen, pulling the meat off with your teeth.',ARRAY['Cut the meat and fat into even 1 cm cubes: this is the only technical point of the dish.','Thread them alternating lean and fat, five or six pieces per 20 cm skewer.','Prepare a bed of red embers in a narrow hearth, longer than it is wide.','Lay the skewers tightly side by side and turn them every 30 seconds, 4 minutes in all.','Salt as they come off the fire and serve on country bread drizzled with oil, which catches the juices.']::text[]),
('parmigiana-di-melanzane','en','Aubergine parmigiana','Salerno, Campania, Italy','Fried aubergine, tomato, basil and two cheeses, stacked and baked. The name comes not from Parma but from Sicilian ''parmiciana'', the slats of a shutter — which overlap exactly as the slices do.',ARRAY['Cut the aubergines into 6 mm slices, salt them and let them drain for an hour under a weight.','Pat them thoroughly dry, flour them and fry until golden; drain on paper.','Make a sauce by reducing the tomatoes with the garlic and olive oil for 20 minutes.','Layer sauce, aubergine, drained mozzarella, basil and parmesan in a dish, four storeys high.','Bake for 45 minutes at 180°C and let it settle for 30 minutes before cutting: that rest is what holds it together.']::text[]),
('sfogliatella','en','Sfogliatella riccia','Naples, Campania, Italy','A shell of pastry in a hundred leaves, crisp enough to shatter, filled with a semolina and ricotta cream scented with cinnamon. Born in a convent on the Amalfi coast, perfected by a Neapolitan pastry cook in 1818.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, water, honey and salt for 15 minutes into a very hard dough; rest for 2 hours.','Pass it through a pasta roller, thinner and thinner, into a translucent sheet several metres long. Brush with melted lard and roll it up tightly into a log. Chill overnight.','Cook the semolina in the milk, let it cool, then mix in ricotta, sugar, yolks, candied fruit, cinnamon and orange blossom water.','Slice the log into 1 cm rounds and push each into a cone with your thumbs; fill with cream and seal.','Bake for 25 minutes at 200°C. The leaves fan open. Dust with icing sugar and eat warm.']::text[]),
('pasta-alla-norma','en','Pasta alla Norma','Catania, Sicily, Italy','Named after the opera by Bellini, a son of Catania: to call a dish ''a Norma'' was to say it approached perfection. Fried aubergine, tomato, basil and grated salted ricotta on top.',ARRAY['Dice the aubergines, salt them and let them drain for 30 minutes.','Fry them in hot oil until golden and melting, then drain.','Make a short sauce with the garlic, olive oil and crushed tomatoes, 20 minutes over a lively flame.','Cook the pasta al dente and toss it with the sauce and half the aubergine.','Plate up, crown with the remaining aubergine, the basil and a generous shower of grated salted ricotta.']::text[]),
('caponata','en','Caponata','Palermo, Sicily, Italy','Aubergine, celery, olives and capers bound by a sweet-and-sour of vinegar and sugar. Every Sicilian family defends its own version; all agree on one point: eat it the next day, at room temperature.',ARRAY['Cut the aubergines into large dice, salt for 30 minutes, pat dry and fry until coloured.','Blanch the celery for 5 minutes, then sweat it in olive oil with the sliced onion.','Add tomatoes, stoned olives, rinsed capers, pine nuts and raisins; stew for 15 minutes.','Pour in the vinegar and sugar, raise the heat and let it evaporate for 3 minutes: that is the sweet-sour strike.','Fold in the aubergines, cut the heat, add the basil and rest overnight before serving at room temperature.']::text[]),
('seadas','en','Seadas','Nuoro, Sardinia, Italy','A fried semolina turnover stuffed with barely sour fresh pecorino, poured over with bitter strawberry-tree honey. Sweet and savoury meet head-on — it began life as a main course, not a dessert.',ARRAY['Knead the semolina, lard, warm water and salt into a smooth dough; rest for an hour.','Melt the fresh pecorino gently with the lemon zest until it pulls into threads, spread it 1 cm thick and let it set.','Cut 8 cm discs of cheese and 10 cm discs of dough.','Seal each cheese disc between two rounds of pastry, crimping with a fluted wheel and pushing out the air.','Fried 2 minutes a side at 170°C, they puff up. Drain and pour over the warm honey.']::text[]),
('koenigsberger-klopse','en','Königsberger Klopse','Berlin, Germany','Veal dumplings poached in broth and served under a white sauce of capers and lemon. They came from East Prussia with the refugees of 1945 and became a staple of Berlin canteens.',ARRAY['Soak the bread roll in water, squeeze it out and mix into the minced veal with the softened onion, crushed anchovies and egg.','Shape golf-ball dumplings and poach them for 15 minutes in broth with bay and allspice, never boiling.','Lift them out and strain the broth.','Make a pale roux with the butter and flour, whisk in half a litre of broth and cook for 10 minutes.','Off the heat, bind with the yolk beaten into the cream, add the capers and lemon juice, then return the dumplings. Serve with steamed potatoes.']::text[]),
('maultaschen','en','Maultaschen','Stuttgart, Swabia, Germany','Big Swabian ravioli whose nickname says everything: Herrgottsbscheißerle, ''little cheaters of the Lord''. The monks of Maulbronn hid meat under spinach so they could eat it during Lent.',ARRAY['Knead the flour and eggs into a firm dough and rest it for an hour.','Blanch the spinach, squeeze it thoroughly dry and chop it with the bacon and the soaked bread rolls.','Combine with the minced pork, softened onion, parsley, marjoram and nutmeg.','Roll the dough into long strips, pipe the filling along them, fold over and press every 6 cm before cutting.','Poach for 12 minutes in simmering broth and serve in that broth — or fried in butter the next day.']::text[]),
('kaesespaetzle','en','Käsespätzle','Ulm, Swabia, Germany','Fresh noodles scraped from a board into boiling water, layered with mountain cheese and crowned with fried onions. Swabian lore said you judged a woman by her Spätzle — the saying has aged badly, the recipe has not.',ARRAY['Beat the flour, eggs, sparkling water and salt hard until the batter blisters and pulls away from the spoon.','Rest for 20 minutes, then scrape it in small batches into a large pan of boiling salted water.','The Spätzle are done the moment they float: drain and barely refresh them.','Fry the sliced onions slowly in the butter until golden and lightly crisp.','Layer Spätzle and grated cheeses in a warm dish, cover and let melt for 5 minutes, then top with the onions and chives.']::text[]),
('schweinshaxe','en','Schweinshaxe','Munich, Bavaria, Germany','A pork knuckle roasted for two and a half hours, its rind turned into a glassy shell you have to crack with a knife. The dish of Munich beer halls, basted with dark beer during cooking as much as poured beside it.',ARRAY['Score the rind into diamonds without cutting into the meat, rub with salt, crushed garlic and caraway.','Set the knuckles on a bed of onion and carrot and pour half the beer into the dish.','Roast for 2 hours at 160°C, basting every 20 minutes with the juices and the rest of the beer.','Raise to 220°C for the last 20 minutes: the rind blisters and hardens.','Rest for 10 minutes, deglaze the roasting juices and serve with sauerkraut and potatoes.']::text[]),
('weisswurst','en','Weißwurst','Munich, Bavaria, Germany','A white sausage of veal and parsley, poached and never grilled. Munich lore says it must not hear the noon bells: made without preservatives, it was eaten the very morning it was made.',ARRAY['Blend the very cold veal and fat with the iced water in several stages, never letting the mixture warm.','Fold in the chopped parsley, cooled softened onion, lemon zest, white pepper and nutmeg.','Fill the casings and twist every 12 cm; prick any air bubbles.','Poach for 12 minutes in water at 75°C: it must never boil, or the sausages burst.','Serve in their water with sweet mustard and a pretzel. You suck out the meat and leave the skin.']::text[]),
('brezel','en','Pretzel','Augsburg, Bavaria, Germany','The knot of dough dipped in an alkaline bath before baking: that is what gives the brown crust and the unmistakable taste. The belly cracks, the arms stay soft — a good pretzel is known by that contrast.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water, yeast, butter, malt and salt for 10 minutes into a firm dough; prove for 45 minutes.','Divide into eight, roll long ropes tapered at the ends and swollen in the middle.','Form the knot: cross the two arms twice and fold them back onto the belly.','Let them dry uncovered in the fridge for 30 minutes, then dip each pretzel for 20 seconds in a litre of boiling water with the bicarbonate.','Slash the belly, scatter coarse salt and bake for 15 minutes at 220°C.']::text[]),
('rinderrouladen','en','Rinderrouladen','Düsseldorf, Rhineland, Germany','A slice of beef rolled around mustard, bacon, onion and a gherkin, braised for two hours in its own juices. The German Sunday lunch par excellence, with red cabbage and potato dumplings.',ARRAY['Flatten the beef slices, season and spread them with mustard.','Lay bacon, sliced onion and a halved gherkin on each, roll up and tie with string.','Sear the rolls on all sides in hot butter and set aside.','Sweat carrot and onion in the same pot, add the tomato paste and deglaze with red wine.','Return the rolls, pour in the broth, add the bay and braise covered for 1¾ hours. Thicken the sauce with the slaked flour.']::text[]),
('labskaus','en','Labskaus','Hamburg, Germany','A bright pink hash of salt beef, potato and beetroot, served with a fried egg, a pickled herring and a gherkin. Deep-sea sailors'' food: everything in it was salted or pickled, and therefore imperishable.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in salted water with the bay leaf, then crush them roughly.','Soften the onion in the lard, add the shredded salt beef and the cooked beetroot in small dice.','Fold into the potato, moisten with the pickle brine and heat through for 10 minutes, stirring: the hash should stay coarse.','Fry the eggs, whites set and yolks running.','Mound the hash on the plate, set the egg on top, herring and gherkin alongside.']::text[]),
('gruenkohl-pinkel','en','Grünkohl mit Pinkel','Bremen, Germany','The kale waits for the first frost before it is picked: cold turns its starch into sugar. It is stewed for two hours with bacon and Pinkel, a smoked oat sausage, during the ritual outings of January.',ARRAY['Strip the kale from its stalks, blanch for 5 minutes and chop it coarsely.','Soften the onion and bacon in the lard, add the kale and let it sweat for 10 minutes.','Moisten with broth, bury the smoked pork and simmer covered for 1 hour.','Lay the Pinkel sausages on the kale without pricking them and continue for 45 minutes: their fat perfumes the whole dish.','Thicken with the oats, sharpen with mustard and serve with fried potatoes.']::text[]),
('handkaese-mit-musik','en','Handkäse mit Musik','Frankfurt, Hesse, Germany','A translucent sour cheese married to a marinade of raw onion and vinegar. The ''music'' of the name is not poetic: it is what the onions produce a few hours later, and the Hessians still find it funny.',ARRAY['Slice the onions as finely as you can and salt them lightly.','Mix them with the vinegar, oil, caraway and a little pepper.','Lay the cheeses in a deep dish and cover them entirely with this marinade.','Rest in the fridge for at least 4 hours, ideally overnight: the cheese should soak without falling apart.','Serve with buttered rye bread and plenty of chives. A glass of apple cider is obligatory.']::text[]),
('thueringer-rostbratwurst','en','Thüringer Rostbratwurst','Erfurt, Thuringia, Germany','Recorded as early as 1404 in the accounts of a convent in Arnstadt, it is grilled over pine-cone embers and eaten standing, in a roll too short for it, with brown mustard.',ARRAY['Mince the meat and fat on a medium plate, keeping everything very cold.','Season with caraway, marjoram, garlic, nutmeg, salt and pepper, and work the mixture until it turns tacky.','Fill the casings loosely and twist every 20 cm; rest overnight in the fridge.','Grill over medium embers for 12 to 15 minutes, turning often: the skin should brown without splitting.','Serve in a split roll with a spoonful of brown mustard.']::text[]),
('spargel-hollandaise','en','White asparagus with hollandaise','Beelitz, Brandenburg, Germany','From April to Midsummer, Germany stops for white asparagus. At Beelitz it is cut before it breaks the sand, which keeps it white and sweet. Butter, ham, steamed potatoes: nothing more.',ARRAY['Peel the asparagus from tip to base, twice rather than once, and snap off the fibrous end.','Simmer the peelings for 15 minutes in salted water with the sugar: this broth will cook the spears.','Poach them 12 to 18 minutes depending on thickness; they should bend without snapping.','Clarify the butter. Whisk the yolks with the wine over a bain-marie to the ribbon, then trickle in the warm butter, whisking without pause.','Season the sauce with lemon, salt and nutmeg, and serve the asparagus with the ham and steamed potatoes.']::text[]),
('saumagen','en','Palatinate Saumagen','Bad Dürkheim, Palatinate, Germany','A pig''s stomach stuffed with meat and potato, poached then sliced and pan-fried. Helmut Kohl served it to Gorbachev, Thatcher and Reagan: the most diplomatic peasant dish in Germany.',ARRAY['Clean the stomach thoroughly in vinegared water and soak it overnight.','Cut the pork and boiled potatoes into large dice, mix with the softened onion, eggs, marjoram and spices.','Fill the stomach only three quarters full — the stuffing swells — and sew the opening shut.','Poach for 2½ hours in water at 80°C, never boiling, pricking it two or three times so it does not burst.','Cool, cut into thick slices and fry them golden in lard. Serve with sauerkraut cooked in white wine.']::text[]),
('himmel-und-erde','en','Himmel und Erde','Cologne, Rhineland, Germany','Heaven is the apples that grow above; earth is the potatoes below. Mashed together and crowned with fried black pudding and onions, they make a Rhineland dish recorded since the 18th century.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in salted water. Meanwhile stew the quartered apples with the sugar and vinegar.','Mash the two together with a fork, keeping some texture, and season with nutmeg.','Fry the sliced onions in the butter until golden, along with the diced bacon.','Cut the black pudding into thick slices and fry for 2 minutes a side, turning no more than once.','Spoon out the mash, lay on the pudding and cover with the onions and crisp bacon.']::text[]),
('stollen','en','Dresden Stollen','Dresden, Saxony, Germany','A Christmas loaf heavy with fruit and butter, its shape recalling the swaddled child. Until 1491 butter was forbidden in it during Advent: it took a papal bull to allow it.',ARRAY['Steep the raisins in the rum for at least one night, two if you can.','Make a starter with the yeast, warm milk and a little flour; let it froth for 20 minutes.','Knead with the remaining flour, 200 g of butter, the sugar and the spices, then work in the fruit and almonds without tearing the dough.','Prove for an hour, flatten, lay a rope of marzipan down the middle and fold the dough over it, off-centre.','Bake for 60 minutes at 160°C, brush with melted butter as it comes out and bury it in icing sugar. Wait three weeks before slicing.']::text[]),
('rote-gruetze','en','Rote Grütze','Kiel, Schleswig-Holstein, Germany','A barely thickened compote of red fruit, served ice cold beneath a veil of cold cream or vanilla sauce. The summer dessert of the North, where the berry season is short and nothing is wasted.',ARRAY['Hull the fruit and stone the cherries; keep a quarter of the raspberries back for the end.','Bring the water, sugar and lemon juice to the boil, add the currants and cherries and cook for 5 minutes.','Add the strawberries and raspberries and take off the heat almost at once: they must not collapse.','Slake the cornstarch in a little cold water, stir it into the hot fruit and let it thicken for barely a minute.','Add the vanilla and the reserved raspberries, cool, then chill for 4 hours. Serve with cold cream on the side.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('tafelspitz','en','Tafelspitz','Vienna, Austria','The most prized cut of beef in Vienna, poached for three hours and served in its broth with two sauces: horseradish with apple, and chive. It was Franz Joseph''s favourite dish, eaten almost daily.',ARRAY['Brown the halved onions dry, cut side down on the pan: this is what gives the broth its golden colour.','Bring the meat and bones to a bare simmer in plenty of water, skimming carefully for twenty minutes.','Add the vegetables, bay and peppercorns, and poach for 2½ hours, never boiling.','Grate the horseradish into cooked apple and a little broth for the first sauce; blend chives, cream and soaked bread for the second.','Carve the meat against the grain, serve it in a tureen with the broth and vegetables, the two sauces alongside.']::text[]),
('kaiserschmarrn','en','Kaiserschmarrn','Salzburg, Austria','A thick pancake torn into golden shreds with a spatula, caramelised in sugar and served with plum compote. The ''Emperor''s scramble'': legend has a cook botching his pancake in front of Franz Joseph.',ARRAY['Plump the raisins in the warm rum.','Whisk the yolks, milk, flour, vanilla and a pinch of salt to a smooth batter; rest for 15 minutes.','Beat the whites to soft peaks with half the sugar and fold them in gently.','Pour into a large buttered pan, scatter the raisins over and let it set for 4 minutes, then flip it in one piece.','Tear the pancake into pieces with the spatula, sprinkle with the remaining sugar and caramelise for 3 minutes, stirring. Dust with icing sugar and serve with the compote.']::text[]),
('apfelstrudel','en','Apfelstrudel','Vienna, Austria','The dough must be stretched by hand over a tablecloth until you can read a newspaper through it — the Viennese rule allows no appeal. It came from Turkey by way of Hungary, a distant cousin of baklava.',ARRAY['Knead flour, warm water, oil and salt for 10 minutes until the dough is elastic and glossy; oil it and rest it for an hour under a warm bowl.','Toast the breadcrumbs in half the butter: they will absorb the apple juices.','Peel and thinly slice the apples, mix with the sugar, cinnamon, rum-soaked raisins, walnuts and lemon.','Stretch the dough by hand over a floured cloth until translucent, brush with melted butter and scatter the crumbs.','Lay the apples along one third, roll up using the cloth, seal the ends and bake for 40 minutes at 190°C, brushing twice with butter.']::text[]),
('tiroler-groestl','en','Tiroler Gröstl','Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria','The leftover meal, made in a pan: yesterday''s potatoes, Sunday''s boiled beef, onion, all fried together and crowned with a fried egg. The refuge lunch after a morning in the mountains.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in their skins the day before: cold, they will not fall apart in the pan.','Peel them, cut into thick rounds and fry them in lard until golden.','Add the bacon and sliced onion, letting them colour without stirring too often.','Fold in the sliced boiled beef, the caraway and the marjoram, and heat through for 5 minutes.','Fry four eggs and lay them on the gröstl, parsley over the top. Serve in the pan.']::text[]),
('steirisches-backhendl','en','Styrian Backhendl','Graz, Styria, Austria','A jointed chicken, breaded and fried in lard, served warm with potato salad dressed in roasted pumpkin-seed oil. In the 18th century only the Viennese aristocracy could afford a whole chicken for one person.',ARRAY['Joint the chicken into eight pieces, salt them and rest for 30 minutes.','Coat each piece in flour, beaten egg and breadcrumbs, without pressing: the crumb must stay loose so it puffs in the fat.','Heat the lard to 165°C and fry the pieces for 12 to 15 minutes depending on size.','Drain on a rack, never on paper, to keep the crust crisp.','Make the warm potato salad with onion, vinegar and pumpkin-seed oil, and serve with lemon wedges.']::text[]),
('roesti','en','Rösti','Bern, Switzerland','A cake of grated potato browned in butter, originally the breakfast of Bernese farmers. The imaginary line dividing French- from German-speaking Switzerland is still called the ''Röstigraben''.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in their skins the day before and leave them to cool overnight: this is the only rule that matters.','Peel and grate them on the coarse side of the grater, without crushing them.','Salt at the last moment, never before: salt would draw out water and the cake would stay soft.','Tip into a hot buttered pan, press down lightly and let it set for 15 minutes over medium heat.','Turn it with a plate, add the remaining butter and brown the other side for 12 minutes. The crust should be even and brittle.']::text[]),
('zuercher-geschnetzeltes','en','Zürcher Geschnetzeltes','Zurich, Switzerland','Veal cut into fine strips, seared very fast and coated in a cream and white wine sauce. In Zurich the kidneys go in; elsewhere they are left out. It is always served with rösti.',ARRAY['Cut the veal into finger-width strips and flour them very lightly.','Sear them in two batches in sizzling butter, barely 90 seconds, and keep them warm: they will finish cooking in the sauce.','Sweat the shallot, add the sliced mushrooms and let them release their water.','Deglaze with white wine, reduce by half, pour in the cream and let it thicken for 5 minutes.','Return the meat and its juices, heat through without boiling, and sharpen with lemon and parsley.']::text[]),
('raclette-valaisanne','en','Valais raclette','Sion, Valais, Switzerland','Half a wheel held to the fire, its melting face scraped onto the plate — hence the name. Valais herdsmen did this from the Middle Ages; the electric grill arrived only six centuries later.',ARRAY['Choose firm-fleshed potatoes of even size and boil them in their skins for 25 minutes.','Keep them warm under a cloth: they must stay scalding throughout the meal.','Hold the half-wheel under the grill, or lay slices in individual pans.','Scrape the cheese the moment it blisters and runs, without waiting for it to colour.','Serve at once over the crushed potato, with gherkins, pickled onions and air-dried beef. Grind pepper over.']::text[]),
('aelplermagronen','en','Älplermagronen','Lucerne, Switzerland','Macaroni, potatoes, cream and cheese cooked in one pan, with fried onions and apple sauce on the side. Alpine herdsmen had one fire and little crockery: everything goes in together.',ARRAY['Dice the potatoes to the size of the macaroni: they must cook together.','Start them in salted water for 5 minutes, then add the pasta for its stated time.','Meanwhile fry the sliced onions slowly in the butter until deep golden.','Drain, return to the pan with the cream and milk, and reduce for 3 minutes.','Off the heat, stir in the grated gruyère and the nutmeg, cover with the fried onions and serve with cold apple sauce.']::text[]),
('souvlaki-pita','en','Souvlaki in pita','Athens, Greece','Cubes of pork marinated in lemon and oregano, grilled over embers and rolled in a warm pita with tomato, red onion, tzatziki and a few chips. The standing meal of Athenians, always has been.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into 3 cm cubes and marinate for 4 hours in lemon juice, oil, oregano, crushed garlic and pepper.','Grate the cucumber, squeeze it hard and mix into the yogurt with a little garlic and oil: that is the tzatziki.','Thread the meat onto skewers and grill for 10 to 12 minutes over lively embers, turning often.','Deep-fry the potatoes as chips and salt them as they come out.','Warm the pitas 30 seconds a side, fill with meat, tzatziki, tomato, onion and chips, then roll tightly in paper.']::text[]),
('horiatiki','en','Greek village salad','Santorini, Greece','No lettuce and no dressing: horiatiki, the ''village salad'', is nothing but tomato, cucumber, onion, pepper, olives and a whole slab of feta laid on top. Olive oil and oregano do the rest.',ARRAY['Cut the tomatoes into large irregular wedges — never dice — and salt them at once.','Peel the cucumber in stripes, slice it thick, and cut the onion and pepper into rings.','Combine the vegetables in a deep dish with the olives and capers, without crushing them.','Lay the slab of feta whole on top, unbroken.','Pour over plenty of olive oil, scatter oregano and serve with bread to mop the bottom of the dish.']::text[]),
('spanakopita','en','Spanakopita','Ioannina, Epirus, Greece','The pie of Epirus, land of great pies: spinach, wild herbs and feta between pastry leaves so thin they are counted by the dozen. Every village had its own version, according to what the mountain gave.',ARRAY['Salt the shredded spinach and let it drain for 30 minutes, then squeeze it out by the handful: this is the step people wrongly skip.','Sweat the leek and spring onions in a little oil, then let them cool.','Combine spinach, leek, chopped herbs, crumbled feta and beaten eggs. Pepper it; the feta usually salts it enough.','Oil the tin, layer seven sheets of pastry brushing each one, dust with semolina and spread the filling.','Close with seven more sheets, score the portions before baking and bake for 50 minutes at 180°C. Let it cool before cutting.']::text[]),
('pastitsio','en','Pastitsio','Athens, Greece','Three storeys: fat macaroni standing on end, a meat sauce with cinnamon and clove, and a thick béchamel browned in the oven. The Greek cousin of baked pasta, marked by that note of sweet spice.',ARRAY['Brown the onion and meat, deglaze with red wine, add tomato, cinnamon, cloves and bay, then reduce for 40 minutes.','Cook the macaroni very al dente, drain and bind with a beaten egg and a little cheese.','Make a thick béchamel with the butter, flour and milk; off the heat stir in two eggs, the nutmeg and half the cheese.','Lay half the pasta in the dish, cover with the meat, then the rest of the pasta.','Pour over the béchamel, dust with the remaining cheese and bake for 45 minutes at 180°C. Wait 30 minutes before cutting.']::text[]),
('gemista','en','Gemista','Nafplio, Peloponnese, Greece','Tomatoes and peppers hollowed out, filled with herbed rice and baked slowly until they slump. A summer dish made in the morning and eaten warm in the afternoon, never hot.',ARRAY['Cut a lid off the tomatoes and peppers, hollow them out and keep the tomato pulp.','Soften the onion in oil, add the raw rice, the blended pulp, herbs, pine nuts and raisins; let it absorb for 5 minutes.','Salt the inside of the vegetables, sugar the tomatoes lightly and fill them three quarters full: the rice swells.','Wedge them tightly in a dish, slip quartered potatoes between them and pour over oil and a glass of water.','Bake for 1¼ hours at 180°C with the lids back on. Serve warm, ideally the next day.']::text[]),
('fasolada','en','Fasolada','Volos, Thessaly, Greece','The white bean soup that Greeks call their national dish, long before moussaka. Nothing but vegetables, a great deal of olive oil, and a long cooking that does all the work.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then bring them to the boil in fresh water and throw that first water away.','Cover with cold water and simmer for 45 minutes with the bay leaves.','Add the carrot, celery and onion in large pieces, along with the tomato purée.','Pour in all the olive oil at once — it is what gives the broth its body — and continue for 40 minutes.','Salt only at the end, add the oregano and parsley, and serve with olives and a wedge of lemon.']::text[]),
('loukoumades','en','Loukoumades','Athens, Greece','Small round fritters drenched in honey and cinnamon. They were given to victors of the ancient Games as ''honey cakes'' — which would make them the oldest sweet in Europe still sold in the street.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast and sugar in the warm water and let it froth for 10 minutes.','Whisk in the flour and salt: the batter should be very slack, almost pourable, halfway between dough and cream.','Cover and prove for 1½ hours somewhere warm, until doubled and pitted with bubbles.','Scoop the batter with a wet spoon and drop it into oil at 175°C; the fritters turn themselves once golden.','Drain, pile them up while still hot, pour over warm honey and scatter cinnamon, crushed walnuts and sesame.']::text[]),
('kleftiko','en','Kleftiko','Chania, Crete, Greece','''In the manner of thieves'': the klephts cooked stolen lamb in a sealed pit oven so that neither smoke nor smell would betray them. Today''s parcel keeps the principle, minus the clandestinity.',ARRAY['Stud the lamb shoulder with slivers of garlic and marinate for 3 hours in lemon, oil and oregano.','Line a large dish with two crossed sheets of baking paper, long and overhanging.','Lay the lamb on a bed of quartered potatoes, add tomatoes, bay and white wine.','Crumble the feta over, fold the paper into a sealed parcel and tie it.','Bake for 3½ hours at 160°C without ever opening it. Open the parcel at the table: the trapped steam is what makes the dish.']::text[]),
('burek-mesni','en','Meat burek','Novi Pazar, Serbia','A long snake of hand-stretched pastry rolled around a filling of beef and onion, then coiled into a round tin. Eaten in the morning with drinking yogurt, standing outside the bakery.',ARRAY['Knead flour, warm water, a little oil and the salt for 15 minutes until very elastic; oil the dough and rest it for an hour.','Mix the raw beef with grated onion, paprika, salt and pepper: the filling is not pre-cooked.','Stretch the dough by hand on a floured cloth until translucent, drizzle with oil and let it dry for 5 minutes.','Lay the filling in a rope along one edge, roll loosely and coil that rope into an oiled round tin.','Brush with beaten egg and bake for 45 minutes at 190°C; splash with sparkling water halfway so the pastry stays soft inside.']::text[]),
('punjene-paprike','en','Punjene paprike','Novi Sad, Vojvodina, Serbia','Sweet white peppers filled with meat and rice, stewed standing upright in a roux-thickened tomato sauce. The dish of Vojvodina, the paprika plain where peppers come in by the cartload in September.',ARRAY['Hollow the peppers through the stalk without piercing them, and keep the tops.','Soften the onion, let it cool and mix with the meat, raw rice, egg, paprika and parsley.','Fill the peppers three quarters full and close them with their tops.','Stand them upright, packed tightly in a pot, cover with tomato purée let down with water and add the bay.','Simmer covered for 1 hour, then thicken the sauce with a roux of lard, flour and paprika stirred in at the last moment.']::text[]),
('peka-dalmate','en','Dalmatian peka','Dubrovnik, Dalmatia, Croatia','Meat and vegetables cooked under a cast-iron bell buried in embers. You must order it a day ahead in Dalmatian konobas: two hours of slow cooking, and the bell is lifted only once.',ARRAY['Joint the chicken into eight and marinate for 2 hours in oil, crushed garlic and rosemary.','Cut all the vegetables into large pieces: small ones would collapse over two hours.','Arrange meat and vegetables in a wide cast-iron dish in a single layer, pour over the white wine and oil.','Cover with a bell or a heavy lid and bake for 1 hour at 220°C.','Take off the lid, stir once only and continue for 45 minutes uncovered to brown everything.']::text[]),
('crni-rizot','en','Black cuttlefish risotto','Rovinj, Istria, Croatia','An ink-black risotto, coloured by the cuttlefish''s own sacs. It stains the lips and teeth of everyone who eats it, which is part of the pleasure on the Istrian quaysides.',ARRAY['Clean the cuttlefish, keeping the ink sacs aside, and cut the flesh into strips.','Sear them hard in oil with the garlic, then lower the heat and let them release their water for 15 minutes.','Add the onion and the rice, and toast for 2 minutes before deglazing with white wine.','Moisten ladle by ladle with hot broth, stirring, for 18 minutes; stir in the ink and tomato paste halfway through.','Off the heat, beat in the butter and parsley. The risotto should stay loose: neither dry nor soupy.']::text[]),
('zagorski-strukli','en','Zagorje štrukli','Zagreb, Croatia','Squares of stretched dough folded over fresh cheese, poached then baked under cream. Savoury at dinner, sweet for pudding: the same parcel, and people in Zagreb happily eat both versions on the same day.',ARRAY['Knead flour, warm water, one egg, the oil and salt for 12 minutes, then rest for an hour under a warm bowl.','Mix the drained fresh cheese with two eggs, half the sour cream and salt.','Stretch the dough by hand on a cloth until translucent, brush with melted butter and spread the cheese.','Roll it up, cut 8 cm squares pressing the edges, and poach them for 5 minutes in simmering water.','Set them in a buttered dish, pour over the remaining sour cream and the cream, and bake for 25 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('ajvar','en','Ajvar','Leskovac, Serbia','The ''red caviar'' of the Balkans: roasted peppers, peeled one by one, crushed and cooked for hours in oil. Making it is a collective autumn ritual, outdoors, in large copper cauldrons.',ARRAY['Roast the whole peppers in the oven or over embers until the skin blackens and blisters.','Shut them in a sealed bag for 20 minutes: the steam will lift the skin by itself.','Peel and seed them, then let them drain for an hour in a colander — water would ruin the cooking.','Roast the aubergines too, scoop out the flesh, then chop everything coarsely by knife.','Cook for 2 hours over low heat, stirring often and trickling in the oil; add the garlic, vinegar and sugar at the end. It is ready when the spoon leaves a furrow.']::text[]),
('pljeskavica','en','Pljeskavica','Niš, Serbia','A meat patty as wide as a plate, grilled and served in a lepinja bread that soaks up the juices. Nothing to do with the hamburger: it descends from the Ottoman kebab, and is eaten with kajmak and ajvar.',ARRAY['Mix the two meats with grated onion, garlic, paprika, bicarbonate and sparkling water.','Work the mixture by hand for 10 minutes until smooth and tacky, then rest it overnight in the fridge.','Shape wide patties 1.5 cm thick and 15 cm across, wetting your hands so they do not stick.','Grill over lively embers for 6 minutes a side, never pressing the meat.','Slide each patty into a warm flatbread, spread with kajmak and top with raw sliced onion.']::text[]),
('shopska-salata','en','Shopska salad','Plovdiv, Bulgaria','White, green and red: the colours of the Bulgarian flag on a plate, and no accident — it was invented in the 1950s by the state tourist board. The grated sirene forms a thick snow on top.',ARRAY['Grill the peppers until the skin blackens, peel them and cut into strips.','Cut the tomatoes and cucumber into one-centimetre dice, slice the onion finely.','Toss with the chopped parsley, oil, vinegar, salt and pepper.','Let it stand for 10 minutes so the vegetables give up a little juice.','Grate the salted white cheese over the top in a thick even layer, without mixing it in: it must stay on the surface.']::text[]),
('tarator','en','Tarator','Varna, Bulgaria','A cold soup of yogurt, cucumber, dill and walnuts, served with ice cubes in the glass. In Bulgaria it is drunk more than eaten, in summer, when the Black Sea heat puts you off everything else.',ARRAY['Grate the cucumber coarsely, salt it and let it weep for 10 minutes; do not squeeze it.','Pound the garlic with a little salt and half the walnuts into a paste.','Whisk the yogurt with this paste, then let it down with iced water to the consistency of a light soup.','Fold in the cucumber and chopped dill, adjust the salt and chill for an hour.','Serve very cold with ice cubes, a thread of olive oil and the rest of the walnuts, crushed.']::text[]),
('kavarma','en','Kavarma','Veliko Tarnovo, Bulgaria','A stew of pork and peppers, simmered then served in an individual clay pot, often with an egg cracked on top. The earthenware keeps it scalding from oven to table.',ARRAY['Sear the pork in large cubes in the lard until well coloured, then set aside.','Soften the onion and sliced peppers in the same fat, without rushing them.','Add the mushrooms, let them release their water, then return the meat.','Pour in the wine, crushed tomatoes, paprika and savory; cover and simmer for 1 hour.','Divide between clay pots, crack an egg onto each and bake for 12 minutes at 200°C, until the white sets.']::text[]),
('fergese','en','Fërgesë of Berat','Berat, Albania','Peppers, tomatoes and fresh cheese melted together in an earthenware dish into an orange-red cream. You dip bread straight into it, and that is the whole meal on a Berat evening.',ARRAY['Grill the peppers until the skin blisters, peel them and cut into strips.','Melt the butter, add the flour and cook for 2 minutes, then pour in the milk for a short béchamel.','Add the crushed tomatoes and garlic and reduce for 10 minutes over a lively flame.','Fold in the peppers and both cheeses crushed with a fork, without smoothing: the dish should stay lumpy.','Pour into an earthenware dish and bake for 15 minutes at 200°C, until the surface browns in patches. Serve with country bread.']::text[]),
('pastrmajlija','en','Pastrmajlija','Štip, North Macedonia','A long boat of dough topped with diced salt pork and an egg cracked over it at the end. Its name comes from pastrma, the dried meat kept for winter; Štip devotes a festival to it each autumn.',ARRAY['Salt the diced pork generously and refrigerate for 12 hours: this salting stands in for the pastrma.','Knead flour, warm water, yeast, oil and salt, and prove for 1½ hours.','Roll the dough into long ovals, raising the edges to hold the juices.','Scatter over the rinsed and dried meat, dust with paprika and bake for 15 minutes at 250°C.','Crack an egg into the centre of each pastrmajlija and return for 4 minutes. Serve with a grilled chilli.']::text[]),
('halaszle','en','Halászlé','Szeged, Hungary','The fishermen''s soup of the Tisza, red with paprika and cooked in a cauldron over a wood fire. In Szeged the broth of small fish is strained before the good carp pieces are poached in it: two cookings for one dish.',ARRAY['Fillet the carp and set the fillets aside; keep the head, bones and trimmings for the broth.','Cook these trimmings for 45 minutes with half the sliced onions, never stirring with a spoon: you shake the pot instead.','Pass the broth through a fine sieve, pressing hard, then return it to the heat.','Add the sweet and hot paprika off the heat — it would scorch and turn bitter — then the pepper and tomato.','Salt the fish, slide the pieces into the simmering broth and poach for 12 minutes without touching them.']::text[]),
('langos','en','Lángos','Budapest, Hungary','A round of risen dough thrown into hot fat, rubbed with garlic and heaped with sour cream and grated cheese. Once baked against the wall of the bread oven; the fryer made it the staple of every Budapest swimming pool.',ARRAY['Mash the cooked potato and work it into the flour: it is what keeps the dough soft for hours.','Dissolve the yeast and sugar in the warm milk, pour onto the flour with the salt and knead for 10 minutes.','Prove for 1½ hours, until the dough has doubled.','Stretch each piece by hand into an uneven 20 cm disc, thinner at the centre than at the edge.','Fried 90 seconds a side at 180°C, they balloon. Rub with crushed garlic and cover with sour cream and grated cheese.']::text[]),
('paprikas-csirke','en','Chicken paprikash','Kalocsa, Hungary','A great deal of onion, a great deal of Kalocsa paprika, a little sour cream: the simplest Hungarian dish and the hardest to spoil. The absolute rule is never to boil the cream once it is in.',ARRAY['Soften the finely sliced onions in the lard for 20 minutes, until they collapse into a golden purée.','Take the pan off the heat, stir in the paprika at once: above 120°C it turns bitter.','Return to the heat, add the chicken, pepper and tomato, pour in broth to come halfway up and cover.','Simmer for 45 minutes; the sauce should coat without any flour in it yet.','Slacken the sour cream with the flour and a ladle of warm sauce, stir into the pan off the heat and never let it boil again.']::text[]),
('dobos-torta','en','Dobos torte','Budapest, Hungary','Five discs of sponge, a chocolate buttercream, and a lid of hard caramel cut into wedges while still hot. József Dobos presented it in 1885 and refused to give up the recipe for twenty years.',ARRAY['Whip the whites, fold in the yolks beaten with 120 g of sugar, then the sifted flour.','Spread the batter into five thin 22 cm discs on baking paper and bake each for 7 minutes at 200°C.','Make the cream: cook a custard with the yolks, milk and 100 g of sugar, cool it, then beat it with the softened butter and melted chocolate.','Build the cake, alternating discs and cream, keeping the most even disc back for the caramel.','Make a pale caramel with the remaining sugar, coat the reserved disc and cut it into ten wedges with an oiled blade before it sets. Fan them over the cake.']::text[]),
('lecso','en','Lecsó','Debrecen, Hungary','Peppers, tomatoes, onion and lard cooked down until everything collapses. Made by the basin in August, when peppers cost nothing, and frozen for winter. With or without egg, with or without sausage: everyone has a version.',ARRAY['Slice the onions and soften them in the lard for 15 minutes without colouring.','Add the smoked sausage in rounds and let it render its fragrant fat.','Off the heat stir in the paprika, then return to the heat with the peppers cut into wide strips.','Cover and sweat for 15 minutes, then add the peeled tomatoes and continue uncovered for 15 minutes.','Crack the eggs straight into the lecsó and barely scramble them: they should stay in visible ribbons.']::text[]),
('bigos','en','Bigos','Warsaw, Poland','The ''hunter''s stew'': sauerkraut, fresh cabbage, several meats and dried plums, stewed then reheated three days running. Mickiewicz gives it twenty lines in Pan Tadeusz — which says something.',ARRAY['Rinse the sauerkraut if it is very sour, squeeze it dry and chop it; shred the fresh cabbage.','Fry the bacon and onion in the lard, then brown the pork in large cubes and the sausage in rounds.','Bring everything together in a large pot with the soaked mushrooms, prunes, wine, tomato paste and spices.','Simmer for 3 hours over very low heat, stirring now and then so nothing catches.','Let it cool, then reheat it the next day and the day after: bigos only becomes itself on the third day.']::text[]),
('zurek','en','Żurek','Kraków, Poland','A sour soup made from rye starter fermented for five days, served at Easter with white sausage and a hard-boiled egg. The sourness comes from a household lactic ferment once kept in a stoneware crock.',ARRAY['Five days ahead, mix the rye flour, warm water and two garlic cloves in a jar; cover with a cloth and ferment at room temperature.','On the day, make a broth with the bacon, onion, bay and allspice for 40 minutes.','Poach the white sausages in it for 20 minutes, lift them out and cut into rounds.','Shake the starter, pour it into the broth in a thin stream and cook for 10 minutes without boiling: it clears and thickens the soup.','Add cream, marjoram, horseradish and crushed garlic. Serve with the sausage, half a hard-boiled egg and potatoes.']::text[]),
('barszcz-czerwony','en','Red barszcz','Poznań, Poland','A beetroot broth as clear as wine, served scalding in a cup on Christmas Eve with tiny mushroom dumplings. Nothing like Ukrainian borscht: this one is strained, limpid, almost a drink.',ARRAY['Soak the dried mushrooms for an hour and carefully keep their liquid.','Roast half the beetroot whole for 50 minutes: this is what gives the broth its depth.','Boil the other half, grated, with the vegetables, spices and mushroom liquid for 45 minutes over low heat.','Strain very carefully through a cloth, without pressing, to keep it clear.','Balance it sweet-sour with the vinegar and sugar, add the garlic and marjoram, and serve scalding in cups.']::text[]),
('kotlet-schabowy','en','Kotlet schabowy','Warsaw, Poland','The Sunday breaded pork chop of Poland, served with dill potatoes and a cabbage salad. It is supposed to hang over the edge of the plate: that is a criterion, not an exaggeration.',ARRAY['Cut the loin into 2 cm slices and beat them out to 8 mm, working from the centre outwards.','Season, then coat in flour, beaten egg and breadcrumbs, without pressing.','Heat the lard half a centimetre deep and fry the cutlets 4 minutes a side over medium heat.','Shake the pan as they cook: the fat should run under the crumb and puff it up.','Serve at once with dill potatoes and a cabbage salad dressed with cream and lemon.']::text[]),
('oscypek-grille','en','Grilled oscypek with cranberry','Zakopane, Tatras, Poland','A smoked ewe''s-milk cheese pressed in carved wooden spindle moulds, grilled until it blisters and served with cranberry jam. Its making is protected and reserved to Tatra shepherds.',ARRAY['Slice the smoked cheese lengthways into pieces a centimetre thick.','Oil the grill very lightly — the cheese sticks easily — and prepare medium embers, never fierce ones.','Grill 3 minutes a side: the surface should stripe and swell without the centre running.','Take it off the moment the edges start to sag: a few seconds more and the cheese pours into the fire.','Serve scalding with a spoonful of cranberry jam and toasted country bread.']::text[]),
('paczki','en','Pączki','Toruń, Poland','The doughnuts of Fat Thursday, filled with rose jam or plum. Tradition says you must eat at least one that day or face a year of bad luck: Poles get through a hundred million of them.',ARRAY['Make a starter with the yeast, warm milk and a little flour; let it froth for 20 minutes.','Whisk the yolks with the sugar to the ribbon, then knead with the flour, starter, rum and salt for 15 minutes.','Work in the soft butter last and prove for 1½ hours: the dough should be very slack.','Shape 60 g balls, fill each with a spoonful of jam and seal carefully; prove for 40 minutes.','Fry 2 minutes a side at 175°C, covered for the first side: that is what makes the pale ring around the middle.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('vepro-knedlo-zelo','en','Vepřo knedlo zelo','Plzeň, Bohemia, Czechia','The Czech national dish comes in three words: roast pork, bread dumplings sliced with a thread, sweet-sour caraway cabbage. Eaten with a Plzeň beer, which is not optional.',ARRAY['Rub the pork with salt, crushed garlic and caraway, set it on a bed of onions and pour over the beer.','Roast for 2 hours at 160°C, basting regularly, then 15 minutes at 220°C for the crackling.','For the knedlíky, knead flour, warm milk, yeast, eggs and salt, work in the diced bread rolls, and prove for an hour.','Shape two large logs and poach them for 20 minutes in simmering water, turning halfway. Slice them with a thread, never a knife.','Fry the sauerkraut with onion, caraway and sugar for 30 minutes, and serve everything under the roasting juices.']::text[]),
('kulajda','en','Kulajda','České Budějovice, South Bohemia','A white soup of potato and mushroom, sharpened with sour cream and vinegar, with a poached egg set in it. The dill is heavy-handed — that is what tells kulajda apart from any other Bohemian soup.',ARRAY['Cook the diced potatoes in a litre of salted water with the caraway and bay.','Fry the sliced mushrooms in butter and add them to the pan halfway through.','Slacken the sour cream with the flour, stir it into the soup in a thin stream and thicken for 5 minutes without boiling.','Poach the eggs separately in vinegared water for 3 minutes, yolks running.','Sharpen the soup with vinegar, sugar and a great deal of chopped dill, then set a poached egg in each bowl.']::text[]),
('trdelnik','en','Trdelník','Prague, Czechia','A ribbon of dough wound around a wooden roller, turned over embers and rolled in sugar and nuts. It came from Transylvania in the 18th century and conquered the streets of Prague — to the irritation of Praguers.',ARRAY['Make a starter with the yeast, warm milk and a spoonful of sugar; let it froth for 15 minutes.','Knead with the flour, eggs, 60 g of sugar, the soft butter and salt for 12 minutes, then prove for an hour.','Cut 2 cm strips and wind them in a spiral around buttered wooden rollers, edges just touching.','Brush with melted butter, roll in sugar and turn over the embers for 12 minutes, never stopping.','While still hot, roll in the sugar, crushed walnut and cinnamon mixture, then slide the cylinder off the roller.']::text[]),
('bramborak','en','Bramborák','Brno, Moravia, Czechia','A cake of grated potato with garlic and marjoram, fried in lard until brittle. Sold at village fairs with nothing but a scrap of paper to hold it.',ARRAY['Grate the potatoes finely and let them drain in a colander for five minutes, without pressing.','Mix with the eggs, flour, milk, crushed garlic, marjoram and caraway.','Salt only at the last moment; the batter should stay thick and barely pourable.','Heat the lard and drop in flattened spoonfuls 12 cm across; let them set for 4 minutes untouched.','Turn, brown for 3 minutes on the other side and drain on a rack. Eaten scalding, with your fingers.']::text[]),
('kapustnica','en','Kapustnica','Košice, Slovakia','The sauerkraut soup of the Slovak Christmas Eve, with smoked sausage, dried mushrooms and prunes. Every family has its own; some add cream, others call that sacrilege.',ARRAY['Soak the mushrooms for an hour and keep the soaking liquid.','Bring the smoked ribs to a simmer in two litres of water with the bay and allspice for 45 minutes.','Add the rinsed and squeezed sauerkraut, the mushrooms with their liquid, the prunes and the sliced sausage; continue for 30 minutes.','Make a pale roux of lard, flour and paprika off the heat, and stir it into the soup to thicken it.','Pull the meat from the bones, return it, adjust the seasoning and serve with a spoonful of sour cream — or not, depending on your camp.']::text[]),
('mamaliga-branza','en','Mămăligă with cheese and cream','Sighetu Marmației, Maramureș, Romania','Romanian polenta, poured onto a board and cut with a thread, served with salted sheep''s cheese, cream and an egg. It fed the countryside for two centuries, when wheat bread was a luxury.',ARRAY['Bring the salted water to the boil and rain in the cornmeal, whisking constantly.','Lower the heat and stir with a wooden spoon for 30 minutes, until the mass pulls away from the sides.','Turn the mămăligă out onto a damp wooden board and let it firm for two minutes before cutting it with a thread.','Fry the diced bacon and cook the eggs in the rendered fat.','Serve the mămăligă with the crumbled cheese, sour cream, egg and crisp bacon.']::text[]),
('ciorba-de-burta','en','Ciorbă de burtă','Bucharest, Romania','A white, sharp tripe soup thickened with egg yolk and cream, lifted with garlic and vinegar at the table. Romanians hold it to be the sovereign cure for a difficult morning after.',ARRAY['Clean the tripe thoroughly with coarse salt and vinegar, rinse it well, then blanch it for 10 minutes.','Cook it for 2½ hours with the veal foot, whole vegetables, bay and peppercorns, until tender to a fork.','Lift out the tripe, cut it into fine strips and strain the broth.','Pound the garlic with a little salt and a little broth, and stir this liquor into the soup.','Beat the yolks with the cream, loosen with a ladle of hot broth and stir in off the heat. Serve with vinegar and chilli on the side.']::text[]),
('mici','en','Mici','Bucharest, Romania','Small skinless meat rolls, made springy by bicarbonate and sparkling water, grilled wherever there are embers in Romania. Eaten with mustard and bread, never with cutlery.',ARRAY['Pound the garlic with a little salt and a little broth to draw out a fragrant liquid.','Mix the meats, spices, bicarbonate, garlic liquid and broth, then work the mass for 10 minutes.','Add the sparkling water little by little: it is what gives the characteristic springiness.','Rest overnight in the fridge, then pipe or shape 8 cm logs with wet hands.','Grill 6 to 8 minutes over lively embers, turning often. Serve with mustard and bread.']::text[]),
('papanasi','en','Papanași','Iași, Romanian Moldavia','A fresh cheese doughnut shaped like a ring, capped with its own little ball, drowned in sour cream and blueberry jam. The Romanian restaurant dessert par excellence, served scalding.',ARRAY['Drain the fresh cheese for an hour in a cloth: too wet and the dough would drink the oil.','Mix it with the eggs, sugar, semolina, zest and vanilla, then add the flour and baking powder without overworking.','Rest for 30 minutes, then shape four 10 cm rings and four small balls.','Fry 3 minutes a side at 165°C — no hotter, or the outside browns before the centre cooks.','Stack the ring, sour cream, jam and then the ball, and serve at once.']::text[]),
('kibinai','en','Kibinai','Trakai, Lithuania','Mutton turnovers brought in the 14th century by the Crimean Karaites, settled at Trakai by Grand Duke Vytautas. Six centuries on, the community numbers only a few dozen; the kibinai, meanwhile, are all over Lithuania.',ARRAY['Rub the cold butter into the flour, add the sour cream, one egg and the salt, and bring together without kneading. Chill for an hour.','Chop the mutton by knife into small dice — never in a processor, the texture would be lost.','Mix with finely chopped onion, the fat, salt and plenty of pepper.','Roll the pastry, cut 14 cm discs, fill them and seal the edge in a plaited crimp.','Pierce a small hole on top, pour in a spoonful of broth, glaze with egg and bake for 35 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('sklandrausis','en','Sklandrausis','Kuldīga, Courland, Latvia','A rye tartlet layered with potato then carrot, sweetened with honey and scented with caraway. It comes from the Livonians, a Finno-Ugric people of the Latvian coast whose language has almost no speakers left.',ARRAY['Knead the rye flour, melted butter, warm water and salt: the dough is short and does not rise.','Roll it thin and line 10 cm rings, raising the edges by hand like a basket.','Mash the boiled potatoes with a little cream, salt and caraway.','Do the same with the boiled carrots, adding the honey and one egg.','Spread the potato first, then the carrot, glaze with egg and bake for 30 minutes at 200°C. Eaten cold.']::text[]),
('kama-dessert','en','Kama with buttermilk','Tallinn, Estonia','A flour of four roasted grains — rye, barley, oat and pea — beaten into buttermilk and sweetened. Once travelling food that kept for months, it is now the childhood taste of every Estonian.',ARRAY['Whisk the buttermilk with the sour cream until perfectly smooth.','Rain in the kama flour, whisking without pause: tipped in at once it forms lumps you will never recover from.','Sweeten with the sugar and honey, and add a pinch of salt that wakes up the roasted grain.','Rest for 30 minutes in the fridge: the kama swells and the mixture thickens by itself.','Serve very cold in glasses, with sliced strawberries at the bottom and a thread of honey.']::text[]),
('verivorst','en','Verivorst','Pärnu, Estonia','The Estonian Christmas blood sausage, made with pearl barley rather than onion alone, served with lingonberry jam and braised cabbage. It is waited for all year: it appears in shops only from December.',ARRAY['Cook the pearl barley in salted water for 25 minutes, then drain and let it cool a little.','Render the diced fat and turn the finely chopped onions golden in it.','Mix the barley, onions, strained blood, marjoram, allspice, salt and plenty of pepper.','Fill the casings loosely — the barley swells further — and poach for 25 minutes in water at 80°C, pricking any bubbles.','Brown them in the oven for 20 minutes at 200°C and serve with lingonberry jam and butter-braised sauerkraut.']::text[]),
('varenyky','en','Cherry varenyky','Poltava, Ukraine','Dumplings of soft dough filled with sour cherries, served with cream and sugar. Savoury with potato the rest of the year, sweet in July when the cherry trees of Poltava are heavy with fruit.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, warm water, egg and salt for 10 minutes into a supple dough; rest for 40 minutes under a cloth.','Stone the cherries and let them macerate for an hour with half the sugar; keep the juice.','Drain them and roll them in the starch: without it the juice would pierce the dough as it cooks.','Roll the dough to 2 mm, cut 7 cm discs, fill with three cherries each and seal carefully.','Poach for 4 minutes in simmering water, drain, toss in butter and serve with the sour cream, sugar and reduced juice.']::text[]),
('salo-lviv','en','Garlic salo','Lviv, Ukraine','Back fat cured for a week in coarse salt and garlic, sliced thinly onto rye bread. Far more than a food in Ukraine: a national symbol, to which Lviv devotes an entire museum.',ARRAY['Choose thick back fat with a fine rind and pearly white flesh, with no streak of lean.','Stud it with slivers of garlic all over, at regular intervals.','Rub it hard with a mixture of coarse salt, cracked pepper, crumbled bay and coriander.','Wrap it in a cloth and leave it a week in the fridge, turning it daily.','Rinse, pat dry, roll in paprika and keep it in the freezer: it only slices paper-thin when very cold. Serve on rye bread with raw onion.']::text[]),
('banosh','en','Banosh','Ivano-Frankivsk, Carpathians, Ukraine','The polenta of Hutsul shepherds, cooked not in water but in sour cream, until the butter rises to the surface. Served with brynza and pork scratchings; tradition holds that a man should stir it.',ARRAY['Bring the salted sour cream to a bare simmer in a cauldron, never letting it boil properly.','Rain in the cornmeal very finely, stirring in one direction and always the same one.','Stir without pause for 25 minutes: the mass thickens then, suddenly, beads of butter rise to the surface. That is the sign.','Take it off the heat at once: a few minutes more and the banosh grains irretrievably.','Serve in the pot with the crumbled brynza, the scratchings and the fried mushrooms.']::text[]),
('kotleta-po-kyivsky','en','Chicken Kyiv','Kyiv, Ukraine','A chicken breast rolled around herb butter, breaded and fried: when cut, the melted butter should spurt out. The whole difficulty lies there — sealing it well enough that it does not escape before the plate.',ARRAY['Beat the soft butter with the chopped herbs, crushed garlic, lemon zest and salt; shape four logs and freeze them hard.','Butterfly the breasts and flatten them between two sheets to 5 mm, without piercing.','Set a frozen butter log at the centre, fold in the sides then roll tightly; wrap and firm up for 30 minutes in the fridge.','Bread them twice — flour, egg, crumb, then egg and crumb again: the double coat is what holds the butter.','Fry for 8 minutes at 165°C, then 8 minutes in the oven at 180°C. Serve immediately, knife at the ready.']::text[]),
('machanka','en','Machanka with blini','Hrodna, Belarus','A creamy pork stew into which large buckwheat pancakes are dipped — machatsi means ''to dip''. The Belarusian Sunday breakfast, which sees you through to the evening.',ARRAY['Brown the pork in large dice and the sausage in the lard, add the onion and let it soften.','Moisten with a glass of water, add the bay and simmer covered for 35 minutes.','Slake two spoonfuls of flour in the sour cream, stir it into the pot and thicken for 5 minutes without boiling.','For the pancakes, mix both flours, the warm milk, eggs, yeast and salt; prove for 40 minutes.','Cook large thick pancakes in a pan and serve them beside the stew, for dipping. Scatter with dill.']::text[]),
('zeama','en','Zeamă','Chișinău, Moldova','A hen broth with home-made noodles, soured with borș — a fermented wheat bran liquor, not Russian beetroot. Lovage is compulsory: without it, Moldovans say, it is not a zeamă.',ARRAY['Bring the hen to a bare simmer in three litres of cold water and skim patiently for twenty minutes.','Add the whole carrot, onion and pepper, and cook for an hour without boiling.','Meanwhile knead the flour and eggs into a hard dough, roll it very thin and cut fine noodles; let them dry on a cloth.','Lift out the bird, bone it, strain the broth and return it to a simmer with the crushed tomatoes.','Drop in the noodles for 5 minutes, return the meat, sharpen with vinegar and cover with chopped lovage off the heat.']::text[]),
('gravlax','en','Gravlax','Stockholm, Sweden','Raw salmon pressed for three days between salt, sugar and dill. The name means ''buried salmon'': medieval fishermen sank it in the sand above the tide line and let it ferment.',ARRAY['Choose a skin-on fillet, pin-boned, and run your hand against the grain to check nothing is left.','Mix the salt, sugar and cracked pepper; rub it into the flesh and sprinkle over the akvavit.','Cover with a thick layer of roughly chopped dill and lay the fillet skin down in a dish.','Wrap, weight it with two kilos and leave three days in the fridge, turning morning and evening and pouring off the brine.','Rinse barely, pat dry and slice on the bias, very thinly. Serve with a mustard, honey and vinegar sauce and rye bread.']::text[]),
('janssons-frestelse','en','Jansson’s temptation','Uppsala, Sweden','A gratin of matchstick potatoes, onion and pickled sprats, drowned in cream. The name is said to come from a Swedish preacher who abandoned his asceticism at the sight of it — the story is probably false and much retold.',ARRAY['Cut the potatoes into fine matchsticks, but never rinse them: their starch is what binds the gratin.','Soften the sliced onions in the butter for 15 minutes without colouring them.','Layer potatoes, onions and sprat fillets in the dish, finishing with potatoes.','Pour over half the cream and the milk, keeping the rest for halfway through.','Scatter breadcrumbs, bake for 50 minutes at 200°C, add the remaining cream partway and let it brown.']::text[]),
('toast-skagen','en','Toast Skagen','Gothenburg, Sweden','North Sea prawns bound with mayonnaise and dill, piled on buttered toast and crowned with fish roe. Created in 1956 by the restaurateur Tore Wretman, aboard his boat.',ARRAY['Peel the prawns and keep them cold; pat them thoroughly dry.','Mix the mayonnaise, sour cream, mustard, lemon zest and juice, chopped dill and finely diced red onion.','Fold in the prawns gently with a spatula, without crushing them. Adjust the salt.','Fry the bread slices golden in foaming butter on both sides and blot them.','Mound the mixture on each toast, top with a spoonful of salmon roe and a frond of dill.']::text[]),
('kanelbullar','en','Kanelbullar','Malmö, Sweden','The cinnamon buns of fika, the Swedish break that is not a coffee break but an institution. The cardamom in the dough is what sets them apart from every other rolled bun in the world.',ARRAY['Warm the milk to 37°C, dissolve the yeast in it, add 100 g of sugar, freshly ground cardamom and the salt.','Work in the flour then 100 g of soft butter, knead for 12 minutes and prove for 45 minutes.','Roll the dough into a 5 mm rectangle and spread with the remaining softened butter mixed with sugar and cinnamon.','Fold in three, cut 2 cm strips, twist them and knot them on themselves; prove for 45 minutes.','Glaze with egg, scatter pearl sugar and bake for 12 minutes at 225°C. Take them out the moment they turn golden: five minutes more and they dry out.']::text[]),
('raggmunk','en','Raggmunk','Linköping, Östergötland, Sweden','A pancake of grated potato in a milk batter, served with crisp bacon and lingonberry jam. Sweet, salt and sour in the same mouthful: the complete Swedish logic.',ARRAY['Whisk the flour, milk and eggs to a smooth batter and rest it for 20 minutes.','Grate the potatoes coarsely at the very last moment, without rinsing, and fold them into the batter.','Season and work quickly: the batter darkens if it waits.','Cook wide pancakes in the butter, 4 minutes a side, until the edges turn lacy and brittle.','Grill the bacon separately and serve it on the pancakes, with lingonberry jam alongside.']::text[]),
('surstromming','en','Surströmming','Ulvöhamn, High Coast, Sweden','Herring fermented for six months in a weak brine, whose tin bulges and whose smell defies description. Opened outdoors, under water, and eaten in late summer on flatbread with potato and onion.',ARRAY['Open the tin outdoors and submerged in a bucket of water, without exception: pressure has built up during fermentation.','Rinse the fillets in cold water, remove skin and bones, then pat them dry.','Boil small yellow-fleshed potatoes and crush them roughly with a fork.','Slice the red onion very finely and stir the chives and dill into the sour cream.','Butter the flatbread, top with potato, onion, a piece of herring and a spoonful of cream. Roll and eat at once.']::text[]),
('rakfisk','en','Rakfisk','Fagernes, Valdres, Norway','Trout fermented for two or three months in cold brine, eaten raw in thin slices. Valdres farms prepared it in autumn to last until spring; Fagernes devotes a November festival to it.',ARRAY['Lift the fish from its brine, rinse it briefly and let it drain for an hour on a rack.','Fillet it and remove the skin in one movement, starting from the tail.','Slice on the bias into 3 mm strips, with a long thin blade.','Boil the potatoes in salted water and let them cool a little.','Serve on buttered flatbread with crushed potato, raw onion, sour cream, dill and a turn of pepper.']::text[]),
('kjottkaker','en','Kjøttkaker','Bergen, Norway','Large beef patties with nutmeg and ginger, served in their brown gravy with peas, potatoes and lingonberry jam. Not the Swedish köttbullar: these are bigger, plainer, more rustic.',ARRAY['Work the minced beef with the salt and starch until it turns tacky — it is the salt that binds, not egg.','Beat in the very cold milk in three additions, then the grated onion, nutmeg and ginger.','Shape large oval patties the size of your palm and brown them in butter for 4 minutes a side.','Lift them out, make a brown roux with the remaining butter and the flour, whisk in the broth and thicken for 5 minutes.','Return the patties to the gravy and simmer covered for 25 minutes. Serve with peas, potatoes and lingonberry jam.']::text[]),
('brunost-vafler','en','Waffles with brunost','Voss, Norway','Heart-shaped waffles, soft rather than crisp, served with brunost — the brown caramel-tasting cheese made by boiling down whey until its lactose browns.',ARRAY['Whisk the eggs and sugar, then stir in the sour cream and warm milk.','Add the flour, baking powder, cardamom and salt without overworking, then the melted butter.','Rest for an hour: the batter relaxes and the waffles come out softer for it.','Cook in a hot heart-shaped waffle iron for about 3 minutes, to a pale gold rather than brown.','Stack them under a cloth — they should stay soft — and serve with thin slices of brunost shaved off with a cheese plane, and jam.']::text[]),
('skrei-poche','en','Poached skrei','Svolvær, Lofoten, Norway','The migratory Barents cod that comes to spawn in the Lofoten from January to April. Its flesh, firmed by two thousand kilometres of swimming in icy water, flakes into large pearly leaves. Barely poached, in heavily salted water.',ARRAY['Bring three litres of water to a bare simmer with the coarse salt: it should be frankly briny, almost unpleasant to taste.','Cut the cod into thick skin-on steaks, leaving the bones in.','Slide them into the barely trembling water and turn off the heat: they cook in 8 minutes of residual warmth.','Melt the butter until it just turns nutty and stir in the crisp bacon and chopped hard-boiled egg.','Serve the fish with steamed potatoes and carrots, the hot butter poured over, and a spoonful of lingonberry.']::text[]),
('flaeskesteg','en','Flæskesteg','Aarhus, Denmark','The Danish Christmas pork roast, whose rind must become a chequerboard of crackling hard enough to snap. A roast with soft rind is considered a failure, and there is no arguing about it.',ARRAY['Score the rind every 5 mm, through to the fat but not into the meat — ask the butcher if need be.','Rub coarse salt into every cut and push in pieces of bay leaf and cloves.','Set the roast on a rack over a dish of water, the rind perfectly horizontal: that is the condition for even crackling.','Roast for 1½ hours at 160°C, then raise to 220°C for 25 minutes, watching constantly.','Meanwhile braise the red cabbage for an hour with the vinegar and sugar. Serve with potatoes and a gravy thickened with the juices.']::text[]),
('frikadeller','en','Frikadeller','Odense, Funen, Denmark','The flat patties every Dane ate weekly as a child, shaped with a spoon rather than by hand. Served hot with potatoes, or cold on rye bread the next day.',ARRAY['Combine the meats, grated onion, egg, flour, salt and pepper, and beat the mixture with a wooden spoon for 5 minutes.','Add the milk little by little until the mixture is soft but still holds on a spoon.','Rest for 30 minutes in the fridge: that rest is what gives frikadeller their texture.','Scoop with a tablespoon dipped in water and drop into hot butter, flattening slightly.','Cook 6 minutes each side over medium heat. Serve with potatoes, red cabbage and gherkins.']::text[]),
('stegt-flaesk','en','Stegt flæsk','Aalborg, Jutland, Denmark','Slices of pork belly grilled until brittle, served with potatoes and a parsley sauce. Voted Denmark''s national dish in 2014 by popular ballot, ahead of all the others.',ARRAY['Have the belly sliced 5 mm thick, rind on: any thicker and it will never crisp.','Lay the slices flat on a tray, salt them and roast for 25 minutes at 220°C without turning.','Boil the potatoes in salted water, in their skins.','Make a white roux with the butter and flour, whisk in the hot milk and cook for 8 minutes.','Off the heat, add a great deal of finely chopped parsley and the nutmeg. The sauce should turn properly green.']::text[]),
('rodgrod-med-flode','en','Rødgrød med fløde','Roskilde, Denmark','The red fruit compote with cream whose name serves as a pronunciation test for foreigners — three silent d''s, two ø''s, and the certainty of getting it wrong. The Danish summer pudding, served warm or chilled.',ARRAY['Cook the redcurrants with the water and sugar for 10 minutes, then pass through a sieve to remove skins and seeds.','Return the juice to the heat, add the raspberries and strawberries and simmer for barely 3 minutes.','Slake the potato starch in a little cold water and pour it in off the heat.','Return to low heat for one minute only: potato starch loses its thickening power if boiled.','Pour into a dish, dust with a little sugar to stop a skin forming, cool and serve with cold cream and flaked almonds.']::text[]),
('wienerbrod','en','Wienerbrød','Copenhagen, Denmark','The ''Vienna bread'' that the rest of the world calls a danish. It arrived in 1850 with Austrian bakers hired during a strike; the Danes added butter, egg and remonce, and never gave it back.',ARRAY['Briefly knead the flour, warm milk, yeast, one egg, sugar, cardamom and salt; chill for an hour.','Flatten 250 g of cold butter into a square between two sheets, enclose it in the dough and give three single turns, resting 30 minutes between each.','Make the remonce by beating the marzipan with the remaining butter and a little sugar.','Roll out to 4 mm, cut 10 cm squares, fill with remonce, pastry cream or jam, and fold two corners to the centre.','Prove for 45 minutes, glaze with egg and bake for 18 minutes at 200°C. Ice them and scatter with chopped almonds.']::text[]),
('kalakukko','en','Kalakukko','Kuopio, Savonia, Finland','A rye loaf sealed around whole small fish and pork fat, baked for five hours in a slow oven until the bones dissolve. The crust serves as the vessel: you cut into it like a lid.',ARRAY['Knead the flours, warm water, melted butter and salt into a dense, unelastic dough; rest for 30 minutes.','Gut and scale the small fish but keep them whole, bones included: they will dissolve during baking.','Roll the dough into a thick oval, lay a layer of salted fish, a layer of pork fat, then fish again.','Close the dough, sealing the edges carefully: the smallest leak would dry out the inside.','Bake for 1 hour at 200°C to set the crust, brush with butter, wrap in paper and continue for 4 hours at 150°C.']::text[]),
('lohikeitto','en','Lohikeitto','Turku, Finland','A creamy salmon soup with potato and leek, the dill going in right at the end. The salmon cooks for only eight minutes: that restraint is what keeps the pieces whole and melting.',ARRAY['Sweat the sliced leek and carrot in the butter, without colouring them.','Add the diced potatoes, the broth, bay and allspice; cook for 15 minutes.','Pour in the cream and bring to a bare simmer, without boiling.','Cut the salmon into large 3 cm cubes, salt them and slide them into the soup. Turn off the heat.','Let it poach for 8 minutes off the heat, then add a large handful of chopped dill and serve at once.']::text[]),
('korvapuusti','en','Korvapuusti','Helsinki, Finland','Literally ''a slap on the ear'': the Finnish cinnamon bun is cut on the slant then pressed down the middle with a finger, which gives it that flattened ear shape and its dense sweet heart.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in the warm milk, add 100 g of sugar, one egg, ground cardamom and the salt.','Work in the flour, knead for 10 minutes, then add 80 g of soft butter and knead 5 minutes more. Prove for 1 hour.','Roll into a large rectangle and spread with the remaining butter mixed with sugar and cinnamon.','Roll into a tight log and cut slices on the slant: wide on one side, narrow on the other.','Set them wide side down and press the centre with two fingers. Prove for 40 minutes, glaze, scatter pearl sugar and bake for 12 minutes at 225°C.']::text[]),
('poronkaristys','en','Poronkäristys','Rovaniemi, Lapland, Finland','Reindeer shaved into curls with a plane while still frozen, seared in butter then stewed in beer. Served with mashed potato and lingonberry jam, it is the dish of the whole Finnish North.',ARRAY['Plane the meat into very fine shavings while still frozen: thawed, it would be impossible to cut this way.','Sear it in small batches in very hot butter, without crowding the pan.','Add the sliced onion, let it soften, then pour in the beer and broth.','Add juniper berries and bay, cover and simmer for 25 minutes over low heat.','Serve on milky mashed potato, with lingonberry jam and gherkins.']::text[]),
('kjotsupa','en','Kjötsúpa','Akureyri, Iceland','The Icelandic lamb soup, with swede, carrot, cabbage and a little oatmeal to thicken it. The lamb tastes distinctive: Icelandic sheep roam entirely free in summer, over moors of wild thyme and angelica.',ARRAY['Cut the lamb on the bone into large pieces and bring it to a simmer in cold water.','Skim patiently for twenty minutes: this is what gives a clear broth.','Add the bay, peppercorns and thyme, and cook covered for an hour.','Add the swede and carrot in large dice, then twenty minutes later the potato, cabbage and leek.','Rain in the oats and give it another 15 minutes: they bind the broth without really thickening it. Salt at the end.']::text[]),
('hangikjot','en','Hangikjöt','Reykjavik, Iceland','The smoked Christmas lamb, hung for weeks over a fire of dried sheep dung and birch — Iceland having few trees to spare. Served cold in thin slices, or hot with a pea béchamel.',ARRAY['Rub the leg with salt, sugar, crushed juniper and bay, and dry-cure it for a week in the cold, turning daily.','Rinse, pat dry, then cold-smoke it for three days over birch — or buy it ready-smoked, which is what Icelanders do themselves.','Poach it for 2 hours in water at 80°C, never boiling, and let it cool completely in its liquor.','Make a béchamel with the butter, flour and milk, and fold in the cooked peas.','Slice the hangikjöt very thinly and serve cold on buttered flatbread, or hot with potatoes and béchamel.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('skerpikjot','en','Skerpikjøt','Tórshavn, Faroe Islands','Mutton wind-dried for nine months in a hjallur, a slatted shed beaten by the salt Atlantic air. Neither salt nor smoke: only wind, constant damp and time.',ARRAY['The leg must have dried for nine months: too soon and the meat stays soft and spoils; too long and it turns crumbly.','Cut away the outer crust, darker and harder, and keep it — it is eaten too, but separately.','Slice the meat against the grain, as thinly as you possibly can, with a well-sharpened knife.','Boil small potatoes in their skins and let them cool a little.','Serve on buttered rye bread with a potato and a glass of akvavit. Never cook this meat.']::text[]),
('lutefisk','en','Lutefisk','Trondheim, Norway','Stockfish rehydrated in a lye of wood ash, which swells and turns translucent as jelly. The method dates from the Middle Ages; it still divides Norwegians between Christmas devotion and frank horror.',ARRAY['Soak the stockfish for six days in cold water, changing it daily, then two days in a lightly alkaline solution, and finally two days in fresh water to desalt it.','The fish should have tripled in volume and turned translucent grey and gelatinous.','Lay the pieces skin down in a dish, salt generously and let them drain for 30 minutes.','Cover tightly and bake for 25 minutes at 200°C: it cooks in its own water, without adding a drop.','Serve at once — it collapses quickly — with crisp bacon, pea purée, potatoes, melted butter and flatbread with brunost.']::text[]),
('sunday-roast','en','Sunday roast with Yorkshire pudding','York, Yorkshire, England','The English Sunday ritual: a beef roast, potatoes crisped in the fat, and those popovers that billow in the oven. Yorkshire pudding was once served first, drenched in gravy, to fill people up before the meat.',ARRAY['Take the joint out two hours ahead and rub it with mustard, salt and pepper.','Sear it at 240°C for 20 minutes, then drop to 180°C and allow 15 minutes per 500 g for rare.','Make the Yorkshire batter: flour, eggs, milk and salt, whisked then rested for an hour. It should run like cream.','Get the beef dripping smoking in the tins at 220°C, pour the batter in all at once and do not open the oven for 20 minutes.','Rest the meat for 20 minutes, deglaze the roasting tin with red wine and broth for the gravy, and serve with the roast vegetables and horseradish.']::text[]),
('cornish-pasty','en','Cornish pasty','Truro, Cornwall, England','The tin miners'' pasty, whose plaited crimp served as a handle: held there with arsenic-covered fingers, the crest then thrown away. Beef, potato, swede and onion, never pre-cooked.',ARRAY['Rub the cold suet and butter into the flour, add the iced water and bring together without kneading. Chill for an hour.','Cut the beef, potato, swede and onion into half-centimetre dice — never grated, never minced.','Season very generously with white pepper: it is the pasty''s signature spice.','Cut 20 cm discs, fill one half in layers, add a knob of butter and fold over.','Crimp the edge by pinching and folding it back on itself, glaze with egg, cut a vent and bake 15 minutes at 220°C then 40 minutes at 170°C.']::text[]),
('shepherds-pie','en','Shepherd’s pie','Manchester, England','Minced lamb under a blanket of mashed potato forked into ridges. The name matters: made with beef it would be a cottage pie — shepherds keep sheep, and in England the distinction is no pedantry.',ARRAY['Brown the minced lamb in small batches over high heat, so it grills rather than boils.','Add the onion, carrot and celery in small dice and let them soften for 10 minutes.','Stir in the tomato paste, Worcestershire sauce, herbs and broth, and reduce uncovered for 30 minutes.','Mash the potatoes with the butter and hot milk into a firm purée: too loose and it would sink into the meat.','Add the peas to the meat, cover with the mash, fork the surface into ridges, dust with cheddar and bake for 35 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('full-english-breakfast','en','Full English breakfast','London, England','Eggs, bacon, sausages, beans, tomato, mushrooms, black pudding and toast: nine elements on one plate, cooked separately and brought together scalding. The order of assembly is the subject of sincere debate.',ARRAY['Start with the sausages, which need 15 minutes over medium heat, turned regularly.','Add the bacon when they are half done, then the sliced black pudding for 3 minutes a side.','Fry the mushrooms in the rendered fat, and the halved tomatoes cut side down.','Warm the beans with the tomato paste, brown sugar and Worcestershire — that is what brings them close to the tinned flavour.','Fry the eggs last, in the bacon fat, and toast the bread. Plate immediately: cold is the enemy of this dish.']::text[]),
('bakewell-tart','en','Bakewell tart','Bakewell, Derbyshire, England','Shortcrust, a layer of raspberry jam, frangipane and flaked almonds. Born, they say, of a mistake by an inn cook who poured the almond mixture over the jam instead of stirring it into the pastry.',ARRAY['Rub 125 g of cold butter into 250 g of flour, add 50 g of sugar, a yolk and a little iced water; chill for an hour.','Line a 24 cm tin, prick the base and blind-bake for 15 minutes at 180°C.','Spread the raspberry jam in an even layer over the cooled base.','Beat 175 g of soft butter with 175 g of sugar, work in three eggs one at a time, then the ground almonds and the extract.','Spread the frangipane, scatter flaked almonds and bake for 30 minutes at 170°C. Ice with sugar and lemon once cool.']::text[]),
('sticky-toffee-pudding','en','Sticky toffee pudding','Cartmel, Cumbria, England','A date sponge drowned in salted butter toffee sauce, served scalding with cold cream. Invented in a Lake District hotel in the 1970s, in fifty years it has become the best-loved English pudding.',ARRAY['Soak the stoned dates in the boiling water with the bicarbonate for 20 minutes, then crush them roughly.','Beat 100 g of butter with 150 g of brown sugar, add the eggs one at a time, then the flour and baking powder.','Fold in the dates and their liquid: the batter will look very loose, which is right.','Bake for 35 minutes at 180°C in a buttered dish.','For the sauce, melt 200 g of butter with 300 g of brown sugar and the cream, boil for 5 minutes and salt it. Pierce the sponge and pour over half the sauce, keeping the rest for serving.']::text[]),
('pie-and-mash','en','Pie and mash with liquor','East London, England','A beef pie, mash flattened with a palette knife and a green parsley sauce called ''liquor'', a survivor of the water in which eels were once poached. East End shops have served this on marble tables since 1890.',ARRAY['Brown the beef, coarsely chopped by knife, with the onion, moisten with broth and simmer for 45 minutes until barely any liquid remains.','Rub the suet and butter into the flour, bind with iced water and chill.','Line small oval tins, fill with the cooled meat, cover with a pastry lid and seal.','Bake for 35 minutes at 200°C, until the pastry is pale gold and firm.','For the liquor, thicken 400 ml of cooking water with the cornstarch, add an enormous quantity of chopped parsley and the vinegar. Serve the flattened mash beside it, liquor poured over everything.']::text[]),
('haggis-neeps-tatties','en','Haggis, neeps and tatties','Edinburgh, Scotland','A sheep''s stomach stuffed with offal, oats and onion, served with mashed swede and potato. On 25 January it is piped in and addressed with the ode Burns wrote for it, before being opened with a knife.',ARRAY['Simmer the pluck for 2 hours in salted water, letting the windpipe hang out of the pan. Keep the broth.','Chop it coarsely once cool and mix with the dry-toasted oats, chopped onion, fat and spices.','Loosen with the broth to a moist but not runny mass; pepper it heavily.','Fill the stomach two thirds full and sew it up; prick it and poach for 1½ hours in water at 85°C.','Serve with buttery mashed swede, mashed potato, and a thread of whisky poured over the opened haggis.']::text[]),
('cullen-skink','en','Cullen skink','Cullen, Moray, Scotland','A thick soup of smoked haddock, potato and onion, born in the fishing village of Cullen. ''Skink'' means a shin of beef in old Scots: the name of a broth that became, for want of meat, a fish soup.',ARRAY['Poach the smoked haddock in the milk with the bay for barely 8 minutes, then lift it out and strain the milk.','Flake the flesh into large pieces, discarding skin and bones.','Soften the onion and leek in the butter without colouring, then add the diced potatoes.','Pour in the infused milk and cook for 20 minutes; crush half the potatoes to thicken the soup.','Return the fish, add the cream and heat without boiling. Season with white pepper and cover with chives.']::text[]),
('cranachan','en','Cranachan','Inverness, Scotland','Whipped cream, raspberries, heather honey, whisky and toasted oats, layered in glasses. Served at harvest festivals, each guest mixing for themselves from ingredients brought separately to the table.',ARRAY['Toast the oats dry in a pan until fragrant and golden, then let them cool completely.','Crush half the raspberries with a fork and the sugar, and let them give up their juice.','Whip the very cold cream to soft peaks, without tightening it, then fold in the honey and whisky.','Fold three quarters of the toasted oats into the cream in one movement, without working it.','Layer in glasses, alternating cream, crushed and whole raspberries. Scatter the remaining oats at the last moment, so they stay crisp.']::text[]),
('scotch-broth','en','Scotch broth','Glasgow, Scotland','A mutton broth with pearl barley and root vegetables, so thick the spoon almost stands up. Samuel Johnson ate three plates of it on his Scottish journey and admitted a liking, which from him was saying a great deal.',ARRAY['Soak the split peas overnight; rinse the pearl barley in cold water.','Bring the mutton on the bone to a simmer in cold water and skim patiently for twenty minutes.','Add the barley, split peas, bay and onion, and cook covered for an hour.','Stir in the diced carrot, swede and leek, then the cabbage twenty minutes before the end.','Lift out the meat, bone it, return it in pieces and rest the soup overnight: scotch broth is better reheated. Pepper it and cover with parsley.']::text[]),
('cawl','en','Cawl','Carmarthen, Wales','The Welsh pot-au-feu: lamb, leek, swede and potato in a clear broth. Eaten in two courses — broth first, meat and vegetables after — with bread and Caerphilly cheese.',ARRAY['Cook the lamb on the bone in cold water, skimming carefully for the first twenty minutes.','Add the onion, bay and thyme, and simmer for 1½ hours without fully covering.','Cool overnight and lift off the set fat: that is what gives cawl its clarity.','Reheat, add the swede and carrot for 20 minutes, then parsnip, potatoes and the white of the leeks for 15 minutes.','Add the finely shredded leek greens two minutes before serving, with the parsley. Serve the broth first.']::text[]),
('welsh-rarebit','en','Welsh rarebit','Cardiff, Wales','Far more than cheese on toast: a thick sauce of cheddar melted with brown ale, mustard and Worcestershire, spread on bread and grilled. The name once mocked the Welsh as too poor to eat rabbit.',ARRAY['Make a roux with the butter and flour, then whisk in the brown ale and cook for 3 minutes.','Add the grated cheddar off the heat, a handful at a time, stirring until it strings.','Stir in the mustard, Worcestershire, cayenne and finally the egg yolks, without letting them cook.','Let it cool: the paste should thicken enough to spread with a spoon.','Toast the bread on one side only, spread the paste thickly right to the edges of the untoasted side, and grill fiercely for 3 minutes until it blisters.']::text[]),
('soda-bread','en','Irish soda bread','Cork, Ireland','A bread without yeast, raised by the meeting of bicarbonate and buttermilk. Made in ten minutes and eaten the same day. The deep cross cut on top lets out the steam — and, they say, the fairies.',ARRAY['Mix the flours, oats, bicarbonate and salt in a large bowl with your fingertips.','Pour in the buttermilk and honey all at once and mix by hand, in a figure of eight, for thirty seconds only.','Do not knead: the bicarbonate starts working the moment it meets the buttermilk and your time is short.','Shape a flattened round, set it on a floured tray and cut a deep cross, halfway down.','Bake for 45 minutes at 200°C. It is done when it sounds hollow underneath. Cool on a rack under a cloth to keep the crust soft.']::text[]),
('colcannon','en','Colcannon','Galway, Ireland','Mashed potato with kale and spring onion, a well of melted butter in the middle. At Halloween a ring was hidden inside: whoever found it was to marry within the year.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in their skins, then peel them while still hot, burning your fingers a little.','Strip the kale, chop it and wilt it for 5 minutes in boiling salted water; drain and squeeze it dry.','Warm the milk and cream with the sliced spring onions, without letting them boil.','Rice the potatoes, work in the infused milk, then the kale, and 100 g of butter.','Mound it up, hollow a well in the centre and fill it with the remaining melted butter. Each forkful is dipped in.']::text[]),
('boxty','en','Boxty','County Leitrim, Ireland','The potato pancake of the Irish north-west, half raw and grated, half boiled and mashed. An old rhyme warns: ''Boxty on the griddle, boxty in the pan, if you can''t make boxty, you''ll never get a man''.',ARRAY['Boil half the potatoes and mash them smooth; grate the other half raw.','Squeeze the grated potato in a cloth over a bowl: keep the liquid and let the starch settle at the bottom.','Pour off the clear water, scrape up the white starch and add it to the mixture: that is what marks out real boxty.','Combine both potatoes, the flour, bicarbonate, salt and buttermilk into a thick batter.','Cook wide pancakes in butter, 4 minutes a side. Serve with grilled bacon and an egg.']::text[]),
('bitterballen','en','Bitterballen','Utrecht, Netherlands','Round croquettes of set beef ragout, breaded and fried, served with mustard in every brown café. The centre is liquid and scalding: biting into a whole one is a beginner''s mistake.',ARRAY['Cook the beef for 2 hours in the broth with the bay, until it shreds with a fork. Strain and keep 600 ml of broth.','Make a roux with the butter and flour, cook it for 3 minutes, then add the hot broth in three stages: the sauce must become very thick.','Add the shredded meat, softened onion, parsley and nutmeg, and salt generously.','Spread 2 cm deep in a dish and refrigerate for at least 4 hours: the mass should cut with a knife.','Roll 3 cm balls, bread them twice in egg and crumbs, and fry for 4 minutes at 180°C. Serve with the mustard.']::text[]),
('hollandse-nieuwe','en','Hollandse nieuwe','Scheveningen, The Hague, Netherlands','The herring of the first June catch, gutted but with the pancreas left in: its enzyme ripens the flesh in a light brine. Eaten raw, by hand, held by the tail with the head tipped back.',ARRAY['Choose herring with pale pink, firm flesh, never grey: the sign of a short and proper maturation.','Remove the skin by pulling from head to tail in one continuous movement.','Open the herring along the backbone and lift it out, keeping both fillets joined at the tail.','Chop the white onion into very small dice — the Dutch custom, disputed by northern purists.','Serve either by hand, held by the tail and dipped in onion, or in a buttered roll with gherkins.']::text[]),
('erwtensoep','en','Erwtensoep','Groningen, Netherlands','The winter split pea soup, so thick the spoon must stand up in it — that is the unofficial test. It is called snert, and it is eaten only from the second day, once it has set.',ARRAY['Soak the split peas overnight, then put them in three litres of cold water with the knuckle and bay.','Bring to a simmer, skim, and cook for 1½ hours until the peas have collapsed entirely.','Add the celeriac, carrot, leek, onion and potato in dice, and continue for 45 minutes.','Lift out the knuckle, bone it, return the meat with the belly and the sliced smoked sausage for 30 minutes.','Rest overnight before reheating. Serve with buttered rye bread and mustard.']::text[]),
('poffertjes','en','Poffertjes','’s-Hertogenbosch, Brabant, Netherlands','Tiny puffed pancakes made with buckwheat flour, cooked in a dimpled iron plate and served under an avalanche of icing sugar and butter. Sold from wooden chalets on the squares in winter.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in the warm milk, then whisk with both flours, the eggs and the salt.','Prove for an hour under a cloth: the batter should blister and smell of fermentation.','Butter each dimple of the plate generously and heat it over medium heat.','Fill the dimples three quarters full; as soon as the edges set, flip each poffertje with a fork.','Give them another minute and serve at once, with a knob of cold butter on top and a great deal of icing sugar.']::text[]),
('hutspot','en','Hutspot','Leiden, Netherlands','A mash of carrot, onion and potato served with braised beef. Legend has an orphan finding it still steaming in the abandoned Spanish camp when the siege of Leiden was lifted on 3 October 1574 — and it is still eaten on that day.',ARRAY['Cook the brisket for 2½ hours in salted water with the bay and cloves, never letting it boil.','An hour before the end, add the carrots and onions in large pieces to the same broth.','Thirty minutes later, add the potatoes.','Drain the vegetables and crush them roughly with the butter and a little broth: the mash should stay rustic, with lumps.','Slice the beef and serve it on the mash, moistened with a little broth, mustard on the side.']::text[]),
('boulets-liegeois','en','Liège meatballs','Liège, Wallonia, Belgium','Large meatballs under a sauce lapée — sweet and sour, made with sirop de Liège, a concentrate of pears and apples boiled for twenty hours. Served with chips, they are the staple of Walloon friteries.',ARRAY['Soak the bread rolls in the milk, squeeze them out and mix with both meats, the eggs and one grated onion.','Work the mixture by hand for 10 minutes, season generously, then shape four meatballs the size of a fist.','Brown them on all sides in the butter, then set aside.','Slowly turn the two remaining sliced onions golden, moisten with broth, and add the syrup, vinegar, sugar, bay and thyme.','Return the meatballs and simmer covered for 45 minutes, turning them halfway. Serve with chips.']::text[]),
('stoemp','en','Stoemp','Brussels, Belgium','A Brussels mash in which potato is crushed with a vegetable — carrot, leek or kale — and never smoothed out. The word comes from the Dutch stampen, to crush: the gesture gives the dish its name.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes and carrots together in salted water with the bay for 25 minutes.','Meanwhile soften the sliced onion and diced bacon in half the butter, then add the sliced leeks and let them soften for 10 minutes.','Drain the potatoes and carrots and return them to the heat for two minutes to dry out.','Crush them roughly with a fork along with the remaining butter, the cream and the nutmeg: stoemp should stay lumpy.','Fold in the leeks and bacon, pepper it, and serve with pan-fried sausages and parsley.']::text[]),
('waterzooi','en','Chicken waterzooi','Bruges, Flanders, Belgium','A creamy stew of chicken and julienned vegetables, halfway between soup and main course, thickened with egg yolk and cream. Once made with fish from the Scheldt; pollution of the river turned it to poultry.',ARRAY['Poach the whole chicken for 45 minutes in the broth with the thyme and bay, without boiling.','Lift it out, let it cool a little and pull the meat into large pieces; strain the broth.','Cut all the vegetables into fine julienne — carrot, leek, celery, celeriac — and sweat them in butter for 10 minutes.','Moisten with the broth, add the potatoes in batons and cook for 20 minutes.','Beat the yolks with the cream, loosen with a ladle of hot broth and stir in off the heat. Return the chicken, sharpen with lemon and cover with parsley.']::text[]),
('anguilles-au-vert','en','Eels in green sauce','Antwerp, Belgium','Sections of eel poached in a broth of fresh herbs — sorrel, chervil, parsley, tarragon — which gives the sauce a colour of spring. Eaten cold or warm, with bread and beer.',ARRAY['Have the fishmonger cut the eel into 5 cm sections, skin removed.','Sweat the shallots in butter, add the eel and let it firm up for 3 minutes without colouring.','Pour in the white wine and as much water again, bring to a simmer and poach for 8 minutes.','Add all the chopped herbs at once and wilt them for two minutes only: any longer and the green turns khaki.','Take off the heat, thicken with the beaten yolks and lemon juice, without boiling. Let it cool: the dish is better cold.']::text[]),
('speculoos','en','Speculoos','Hasselt, Limburg, Belgium','The brown biscuit of Saint Nicholas, pressed into wooden moulds carved with images. Its colour comes not from chocolate but from brown candi sugar and spices — cinnamon, clove, ginger, cardamom, nutmeg.',ARRAY['Beat the soft butter with the brown sugar until the mixture barely lightens.','Add the spices, salt, bicarbonate and milk, then the flour, and bring together without kneading.','Wrap and rest for 24 hours in the fridge: this rest is essential, it lets the spices spread through.','Roll out to 4 mm and press the dough into floured wooden moulds, or cut with a cutter.','Bake for 15 minutes at 160°C. They are still soft when they come out and harden as they cool: do not bake them longer.']::text[]),
('chicons-au-gratin','en','Endive and ham gratin','Brussels, Belgium','Braised endives wrapped in ham and covered in cheese béchamel. The endive was born in Brussels around 1830, from a farmer who forgot chicory roots in his cellar: they had blanched.',ARRAY['Cut out the core of each endive with a pointed knife: that is where the bitterness sits.','Braise them for 25 minutes in a little butter, water, sugar and lemon juice, covered, turning halfway.','Drain them thoroughly and press them gently in a cloth: any remaining water would flood the gratin.','Make a béchamel with the butter, flour and milk, and add three quarters of the gruyère and the nutmeg.','Wrap each endive in a slice of ham, pack them tightly, coat with the sauce, dust with the remaining cheese and breadcrumbs, and bake for 25 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('lancashire-hotpot','en','Lancashire hotpot','Preston, Lancashire, England','Lamb chops and onions under a roof of overlapping potato slices, baked for three hours untouched. Cotton mill workers put it in the oven before their shift; it was waiting for them at night.',ARRAY['Flour and sear the lamb pieces and trimmed kidneys hard, in small batches.','Soften the sliced onions and carrot in the same pot, lifting the caramelised juices.','Layer meat and onions in a deep casserole, seasoning each layer, then pour in broth and Worcestershire to come only halfway up.','Slice the potatoes 3 mm thick and lay them in tight overlapping scales on top, in a spiral.','Brush with melted butter, cover and bake for 2½ hours at 150°C, then uncover for 30 minutes at 200°C to brown the roof.']::text[]),
('escalivada','en','Escalivada','Tarragona, Catalonia, Spain','Aubergines, peppers and onions roasted whole over embers until the skin blackens, then peeled and pulled apart by hand. The Catalan verb escalivar means to cook under ashes.',ARRAY['Lay the whole vegetables, unoiled, directly on the embers or under the oven grill at 220°C.','Turn them every ten minutes: the skin must blacken and blister all over, or it will not come away.','Shut them in a covered dish for 20 minutes: the steam lifts the skin by itself.','Peel and pull the flesh apart by hand, along the fibres — never with a knife.','Dress with olive oil and sherry vinegar, salt, and rest for two hours. Serve warm on grilled bread with anchovies.']::text[]),
('crema-catalana','en','Crema catalana','Girona, Catalonia, Spain','A milk custard scented with lemon and cinnamon, thickened with starch rather than baked, and burnt with a hot iron just before serving. Eaten on 19 March, Saint Joseph''s day.',ARRAY['Infuse the milk for 20 minutes with the zests and cinnamon sticks, then strain.','Whisk the yolks with 150 g of sugar to the ribbon, then add the cornstarch without lumps.','Pour the hot milk onto the yolks in a thin stream, whisking, then return to low heat.','Stir constantly in figures of eight until it thickens, about 5 minutes, never letting it boil.','Divide between earthenware dishes, cover with film and chill for 4 hours. Dust with the remaining sugar and burn with a red-hot iron at the last second.']::text[]),
('suquet-de-peix','en','Suquet de peix','Palamós, Costa Brava, Spain','The fish stew of Catalan fishermen, cooked on board with whatever would not sell. It is thickened with picada — garlic, almonds, fried bread and parsley pounded in a mortar — added in the final minutes.',ARRAY['Make a sofregit: sliced onion softened for 20 minutes in oil, then grated tomato cooked down completely.','Add the potatoes in thick pieces broken by hand, and the hot saffron broth.','Cook for 20 minutes, then lay the fish in large sections on top of the potatoes, without burying them.','Pound the garlic, almonds, fried bread and parsley into a paste, loosen it with broth and pour it into the pan.','Add the prawns and mussels, shake the pan in a circular motion — never stir with a spoon — and give it 5 minutes.']::text[]),
('bacalao-al-pil-pil','en','Bacalao al pil-pil','Bilbao, Basque Country, Spain','Four ingredients — salt cod, oil, garlic, chilli — and an emulsion that is pure sleight of hand: the gelatine from the skin binds the oil into a glossy yellow sauce, achieved by moving the pan alone.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod for 48 hours in cold water, changing it six times; keep the skin at all costs.','Turn the sliced garlic and chilli golden in warm oil, lift them out and let the oil cool to 60°C.','Lay the pieces skin down and let them sweat for 8 minutes over very low heat: white beads of gelatine should appear.','Remove the fish, let the oil cool a little, then swirl it round the pan in a slow, continuous circular motion.','The oil suddenly whitens and thickens. Return the cod, garlic and chilli, shake for another minute and serve at once.']::text[]),
('txangurro','en','Txangurro donostiarra','Hondarribia, Gipuzkoa, Spain','Spider crab picked and stewed in a brandy sofrito, then packed back into its shell and browned. Txangurro is both the crab and the dish — the Basque language makes no distinction.',ARRAY['Boil the crabs for 15 minutes in heavily salted water, then cool and pick them clean.','Carefully save the coral and the creamy parts from the shell: that is where all the flavour is.','Soften finely chopped onion, leek and carrot in oil for 25 minutes, then add the grated tomato.','Flame with brandy, moisten with white wine and reduce, then fold in the meat, coral and cayenne.','Fill the cleaned shells, scatter with breadcrumbs and knobs of butter, and brown for 10 minutes at 220°C.']::text[]),
('porrusalda','en','Porrusalda','Vitoria-Gasteiz, Álava, Spain','Literally ''leek broth'': leeks, potatoes, carrots, and nothing else — save sometimes a few flakes of salt cod. Simplicity here is a principle, not a constraint.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod for 24 hours and flake it into small pieces.','Turn the sliced garlic golden in the oil, lift it out and keep the flavoured oil.','Sweat the onion and the white of the leeks in lengths, then add the carrot.','Break the potatoes by hand rather than cutting them: the ragged edges release the starch that will thicken the broth.','Cover with water, cook for 30 minutes, add the cod for 5 minutes, then pour in the garlic oil and paprika off the heat.']::text[]),
('chilindron-de-cordero','en','Lamb chilindrón','Zaragoza, Aragon, Spain','A lamb stew with dried red peppers, the choriceros, of which only the pulp scraped out with a spoon is used. That pulp gives the sauce its deep colour, without a scrap of tomato.',ARRAY['Soak the dried peppers in hot water for an hour, then scrape out the pulp with a spoon and discard the skin.','Sear the lamb in large pieces in very hot oil, in small batches, until well coloured.','Soften the onion, garlic and diced ham in the same pot, slowly.','Add the fresh peppers in strips, the dried pepper pulp and the grated tomato; stew for 10 minutes.','Return the meat, deglaze with white wine, add the bay and simmer covered for 1¼ hours.']::text[]),
('ternasco-asado','en','Roast ternasco','Teruel, Aragon, Spain','The Aragonese milk-fed lamb, under three months old, roasted simply with lard, garlic and white wine. No herbs, no spices: the meat is too delicate to carry anything else.',ARRAY['Rub the lamb with melted lard, salt and crushed garlic an hour before it goes in the oven.','Lay thick potato slices in the base of an earthenware dish and set the meat on top.','Pour the white wine and half a glass of water into the base, never over the meat.','Roast for 1¾ hours at 170°C, basting every 20 minutes with the juices.','Raise to 220°C for ten minutes to brown, then rest for 15 minutes and squeeze over lemon before carving.']::text[]),
('menestra-de-verduras','en','Menestra de verduras','Tudela, Navarre, Spain','The vegetables of the Navarrese Ribera cooked separately then brought together in a ham broth: artichokes, cardoons, broad beans, white asparagus, each to its own timing. The discipline is the whole recipe.',ARRAY['Turn the artichokes and drop them into lemon water; string the cardoons the same way.','Cook each vegetable separately in salted water, taking it out al dente, and refresh in iced water to fix the colour.','Keep a litre of the broad bean cooking water: that will make the broth.','Fry the onion, garlic and diced ham in oil, dust with flour and moisten with white wine then the bean water.','Bring all the vegetables together in this sauce, heat for 5 minutes without boiling, and finish with chopped hard-boiled egg.']::text[]),
('piquillos-rellenos','en','Piquillo peppers stuffed with cod','Lodosa, Navarre, Spain','Small red peppers roasted over a wood fire, peeled by hand without water, stuffed with a cod brandade and coated in a sauce made from the broken ones. Those from Lodosa carry a protected origin.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod for 36 hours, then poach it for 8 minutes in the milk; flake it and keep the milk.','Soften one finely chopped onion and two garlic cloves, add the flaked cod and the cream, and work with a spoon into a bound stuffing.','Fill each piquillo carefully with a piping bag, without tearing it.','For the sauce, fry the second onion, add four broken piquillos, dust with flour, moisten with broth and milk, then blend and sieve.','Arrange the peppers in a dish, coat with the sauce and bake for 15 minutes at 180°C.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('patatas-a-la-riojana','en','Patatas a la riojana','Logroño, La Rioja, Spain','A potato and chorizo stew of which Paul Bocuse said, after tasting it in 1987, that nothing should be changed. The secret is in the gesture: you break the potatoes, you do not cut them.',ARRAY['Push the knife halfway into the potato and snap it off with a twist of the wrist: the fracture releases the starch.','Sweat the onion, garlic and pepper in the oil, without colouring them.','Add the chorizo in thick rounds and let it render its red fat for two minutes.','Stir in the potatoes to coat them, then add the paprika off the heat.','Cover with water, add the bay and dried peppers, and simmer for 35 minutes without stirring — shake the pan instead.']::text[]),
('lechazo-asado','en','Wood-oven lechazo','Aranda de Duero, Castile and León, Spain','Milk-fed lamb roasted in an earthenware dish in a wood oven, seasoned with nothing but salt and water. Castilian tradition has it carved with the edge of a plate, so readily does the meat fall away.',ARRAY['Choose a lamb under thirty days old, fed on milk alone: that is the whole difference.','Lay the quarters skin down in an earthenware dish, rub them with lard and salt generously.','Pour the water into the base of the dish, never onto the skin.','Roast for 1 hour at 180°C, turn skin up and continue for another hour, basting with the juices.','Raise to 230°C for the last 15 minutes to crackle the skin. Serve with a plain lettuce and onion salad.']::text[]),
('sopa-de-ajo','en','Castilian garlic soup','Burgos, Castile and León, Spain','Stale bread, garlic, paprika and an egg poached in it: the shepherd''s soup of the meseta, made from what was left. Also called sopa castellana, and served in scalding earthenware bowls.',ARRAY['Turn the sliced garlic gently golden in the oil, without burning it: bitter, it would ruin the whole dish.','Add the diced ham, then the stale bread in pieces, and let it soak up the oil for 3 minutes.','Off the heat, stir in both paprikas briskly — above 120°C they turn acrid.','Moisten with hot broth, add the bay and simmer for 15 minutes: the bread should collapse.','Divide between earthenware bowls, crack an egg into each and bake for 6 minutes at 200°C, until the white sets.']::text[]),
('arroz-a-banda','en','Arroz a banda','Alicante, Valencian Community, Spain','The rice is cooked in a rockfish stock and the fish served separately — ''a banda'', on the side. Two courses from one dish: fishermen ate the rice on board and sold the fish at the harbour.',ARRAY['Make a stock by cooking the rockfish for 25 minutes with the potatoes, then strain it, pressing well.','Fry the soaked ñora pulp, garlic and grated tomato in the paella pan until the sofrito barely darkens.','Add the rice and toast it for 2 minutes, stirring, then pour in the boiling saffron stock — two and a half times the volume of rice.','Cook for 18 minutes over high then low heat, without stirring even once.','Rest off the heat for 5 minutes under a cloth. Serve the rice first, the fish and aioli after.']::text[]),
('caldero-del-mar-menor','en','Caldero del Mar Menor','Cartagena, Murcia, Spain','The rice of the lagoon fishermen, cooked in the iron cauldron that gives it its name, red with ñoras and mullet stock. Served with an aioli so stiff it stays on an upturned spoon.',ARRAY['Fry the whole ñoras in the oil for a few seconds, without burning them, then pound them in a mortar with two garlic cloves.','Fry the grated tomato in the same oil, add the ñora paste and the paprika.','Moisten with two litres of water, add the gutted fish and cook for 20 minutes; lift them out and strain the broth.','Bring the broth to a rolling boil, tip in the rice and cook for 18 minutes uncovered and unstirred.','Mount the aioli in a mortar with the remaining garlic, the yolk, salt and oil in a thread. Serve the rice, then the fish with the aioli.']::text[]),
('pa-amb-oli-sobrasada','en','Pa amb oli with sobrassada','Palma, Mallorca, Spain','Country bread rubbed with hanging tomato and oil, spread with sobrassada — the raw, spreadable paprika sausage that the island''s damp air prevented from ever fully drying. Melted in the oven with honey.',ARRAY['Choose a close-crumbed loaf, cut into one-centimetre slices, and toast it barely.','Rub it with half a garlic clove, then with a halved hanging tomato, pulp against the crumb.','Douse generously with olive oil and salt it.','Spread the sobrassada thickly, drizzle with honey and put it under the grill for 4 minutes: it melts and reddens.','Serve with sheep''s cheese, olives and a glass of island wine.']::text[]),
('ensaimada','en','Ensaïmada','Palma, Mallorca, Spain','A spiral of dough stretched to transparency and brushed with lard — saïm in Mallorcan, hence the name. It still travels in large octagonal boxes seen in every airport on the island.',ARRAY['Make a starter with the yeast, warm water and 100 g of flour; let it double for an hour.','Knead with the remaining flour, sugar, eggs and salt for 20 minutes: the dough must become very elastic.','Prove for 2 hours, divide, then stretch each piece by hand on an oiled surface until translucent.','Brush generously with soft lard, roll into a long thin rope and coil it into a loose spiral, leaving space between the turns.','Prove for 12 hours at room temperature, bake for 20 minutes at 180°C and dust heavily with icing sugar.']::text[]),
('papas-arrugadas','en','Papas arrugadas with mojo','Tenerife, Canary Islands, Spain','Small potatoes boiled in water saturated with salt until the skin wrinkles and frosts over with white crystals. Dipped into two sauces: red mojo with chilli, green mojo with coriander.',ARRAY['Wash the small potatoes without peeling and put them in a pan where they sit tightly packed.','Cover halfway with water and tip in all the coarse salt: the water should be saturated, salty enough to float a potato.','Boil covered for 25 minutes, then pour off the water and return to high heat for two minutes, shaking: the salt crystallises on the skin.','For the red mojo, pound garlic, cumin, chilli, paprika and bread, then mount with oil and vinegar.','For the green mojo, replace the chilli with coriander and green pepper. Serve both on the side.']::text[]),
('rancho-canario','en','Rancho canario','Las Palmas, Gran Canaria, Spain','A thick soup in which short pasta and chickpeas cook together with pork and chorizo. The dish of neighbourhood fiestas, made in hundred-litre pots and handed out in the street.',ARRAY['Soak the chickpeas overnight with a handful of coarse salt.','Cook them in two litres of water with the pork and bacon for 1 hour at a gentle boil.','Fry onion, garlic and grated tomato in oil for a sofrito, add the paprika off the heat, then tip it all into the pot.','Add the potatoes in large cubes, the sliced chorizo, saffron and thyme, and continue for 20 minutes.','Throw in the short pasta for the last 10 minutes. The rancho should stay thick: the spoon must almost stand up in it.']::text[]),
('cocido-montanes','en','Cocido montañés','Santander, Cantabria, Spain','White beans and green cabbage stewed with the compango — chorizo, black pudding, bacon and salt ribs. Unlike other Spanish cocidos, this one has no chickpeas and no broth served separately.',ARRAY['Soak the beans and desalt the ribs and ear overnight, separately.','Put the beans in cold water with the salt meats, bring to a bare simmer and skim.','Check the boil with cold water twice during the first half hour: this stops the bean skins bursting.','After 1 hour, add the washed and roughly chopped greens, along with the chorizo and black pudding.','Make a refrito of garlic and paprika in the oil, pour it into the pot and give it another 30 minutes. Serve everything together.']::text[]),
('rabo-de-toro','en','Rabo de toro','Jerez de la Frontera, Andalusia, Spain','Oxtail braised in sherry for over three hours, until the meat leaves the bone of its own accord. It was made with the animals killed in the ring, on the evening of the bullfight.',ARRAY['Flour the oxtail sections and sear them on all sides in very hot oil, in small batches.','Soften onion, carrot, leek and garlic in the same pot for 25 minutes, until golden.','Add the grated tomato, reduce, then deglaze with the red wine and sherry vinegar.','Return the meat, add broth to cover, along with the bay and cloves, and simmer covered for 3 hours.','Lift out the meat, pass the sauce through a sieve pressing the vegetables, reduce it and return the oxtail to it.']::text[]),
('arroz-de-cabidela','en','Arroz de cabidela','Viana do Castelo, Minho, Portugal','A poultry rice bound with the bird''s blood, sharpened with vinegar to stop it setting. The dish of Minho farms, where nothing of the animal was wasted — not even what elsewhere is thrown away.',ARRAY['Collect the blood in a bowl already holding the vinegar, and whisk at once: otherwise it would set solid.','Joint the chicken and fry it in the oil with the onion, garlic and bay.','Add the grated tomato and white wine, cover with water and simmer for 30 minutes.','Tip in the rice and cook for 15 minutes, keeping the dish very wet.','Off the heat, pour in the soured blood in a thin stream, stirring constantly: the sauce browns and thickens. Do not let it boil again.']::text[]),
('sarrabulho','en','Papas de sarrabulho','Ponte de Lima, Minho, Portugal','A thick porridge of bread, pork meats and blood, sharpened with cumin and lemon. Served with rojões and a glass of vinho verde; the dish of Minho winter markets.',ARRAY['Cook the meats for 1½ hours in water with the onion, garlic, bay and white wine, until they fall apart.','Lift them out, chop them finely by knife and strain the broth.','Crumble the stale bread into the hot broth and let it swell for 15 minutes, then crush it with a wooden spoon.','Return the meat, add the cumin, piri-piri and plenty of pepper, and thicken for 20 minutes over low heat, stirring.','Pour in the blood off the heat, stirring without pause, and finish with lemon juice. It should have the consistency of a loose purée.']::text[]),
('caldeirada-de-peixe','en','Caldeirada de peixe','Nazaré, Estremadura, Portugal','The fish stew of Nazaré fishermen, built in layers in the pot and never stirred: you only shake the pan. The more different species it holds, the better it is.',ARRAY['Cut all the vegetables into rounds of the same thickness: they must cook together.','Build the pot in layers — onion, potato, pepper, tomato, fish — salting and oiling each storey.','Repeat to the top, finishing with vegetables to protect the fish.','Pour in the white wine, water to come only halfway, the bay and paprika, then cover.','Cook for 35 minutes over medium heat without ever stirring: shake the pot in a circular motion every ten minutes.']::text[]),
('queijadas-de-sintra','en','Queijadas de Sintra','Sintra, Portugal','Small tartlets of fresh cheese and cinnamon in a hand-pleated crust, recorded in Sintra since the 13th century — they were once used to pay part of the local taxes.',ARRAY['Knead 150 g of flour, the warm water, melted butter and salt into a supple dough, and rest it for 30 minutes.','Push the fresh cheese through a sieve to remove every lump — the final texture depends entirely on this.','Mix it with the sugar, eggs, remaining flour, cinnamon and zest into a smooth batter.','Roll the dough very thin and line 6 cm moulds, leaving an overhanging edge that you pleat by hand.','Fill three quarters full and bake for 20 minutes at 220°C: the tops should brown in patches.']::text[]),
('arroz-doce','en','Arroz doce','Viseu, Beira Alta, Portugal','The Portuguese rice pudding, yellow with egg yolks, served cold in wide dishes and stencilled with cinnamon. There is no village festa in Portugal without a whole table covered in these dishes.',ARRAY['Cook the rice for 10 minutes in salted water with a cinnamon stick and the zest, then drain without rinsing.','Bring the milk to a simmer with the remaining cinnamon and zest, tip in the rice and cook for 30 minutes over low heat, stirring often.','Add the sugar only at this point: any earlier and it would toughen the grain.','Off the heat, stir in the yolks beaten with a little hot rice, then return to very low heat for two minutes without boiling.','Pour into wide dishes, cool, and stencil a pattern with ground cinnamon through cut paper.']::text[]),
('pastel-de-tentugal','en','Pastel de Tentúgal','Tentúgal, Beira Litoral, Portugal','A pastry stretched so thin you can read through it, rolled around an egg cream. The Carmelite nuns of Tentúgal kept the secret; the dough is still worked in rooms without a draught, windows shut.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, warm water and salt for 25 minutes: the dough must become smooth and supple as fine leather.','Rest it for 2 hours under a bowl, then stretch it by hand over a large floured cloth until you can see the weave through it.','Make the cream: cook the sugar and water to soft ball, cool a little, stir in the yolks off the heat, then thicken over low heat.','Cut strips of pastry, brush with melted butter, pipe the cream along them and roll loosely.','Fold in the ends, bake for 15 minutes at 220°C and dust with icing sugar and cinnamon.']::text[]),
('carne-de-porco-a-alentejana','en','Carne de porco à alentejana','Alcácer do Sal, Alentejo, Portugal','Pork marinated in red pepper paste, sautéed with clams and fried potatoes. The surf-and-turf pairing seems odd until you learn that Alentejo pigs grazed along the estuaries.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into 3 cm cubes and marinate for 12 hours in the pepper paste, white wine, crushed garlic and bay.','Purge the clams for 2 hours in salted water so they release their sand.','Drain and dry the meat, then sear it over high heat in olive oil, in small batches.','Pour in the strained marinade, reduce by half, then add the clams and cover: they open in 4 minutes.','Deep-fry the diced potatoes, fold them through, cover with chopped coriander and serve with lemon and gherkins.']::text[]),
('arroz-de-lampreia','en','Lamprey rice','Monção, Minho, Portugal','The Minho lamprey, fished from January to April, stewed in its own blood and Alvarinho wine. A prehistoric jawless fish whose taste is closer to game than to the sea.',ARRAY['Scald the lamprey to remove the mucus, then collect the blood in a bowl holding the vinegar.','Cut it into 4 cm sections and marinate for 3 hours in the white wine with the bay and cloves.','Soften the onion and garlic in the oil, add the grated tomato and let it cook down.','Pour in the marinade, bring to a simmer and poach the sections for 25 minutes; lift them out and keep warm.','Cook the rice for 15 minutes in that broth, then bind with the blood off the heat, without boiling. Return the fish and serve at once.']::text[]),
('maranhos','en','Maranhos','Sertã, Beira Baixa, Portugal','A kid''s stomach filled with rice, mint and meats, sewn up and simmered. An Easter preparation in the Portuguese serra, where the animal was killed once a year and nothing was wasted.',ARRAY['Clean the stomachs thoroughly with salt, lemon and vinegared water, then cut them into 15 cm pouches.','Dice all the meats small and mix them with the raw rice, onion, garlic, chopped mint and white wine.','Fill the pouches only half full — the rice doubles — and sew them carefully with kitchen thread.','Poach for 1¾ hours in a barely simmering broth with the bay, never boiling, pricking them twice.','Let them cool before slicing: hot, maranhos fall apart. Serve with a squeeze of lemon.']::text[]),
('bolo-levedo','en','Bolo lêvedo','Ribeira Grande, São Miguel, Azores','A flat sweet bread cooked on a griddle, halfway between brioche and English muffin, born in the ovens of Furnas. Eaten warm at breakfast, simply buttered, or split and filled.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in the warm milk with a spoonful of sugar and let it froth for 15 minutes.','Knead with the flour, remaining sugar, egg, soft butter and salt for 12 minutes.','Prove for 1½ hours, until the dough doubles.','Divide into eight balls, flatten them into 12 cm discs 1.5 cm thick, and prove for 40 minutes under a cloth.','Cook on a dry griddle over low heat, 7 minutes a side: they should brown without burning and stay soft in the middle.']::text[]),
('pudim-abade-de-priscos','en','Abade de Priscos pudding','Braga, Minho, Portugal','A custard of fifteen egg yolks, port and — this is its secret — pork fat, which gives it an unmatched silkiness. Father Manuel Rebelo, the greedy priest of Priscos, never gave up the recipe in his lifetime.',ARRAY['Make a pale caramel with 150 g of sugar and a little water, pour it into a ring mould and swirl to coat the sides.','Cook the remaining sugar with the water, zest, cinnamon and pork fat into a syrup, 10 minutes at a gentle boil.','Lift out the fat, let the syrup cool a little and strain it.','Beat the yolks without frothing them — bubbles would spoil the texture — then pour in the warm syrup in a thread, and finally the port.','Pass through a sieve, pour into the mould and bake in a bain-marie for 1¼ hours at 180°C. Cool overnight before turning out.']::text[]),
('garbure','en','Béarn garbure','Pau, Béarn, France','A cabbage and bean soup into which a leg of duck confit is plunged. The Béarn test is well known: a spoon stood in the middle must stay upright on its own, or it is merely soup.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then cook them in three litres of water with the smoked collar for an hour.','Add carrots, turnips and leeks in large pieces, along with the garlic, thyme and bay.','Thirty minutes later, add the potatoes and the cabbage, leaves separated and roughly chopped.','Warm the confit legs in their fat and lay them in the soup for the last twenty minutes.','Serve very thick, over slices of stale bread. Finish in the Béarn manner: a glass of red wine poured into the bottom of the plate.']::text[]),
('poule-au-pot','en','Stuffed poule au pot','Lescar, Béarn, France','A hen stuffed with ham and bread, poached with its vegetables. Henri IV, born in Pau, is said to have wished every ploughman could put one in the pot on Sundays — the phrase may be invented, the dish stayed with him.',ARRAY['Make the stuffing: bread soaked in milk and squeezed, chopped ham, the bird''s liver, garlic, parsley and eggs.','Stuff the hen without packing it — the stuffing swells — and sew the opening; truss it firmly.','Bring three litres of water to a simmer, lower in the hen and skim patiently for twenty minutes.','Add the clove-studded onion, bay and salt, and poach for 2 hours without ever boiling.','Add the vegetables in large pieces for the last 40 minutes. Serve the broth first, then the hen, stuffing and vegetables.']::text[]),
('coq-au-vin','en','Coq au vin','Beaune, Burgundy, France','A cockerel — a real one, firm and old — marinated then braised overnight in red wine. The sauce is bound with the bird''s blood or, failing that, its blended liver: that is what sets it apart from a mere chicken bourguignon.',ARRAY['Joint the cockerel into eight and marinate for 24 hours in the red wine with carrot, garlic, thyme and bay.','Drain and dry the pieces thoroughly: wet, they would not colour.','Sear them in butter, flame with marc, dust with flour and cook for two minutes.','Pour in the strained marinade, brought to the boil beforehand, and simmer covered for 2 hours over very low heat.','Glaze the button onions and fry the mushrooms separately, adding them at the end with the browned lardons.']::text[]),
('gratin-dauphinois','en','Gratin dauphinois','Grenoble, Dauphiné, France','Sliced potatoes cooked in milk and cream with garlic, and nothing else. No cheese, no egg: the recipe recorded in 1788 by the lieutenant general of Dauphiné mentions neither.',ARRAY['Choose firm-fleshed potatoes and cut them into 3 mm rounds, never rinsing them: the starch makes the binding.','Rub the dish with a cut garlic clove, then butter it generously.','Heat the milk with the remaining crushed garlic, nutmeg, salt and pepper, and add the potatoes. Simmer for 10 minutes, stirring gently.','Transfer to the dish, pressing the layers down, then pour the cream over.','Bake for 1¼ hours at 150°C. A knife blade should pass through without resistance and the top be golden, not crusted.']::text[]),
('socca','en','Socca','Antibes, French Riviera, France','A chickpea pancake baked in a wood oven on a copper tray, served scalding, peppered and torn by hand. Cousin to Genoese farinata, sold by weight in the markets of the coast.',ARRAY['Whisk the chickpea flour into warm water without a single lump, and salt it.','Rest for at least an hour, skimming the foam that rises.','Stir in the olive oil: the batter should stay very liquid, like a crêpe batter.','Heat an oiled tray in the hottest part of your oven, under the grill, then pour in a layer barely 3 mm deep.','Bake for 8 minutes at the very top: the surface should scorch in places. Pepper generously, tear by hand and eat standing up.']::text[]),
('daube-provencale','en','Provençal daube','Aix-en-Provence, Provence, France','Beef marinated in wine and orange, stewed for four hours in a narrow-necked daubière that traps the steam. Black olives and a dried orange peel go in: that is what separates it from a bourguignon.',ARRAY['Marinate the beef in large cubes for 24 hours in red wine with carrot, onion, garlic, thyme, bay and orange peel.','Drain and dry the meat, then sear it in very hot oil in small batches.','Fry the lardons and the marinade vegetables in the same pot.','Return everything, pour in the marinade brought to the boil and the tomato, and simmer for 4 hours over very low heat, covered.','Add the stoned olives for the last half hour. Serve with macaroni, as they do in Nice, or cool it for the next day.']::text[]),
('tielle-setoise','en','Tielle from Sète','Sète, Languedoc, France','An octopus pie in a spiced tomato sauce, brought by fishermen from Gaeta who settled in Sète in the 19th century. Made only at home for decades, it was not sold until 1937.',ARRAY['Cook the octopus for 45 minutes at a gentle boil with the bay, then let it cool in its water and dice it small.','Knead the flour, warm water, yeast, oil and salt, and prove for an hour.','Make a sauce by reducing tomato, onion, garlic, white wine, paprika and chilli for 30 minutes, until very thick.','Mix the octopus into the sauce and let it cool: hot, the filling would sog the pastry.','Line small tins, fill them, cover with a disc of dough, seal by crimping the edge and bake for 30 minutes at 200°C. Eaten warm.']::text[]),
('cargolade','en','Cargolade','Perpignan, Roussillon, France','Snails grilled over a vine-shoot fire, opening upwards, basted drop by drop with melting bacon fat. Less a dish than a gathering: an afternoon spent over aioli and valley wine.',ARRAY['Starve the snails for a week, then wash them at length in vinegared water until it runs clear.','Set them opening upwards on a grill, packed tightly together, and salt each opening.','Light a fire of vine shoots and let it fall to embers: an open flame would make them burst.','Grill for 15 minutes, then lay the bacon slices on a rack above: the melting fat drips into the shells.','Mount an aioli in a mortar with the garlic, yolks, salt and oil in a thread. Eat the snails by hand, dipped in it.']::text[]),
('mouclade-charentaise','en','Mouclade charentaise','La Rochelle, Aunis, France','Bouchot mussels in a cream sauce with curry and Pineau des Charentes. The curry surprises: it arrived in the 17th century through the port of La Rochelle, on East India Company ships.',ARRAY['Scrape and beard the mussels, discarding any that stay open after a tap on the worktop.','Open them over high heat for 4 minutes with the shallots, white wine and bay, then lift them out.','Strain the liquor through a fine cloth: there is always a little sand at the bottom.','Make a pale roux with the butter and flour, add the curry, whisk in the strained liquor and cook for 5 minutes.','Bind with the yolks beaten into the cream, off the heat. Remove one shell from each mussel, coat them and scatter parsley.']::text[]),
('lamproie-a-la-bordelaise','en','Lamprey à la bordelaise','Libourne, Gironde, France','Garonne lamprey stewed in red wine with leek and bound with its own blood. A spring dish, increasingly rare: the fish is now protected and the fishery closely regulated.',ARRAY['Scald the lamprey to remove the mucus, bleed it and keep the blood in a bowl with a glass of wine.','Pull out the dorsal nerve by tugging the white cord near the head: without this, the dish would be bitter.','Cut into sections and fry with the onion, garlic and ham in butter, then flame with marc.','Add the white of the leeks in lengths, dust with flour, pour in the red wine and simmer for 1½ hours over low heat.','Bind with the blood off the heat, never boiling again. Serve on garlic-rubbed croutons — better still reheated the next day.']::text[]),
('mique-du-perigord','en','Périgord mique','Périgueux, Périgord, France','A large ball of leavened bread poached in the broth of a salt-pork stew. It swells as it cooks and is sliced at the table; the poor man''s bread, baked without an oven, in the pot.',ARRAY['Desalt the pork for 12 hours, then cook it in three litres of water with the bay for an hour.','Knead the flour, the yeast dissolved in warm milk, the eggs, duck fat and salt; prove for 2 hours.','Add the vegetables in large pieces to the broth.','Shape the dough into a large ball without reworking it, and lower it gently onto the simmering broth.','Cover and poach for 45 minutes, turning it halfway: it doubles in size. Slice it at the table, with the stew.']::text[]),
('tourin-a-l-ail','en','Garlic tourin','Agen, Lot-et-Garonne, France','A garlic soup threaded with egg white and finished with a yolk beaten with vinegar. It was brought to newlyweds in the middle of their wedding night, in a tureen, with an insistence whose motive one can guess.',ARRAY['Peel two whole heads of garlic, remove the green germ and crush the cloves with the flat of a knife.','Soften them very gently in the duck fat for 10 minutes, never letting them colour: burnt garlic would make the soup bitter.','Dust with flour, pour in the boiling water and simmer for 20 minutes.','Trickle the beaten whites into the simmering broth, stirring: they set into threads.','Off the heat, bind with the yolks beaten with vinegar. Serve over stale bread, with parsley.']::text[]),
('estofinado','en','Estofinado','Villefranche-de-Rouergue, Rouergue, France','Dried cod rehydrated and crushed with potatoes, garlic and walnut oil. Norwegian stockfish came up the Lot by barge as far as Decazeville: which is why this sea dish is eaten three hundred kilometres inland.',ARRAY['Soak the stockfish for four days in cold running water, or changing the water morning and evening.','Poach it for 20 minutes without boiling, then flake it patiently, removing skin and bones.','Boil the potatoes in salted water and rice them while still hot.','Combine the fish, potatoes, crushed garlic, chopped hard-boiled eggs and pounded walnuts.','Work in the walnut oil and cream with a wooden spoon over very low heat, until the mass turns silky. Sharpen with lemon and cover with parsley.']::text[]),
('truffade','en','Truffade','Aurillac, Cantal, France','Fried potatoes crushed with fresh Cantal tomme until the cheese pulls into long ribbons. This is not aligot: here the potatoes stay in pieces, and there is no cream.',ARRAY['Cut the potatoes into 5 mm slices and fry them in duck fat for 20 minutes, covered then uncovered.','Add the lardons and crushed garlic, and let everything colour.','Crush roughly with a spatula, keeping whole pieces: truffade is not a purée.','Slice the fresh tomme thinly and fold it in off the heat, lifting the mass.','Return to low heat and work with the spatula until the cheese pulls into threads. Serve at once, in the pan.']::text[]),
('rillettes-du-mans','en','Rillettes du Mans','Le Mans, Maine, France','Pork cooked for five hours in its own fat then pulled apart with forks, never ground. Those of Le Mans are brown and fibrous, unlike the rillettes of Tours, paler and fattier.',ARRAY['Cut the meats into large 5 cm strips and salt them the day before with the pepper and aromatics.','Melt the fat in the base of a cast-iron pot, then add the meats without colouring them.','Moisten with white wine, cover and cook for 5 hours at 120°C, stirring every hour.','The meat should fall apart on its own: shred it with two forks along the fibres, never in a machine.','Adjust the salt when cold — hot fat masks it — then pot them up and seal with melted fat.']::text[]),
('far-breton','en','Far breton with prunes','Quimper, Cornouaille, France','A thick dense custard, between clafoutis and pudding, studded with steeped prunes. Far comes from the Latin far, spelt flour: it began as a savoury porridge, served with bacon.',ARRAY['Steep the prunes in warm rum for at least two hours, ideally overnight.','Whisk the eggs and sugar, add the sifted flour and salt without working it, then the warm milk little by little.','Stir in the melted butter and vanilla; the batter should be as fluid as crêpe batter.','Rest for an hour, then pour into a buttered dish and scatter over the drained prunes.','Bake for 50 minutes at 200°C: the far rises then sinks as it cools, which is normal. Eat it warm or cold, never hot.']::text[]),
('teurgoule','en','Teurgoule','Honfleur, Normandy, France','A rice pudding baked for five hours in a bread oven, until it forms a thick brown skin. The name is said to come from ''tord-gueule'', a mouth-twister: children burnt themselves trying to eat it too soon.',ARRAY['Mix the rice, sugar, cinnamon, nutmeg and a pinch of salt in a tall narrow dish, preferably stoneware.','Pour the cold milk over and stir once only: that is the last gesture, it will not be touched again.','Bake at 150°C for an hour, then lower to 130°C.','Cook for four more hours without ever opening or stirring: a thick brown skin forms on the surface.','The teurgoule is ready when that skin is almost black and the rice a deep beige. Eat it warm, with fallue brioche if you can find it.']::text[]),
('paris-brest','en','Paris-Brest','Versailles, Île-de-France, France','A ring of choux pastry filled with praline mousseline cream, its shape recalling a bicycle wheel. Created in 1910 for the Paris-Brest-Paris cycle race, from which it takes its name.',ARRAY['Make the praline: caramelise 100 g of sugar, pour it over the roasted hazelnuts, let it set and blend at length into a flowing paste.','Make choux pastry: bring the water, 125 ml of milk, 100 g of butter and the salt to the boil, tip in the flour, dry it out, then beat in the eggs one at a time.','Pipe a 22 cm ring with a fluted nozzle, double it with a second circle and a third on top, and scatter flaked almonds.','Bake for 40 minutes at 180°C without opening the oven for the first 25 minutes.','Make a pastry cream with the remaining milk, yolks, sugar and cornstarch; once cold, beat it with 250 g of soft butter and the praline. Fill the ring, split in two.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('flammekueche','en','Tarte flambée','Obernai, Alsace, France','Dough rolled paper-thin, spread with cream, onion and bacon, and baked for two minutes against the floor of a bread oven. It was used to judge the oven''s heat before the loaves went in.',ARRAY['Knead flour, water, oil and salt for 10 minutes into a supple yeastless dough, then rest it for 30 minutes.','Mix the sour cream and drained fromage blanc, season with salt, pepper and nutmeg: the mixture must be thick, never runny.','Slice the onions thinly and cut the bacon into large lardons; do not cook them, they cook on the tart.','Roll the dough until you can see the table through it, into a rectangle that fits your oven.','Spread the cream, scatter onion and bacon, and bake in the hottest part of the oven on a hot floor for 8 to 10 minutes. The edges should blacken in places.']::text[]),
('matelote-au-riesling','en','Riesling matelote','Sélestat, Alsace, France','Four river fish — eel, pike, zander, trout — poached in Riesling and coated in a cream sauce. The inns along the Ill have long made it their feast dish, served with noodles.',ARRAY['Sweat the sliced shallots in half the butter, add the Riesling and bay, and bring to a simmer.','Poach the fish from firmest to most delicate — eel first, trout last — 4 to 8 minutes each, then keep them warm.','Strain the broth and reduce it by half over high heat.','Make a roux with the remaining butter and the flour, whisk in the reduced broth and cook for 8 minutes.','Add the sautéed mushrooms and the cream, bind with the yolks off the heat, coat the fish and serve with buttered noodles.']::text[]),
('coq-au-riesling','en','Coq au Riesling','Ribeauvillé, Alsace, France','The white cousin of coq au vin: the wine is dry and mineral, the sauce stays pale, and morels replace the bacon. Served with spätzle, never with potatoes.',ARRAY['Soak the morels for an hour in warm water; strain that water carefully and keep it.','Brown the chicken pieces in the butter without scorching them: the sauce must stay pale.','Flame with marc, dust with flour, then pour in the Riesling and the morel liquid.','Add thyme and bay, cover and simmer for 50 minutes over low heat.','Lift out the chicken, add the cream and mushrooms to the sauce, reduce for 10 minutes, then return the pieces. Serve with spätzle.']::text[]),
('bouchee-a-la-reine','en','Bouchée à la reine','Lunéville, Lorraine, France','A vol-au-vent filled with chicken, quenelles and mushrooms in a white sauce. It bears the name of Marie Leszczyńska, queen of France and daughter of the Duke of Lorraine, who is said to have had it served at Versailles.',ARRAY['Cut twelve discs of puff pastry, hollow the centre from six of them to make rings, stack and glaze with egg.','Bake for 25 minutes at 200°C, then carefully lift the lid and hollow out the inside.','Poach the chicken and veal for 25 minutes in the broth, then cut into one-centimetre dice.','Make a velouté with the butter, flour and strained broth, and cook it for 15 minutes over low heat.','Add the sautéed mushrooms and the meats, bind with the yolks beaten into the cream off the heat, sharpen with lemon, and fill the scalding cases.']::text[]),
('gougeres','en','Gougères','Tonnerre, Burgundy, France','Small comté choux buns, served warm as an aperitif in every Burgundy cellar. They were offered to buyers during tastings: the cheese rounds out the wine and makes the tannins seem softer.',ARRAY['Bring the water, butter and salt to the boil, tip in the flour all at once and beat briskly.','Dry the paste out for two minutes over the heat, until it forms a ball that leaves the pan.','Off the heat, work in the eggs one at a time: the paste should form a soft beak on the spatula.','Add three quarters of the grated comté, the nutmeg and the white pepper.','Pipe well-spaced mounds, dust with the remaining cheese and bake for 30 minutes at 180°C without opening the oven.']::text[]),
('oeufs-en-meurette','en','Œufs en meurette','Nuits-Saint-Georges, Burgundy, France','Eggs poached in red wine, served on a crouton under a sauce of lardons and button onions. The poaching wine becomes the sauce: nothing is wasted, and the whites take on a disconcerting purple tint.',ARRAY['Fry the lardons, button onions and mushrooms in the butter, then set them aside.','Sweat the shallots, dust with flour, pour in the red wine and broth, add thyme and bay, and reduce for 25 minutes.','Strain the sauce and set a good glassful aside in a small pan for poaching the eggs.','Break the eggs one at a time into that simmering wine and poach for 3 minutes; drain them on a cloth.','Rub the toasted croutons with garlic, set the eggs on them, coat with sauce and cover with the garnish.']::text[]),
('jambon-persille','en','Jambon persillé','Mâcon, Burgundy, France','Ham cooked on the bone, pulled apart and set in a jelly of white wine and parsley. The Burgundian Easter terrine, whose green and pink appear at the cut like marble.',ARRAY['Desalt the ham for 12 hours in cold water, changing it three times.','Simmer it for 3 hours with the veal foot, white wine, vegetables and aromatics.','Let it cool in the broth, then pull the meat apart by hand into large pieces, without chopping it.','Strain the broth, cool it and check that it sets; if not, reduce it further.','Layer ham and parsley mixture — parsley, garlic, shallot, tarragon — in a terrine, pour over the warm jelly and press overnight in the fridge.']::text[]),
('poulet-de-bresse-a-la-creme','en','Bresse chicken in cream','Bourg-en-Bresse, Ain, France','The only poultry in the world with a protected designation of origin, cooked in butter and drowned in Étrez cream. The dish is disarmingly simple: the bird does all the work.',ARRAY['Joint the bird into eight, salt it and let it colour skin-side down in foaming butter, without browning it hard.','Add the sliced shallots, cover and stew for 25 minutes over very low heat.','Take out the breasts, which cook faster, and continue for 15 minutes with the legs.','Deglaze with white wine, reduce until dry, then pour in the cream and let it coat for 10 minutes.','Return all the chicken, add the sautéed mushrooms, the tarragon and a dash of vin jaune. Sharpen with lemon at the last moment.']::text[]),
('quenelles-sauce-nantua','en','Pike quenelles, sauce Nantua','Nantua, Bugey, France','Pike quenelles that swell in the oven until they treble in size, under a pink sauce of crayfish butter. Lake Nantua supplied both: the fish and the red-clawed crayfish.',ARRAY['Make a panade: bring the milk and 100 g of butter to the boil, tip in the flour, dry it out, then cool completely.','Blend the very cold pike flesh, work in the panade then the eggs one at a time, and finally 100 g of softened butter. Chill for 3 hours.','Fry the crayfish with shallot and carrot, flame with marc, add the tomato paste, cover with water and cook for 20 minutes. Strain, pressing hard, then enrich with the cream.','Shape the quenelles with two spoons and poach them for 10 minutes in simmering salted water: they should rise.','Arrange them in a dish, coat with the Nantua sauce and bake for 25 minutes at 200°C. They swell and brown.']::text[]),
('tablier-de-sapeur','en','Tablier de sapeur','Lyon, France','Tripe marinated in white wine, breaded and grilled, served with sauce gribiche. Marshal de Castellane, governor of Lyon and a former sapper, is said to have given his nickname to this cut, flat as a leather apron.',ARRAY['Marinate the pre-cooked tripe for 12 hours in the white wine with salt and pepper.','Pat it dry, spread mustard on both sides, then coat in beaten egg and breadcrumbs.','Let the crumb dry for 30 minutes in the fridge: it will hold better during cooking.','Cook in clarified butter over medium heat, 6 minutes a side, until the crust is golden and crisp.','Make the gribiche by combining mayonnaise, chopped hard-boiled egg, gherkins, capers and herbs. Serve with a lemon wedge.']::text[]),
('gratinee-a-l-oignon','en','French onion soup','Paris, France','The soup of Les Halles, swallowed scalding at five in the morning by market porters and night owls. It is all patience: the onions must melt for a full hour before they take on their mahogany colour.',ARRAY['Slice the onions very finely and melt them in the butter, covered, for 30 minutes without colouring.','Uncover and continue for 40 minutes, stirring often: they should turn brown and jammy, never burnt.','Dust with flour, deglaze with white wine and marc, then pour in the hot broth.','Add thyme and bay and simmer for 25 minutes; salt late, as the broth reduces.','Divide between bowls, lay on the garlic-rubbed croutons, cover with a thick layer of gruyère and grill for 8 minutes.']::text[]),
('hachis-parmentier','en','Hachis Parmentier','Paris, France','Beef from the pot-au-feu, chopped and topped with golden mash. The name honours Antoine Parmentier, who got the French to accept the potato by having it guarded by soldiers — so that it would be stolen.',ARRAY['Cook the beef for 2 hours as a pot-au-feu, or use yesterday''s leftovers — that is the spirit of the dish.','Chop it by knife, never in a machine: a blade crushes the fibres and makes the filling pasty.','Soften onion, carrot and garlic, add the meat, tomato paste, wine and broth, and stew for 20 minutes.','Rice the potatoes with the butter and hot milk, then bind with the egg yolk.','Spread the meat, cover with the mash, fork the surface, dust with gruyère and bake for 35 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('navarin-d-agneau','en','Spring lamb navarin','Paris, France','A lamb stew with spring vegetables, each turned by hand and glazed separately so they stay distinct. The name may come from the turnip, or from the battle of Navarino — cooks have never settled it.',ARRAY['Sear the lamb in large pieces over high heat, in small batches, until well coloured.','Sprinkle with sugar and let it caramelise for a minute: this is what gives the sauce its colour.','Dust with flour, add the tomato paste and garlic, pour in the broth and simmer for 1 hour.','Turn the turnips and carrots into olive shapes and glaze them in butter and sugar in a separate pan.','Add the potatoes and glazed vegetables for the last 25 minutes; the blanched peas and beans only at the very end.']::text[]),
('sole-normande','en','Sole normande','Dieppe, Normandy, France','Sole poached in cider, crowned with mussels, prawns and mushrooms under a cream sauce. Created around 1830 at the Rocher de Cancale in Paris, it was the dish that made Norman cooking famous.',ARRAY['Open the mussels in the cider with a shallot for 4 minutes, then shell them and strain the liquor through a cloth.','Fillet the sole, roll the fillets and lay them in a buttered dish with the remaining shallots.','Moisten with the strained mussel liquor, cover with buttered paper and bake for 12 minutes at 180°C.','Pour off the cooking juices, reduce them by half, then thicken with a white roux and the cream.','Off the heat, add the yolks and lemon. Coat the fillets and surround them with the mussels, prawns and sautéed mushrooms.']::text[]),
('andouille-de-vire','en','Vire andouille with cider apples','Vire, Normandy, France','An andouille made of strips of pig''s stomach threaded by hand and then smoked for six weeks over beech. Served cold in thin slices, or warm with apples softened in cider.',ARRAY['Choose a genuinely smoked andouille, black-skinned with a visible grain in the slice: the stomach strips should be distinguishable.','Peel the apples, cut them into thick wedges and fry them in butter until golden.','Flame with calvados, moisten with cider and reduce by half, covered.','Add the cream and mustard, and let it coat for 5 minutes.','Fry the andouille slices for 2 minutes a side, no longer — it is already cooked — and serve on the apples, with toast.']::text[]),
('canard-a-la-rouennaise','en','Duck à la rouennaise','Rouen, Normandy, France','A duck smothered rather than bled, roasted rare, its carcass then pressed to extract the blood that will bind the sauce. The ritual of the silver press, at the table, belongs to the grand cooking of the 19th century.',ARRAY['Roast the duck for only 20 minutes at 220°C: the flesh must stay frankly pink at the centre.','Carve off the breast slices and legs, keep them warm, and break up the carcass.','Press the carcass — failing a press, crush it firmly in a sieve — to collect the blood and juices.','Reduce the red wine by half with the chopped shallots and cracked pepper.','Off the heat, work in the crushed liver, the carcass juices and the butter in pieces, never boiling. Sharpen with lemon and coat the breast slices.']::text[]),
('cotriade','en','Cotriade','Lorient, Brittany, France','The soup of Breton sailors, made on board with the share of the catch that was theirs. The broth is eaten first over bread, then the fish and potatoes, dressed with a sharp vinaigrette.',ARRAY['Melt the sliced onions in the salted butter, then add the garlic, thyme and bay.','Cover with two litres of water, salt it, and cook the potatoes in large pieces for 20 minutes.','Add the fish from firmest to most delicate, in three stages, allowing 5 to 10 minutes each.','Lift out the fish and potatoes with a slotted spoon and keep them warm on a platter.','Serve the broth as a soup over slices of bread, then the fish dressed with a mustardy vinaigrette and parsley.']::text[]),
('coquilles-saint-jacques-bretonne','en','Breton scallops','Saint-Brieuc, Brittany, France','Scallops in their shell under a parsley crust of salted butter and breadcrumbs. The bay of Saint-Brieuc is France''s largest bed: fishing there is limited to forty-five minutes a day.',ARRAY['Shuck the scallops, separate the white from the coral and wash the deep shells thoroughly.','Cream the salted butter with very finely chopped shallot and garlic, the parsley and a little lemon.','Sweat the remaining shallots in white wine and reduce until dry.','Set a scallop and its coral in each shell, cover with a knob of parsley butter and dust with breadcrumbs.','Bake for 8 minutes at 240°C at the top of the oven: the scallops should stay pearly at the centre.']::text[]),
('gateau-breton','en','Gâteau breton','Vannes, Morbihan, France','A dense cake of salted butter and egg yolks, cross-hatched with a fork before baking. It kept for weeks: sailors took it to sea, where it lasted better than bread.',ARRAY['Take the butter out an hour ahead: it must be soft, never melted.','Beat it at length with the sugar, until the mixture pales and nearly doubles in volume.','Work in seven yolks one at a time, then the rum and vanilla.','Fold in the flour and a pinch of salt, lifting rather than beating: the cake should stay short.','Spread 3 cm deep in a buttered tin, glaze with the last yolk and score diamonds with a fork. Bake for 45 minutes at 170°C.']::text[]),
('rillons-de-tours','en','Rillons de Tours','Tours, Touraine, France','Large cubes of pork belly confited in lard then caramelised in Vouvray wine, crisp outside and melting within. Balzac, a son of Touraine, cites them in Le Lys dans la vallée as a childhood memory.',ARRAY['Cut the belly with its rind into 5 cm cubes and salt them for 12 hours in coarse salt.','Rinse, dry thoroughly, then colour them on all sides in the melted lard.','Turn the heat to its lowest, add garlic, thyme and bay, and confit for 2½ hours covered: the fat must never bubble hard.','Take off the lid, raise the heat and let the rind crisp for 15 minutes.','Deglaze with the white wine and sugar, let it caramelise while stirring, and serve warm with a lamb''s lettuce salad.']::text[]),
('sandre-au-beurre-blanc','en','Zander with beurre blanc','Nantes, Pays de la Loire, France','A Loire fish under an emulsion of butter, shallot and Muscadet. Clémence Lefeuvre, a cook at Saint-Julien-de-Concelles, is said to have invented beurre blanc around 1890 by botching a béarnaise.',ARRAY['Chop the shallots as finely as you can and reduce them with the wine and vinegar until dry.','Two spoonfuls of moist, almost syrupy compote should remain: that is the base of the emulsion.','Add the cream — it stabilises the sauce — then whisk in the very cold butter, cube by cube, off the heat.','The sauce must stay warm: too hot it splits, too cold it sets. Never let it boil.','Cook the zander fillets skin-side down for 6 minutes, turn for a minute, and coat with the beurre blanc.']::text[]),
('gateau-nantais','en','Gâteau nantais','Nantes, Pays de la Loire, France','An almond cake soaked in rum under a white icing. It was born of the triangular trade, which brought sugar and rum from the Caribbean up to Nantes — an origin the city now faces squarely.',ARRAY['Cream the softened butter with the sugar until the mixture pales.','Add the ground almonds, then the eggs one at a time, the flour, vanilla and half the rum.','Pour into a buttered tin and bake for 40 minutes at 170°C; the cake should stay moist at the centre.','As it comes out, prick it and soak it with the remaining rum, then let it cool in the tin.','Make a thick icing with the icing sugar and a spoonful of water, spread it over the top and let it set for two hours.']::text[]),
('mogettes-au-jambon','en','Mogette beans with ham','Luçon, Vendée, France','The white bean of the Vendée, cooked in plain water and served under a grilled slice of ham, on bread rubbed with garlic. Nothing else: the mogette has a skin so fine it melts, and needs no help.',ARRAY['Soak the beans for two hours only — any longer and they would burst.','Put them in cold unsalted water with onion, carrot, garlic, thyme and bay; salt only halfway through.','Simmer for 1½ hours without ever boiling: a hard boil would loosen the skins.','Drain, keeping a little of the liquor, and enrich with the salted butter and cream.','Toast the bread, rub it with garlic, spoon the beans on top and crown with a slice of just-seared ham.']::text[]),
('cagouilles-charentaise','en','Charente snails','Angoulême, Charente, France','Small grey snails stewed in the Charente manner, with ham, sausage meat and a great deal of garlic. The people of Charente call themselves cagouillards: slow as the snail, and proud of it.',ARRAY['Starve the snails for a week, wash them in vinegared water until it runs clear, then blanch them for 10 minutes.','Take them from their shells, remove the black coil and rinse once more.','Fry the diced ham, sausage meat and shallots in the oil, then dust with flour.','Add the snails, crushed garlic, tomato, white wine, thyme and bay; cover with water.','Simmer for 1¼ hours over low heat, add a great deal of parsley and serve with country bread.']::text[]),
('ttoro','en','Ttoro','Saint-Jean-de-Luz, Basque Country, France','The fish soup of Basque cod fishermen, sharpened with Espelette pepper and thickened with bread. Made on board with hake heads and bones; ashore, it became a feast dish.',ARRAY['Make a stock with the hake heads and bones for 30 minutes, then strain it, pressing well.','Soften the onions, garlic and peppers in the oil, add the crushed tomatoes and reduce for 20 minutes.','Moisten with the stock and white wine, add the Espelette pepper and crumbled stale bread; thicken for 15 minutes.','Poach the hake sections for 8 minutes in this broth, without letting it boil.','Add the mussels and prawns, cover for 4 minutes until the shells open, and cover with parsley.']::text[]),
('gateau-basque','en','Gâteau basque','Cambo-les-Bains, Basque Country, France','A thick shortcrust enclosing either pastry cream or black cherry jam from Itxassou. The two camps do not agree, and every village holds its own version to be the only authentic one.',ARRAY['Cream the softened butter with 200 g of sugar, add two eggs, then the flour, ground almonds and baking powder.','Bring together without kneading, wrap and rest for 3 hours in the fridge: this dough is soft and only handles well when cold.','Make a pastry cream with the milk, yolks, remaining sugar and cornstarch; flavour with rum and cool it.','Line a deep tin with two thirds of the dough and fill with the cream — or cherry jam — stopping short of the edge.','Cover with the remaining dough, seal, glaze with egg and score with a fork. Bake for 40 minutes at 180°C.']::text[]),
('tourtiere-landaise','en','Landes tourtière','Mont-de-Marsan, Landes, France','Sheets of dough stretched by hand until translucent, buttered, crumpled and piled over apples in armagnac. Cut into, it collapses into a thousand shards — the sign of a successful one.',ARRAY['Knead flour, warm water, oil and salt for 20 minutes into a very elastic dough; oil it and rest for 2 hours.','Fry the sliced apples with 100 g of sugar, flame with armagnac and let them cool.','Stretch the dough by hand over a large floured cloth, working from the centre, until you can see the weave through it.','Brush generously with melted butter and cut into wide squares.','Layer crumpled pastry and apples in a tin, finish with three crumpled layers, sugar them and bake for 30 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('millas','en','Millas','Toulouse, Occitania, France','A maize porridge set in a slab, cut into diamonds and fried in butter, sugared as it comes out. Savoury, it accompanied confit; sweetened, it became the after-school treat of the South-West.',ARRAY['Bring the milk, water, zest and a pinch of salt to a simmer.','Rain in the cornmeal, whisking constantly to avoid lumps.','Stir with a wooden spoon for 40 minutes over low heat: the mass should pull away from the sides.','Off the heat, work in the eggs, 50 g of butter and the orange blossom water, then spread 2 cm deep in a dampened dish. Set overnight.','Cut into diamonds, fry them in salted butter 4 minutes a side and sugar them generously as they leave the pan.']::text[]),
('gardiane-de-taureau','en','Camargue bull stew','Aigues-Mortes, Camargue, France','Camargue bull meat stewed in red wine with black olives and orange peel, served over Camargue rice. The dish of the gardians, the mounted herdsmen of the delta marshes.',ARRAY['Marinate the meat in large cubes for 24 hours in the red wine with the vegetables, orange peel and aromatics.','Drain, dry thoroughly, then sear the meat in very hot oil, in small batches.','Fry the lardons and the marinade vegetables, then dust with flour.','Pour in the marinade brought to the boil, add the juniper, and simmer for 3 hours over very low heat.','Add the stoned olives for the last half hour. Serve over plain Camargue rice.']::text[]),
('petits-pates-de-pezenas','en','Pézenas little pies','Pézenas, Languedoc, France','Small cylinders of pastry filled with mutton and sugar, sweet and savoury like a medieval recipe. They are said to come from the Indian cooks of Lord Clive of India, who stayed at Pézenas in 1768.',ARRAY['Rub the cold butter into the flour, bind with an egg and a little iced water, and chill for an hour.','Chop the mutton and fat by knife into small dice, then mix with the brown sugar, zest, spices and rum.','Rest the filling overnight: the sugar must dissolve into it completely.','Cut strips of pastry, form small cylinders 5 cm high, fill them and close with a pleated disc of dough.','Glaze with egg and bake for 35 minutes at 190°C. Eaten warm, never scalding.']::text[]),
('bourride','en','Bourride','Martigues, Provence, France','The white sister of bouillabaisse: the fish broth is bound with aioli off the heat until it becomes a silky cream. The whole difficulty is there — one degree too hot and the sauce splits.',ARRAY['Make a stock with the trimmings, leek, fennel, onion, white wine and orange peel for 25 minutes; strain it.','Mount an aioli in a mortar: garlic crushed with salt, three yolks, then oil in a thread until firm.','Poach the fish sections for 10 minutes in the simmering stock, then lift them out and keep them warm.','Slacken half the aioli with two yolks, then pour a ladle of hot stock over it, whisking.','Return everything to the pan off the heat and stir constantly until it thickens, never exceeding 80°C.']::text[]),
('pieds-et-paquets','en','Pieds et paquets','Sisteron, Provence, France','Lamb tripe stuffed with bacon and garlic, tied into small parcels and stewed for eight hours with sheep''s trotters. A dish of absolute patience, prepared the day before for Sunday.',ARRAY['Blanch the tripe for 15 minutes, rinse it and cut it into 10 cm squares.','Mix the chopped bacon, garlic and parsley; place a spoonful on each square and fold into a parcel.','Fasten each parcel by cutting a slit in the tripe and threading the opposite corner through — never string, tradition forbids it.','Pack the parcels and trotters tightly in a pot with onion, carrot, tomato, white wine, zest and aromatics.','Cover with water, bring to a bare simmer and cook for 8 hours at 120°C. Serve the next day, reheated.']::text[]),
('tourte-de-blettes','en','Swiss chard pie','Menton, French Riviera, France','A sweet pie of chard leaves, apple, pine nuts and raisins. The vegetable vanishes under the sugar and leaves only a green freshness: nobody guesses, and that is the whole point.',ARRAY['Rub together the flour, butter and 80 g of sugar, bind with an egg and rest for an hour in the fridge.','Strip the ribs from the chard and keep only the green; shred it finely and salt it for 20 minutes to draw out water.','Squeeze it dry by the handful, then mix with the grated apple, pine nuts, rum-soaked raisins, remaining sugar, zest and parmesan.','Line a tin, fill it, cover with a lid of pastry and seal the edges by crimping.','Bake for 45 minutes at 180°C, cool completely and dust with icing sugar. Eaten cold.']::text[]),
('crozets-au-beaufort','en','Crozets with beaufort','Bourg-Saint-Maurice, Savoie, France','Tiny squares of buckwheat pasta, baked under beaufort with local sausage. The name comes from the Savoyard croé, cross: they were once cut one by one with a knife on the farmhouse table.',ARRAY['Knead the flours and eggs into a hard dough, rest it for an hour, then roll it very thin.','Cut 5 mm squares — it takes time, but that is what makes a crozet.','Fry the lardons and onions in the butter, deglaze with white wine and reduce.','Cook the crozets for 12 minutes in boiling salted water: they stay firm.','Toss everything with the cream and two thirds of the beaufort, tip into a dish, cover with the rest and brown for 15 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('farcon-savoyard','en','Savoyard farçon','Megève, Savoie, France','A cake of grated potato, prunes and bacon, cooked for four hours in a bain-marie in a mould lined with bacon. Sweet and savoury, it went to the village oven in the morning and came back for supper.',ARRAY['Grate the raw potatoes finely and squeeze them in a cloth: the water would ruin the texture.','Mix with the eggs, cream, sugar, spices, stoned prunes, raisins and diced pears.','Line a charlotte mould with bacon rashers, letting them hang over the sides.','Fill it, fold the rashers over the top and cover with buttered paper and a lid.','Cook for 4 hours in a bain-marie at 150°C. Let it settle for 20 minutes before turning out and slicing.']::text[]),
('bacalhau-com-natas','en','Bacalhau com natas','Lisbon, Portugal','Flaked salt cod and matchstick fried potatoes bound with béchamel and cream, browned in the oven. Said to be the most ordered restaurant dish in Portugal — and certainly the most consoling.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod for 36 hours, changing the water six times, then poach it for 8 minutes in the milk with the bay; flake it and keep the milk.','Cut the potatoes into matchsticks and fry them until barely golden: they will finish cooking in the oven.','Soften the onion and garlic in the olive oil, add the flaked cod and let it take up the flavour.','Make a béchamel with the butter, flour and infused milk, then loosen it with the cream and season with nutmeg.','Combine cod, potatoes and sauce, tip into a dish and bake for 25 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('carne-vinha-d-alhos','en','Carne de vinha d’alhos','Funchal, Madeira, Portugal','Pork marinated for a week in wine and garlic, then fried and served in a bolo do caco. Portuguese navigators carried this marinade as far as Goa, where it became vindaloo.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into 4 cm cubes and put them in a jar with the wine, vinegar, crushed garlic, bay, thyme and chilli.','Marinate for five to seven days in the cold, stirring daily: the meat should pale and tenderise.','Drain and dry thoroughly, then fry the cubes in lard until well coloured.','Pour a glass of strained marinade into the pan and reduce to a glaze, coating the meat.','Serve with orange slices, roast sweet potato and warm bolo do caco.']::text[]),
('bolo-de-mel','en','Madeira honey cake','Funchal, Madeira, Portugal','The Madeiran Christmas cake, made with cane honey and thirteen spices, prepared on 8 December and broken only by hand — never cut. It keeps for a year and improves with age.',ARRAY['Warm the cane honey with the butter and lard until melted, then let it cool a little.','Pound the cinnamon, cloves, fennel and star anise in a mortar and mix them into the flour with the bicarbonate.','Work in the warm honey, sugar, rum and zest, and knead for 10 minutes: the dough is heavy and sticky.','Add the candied fruit, chopped walnuts and almonds, then rest the dough for three days in the cold, covered.','Divide between shallow round tins and bake for 50 minutes at 170°C. Wait three weeks before touching it.']::text[]),
('arroz-de-tamboril','en','Monkfish rice','Peniche, Estremadura, Portugal','A monkfish rice served loose in an earthenware dish, with prawns and coriander. In Portugal a fish rice must be malandrinho — badly behaved, they say: it must on no account hold together.',ARRAY['Make a stock with the monkfish head and backbone for 25 minutes, then strain it.','Make a refogado: onion, garlic and pepper softened slowly in oil, then grated tomato cooked down.','Pour in the white wine, let it evaporate, add the rice and toast it for two minutes.','Moisten with boiling stock — three times the volume of rice, the dish must stay very wet — and cook for 12 minutes.','Add the monkfish in large medallions and the prawns for 6 minutes, then cut the heat and cover with chopped coriander.']::text[]),
('caldeirada-de-enguias','en','Eel caldeirada','Murtosa, Ria de Aveiro, Portugal','Eels from the Ria de Aveiro stewed in layers with potatoes, onions and peppers. The moliceiro fishermen — whose sailing boats harvested seaweed — cooked it on board in a cast-iron pot.',ARRAY['Have the eels cut into 6 cm sections and let them draw with coarse salt for 30 minutes, then rinse them.','Cut all the vegetables into even 5 mm rounds.','Build the pot in layers: onion, potato, pepper, tomato, eel, salting and oiling each storey.','Repeat, finish with vegetables, pour in the white wine and water to come only halfway, and add paprika, chilli and bay.','Simmer for 35 minutes without stirring — shake the pot instead — and cover with coriander. Serve with firm polenta.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('pao-de-lo-de-ovar','en','Ovar pão de ló','Ovar, Beira Litoral, Portugal','An egg cake deliberately underbaked, its heart left runny as custard. It is baked in a paper-lined mould, and success is judged by the wobble of the centre as it leaves the oven.',ARRAY['Line a round mould with baking paper, letting it overhang generously, without buttering it.','Whisk the yolks, whole eggs and sugar over a warm bain-marie for 15 minutes: the mixture should treble and pale.','Take it off the heat and keep whisking until completely cold.','Fold in the sifted flour in three additions, lifting with a spatula and never knocking out the air.','Bake for 30 to 35 minutes at 180°C. The rim should be set and the centre still liquid: take it out before you feel reassured.']::text[]),
('sopa-de-peixe-algarvia','en','Algarve fish soup','Olhão, Algarve, Portugal','A rockfish soup thickened with bread, scented with coriander and chilli. Olhão, a port of tuna boats and canneries, has made it its everyday soup since the town began.',ARRAY['Cook the rockfish for 25 minutes in two litres of water with the bay, then pass the broth through a food mill.','Soften the onion, garlic and pepper in the oil, add the grated tomato and reduce for 20 minutes.','Pour in the strained broth and white wine, add the chilli and simmer for 10 minutes.','Crumble the stale bread into the soup, let it swell, then crush it with a wooden spoon.','Poach the eggs directly in the soup for 3 minutes, and cover with chopped coriander to serve.']::text[]),
('xerem-de-conquilhas','en','Xerém with cockles','Faro, Algarve, Portugal','A porridge of cracked maize from the Algarve, crowned with cockles opened in their own juice. Cousin to polenta and American grits, it bears an Arabic-derived name, like so much in the Portuguese south.',ARRAY['Purge the cockles for two hours in salted water so they release their sand.','Fry the bacon and chorizo, add the onion, garlic and tomato, and stew for 15 minutes.','Open the cockles separately with the white wine, 3 minutes covered, then strain their liquor carefully.','Pour a litre and a half of water and the cockle liquor over the refogado, bring to the boil, then rain in the cracked maize.','Stir for 35 minutes over low heat until thick, then add the cockles and plenty of coriander off the heat.']::text[]),
('ensopado-de-borrego','en','Lamb ensopado','Borba, Alentejo, Portugal','A lamb stew served over slices of bread that soak up the broth at the bottom of the plate. Ensopado means ''soaked'': the bread is not a side dish, it is half the dish.',ARRAY['Marinate the lamb in large pieces for 4 hours in the white wine, crushed garlic, paprika and bay.','Drain, dry and sear the meat in olive oil until well coloured.','Add the sliced onion and grated tomato, let them cook down, then pour in the strained marinade.','Cover with water and simmer for 1¼ hours; add the potatoes for the last 25 minutes.','Line deep plates with slices of bread, pour the scalding stew over them and cover with fresh mint.']::text[]),
('sericaia','en','Sericaia','Elvas, Alentejo, Portugal','An egg and cinnamon pudding, baked fiercely in an earthenware dish where it cracks into fissures. Served with candied Elvas plums, and its name is said to come from Serica — the ancients'' name for China.',ARRAY['Infuse the milk with the lemon zest and a cinnamon stick, then strain it.','Slake the flour in a little cold milk, then pour in the hot milk while whisking and thicken for 5 minutes.','Whisk the yolks and sugar to the ribbon, fold in the warm cream, then the whites beaten to peaks.','Ladle into an earthenware dish, letting each ladleful form a wave, and dust generously with cinnamon between each.','Bake for 25 minutes at 220°C: the surface should crack deeply. Serve warm with the plums.']::text[]),
('empadas-de-vila-vicosa','en','Vila Viçosa empadas','Vila Viçosa, Alentejo, Portugal','Small chicken pies in lard pastry, moulded in tin cups. They were sold outside mass and on the forecourts of shrines, warm, wrapped in paper.',ARRAY['Poach the hen for 1 hour with onion, garlic, bay and white wine; let it cool in its broth.','Bone it and chop the meat by knife into small pieces; mix with a little reduced broth, the nutmeg and lemon.','Knead the flour, melted lard, warm water and salt into a supple dough; rest it for 30 minutes.','Line small cylindrical moulds, fill with the mixture and cover with a disc of dough sealed with your thumb.','Glaze with egg, cut a vent and bake for 35 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('folar-de-chaves','en','Meat folar','Valpaços, Trás-os-Montes, Portugal','A brioche loaf stuffed with four smoked meats, baked at Easter in the communal ovens. The folar was given to godchildren on Easter Sunday, in exchange for the violets offered on Palm Sunday.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in the warm milk, then knead with the flour, eggs, oil and salt for 15 minutes.','Prove for 1½ hours: the dough should double and stay supple, almost sticky.','Cut all the cured meats into 1 cm dice and fry them briefly so they release a little fat.','Roll out the dough, scatter over the meats and paprika, roll it up, then coil the log into a ring in a round tin.','Prove for 45 minutes, glaze with egg and bake for 45 minutes at 180°C.']::text[]),
('cabrito-a-serrana','en','Serra-style roast kid','Bragança, Trás-os-Montes, Portugal','A milk-fed kid roasted in a wood oven with rice cooked in its juices, the Christmas and Easter dish of Portugal''s north-east. A marinade of red wine and paprika gives it its near-black crust.',ARRAY['Marinate the kid for 12 hours in the red wine, crushed garlic, paprika, chilli and bay.','Rub the meat with lard, set it on a bed of onions in an earthenware dish and pour over the marinade.','Roast for 1½ hours at 170°C, basting every 20 minutes, then add the quartered potatoes.','Take two ladles of the juices, use them to moisten the raw rice in a separate dish with water, and bake it alongside for 25 minutes.','Raise to 220°C for ten minutes to brown the meat. Serve the kid and rice together.']::text[]),
('pica-pau','en','Pica-pau','Leiria, Beira Litoral, Portugal','Cubes of beef sautéed with white wine and mustard, served scalding with gherkins and olives, speared with forks from a dish in the middle of the table. The name means ''woodpecker'': you peck at it.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into 2 cm cubes and salt them only at the last moment.','Heat the oil and butter until foaming, then sear the meat over very high heat, in small batches.','Add the crushed garlic, bay and chilli, and let them perfume the pan for 30 seconds without burning the garlic.','Deglaze with white wine, add the mustard and reduce for 3 minutes: the sauce should stay short and glossy.','Tip into a deep dish with the gherkins and olives, cover with parsley and serve with bread for mopping.']::text[]),
('travesseiros-de-colares','en','Travesseiros','Colares, Sintra, Portugal','''Pillows'' of puff pastry filled with an almond and egg cream, rolled in sugar as they leave the oven. The queue outside the Sintra pastry shop that created them has not thinned since 1943.',ARRAY['Make a puff pastry: a base of flour, iced water and salt, then three single turns with the butter, resting 30 minutes between each.','Cook the sugar and water to soft ball with the zest, then take off the heat.','Stir in the ground almonds, let it cool a little, then add the eggs and yolks off the heat.','Thicken this cream over very low heat, stirring constantly, then cool it completely.','Cut rectangles of pastry, pipe the cream along them, fold into pillows and seal. Bake for 20 minutes at 200°C and roll in sugar while still hot.']::text[]),
('porchetta-di-ariccia','en','Ariccia porchetta','Ariccia, Lazio, Italy','A whole boned pig, stuffed with herbs and roasted for five hours until the rind becomes a glazed shell. It is carved by knife in the markets of Lazio and eaten in a bread roll, with nothing else.',ARRAY['Lay the belly flat, rind down, and score the meat in a lattice without piercing the skin.','Pound the garlic, rosemary, fennel, pepper and salt into a paste, and rub it over the whole inner surface.','Lay the loin down the centre, roll tightly and tie every three centimetres.','Prick the rind all over, rub it with coarse salt and let it dry uncovered overnight in the fridge.','Roast for 4 hours at 150°C, then 45 minutes at 240°C to glaze the skin. Rest for an hour before carving.']::text[]),
('vincisgrassi','en','Vincisgrassi','Macerata, Marche, Italy','The ancestor of lasagne, with poultry offal, truffle and béchamel, its recipe printed as early as 1799. The name is said to come from the Austrian prince Windisch-Graetz, then besieging Ancona.',ARRAY['Knead the flour and eggs, rest for an hour, then roll very thin sheets and blanch them for a minute.','Fry the diced ham, minced meat and offal, moisten with white wine and tomato, and simmer for 1 hour.','Add the soaked and chopped porcini and give it another 20 minutes.','Make a thick béchamel with butter, flour and milk, seasoned with nutmeg.','Layer six sheets of pasta with sauce, béchamel and parmesan, grating truffle between the layers, and bake for 45 minutes at 180°C.']::text[]),
('cacciucco','en','Cacciucco','Livorno, Tuscany, Italy','The fish stew of Livorno, which must contain at least five species — as many as there are C''s in its name, says the harbour rule. The rockfish collapse into the sauce, the finer ones stay whole.',ARRAY['Start with the octopus and cuttlefish: they need 40 minutes, everything else needs ten.','Fry them in oil with the garlic and chilli, deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate.','Add the crushed tomato and sage, cover and simmer for 40 minutes.','Pass the separately cooked rockfish through a food mill and stir that purée into the pot.','Finally add the prawns and mussels for 5 minutes. Serve over garlic-rubbed toast in a deep plate.']::text[]),
('schlutzkrapfen','en','Schlutzkrapfen','Brixen, South Tyrol, Italy','Rye half-moons filled with spinach and ricotta, served in browned butter with chives. The rye in the dough is what sets them apart from southern ravioli: it is the grain of the high valleys.',ARRAY['Knead the two flours, eggs, milk and salt into a firm dough; rest it for an hour under a cloth.','Wilt the spinach, squeeze it dry between your hands — not a drop should run — and chop it.','Mix it with butter-softened onion, drained ricotta and nutmeg.','Roll the dough very thin, cut 8 cm discs, fill them and fold into half-moons, pressing out the air.','Poach for 4 minutes in barely simmering water, plate and coat with browned butter, parmesan and chives.']::text[]),
('pasta-e-fagioli','en','Pasta e fagioli','Bari, Apulia, Italy','A poor man''s dish turned emblem: beans, mismatched short pasta and olive oil. The ends of pasta packets were once tipped in, hence the mixed shapes now deliberately sought out.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then cook them for 1 hour in unsalted water with the bay and a garlic clove.','Take out a third of the beans and crush them to a purée: that is what thickens the soup without flour.','Turn the remaining garlic golden in the oil with the rosemary and chilli, then discard the rosemary.','Add the crushed tomato, the whole beans and the purée, and pour in their cooking water.','Drop in the short pasta and cook for 10 minutes, stirring: the dish should stay thick. Serve with raw oil and pecorino.']::text[]),
('coda-alla-vaccinara','en','Coda alla vaccinara','Rome, Lazio, Italy','The oxtail of the Testaccio slaughtermen, stewed for four hours in wine and celery, lifted with cocoa and pine nuts. Paid in offal, the vaccinari built a whole cuisine out of this quinto quarto.',ARRAY['Soak the oxtail sections in cold water for an hour, then pat them dry and sear them in the oil.','Fry the chopped guanciale, onion and carrot, then return the meat and deglaze with white wine.','Add the tomato purée, the cloves and water to cover; put the lid on and simmer for 3½ hours.','Blanch the celery in lengths and add it for the last half hour.','Off the heat, stir in the pine nuts, raisins and cocoa: they give the sauce its almost sweet depth.']::text[]),
('pane-cunzato','en','Pane cunzato','Trapani, Sicily, Italy','Literally ''seasoned bread'': a loaf split open, doused in oil and filled with tomato, anchovy and fresh cheese. It was the meal of those who had only bread — dressed with whatever came to hand.',ARRAY['Choose a thick-crusted semolina loaf baked the same day, and split it lengthways.','Rub the crumb with a cut garlic clove and douse it generously with olive oil.','Crush the tomatoes by hand straight onto the bread, juices and all, and salt them.','Lay on the anchovies, the fresh cheese in thick slices and the stoned olives.','Scatter oregano, pepper it, close the loaf and press firmly for a minute before cutting into portions.']::text[]),
('zuppa-gallurese','en','Zuppa gallurese','Tempio Pausania, Sardinia, Italy','Despite the name it is not a soup but a gratin: stale bread soaked in mutton broth between layers of fresh cheese. It is served at Gallura weddings, where it opens the meal.',ARRAY['Make a broth by cooking the mutton for 2 hours, then strain it and keep it very hot.','Cut the bread into thick slices and let them dry: they must soak without collapsing.','Grate the pecorino and slice the fresh cheese.','Layer bread, cheeses, herbs and a ladle of broth in a buttered dish, three storeys high.','The bread should be soaked but not swimming. Bake for 40 minutes at 180°C: a golden crust forms, the base stays melting.']::text[]),
('fideua','en','Fideuà','Gandia, Valencian Community, Spain','A paella in which vermicelli replaces the rice. The story goes that a ship''s cook, out of rice and tired of the captain serving himself first, tried pasta to put him off — the captain loved it.',ARRAY['Fry the diced cuttlefish in the paella pan, then the prawns for one minute only; set them aside.','Add the garlic, the soaked ñora pulp and the grated tomato, and reduce to a sofrito.','Tip in the broken vermicelli and toast them dry for two minutes: this stops them sticking.','Moisten with boiling saffron stock — twice the volume of pasta — and cook for 8 minutes over high heat.','Arrange the seafood on top and bake for 6 minutes at 220°C: the vermicelli ends should stand upright. Serve with aioli.']::text[]),
('lacon-con-grelos','en','Lacón con grelos','Lugo, Galicia, Spain','Salted pork shoulder cooked with bitter turnip tops, chorizo and potatoes. The dish of the Galician carnival: it is served only between Saint Anthony''s day and Ash Wednesday.',ARRAY['Soak the salted shoulder for 36 hours in cold water, changing it five or six times.','Simmer it for 2 hours with the bay, in water that is never salted.','Blanch the turnip tops for 5 minutes in separate water and throw that water away: this removes the excess bitterness without making them bland.','Add them to the broth with the whole chorizos for 30 minutes, then the potatoes for 25 minutes.','Serve on a wooden board: the meat cut thick, the greens drained, the chorizo in rounds and the potatoes, dressed with paprika oil.']::text[]),
('gazpachos-manchegos','en','Gazpachos manchegos','Albacete, Castile-La Mancha, Spain','Nothing to do with the cold soup: this is a game stew served on an unleavened flatbread broken into pieces. La Mancha shepherds cooked it on the flatbread itself, which served as the plate.',ARRAY['Joint the rabbit and chicken into small pieces and colour them in the oil with the garlic.','Add the onion, grated tomato, mushrooms and the paprika, the latter off the heat.','Pour in two litres of water, add rosemary, thyme and bay, and cook for 45 minutes.','Break the flatbread by hand into uneven 4 cm pieces and drop them into the broth.','Cook for 20 minutes, stirring: the bread absorbs almost all the liquid. Rest for 10 minutes before serving.']::text[]),
('zarangollo','en','Zarangollo','Murcia, Spain','Courgettes and onions cooked down slowly then bound with egg, barely scrambled. The market gardens of the Murcian huerta in a pan: three ingredients, and the skill is in not rushing them.',ARRAY['Slice the onions and soften them covered in the oil for 20 minutes, without letting them colour.','Cut the courgettes into thin rounds, unpeeled, and add them with a pinch of salt.','Cover and let them give up their water for 15 minutes, then uncover and let it evaporate entirely.','Crack the eggs straight into the pan and barely stir: they should stay in visible ribbons, not become an omelette.','Cut the heat as soon as the egg sets and serve warm, on toast.']::text[]),
('pote-gallego','en','Galician pote','Santiago de Compostela, Galicia, Spain','The Galician one-pot with grelos — the bitter turnip tops that grow in winter — with beans, potatoes and pork. It is the meal that waited for pilgrims at the end of the road.',ARRAY['Desalt the ribs, bacon and ear for 12 hours in cold water; soak the beans the same night.','Put the meats and beans in cold water, bring to a simmer and skim carefully.','Cook for 1½ hours at a gentle boil, then lift out the meats and keep them warm.','Add the washed and roughly chopped greens, then the potatoes broken by hand, and cook for 30 minutes.','Return the cut-up meats, add the sliced chorizo for 15 minutes and serve everything from the pot.']::text[]),
('torrijas','en','Torrijas','Salamanca, Castile and León, Spain','Stale bread soaked in sweetened milk, coated in egg and fried, then rolled in cinnamon. A Holy Week recipe, mentioned as early as the 15th century as a restorative for women who had just given birth.',ARRAY['Infuse the milk with 100 g of sugar, the zest and a cinnamon stick, then let it cool a little.','Cut the bread into 3 cm slices, ideally the day before: too fresh and it falls apart.','Soak each slice for 30 seconds a side — no longer — and let them drain on a rack for 10 minutes.','Coat them in beaten egg and fry in hot oil, 2 minutes a side, until well coloured.','Roll them at once in cinnamon sugar, or pour over honey loosened with red wine.']::text[]),
('himmelstorte','en','Redcurrant Himmelstorte','Bremerhaven, Lower Saxony, Germany','Two sponge discs capped with crisp meringue, separated by cream and sharp redcurrants. The contrast between the sugar of the meringue and the acidity of the fruit is the whole point of the cake.',ARRAY['Cream the butter with 100 g of sugar, add the yolks, milk, flour and baking powder.','Spread this batter thinly in two 24 cm tins.','Beat the whites to very firm peaks with 200 g of sugar, spread them over the raw batter and scatter flaked almonds.','Bake for 30 minutes at 160°C: the meringue should dry without browning. Cool in the tins.','Whip the cream with the vanilla, spread it and the redcurrants over the first disc, and top with the second, meringue upwards.']::text[]),
('birnen-bohnen-speck','en','Pears, beans and bacon','Rendsburg, Schleswig-Holstein, Germany','Green beans, whole cooking pears and smoked bacon in a single pan. The pairing seems improbable in southern Germany and self-evident in the north, where it marks the end of summer.',ARRAY['Cook the smoked bacon for 20 minutes in a litre of water, at a bare simmer.','Set the whole unpeeled pears on the bacon and poach them for 15 minutes: they should stay firm.','Add the trimmed beans and the savory, and continue for 15 minutes.','Boil the potatoes separately in salted water.','Thicken the broth with a spoonful of slaked flour, sharpen with a dash of vinegar and a pinch of sugar, and serve everything together.']::text[]),
('zwiebelkuchen','en','New wine onion tart','Stuttgart, Baden-Württemberg, Germany','A thick tart of onions in cream with caraway, eaten in September with Federweisser, the new wine still fermenting in the glass. The two are found only together, and only for a month a year.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, the yeast dissolved in warm water, the butter and salt, and prove for an hour.','Slice the onions and soften them covered for 30 minutes in the butter, without colouring: they should turn translucent.','Add the diced bacon and let everything cool completely.','Mix the sour cream, eggs, a spoonful of flour and the caraway, then fold in the onions.','Line a deep tin with the dough, fill to 3 cm and bake for 45 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('salzburger-nockerl','en','Salzburger Nockerl','Salzburg, Austria','Three domes of sweet soufflé said to represent the hills around Salzburg. They must be served within two minutes: after that the mountains sink and the effect is lost.',ARRAY['Butter an oval dish, pour in the milk and jam, and heat on the hob until simmering.','Whip the six whites to very firm peaks, adding the sugar in three stages.','Fold in three yolks, the sifted flour, vanilla and zest, lifting rather than stirring.','Spoon three large mounds of the mixture into the hot dish, forming three distinct domes.','Bake for 10 minutes at 200°C: the peaks should brown and the inside stay runny. Dust and serve at once.']::text[]),
('buendnerfleisch','en','Bündnerfleisch','Chur, Grisons, Switzerland','Beef cured with mountain herbs then air-dried for months in the dry Alpine air, pressed regularly to give it its square shape. It is sliced so thinly it turns translucent.',ARRAY['Trim the beef of all fat and sinew: only pure muscle dries properly.','Pound the salt, juniper, bay, thyme, rosemary, pepper and garlic, and rub the meat all over.','Put it in a covered container and cure it for three weeks in the cold, turning it every two days.','Rinse, pat dry, wrap in a cloth and hang it in a cool, dry, airy place for five to eight weeks.','Press it flat under a weight once a week. Slice it at the last moment, paper-thin, and serve on buttered rye bread.']::text[]),
('papet-vaudois','en','Papet vaudois','Lausanne, Vaud, Switzerland','A stew of leeks and potatoes topped with a cabbage sausage cooked in it. The leek must literally vanish: papet comes from papette, porridge.',ARRAY['Slice the whole leeks, white and green, and soften them in the butter with the onion for 20 minutes, covered.','Deglaze with white wine, let it evaporate, then add the potatoes in large cubes and the broth.','Simmer for 40 minutes over low heat: the leeks should collapse and the potatoes barely crush.','Lay the cabbage sausages on top, without pricking them, cover and poach for 30 minutes.','Lift out the sausages, enrich the papet with the cream and serve them on top, whole.']::text[]),
('stifado','en','Stifado','Patras, Peloponnese, Greece','A beef stew with whole small onions, cinnamon and vinegar, in which the onions weigh as much as the meat. The word comes from the Venetian stufado: Venice long held the Greek coasts.',ARRAY['Scald the small onions for a minute: the skin will then come away without damaging them.','Sear the meat in large cubes in the oil, in small batches, then set aside.','Brown the whole onions in the same oil, without stirring, until golden on all sides.','Return the meat, deglaze with the wine and vinegar, and add the tomato, spices and sugar.','Cover and simmer for 2¼ hours without ever stirring with a spoon — shake the pot instead, or the onions would break up.']::text[]),
('saganaki-crevettes','en','Prawn saganaki','Thessaloniki, Macedonia, Greece','Prawns cooked in tomato with feta, in the small two-handled pan that gives the dish its name. The feta does not really melt: it softens and salts the sauce at the same time.',ARRAY['Peel the prawns, keeping the tails, and make a quick stock with the heads and shells.','Soften the onion and garlic in the oil, add the crushed tomato and chilli, and reduce for 15 minutes.','Moisten with a ladle of the strained stock and let it thicken.','Add the prawns, flame with ouzo and cook them for barely 3 minutes: any longer and they turn rubbery.','Crumble the feta coarsely over, grill for 5 minutes and serve scalding, with bread for mopping.']::text[]),
('afelia','en','Afelia','Paphos, Cyprus','Pork stewed in red wine with crushed coriander seed — a great deal of it, the Cypriot signature. Served with bulgur pilaf and sheep''s yogurt.',ARRAY['Crush the coriander seeds coarsely in a mortar: ground, they would lose all their scent.','Marinate the diced pork for 12 hours in the red wine with two thirds of the coriander.','Drain, pat dry and colour the meat in the oil over high heat.','Pour in the marinade, add the cinnamon and remaining coriander, cover and simmer for 1 hour.','For the pilaf, brown the broken vermicelli in oil, add the bulgur and broth, and let it swell for 15 minutes, covered.']::text[]),
('lampuki-pie','en','Lampuki pie','Valletta, Malta','A pie of dorado, the fish Malta catches from August to December beneath rafts of floating palm fronds. The fish shares the crust with spinach, olives and cauliflower: a late-summer pie, never served out of season.',ARRAY['Flour the fish slices and fry them for two minutes a side, then bone them and flake the flesh.','Soften the onion and garlic, add the tomato and reduce for 15 minutes.','Blanch the cauliflower in small florets and wilt the spinach; squeeze both thoroughly dry.','Combine all the vegetables with the sauce, olives, capers and mint, then fold in the fish without breaking it up.','Line a tin, fill it, cover with a pastry lid, glaze with egg and bake for 45 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('sarma-bosniaque','en','Bosnian sarma','Travnik, Bosnia and Herzegovina','Sour cabbage leaves rolled around meat and rice, stewed for two hours on a bed of smoked ribs. The cabbage is fermented whole, in heads, in barrels — every Bosnian family keeps one in the cellar.',ARRAY['Separate the leaves of the fermented cabbage, rinse them if very sour and flatten the central rib with a knife.','Soften the onion, let it cool, then mix with the meats, raw rice, garlic, egg and paprika.','Place a spoonful of filling on each leaf, fold in the sides and roll tightly, tucking in the ends.','Line a pot with the smoked ribs and chopped cabbage, and pack the rolls upright, tightly.','Cover with water, set a plate on top to hold them down, and simmer for 2 hours. Thicken with a lard and paprika roux at the end.']::text[]),
('jota-triestina','en','Triestine jota','Trieste, Friuli, Italy','A soup of beans, sauerkraut and potato, on the border of three cuisines: Italian, Slovene and Austrian. Trieste was the Habsburg port, and its cooking never picked a side.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then cook them for 1 hour with the smoked ribs and bay.','Cook the sauerkraut separately in a little water with the cumin for 40 minutes: cooked with the beans, its acidity would toughen them.','Add the potatoes in large cubes to the beans and continue for 25 minutes.','Crush a third of the beans and potatoes with a fork to thicken the soup.','Make a brown roux of lard, flour, onion and garlic, stir it into the pot with the sauerkraut, and let it marry for 20 minutes.']::text[]),
('gibanica','en','Gibanica','Belgrade, Serbia','Sheets of pastry crumpled and layered with fresh cheese and eggs, baked until golden and puffed. The name comes from gibati, to fold: the pastry is never smooth, always crumpled.',ARRAY['Mash the cheeses with a fork and mix with the beaten eggs and half the sour cream.','Butter a dish and lay two sheets of pastry flat, letting them overhang.','Dip the next sheets briefly in the mixture, crumple them by hand and arrange them in waves in the dish.','Alternate until everything is used, finishing with two smooth sheets folded over the top.','Mix the remaining cream with the sparkling water, pour it over the pie and bake for 50 minutes at 180°C. Let it cool a little before cutting.']::text[]),
('jagnjetina-ispod-sacha','en','Lamb under the bell','Podgorica, Montenegro','Lamb and potatoes cooked under a cast-iron bell buried in embers. The sač is found across the Balkans, but Montenegro has made it the obligatory meal of family reunions.',ARRAY['Stud the lamb with slivers of garlic and rub it with oil, rosemary and salt an hour ahead.','Cut the potatoes and vegetables into large pieces: small ones would dissolve over two and a half hours.','Arrange everything in a wide cast-iron dish and pour over the white wine and a glass of water.','Cover with the bell and bury it under embers, or bake at 200°C under a heavy lid.','Cook for 2 hours without ever lifting it, then uncover for 20 minutes to brown. Serve with kajmak.']::text[]),
('jota-slovene','en','Ričet','Ljubljana, Slovenia','A soup of pearl barley and beans with smoked pork, thick enough to hold a spoon. It was the soup of Austro-Hungarian soldiers, served in every barracks of the Empire, and Slovenia kept it for itself.',ARRAY['Soak the barley and beans separately overnight.','Put them in cold water with the smoked ribs and bay, and simmer for 1 hour.','Add the carrot, celeriac and potato in dice, and continue for 30 minutes.','Make a pale roux with the lard, flour, onion and garlic, and stir it into the pot.','Shred the meat, return it, add the marjoram and let it marry for 10 minutes. The soup should be very thick.']::text[]),
('pierogi-ruskie-zapiekane','en','Lentil and buckwheat pierogi','Lublin, Poland','Polish dumplings filled with buckwheat, lentils and mushrooms — the Lenten version, without meat or cheese. Buckwheat, kasza gryczana, is to eastern Poland what the potato is to the west.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, warm water, egg and salt for 10 minutes, then rest for 40 minutes under a cloth.','Cook the buckwheat and lentils separately, and soak the mushrooms for an hour.','Turn two onions golden in the butter, add the chopped mushrooms, then mix with the buckwheat, lentils and marjoram.','Roll the dough to 2 mm, cut 8 cm discs, fill them and seal by pinching firmly.','Poach for 4 minutes, then fry them golden with the third onion, sliced. Serve with the sour cream.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('koprovka','en','Koprová omáčka','Hradec Králové, Bohemia, Czechia','Boiled beef under a white dill sauce, sweet-sour with vinegar and sugar, with a hard-boiled egg and knedlíky. The Czech canteen dish par excellence, loved and mocked in equal measure.',ARRAY['Cook the brisket for 1¾ hours with the vegetables, bay and allspice; keep the strained broth.','Make a pale roux with the butter and flour, without letting it colour, and whisk in a litre of hot broth.','Cook for 15 minutes over low heat to lose the floury taste, then add the cream.','Chop the dill very finely and add it off the heat: cooked, it loses all its scent.','Balance with vinegar and sugar — the sauce should be frankly sweet-sour — and serve over the sliced meat with half a hard-boiled egg and knedlíky.']::text[]),
('szegedi-gulyas','en','Székely gulyás','Kecskemét, Hungary','Pork with paprika and sauerkraut, enriched with sour cream. It owes nothing to the town of Szeged but to József Székely, a Hungarian archivist who, arriving late at the inn, was served a mixture of leftovers.',ARRAY['Soften the finely sliced onions in the lard for 20 minutes, until they collapse.','Take off the heat, stir in the paprika at once: scorched, it would make the dish bitter.','Add the diced pork, garlic, caraway and tomato, moisten with a glass of water and simmer covered for 1 hour.','Add the rinsed and squeezed sauerkraut, and continue for 40 minutes.','Slacken the sour cream with the flour and a ladle of warm juices, stir in off the heat and never let it boil again.']::text[]),
('bryndzove-pirohy','en','Bryndza pirohy','Liptovský Mikuláš, Slovakia','Potato dumplings filled with bryndza, the salty fresh sheep''s cheese of the Tatra shepherds'' huts. Doused in melted bacon fat and sour cream, with no pretence at lightness.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes, rice them and let them cool completely.','Knead with the flour, eggs and salt, without working them long: overworked, the dough turns elastic.','Mash the bryndza with a fork, a little sour cream and the grated onion.','Roll the dough to 3 mm, cut 7 cm discs, fill them and seal carefully, pushing out the air.','Poach for 5 minutes in simmering water, drain and pour over crisped lardons with their fat, cream and chives.']::text[]),
('smorrebrod-roget-al','en','Smoked eel smørrebrød','Nyborg, Funen, Denmark','The grandest of Danish open sandwiches: smoked eel, firm scrambled egg and chives on black rye bread. The order of the layers is codified, and one never eats two smørrebrød out of sequence.',ARRAY['Choose a dense rye loaf, barely sliced: it must hold the topping without bending.','Beat the eggs with the cream and a pinch of salt, then set them over a bain-marie, stirring constantly.','The eggs should stay creamy but hold on a spoon: too loose and they would soak the bread.','Butter the bread generously right to the edges — the butter is a barrier against moisture.','Lay on the eggs, then the smoked eel fillets, the chives, a few radish slices and a squeeze of lemon.']::text[]),
('palt','en','Palt','Luleå, Norrbotten, Sweden','Large dumplings of raw potato stuffed with pork, poached for an hour and served with butter and lingonberry jam. The dish of the Swedish north, dense and sustaining, that sees you through a whole day.',ARRAY['Grate the raw potatoes very finely and squeeze them in a cloth: keep the starch that settles at the bottom of the liquid.','Mix the squeezed pulp, the recovered starch, both flours and the salt into a sticky dough.','Dice the bacon, fry it with the onion and allspice, then let it cool.','Shape fist-sized balls around a spoonful of the pork, sealing them well.','Poach for 1 hour in barely simmering salted water; they float long before they are cooked. Serve with butter and lingonberry.']::text[]),
('kalarullad','en','Estonian cabbage rolls','Tartu, Estonia','Blanched cabbage leaves rolled around a filling of pork and barley, browned in the oven and doused in sour cream. Barley stands in for rice: it grew where rice never reached.',ARRAY['Cut out the cabbage core and plunge the whole head into boiling water for 8 minutes; peel off the leaves as they soften.','Cook the pearl barley for 25 minutes and let it cool.','Mix the minced pork, barley, softened onion, egg, marjoram, salt and pepper.','Flatten the rib of each leaf, fill and roll tightly, tucking in the sides.','Pack the rolls in a buttered dish, cover with lardons and broth, and bake for 50 minutes at 180°C. Coat with sour cream thickened with flour.']::text[]),
('sildi-kartuli','en','Herring under a beetroot coat','Riga, Latvia','A cold layered dish of salt herring, potato, egg and beetroot, bound with mayonnaise and served in bright pink strata. The obligatory Baltic New Year dish, assembled the day before so it sets.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes, carrots and beetroot separately in their skins, then cool them completely.','Dice the herring fillets small and marinate the chopped onion for 15 minutes in the vinegar and sugar.','Grate each vegetable coarsely into a separate bowl: the colours must never mix.','Build up in a dish: herring, onion, potato, mayonnaise, carrot, egg, mayonnaise, beetroot.','Smooth the top, cover and chill for 12 hours. Decorate with dill and grated egg to serve.']::text[]),
('kotleti-pozharskie','en','Pozharsky cutlets','Torzhok, Russia','Poultry croquettes coated in cubes of bread rather than crumbs, which makes them bristle as they fry. Pushkin recommended them in a letter in verse: ''dine at leisure at Pozharsky''s in Torzhok''.',ARRAY['Chop the poultry by knife into small dice: a mincer would turn it into an elastic paste.','Mix it with cold butter in flakes, the cream, softened and cooled onion, nutmeg and salt; chill for an hour to firm up.','Cut stale bread into tiny 3 mm cubes, crusts removed: this is the true coating.','Shape slightly domed cutlets, flour them, dip in beaten egg then roll in the bread cubes.','Fry them 4 minutes a side in clarified butter over medium heat, then finish 6 minutes in a 180°C oven.']::text[]),
('rasstegai','en','Rasstegai','Vladimir, Russia','Small pies left open at the top, into which a spoonful of scalding broth is poured just before serving. The name comes from rasstegnut, ''to unfasten'': the pie stays ajar like an unbuttoned collar.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm milk with the sugar, let it froth, then knead with the flour, butter, one egg and salt. Prove for 1½ hours.','Cook the rice in salted water and let it cool completely.','Dice the fish, mix with the rice, butter-softened onion and dill; season with plenty of pepper.','Divide the dough into 60 g balls, flatten, fill and pinch the edges shut, leaving an oval opening at the centre.','Glaze with egg and bake for 22 minutes at 200°C. Pour a spoonful of scalding fish stock into each opening as you serve.']::text[]),
('deruny','en','Deruny','Sumy, Ukraine','The Ukrainian potato pancakes, fried golden in lard and served under a spoonful of sour cream. The town of Korosten holds an annual festival in their honour, complete with a monument.',ARRAY['Grate the potatoes finely and the onion with them: its acidity stops them darkening.','Let them drain for ten minutes in a colander, without pressing, then recover the starch settled at the bottom of the bowl.','Mix pulp, starch, eggs, flour, salt and pepper: the batter should be thick and barely pourable.','Heat the lard and drop in flattened spoonfuls; let them set for 4 minutes untouched.','Turn, brown for 3 minutes, and serve scalding with sour cream, garlic-fried mushrooms and dill.']::text[]),
('draniki-kolduny','en','Potato kolduny','Homiel, Belarus','Grated potato cakes enclosing a meat filling — the stuffed version of draniki. The word kolduny means ''sorcerers'': you never know what they conceal until you bite.',ARRAY['Fry the minced pork with one onion and the marjoram, salt generously and let it cool.','Grate the potatoes and the second onion, drain them and recover the starch from the bottom.','Mix with the egg, flour and salt: the batter should hold on a spatula.','Drop a spoonful of batter into the hot lard, hollow it, add a nut of filling and cover with a little more batter.','Brown for 5 minutes a side over medium heat — any hotter and the outside would burn before the centre cooked. Serve with sour cream and lardons.']::text[]),
('scouse','en','Scouse','Liverpool, England','A stew of lamb and potato brought by Norwegian sailors as lapskaus. The people of Liverpool took their nickname from it — Scousers — and their accent bears the same name.',ARRAY['Brown both meats in large pieces in a heavy pot, in small batches.','Add the onions and carrots in thick rounds, then the broth, bay, thyme and Worcestershire.','Simmer covered for 1½ hours, untouched.','Add half the potatoes in large pieces and the other half grated: the grated ones collapse and thicken the stew.','Continue for 45 minutes. Serve with pickled red cabbage, beetroot and bread — that is the tradition, and it is not up for discussion.']::text[]),
('bara-brith','en','Bara brith','Caernarfon, Wales','The Welsh ''speckled bread'', its dried fruit steeped overnight in black tea. The tea replaces the fat: this loaf contains not an ounce of butter, and is buttered on the plate instead.',ARRAY['Steep the raisins and chopped prunes in the hot tea with the brown sugar, overnight.','By morning the fruit should have drunk nearly all the liquid and trebled in size.','Work in the beaten eggs, zest and spices, then the flour and baking powder, without overworking.','Pour into a lined loaf tin and bake for 1¼ hours at 160°C; cover with paper if the top colours too fast.','Brush with warm honey as it comes out and let it cool for a full day before slicing. Eaten buttered.']::text[]),
('dublin-coddle','en','Dublin coddle','Dublin, Ireland','Sausages and bacon poached — never grilled — with potatoes and onions in a pale broth. It was the Thursday night dish, made with leftovers before Friday''s abstinence from meat.',ARRAY['Poach the sausages and bacon for 5 minutes in boiling water, then throw that water away: this is what gives coddle its characteristic pallor.','Layer in a pot: onions, potatoes in large pieces, carrots, sausages and bacon.','Moisten with the broth and stout to cover only three quarters of the way up.','Add the bay, cover and simmer for 1¾ hours over very low heat, without ever stirring.','The potatoes on top stay whole, those at the bottom collapse and thicken the broth. Cover with parsley.']::text[]),
('asperges-a-la-flamande','en','Asparagus à la flamande','Mechelen, Antwerp, Belgium','White asparagus under melted butter with crushed hard-boiled egg, which each diner mixes on their own plate. Mechelen is Belgium''s white asparagus capital, grown under ridges to stay pale.',ARRAY['Peel the asparagus from tip to base, twice over, and snap off the fibrous end.','Simmer the peelings for 15 minutes in salted water: this fragrant broth will poach the spears.','Poach them for 15 minutes depending on thickness, then drain on a cloth — never in a colander, they would break.','Melt the butter until it just begins to turn nutty, then take it off the heat.','Crush the hard-boiled eggs into the warm butter with the parsley, nutmeg and lemon. Each diner mixes it on their own plate.']::text[]),
('rommegrot','en','Rømmegrøt','Sogndal, Norway','A porridge of sour cream worked until the butter separates and rises in golden pools. Served with that butter, sugar and cinnamon; it marked harvest feasts and births.',ARRAY['Bring the sour cream to a simmer in a heavy pan and let it bubble gently, covered, for 10 minutes.','Rain in half the flour while whisking, and keep stirring: after a few minutes the butter starts to bead on the surface.','Spoon off that butter and keep it: it is what will be poured over the porridge at the end.','Add the rest of the flour, then the hot milk little by little, whisking to a smooth glossy cream.','Cook for 15 minutes over low heat, salt it, and serve with the reserved butter, sugar and cinnamon.']::text[]),
('kalakeitto-saaristo','en','Archipelago fish soup','Nagu, Turku archipelago, Finland','A soup of Baltic white fish with milk and allspice, without cream. The Finnish archipelago islands make it the plain, clear version of lohikeitto, with whatever the nets bring in.',ARRAY['Fillet the fish and make a stock with the heads and bones for 20 minutes with the bay and allspice; strain it.','Sweat the onion and carrot in butter without colouring, then add the diced potatoes.','Moisten with the stock and cook for 15 minutes.','Pour in the milk and bring to a bare simmer — boiling would curdle it — then slide in the fish pieces.','Cut the heat and let them poach for 8 minutes in the residual warmth. Add a large handful of dill and serve with buttered rye bread.']::text[]),
('ciorba-radauteana','en','Ciorbă rădăuțeană','Rădăuți, Bukovina, Romania','A chicken soup whitened with cream and egg yolk, invented in 1979 by a cook in Rădăuți who wanted a gentle version of tripe soup. It conquered the country within a decade.',ARRAY['Poach the chicken for 40 minutes with the whole vegetables and bay, skimming: the broth must stay clear.','Lift out the meat and cut it into fine strips; julienne the carrot and discard the other vegetables.','Pound the garlic with salt and two spoonfuls of broth.','Beat the cream and yolks, temper them with a ladle of hot broth trickled in, then thicken the soup off the heat.','Sharpen it with vinegar, add the garlic and parsley, and never let it boil again.']::text[]),
('selsko-meso','en','Selsko meso','Prilep, North Macedonia','Pork, mushrooms and sheep''s white cheese baked in an individual earthenware pan, the tavče. ''Village meat'': each guest gets their own dish, brought from the oven still bubbling.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into fine strips and sear it briskly in oil, in small batches, so it colours rather than stews.','Add the onion in half-moons and the pepper, then the sliced mushrooms, and let them release their water.','Deglaze with white wine, add the crushed tomato, savory and paprika off the heat.','Divide between earthenware pans, crumble the cheese over and break half an egg onto each.','Bake for 25 minutes at 220°C: the top should gratinate and the rim of the pan crackle. Serve in the dish it cooked in.']::text[]),
('kapama-bansko','en','Bansko kapama','Bansko, Bulgaria','Four meats, rice and sauerkraut packed into a jar sealed with dough and baked for three hours in the village oven. Kapama comes from the Turkish kapamak, ''to cover'': nothing must escape.',ARRAY['Brown the chicken, pork and veal separately in the lard, then the sliced sausage.','Fry the onions, add the paprika off the heat and the savory.','Squeeze the sauerkraut and line the base of an earthenware jar with it; alternate meats, raw rice and sauerkraut to the top.','Moisten with a litre of sauerkraut juice let down with water, without going above the level of the ingredients.','Knead a dough of flour and water, seal the lid hermetically with it and bake for 3 hours at 160°C. The seal is broken at the table.']::text[]),
('shuizhu-yu','en','Sichuan water-boiled fish','Chongqing, China','The name promises boiling water and delivers a lake of red oil: slivers of fish poached under an avalanche of dried chilli and Sichuan pepper. The oil layer is not a sauce, it is a thermal lid.',ARRAY['Fillet the fish and slice it thinly on the bias; massage with the egg white, starch and rice wine, and leave for 20 minutes.','Blanch the bean sprouts for a minute and lay them in the bottom of a large bowl.','Fry the doubanjiang in a little oil until it turns red, add the garlic and ginger, then the broth.','Poach the fish slices one by one for barely 90 seconds: they whiten and rise. Pour everything over the sprouts.','Heat the remaining oil with the dried chillies and Sichuan pepper until smoking, and pour it scalding over the fish.']::text[]),
('char-siu','en','Char siu','Guangzhou, Guangdong, China','Cantonese lacquered pork, red and glossy, hanging on hooks in shop windows. Char siu means ''fork roasted'': the meat was threaded on long skewers and cooked over the fire, never laid down.',ARRAY['Cut the pork into long strips 5 cm thick: that shape allows caramelisation on every side.','Mix all the marinade ingredients except half the honey, and marinate the meat for 24 hours.','Roast on a rack over a tray of water for 30 minutes at 200°C.','Turn, brush with marinade and continue for 20 minutes.','Brush with the remaining honey and grill for 8 minutes, watching closely: the lacquer should brown in patches without burning.']::text[]),
('dongpo-rou','en','Dongpo pork','Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China','Cubes of pork belly braised for three hours in Shaoxing wine until the fat turns translucent. Su Dongpo, an 11th-century poet and governor, is said to have given it to the labourers dredging West Lake.',ARRAY['Blanch the whole belly for 5 minutes, rinse it and cut it into 5 cm cubes with the rind.','Line the base of a pot with spring onion stalks and ginger slices: the meat must never touch the metal.','Pack the cubes rind upwards, tightly, in a single layer.','Pour in the rice wine, soy sauce and sugar, add the star anise and cinnamon, and cover.','Simmer for 2½ hours over very low heat, then steam each cube for 20 minutes. The rind should wobble.']::text[]),
('jianbing','en','Jianbing','Jinan, Shandong, China','A grain crêpe spread with a wooden rake on a scorching griddle, cracked with an egg, folded around a crisp cracker. The Chinese street breakfast par excellence, served in ninety seconds.',ARRAY['Whisk the three flours into the water to a very thin batter, and rest for 30 minutes.','Fry the wonton squares until they puff and crisp; drain them.','Pour a ladle of batter onto an oiled griddle and spread it into a very thin circle with a circular sweep.','Crack an egg on top, spread it with a spatula, scatter sesame, spring onion and coriander, then flip the crêpe.','Brush with hoisin and chilli oil, lay the crisp cracker in the centre, fold into quarters and serve at once.']::text[]),
('guilin-mifen','en','Liuzhou rice noodles','Liuzhou, Guangxi, China','Rice noodles in a broth of fermented bamboo shoots, whose powerful smell reaches you several metres before the bowl. Locals find it irresistible; visitors sometimes need a mouthful to come round.',ARRAY['Blanch the pork bones, rinse them, then simmer for 3 hours with the ginger, star anise and dried fish.','Strain the broth: it should be cloudy and deeply fragrant.','Fry the fermented bamboo shoots for two minutes in oil — this is the moment their smell is released — then tip them into the broth.','Fry the peanuts in oil until golden, and drain them.','Cook the rice noodles for 3 minutes, put them in bowls, pour over the scalding broth and top with bamboo, pickled beans, peanuts, sprouts and herbs.']::text[]),
('foshan-fotiaoqiang','en','Buddha jumps over the wall','Fuzhou, Fujian, China','A soup of eighteen ingredients simmered in a jar sealed with Shaoxing wine. The name comes from a poem: a monk, catching the scent over the monastery wall, is said to have vaulted the fence to taste it.',ARRAY['Soak the shiitake, dried scallops and dried shrimp separately overnight; keep all the soaking liquids.','Blanch each meat separately, rinse it, then fry it briefly with ginger.','Layer the ingredients in a jar, longest-cooking at the bottom, most delicate on top: meats below, seafood above.','Pour in the rice wine, broth and strained soaking liquids, filling no more than three quarters.','Seal the jar with oiled paper and string, and steam gently for 5 hours. Open it at the table: the scent is half the dish.']::text[]),
('guoqiao-mixian','en','Crossing-the-bridge noodles','Mengzi, Yunnan, China','A broth so rich that a film of oil keeps it scalding, into which raw meats and vegetables are dipped at the table. A wife carried her husband''s meal this way to an island where he studied — the bridge was long, the soup stayed hot.',ARRAY['Simmer the chicken and pork bones for 2 hours with ginger and spring onion, then strain: the broth must keep a layer of fat on top.','Slice the raw meats almost transparently thin, under 2 mm: that is what allows them to cook on contact.','Arrange the raw meats, mushrooms, sprouts and chives on separate plates.','Cook the rice noodles and keep them warm in a separate bowl.','Bring the broth to 95°C, pour it into a large scalding bowl, and add the ingredients at the table: meats first, noodles last.']::text[]),
('suan-cai-yu','en','Dongbei fish with sour cabbage','Harbin, Heilongjiang, China','Freshwater fish stewed with fermented Chinese cabbage in large earthenware pots. China''s north-east ferments cabbage by the barrel before winter, like Poland or Alsace, and for the same reason.',ARRAY['Rinse the fermented cabbage, squeeze it and cut it into very fine shreds: the finer it is, the more sourness it gives the broth.','Fry the pork belly in lardons until it renders its fat, then add ginger, garlic and spring onion.','Add the cabbage and stir-fry for 10 minutes over high heat: this dry stage is what concentrates the flavour.','Pour in the rice wine, soy sauce and broth, and simmer for 20 minutes.','Slide in the fish sections, the tofu and the glass noodles, and poach for 10 minutes without stirring. Season with white pepper.']::text[]),
('thukpa-tibetain','en','Thukpa','Lhasa, Tibet, China','A soup of hand-pulled noodles in yak broth, thickened with roasted barley flour. At four thousand metres water boils at 87°C: everything cooks slowly, and nothing is eaten cold.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, water and salt for 15 minutes, oil the dough and rest it for an hour under a bowl.','Simmer the diced beef for 45 minutes with ginger, garlic and Sichuan pepper.','Add the onion, carrot and daikon in large pieces, and continue for 20 minutes.','Pull the dough by hand into long ribbons, stretching and folding it several times, then drop them straight into the broth.','Cook for 5 minutes, thicken with a spoonful of slaked barley flour, and cover with coriander and spring onion.']::text[]),
('wenchang-ji','en','Wenchang chicken','Wenchang, Hainan, China','A chicken barely poached, then plunged into iced water so the skin turns gelatinous, and served cold with ginger sauce. It is the direct ancestor of Singapore''s Hainanese chicken rice.',ARRAY['Rub the chicken with coarse salt inside and out, and leave it for an hour at room temperature.','Lower it into a large pan of simmering water with ginger and spring onion, cut the heat and poach covered for 40 minutes, never boiling.','Lift it out and plunge it straight into an iced water bath for ten minutes: the skin contracts and turns firm and translucent.','Cook the rice in the poaching broth with the garlic and a little chicken fat.','Chop the ginger and spring onion very finely, salt them, and pour smoking oil over. Carve the cold chicken and serve with the sauce and rice.']::text[]),
('okonomiyaki','en','Hiroshima okonomiyaki','Hiroshima, Japan','In Hiroshima the pancake is not mixed but built in layers: crêpe, mountain of cabbage, noodles, egg. The Osaka version mixes everything; the two cities have argued about it for seventy years.',ARRAY['Whisk the flour and water into a thin batter and rest it for 30 minutes.','Pour a ladle onto the griddle and spread it into a thin circle; pile finely shredded cabbage and sprouts on top.','Lay on the bacon slices, drizzle a little batter to bind, and flip the whole thing in one movement — this is the difficult part.','Cook covered for 8 minutes: the cabbage halves in volume as it steams.','Alongside, fry the noodles with a little sauce, cook an egg into a thin omelette, stack noodles, pancake and egg, then coat with sauce and mayonnaise.']::text[]),
('oden','en','Oden','Kyoto, Japan','A Japanese one-pot in which each ingredient simmers for hours in a clear dashi and soy broth. It is sold in convenience stores in winter, and everyone picks with tongs from the steaming tray.',ARRAY['Peel the daikon into thick rounds, bevel the edges with a knife and pre-cook it for 30 minutes in rice water: it loses its bitterness.','Make the broth with dashi, soy sauce, mirin and sugar; it should stay clear and barely salty.','Hard-boil the eggs, blanch the konjac and briefly grill the tofu.','Add the ingredients by cooking time: daikon and octopus first, for two hours; eggs and tofu next; fish cakes last.','Never let it boil: the broth should barely tremble. Serve with a dab of yellow mustard.']::text[]),
('unadon','en','Unadon','Hamamatsu, Shizuoka, Japan','Grilled lacquered eel over a bowl of rice, eaten on the midsummer Day of the Ox to withstand the heat. The sauce in old houses is never thrown away: it has been topped up for generations.',ARRAY['Reduce the soy sauce, mirin, sake and sugar by half: this is the tare, the base sauce.','Butterfly the eel, remove the backbone and cut it into bowl-sized sections.','Steam it first for 10 minutes, flesh side up: this is what makes it melting and renders the fat.','Then grill it over embers, dipping it three times in the tare between passes, until the lacquer shines.','Serve over hot vinegared rice, spoon over a little sauce and dust with sanshō pepper.']::text[]),
('kaiseki-chawanmushi','en','Chawanmushi','Nagoya, Aichi, Japan','A savoury dashi custard steamed in a lidded cup, so tender it must be eaten with a spoon. It is the only Japanese dish eaten hot with a spoon, and that alone makes it singular.',ARRAY['Beat the eggs without frothing them: the smallest bubble would give a grainy texture.','Mix them with the cooled dashi, soy sauce and mirin, in a ratio of one part egg to three parts broth.','Pass the mixture through a fine sieve, twice: that is what gives the velvet texture.','Divide chicken, prawns, shiitake and ginkgo nuts between the cups, pour in the mixture and cover.','Steam for 12 minutes over gentle heat with the lid ajar: above 85°C the custard turns holey.']::text[]),
('motsunabe','en','Motsunabe','Fukuoka, Kyushu, Japan','A pot of beef tripe, cabbage and garlic chives in a broth of garlic and chilli. Born in post-war Fukuoka, when offal was all there was, it became the city''s winter dish.',ARRAY['Blanch the tripe for 10 minutes, rinse it in cold water and cut it into 4 cm pieces.','Cook it for 40 minutes in the dashi with half the garlic and the ginger, until tender.','Season the broth with soy sauce and miso, without boiling it once the miso is in.','Arrange the cabbage in large pieces, the tofu and the tripe in the pot, and crown with a mountain of garlic chives and sliced garlic.','Bring to a simmer at the table for 10 minutes. Finish the broth with ramen noodles — the shime, the closing of the meal.']::text[]),
('zunda-mochi','en','Zunda mochi','Sendai, Miyagi, Japan','Glutinous rice cakes under a sweetened purée of edamame, brilliant green. The name may come from zuda, the pestle: Tōhoku farmers pounded fresh beans in a mortar after the summer harvest.',ARRAY['Soak the glutinous rice overnight, then steam it for 40 minutes over a cloth.','Pod the edamame, blanch them for 4 minutes and slip the second skin off each bean — tedious, and what gives the colour.','Pound the beans in a mortar with the sugar and a pinch of salt, adding a little water: the purée should stay slightly grainy.','Pound the hot rice in a mortar until it becomes an elastic mass with no visible grain.','Shape balls with hands wetted in starch and coat them in the green purée. Eat the same day.']::text[]),
('samgyetang','en','Samgyetang','Seoul, South Korea','A whole young chicken stuffed with glutinous rice, ginseng and jujubes, poached until it collapses. It is eaten on the hottest days of summer — Korean logic holds that heat is fought with heat.',ARRAY['Soak the glutinous rice for 2 hours, then drain it.','Clean the inside of the chicken thoroughly and stuff it with rice, ginseng, jujubes, garlic and chestnuts, filling only two thirds.','Close the opening by crossing the legs, or sew it: the rice swells and would otherwise escape.','Cover with cold water, bring to a simmer and skim, then poach for 1¼ hours without ever boiling.','Serve the whole chicken in its broth, unsalted: each diner seasons from a saucer of salt and pepper, to taste.']::text[]),
('jjajangmyeon','en','Jjajangmyeon','Incheon, South Korea','Noodles under a black sauce of fried soybean paste, pork and onion. Born in Incheon''s Chinatown around 1905, it became so Korean that on 14 April single people eat it dressed in black.',ARRAY['Fry the black bean paste in oil for 4 minutes over low heat: this step removes its bitterness, and cannot be skipped.','Fry the diced pork with the ginger, then add the onion, courgette and potato in small cubes.','Sweat for 10 minutes: the onion must give up its sugar, and the sauce depends on it.','Add the fried paste and a glass of water, simmer for 10 minutes, then thicken with slaked starch.','Cook the noodles, plate them, pour the sauce over and crown with finely julienned cucumber.']::text[]),
('galbijjim','en','Galbijjim','Busan, South Korea','Beef short ribs braised in a sweet soy sauce with radish, carrot, jujubes and chestnuts. A dish for great Korean occasions, served at Chuseok and weddings, never on an ordinary day.',ARRAY['Soak the ribs for 2 hours in cold water, changing it twice: this is what gives a clear sauce.','Blanch them for 10 minutes, discard the water and rinse each piece carefully.','Blend the pear, onion, garlic and ginger: the pear''s enzymes tenderise the meat better than any amount of cooking.','Mix this purée with the soy sauce and sugar, pour it over the meat and marinate for 4 hours.','Braise covered for 1½ hours, then add the radish, turned carrots, jujubes, chestnuts and mushrooms for 40 minutes.']::text[]),
('haemul-pajeon','en','Haemul pajeon','Busan, South Korea','A pancake of whole spring onions and seafood, crisp at the edge and soft at the centre. Koreans eat it on rainy days: the sound of the frying, they say, resembles the sound of the downpour.',ARRAY['Whisk the flours into iced water without overworking: a few lumps are better than an elastic batter.','The batter must stay thin and very cold — it is the thermal shock with the oil that makes it crisp.','Lay the whole spring onions side by side in a hot oiled pan, in a single tight layer.','Pour the batter over them, scatter the seafood, then pour the beaten egg on top.','Cook for 6 minutes untouched, flip it in one piece and press with a spatula for 5 minutes. Serve with the soy and vinegar sauce.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('dakgalbi','en','Dakgalbi','Chuncheon, Gangwon, South Korea','Chicken marinated in gochujang, stir-fried at the table on a hotplate with cabbage, sweet potato and rice cakes. Born in Chuncheon in the 1960s as a cheap alternative to pork, it became the students'' dish.',ARRAY['Bone the thighs and cut them into 4 cm pieces, keeping the skin.','Mix the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, sake, garlic, ginger and sugar, and marinate the chicken for 4 hours.','Soak the rice cakes for 20 minutes in warm water so they soften.','Heat a wide plate, tip in the chicken and its marinade, and let it sear without stirring for 5 minutes.','Add the cabbage, sweet potato and rice cakes, and stir-fry for 15 minutes. Finish with perilla leaves and sesame oil.']::text[]),
('yukhoe','en','Yukhoe','Daegu, South Korea','Raw beef cut into fine batons, dressed with sesame oil and crowned with an egg yolk. The grated Korean pear is not decorative: its enzymes tenderise the meat while it waits.',ARRAY['Choose beef of impeccable freshness and keep it in the freezer for 30 minutes: firm, it slices cleanly.','Cut it into 5 mm batons by knife, against the grain, never chopping it.','Mix the sesame oil, soy, sugar, crushed garlic and chopped spring onion, and dress the meat at the last moment.','Julienne the pear finely and lay it in a ring on the plate.','Mound the beef in the centre, hollow a well, set the egg yolk in it and scatter sesame and pine nuts. Serve immediately.']::text[]),
('kongguksu','en','Kongguksu','Jeonju, Jeolla, South Korea','Cold noodles in a thick unsweetened soy milk, served with cucumber and ice. The opposite of samgyetang: here summer heat is fought with the most complete cold.',ARRAY['Soak the soybeans for 8 hours, then rub them between your hands to remove the skins, which float to the surface.','Boil them for 12 minutes only: overcooked, they lose their sweetness and take on a floury taste.','Blend them at length with the toasted sesame, pine nuts and cold water, then pass through a fine sieve.','Chill this milk for at least 3 hours: it must be ice cold, not merely cool.','Cook the noodles, rinse them thoroughly in iced water, plate them, pour over the soy milk and add cucumber, tomato and ice cubes. Salt at the table.']::text[]),
('laksa-sarawak','en','Sarawak laksa','Kuching, Sarawak, Malaysia','Neither curry nor assam laksa: a third way, built on sambal paste, tamarind and coconut milk, over vermicelli with prawns and shredded chicken. Anthony Bourdain called it the breakfast of the gods.',ARRAY['Peel the prawns and make a stock with the heads and shells for 20 minutes, then strain it: this is the base of the broth.','Poach the chicken in that stock, lift it out and shred it by hand, never with a knife.','Pound the soaked chillies, shallot, garlic, lemongrass, galangal, candlenuts, coriander, cumin and shrimp paste into a very fine paste.','Fry it for 15 minutes in the oil, until it darkens and the oil separates.','Pour in the stock, the tamarind then the coconut milk, and simmer for 20 minutes without boiling. Serve over vermicelli with prawns, chicken, shredded omelette, sprouts and lime.']::text[]),
('plov-samarkand','en','Samarkand plov','Samarkand, Uzbekistan','Unlike Tashkent plov, this one is never stirred: rice, carrots and meat stay in distinct layers until serving. Valley devzira rice and yellow carrot make it a wedding dish.',ARRAY['Rinse the rice until the water runs clear and soak it in warm salted water while the rest cooks.','Render the mutton fat in the kazan, remove the scratchings, and sear the meat in large pieces.','Add the onion in half-moons, then the yellow carrot in thick batons — never grated — and let it soften without hurrying it.','Cover with water, add the soaked chickpeas, whole garlic heads, quince and cumin, and simmer for 45 minutes: this is the zirvak.','Spread the drained rice on top without stirring, pour in boiling water to two fingers, let it evaporate uncovered then cover for 25 minutes. Serve in inverted layers.']::text[]),
('rogan-josh','en','Rogan josh','Srinagar, Kashmir, India','A deep red lamb curry made without hot chilli, coloured only by mild Kashmiri chillies and cockscomb flower. In its Kashmiri Brahmin version it contains neither onion nor garlic.',ARRAY['Heat the ghee and infuse the whole spices in it — cardamom, cloves, cinnamon, bay — until they sing.','Add the asafoetida, then the meat in large cubes, and sear it over high heat without stirring too often.','Stir in the Kashmiri chilli off the heat: it provides the colour, and would scorch over a high flame.','Add the beaten yogurt a spoonful at a time, stirring constantly: tipped in at once it would split.','Add the ground ginger and fennel, cover and simmer for 1½ hours over low heat. Finish with infused saffron.']::text[]),
('vindaloo-goanais','en','Goan vindaloo','Panaji, Goa, India','The direct descendant of Portuguese carne de vinha d''alhos, in which palm vinegar replaced wine and chillies invited themselves. The name has nothing to do with potato: aloo is a false trail.',ARRAY['Soak the dried chillies for 30 minutes in warm cane vinegar.','Blend them with the garlic, ginger, cumin, cloves, cinnamon and pepper into a smooth paste — no water, only vinegar.','Marinate the diced pork in this paste for 12 hours in the fridge.','Fry the sliced onion golden in the oil, then add the meat and its marinade, and sear for 10 minutes.','Add the tamarind, sugar and a glass of water, cover and simmer for 1¼ hours. The dish is better the next day.']::text[]),
('chettinad-kozhi','en','Chettinad chicken','Karaikudi, Tamil Nadu, India','The most heavily spiced curry of Tamil Nadu, with stone flower and roasted coconut. The Chettiars were merchants trading as far as Burma: their cooking brought back spices the rest of India does not use.',ARRAY['Dry-roast the grated coconut, chillies, coriander, fennel, pepper, star anise and stone flower separately, until each becomes fragrant.','Let them cool before grinding: ground hot, spices turn bitter.','Fry the whole shallots and curry leaves in the sesame oil, then add the ginger-garlic paste.','Add the jointed chicken, turmeric and tomato, and sear for 10 minutes.','Stir in the spice powder, add a glass of water and simmer for 30 minutes: the sauce should coat the meat, not drown it.']::text[]),
('undhiyu','en','Undhiyu','Surat, Gujarat, India','A stew of every winter vegetable, originally cooked in an earthenware pot turned upside down and buried under a fire. Undhu means ''upside down'': the dish is named after its vanished cooking method.',ARRAY['Make the green filling: blend coriander, coconut, green chillies, ginger, sugar and salt into a thick paste.','Slit the aubergines and potatoes crosswise without separating them, and stuff them with this paste.','Make the muthia: mix the chickpea flour, a little of the paste, carom seeds and water, shape into logs and fry them.','Heat the oil with the asafoetida, add the hard vegetables first and the tender ones after, never stirring with a spoon.','Cover and cook for 50 minutes over very low heat, shaking the pot. Add the muthia and remaining paste ten minutes before the end.']::text[]),
('litti-chokha','en','Litti chokha','Patna, Bihar, India','Wheat balls stuffed with roasted gram flour, baked in embers and dipped in ghee, served with a smoked aubergine mash. The field food of Bihar, cooked without a pan or a drop of oil.',ARRAY['Knead the wholemeal flour, a little ghee, salt and water into a firm dough; rest for 30 minutes.','Dry-roast the gram flour until it browns, then mix it with garlic, ginger, chillies, carom seeds, lemon and mustard oil.','Shape balls of dough, hollow them, fill with two spoonfuls of the mixture and seal carefully.','Cook them for 25 minutes in embers, turning, until the crust blackens in places; or 40 minutes in an oven at 200°C.','Char the aubergines, tomatoes and potatoes over a flame, peel and mash them with onion, coriander and mustard oil. Dip the litti in melted ghee.']::text[]),
('khao-soi','en','Khao soi','Chiang Mai, Thailand','A coconut curry with egg noodles, crowned with the same noodles fried crisp. It came from Burma with Yunnanese Muslim caravans, and still bears the mark of that route in its turmeric and cardamom.',ARRAY['Soak the chillies, then pound them with shallot, garlic, ginger and lemongrass into a fine paste.','Reduce the thick cream from the top of the coconut tin until the oil separates, then fry the paste in it for 5 minutes.','Add the curry powder and turmeric, then the chicken, and let it coat before pouring in the rest of the coconut milk.','Simmer for 30 minutes, and season with fish sauce, palm sugar and lime.','Deep-fry a quarter of the noodles until they puff. Plate the boiled noodles, the curry, then the fried noodles, with raw shallot and pickled greens.']::text[]),
('massaman-curry','en','Massaman curry','Songkhla, Thailand','A mild curry of dry spices — cinnamon, cardamom, clove — brought by 17th-century Persian traders. The name comes from ''Musulman'': it is the only Thai curry that is barely hot at all.',ARRAY['Dry-roast the cinnamon, cardamom and cloves, then grind them with the soaked chillies, shallot, garlic, lemongrass and galangal.','Reduce the coconut cream until the oil beads, then fry the paste in it for 8 minutes: this step builds the whole flavour.','Add the beef in large cubes and let it coat, then pour in the rest of the coconut milk.','Simmer covered for 1 hour, then add the potatoes and peanuts for 25 minutes.','Season with tamarind, palm sugar and fish sauce: the curry should be sweet, salty and sour at once.']::text[]),
('bun-bo-hue','en','Bún bò Huế','Huế, Vietnam','The noodle soup of the old imperial capital, hotter and more fragrant than phở, with lemongrass and fermented shrimp paste. The noodles are round, never flat: the mark of Huế.',ARRAY['Blanch the bones, shank and knuckle for 10 minutes, then rinse them thoroughly: this is what gives a clear broth.','Char the whole onion and ginger over a flame until blackened, then add them to the broth with the bruised lemongrass.','Simmer for 3 hours without ever boiling, skimming regularly.','Slake the shrimp paste in a little broth, let it settle and pour in only the clear liquid: the sediment would cloud the soup.','Season with fish sauce and sugar, and add the lemongrass chilli oil. Serve over round vermicelli, with sprouts, banana flower and herbs.']::text[]),
('cao-lau','en','Cao lầu','Hội An, Vietnam','Thick firm noodles, lacquered pork and very little broth. Tradition holds that true noodles are made with water from one specific Cham well in Hội An and wood ash from the Cham Islands.',ARRAY['Marinate the pork for 4 hours in the soy sauce, five spice, garlic and shallot.','Roast it for 45 minutes, basting, then cool it before slicing it very thinly.','Make a short broth with the bones and roasting juices: only three spoonfuls per bowl are needed, no more.','Blanch the bean sprouts and noodles separately — the noodles must stay firm to the bite, almost crunchy.','Layer sprouts, noodles, pork, herbs and crisp rice crackers, then spoon over the seasoned broth. Each diner mixes.']::text[]),
('soto-betawi','en','Soto Betawi','Jakarta, Indonesia','A soup of beef and offal with both coconut milk and cow''s milk, the speciality of the Betawi, the mixed people of Batavia. Using both milks is a signature: coconut for the fat, cow for the sweetness.',ARRAY['Cook the brisket and tripe for 1½ hours in water with the lemongrass and galangal, then lift them out and dice them.','Strain the broth and keep it.','Pound the shallot, garlic, candlenuts, ginger, cumin and coriander into a fine paste, and fry it until fragrant.','Stir it into the broth with the kaffir lime leaves, then add the coconut milk and the cow''s milk.','Simmer for 20 minutes without boiling, return the meat, and serve with fried potato, tomato, fried shallots and lime.']::text[]),
('babi-guling','en','Babi guling','Ubud, Bali, Indonesia','A suckling pig stuffed with spice paste and spit-roasted for four hours, until the skin turns to glass. A Hindu ceremonial dish in Bali, the only Indonesian island where pork is not forbidden.',ARRAY['Pound all the aromatics in a mortar into a thick paste — the Balinese base gede — and fry it in oil for 20 minutes.','Rub the inside of the pig with half the paste, stuff it with cassava leaves mixed with the rest, and sew the opening.','Rub the skin with turmeric and salted water, and let it dry in the air for two hours.','Spit it and roast over a low fire for 4 hours, turning continuously and basting with coconut water.','Raise the heat for the final twenty minutes: the skin should blister and turn brittle as glass.']::text[]),
('kare-kare','en','Kare-kare','Manila, Philippines','Oxtail in a thick sauce of peanuts and toasted rice, yellow with annatto. It is served without fail with bagoong, the fermented shrimp paste that provides all the salt: the stew itself contains none.',ARRAY['Cook the oxtail and tripe for 2½ hours in water, until the meat leaves the bone; keep the broth.','Dry-toast the raw rice until it browns, then grind it finely: this is what will thicken the sauce.','Fry the onion and garlic, add the slaked annatto, the peanut butter and two litres of broth.','Stir in the toasted rice powder and thicken for 15 minutes, stirring.','Add the vegetables in order of firmness — beans, aubergine, banana flower, pak choi — and the meat. Do not salt: the bagoong does that at the table.']::text[]),
('nasi-lemak','en','Nasi lemak','Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia','Rice cooked in coconut milk and pandan, served in a banana leaf with sambal, fried anchovies, peanuts, cucumber and egg. Malaysia''s national breakfast, sold on street corners from six in the morning.',ARRAY['Rinse the rice until the water runs clear, then cook it in coconut milk let down with water, with the knotted pandan and ginger.','For the sambal: soak the chillies, then pound them with shallot, garlic and shrimp paste.','Fry this paste for 20 minutes over low heat, until the oil separates and rises, red and glossy.','Add the tamarind and palm sugar, and cook down for another 10 minutes.','Fry the dried anchovies and peanuts separately. Serve the rice in a banana leaf with sambal, anchovies, peanuts, cucumber and half a hard-boiled egg.']::text[]),
('ohn-no-khao-swe','en','Ohn no khao swè','Yangon, Myanmar','Wheat noodles in a chicken and coconut broth thickened with chickpea flour. The Burmese cousin of khao soi, gentler: it is Yangon''s street lunch, served with a wedge of lime.',ARRAY['Blend the onion, garlic and ginger into a paste, and fry it in the oil for 15 minutes with the turmeric.','Add the jointed chicken and let it coat, then pour in a litre of water and cook for 25 minutes.','Slake the chickpea flour in a little cold water, lump-free, and trickle it into the broth while stirring.','Add the coconut milk and simmer for 10 minutes: the soup should coat the spoon.','Serve over the noodles with a halved hard-boiled egg, coriander, fried onion, lime and chilli oil.']::text[]),
('lok-lak','en','Beef lok lak','Phnom Penh, Cambodia','Cubes of beef seared very fast, served over salad with a Kampot pepper and lime sauce. Before the war Kampot pepper was the most expensive in the world: Paris tables swore by nothing else.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into 2 cm cubes and marinate for 30 minutes in the soy, oyster sauce, garlic and sugar.','Crack the pepper coarsely in a mortar and mix it with lime juice and salt: this is the tuk meric sauce, served on the side.','Arrange the lettuce, tomato, cucumber and red onion in a ring on the plates.','Sear the beef in a scorching pan, in small batches, for 90 seconds in all: it should stay pink.','Set the meat on the salad, crown with a fried egg and serve with rice and the pepper sauce.']::text[]),
('lamprais','en','Lamprais','Colombo, Sri Lanka','Rice cooked in stock, three meats in curry, a candied aubergine and a shrimp rissole, all folded into a banana leaf and returned to the oven. A Dutch Burgher legacy: the name comes from lomprijst, ''packet rice''.',ARRAY['Make a stock with the three meats, cardamom, cloves and cinnamon; set the meats and stock aside separately.','Fry the rice in ghee with the whole spices, then cook it in the strained stock, never in water.','Dice the meats and simmer them in curry and coconut milk until the sauce is short.','Fry the aubergine in slices and candy it with tamarind, sugar and chillies: this is brinjal moju.','Pass the banana leaves over a flame to soften them, fill with rice and the accompaniments, fold into parcels and bake for 25 minutes at 180°C.']::text[]),
('dal-bhat-tarkari','en','Dal bhat tarkari','Kathmandu, Nepal','Rice, lentils and seasonal vegetables served together on a tray, twice a day, for a lifetime. Himalayan porters made a saying of it: ''dal bhat power, twenty-four hour''.',ARRAY['Rinse the lentils and cook them for 30 minutes with the turmeric, until they collapse completely.','Prepare the jhaneko, the tempering: sizzle the fenugreek, cumin, garlic, ginger and dried chillies in scorching ghee.','Pour this fragrant butter into the dal — it spits violently — and cover at once for a minute to trap the aromas.','For the tarkari, fry the vegetables with the same spices and a little tomato, covered, for 20 minutes.','Serve the rice in the centre of the tray, the dal in a bowl, the tarkari alongside, with yogurt and a pickle.']::text[]),
('panch-phoron-torkari','en','Five-spice torkari','Dhaka, Bangladesh','A vegetable stew with panch phoron, the Bengali blend of five whole seeds that is never ground. Nigella, fennel, fenugreek, cumin and mustard: five flavours that reveal themselves one after another in hot oil.',ARRAY['Heat the mustard oil until it smokes then let it settle: raw it is acrid; smoked it turns sweet.','Drop in the whole panch phoron and the bay, and wait for the mustard seeds to pop — not before, not after.','Add the grated ginger and split chillies, then the vegetables in large pieces, firmest first.','Dust with turmeric, salt, add a pinch of sugar, and cover: the vegetables cook in their own water, with none added.','Give them 25 minutes over low heat, shaking the pan, and cover with coriander off the heat.']::text[]),
('shivit-oshi','en','Shivit oshi','Khiva, Khorezm, Uzbekistan','Deep green noodles, coloured by dill juice kneaded into the dough, under a stew of vegetables and meat. They are found only in Khiva and the villages around it: elsewhere in Uzbekistan nobody knows them.',ARRAY['Blend the dill with a glass of water and press it through a cloth: it is this green juice, and nothing else, that colours the dough.','Knead the flour, eggs, salt and dill juice for 15 minutes; rest for an hour.','Roll very thin, fold and cut 2 mm noodles, then flour them generously.','Sear the diced meat, add onion, carrot, pepper, tomato, garlic and cumin, cover with water and simmer for 40 minutes with the potatoes.','Cook the noodles for 3 minutes, arrange them in a nest and crown with the stew. Serve the yogurt alongside.']::text[]),
('kuurdak','en','Kuurdak','Bishkek, Kyrgyzstan','A fry-up of mutton and offal with onions and potatoes, cooked in a cast-iron kazan. It was the first dish made after slaughtering an animal, with the cuts that would not keep.',ARRAY['Render the diced mutton fat in the kazan until only scratchings remain, and lift them out.','Sear the meat in large cubes in that fat over high heat, without stirring too soon: it must brown.','Add the trimmed offal, then the sliced onions — a great many, as much as there is meat — and let them melt for 20 minutes.','Add the cumin, cracked pepper and bay, cover and simmer for 40 minutes in nothing but the released juices.','Add the potatoes in large pieces for the last 25 minutes, and serve with flatbread and coriander.']::text[]),
('chelo-kabab-barg','en','Kabab barg with saffron rice','Isfahan, Iran','Slices of fillet marinated in onion and saffron, grilled on flat skewers, served with rice crisped at the bottom of the pan. That golden tahdig is fought over at the table more than the meat itself.',ARRAY['Grate the onions and press out their juice; mix it with infused saffron, yogurt and lemon, and marinate the meat for 12 hours.','Rinse the rice seven times then soak it for 2 hours in salted water: this is what gives long separate grains.','Par-boil it for 6 minutes in plenty of water, drain, then mound it in a buttered pan with saffron.','Cover with a cloth and a lid and cook for 45 minutes over very low heat: a golden crust forms at the bottom.','Thread the meat onto flat skewers and grill for 3 minutes a side over fierce embers. Serve with the rice, the tahdig, grilled tomato and sumac.']::text[]),
('maqluba','en','Maqluba','Nablus, Palestine','A dish of rice, aubergine and chicken built in layers then turned out in one piece onto a tray. Maqluba means ''upside down'': the moment of inversion is done in silence, and applauded if it holds.',ARRAY['Poach the chicken for 40 minutes with onion, bay, cardamom and allspice; keep the broth, which will cook the rice.','Fry the aubergine slices and cauliflower florets until golden, then drain them.','Soak the rice for 30 minutes, drain it and mix it with the ground spices.','Line the base of a pot with aubergine in a rosette, then the boned chicken, the cauliflower, and finally the rice, well packed.','Pour in hot broth to two centimetres above the rice, cover and cook for 40 minutes. Rest for 15 minutes before inverting in one decisive movement.']::text[]),
('kibbeh-nayyeh','en','Kibbeh nayyeh','Tripoli, Lebanon','Raw lamb pounded in a stone mortar with fine bulgur and spices, served as a smooth carpet under a thread of oil. It is made on the day of slaughter, and eaten within the hour: that is the rule.',ARRAY['Choose a leg of lamb slaughtered the same day, trim it of all fat and sinew, and keep it ice cold until the last moment.','Rinse the fine bulgur and squeeze it: it should be damp but not sodden.','Pound the onion and spices in a mortar into a paste, then add the meat in small amounts.','Pound at length, working in the bulgur and a few spoonfuls of iced water, until the mass is perfectly smooth.','Spread it thinly on a tray, draw furrows with your fingers, pour over olive oil and serve at once with mint, spring onions and bread.']::text[]),
('ash-e-reshteh','en','Ash-e reshteh','Tehran, Iran','A thick soup of pulses, herbs and noodles, finished with kashk — a dried fermented whey. Made for Nowruz, the Persian new year: the noodles stand for the threads of fate being untangled.',ARRAY['Soak the chickpeas and beans overnight, then cook them for 1 hour with the turmeric and one onion.','Add the lentils and continue for 25 minutes.','Chop all the herbs very finely — they should make an enormous volume, nearly two packed litres — and add them to the soup.','Break the noodles and drop them into the broth for 10 minutes; stir, as they catch easily.','Prepare two garnishes: onions fried to deep brown, and dried mint fried in oil with garlic. Serve with kashk swirled over.']::text[]),
('khachapuri-megrelien','en','Megrelian khachapuri','Zugdidi, Samegrelo, Georgia','A round cheese bread, filled and also topped with sulguni, unlike the boat-shaped Adjarian version. Samegrelo doubles the dose: cheese inside and on top, and nobody apologises for it.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast and sugar in the warm milk, then knead with the flour, yogurt, one egg, melted butter and salt.','Prove for 1½ hours: the dough should be very supple, almost sticky.','Grate the sulguni and work two thirds of it by hand until it turns elastic.','Roll the dough into a disc, pile the cheese in the centre, gather the edges to the middle and turn the ball over. Flatten it gently with a rolling pin.','Glaze with egg, cover with the remaining cheese and bake for 20 minutes at 240°C. Butter it generously as it comes out.']::text[]),
('rfissa','en','Rfissa','Rabat, Morocco','Chicken with fenugreek and ras el hanout served over hand-torn msemen pancakes. It is traditionally made for a new mother on the third day: the fenugreek is said to restore her strength.',ARRAY['Soak the fenugreek overnight: without this it stays hard and bitter.','Fry the sliced onions in the oil and smen until they melt, then add the spices and saffron.','Add the whole chicken, the lentils, the drained fenugreek and the tied bunch of herbs; cover with water.','Simmer for 1¼ hours, then lift out the chicken and reduce the sauce until it coats.','Tear the msemen by hand into wide shreds, lay them in the dish, ladle broth over so they swell, and crown with the chicken and lentils.']::text[]),
('tanjia-marrakchia','en','Marrakchi tanjia','Marrakesh, Morocco','Veal shank sealed in an earthenware jar and entrusted to the hammam stoker, who buries it in the ashes of the water furnace for five hours. It is the dish of the medina''s bachelors: nobody cooks it at home.',ARRAY['Choose a well-marbled shank, cut into large pieces with the bone: the collagen makes the whole texture.','Crush the whole garlic, mix it with the cumin, ras el hanout, infused saffron and quartered preserved lemon.','Put everything into a narrow-necked jar with the smen, the oil and half a glass of water — no more.','Close the neck with tied greaseproof paper, pierced with a single needle hole.','Bury the jar in hot ash or cook it for 5 hours at 140°C. It is tipped onto a dish without ever being stirred.']::text[]),
('chakhchoukha','en','Chakhchouka des Aurès','Batna, Aurès, Algeria','Rougag flatbreads torn into small pieces and drenched in a lamb and chickpea sauce with chilli. The tearing is done by several people, by hand, the day before: women''s work and an occasion to gather.',ARRAY['Knead fine semolina with warm water and salt, rest it, then roll very thin flatbreads cooked dry on an earthenware griddle.','Stack them, let them cool, then tear them by hand into pieces the size of a fingernail.','Fry the lamb and onion in the oil, add the tomato paste, paprika, harissa and caraway.','Add water, the soaked chickpeas and the cinnamon, and simmer for 1¼ hours.','Steam the torn bread for 10 minutes, moisten with a ladle of sauce, toss, and lay the meat over the top.']::text[]),
('mloukhia-tunisienne','en','Tunisian mloukhia','Tunis, Tunisia','An almost black sauce of pounded jute leaves, cooked for five hours until the oil rises to the top. It is served at the Muslim new year so that the year will be green — that is, prosperous.',ARRAY['Slake the jute powder in cold water very gradually, whisking: tipped in at once it forms lumps that cannot be undone.','Heat the olive oil in a tall pot, throw in the garlic and bay, then pour in the green paste.','Cook uncovered for 2 hours over very low heat, stirring every twenty minutes with a wooden spoon.','Add the meat in large cubes, the spices and hot water, and continue for 3 hours.','The sauce is ready when it has blackened and the oil beads on the surface. It keeps a week and improves every day.']::text[]),
('lablabi','en','Lablabi','Bab Souika, Tunis, Tunisia','A chickpea broth with garlic and cumin poured over stale bread crumbled in the bottom of the bowl, with a poached egg and harissa. The winter breakfast of Tunis labourers, served from five in the morning.',ARRAY['Soak the chickpeas overnight with a pinch of bicarbonate, then cook them for 1¼ hours with half the garlic.','On no account throw away the cooking water: cloudy and fragrant, it is what makes the dish.','Pound the rest of the garlic with the cumin and salt, and slake it in a little broth.','Crumble the stale bread into each bowl, ladle scalding broth over it generously and let it drink.','Add the chickpeas, poached egg, a spoonful of harissa, olive oil, capers, olives, tuna and lemon. Each diner mixes.']::text[]),
('bazeen','en','Bazeen','Tripoli, Libya','A dome of boiled barley dough set in the middle of the dish, ringed by a lamb sauce and crowned with hard-boiled eggs. It is eaten with the hand, pinching the dough and dipping it: never with a spoon.',ARRAY['Fry the lamb and onion, add the tomato paste and let it darken for two minutes: raw, it stays acidic.','Add the spices, the soaked chickpeas and water, and simmer for an hour with the potatoes.','Bring a litre of salted water to the boil and tip in the barley flour all at once, without stirring, leaving a well.','Cover and leave for 15 minutes, then work vigorously with a wooden stick until the dough is smooth and dense.','Shape a dome in the middle of the dish with wet hands, ring it with the sauce and set the hard-boiled eggs around.']::text[]),
('mafe-thiof','en','Baked grouper yassa','Gorée, Dakar, Senegal','A whole grouper stuffed with pounded herbs and baked under a mountain of lemony onions. Thiof is Senegal''s emblematic fish, so prized that the word is a compliment: to call someone thiof is to call them handsome.',ARRAY['Pound the parsley, six garlic cloves, a chilli and salt into a green paste, the roff.','Score the fish diagonally on both sides and push the roff deep into each cut.','Marinate the sliced onions with lemon, mustard and bay for 2 hours, then soften them for 30 minutes in the oil.','Set the fish in a dish, cover it entirely with the onions and bake for 35 minutes at 190°C.','Never turn the fish: serve it in its dish, with white rice and the onion juices.']::text[]),
('yassa-poulet','en','Chicken yassa','Ziguinchor, Casamance, Senegal','Chicken marinated in lime and grilled, then simmered in a mountain of melted onions. The proportion is disconcerting: as much onion as meat, and that is what makes the dish.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken and sliced onions for 6 hours in lime juice, mustard, garlic and bay.','Drain and keep the marinade; grill the chicken pieces over charcoal until the skin is charred in places.','Melt the onions in the oil for 30 minutes, covered: they should turn golden and translucent without burning.','Pour in the marinade, add the chilli whole — unsplit, or the dish becomes inedible — and the olives.','Return the chicken, cover and simmer for 25 minutes. Serve over white rice.']::text[]),
('to-au-gombo','en','Tô with okra sauce','Ségou, Mali','A firm smooth paste of millet or maize, dipped in a slippery okra and soumbala sauce. It is swallowed unchewed, in balls shaped by hand: the stringy texture is precisely the point.',ARRAY['Bring two litres of water to the boil and slake a handful of millet flour in it to make a thin gruel.','Rain in the rest of the flour and work it with a wooden stick, pressing against the side, for 20 minutes.','The tô is ready when the paste comes away in one mass; mould it into a dome in a wetted bowl.','For the sauce, fry the onion and tomato in palm oil, add the dried fish, soumbala and chilli.','Add the sliced okra and baobab leaf powder, and give it 15 minutes: the sauce should trail in threads behind the spoon.']::text[]),
('poulet-dg','en','Poulet DG','Douala, Cameroon','Chicken and fried plantain tossed with crisp vegetables. DG stands for directeur général: in the 1980s only bosses could afford a whole chicken, and the name stuck as a joke.',ARRAY['Marinate the jointed chicken for an hour in pounded garlic and ginger, white pepper and thyme.','Fry it until golden and cooked through, then drain.','Fry ripe plantains cut on the bias in the same oil: they should caramelise at the edges.','Fry the onion, tomato and chilli in a little clean oil, then add the vegetables cut into batons.','Toss over high heat for 5 minutes — the vegetables must stay crunchy — then fold in the chicken and plantain off the heat.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('koki-beans','en','Koki','Bafoussam, Cameroon','A pudding of hulled cowpeas beaten with palm oil, steamed in banana leaves. The beating is the secret: the batter must become so light that a spoonful floats on a glass of water.',ARRAY['Soak the cowpeas for 6 hours, then rub them hard between your hands to remove every skin.','Blend them with the onion and chilli into a thick paste, without adding too much water.','Warm the palm oil and trickle it into the paste while beating with a wooden spoon, for a full 15 minutes.','Test the lightness: a spoonful dropped on a glass of water should rise and float. If not, beat on.','Fold in the shredded spinach, fill the softened banana leaves, close into parcels and steam for 1¼ hours.']::text[]),
('egusi-soup','en','Egusi soup','Benin City, Nigeria','A thick soup of ground melon seeds, which set into small golden lumps in the palm oil. It is not stirred for the first twenty minutes: that is what lets the lumps form.',ARRAY['Cook the beef, tripe and stockfish for 45 minutes with the onion; keep the broth.','Grind the egusi seeds and mix them with a little water into a thick paste.','Heat the palm oil until it clears, then drop in the paste in spoonfuls, without stirring.','Let the lumps set for 10 minutes, then pour the broth gently around them and simmer for 20 minutes untouched.','Add the crayfish powder, locust beans, chilli and spinach, and continue for 10 minutes. Serve with cassava fufu.']::text[]),
('red-red','en','Red red','Jamestown, Accra, Ghana','Cowpeas stewed in red palm oil with very ripe fried plantain. The name comes from the double redness: that of the palm oil and that of the tomato sauce, never one without the other.',ARRAY['Soak the cowpeas for 4 hours, then cook them for 45 minutes with the bay, unsalted: salted too early, they toughen.','Heat the palm oil until it clears, without letting it smoke.','Soften the onion, garlic and ginger in it, add the tomato paste and let it brown for two minutes.','Add the crushed tomato, ground dried shrimp and whole chilli, and reduce for 15 minutes.','Fold in the drained beans and stew for 20 minutes. Serve with very ripe plantain fried on the bias.']::text[]),
('poulet-kedjenou','en','Chicken kedjenou','Yamoussoukro, Ivory Coast','Chicken smothered in a sealed clay canari, without a drop of added water. Kedjenou comes from Baoulé and means ''to shake'': the pot is never opened, only shaken to stop it catching.',ARRAY['Joint the chicken and combine it with crushed tomatoes, quartered onions, African aubergines and the aromatics.','Season, pour in the oil and pack everything into an earthenware pot.','Seal the lid with a band of flour-and-water dough, or failing that a double sheet of foil pinched tight.','Cook for 1½ hours over low heat or in a 160°C oven, shaking the pot every fifteen minutes, never opening it.','On opening, the vegetables will have given up all their juice: it is the only liquid in the dish. Serve with rice or attiéké.']::text[]),
('thiakry','en','Thiakry','Banjul, Gambia','Steamed millet couscous pearls drowned in sweetened soured milk, served very cold. It is the Ramadan dessert of all Senegambia, and breakfast for the rest of the year.',ARRAY['Moisten the millet couscous with a little salted water and let it swell for 10 minutes, separating the grains by hand.','Steam it for 15 minutes, separate the grains again, then let it cool completely: warm, it would turn the milk.','Whisk the yogurt, soured milk, condensed milk, sugar and scraped vanilla.','Fold in the cold couscous, grated nutmeg, raisins and orange blossom water.','Chill for at least 3 hours: the pearls should drink the milk without collapsing.']::text[]),
('kitfo','en','Kitfo','Hosaena, Gurage, Ethiopia','Raw beef chopped by knife and warmed in spiced niter kibbeh butter, served with fresh cheese and collard leaves. Among the Gurage it is eaten on Meskel, around a fire lit at dusk.',ARRAY['Choose a fillet slaughtered the same day, trim it completely and keep it cold until the last minute.','Chop it by knife, never in a mincer: the fibres must stay distinct to the bite.','Warm the niter kibbeh without letting it smoke — it should not cook the meat, only warm it.','Combine the meat, butter, mitmita and ground cardamom with your fingertips.','Serve at once with the fresh cheese, chilli-stewed collards and injera. You may ask for leb leb, barely warmed, or yebesele, cooked.']::text[]),
('shiro-wat','en','Shiro wat','Addis Ababa, Ethiopia','A purée of roasted, spice-ground chickpeas simmered until it bubbles like lava. It is the dish of the Orthodox fast, free of animal products, eaten more than two hundred days a year.',ARRAY['Soften the very finely chopped onion dry, without fat, for 10 minutes: it gives up its water and concentrates.','Only then add the oil, the pounded garlic and ginger, and fry for 5 minutes.','Stir in the berbere off the heat, then the tomato, and cook for 5 minutes.','Slake the shiro powder in half a litre of cold water, lump-free, and trickle it in while whisking.','Simmer for 15 minutes, stirring often: the purée should make large lazy bubbles. Serve on injera with a green chilli.']::text[]),
('tsebhi-birsen','en','Tsebhi birsen','Keren, Eritrea','A red lentil stew with berbere, cooked until the lentils collapse into a thick purée. It is Eritrea''s commonest fasting dish, served on injera with a raw green chilli to bite into.',ARRAY['Soften the very finely chopped onion dry for 10 minutes, without fat: it gives up its water and concentrates.','Add the oil and the pounded garlic and ginger, and fry for 5 minutes.','Stir in the berbere off the heat, then the tomato paste and tomato, and cook for 5 minutes.','Add the rinsed lentils and a litre of hot water, and simmer uncovered for 45 minutes.','Stir often for the last half hour: the lentils must collapse completely. Serve on injera.']::text[]),
('fah-fah','en','Fah-fah','Djibouti City, Djibouti','A lamb broth intensely green with herbs and chilli, served with flatbread to mop it up. Also called soup number five, Djiboutians hold it a remedy for fatigue and fever.',ARRAY['Blanch the lamb and bones for 10 minutes, discard the water and rinse: the broth must stay clear.','Return them to three litres of water with the onion, garlic and cumin, and simmer for 1 hour, skimming.','Add the vegetables in large pieces and continue for 25 minutes.','Blend the coriander, parsley and green chillies with a ladle of broth into a vivid green purée.','Stir it into the soup off the heat — cooked, it would brown — and serve with lime and flatbread.']::text[]),
('suqaar','en','Suqaar','Mogadishu, Somalia','Cubes of beef fried with xawaash, the Somali blend of cumin, coriander and cardamom, with pepper and onion. It is served with a raw banana, split lengthways.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into 1.5 cm dice and marinate for 30 minutes in the xawaash, garlic and lemon.','Heat the oil until it just smokes, and sear the meat in small batches.','Lift it out as soon as it is coloured: it will finish cooking when it returns to the pan.','Fry the onion and pepper for 5 minutes, add the tomato and chilli, and let it reduce.','Return the meat, warm through for a minute, cover with coriander and serve with rice and a raw banana.']::text[]),
('mukimo','en','Mukimo','Nyeri, Kikuyu country, Kenya','A green mash of potato, maize, beans and pumpkin leaves, pounded with a stick. Among the Kikuyu it is brought to the bridegroom on the wedding day: its vivid colour is a wish for abundance.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight then cook them for 50 minutes; the maize needs as long as they do.','Boil the peeled potatoes for 25 minutes in salted water.','Blanch the pumpkin leaves and spinach for 5 minutes, squeeze them dry and chop them very finely.','Bring everything together while still hot and pound with a wooden stick: the mash should stay lumpy, the grains still recognisable.','Fold in the butter-softened onion and adjust the salt. Serve as a dome, with a meat stew alongside.']::text[]),
('urojo','en','Urojo','Forodhani, Zanzibar, Tanzania','A tangy yellow soup of green mango, topped with fritters, potatoes and skewers. Sold in the evening on the Forodhani gardens, it is also called ''Zanzibar mix'': each diner builds their own bowl.',ARRAY['Cook the diced potatoes in a litre of water with the turmeric, garlic, ginger and grated green mango.','Slake the chickpea flour in cold water and trickle it in while whisking: the soup should coat without being thick.','Add the tamarind and coconut milk, and simmer for 15 minutes.','Prepare the toppings: potato fritters, fried cassava, grilled beef skewers.','Each diner builds their bowl: scalding soup, toppings, chilli sauce, coriander chutney and lime.']::text[]),
('matoke-luwombo','en','Chicken luwombo','Kampala, Uganda','Chicken and a groundnut sauce sealed in a banana-leaf parcel and steamed for three hours. Created in 1887 by the chef of Kabaka Mwanga II, it was originally served only at the Buganda court.',ARRAY['Pass the banana leaves over a flame until they green and soften: otherwise they split when folded.','Fry the onion, tomato and pepper, add the curry powder, then the jointed chicken.','Slake the peanut butter in a little hot water and stir it in: the sauce should stay very thick.','Set everything on two crossed leaves, gather into a purse and tie with a strip of leaf rib.','Steam for 2½ hours above the plantains cooking in the same pot. The parcel is opened at the table.']::text[]),
('muamba-de-galinha','en','Muamba de galinha','Luanda, Angola','Chicken with palm oil and palm-nut pulp, with okra and squash. Angola''s national dish, a direct cousin of Congolese moambe, which Portugal carried as far as Brazil.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken for 2 hours in pounded garlic, lemon, salt and pepper.','Colour it in the palm oil, then add the onion and tomato.','Slake the palm-nut pulp in a litre of hot water and pass it through a sieve to remove the fibres.','Simmer uncovered for 40 minutes, until the orange oil rises clearly to the surface.','Add the cubed squash and whole okra for 20 minutes, with the chilli. Serve with cassava funge.']::text[]),
('chikanda','en','Chikanda','Lusaka, Zambia','A dense cake of wild orchid tubers, groundnut and bicarbonate, whose texture recalls meat. Known as ''African polony'', its popularity now threatens the orchids of the high plateaus.',ARRAY['Dry the tubers then grind them to a fine flour in a mortar or mill.','Roast the groundnuts, rub off the skins and grind them in turn into an oily powder.','Bring a litre of water to the boil with the bicarbonate, salt, chilli and oil.','Rain in the tuber flour and groundnut powder, whisking constantly to avoid lumps.','Work with a wooden spoon for 30 minutes over low heat, until the paste leaves the bottom, then pour into a mould and let it set. Slice cold.']::text[]),
('bunny-chow','en','Bunny chow','Durban, South Africa','A quarter loaf hollowed out and filled with curry, the crumb serving as both lid and spoon. Invented by Durban Indians under apartheid, to sell takeaway to those who could not enter a restaurant.',ARRAY['Fry the onion, cinnamon, star anise and curry leaves in the oil until the onion turns golden.','Add the pounded garlic and ginger, then the curry powder, and let it open up for 2 minutes.','Add the lamb and let it coat, then the grated tomato, and barely moisten.','Simmer covered for 1 hour, add the potatoes for 25 minutes: the sauce must be very thick, or the bread drowns.','Cut the loaf into four, hollow each quarter, fill with curry and set the removed crumb on top as a cap. Serve with a grated carrot salad.']::text[]),
('potjiekos','en','Potjiekos','Cape Town, South Africa','A stew cooked in layers in a three-legged cast-iron pot set on embers and never stirred. The Voortrekkers carried it by wagon; the rule has not changed: whoever stirs ruins the potjie.',ARRAY['Render the bacon in the pot set over moderate embers, then sear the meat on all sides.','Add the onion and let it turn golden, pour in the wine and stock, and simmer covered for 1½ hours.','Arrange the vegetables in distinct layers, longest-cooking first: carrot, potato, squash, beans.','Salt only the surface and on no account stir: each tier must cook in the steam of the one below.','Give it another hour, then serve by digging in with a ladle from the edge, so the layers stay visible.']::text[]),
('chakalaka','en','Chakalaka','Soweto, Johannesburg, South Africa','A relish of grated vegetables with curry and beans, born in the miners'' hostels of the Witwatersrand. Every family swears its version is the right one, and no two use the same vegetables.',ARRAY['Fry the sliced onion in the oil until translucent.','Add the garlic, ginger, chilli and curry powder, and let it open up for a minute.','Add the peppers in strips, then the coarsely grated carrots — never finely, they would melt away.','Add the crushed tomato, cover and cook for 15 minutes: the carrots must stay firm.','Fold in the beans and cabbage at the end. Serve warm or cold, with pap or a braai.']::text[]),
('ravitoto','en','Ravitoto sy henakisoa','Antananarivo, Madagascar','Pounded cassava leaves, almost black, stewed with fatty pork and coconut milk. The pounding takes an hour in a wooden mortar: the raw leaves are toxic, and only this long cooking makes them safe.',ARRAY['Pound the cassava leaves at length in a wooden mortar, until you have a dark fibrous mass.','Blanch them for 15 minutes in plenty of water and squeeze them dry: this step is not optional.','Sear the pork in large lardons in its own fat, then add the onion, garlic and ginger.','Add the pressed leaves and a glass of water, cover and simmer for 1 hour over low heat.','Pour in the coconut milk for the last twenty minutes. Serve with white rice and a fiery sakay.']::text[]),
('civet-de-zourite','en','Octopus civet','Saint-Pierre, Réunion','An octopus civet with red wine, turmeric and kaffir lime, stewed until the flesh yields. Zourite comes from Malagasy orita; in Réunion it is beaten against a rock before cooking, and that is still done.',ARRAY['Beat the octopus, or freeze and thaw it: the fibres break and the flesh turns tender.','Cut it into lengths and stiffen it dry in the pot until it releases its water; throw that water away.','Add the oil, sliced onions, pounded garlic and ginger, and the turmeric.','Add the tomato, red wine, thyme, kaffir lime and whole chilli, and simmer covered for 1 hour.','Uncover for the last twenty minutes so the sauce coats. Serve with rice and red beans.']::text[]),
('bobo-de-camarao','en','Bobó de camarão','Salvador, Bahia, Brazil','Prawns in a cassava cream with coconut milk and palm oil. The word bobó comes from Fon; the dish descends directly from West African ipeté, which reached Bahia with the slave trade.',ARRAY['Peel the prawns and make a stock with the shells; marinate them in lime and garlic.','Cook the cassava for 40 minutes in the stock, pull out the central fibre and blend it with the liquid into a smooth cream.','Make a refogado of onion, garlic, tomato and pepper in olive oil, 15 minutes.','Pour in the cassava cream and coconut milk, and simmer for 10 minutes, stirring.','Add the prawns and the palm oil for 4 minutes only: any longer and they turn rubbery. Coriander off the heat.']::text[]),
('moqueca-capixaba','en','Capixaba moqueca','Vitória, Espírito Santo, Brazil','The moqueca of Espírito Santo, without coconut milk or palm oil, coloured only with annatto and cooked in a black clay pan. It has quarrelled forever with the richer, redder Bahian version.',ARRAY['Marinate the fish steaks for 30 minutes in lime, pounded garlic, salt and pepper.','Heat the clay pan gently — a thermal shock would crack it — and pour in the oil coloured with annatto.','Lay a bed of sliced onion, then the fish, then the tomato, never stirring.','Cover and cook for 20 minutes over medium heat: the fish gives up its juice, which becomes the broth.','Crown with coriander and spring onion off the heat. Serve with rice and pirão made from the broth thickened with cassava flour.']::text[]),
('baiao-de-dois','en','Baião de dois','Fortaleza, Ceará, Brazil','Rice and cowpeas cooked together with dried beef and coalho cheese. The name comes from a dance for two: rice and bean, they say, dance in the same pot.',ARRAY['Soak the dried beef for 12 hours, changing the water three times, then cook it for 40 minutes and shred it.','Cook the cowpeas for 30 minutes with the bay, keeping the cooking liquid.','Fry the onion, garlic, sausage and shredded beef in the butter and oil.','Add the rice, let it turn pearly, then pour in the beans and their liquid: two parts liquid to one of rice.','Cover and cook for 18 minutes, then fold in the diced coalho cheese and the herbs off the heat.']::text[]),
('barreado','en','Barreado','Morretes, Paraná, Brazil','Beef cooked twelve hours in a jar sealed with cassava-flour paste, until it falls into fibres. It was served at carnival to feed the men for three days without relighting the fire.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into large cubes and marinate for 4 hours with the onion, garlic, cumin, bay and cracked pepper.','Line the base of an earthenware jar with bacon, then pack in the meat and marinade without adding water.','Knead a paste of cassava flour and water, and seal the lid all the way round with it.','Cook for 10 to 12 hours over very low heat — the jar must never boil hard, only tremble.','Break the seal, shred the meat with a fork and serve with cassava flour, mashed banana and a slice of orange.']::text[]),
('chupe-de-camarones','en','Chupe de camarones','Arequipa, Peru','A soup of river prawns with milk, fresh cheese and ají panca, with a poached egg and corn. It is Friday''s dish in Arequipa, and the heads traditionally go in, for flavour.',ARRAY['Peel the prawns and make a stock with the heads and shells for 20 minutes, then press it through a sieve.','Fry the onion, garlic and ají panca for 10 minutes: this base, the aderezo, must darken without burning.','Add the tomato, stock, rice, potatoes, corn and broad beans, and cook for 20 minutes.','Pour in the milk and let it steam without boiling, then add the diced fresh cheese and the huacatay.','Slide in the prawns and crack the eggs into the soup for the last three minutes only.']::text[]),
('juane','en','Juane','Tarapoto, Amazonia, Peru','A ball of turmeric rice and chicken wrapped in a bijao leaf and boiled. It is eaten on Saint John''s day, 24 June: the ball is said to represent the Baptist''s head on its platter.',ARRAY['Marinate the jointed chicken for 2 hours in garlic, cumin, turmeric and sacha culantro.','Colour it, then poach it for 25 minutes in the stock; keep the liquid.','Cook the rice three-quarters through in that yellow stock, drain it and let it cool.','Mix in four beaten eggs, then mould balls around a piece of chicken, half a hard-boiled egg and an olive.','Soften the leaves over a flame, wrap each ball in two crossed leaves, tie, and boil for 45 minutes.']::text[]),
('rocoto-relleno','en','Rocoto relleno','Arequipa, Peru','Rocoto peppers stuffed with minced beef, olives and hard-boiled egg, gratinated with cheese, alongside a potato gratin. The rocotos are blanched three times in sweetened water: without that, nobody could swallow them.',ARRAY['Cut the tops off the rocotos, remove the seeds and white ribs, which carry most of the heat.','Boil them three times for five minutes in sweetened, vinegared water, changing the water each time.','Fry the onion, garlic, ají panca and cumin, add the minced beef, then the peanuts, raisins and olives.','Stuff the peppers with this mixture and a slice of hard-boiled egg, cap them with cheese and their own lids.','Set them on a gratin of thinly sliced potatoes in milk, and bake for 35 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('locro-argentino','en','Locro','San Miguel de Tucumán, Argentina','A stew of white hominy, squash and several meats, cooked for three hours and served with a chilli oil. It is eaten on 25 May and 9 July, the two patriotic dates: a national-holiday dish.',ARRAY['Soak the hominy and the beans overnight, separately.','Cook them for 1½ hours in four litres of water, without salt, until the grains begin to split.','Add the meats in large pieces and the cubed squash, and simmer for another 1½ hours.','Crush some of the squash against the side of the pot: it is what thickens the stew, never flour.','Make the salsa de freír: soften spring onion in the oil with paprika and chilli, off the heat. Each diner helps themselves.']::text[]),
('humita-en-chala','en','Humita en chala','Salta, Argentina','A paste of freshly grated corn, onion and cheese, cooked in the husks of the cob itself. Far older than the conquest, it is made across the former Inca empire under different names.',ARRAY['Husk the cobs keeping the leaves whole: they will form the parcels. Blanch them for 2 minutes.','Grate the raw kernels on a coarse grater — never in a blender, which makes the paste watery.','Soften the onion and pepper in the oil with the paprika, without colouring them.','Combine the grated corn, vegetables, milk, basil and diced cheese; salt, add a pinch of sugar.','Cross two leaves, spoon on two tablespoons, fold into a parcel, tie with a strip of husk and steam for 40 minutes.']::text[]),
('carbonada-criolla','en','Carbonada criolla','Mendoza, Argentina','A beef stew with dried and fresh fruit, served in a hollowed pumpkin that has baked in the oven with it. The sweet-savoury balance comes from Andalusia, but the pumpkin as vessel is a New World idea.',ARRAY['Cut a lid from the pumpkin, hollow it out and butter the inside; bake it for 25 minutes at 180°C.','Sear the cubed beef, lift it out, then soften the onion and pepper in the same fat.','Add the tomato, stock, bay and the meat, and simmer for 50 minutes.','Add the potatoes, sweet potatoes, corn in lengths and dried fruit for 25 minutes.','Pour the stew into the hot pumpkin, replace the lid and return it to the oven for 20 minutes. Serve scraping flesh from the walls.']::text[]),
('curanto','en','Curanto en hoyo','Castro, Chiloé, Chile','Shellfish, meats and potato cakes cooked in a pit lined with scorching stones and covered with nalca leaves. It is as much a party as a dish: it takes twenty people and half a day.',ARRAY['Dig a pit a metre deep, line it with stones and light a fire on top until they glow red: allow two hours.','Rake out the embers, cover the stones with nalca leaves and lay the shellfish first, as they release the juice.','Add the meats, then the milcaos and chapaleles — cakes of grated potato and flour — then the peas.','Cover with leaves, a wet cloth and earth, letting not the least wisp of smoke escape.','Cook for 2½ hours without opening. The curanto is uncovered in one movement, and the shellfish juice is drunk first.']::text[]),
('charquican','en','Charquicán','Valparaíso, Chile','A thick mash of squash, potato and dried meat, crowned with a fried egg. The name mixes Quechua charqui, dried meat, and Mapudungun can, stew: two languages in one dish.',ARRAY['Soak the dried meat for 4 hours, drain it and chop it finely by knife.','Fry the onion and garlic in the oil, add the paprika, cumin and oregano off the heat.','Add the meat, then the squash and potatoes in large cubes, and add water only halfway up.','Cook covered for 35 minutes, then mash roughly with a fork against the side: neither smooth purée nor whole pieces.','Add corn, peas and green beans for 10 minutes, and serve each plate crowned with a fried egg.']::text[]),
('lechona-tolimense','en','Tolima lechona','Ibagué, Tolima, Colombia','A whole pig emptied, stuffed with yellow peas, rice and its own meat, then baked ten hours in a wood oven. The skin must ring like glass: the only criterion Tolimenses recognise.',ARRAY['Soak the yellow peas overnight, then half-cook them: they will finish swelling inside the pig.','Bone the pig keeping the skin whole, and dice the meat, frying it with onion, garlic and cumin.','Mix meat, peas, raw rice and annatto; season very generously, the stuffing should taste saltier than seems reasonable.','Fill the skin without stretching it, sew it up with a trussing needle and prick the rind all over.','Rub with coarse salt and sour orange juice, and cook for 10 hours at 140°C, then 30 minutes at 220°C to blister the skin.']::text[]),
('sancocho-valluno','en','Sancocho valluno','Cali, Valle del Cauca, Colombia','A hen, green plantain and cassava soup, cooked over a wood fire on the riverbank. On Sundays Cali families go down to the river with the pot: the ritual matters as much as the recipe.',ARRAY['Cook the hen for 1 hour in four litres of water with the garlic, cumin and spring onion.','Break the green plantains by hand into irregular pieces: cut with a knife they blacken the broth.','Add them with the corn in lengths and cook for 25 minutes.','Add the cassava and potatoes for 25 minutes; the cassava should split into fibres.','Cover with coriander off the heat and serve with white rice, avocado and a wedge of lime.']::text[]),
('pandebono','en','Pandebono','Cali, Valle del Cauca, Colombia','Small yeastless rolls of cassava starch and fresh cheese, crisp outside and elastic within. The name is said to come from an Italian baker crying ''pan de bono'', good bread, in the streets of Cali.',ARRAY['Grate the fresh cheese very finely: it, not the flour, gives the dough its structure.','Mix it with the cassava starch, precooked maize flour, sugar and salt.','Add the eggs and butter, and knead by hand into a smooth supple dough, without adding water.','Shape rings or balls, tucking a cube of guava paste inside if you like.','Bake for 18 minutes at 220°C: they should puff and colour. They are eaten warm, never cold.']::text[]),
('pabellon-criollo','en','Pabellón criollo','Caracas, Venezuela','Shredded beef, black beans, white rice and fried plantain, laid out in four separate quarters. It is often read as the country''s three peoples: the white of the rice, the black of the beans, the brown of the meat.',ARRAY['Cook the flank steak for 1½ hours in water with the onion and bay, then let it cool in its broth.','Shred it by hand along the grain — never with a knife, the strands must stay long.','Fry the onion, pepper, garlic and tomato, add the cumin and the meat, and let it stew for 25 minutes with a little broth.','Cook the soaked black beans for 1 hour with a sofrito, finishing with a pinch of sugar: that contrast makes the dish.','Fry very ripe plantains on the bias and plate the four elements side by side, unmixed.']::text[]),
('hallaca','en','Hallaca','Maracaibo, Zulia, Venezuela','Annatto-coloured corn dough filled with a stew of three meats, olives and capers, folded in banana leaf. They are made fifty or a hundred at a time in December, the family working a production line.',ARRAY['The day before, make the guiso: dice the three meats small, fry them with onion, pepper and garlic, add wine and simmer for 1 hour. Let it rest overnight.','Heat the oil with the annatto until it turns bright orange, and strain it.','Knead the precooked maize flour with the guiso broth and the coloured oil, into a supple dough that does not stick.','Pass the banana leaves over a flame, spread a flattened ball of dough, fill with guiso, olives, capers and raisins.','Fold into a rectangle, wrap in a second leaf, tie crosswise and boil for 45 minutes. They keep a week.']::text[]),
('majadito','en','Majadito','Santa Cruz de la Sierra, Bolivia','A rice of the Bolivian lowlands cooked with dried charque beef, coloured with annatto, served with a fried egg and plantain. It is wanted batido, soft, or seco, grain by grain — two camps that will never agree.',ARRAY['Soak the charque for 8 hours, then boil it for 30 minutes and shred it, pounding it lightly in a mortar.','Fry it in the oil until the fibres crisp, then set half aside for serving.','Fry the onion, pepper, garlic, tomato, cumin and annatto for 10 minutes.','Add the rice, let it turn pearly, then add three parts water to one of rice if you want it batido.','Cook covered for 25 minutes. Serve with fried plantain, a fried egg and the reserved crisp meat.']::text[]),
('fanesca','en','Fanesca','Quito, Ecuador','A soup of twelve grains and salt cod, made just one week a year, at Easter. The twelve grains are the apostles and the cod is Christ: the dish is a catechism.',ARRAY['Soak the cod for 24 hours, changing the water four times, then poach it in the milk; keep that fragrant milk.','Cook each pulse separately: they do not share a cooking time, and together some would collapse.','Make a refrito of onion, garlic, cumin and annatto, the base of the whole soup.','Add the mashed pumpkin and courgette, then the cod milk, the cream and the peanut purée.','Bring all the grains together, simmer for 30 minutes without boiling, add the flaked cod and the cheese. Serve with hard-boiled egg, fried plantain and empanadas.']::text[]),
('seco-de-chivo','en','Seco de chivo','Cuenca, Ecuador','A kid stew simmered in beer and naranjilla juice, served with rice and plantain. Seco means ''dry'', though it is saucy: the word means a dish without separate broth, not without liquid.',ARRAY['Marinate the kid for 6 hours in the beer, pounded garlic, cumin and naranjilla juice.','Drain it, pat it dry and sear it over high heat in oil coloured with annatto.','Make a refrito of onion, tomato and pepper, then return the meat with all its marinade.','Simmer covered for 2 hours, adding a little water if the sauce catches.','Blend the coriander with a little sauce and stir it in at the end, with the panela. Serve with rice, fried plantain and avocado.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('vori-vori','en','Vori vori','Luque, Paraguay','A hen soup with dumplings of maize flour and cheese, rolled by hand one at a time. The name comes from Guaraní vorý vorý, ''ball ball'': the doubling says there are many.',ARRAY['Fry the onion, pepper and tomato, add the jointed hen and let it colour.','Add three litres of water, the bay and the turmeric, and simmer for 1 hour.','Mix the maize flour and grated cheese, then add a ladle of hot broth, a spoonful at a time.','Roll small balls the size of a walnut between lightly oiled palms.','Drop them gently into the simmering soup and give them 15 minutes without stirring: they rise when they are done.']::text[]),
('chaja','en','Chajá','Paysandú, Uruguay','A cake of sponge, whipped cream, peaches and crumbled meringue, created in 1927 in Paysandú. The pastry cook Orlando Castellano named it after a marsh bird whose ruffled plumage it resembles.',ARRAY['Whisk four whole eggs with 150 g of sugar over a warm bain-marie to the ribbon, then fold in the sifted flour.','Bake the sponge for 20 minutes at 180°C and let it cool before cutting it into three discs.','Whip the remaining whites with 250 g of sugar into a firm meringue, pipe it in strips and dry it for 90 minutes at 100°C.','Whip the very cold cream with the vanilla, without over-beating: it should stay soft.','Build the cake alternating sponge soaked in peach syrup, cream, dulce de leche and peaches, and cover it entirely in crushed meringue.']::text[]),
('chupin-de-pescado','en','Chupín de pescado','Rosario, Santa Fe, Argentina','A stew of river fish — surubí or dorado from the Paraná — layered with potatoes and peppers. Fishermen cooked it on the bank in a single pot, layering and never stirring.',ARRAY['Cut the fish into thick steaks on the bone: the bone is what gives the broth body.','Line the pot with oil, then a layer of onion, pepper and sliced tomato.','Add a layer of potatoes, then the fish, then the vegetables again, salting each tier.','Pour in the white wine and just enough water to come level, add the bay and paprika.','Cover and cook for 40 minutes without ever stirring: only shake the pot. Parsley off the heat.']::text[]),
('caldo-de-piedra','en','Caldo de piedra','San Felipe Usila, Oaxaca, Mexico','A fish soup cooked in seconds by a white-hot river stone plunged into a gourd. Among the Chinantec only men prepare it, and the gesture is as old as the village.',ARRAY['Heat smooth river stones in a wood fire for two hours, until they glow red.','Cut the fish into large pieces and peel the prawns; on no account cook them in advance.','Divide between gourds the raw fish, prawns, tomato, onion, garlic, chilli and herbs.','Pour in cold salted water to cover, then lower in a scorching stone with wooden tongs.','The water erupts into a boil and cooks everything in thirty seconds. Lift out the stone and serve at once with lime.']::text[]),
('tlayuda','en','Tlayuda','Ocotlán, Oaxaca, Mexico','A large dried tortilla, grilled until it cracks, spread with black bean lard and covered in quesillo. It is folded in two over the coals: eaten flat it is a tlayuda; folded, a late-night clayuda.',ARRAY['Cook the black beans with epazote, then mash them and reduce them in the lard into a thick black paste, the asiento.','Grill the cecina and chorizo over the coals and cut them into pieces.','Lay the large tortilla on the grill and spread it with asiento as it heats.','Add the shredded quesillo and let it melt: the tortilla should crisp without burning, about 4 minutes.','Crown with finely shredded cabbage, meat, tomato, avocado and a pasilla chilli salsa. Fold in two and eat by hand.']::text[]),
('papadzules','en','Papadzules','Mérida, Yucatán, Mexico','Tortillas rolled around hard-boiled egg, cloaked in a green pumpkin-seed sauce with its separated pepita oil. The Maya name is said to mean ''food of the lords''; the dish predates the conquest.',ARRAY['Toast the pumpkin seeds dry without letting them brown, then grind them very finely.','Infuse the epazote in half a litre of hot salted water, and strain.','Work the seed powder with this warm water, kneading by hand: the green oil separates and rises. Spoon it off.','Make a tomato sauce with the onion, garlic and whole habanero, and pass it through a sieve.','Dip each tortilla in the seed paste, roll it around chopped hard-boiled egg, cloak with tomato sauce and drizzle over the reserved green oil.']::text[]),
('chiles-en-nogada','en','Chiles en nogada','Puebla, Mexico','A poblano chilli stuffed with meat and fruit, cloaked in a fresh walnut cream and scattered with pomegranate. Green, white, red: the colours of the flag. It is served only in August and September, when the walnut is milky.',ARRAY['Char the poblanos over a flame until the skin blisters, close them in a bag for 10 minutes, then peel and empty them through a slit.','Make the picadillo: fry onion and garlic, add the meats, the tomato, then the diced fruit, raisins, almonds and cinnamon. Cook down for 30 minutes.','Peel the fresh walnuts one by one, removing the thin bitter skin: it is slow, and it is essential.','Blend them with the fresh cheese, cream and sherry, without sugar: the nogada must stay a frank white.','Stuff the chillies, cloak them in cold nogada and scatter pomegranate seeds and parsley. Never reheat.']::text[]),
('birria-de-res','en','Birria de res','Guadalajara, Jalisco, Mexico','A beef stew with guajillo chillies and cinnamon, whose red broth is used to dip the tacos. Birria once meant ''worthless thing'': the colonists scorned goat, and Mexicans made a masterpiece of it.',ARRAY['Toast the chillies dry for 30 seconds a side — no more, or they turn bitter — and soak them for 30 minutes.','Blend them with the tomato, garlic, spices and vinegar into a smooth sauce, and pass it through a sieve.','Rub it over the meat and marinate for 6 hours.','Cook for 3 hours covered, with water halfway up and the bay, until the meat falls apart.','Shred the meat and skim the broth, keeping the red oil from the top: it is what the filled tortillas are fried in before being dipped in the consommé.']::text[]),
('machaca-con-huevo','en','Machaca con huevo','Monterrey, Nuevo León, Mexico','Sun-dried beef pounded until fluffy, fried with eggs and a pico de gallo. Machacar means ''to pound'': in the northern desert the dried meat was beaten with a stone to tenderise it.',ARRAY['Soak the dried beef in warm water for 15 minutes, squeeze it dry, then pound it in a mortar until it falls into strands.','Toast it dry in a very hot pan to rehydrate it and concentrate its flavour.','Add the oil, onion, garlic and serrano chilli, and soften for 4 minutes.','Add the crushed tomato and let it evaporate completely: moisture would make the eggs runny.','Pour in the beaten eggs and scramble over low heat, barely setting them. Serve in flour tortillas.']::text[]),
('kak-ik','en','Kak’ik','Cobán, Alta Verapaz, Guatemala','A vivid red Q''eqchi'' turkey soup with cobanero chilli and annatto. The name comes from kak, red, and ik, chilli; a Maya ceremonial dish declared Guatemalan national heritage.',ARRAY['Cook the jointed turkey for 1 hour in three litres of water with the onion and garlic; keep the broth.','Char the chillies, tomatoes and tomatillos dry on a griddle, until they blacken in patches.','Blend them with the annatto and a ladle of broth, then pass through a fine sieve.','Cook this purée for 20 minutes over low heat, stirring, then stir it into the broth.','Return the turkey, add the coriander and mint ten minutes before the end, and serve with tamalitos or rice.']::text[]),
('sopa-de-caracol','en','Sopa de caracol','La Ceiba, Garifuna coast, Honduras','A Garifuna conch soup with coconut milk, cassava and green plantain. It travelled the world in 1991 on a Banda Blanca song, and many know the chorus without knowing the dish.',ARRAY['Beat the conch with a mallet to tenderise it, then cut it into strips and rub it with lime.','Fry the onion, garlic and annatto in the oil, and add the cumin.','Add a litre of water with the cassava, the green plantain broken by hand and the carrot.','Cook for 30 minutes, then pour in the coconut milk and let it steam without boiling.','Add the conch for 8 minutes only: overcooked it turns to rubber. Coriander and lime off the heat.']::text[]),
('olla-de-carne','en','Olla de carne','Cartago, Costa Rica','A Costa Rican boiled dinner with seven different roots, cooked for three hours on Sundays. The broth is drunk first from a cup, before the meat and vegetables come to the table.',ARRAY['Cook the shank on the bone for 2 hours in four litres of water, with the onion and garlic, skimming regularly.','The collagen from the bone is essential: without it the broth stays thin and flat.','Add the roots in order of firmness — cassava, yautía, sweet potato — at twenty-minute intervals.','Finish with the squash, chayote, corn and plantain, which need 20 minutes.','Serve the broth in a cup first, then meat and vegetables in a deep plate, with rice and lime.']::text[]),
('tamal-en-cazuela','en','Tamal en cazuela','Havana, Cuba','The Cuban tamal freed from its husk: fresh corn and pork cooked together in a pot, like polenta. It was invented out of frank laziness, and nobody in Havana apologises for it.',ARRAY['Marinate the diced pork for 2 hours in sour orange, garlic, cumin and oregano.','Render the bacon, sear the pork, then lift it out and make the sofrito: onion, pepper, garlic, tomato, 20 minutes.','Grate the fresh corn and blend it with the maize flour and a litre of water; sieve to remove the fibres.','Pour this cream into the sofrito and cook for 45 minutes over low heat, stirring constantly with a wooden spoon.','Return the pork for the last 20 minutes. The cazuela is ready when the spoon stands up in the middle.']::text[]),
('arroz-con-gandules','en','Arroz con gandules','Bayamón, Puerto Rico','Puerto Rico''s national rice, with pigeon peas and sofrito, prized for its caramelised bottom crust, the pegao. Serving rice with no pegao is taken as an admission of clumsiness: it is scraped up and fought over.',ARRAY['Render the salt pork and smoked ham in a cast-iron caldero, until they crisp.','Add the sofrito and annatto, and fry for 5 minutes: the fat should turn orange.','Add the tomato sauce, olives, capers and spices, then the drained pigeon peas.','Pour in the rice and water to one centimetre above it, and never stir after that moment.','Let it evaporate uncovered, then cover for 20 minutes over very low heat. Raise the heat for 2 minutes at the end to form the pegao.']::text[]),
('soup-joumou','en','Soup joumou','Port-au-Prince, Haiti','A pumpkin and beef soup drunk every 1 January. Under the colony it was forbidden to the enslaved; independence in 1804 made it the symbol of freedom regained, and UNESCO listed it in 2021.',ARRAY['Wash the meat with lime, as custom requires, then marinate it for 4 hours in epis.','Cook it for 1 hour with the thyme, cloves and whole chilli; keep the broth.','Boil the pumpkin in pieces until it collapses, then blend it with its water into a smooth cream.','Combine the purée and the broth, add the vegetables in order of firmness and give them 30 minutes.','Add the vermicelli for the last eight minutes. The soup should coat the spoon without being thick.']::text[]),
('sancocho-dominicano','en','Sancocho de siete carnes','Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic','A thick soup of seven meats and seven root vegetables, served on great occasions. Each meat goes in at its own moment; a cook who adds them all at once is shown the door.',ARRAY['Marinate each meat separately in sour orange, garlic and oregano for 2 hours.','Sear them one after another in the pot, starting with the goat and beef, which take longest.','Add five litres of water and simmer for 1 hour before adding pork, chicken and sausage.','Add the roots in order of firmness: yam, yautía, cassava, then plantain and squash.','Crush a little squash and plantain against the side to thicken the soup. Serve with white rice and avocado.']::text[]),
('pelau','en','Pelau','Port of Spain, Trinidad and Tobago','Chicken caramelised in burnt sugar, then cooked with rice, pigeon peas and coconut milk. The sugar must blacken and smoke before the meat goes in: the gesture that defines the whole dish.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken for 4 hours in green seasoning, the Trinidadian herb and garlic paste.','Heat the oil and brown sugar in a heavy pot, without stirring, until the caramel smokes and blackens.','Drop the chicken in at once and stir to coat: it should take on a mahogany colour.','Add onion, carrot, squash, pigeon peas, thyme and ketchup, then the rinsed rice.','Pour in the coconut milk and water to one centimetre above the rice, set the whole chilli on top, cover and cook for 25 minutes.']::text[]),
('hangi','en','Hāngī','Rotorua, New Zealand','Meat and vegetables cooked in an earth oven on white-hot volcanic stones, under wet sacking. In Rotorua some families use geothermal steam directly: there the ground cooks the meal by itself.',ARRAY['Dig a pit a metre square and sixty centimetres deep, and stack volcanic stones on a hardwood fire in it.','Let them heat for three hours: the stones must be grey with heat and no longer black.','Rake out the wood, level the stones, and cover them with green leaves or wet cloths to prevent scorching.','Stack the baskets: meat at the bottom, roots in the middle, greens on top. Throw on a bucket of water to raise the steam.','Cover with wet sacking then earth, letting no steam escape, and leave for 3 hours. The hāngī is unearthed in silence.']::text[]),
('boil-up','en','Boil-up','Wellington, New Zealand','A broth of smoked pork bones, wild pūhā and kūmara, with dumplings called doughboys. It is the everyday Māori dish, made when the pot must feed everyone without warning.',ARRAY['Blanch the smoked bones for 10 minutes and throw away that first, very salty water.','Return them to three litres of clean water with the onion and simmer for 1 hour.','Wash the pūhā several times and rub it between your hands until the milky sap runs: this is what removes the bitterness.','Add the roots in large pieces and cook for 25 minutes.','Make the doughboys from flour, baking powder, butter and milk, drop them on the broth, cover and let them swell for 15 minutes. Add the pūhā last.']::text[]),
('damper-au-feu','en','Damper','Alice Springs, Northern Territory, Australia','An unleavened bread baked under ash, invented by bush drovers who had neither oven nor yeast. It is broken by hand and eaten with golden syrup, never cut with a knife.',ARRAY['Build a hardwood fire and let it fall to thick embers: you need hot ash, not flame.','Mix the flour, baking powder and salt, then rub in the cold butter with your fingertips.','Pour in the milk all at once and bring the dough together in a few movements only: overworked, it bakes hard as a stone.','Shape a flattened round, cut a cross in the top and flour it generously.','Bury it under hot ash for 40 minutes, or bake for 35 minutes at 200°C. The loaf sounds hollow when it is done.']::text[]),
('vakalolo','en','Vakalolo','Nadi, Viti Levu, Fiji','A cake of grated cassava cooked in leaves, coated with a caramel of coconut milk and cane sugar. It is shared at the lovo, the Fijian earth oven, and the first portion goes to the village elders.',ARRAY['Grate the peeled cassava finely and squeeze out some of the juice: too wet, the cake will not set.','Mix it with half the coconut cream, a third of the sugar and a pinch of salt.','Spread the paste 3 cm thick on banana leaves softened over a flame, and fold into a flat parcel.','Cook for 50 minutes by steaming or in the lovo, until the paste turns translucent.','Make a caramel with the remaining sugar, pour in the rest of the coconut cream off the heat and coat the warm cake with it.']::text[]),
('panipopo','en','Panipopo','Apia, Samoa','Yeasted buns baked in a coconut milk sauce that caramelises around them. Pani comes from English ''bun'', popo means coconut: the name alone tells the history of the Pacific.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast and a spoonful of sugar in the warm milk, and let it froth for ten minutes.','Knead with the flour, melted butter, egg, 50 g of sugar and the salt for 10 minutes, then prove for 1 hour.','Shape 60 g balls and set them close together in a buttered dish, without quite touching.','Let them rise for 40 minutes, then pour round the coconut milk whisked with the remaining sugar and the cornstarch.','Bake for 30 minutes at 190°C: the buns swell as they drink the sauce, which caramelises underneath.']::text[]),
('ota-ika','en','ʻOta ʻika','Nukuʻalofa, Tonga','The Tongan cousin of kokoda, drier and more peppery, where the fish steeps only a few minutes. In Tonga it is made on Sunday after church, on the beach, with fish caught the day before.',ARRAY['Cut very fresh tuna into even cubes and keep it on ice until the last moment.','Salt it and pour over the lime juice: leave only ten minutes, the flesh must stay red at the centre.','Drain without pressing and discard the juice.','Add the coconut cream, thinly sliced red onion, tomato, cucumber and chilli.','Season generously with pepper and serve at once, before the salt draws water from the vegetables.']::text[]),
('saksak','en','Saksak','Rabaul, New Britain, Papua New Guinea','A pudding of sago starch and banana cooked in coconut cream, translucent and elastic. Sago comes from the heart of a felled palm: one tree feeds a family for months.',ARRAY['Slake the sago starch in cold water and let it settle, then pour off the clear water on top.','Mash the very ripe bananas with a fork and combine them with the sago paste.','Heat the coconut cream with the sugar and a pinch of salt, without letting it boil.','Pour in the mixture, stirring constantly: the paste turns translucent and starts to stretch within minutes.','Turn it out onto banana leaves, roll into a log, tie it and let it firm for an hour before slicing.']::text[]),
('simboro','en','Simboro','Luganville, Espiritu Santo, Vanuatu','Rolls of grated cassava wrapped in island cabbage leaves and poached in coconut milk. It is the everyday laplap: quicker, without an earth oven, but still grated on stone.',ARRAY['Grate the peeled cassava very finely and squeeze out some of its juice, without drying it out.','Salt the paste and shape finger-sized rolls between wet palms.','Wrap each in two or three island cabbage leaves, pulled tight, and tie with a strip of leaf.','Pack them closely in a pot and cover with coconut milk let down with a little water.','Poach for 50 minutes over low heat without boiling, then finish with coconut cream and spring onion.']::text[]),
('ika-mata-rarotonga','en','Ika mata','Rarotonga, Cook Islands','Raw tuna with coconut milk and lime, and very little else: spring onion, cucumber and salt. Restraint is the rule in the Cooks — you are meant to taste the fish, not the garnish.',ARRAY['Cut the tuna into 1.5 cm dice, discarding sinewy parts.','Toss it with salt and lime juice and leave 15 minutes, no longer.','Drain thoroughly: the fish should stay pink at the centre.','Add the fresh coconut milk, sliced spring onion, diced cucumber and tomato.','Serve immediately, in very cold bowls or half coconut shells.']::text[]),
('poe-banane','en','Banana poʻe','Papeete, Tahiti, French Polynesia','A dense pudding of mashed banana and cassava starch, baked and drowned in fresh coconut milk. It was once cooked underground in the ahimaʻa; the modern oven shortened it without betraying it.',ARRAY['Choose very ripe, almost black bananas: all the sweetness of the dish comes from them.','Mash them with a fork, never in a blender, to keep some texture.','Mix them with the cassava starch, sugar, scraped vanilla and lime zest.','Pour into a buttered dish 4 cm deep and bake for 50 minutes at 180°C: the top should brown and the centre stay wobbly.','Let it cool, cut into squares and serve drowned in fresh coconut milk, never heated.']::text[]),
('ali-nazik','en','Ali Nazik','Gaziantep, Turkey','A purée of smoked aubergine with yogurt and garlic, crowned with knife-chopped lamb. Legend says Sultan Selim, served in Gaziantep, asked whose gentle hand had made it: ''eli nazik''.',ARRAY['Char the whole aubergines directly over a flame until the skin is black and the flesh collapses.','Let them steam in a bag, peel them and drain them for 20 minutes: the bitter water must go.','Mash the flesh with a fork with the yogurt and pounded garlic, and keep it warm over a bain-marie — never direct heat.','Chop the lamb and fat by knife, sear them over high heat without stirring, add the tomato paste and tomato.','Spread the purée in the dish, hollow a well, spoon in the meat and pour over butter melted with Aleppo pepper.']::text[]),
('perde-pilav','en','Perde pilav','Siirt, Turkey','A pilaf of chicken, almonds and currants sealed in a golden dome of pastry. It was served to newlyweds: the curtain of dough stands for the home, the almonds for children to come.',ARRAY['Poach the chicken for 40 minutes; bone it, dice it and keep the stock.','Fry the blanched almonds and pistachios in butter, add the rinsed rice, then the stock, currants and spices. Cook for 15 minutes and let it cool.','Knead the flour, butter, eggs, yogurt and baking powder into a supple dough; rest it for 30 minutes.','Butter a deep mould and line it with dough rolled to 5 mm, leaving the edges hanging.','Fill with the pilaf, well packed, fold the dough over, glaze with egg and bake for 45 minutes at 180°C. Turn it out hot, in one movement.']::text[]),
('manti-kayseri','en','Kayseri mantı','Kayseri, Turkey','Minuscule lamb dumplings served under garlic yogurt and chilli butter. In Kayseri they say a good daughter-in-law fits forty on one spoon: the size is an examination.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, eggs, water and salt for 15 minutes into a very firm dough, and rest it for an hour.','Chop the lamb with the onion and parsley, and season generously.','Roll the dough until you can see the grain of the table through it, then cut 2 cm squares.','Place a speck of filling the size of a rice grain on each, and pinch the four corners together at the centre.','Poach for 8 minutes in salted water, plate under garlic yogurt and pour over butter melted with chilli and dried mint.']::text[]),
('tepsi-baytinijan','en','Tepsi baytinijan','Baghdad, Iraq','A gratin of fried aubergine, meatballs and tomato, layered in a tray. Tepsi means the tray itself: in Baghdad it was sent to the neighbourhood oven, since homes had none.',ARRAY['Cut the aubergines into thick rounds, salt them and let them drain for 30 minutes; pat them thoroughly dry.','Fry them until golden, then the potatoes and peppers in the same oil.','Mix the minced meat, grated onion, parsley and baharat, and shape small flat patties, which you sear.','Layer in a tray: aubergine, patties, potato, pepper, sliced tomato.','Pour over the tomato paste slaked in a glass of water and bake for 45 minutes at 190°C. Serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('kibbeh-bil-sanieh','en','Kibbeh bil sanieh','Damascus, Syria','Two layers of pounded bulgur and lamb, hiding a filling of meat, onion and pine nuts. Diamonds are scored before baking: decorative, but above all it lets you cut portions without breaking the crust.',ARRAY['Rinse the fine bulgur and squeeze it; it should be damp, never sodden.','Pound the trimmed leg with a third of the onion and the spices, then work in the bulgur, adding iced water little by little until the paste is smooth.','Make the filling: softened onion, minced lamb, toasted pine nuts, pomegranate molasses, cinnamon.','Spread half the paste 1 cm thick in a buttered tray, cover with the filling, then the second half, smoothing with a wet hand.','Score diamonds with a blade, pour over melted butter and bake for 40 minutes at 200°C. Serve with minted yogurt.']::text[]),
('maftoul','en','Maftoul','Ramallah, Palestine','A Palestinian couscous hand-rolled into pearls far larger than in the Maghreb, served with chicken and chickpeas. Rolling maftoul is village knowledge: it is done by several people, on a large tray.',ARRAY['Moisten the fine bulgur with salted water and roll it on a tray, dusting with flour, in circular movements.','Continue until the pearls are the size of a peppercorn, then sift to remove loose flour.','Poach the chicken for 50 minutes with the whole spices and onion; keep the broth.','Steam the maftoul for 25 minutes above the broth, fluffing it twice with a fork.','Soften the sliced onions in olive oil, fold them into the maftoul with the chickpeas, and set the chicken on top.']::text[]),
('galayet-bandora','en','Galayet bandora','Amman, Jordan','Tomatoes seared in olive oil with garlic and green chilli, reduced until jammy. It is the September harvest dish: when tomatoes are abundant, they are scorched rather than stewed.',ARRAY['Choose very ripe tomatoes and cut them into large wedges, without peeling or seeding them.','Heat the olive oil until it shimmers, and drop in the crushed whole garlic and split green chillies.','Add the tomatoes all at once and let them sear untouched for 5 minutes: they should blacken in places.','Crush them roughly with a spoon and reduce uncovered for 20 minutes.','Season, add the Aleppo pepper and mint off the heat. It is eaten from the pan, dipping bread.']::text[]),
('sayadieh','en','Sayadieh','Tripoli, Lebanon','Brown rice cooked in a broth of fish and caramelised onions, topped with fried steaks and a tahini sauce. The colour comes from no spice: only from onions taken to a deep brown.',ARRAY['Salt the fish steaks and let them stand for 30 minutes, then pat dry and fry them; set aside.','Fry the sliced onions in the same oil for 30 minutes, to a deep brown: this step, and nothing else, gives the colour.','Take out a third for serving, and blend the rest with a litre of hot water; strain this brown broth.','Cook the rice in it with the cumin, cinnamon and bay, 18 minutes covered.','Whisk the tahini with lemon, garlic and a little water until it whitens. Plate rice, fish, fried onions, almonds and pine nuts, sauce alongside.']::text[]),
('zurbian','en','Zurbian','Aden, Yemen','A lamb rice with yogurt, saffron and potato, cooked in layers and scented with agarwood. It is smoked at the end: a coal set in a small dish in the heart of the rice, under the lid.',ARRAY['Marinate the lamb for 4 hours in the yogurt, garlic, ginger and hawaij.','Fry the sliced onions to a deep brown and set half aside.','Cook the lamb and its marinade for 1 hour with the whole spices, then add the potatoes.','Par-boil the rice for 6 minutes in plenty of salted water and drain it half cooked.','Layer meat, rice, fried onion, saffron and ghee; cover for 30 minutes over low heat, then smoke with a coal for 5 minutes.']::text[]),
('qoozi-bahreini','en','Qoozi','Manama, Bahrain','A whole lamb set on a mountain of rice with dried fruit, cooked four hours at low temperature. It is served at the centre of the table for weddings, and taken apart by hand, without a knife.',ARRAY['Rub the lamb with baharat, salt and melted ghee, and leave it 6 hours in the cold.','Seal it in a covered dish with a little water and cook for 3½ hours at 150°C: the steam does all the work.','Strain the released juices and use them to cook the soaked rice, with the pierced dried limes and whole spices.','Fry the almonds, pine nuts and raisins separately in ghee, and candy the carrots cut into fine julienne.','Uncover the lamb and give it 25 minutes at 220°C to colour. Set it on the rice, covered with the nuts and saffron in rose water.']::text[]),
('mashuai','en','Mashuai','Muscat, Oman','A whole kingfish grilled over coals, served on black-lime rice. The fish is rubbed only with salt and turmeric: in Muscat a good fish is held to need nothing else.',ARRAY['Gut and scale the fish, and score it three times on each side down to the bone.','Rub it with turmeric, coarse salt and pounded garlic, working into the cuts, and leave it 1 hour.','Grill it over moderate coals for 20 minutes a side, basting with lemon ghee.','Cook the rice with the pierced dried limes, cardamom and fried onion, in barely salted water.','Serve the whole fish on the rice, with lemon and coriander. It is opened at the table, by hand.']::text[]),
('balaleet','en','Balaleet','Dubai, United Arab Emirates','Sweet vermicelli with saffron and cardamom, covered with a thin savoury omelette. The simultaneous sweet and salty disconcerts visitors; in the Emirates it has always been the Eid breakfast.',ARRAY['Break the vermicelli by hand into 4 cm lengths and colour them in the ghee, stirring constantly.','They should turn hazelnut, not brown: ten seconds too long and they turn bitter.','Pour in boiling water to cover, barely salt it, and cook for 6 minutes.','Add the sugar, infused saffron, cardamom and rose water, and let it evaporate completely, uncovered.','Make a very thin, barely set omelette, seasoned, and lay it whole over the vermicelli. Pistachios on top.']::text[]),
('khoresh-fesenjan','en','Khoresh fesenjan','Sari, Mazandaran, Iran','A dark brown stew of ground walnuts and pomegranate molasses, sweet-sour and very old. It appears on Assyrian tablets; in Sari it is served with duck rather than chicken.',ARRAY['Grind the walnuts very finely, almost to a paste, then dry-toast them for 10 minutes over low heat, stirring constantly.','Fry the onions golden, add the turmeric, then the chicken, and let it colour.','Add the walnuts and a litre of hot water, and simmer uncovered for 1½ hours, stirring often.','The walnut oil must rise and the stew darken to almost black-brown: it is the only test of doneness.','Add the pomegranate molasses, sugar and saffron, and give it another 30 minutes. Serve over saffron rice with pomegranate seeds.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('dolma-azerbaidjan','en','Three sisters dolma','Baku, Azerbaijan','Three stuffed vegetables served together — tomato, pepper and aubergine — called the three sisters. Azerbaijani dolma is UNESCO-listed; the mint and fennel in the filling set it apart from its neighbours.',ARRAY['Cut the tops off the three vegetables and hollow them carefully, keeping the lids: the walls must stay thick enough to hold.','Salt the inside of the aubergines and let them drain for 20 minutes, then rinse them.','Mix the minced lamb, raw rice, very finely chopped onion, chopped herbs and ground fennel.','Fill without packing — the rice swells — and replace each vegetable''s lid.','Pack them tightly in a pot, add a glass of water and the butter, cover and cook for 50 minutes over low heat. Serve with garlic yogurt.']::text[]),
('harissa-armenienne','en','Armenian harissa','Yerevan, Armenia','A porridge of hulled wheat and chicken beaten until silky, unsalted until the very end. Tradition links it to the siege of Musa Dagh: villagers are said to have pooled the last of their wheat and poultry.',ARRAY['Soak the hulled wheat overnight in cold water.','Poach the chicken for 1 hour in four litres of water, bone it and return the meat to the broth.','Add the drained wheat and cook for 4 hours over very low heat, stirring every half hour so nothing catches.','Beat with a whisk or pestle for the last hour, until wheat and meat become a single cream.','Salt only at the very end — salted too early, harissa never binds. Serve under a pool of cumin-browned butter.']::text[]),
('khinkali','en','Khinkali','Pasanauri, Khevsureti, Georgia','Large meat dumplings whose broth is drunk first, biting into the side. You hold the khinkali by its dough tail, which is never eaten: guests count the tails left on the plate.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, water and salt for 15 minutes into a very firm dough, and rest it for an hour under a cloth.','Mix the minced meats, grated onion, garlic, coriander and spices with 200 ml of iced water: the filling must be frankly liquid, as it becomes the broth.','Roll 12 cm discs, thicker at the centre than at the edges.','Place a large spoonful of filling and pleat the edge round, counting at least nineteen folds, then pinch the tail.','Poach for 12 minutes in barely simmering salted water. Pepper generously, bite the side, drink the juice, then eat — leaving the tail.']::text[]),
('chakapuli','en','Chakapuli','Telavi, Kakheti, Georgia','Milk-fed lamb stewed in white wine with sour green plums and a mountain of tarragon. A strictly seasonal spring dish: without green tkemali and fresh tarragon, it is not made.',ARRAY['Cut the lamb into large pieces on the bone and lay it in a pot, with no fat and no prior browning.','Cover with a thick layer of sliced spring onions and the whole green plums.','Pour in the white wine — it should not cover the meat — put on the lid and cook for 40 minutes over low heat.','Only then add the picked tarragon in very large quantity, with coriander, parsley, garlic and chilli.','Continue for 20 minutes covered: the herbs should wilt without blackening. Serve in the pot, with bread.']::text[]),
('beshbarmak-kazakh','en','Almaty besbarmaq','Almaty, Kazakhstan','Boiled horse and lamb served on wide squares of pasta and moistened with onions in broth. The name means ''five fingers'': it is eaten by hand, and the eldest distributes the pieces by rank.',ARRAY['Simmer the meats for 2½ hours in a large pot, skimming relentlessly.','Knead the flour, eggs, a little broth and salt into a firm dough; rest it for 40 minutes.','Roll it very thin and cut 8 cm diamonds.','Poach them for 5 minutes in the skimmed broth — never in plain water, they would lose all their flavour.','Wilt the onion rings in a ladle of scalding broth. Plate the pasta, sliced meat and onions, and serve the broth separately, in bowls.']::text[]),
('oshi-palav-tadjik','en','Oshi palav','Dushanbe, Tajikistan','Tajik palov, with yellow and red carrots mixed, cooked in a kazan and served on a single platter. UNESCO-listed in 2016: it accompanies every birth and every wedding in the country.',ARRAY['Rinse the rice until the water runs clear and soak it in warm salted water.','Render the mutton fat in the kazan, remove the scratchings, and sear the meat over high heat.','Add the onion, then the yellow and red carrots in thick batons, never grated.','Cover with water, add chickpeas, quince, garlic heads and cumin, and let the zirvak simmer for 40 minutes.','Spread the rice on top without stirring, add boiling water to two fingers, let it evaporate then cover for 25 minutes. Invert onto the platter to serve.']::text[]),
('dograma','en','Dograma','Ashgabat, Turkmenistan','Flatbread, meat and onion crumbled by hand into a scalding lamb broth. Everything must be torn by finger, never cut: the knife is banned, and the blade is said to spoil the taste.',ARRAY['Cook the lamb and fat for 2 hours in three litres of water with the bay, skimming.','Lift out the meat, let it cool, then shred it entirely by hand.','Crumble the churek bread between your fingers, into irregular pieces the size of a fingernail.','Slice the onion very finely, salt it and crush it by hand to soften it.','Combine bread, meat, onion and tomato in a large bowl, pour the scalding broth over and serve at once, before the bread collapses.']::text[]),
('ashak','en','Ashak','Kabul, Afghanistan','Dumplings of wild garlic chives under garlic yogurt and a meat sauce with split peas. The gandana chive grows only in Afghanistan; exiles substitute leek, and always apologise for it.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, water and salt for 12 minutes into a firm dough and rest it for an hour.','Slice the leek and spring onions very finely, salt them and let them drain for 20 minutes, then squeeze them hard: every drop must go.','Roll the dough very thin, cut 8 cm discs, fill them and seal into half-moons, pleating the edge.','Make a sauce with the onion, minced beef, soaked split peas, tomato paste and ground coriander, 40 minutes.','Poach the ashak for 6 minutes, set them on the garlic yogurt, spoon over the sauce and dust with dried mint.']::text[]),
('sajji-baloutche','en','Sajji','Quetta, Balochistan, Pakistan','A whole chicken staked on a wooden skewer around an open fire, salted only, without spices. The Baloch judge doneness by sound: the skin must crack when struck with the back of a knife.',ARRAY['Score the chicken deeply and rub it with coarse salt, lemon and green papaya paste, which tenderises the fibres.','Leave it 4 hours: any longer and the papaya breaks the flesh down.','Stuff it with half-cooked rice, sew the opening shut and stake it on a green wooden skewer.','Plant the skewer at an angle forty centimetres from a wood fire and turn it every ten minutes, for 1½ hours.','Strike the skin: if it sounds hollow and splits, it is ready. Dust with chaat masala and serve with bread and raw onion.']::text[]),
('buuz-mongol-oulan','en','Khuushuur','Ulaanbaatar, Mongolia','Flat mutton pasties fried in fat, eaten by the dozen during Naadam. They are counted: a man who eats fewer than ten over the three days of festivities is thought unserious.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, water and salt into a firm dough and rest it for 40 minutes.','Chop the mutton and its fat by knife, mix with very finely chopped onion, garlic, caraway and a little water: the filling must stay moist.','Roll 12 cm discs, place a spoonful of filling, fold over and seal the edge with a flat pleat.','Flatten each pasty with the palm: this is what distinguishes them from steamed buuz.','Fry them 4 minutes a side in mutton fat at 170°C, until they blister and turn golden.']::text[]),
('welsh-rarebit-nordiste','en','Welsh','Dunkirk, Nord, France','Cheddar melted in brown ale, poured over toast lined with ham and crowned with an egg. Brought by Welsh miners to the north, it became the dish of the Opal Coast taverns.',ARRAY['Dice the cheddar and melt it very gently in the ale, never boiling: too hot and it strings and splits.','Add the mustard and Worcestershire sauce, and season well with pepper.','Toast the bread, line it with ham and set everything in individual dishes.','Cover with the melted cheese and give it 8 minutes under the grill, until the surface blisters and browns.','Set a fried egg on each welsh and serve with chips, as they do in the north.']::text[]),
('ficelle-picarde','en','Ficelle picarde','Amiens, Picardy, France','A pancake rolled around ham and mushrooms, gratinated under a cheese cream. Created in 1950 for a food fair in Amiens, it became the Sunday starter of the whole of Picardy.',ARRAY['Make a smooth pancake batter and rest it for an hour: without resting, the pancakes tear when rolled.','Sweat the sliced mushrooms with the shallot until all their water has gone.','Bind them with a third of the cream and let it cool.','Fill each pancake with a slice of ham and a little duxelles, then roll it tightly.','Pack them in a buttered dish, coat with the remaining cream, scatter gruyère and gratinate for 20 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('flamiche-aux-poireaux','en','Leek flamiche','Dinant, Ardennes, France','A closed pie of leeks softened in cream, without a trace of bacon. The northern leek is cooked here almost to a jam: that long melting is what matters, not the pastry.',ARRAY['Rub the flour and 175 g of butter together, bind with a yolk and iced water, and rest for an hour in the cold.','Slice the leek whites very finely and melt them for 40 minutes, covered, in the remaining butter, without colouring.','They should lose three quarters of their volume and turn translucent: only then salt them.','Add the cream and two beaten eggs off the heat, and grate over the nutmeg.','Line a tin with pastry, fill it, cover with a second disc, seal the edges and bake for 45 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('caudiere-berckoise','en','Berck caudière','Berck-sur-Mer, Pas-de-Calais, France','The bouillabaisse of the north: Channel fish, potatoes and cream, without saffron or tomato. The name comes from the cauldron, the caudière, that Berck fishermen set on the sand when the tide returned.',ARRAY['Open the mussels in white wine, shell them and strain their liquor carefully through a cloth.','Soften the onion and leek in butter without colouring, then add the potatoes in thick slices.','Moisten with the mussel liquor let down with water, add the thyme and bay, and cook for 20 minutes.','Cut the heat and slide in the fish in large pieces: they poach in the residual warmth, 8 minutes.','Add the cream and the mussels, warm through without boiling, and cover with parsley. Serve with toast.']::text[]),
('salade-au-lard-vosgienne','en','Vosges warm bacon salad','Gérardmer, Vosges, France','Dandelion or lamb''s lettuce dressed with hot bacon and its deglazing vinegar, with warm potatoes and a soft-boiled egg. The scorching pan is poured over the leaves: they should hiss.',ARRAY['Wash the dandelion in several changes of water and dry it thoroughly: wet leaves refuse a hot dressing.','Boil the potatoes in their skins and peel them while warm.','Fry the bacon dry until crisp, and add the shallot off the heat.','Deglaze with vinegar — the pan should smoke — and pour it over the leaves at once, tossing.','Add the sliced potatoes, croutons, diced Munster and one soft-boiled egg per plate.']::text[]),
('pate-lorrain','en','Pâté lorrain','Baccarat, Lorraine, France','A puff pastry case filled with pork and veal marinated in vin gris from Toul. The marinade takes twenty-four hours: without it the filling stays flat, and Lorraine will not recognise it.',ARRAY['Cut the meats into 1 cm dice by knife: minced, they would make a dense filling.','Marinate them for 24 hours in the cold with the white wine, shallot, garlic, herbs, salt and plenty of pepper.','Drain them thoroughly and discard the marinade: kept, it would sog the pastry.','Roll the pastry into two rectangles, fill the first leaving a two-centimetre border, and cover with the second.','Seal, crimp the edges, glaze with egg, cut a steam hole and bake for 45 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('poulet-au-vinaigre','en','Chicken in vinegar','Lyon, France','Chicken deglazed with wine vinegar, finished with cream and tomato. Paul Bocuse made it a bouchon dish: the sharpness should stay frank, and is not meant to be softened.',ARRAY['Joint the chicken into eight and colour it in butter on all sides, without hurrying.','Add the unpeeled garlic and the shallot, cover and sweat for 10 minutes.','Pour in the vinegar and reduce it by half uncovered: this is where the harshness goes and the flavour stays.','Add the wine, tomato, tomato paste and stock, and simmer covered for 30 minutes.','Lift out the chicken, finish the sauce with cream and cold butter, and add the tarragon off the heat.']::text[]),
('gratin-de-cardons','en','Cardoons with marrow','Lyon, France','Cardoon ribs long-blanched and gratinated under a beef marrow sauce. It is the Christmas vegetable in Lyon: found only in December, and it takes three hours to tame.',ARRAY['String the cardoon ribs like celery, removing every fibre, and drop them straight into acidulated water: in the air they blacken within a minute.','Cook them for 1½ hours in a blanc — water, slaked flour and lemon — which keeps them pale.','Soak the marrow for 2 hours in iced water, then poach it for 10 minutes and cut it into rounds.','Make a roux, moisten with beef stock, cook for 15 minutes and add cream.','Lay the drained cardoons in a dish, arrange the marrow, coat with sauce, scatter gruyère and gratinate for 30 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('poulet-gaston-gerard','en','Poulet Gaston Gérard','Dijon, Burgundy, France','Chicken with cream, mustard and comté, gratinated with breadcrumbs. Born of an accident in 1930: the Dijon mayor''s wife spilt paprika into the pan and rescued it with cheese in front of Curnonsky.',ARRAY['Joint the chicken and colour it in butter, skin down first, in an ovenproof pot.','Dust with paprika, cover and cook for 30 minutes over low heat.','Lift out the chicken, deglaze with white wine and reduce by half.','Add the cream and mustard off the heat — boiled, mustard turns bitter — then half the comté.','Return the chicken, coat it, scatter the remaining cheese and the breadcrumbs, and gratinate for 15 minutes at 220°C.']::text[]),
('gougeres-au-jambon-persille','en','Burgundian rigodon','Auxerre, Burgundy, France','A pudding of stale brioche, milk and walnuts, baked in the bread oven after the batch. It is made savoury with bacon or sweet depending on the village: Burgundy never agreed on it.',ARRAY['Cut the stale brioche into large cubes and pack them into a buttered dish.','Bring the milk to a bare simmer with the split vanilla and cinnamon, and infuse off the heat for 15 minutes.','Beat the eggs and sugar, pour in the hot milk in a thin stream while whisking, without scrambling them.','Pour over the brioche and let it drink for 30 minutes: it must be thoroughly soaked before it goes in the oven.','Scatter crushed walnuts and bake for 50 minutes at 170°C in a water bath. Serve warm with redcurrant jelly.']::text[]),
('saupiquet-morvandiau','en','Morvan saupiquet','Château-Chinon, Morvan, France','Slices of Morvan ham under a sauce of cream, juniper and vinegar. Saupiquet comes from ''salt that stings'': the medieval sauce was sharp, and it has stayed so.',ARRAY['Sweat the chopped shallots in butter without colouring, with the crushed juniper berries.','Add the vinegar and reduce it almost to nothing: the smell should stop catching the nose.','Add the wine, reduce by half, then dust in a spoonful of flour and moisten with the stock.','Cook gently for 15 minutes, strain through a fine sieve pressing well, and finish with cream.','Warm the ham slices through in the sauce without letting them boil, and serve with steamed potatoes.']::text[]),
('pounti-auvergnat','en','Pounti','Aurillac, Cantal, France','A meat loaf with chard and prunes, halfway between savoury and sweet. Auvergne serves it warm as a starter or cold in slices: the harvest dish, always.',ARRAY['Soak the prunes in warm tea while you prepare everything else.','Roughly chop the chard, onion, parsley, bacon and pork — by knife, to keep some texture.','Make a smooth batter with the flour, eggs and milk, then fold in the chopped mixture.','Pour half into a buttered tin, arrange the stoned prunes, and cover with the rest.','Bake for 55 minutes at 180°C. The pounti turns out warm and slices more cleanly cold.']::text[]),
('chou-farci-auvergnat','en','Stuffed cabbage','Clermont-Ferrand, Auvergne, France','A whole cabbage rebuilt leaf by leaf around a stuffing, tied and braised for three hours. It is not chopped: it is reassembled, leaf after leaf, until the original cabbage takes shape again.',ARRAY['Blanch the whole cabbage for 10 minutes, refresh it and peel the leaves off one by one, keeping them in order.','Pare away the thickest ribs with a knife, without piercing the leaf.','Mix the pork, bacon, squeezed soaked bread, softened onion, garlic, eggs and parsley.','In a cloth, rebuild the cabbage: the largest leaves at the bottom, then a layer of stuffing, leaves, stuffing, up to the heart.','Tie the cloth, set the cabbage on a bed of carrots, add wine and stock, and braise for 2½ hours at 160°C.']::text[]),
('sanciau-bourbonnais','en','Sanciau','Moulins, Bourbonnais, France','A thick pancake of wheat and buckwheat flour, cooked in lard, served savoury or dusted with sugar. It was supper in poor Bourbonnais: flour, an egg, and whatever milk was left.',ARRAY['Mix the two flours and the salt, make a well, break in the eggs and slacken with milk, lump-free.','Rest for an hour: the batter should stay thick, closer to blini batter than to crêpe batter.','Melt a good spoonful of lard in a cast-iron pan, until it just smokes.','Pour in a thick ladleful and let it set for 6 minutes untouched: the sanciau is flipped in one movement.','Serve it savoury with fried bacon, or sweet with pan-fried apples.']::text[]),
('clafoutis-limousin','en','Clafoutis','Limoges, Limousin, France','A baked black cherry custard whose fruit is emphatically not stoned. The stone perfumes the custard with almond as it bakes: removing it, in Limousin, is making something else.',ARRAY['Wash and stem the cherries without stoning them, and macerate them for 30 minutes in sugar and brandy.','Butter an earthenware dish generously and sugar it: the caramelised rim depends on it.','Whisk the eggs and sugar, add the flour, then the milk and cream in a thin stream, and the scraped vanilla.','Pack the cherries tightly over the base and pour the batter over them.','Bake for 40 minutes at 180°C. Serve warm, never cold, and warn your guests about the stones.']::text[]),
('poulet-basquaise','en','Basque chicken','Bayonne, Basque Country, France','Chicken stewed in a piperade of sweet peppers, tomatoes and Espelette chilli. The peppers must melt for an hour before the bird goes in: they are the dish, the meat is merely invited.',ARRAY['Colour the jointed chicken in olive oil, then set it aside.','Soften the onion, peppers in strips and garlic in the same oil, covered, for 40 minutes.','Add the peeled crushed tomatoes and reduce for another 20 minutes: the piperade should thicken, not swim.','Deglaze with white wine, add the Espelette chilli and the diced Bayonne ham.','Return the chicken, cover and simmer for 35 minutes. Serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('chipirons-a-l-encre','en','Squid in their ink','Saint-Jean-de-Luz, Basque Country, France','Small squid stewed in a black sauce made from their own ink. They are stuffed with their chopped tentacles: nothing is wasted, and the ink colour frightens as much as it draws.',ARRAY['Clean the squid keeping the ink sacs intact in a bowl: burst too early, they are lost.','Chop the tentacles with a little garlic and parsley, and stuff the bodies with them, closed with a cocktail stick.','Soften the onion, pepper and garlic at length in the oil — 40 minutes covered — until jammy.','Add the tomato, wine, breadcrumb and the ink slaked in a little water, then blend and pass through a sieve.','Poach the squid for 25 minutes in this black sauce, over the gentlest heat: boiled, they turn to rubber.']::text[]),
('aligot-saucisse-de-toulouse','en','Toulouse sausage with beans','Toulouse, Occitanie, France','The coiled sausage, pricked and grilled slowly, on a bed of beans in goose fat. It is never cut before cooking: it is seared whole, coiled on itself, and shared at the table.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then cook them for 1 hour with the carrot, clove-studded onion and bay.','Prick the sausage with a needle in a dozen places: without this it bursts and loses all its juice.','Cook it in goose fat for 25 minutes over very low heat, turning it halfway with two spatulas.','Fry the garlic and tomato in the rendered fat, add the drained beans and stew for 15 minutes.','Serve the whole coil set on the beans, with parsley, and cut it in front of your guests.']::text[]),
('brouillade-aux-truffes','en','Truffle brouillade','Carpentras, Vaucluse, France','Eggs scrambled over a water bath, perfumed by a black truffle shut in with them the day before. The shell is porous: the egg takes the scent without being cracked, and that is the real secret.',ARRAY['The day before, shut the brushed truffle and the whole eggs in an airtight box in the cold.','On the day, beat the eggs barely, without aerating them: a brouillade is made with a fork, not a whisk.','Cook them over a simmering water bath, stirring constantly with a spatula, for a full 15 minutes.','Take them off the heat while still too runny: residual warmth finishes the job.','Fold in the cold butter, cream and grated truffle off the heat, and serve at once on toast.']::text[]),
('grand-aioli','en','Grand aïoli','Aix-en-Provence, Provence, France','Poached salt cod and every vegetable from the garden around a mortar of aïoli. It is the Provençal Friday meal, and the whole dish takes its name from the sauce: without it, it is just a vegetable platter.',ARRAY['Soak the salt cod for 24 hours in cold water, changing the water five times.','Pound the degermed garlic with a pinch of coarse salt into a perfectly smooth cream: the mortar is not negotiable.','Add the yolks, then the olive oil drop by drop at first, always turning the same way.','If the sauce splits, start again with a fresh yolk and beat the failed sauce into it a spoonful at a time.','Poach the cod for 10 minutes without boiling, cook each vegetable separately, and arrange everything around the mortar.']::text[]),
('boles-de-picolat','en','Boles de picolat','Perpignan, Roussillon, France','Pork and beef meatballs stewed with green olives and ceps, Catalan style. The Sunday dish of Roussillon; reheated on Monday, and everyone agrees it is better then.',ARRAY['Soak the breadcrumb in milk, squeeze it, and mix it with the meats, eggs, garlic and parsley. Rest for an hour in the cold.','Roll walnut-sized balls, flour them and brown them in lard, without stirring too soon.','Soften the onions in the same fat, add the tomato and the white wine.','Add the soaked ceps and their strained liquor, then return the meatballs.','Simmer for 45 minutes, adding the desalted olives for the last 15.']::text[]),
('anchoiade-collioure','en','Collioure anchoïade','Collioure, Roussillon, France','A paste of salted anchovies, garlic and olive oil, served warm with raw vegetables. Collioure has salted anchovies since the Middle Ages; they are rinsed but never fully desalted.',ARRAY['Rinse the salted anchovies in clean water and pat them dry, but do not soak them: they would lose their character.','Pound them in a mortar with the degermed garlic and blanched almonds, into a coarse paste.','Work in the olive oil in a thin stream, as for a mayonnaise, then the vinegar and picked thyme.','Warm the sauce over a water bath without ever letting it tremble: heated, it splits.','Serve in the mortar, with raw celery, radishes, fennel and thick slices of country bread.']::text[]),
('tourte-aux-herbes-nicoise','en','Niçois squash tart','Nice, Côte d’Azur, France','A tart of long Nice squash, rice and parmesan, glazed with olive oil. It is eaten cold, cut into squares, and travels well: it was the dockworkers'' lunch.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, olive oil, warm water and salt into a supple dough; rest it for an hour.','Coarsely grate the squash, salt it and let it drain for 30 minutes, then squeeze it hard between your hands.','Soften the onion in oil, cook the rice halfway, and let both cool.','Mix the squeezed squash, rice, onion, parmesan, eggs, chopped basil and nutmeg.','Line a tin with thin pastry, fill it, cover with a second disc, oil the top and bake for 45 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('sardines-farcies-provencales','en','Stuffed sardines','Marseille, Provence, France','Sardines opened flat, filled with spinach and parmesan, packed tightly in a dish. They are opened with the thumb, not a knife: the fish parts along the bone by itself if you do it right.',ARRAY['Scale the sardines, open them from the belly with your thumb and pull the backbone out towards the tail.','Wilt the spinach, squeeze it very dry and chop it.','Mix it with the squeezed soaked bread, parmesan, eggs, garlic and parsley.','Fill half the sardines, cover each with a second one flesh to flesh, and pack them tightly.','Drizzle with olive oil, scatter breadcrumbs and bake for 18 minutes at 200°C. Serve warm with lemon.']::text[]),
('omelette-de-la-mere-poulard','en','Mère Poulard omelette','Mont-Saint-Michel, Normandy, France','An omelette beaten for twenty minutes and cooked over a wood fire in a long-handled pan. Annette Poulard fed pilgrims this way from 1888; she always refused to give the recipe, saying there wasn''t one.',ARRAY['Separate the whites from the yolks. Beat the yolks with the cream, salt and pepper.','Whisk the whites to very stiff peaks, at length — that is the secret, and there is no other.','Fold the yolks gently into the whites, lifting the mass, never stirring.','Foam a large knob of butter in a scorching pan and pour in the mixture all at once.','Let it puff for 5 minutes untouched, fold it in two and serve immediately: it collapses within a minute.']::text[]),
('douillons-normands','en','Pear douillons','Honfleur, Normandy, France','Whole pears sealed in shortcrust and baked, the stalk left poking out. In Normandy they are made with apple, as bourdelots; with pear they keep an elegance the apple lacks.',ARRAY['Rub the flour and cold butter together, bind with a yolk and iced water, and rest for an hour in the cold.','Peel the pears keeping the stalks, and core them from below with a corer.','Fill the hollow with a mixture of butter, sugar, cinnamon and a drop of calvados.','Roll out the pastry, cut discs, and wrap each pear by drawing the pastry up, leaving the stalk out.','Glaze with egg and bake for 40 minutes at 190°C. Serve warm with cream and redcurrant jelly.']::text[]),
('lentilles-au-petit-sale','en','Green lentils with salt pork','Le Puy-en-Velay, Haute-Loire, France','Green Puy lentils and salted pork ribs, simmered together and never boiled. Puy lentils are not soaked: the skin is so fine it would burst, and they must stay whole.',ARRAY['Soak the salt pork for 4 hours in cold water, then blanch it for 10 minutes and discard that water.','Rinse the lentils without soaking and put them in cold unsalted water: salted in advance, they stay hard.','Add the clove-studded onion, carrot, garlic, thyme and bay, and bring to a bare simmer.','Add the meat and cook for 45 minutes without ever boiling: a rolling boil would split the skins.','Add the sausage for the last 20 minutes. Salt at the end, add butter and serve with mustard.']::text[]),
('kouign-amann-malouin','en','Saint-Malo kouign-amann','Saint-Malo, Ille-et-Vilaine, France','The individual version of the Douarnenez cake, baked in a mould rather than as one large round. Saint-Malo adopted it late, in the 20th century, and cut it to fit the hand: the edges caramelise more, which is the whole point.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, yeast slaked in warm water and salt, with no butter at all, and prove for 1½ hours.','Roll the dough into a rectangle, lay the very cold salted butter as a slab in the centre, and enclose it like a letter.','Give three single turns, sugaring generously between each, with 20 minutes'' rest in the cold: the sugar must stay in layers, never dissolve into the dough.','Cut 10 cm squares, fold the four corners to the centre and set them in buttered, sugared muffin moulds.','Prove for 45 minutes, scatter pearl sugar, and bake for 35 minutes at 200°C. Turn out while scalding, or the caramel sets and the cake stays in the tin.']::text[]),
('moules-a-la-malouine','en','Saint-Malo mussels','Saint-Malo, Ille-et-Vilaine, France','Bouchot mussels opened in dry cider and finished with cream, with a hint of curry. The curry came from Saint-Malo shipowners, who brought back from the Indies what Brittany lacked: it barely registers, and is missed the moment it is left out.',ARRAY['Scrub the mussels and pull the beard towards the hinge; discard any that stay open after a sharp tap.','Soften the chopped shallots in salted butter, without colouring, with the thyme and bay.','Add the curry off the heat — thirty seconds too long and it turns powdery — then pour in the cider and bring to the boil.','Tip in the mussels all at once, cover, and shake the pot twice: they open within 4 minutes, no longer.','Lift them out, reduce the liquor by half, add the cream off the heat, and pour it back over. Never salt: the sea has seen to that.']::text[]),
('madeleines-au-beurre-bordier','en','Madeleines with Bordier butter','Saint-Malo, Ille-et-Vilaine, France','Madeleines made with churned Saint-Malo butter, whose hump depends on a shock of temperature. Jean-Yves Bordier has churned by the old method since 1985 in rue de l''Orme: his butter, worked with a wooden roller, stands comparison with any.',ARRAY['Melt the butter until it foams and smells of hazelnut, then let it cool: that is where the flavour comes from.','Whisk the eggs, sugar and honey to the ribbon, then fold in the flour sifted with the baking powder.','Add the warm browned butter and the zest, cover with cling film touching the surface and rest 12 hours in the fridge.','That rest is not a precaution: it creates the hump, by holding the rise back until the oven.','Fill very cold buttered moulds three-quarters full and bake 4 minutes at 240°C, then 8 minutes at 180°C.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('craquelins-malouins','en','Saint-Malo craquelins','Saint-Servan, Saint-Malo, France','A ball of dough poached in boiling water before baking, which dries out hollow. Saint-Malo sailors took it to sea: with no yeast and no fat, it keeps for months and does not mould.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, eggs, milk, sugar and salt for 15 minutes: the dough should be very firm, almost hard to work.','Rest it for an hour under a cloth, then divide it into smooth 40 g balls.','Poach them in small batches in barely simmering water: they sink, then rise after about 3 minutes.','Drain them and let them dry for 30 minutes on a cloth, without stacking: that drying is what makes them hollow.','Bake for 25 minutes at 180°C, until pale gold and hollow-sounding. They keep for weeks in a tin.']::text[]),
('huitres-de-cancale-gratinees','en','Grilled Cancale oysters','Cancale, Ille-et-Vilaine, France','Cancale flat oysters given a few minutes under the grill, under a cider and shallot butter. The Cancale beds supplied Louis XIV''s table, the oysters carried to Versailles by relays of horses.',ARRAY['Open the oysters with a flat knife and discard the first liquor: they make a second, finer one within minutes.','Free the oyster from its muscle so nobody has to do it at the table.','Reduce the chopped shallot in the cider until almost no liquid remains, then let it cool.','Work this into the softened salted butter with the parsley, and set a hazelnut of it on each oyster.','Steady the shells in a bed of coarse salt, scatter breadcrumbs, and give them 3 minutes under a fierce grill: the edge should barely curl. Longer and the oyster shrivels to rubber.']::text[]),
('ravioles-au-saint-marcellin','en','Saint-Marcellin ravioles','Saint-Marcellin, Isère, France','Tiny squares of pasta, sixteen to a sheet, filled with the town''s own cheese rather than Comté alone. The word raviole appears in Dauphiné as early as 1228; the cheese gave the town its name — or the reverse, the argument is unsettled.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, eggs, water and salt for 10 minutes, then rest the dough for an hour in the cold under a cloth.','Choose Saint-Marcellin at their peak, creamy under the rind: too young they stay chalky, too ripe they run and soak the filling.','Mash them with a fork together with the grated Comté, well-drained fromage blanc, crushed garlic, herbs and nutmeg.','Roll the dough to 0.6 mm — you should be able to read through it — set hazelnuts of filling every 3 cm, cover with a second sheet and seal with your fingertips before cutting with a wheel.','Poach for 1 minute in barely trembling water, never at a boil: they rise and it is done. Serve them coated in cream reduced with butter, or given 2 minutes under the grill.']::text[]),
('galette-saucisse','en','Galette-saucisse','Rennes, Brittany, France','A grilled pork sausage rolled in a cold buckwheat pancake, eaten standing at the Lices market and in the stands of the Roazhon Park. No butter, no mustard, no ketchup: in Rennes the question was settled long ago and is reopened every Saturday.',ARRAY['Mix the buckwheat flour, coarse salt and a third of the water, and work the batter by hand until it ribbons and bubbles.','Loosen it with the rest of the water and the egg, then rest it at least 3 hours — overnight is better: the buckwheat must hydrate fully.','Cook the pancakes on a very hot, barely greased griddle, 90 seconds on one side, 30 on the other, and stack them.','Grill the sausages slowly, without pricking them, until the skin is brown and crackling.','Set the sausage on a cold pancake and roll it tight. Hot on cold: the contrast is the whole point, and a reheated pancake cracks.']::text[]),
('andouille-de-guemene','en','Guémené andouille','Guémené-sur-Scorff, Brittany, France','Pork chitterlings drawn one inside another then smoked over beech, which show perfect concentric rings when sliced. That Russian-doll assembly, not any mincing, is what draws the pattern: a badly threaded andouille shows at the first cut.',ARRAY['Clean and scrape the chitterlings in vinegared water, then dry-salt them for 24 hours with the thyme and bay.','Thread them smallest inside largest, like Russian dolls, pulling but never forcing: a torn casing ruins the pattern.','Truss the roll with string in a tight spiral and let it dry overnight in the cold.','Cold-smoke it over beech, 6 hours a day for several weeks for a keeping andouille — 12 hours is enough for one to be eaten soon.','Poach it for 3 hours in a cider and onion broth, never boiling. Serve warm, in thick slices, with buttered potatoes.']::text[]),
('moules-a-la-normande','en','Normandy mussels','Trouville-sur-Mer, Normandy, France','Marinière enriched with dry cider and Isigny cream, born on the Côte Fleurie when restaurateurs were trying to keep holidaying Parisians. Cider replaces white wine and leaves an edge of acidity that the cream rounds without erasing.',ARRAY['Scrub and beard the mussels in cold water and discard without hesitation any that stay open under finger pressure.','Sweat the chopped shallot in butter without colour, pour in the cider and let it bubble for 2 minutes to drive off the harshness.','Tip in the mussels, cover and shake the pot twice: they are done the moment they open, 3 to 4 minutes, no more.','Lift them out and keep them warm; strain the liquor, thicken it with beurre manié and add the cream off the heat.','Salt only at the end, and often not at all: the mussel liquor has already given everything. Coat and serve with chips.']::text[]),
('tarte-au-maroilles','en','Maroilles tart','Maroilles, Hauts-de-France, France','A risen-dough tart covered with melted Maroilles, served warm with a bière de garde. The cheese was born at Maroilles abbey in the 10th century; its formidable smell vanishes in the oven and leaves only hazelnut and cooked milk.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, the yeast dissolved in warm milk, one egg, the softened butter and salt for 10 minutes.','Prove for 1½ hours covered, then spread the dough in a deep tin and let it rise another 30 minutes.','Cut away the orange rind of the Maroilles and slice it thickly: it is the rind, not the paste, that carries the smell.','Beat the remaining eggs with the cream, pepper generously, and do not salt — the cheese sees to that.','Lay the slices on the dough, pour over the custard and bake for 35 minutes at 200°C. Let it settle for 15 minutes before cutting, or the tart runs.']::text[]),
('croque-monsieur','en','Croque-monsieur','Paris, France','It appeared around 1910 in a café on the rue des Capucines, and Proust mentions it as early as Within a Budding Grove. The name comes from the crunch; crowned with a fried egg it becomes a madame — the hat comparison is the explanation most often offered.',ARRAY['Make a tight béchamel: pale roux, hot milk in three additions, 8 minutes at a gentle boil, nutmeg and pepper.','It must coat the spoon and hold: too thin and it soaks the bread, and the croque collapses.','Butter the outside of the slices — never the inside, the béchamel handles that — and spread sauce on the inside.','Fill with ham and half the gruyère, close, then cover the top with the remaining béchamel and cheese.','Bake for 12 minutes at 200°C, high in the oven, until the top blisters and browns in patches.']::text[]),
('jambon-beurre','en','Jambon-beurre','Paris, France','The Parisian sandwich: half a baguette, butter, cooked ham, nothing else. There is nothing to hide behind — quality rests entirely on the three products, and the butter must be at room temperature, never straight from the fridge.',ARRAY['Choose a traditional baguette baked the same day, still ringing under the finger: a soft baguette makes a soft sandwich.','Take the butter out an hour ahead: cold, it tears instead of spreading, and tears the crumb with it.','Split the baguette without cutting through, and butter both faces generously, right to the ends.','Fold the ham in accordion pleats rather than laying it flat: you want thickness and air, not a flattened sheet.','Add a few gherkins, a touch of fleur de sel on the butter, and eat within the hour.']::text[]),
('kouglof-alsacien','en','Kouglof','Ribeauvillé, Alsace, France','A raisin brioche baked in a glazed earthenware mould with twisted flutes, served on Sunday mornings with a glass of white wine. The mould is not decorative: its porous clay draws off moisture and makes the thin crust a metal tin never gives.',ARRAY['Plump the raisins in warm kirsch for at least 2 hours, and drain them thoroughly.','Knead the flour, the yeast dissolved in milk, the eggs, sugar and salt for 15 minutes, then work in the soft butter piece by piece.','The dough must leave the bowl and slap: it takes a while, and it is what makes the stringy crumb.','Prove for 1½ hours, fold in the raisins by hand, and butter the mould generously before laying a whole almond in the base of each flute.','Fill the mould two-thirds full, prove for an hour, and bake for 45 minutes at 180°C. Turn out hot and dust with icing sugar once cold.']::text[]),
('andouillette-de-troyes','en','Troyes andouillette','Troyes, Champagne, France','An andouillette of chitterlings cut into lengthwise strips, which gives it a fibrous grain rather than a smooth charcuterie paste. Legend has it that in 1560 royal troops broke off sacking Troyes to eat them, which gave the townspeople time to flee.',ARRAY['Prick the skin just three times with a needle: too many cuts and the andouillette empties as it cooks.','Brown it in a pan in butter over medium heat for 12 minutes, turning often — fierce heat would burst the skin before the centre is hot.','Set it aside in the warm and sweat the shallot in the same pan, without burning it.','Deglaze with white wine, reduce by half, add the cream then the mustard off the heat: boiled, mustard turns bitter.','Coat and serve with steamed potatoes. A cold Chablis, and nothing else.']::text[]),
('escargots-de-bourgogne','en','Burgundy snails','Dijon, Burgundy, France','Snails returned to their shells under a butter of garlic, parsley and shallot, given a blast of hot oven. The butter is the dish: the snail itself is nearly neutral, and one mops the shell with bread long after emptying it.',ARRAY['If starting from live snails, fast them for a week, purge them in coarse salt, then poach them for 2 hours in court-bouillon.','Chop the garlic, shallot and parsley very finely, then work them into softened butter with salt and plenty of pepper.','The butter must be green and even: chop by hand rather than in a blender, which turns the parsley bitter and the garlic acrid.','Slip a hazelnut of butter into each shell, the snail on top, and plug it with a generous amount more.','Wedge the shells opening upward, pour a splash of white wine into the dish, and bake for 10 minutes at 220°C: the butter should sizzle without darkening.']::text[]),
('praluline','en','Praluline','Roanne, Loire, France','A brioche studded with crushed pink pralines, created in Roanne in 1955 and trademarked since. The pralines partly melt in the oven and stain the crumb with raspberry streaks; crushed too fine they vanish, too coarse they pierce the dough.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, yeast, eggs, sugar, milk and salt for 15 minutes, until the dough slaps against the bowl.','Add the cold butter in pieces, one at a time, and knead 10 minutes more: the dough must come back smooth and glossy.','Prove for 2 hours, knock back, then chill overnight — warm brioche dough is impossible to handle.','Crush the pralines with a rolling pin to pea-sized pieces, and fold them in by hand, in only a few turns.','Shape a crown, prove for 2 hours, and bake for 35 minutes at 165°C. Any hotter and the praline sugar would burn before the brioche was done.']::text[]),
('fondue-savoyarde','en','Savoyard fondue','Chambéry, Savoy, France','Three Savoy cheeses — Beaufort, Comté and Savoy Emmental — melted in white Apremont wine. Unlike the Fribourg version, it mixes cooked pressed cheeses, which need more wine and more patience if they are not to string.',ARRAY['Rub the pot with a cut garlic clove, then discard it — raw garlic in cheese dominates everything.','Grate the three cheeses rather than dicing them: they melt evenly and do not form lumps.','Pour in the wine, heat it without boiling, and add the cheese by handfuls, stirring in figures of eight.','Add the next handful only when the previous one has disappeared: it is the only way to stop the fondue splitting.','Bind with a spoonful of cornstarch slaked in kirsch, add pepper and nutmeg, and keep it over a very low burner. The bread must be a day old, or it falls off the fork.']::text[]),
('pate-de-pommes-de-terre','en','Potato pie','Guéret, Limousin, France','A puff-pastry pie filled with raw sliced potatoes, into which cream is poured through a chimney as it leaves the oven. The dish of Creuse farms, where potato stood in for the meat there was none of.',ARRAY['Slice the potatoes 2 mm thick and on no account rinse them: their starch is what will bind the filling.','Mix them with the sliced onion, garlic, parsley, salt and plenty of pepper, with a few knobs of butter.','Line a base with pastry, cover with a second disc, seal with a fork and glaze with egg yolk.','Cut a chimney in the centre, a finger wide, held open with a paper cone: without it the steam lifts the lid.','Bake for 1 hour at 180°C. On coming out, pour the scalding cream through the chimney and rest for 10 minutes before cutting.']::text[]),
('pissaladiere','en','Pissaladière','Nice, French Riviera, France','A bread dough covered with a kilo of melted onions, anchovies and Nice olives. The name comes from pissalat, a paste of salted fry once brushed over the dough; anchovies replaced it, but the word stayed.',ARRAY['Slice the onions very finely and melt them in olive oil with thyme and bay, covered, for an hour over very low heat.','They must become a pale, dry compote, never golden: the moment they colour they turn sweet and the dish loses its balance.','Meanwhile knead a soft olive-oil bread dough and let it rise for 1½ hours.','Roll it into a rectangle half a centimetre thick, spread with the cooled onions, and lay a lattice of anchovies.','Set an olive in each diamond and bake for 20 minutes at 220°C. Eaten warm, never scalding.']::text[]),
('salade-nicoise','en','Salade niçoise','Nice, French Riviera, France','Tomatoes, broad beans or small artichokes, anchovies and hard-boiled egg, dressed with olive oil and nothing else. The Niçois are firm about it: no potatoes, no green beans, no vinegar, no cooked vegetable at all — it is a raw vegetable salad.',ARRAY['Quarter the tomatoes, salt them and let them drain for 20 minutes in a colander: their water would wash out the salad.','Pod the broad beans and slip off the inner skin if they are no longer young; slice the pepper and cucumber thinly.','Rub the bowl with garlic, then arrange the raw vegetables without tossing them.','Add the quartered hard-boiled eggs, anchovies, tuna and olives, and tear the basil by hand — a knife blackens it.','Dress with olive oil only, salt, and serve at once. Vinegar is the one prohibition all Nice agrees on.']::text[]),
('tapenade','en','Tapenade','Nyons, Provence, France','A paste of black olives, capers and anchovies pounded in a mortar. The name comes from tapeno, Provençal for caper, not from the olive: the caper, a minority by weight, gives the dish its name and the whole its acidity.',ARRAY['Stone the olives one by one with a knife rather than by machine, which crushes the pit and leaves bitterness.','Rinse the capers if they are salted, pat them dry, and soak the anchovies a few minutes in cold water.','Pound the garlic, then the capers, then the anchovies in a mortar, before adding the olives by handfuls.','Trickle in the oil as for mayonnaise, until you have a lumpy paste — never smooth: a blender gives a purée without character.','Sharpen with lemon juice, picked thyme, a spoonful of brandy and pepper. Do not salt: capers and anchovies see to that. Rest overnight.']::text[]),
('calissons-d-aix','en','Calissons of Aix','Aix-en-Provence, Provence, France','Diamonds of almond and candied melon paste set on rice paper and iced with egg white. Tradition ties them to King René''s wedding in 1454, where the queen, said to smile rarely, tasted the sweet: di calin soun, they are little hugs.',ARRAY['Blot the candied melon and orange peel thoroughly: too wet a paste will never dry and the icing will craze.','Blend them with the ground almonds and sugar to a supple paste, then scent with orange blossom water.','Dry it out for 10 minutes in a pan, stirring constantly, until it comes away from the sides.','Spread it on the rice paper 1 cm thick, leave to dry for 12 hours in the open air, then cut out the diamonds.','Whisk the egg white with icing sugar and lemon juice, ice the tops, and give them 8 minutes at 100°C with the door ajar: the icing must set without colouring.']::text[]),
('magret-de-canard','en','Duck magret','Samatan, Gascony, France','The breast of the fattened duck, seared skin-side until the fat renders, served pink. The word magret is a Gascon invention of the 1960s: until then fattened duck was eaten only as confit, and treating it like a piece of beef was thought a provocation.',ARRAY['Score the skin in a lattice without cutting the flesh: the cuts let the fat escape rather than swelling the skin.','Lay the breasts skin down in a cold pan, then bring it to medium heat: starting cold is what renders the fat instead of burning it.','Give them 8 minutes on the skin, pouring off the fat three times, then 3 minutes on the flesh side only.','Rest for 8 minutes under foil, flesh upward — without that rest all the juice ends up on the board.','Deglaze the pan with vinegar and honey, add the shallot and wine, reduce. Serve sliced on the bias, with sarladaise potatoes in duck fat.']::text[]),
('foie-gras-du-perigord','en','Périgord foie gras terrine','Sarlat-la-Canéda, Périgord, France','A goose or duck liver deveined, seasoned, packed into a terrine and cooked at low temperature. The whole difficulty is in the deveining: a liver brought to room temperature opens without breaking, a cold one shatters.',ARRAY['Take the liver out an hour ahead and let it reach 18°C: it is the only temperature at which it deveins cleanly.','Open each lobe flat and draw out the main veins with tweezers, following their branching, without chasing the finest ones.','Season both faces — 12 g of salt and 3 g of pepper per kilo, never by eye — then moisten with port and brandy, and marinate 12 hours in the cold.','Pack into the terrine, expelling air at each layer, and cook in a bain-marie at 90°C until the centre reaches 55°C, about 25 minutes.','Press under a weight, set for 48 hours in the fridge, and slice with a knife dipped in hot water. Serve cool but not chilled, on warm toast.']::text[]),
('farci-poitevin','en','Poitevin farci','Coulon, Marais Poitevin, France','A loaf of greens — sorrel, spinach, cabbage, leek — bound with egg and wrapped in green cabbage leaves, poached for three hours. The Easter dish of the Marais Poitevin, eaten cold, in slices, in the punts.',ARRAY['Blanch the large cabbage leaves for 3 minutes and refresh them: they must fold without cracking to wrap the farci.','Chop all the greens raw, very finely, and salt them for half an hour so they give up their water; press them hard.','Poorly drained greens make a farci that falls apart as it cooks: that is the only real difficulty in the dish.','Mix them with the beaten eggs, the crumb soaked in milk, the chopped bacon, the garlic and parsley.','Line a cloth with cabbage leaves, fill it, tie it tight like a bundle and poach for 3 hours in barely simmering water. Cool under a weight before slicing.']::text[]),
('pate-de-chartres','en','Chartres pâté','Chartres, Eure-et-Loir, France','A raised game pie of feathered game, traditionally red-legged partridge, presented to the bishops of Chartres since the 16th century. The jelly is not decoration: poured in cold through the chimney, it drives out the air and keeps the pie for weeks.',ARRAY['Bone the partridges, keep the breasts whole and mince the rest with the pork, shallot and juniper.','Marinate breasts and forcemeat for 12 hours in brandy: without it the game stays dry and scentless.','Line a pie mould with pastry, alternate forcemeat, breasts, foie gras and pistachios, pressing at each layer.','Cover, seal, glaze with yolk and cut two chimneys; bake 15 minutes at 210°C then an hour at 160°C.','Cool completely, then pour the jelly, warm but not set, through the chimneys. Wait 48 hours before slicing: the pie must firm up.']::text[]),
('gaufre-lilloise','en','Lille waffle','Lille, Hauts-de-France, France','A flat oval waffle, split while hot and filled with a cream of brown beet sugar and Madagascar vanilla. It keeps for days and travels: which is what has made it, since 1849, the thing you bring back from Lille.',ARRAY['Knead a rich yeasted dough with the flour, yeast, warm milk, eggs, 80 g of butter and the sugar, and prove for 1½ hours.','Divide into 50 g balls, prove for 30 minutes, then bake 90 seconds in a hot flat waffle iron.','Split each waffle horizontally while it is scalding: once cool it crumbles under the knife.','Cream the remaining butter with the vergeoise, scraped vanilla and rum, until the sugar no longer grates under the spatula.','Fill with a thin layer, close and press lightly. Rest overnight: the cream must soak into the dough.']::text[]),
('figatellu','en','Figatellu','Bastia, Upper Corsica, France','A Corsican sausage of pork liver and meat, smoked over chestnut wood, grilled on coals and eaten on bread that catches the fat. It is made only in winter, from November to March, when the free-ranging pigs have finished the chestnuts.',ARRAY['Cut the liver and meats by knife into small dice: a mincer crushes the liver and gives a bitter paste.','Salt at 25 g per kilo, pepper heavily, add the crushed garlic and red wine, and rest for 24 hours in the cold.','Fill the casings loosely and dry for three days somewhere cool and airy.','Cold-smoke over chestnut wood for a week, an hour or two a day: the smoke should scent, not cook.','Grill the figatellu on the coals, split lengthwise, and serve it on a slice of bread placed beneath it while it cooks, with fresh brocciu.']::text[]),
('fougasse-aux-olives','en','Olive fougasse','Aubagne, Provence, France','A flat bread slashed into an ear of wheat, thrown into the oven to test its heat before the real batches went in. From that use — the Roman focacia, the hearth bread — the fougasse takes its name and its open-worked shape.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, yeast, warm water, salt and half the oil for 12 minutes, to a supple, slightly tacky dough.','Prove for 1½ hours, then fold in the stoned olives and rosemary, without kneading again.','Stretch by hand into a one-centimetre oval on a tray, and let it relax for 20 minutes.','Cut boldly right through with a scraper, then pull the cuts open by hand: if they do not go through, they close again in the oven.','Oil, scatter coarse salt and bake for 18 minutes at 240°C with a little steam.']::text[]),
('pompe-a-huile','en','Pompe à huile','Avignon, Provence, France','A brioche-like flatbread with olive oil and orange blossom, one of the thirteen desserts of the Provençal Christmas supper. It is broken by hand and never cut: cutting the pompe, they say, would ruin the coming year.',ARRAY['Make a starter with the yeast, a little warm water and 100 g of flour, and let it double for an hour.','Add the rest of the flour, the sugar, olive oil, orange blossom water and zest, and knead for 10 minutes.','The dough must stay soft and oily: add water rather than flour if it resists.','Prove for 2 hours, flatten to a 2 cm disc and cut six well-spaced slashes in a star.','Bake for 25 minutes at 180°C and brush with olive oil and orange blossom water as it leaves the oven.']::text[]),
('bugnes-lyonnaises','en','Lyon bugnes','Lyon, Rhône, France','Diamonds of dough fried for carnival, thin and crisp in Lyon, thick and soft in Savoy. The quarrel is old and unrefereed: it hangs on how thin the dough is rolled and how long it rests, not on the recipe.',ARRAY['Knead everything except the oil and icing sugar for 10 minutes, to a smooth, non-sticky dough.','Rest it in the cold for at least 4 hours: that rest is what makes a bugne snap rather than chew.','Roll very thin — 2 mm for the Lyon version — and cut slit diamonds, passing one tip through the slit.','Heat the oil to 170°C: hotter, and the bugne browns without puffing; cooler, and it drinks the oil.','Fry 40 seconds a side, drain on paper, dust with icing sugar once warm. They are eaten the same day.']::text[]),
('barboton-ardechois','en','Chard barboton','Aubenas, Ardèche, France','A gratin of chard and potatoes with ewe''s tome, the garden dish for when the chard all bolts at once. To barboter, in Ardèche dialect, is to soak: the stems soak in the cream while the leaves stay on top.',ARRAY['Separate the stems from the leaves: the stems need 15 minutes'' cooking, the leaves three.','Cut the stems into lengths and blanch them in salted water, then drain them well.','Wilt the leaves in a pan with the onion and garlic, and press them to drive out all the water.','Combine both with thinly sliced raw potato, the cream beaten with the eggs, nutmeg and half the tome.','Turn into a buttered dish, cover with the remaining cheese and bake for an hour at 180°C. Rest 10 minutes before serving.']::text[]),
('roquefort-poires-noix','en','Roquefort with pears and walnuts','Roquefort-sur-Soulzon, Aveyron, France','France''s first protected cheese — a Toulouse parliament ruling in 1666, an appellation in 1925 — ripened in the fleurines of the Combalou, the fissures that naturally ventilate the caves. Served plainly, with pears and walnuts, it needs nothing else.',ARRAY['Take the Roquefort out an hour ahead: chilled, its paste is dry and its bite crushes everything else.','Cut it with a wire rather than a knife, in wedges taken from the centre out to the rind, so each portion carries as much blue.','Choose pears just ripe, firm under the thumb, and cut them at the last moment.','Toast the walnut halves dry for 3 minutes, until they smell — that short roasting is enough to wake the oil.','Serve on a board with buttered country bread and a trickle of honey. A sweet wine, never a tannic red.']::text[]),
('pogne-de-romans','en','Pogne de Romans','Romans-sur-Isère, Drôme, France','A brioche crown scented with orange blossom, golden and light, born as an Easter cake in the Dauphiné. The name comes from poignée, a handful: the dough was shaped with full hands, and the crown formed around the fist.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm milk with a spoonful of sugar and let it foam for 15 minutes.','Knead the flour, eggs, sugar, salt and orange blossom water for 15 minutes, then add the softened butter in three goes.','Prove for 2 hours, knock back, and chill the dough for 4 hours — a pogne is shaped cold, otherwise it sticks to the hands.','Form a ball, punch a hole through the centre with your fist and widen it generously: it will close by half as it rises.','Prove for 2 hours, glaze, scatter pearl sugar and bake for 35 minutes at 170°C. The crumb must stay stringy and yellow.']::text[]),
('pain-d-epices-de-dijon','en','Dijon spice bread','Dijon, Burgundy, France','A honey and spice loaf made with wheat flour, softer than the Reims version made with rye. The dukes of Burgundy brought it back from Flanders in the 14th century; the Dijon guild of spice-bread makers was recognised in 1711.',ARRAY['Warm the honey with the milk and butter, without going above 50°C: any hotter and the honey loses its scent.','Combine the flour, sugar, baking powder, salt and all the finely ground spices.','Pour in the warm honey in a stream, mixing with a spatula, then add the egg and zest.','Rest the batter for 24 hours, covered, at room temperature: that rest alone gives the softness and marries the spices.','Pour into a lined loaf tin and bake for 1 hour at 150°C. Wrap it warm in a cloth and wait two days before slicing.']::text[]),
('crepes-de-froment','en','Wheat crêpes','Quimper, Brittany, France','The sweet wheat crêpe, distinct from the savoury buckwheat galette with which it is often confused. In Lower Brittany both carry the same Breton name, krampouezh; it is the flour, not the filling, that separates them.',ARRAY['Mix the flour, sugar and salt, make a well and break in the eggs, then work outward from the centre.','Add the milk little by little: poured in at once it makes lumps no whisk really rescues.','Add the melted butter and rum, and rest the batter for an hour — the gluten must relax, or the crêpes shrink in the pan.','Heat the griddle until a drop of water dances, grease it with a buttered cloth, and spread a ladleful with the rozell in one circular sweep.','A minute on one side, thirty seconds on the other. Salted butter and sugar, and nothing more for the first one.']::text[]),
('pate-de-paques-berrichon','en','Berry Easter pie','Châteauroux, Berry, France','A pork and veal pie in which whole hard-boiled eggs are lined up, so that every slice shows a perfect round. It is eaten cold on Easter Monday: the eggs mark the end of Lent, and the cut must reveal them whole.',ARRAY['Hard-boil six eggs for 9 minutes, refresh them in iced water and peel them carefully: a torn white shows in the slice.','Chop the meats and bacon coarsely, mix them with the shallot, parsley, wine, four-spice, salt and the remaining egg.','Line a mould with pastry, spread half the forcemeat, line the whole eggs end to end down the centre, and cover with the rest.','The eggs must be buried and well centred: set too high, they break through the lid as it bakes.','Close, seal, glaze, cut a chimney and bake 15 minutes at 210°C then 45 minutes at 170°C. Eaten cold, the next day.']::text[]),
('rillettes-de-tours','en','Tours rillettes','Tours, Touraine, France','Brown, dry rillettes, cooked long and uncovered until the meat caramelises — the opposite of those of Le Mans, pale and fatty. Balzac, a Touraine man, called them ''that brown pork confit'' and held them to be his province''s glory.',ARRAY['Cut the meats into finger-sized strips along the grain: they must shred, not turn to purée.','Salt them and rest for 12 hours in the cold with the thyme and bay.','Melt them in lard uncovered over very low heat for 4 hours, never putting a lid on: evaporation is what gives the brown colour.','Raise the heat for the last half hour until the meat catches lightly on the base and turns mahogany — that is the whole difference from Le Mans.','Deglaze with white wine, shred with a fork, pepper, and pot them, pressing down. Cover with fat and wait three days.']::text[]),
('canistrelli','en','Canistrelli','Corte, Upper Corsica, France','Corsican shortbread biscuits made with oil and white wine, scented with anise, lemon or almonds. They hold neither egg nor butter: the wine binds the dough, which is why they keep for weeks in a tin.',ARRAY['Combine the flour, sugar, baking powder, salt and lightly crushed anise seeds dry.','Pour in the oil then the white wine and bring the dough together with your fingertips, without kneading: overworked, it turns hard instead of short.','Roll it 1.5 cm thick and cut diamonds with a knife, not a cutter.','Roll each diamond in caster sugar before it goes in the oven: that is what gives the crackling crust.','Bake for 25 minutes at 180°C until pale gold and cracked. Cool completely on a rack: warm, they seem soft.']::text[]),
('aziminu','en','Aziminu','Bastia, Upper Corsica, France','The Corsican rockfish soup, cousin to bouillabaisse but scented with the maquis — myrtle, immortelle, wild fennel — and without saffron or orange. It is made from whatever the boat brought back: scorpionfish, conger, hogfish, never a noble fish.',ARRAY['Scale and gut the fish, and keep the heads: they are where the body of the broth comes from.','Soften the onion, garlic and fennel in olive oil, add the tomato and paste, and let it melt down for 15 minutes.','Moisten with boiling water — never cold, which would seize the flesh — and cook the heads and bones for 25 minutes.','Pass through a food mill, pressing hard, return to the heat and cook thick potato rounds in it for 15 minutes.','Lay the whole fish on top for the last 8 minutes, without stirring. Serve the broth first, on garlic-rubbed bread, and the fish after.']::text[]),
('pulenda','en','Pulenda','Piedicroce, Castagniccia, Corsica, France','A Corsican polenta of chestnut flour, turned out onto a cloth and cut with a thread, served with figatellu and brocciu. The Castagniccia takes its name from its chestnut trees: until the 20th century they fed the mountains in place of the wheat they could not grow.',ARRAY['Bring the salted water to the boil and rain in the chestnut flour very finely with one hand, whisking with the other.','Chestnut flour lumps far faster than maize: never stop stirring, and pour slowly.','Then work with a wooden spoon, lifting the mass and folding it back, for 30 minutes over low heat.','It is ready when it comes away from the sides of the pot in one block and a crust forms on the base.','Turn it out onto a floured cloth, shape a ball and cut it with a butter wire — never a knife, which tears it. Serve with fresh brocciu.']::text[]),
('cacavellu','en','Cacavellu','Venaco, Upper Corsica, France','An Easter brioche crown enclosing a whole egg, shell and all, held down by two crossed strips of dough. The egg bakes with the bread; godparents gave them to their godchildren, and the size said how much they were loved.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, the yeast dissolved in warm milk, three eggs, the sugar, zest, anise and salt for 15 minutes, then work in the butter.','Prove for 2 hours: the dough must double and smell of anise before it is handled.','Shape a thick crown and set the two whole raw eggs on it, unwashed, pressing them halfway into the dough.','Cross two strips of dough over each egg and seal them firmly: as it bakes the dough swells and ejects a badly anchored egg.','Prove for an hour, glaze the bread only — never the eggs — and bake for 35 minutes at 175°C.']::text[]),
('presskopf-alsacien','en','Presskopf','Haguenau, Alsace, France','A pressed pork head set in its own jelly, sliced thin and served as a salad with onion vinaigrette. Nothing is wasted: the whole head, the tongue, the rinds, and it is the rind alone that sets the jelly, without a gram of added gelatine.',ARRAY['Soak the head for 12 hours in cold water, changing it three times, to draw out the blood.','Poach it for 3 hours with the rinds, vegetables, white wine, bay and cloves, never boiling: a cloudy broth gives a cloudy jelly.','Bone it while hot and cut the meat, tongue and rind into one-centimetre dice, discarding the cartilage.','Mix them with chopped parsley and a little reduced broth, pack into a terrine and press under a weight for 24 hours in the cold.','Slice very thinly and serve with a vinaigrette of raw onion and gherkin, and rye bread.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('berawecka','en','Berawecka','Strasbourg, Alsace, France','An Alsatian Christmas loaf made almost entirely of dried fruit — pears, figs, prunes, nuts — held together by a few spoonfuls of dough. Berawecka means pear bread: the dried pear is its base, and its steeping in kirsch lasts a week.',ARRAY['Soak the dried pears overnight in water, then poach them for 20 minutes; keep the cooking water.','Dice all the fruit, mix with the chopped nuts, spices and orange peel, douse with kirsch and steep for a week in the cold, stirring every other day.','Make a small yeasted dough with the flour, yeast, sugar and warm pear water, and let it double.','Fold it into the fruit: there must be no more than is strictly needed to bind, a berawecka is a fruit loaf and not a cake.','Shape short, dense loaves, rest for an hour and bake for an hour at 160°C. Wrap them and wait three weeks before slicing, very thinly.']::text[]),
('andouille-de-cambrai','en','Cambrai andouille','Cambrai, Hauts-de-France, France','A Cambrésis andouille of chitterlings rolled in a spiral and gently smoked, served cold in slices with a mustard vinaigrette. Unlike the Vire version it is not smoked for weeks: a few days suffice, and the flesh stays pale.',ARRAY['Scrape and turn the chitterlings, wash them in vinegared water then dry-salt them for 24 hours with thyme and bay.','Cut them into wide bands and roll them in a spiral one over another, tightening as you go.','Truss with string into an even roll: it is the evenness of the rolling that gives the marbled slice.','Cold-smoke over beech for three days, then poach for 2½ hours in a white wine and onion broth at 80°C, never boiling.','Cool in the broth, slice thinly and serve with a mustard vinaigrette of onion and gherkin.']::text[]),
('boeuf-miroton','en','Bœuf miroton','Paris, France','The leftovers of a pot-au-feu sliced and gratinated under an onion and vinegar sauce. It was the Tuesday dish of 19th-century bourgeois kitchens; it only works with yesterday''s meat, fresh meat falls apart in it.',ARRAY['Slice the onions finely and let them turn golden in butter for 25 minutes: they must colour, not merely soften — that is where the dish''s flavour is born.','Dust with flour, cook it for two minutes, then moisten with the pot-au-feu broth and let it thicken for 10 minutes.','Add the vinegar and mustard off the heat: a miroton sauce must be frankly sharp, or the boiled beef stays dull.','Slice the cold beef 5 mm thick — hot, it shreds — and lay the slices overlapping in a buttered dish.','Coat with the sauce, scatter breadcrumbs and gratinate for 15 minutes at 200°C. Gherkins and parsley on serving.']::text[]),
('macarons-de-nancy','en','Macarons of Nancy','Nancy, Lorraine, France','An old-style macaron, cracked, with no smooth shell and no filling, made of three ingredients only. Two nuns turned out of their convent by the Revolution sold them to live in Nancy in 1793: they were called the macaron sisters, and the recipe has not moved since.',ARRAY['Mix the ground almonds and sugar, and add the whites unbeaten, merely loosened with a fork.','On no account whip the whites: that is what separates the Nancy macaron from the Parisian one, and a meringue would give a smooth shell.','Work with a spatula to a thick, sticky paste, and rest it for 30 minutes.','Drop spoonfuls onto baking paper, well spaced, without smoothing them: the crack must come by itself.','Bake for 18 minutes at 160°C. They must stay soft in the middle — take them out when the top is golden but the centre still yielding.']::text[]),
('souffle-au-fromage','en','Cheese soufflé','Paris, France','A béchamel lightened with whipped whites, which triples in the oven and falls within three minutes. The word appears in Beauvilliers in 1814: the first French dish whose success is measured to the second, and one waits for the soufflé, never the reverse.',ARRAY['Butter the dish from bottom to top in vertical strokes, and line it with grated cheese: the soufflé grips the sides to climb.','Make a thick béchamel, let it cool a little, then beat in the yolks one at a time, the cheese, nutmeg and pepper.','Whip the whites with a pinch of salt to the bird''s beak, firm but not grainy — overbeaten, they will no longer fold in.','Loosen the base with a third of the whites using the whisk, then fold in the rest with a spatula, lifting, in some twenty movements only.','Fill three-quarters full, run your thumb round the rim to free the mixture, and bake 30 minutes at 190°C without once opening the door. Serve within the minute.']::text[]),
('tarte-normande','en','Norman apple tart','Lisieux, Normandy, France','An apple tart flooded with a cream custard and scented with calvados. The choice of apple is everything: a reinette or a boskoop holds its shape, a golden collapses into purée and the custard turns watery.',ARRAY['Line the tin, prick the base and blind-bake it for 12 minutes at 190°C under baking beans.','Dust the pre-baked base with ground almonds: they will drink the apple juices and stop the pastry going soggy.','Cut the apples into thick wedges and cook them 5 minutes in butter with a little sugar, then flame with calvados.','Arrange them in a tight rosette and pour over the custard of eggs, remaining sugar, cream and cinnamon.','Bake for 45 minutes at 180°C. The custard should still tremble at the centre as it comes out: it finishes setting as it cools.']::text[]),
('gateau-a-la-broche','en','Spit cake','Arreau, Pyrenees, France','A spiked cone of cake built layer upon layer on a spit turning before a wood fire. Each ladleful poured on the scalding cone hardens before the next; the spikes are not decoration, they are the drips of batter set hard.',ARRAY['Beat whole eggs with the sugar at length, at least 10 minutes, until the mixture triples and ribbons thickly.','Fold in the warm melted butter, then the sifted flour, rum, orange blossom water and zest: the batter must run off the whisk in an unbroken ribbon.','Line the cone with buttered paper and turn it before the coals until it is scalding.','Pour a ladleful of batter onto the turning cone and wait for it to brown completely before the next: fifteen to twenty layers, two hours of patience.','Cool on the spit before unmoulding. The cake keeps a month and is cut crosswise, with a saw or a long knife.']::text[]),
('fouace-nantaise','en','Nantes fouace','Nantes, Pays de la Loire, France','An orange-blossom brioche flatbread, shaped as a six-pointed star and baked flat. Rabelais made it famous: the Picrocholine war in Gargantua breaks out over a few fouaces refused to some shepherds.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, the yeast dissolved in warm milk, the eggs, sugar, salt and orange blossom water for 12 minutes.','Work in the softened butter and knead 8 minutes more, until the dough clearly leaves the bowl.','Prove for 1½ hours, knock back, then roll the dough into a 3 cm thick disc on a tray.','Cut six even points with a knife, from the edge toward the centre without reaching the middle: that star shape is what makes a fouace.','Prove for an hour, glaze with yolk and bake for 30 minutes at 180°C. Eaten warm, with salted butter.']::text[]),
('briau-sarthois','en','Sarthe briau','Le Mans, Sarthe, France','A savoury custard of spinach, bacon and cream on shortcrust, baked in a deep dish. The briau is the Sarthe garden dish for when the spinach bolts: as much is made as grows, and it is eaten warm or cold.',ARRAY['Wilt the spinach in a pan in three batches, without water, then press it hard between your hands.','A kilo of raw spinach gives barely three hundred grams once pressed: that is normal, and it is the remaining water that would ruin the custard.','Fry the bacon and onion in butter, and mix them with the knife-chopped spinach.','Beat the eggs with the cream, nutmeg and pepper, and salt barely — the bacon salts it already.','Fill the pastry case, pour in the custard and bake for 45 minutes at 180°C. A knife point must come out dry.']::text[]),
('prefou-vendeen','en','Préfou','Noirmoutier, Vendée, France','A flat bread split and filled with garlic butter, warmed through and torn by hand. Its name comes from pré-four, before the oven: Vendée bakers threw in a piece of dough to test the heat, and ate it with whatever came to hand.',ARRAY['Knead a soft bread dough and prove it for an hour.','Roll it into two oval flatbreads 1.5 cm thick and bake them 10 minutes at 220°C: they must stay very pale, barely set.','That short pre-bake is what matters — a browned bread will not open again without breaking.','Cream the salted butter with finely crushed garlic and parsley, adding nothing else.','Split each warm flatbread horizontally, spread generously, close and return for 8 minutes at 200°C, until the butter bubbles at the edges.']::text[]),
('tourton-des-alpes','en','Champsaur tourtons','Gap, Southern Alps, France','Small fried pillows of thin dough, savoury with potato or sweet with apple, known as the infant Jesus''s pillows. They were made at evening gatherings in the Champsaur, several pairs of hands at once: one rolls, one fills, a third fries.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, egg, melted butter, water and salt to a smooth dough, and rest it for an hour under a cloth.','Prepare the two fillings: mashed potato with cheese and parsley for the savoury, apples softened with sugar and cinnamon for the sweet.','Roll the dough as thinly as possible — you should half see your hand through it — and cut 8 cm squares.','Set a spoonful of filling on each, damp the edges, fold into a rectangle and seal with a fork: a badly sealed tourton opens in the fryer.','Fry at 175°C, 2 minutes a side, until they blister and brown. Drain and serve scalding.']::text[]),
('tarte-tropezienne','en','Tarte tropézienne','Saint-Tropez, French Riviera, France','A pearl-sugar brioche split and filled with a cream half pastry cream, half butter. Created in 1955 by a Polish pastry cook settled in Saint-Tropez, it was named by Brigitte Bardot during the filming of And God Created Woman.',ARRAY['Knead the brioche for 15 minutes, work in the butter, prove for 2 hours then rest overnight in the cold.','Make a pastry cream with the milk, yolks, sugar, cornstarch and vanilla, and cool it completely, covered in contact.','Loosen it with a whisk, then fold in the stiffly whipped cream and the orange blossom water: that marriage of two creams is what gives the lightness.','Roll the brioche into a 2 cm disc, prove for 1½ hours, scatter pearl sugar and bake 25 minutes at 175°C.','Split it horizontally once cold, pipe in the filling generously and chill for 2 hours before serving.']::text[]),
('bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa','en','Bacalhau à Gomes de Sá','Porto, Portugal','Flaked salt cod, potatoes and onion, gratinated in olive oil with black olives and hard-boiled egg. José Luís Gomes de Sá traded cod in Porto; he bequeathed the recipe with the note ''change nothing''.',ARRAY['Soak the cod for 36 hours in cold water, changing it six times.','Poach it for 10 minutes then let it stand in hot milk for an hour: this is what makes it tender and creamy.','Flake it by hand into large petals, removing skin and bones.','Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them warm and cut into thick rounds.','Soften the sliced onion in olive oil with the garlic, combine everything in an earthenware dish and bake for 25 minutes at 200°C. Garnish with hard-boiled egg, olives and parsley.']::text[]),
('bacalhau-a-lagareiro','en','Bacalhau à lagareiro','Coimbra, Beira Litoral, Portugal','A loin of salt cod roasted under a rain of olive oil, with potatoes crushed by fist. Lagareiro means the man at the oil mill: the dish exists to taste the oil, and the cod is its pretext.',ARRAY['Soak the cod loins for 36 hours, then pat them thoroughly dry.','Boil the potatoes whole and unpeeled in heavily salted water for 25 minutes.','Crush them with your fist or the flat of your hand, without breaking them up: the skin should split and the flesh stay in pieces.','Lay the cod and potatoes in an earthenware dish, cover with sliced garlic and onion.','Drown it in olive oil — half the dish, and that is not an exaggeration — and bake for 35 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('feijoada-de-buzios','en','Whelk feijoada','Olhão, Algarve, Portugal','Whelks stewed with white beans, chouriço and coriander. The Algarve makes its feijoada from the sea: shellfish replace the pork, and coriander goes in by the handful.',ARRAY['Purge the whelks for 2 hours in salted water, then boil them for 25 minutes and pull them from their shells.','Remove the operculum and the black tip: it is the only bitter part of the shellfish.','Soak the beans overnight and cook them for 50 minutes with the bay.','Make a refogado of onion, garlic, pepper and tomato in olive oil, 20 minutes.','Bring together the beans, whelks and chouriço, add white wine and give it 20 minutes. Cover with coriander off the heat.']::text[]),
('frango-a-cafreal','en','Frango à cafreal','Lisbon, Portugal','A spatchcocked chicken marinated in coriander and piri-piri, grilled until charred at the edges. Brought back from Mozambique and Goa, it became the Sunday-night dish of Lisbon''s churrasqueiras.',ARRAY['Spatchcock the chicken by cutting along the backbone, and flatten it with the heel of your hand on the breastbone.','Blend coriander, garlic, piri-piri, lemon, white wine and olive oil into a fragrant green marinade.','Score the flesh where it is thick and work the marinade in; leave 12 hours in the cold.','Grill the chicken skin side first, away from the coals, for 25 minutes, then turn it for 15 more.','Baste with reserved marinade at the end. Serve with roast potatoes and lemon.']::text[]),
('bifes-de-cebolada','en','Bifes de cebolada','Castelo Branco, Beira Baixa, Portugal','Slices of beef drowned under onions melted in white wine and bay. The cebolada is not a garnish but the sauce itself: three onions per person, and nobody finds that excessive.',ARRAY['Flatten the beef slices with a mallet and salt them only at the last moment.','Sear them 90 seconds a side in scorching oil and lift them out: they will finish in the sauce.','Melt the sliced onions in the same oil, covered, for 30 minutes, until golden.','Add the garlic, bay, paprika, tomato and white wine, and reduce for 10 minutes.','Return the meat and its juices for 5 minutes. Serve with chips and a fried egg on each plate.']::text[]),
('acorda-de-savel','en','Shad açorda','Setúbal, Estremadura, Portugal','An açorda thickened with bread and egg, with shad from the Sado and a great deal of coriander. Shad is full of fine bones: you poach it, flake it patiently, and the soup repays the work.',ARRAY['Poach the shad for 12 minutes in salted water with the onion and bay; strain and keep the broth.','Flake the fish by hand with patience, removing every bone: the only long job in the dish.','Pound the degermed garlic and coriander in a mortar with coarse salt and olive oil.','Crumble the bread into a deep dish, spoon the green paste over it, then the scalding broth, and stir with a fork.','Break the eggs into the soup and stir for 2 minutes off the heat: they should thread, not set. Add the fish.']::text[]),
('perdiz-a-alcantara','en','Alcántara convent partridge','Portalegre, Alentejo, Portugal','Partridges stuffed with foie gras and truffles, stewed in port. The recipe was seized from a convent at Alcántara by one of Napoleon''s generals in 1807; his widow published it, and Portugal has never forgiven it.',ARRAY['Draw the partridges and keep the livers. Mix them with the foie gras and chopped truffle, and season.','Stuff the birds with this and truss them tightly, so the filling cannot escape.','Marinate them for 24 hours in the port with the bay and thyme, turning twice.','Colour them in duck fat, add the onion and carrot, then the marinade and stock.','Cover and simmer for 1½ hours over very low heat. Strain the sauce, reduce it and coat the birds.']::text[]),
('moelas-guisadas','en','Moelas guisadas','Alfama, Lisbon, Portugal','Chicken gizzards stewed in white wine, tomato and piri-piri, served as petiscos in the tascas. They need an hour to stop being tough: that is the only secret.',ARRAY['Trim the silvery membrane from the gizzards: left on, it stays tough whatever you do.','Blanch them for 10 minutes and discard that water.','Make a refogado of onion, garlic and bay in olive oil for 15 minutes, then add the paprika off the heat.','Add the gizzards, tomato, white wine and piri-piri, and cover with water.','Simmer covered for 1 hour, then 15 minutes uncovered so the sauce coats. Serve with bread.']::text[]),
('arroz-de-forno-minhoto','en','Minho baked rice','Vila do Conde, Minho, Portugal','An oven-baked rice with smoked meats and chouriço, crisp on top and yielding below. The Minho bakes what other regions leave on the hob: the crust is half the pleasure.',ARRAY['Cook the pork for 40 minutes with the bay; keep the broth and dice the meat.','Make a refogado of onion and garlic in oil, add the paprika off the heat, then the white wine.','Add the rice and let it turn pearly, then add two parts broth to one of rice.','Add the diced meats, chouriço and presunto, and cook for 12 minutes on the hob: the rice must stay very wet.','Turn into an earthenware dish, break three eggs on top and bake for 20 minutes at 210°C to form the crust.']::text[]),
('bola-de-berlim','en','Bola de Berlim','Albufeira, Algarve, Portugal','A doughnut filled with egg cream, split rather than injected, sold by hawkers on Algarve beaches. Brought by German Jewish refugees in the 1930s, it acquired a yellow cream Berlin never knew.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm milk, add 60 g of sugar, the flour, two eggs, the butter and the zest. Knead for 12 minutes.','Prove for 1½ hours, then shape 60 g balls and let them rise another 45 minutes on a floured cloth.','For the cream: bring the remaining milk to a simmer, pour it over the yolks beaten with sugar and cornstarch, and thicken for 3 minutes.','Fry the balls at 165°C, 3 minutes a side: hotter, and they brown before the centre is cooked.','Roll them in sugar while warm, split them halfway without cutting through, and pipe in the cream generously.']::text[]),
('tigelada-beira','en','Beira tigelada','Covilhã, Beira Baixa, Portugal','An egg and cinnamon custard baked in an earthenware bowl in the wood oven. No caramel, no pastry: eggs, milk and a great deal of cinnamon, and the brown crust that forms on top.',ARRAY['Bring the milk to a simmer with the lemon zest and a cinnamon stick, then infuse off the heat for 20 minutes.','Beat the whole eggs with the sugar without aerating them: a tigelada should not rise.','Add the sifted flour, then the cooled milk in a thin stream, and strain the mixture.','Butter earthenware bowls and fill them three-quarters full.','Bake for 30 minutes at 200°C: the top should brown frankly and the centre stay wobbly. Dust with cinnamon.']::text[]),
('polvo-guisado-acoreano','en','Azorean stewed octopus','Horta, Faial, Azores, Portugal','Octopus stewed in Pico wine with local chilli and sweet potato. In the Azores a cork goes into the pot with it: the custom is stubborn, the explanation less so.',ARRAY['Freeze the octopus then thaw it: the fibres break and the flesh turns tender without effort.','Stiffen it dry in the pot until it gives up its water, and discard that first water.','Add the oil, sliced onion, garlic and bay, and soften for 20 minutes covered.','Pour in the white wine, tomato and piri-piri, and simmer for 40 minutes without adding water.','Add the sweet potato in large cubes for the last 25 minutes, and cover with parsley.']::text[]),
('molho-de-figado','en','Molho de fígado','Ponta Delgada, São Miguel, Azores, Portugal','Pig''s liver marinated in wine and garlic, stewed with local chilli, served at Christmas. In the Azores the molho is made on the evening of the 24th and eaten for breakfast on the 25th, with maize bread.',ARRAY['Cut the liver and belly into cubes and marinate them for 24 hours in the wine, pounded garlic, chilli and spices.','Drain the meat keeping the marinade, and pat it dry: wet, it would boil instead of searing.','Colour it in small batches in the lard, over high heat.','Pour in the strained marinade, add the orange zest, and simmer covered for 1 hour.','Uncover for the last 20 minutes: the sauce should coat. Serve with maize bread.']::text[]),
('sopa-de-castanhas','en','Chestnut soup','Vila Real, Trás-os-Montes, Portugal','A soup of chestnuts and white beans, thickened without cream or flour. Before the potato, chestnuts fed Trás-os-Montes all winter: they were called the bread of the poor.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then cook them for 50 minutes with the bay.','Score the chestnuts with a cross and boil them for 20 minutes: both skins then come away together, while hot.','The second, thin skin must come off completely: left on, it makes the soup bitter.','Soften the onion, leek and garlic in olive oil, add the chestnuts and carrot, and moisten with the bean broth.','Cook for 30 minutes, blend only half of it, and serve with crisped presunto dice and mint.']::text[]),
('bolas-de-berlim-natario','en','Natário bolas de Berlim','Viana do Castelo, Minho, Portugal','Manuel Natário''s shop, opened around 1935 near the costume museum, sells more than a thousand a day and the queue spills onto the pavement. Unlike the Algarve beach version, which is split open, this one is piped through the side after being rolled in cinnamon sugar — and it is eaten warm, straight from the fryer.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in 150 ml of warm milk with a spoonful of sugar, and wait for it to foam.','Knead it for 15 minutes with the flour, whole eggs, two yolks, 80 g of sugar, the butter, zest and salt: the dough should leave the bowl and stay supple.','Prove for 1½ hours, shape 55 g balls, flatten them barely and let them rise for an hour on a floured cloth — that second rise is what gives the pale belt around the middle.','Meanwhile make a custard with the remaining milk, six yolks, 100 g of sugar, the cornstarch and the vanilla; cover it in contact and let it cool completely before whisking it smooth.','Fry at 165°C, 3 minutes a side, roll them straight away in the cinnamon sugar, then pipe in the cream through a hole made in the side. They do not keep: that is the price of the queue.']::text[]),
('poulet-de-mamie','en','Grandmother''s chicken','Areosa, Viana do Castelo, Portugal','A chicken stewed in white wine and colorau, the sweet paprika the Minho measures by the tablespoon rather than the pinch. A family recipe from Areosa, where the skin comes off and the olive oil is never stinted: it is the oil that carries the colour of the pepper.',ARRAY['Joint the chicken and take off the skin: without it the sauce stays clean and takes the colour of the pepper rather than of fat.','Sweat the sliced onions and the lardons in olive oil, generously — a good glassful at least.','Add the chicken pieces and turn them for 5 minutes, without trying to brown them hard.','Pour in two glasses of white wine, the sweet paprika, the cayenne and salt, then water to cover. Simmer covered for 1 hour over low heat.','Add the peas a quarter of an hour before the end. Serve with plain rice, or oven-baked rice if the meal is a Sunday one.']::text[]),
('pataniscas-de-bacalhau','en','Salt cod pataniscas','Lisbon, Portugal','Flat fritters of shredded salt cod, served with a bean rice black with coriander. This is the lunch of Lisbon tascas: a patanisca must be irregular and ragged at the edges, a perfectly round one betrays too much flour.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod for 24 hours, changing the water four times, then poach it for 8 minutes and shred it by hand into coarse strands.','On no account chop it: a patanisca must keep fibres you can recognise between the teeth.','Beat the eggs with the flour and milk, add grated onion, garlic and plenty of parsley, and rest for 30 minutes.','Fold in the cod: the batter should hold on the spoon but stay loose, otherwise add milk.','Drop spoonfuls into oil at 175°C without spreading them and turn halfway. Serve with a coriander bean rice.']::text[]),
('prego-no-pao','en','Prego no pão','Lisbon, Portugal','A garlic steak in a soft roll, eaten standing at the counter or at the end of a meal, after the fish. Prego means nail: garlic cloves were once nailed into the meat to tenderise it, and the name outlived the practice.',ARRAY['Flatten the steaks with the palm, not a mallet: the aim is to widen them, not to break the fibres.','Pound the garlic with salt and crumbled bay, and rub it into both sides; rest for 20 minutes.','Heat the pan white hot, barely oil it, and sear 90 seconds a side: any longer and a thin steak turns to shoe leather.','Deglaze with white wine and a knob of butter and scrape up the sediment — that spoonful of sauce is all that separates a good prego from a bad one.','Split the roll, pour the juices inside so the bread drinks them, add the steak, a little mustard and piri-piri. Eaten at once.']::text[]),
('bife-a-marrare','en','Bife à Marrare','Chiado, Lisbon, Portugal','The cream-and-pepper steak of the Marrare café, where literary Lisbon gathered in the 19th century. A single pan served the whole evening unwashed: the sediment built up, and the last customer''s sauce was better than the first''s.',ARRAY['Take the meat out an hour ahead and pat it dry: a wet steak boils instead of searing.','Crack the pepper in a mortar rather than grinding it, and press it onto both faces.','Sear in foaming butter with crushed unpeeled garlic and bay, 2 minutes a side for a centimetre and a half.','Set the meat aside, deglaze with wine, reduce almost dry, then pour in the cream and let it thicken without boiling.','Return the steaks for a minute so the sauce coats them. Serve with thin chips and nothing else.']::text[]),
('ovos-moles-de-aveiro','en','Ovos moles of Aveiro','Aveiro, Beira Litoral, Portugal','A cream of egg yolks and sugar enclosed in wafer shells moulded as seashells and salt barrels. The convents of Aveiro made them from the 16th century with the yolks left over from starching sails and linen.',ARRAY['Bring the sugar and water to 108°C — soft ball — and let it cool to 40°C with the zest.','Push the yolks through a fine sieve to remove the chalazae: they are what form the white specks nothing can undo.','Pour the warm syrup onto the yolks in a stream, whisking, then return to very low heat.','Stir constantly with a spatula, scraping the base, until the cream pulls away in one mass — about 12 minutes. A second''s inattention and the eggs scramble.','Cool for 12 hours, then fill the wafer shells, seal them with water and trim with scissors.']::text[]),
('sopa-de-cacao','en','Dogfish soup','Alcácer do Sal, Alentejo, Portugal','A dogfish soup thickened with flour, green with coriander and sharpened with vinegar, poured over stale bread. The Alentejo is a land without a coast: dogfish, cheap and free of small bones, came up salted from Setúbal.',ARRAY['Soak the fish for an hour if salted, then poach it for 10 minutes with the onion and bay; keep the broth.','Flake the flesh in large pieces and remove the central cartilage, which comes away in one piece.','Pound the garlic, coriander and salt in a mortar: this is the base of the dish, and a mortar releases coriander oil as no knife does.','Turn the flour golden in olive oil, moisten with the cooking broth and let it thicken for 10 minutes.','Add the pounded paste, the vinegar and the fish, heat without boiling, and pour over slices of stale bread in the plates.']::text[]),
('dom-rodrigo','en','Dom Rodrigo','Lagos, Algarve, Portugal','Egg threads and a cinnamon almond cream, twisted in coloured foil like a sweet. The name is said to come from Dom Rodrigo Lobo da Silveira, governor of Lagos; the wrapper came much later, when the sweet took to the markets.',ARRAY['Make a syrup at 105°C with 300 g of the sugar, the water and the zest, and keep it at a tremble.','For the fios de ovos, sieve eight yolks and pour them in a fine stream into the syrup through a funnel, moving in circles: they set into golden threads in 20 seconds.','Lift them out with a skimmer the moment they float — a second too long and the threads turn brittle.','Make an almond cream with the remaining sugar, the ground almonds, six yolks and the cinnamon, cooked gently until it pulls away.','Set a spoonful of cream on a square of foil, crown it with egg threads and twist the ends. Keeps for two weeks.']::text[]),
('conquilhas-a-algarvia','en','Algarve wedge clams','Faro, Algarve, Portugal','Wedge clams gathered at low tide from the sand of the Ria Formosa, opened in two minutes with garlic and coriander. Nothing is ruined faster: thirty seconds too long and a wedge clam shrinks to rubber, past saving.',ARRAY['Purge the clams for 2 hours in water salted to 35 g per litre, changing it twice: they must give up all their sand.','Discard any that stay open, then rinse them under plenty of cold water and drain.','Turn the sliced garlic pale gold in olive oil without burning it — burnt, it makes the sauce bitter and nothing saves it.','Tip in the clams over high heat with the white wine, cover and shake the pan: they open in 90 seconds.','Kill the heat the moment the last one opens, add chopped coriander and lemon juice. Serve in the pan, with bread for the juices.']::text[]),
('espada-com-banana','en','Scabbardfish with banana','Funchal, Madeira, Portugal','Breaded fillets of black scabbardfish, served with pan-fried Madeiran banana and passion fruit. The espada is caught a thousand metres down, at night, on longlines: its huge eyes and silver skin do not survive the ascent.',ARRAY['Marinate the fillets for 20 minutes in lemon juice, crushed garlic and salt: the flesh is mild and short of acidity.','Pat them thoroughly dry, flour them then dip in beaten egg — in that order, or the flour slides off.','Fry in oil 2 minutes a side: the fillet is thin, and cooks before you have finished turning it.','Halve the bananas lengthwise and caramelise them in butter, without stirring, until golden.','Plate the fish, banana on top, and spoon over passion fruit pulp. The acidity is what links the sweetness to the fish.']::text[]),
('picado-madeirense','en','Madeiran picado','Funchal, Madeira, Portugal','Cubes of beef with garlic and Madeira wine, tipped over a bed of chips, set in the middle of the table with as many forks as there are people. There are no plates: you pick — picar — straight from the dish, and that is where the name comes from.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into 2 cm dice and marinate for an hour with crushed garlic, bay, salt and piri-piri.','Cut the potatoes into thick chips and fry them twice: blanched at 150°C, browned at 180°C.','Pat the meat dry and sear it in butter over fierce heat, in small batches: crowded, it would weep and boil.','Deglaze with Madeira, reduce by half and return the meat for a minute.','Tip the meat and its juices over the scalding chips, scatter parsley and pepper, and set the dish in the middle of the table.']::text[]),
('milho-frito','en','Milho frito','Câmara de Lobos, Madeira, Portugal','Cubes of cornmeal with cabbage and wild fennel, chilled overnight then fried until they crack. The obligatory partner to Madeiran espetada: the centre must stay creamy under a crust that resists, which only a full chilling allows.',ARRAY['Boil the salted water with the finely shredded cabbage, garlic and chopped wild fennel for 15 minutes.','Rain in the cornmeal, whisking constantly, to avoid the smallest lump.','Stir for 25 minutes with a wooden spoon over low heat: it is long, and it is what stops the porridge tasting raw.','Add the lard, pour into a dish 3 cm deep, smooth the top and chill overnight.','Cut 4 cm cubes and fry at 180°C until golden on every side. A warm cube falls apart in the oil.']::text[]),
('lapas-grelhadas','en','Grilled limpets','Horta, Faial, Azores, Portugal','Limpets prised off the black rocks, grilled in their shells under garlic butter and doused with lemon. They are lifted with a knife in a single movement: hesitate a second and the limpet clamps to the stone with a force nothing overcomes.',ARRAY['Scrub the shells under cold water and let the limpets purge for 30 minutes in seawater or heavily salted water.','Cream the butter with the crushed garlic, parsley and piri-piri.','Set the limpets flesh upward on a scalding plate or straight on the coals.','Drop a hazelnut of butter on each as soon as they start to sizzle, and give them 4 minutes in all — cooked longer they toughen and cling.','Squeeze lemon over as they come off and serve with corn bread for the butter left in the shells.']::text[]),
('cracas-cozidas','en','Cracas','Praia da Vitória, Terceira, Azores, Portugal','Giant barnacles chiselled off the volcanic rock, boiled in seawater and eaten with a pin. They grow only on lava beaten by the Atlantic swell and are sold as whole plates, like a piece of reef.',ARRAY['Rinse the plates under plenty of cold water, scrubbing the rock to remove sand and weed.','Bring heavily salted water to the boil — 40 g per litre, or seawater if you have it — with the bay.','Drop in the barnacles and count 6 minutes from the return to the boil, no longer: overcooked, they retract to the bottom of their cone and become impossible to extract.','Drain and serve scalding, with a pin or a snail fork per person.','Melted butter with piri-piri and a wedge of lemon are enough. The flesh is already salty: do not salt again.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('pastel-de-chaves','en','Pastel de Chaves','Chaves, Trás-os-Montes, Portugal','A puff-pastry half-moon filled with raw minced veal and pork, protected by a geographical indication since 2011. The original recipe, appearing around 1862, is passed on by word of mouth: the filling goes in raw and cooks in its own juices, sealed by the pastry.',ARRAY['Chop the meats with a knife rather than a processor: the filling must stay in distinct grains, not a paste.','Mix them with grated onion, garlic, parsley, white wine, nutmeg, salt and plenty of pepper, and rest for 4 hours in the cold.','Roll the pastry to 2 mm and cut ovals: it must be thin, because it puffs a great deal.','Set the raw filling on one half, damp the edge, fold and seal by pinching firmly — the least leak empties the pastel of its juices.','Glaze with yolk and bake for 25 minutes at 200°C. Eaten warm, within the hour: cold, they lose the crispness that is the whole point.']::text[]),
('bola-de-lamego','en','Bola de Lamego','Lamego, Douro, Portugal','A flat bread filled with presunto, chouriço and bacon, baked as a golden slab and cut into squares. This is the Douro harvest snack: it travels, it is eaten cold, and it keeps a man going until evening.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, the yeast dissolved in warm milk, two eggs, the softened butter and salt for 12 minutes, and prove for 1½ hours.','Slice the charcuterie very thinly: diced, it would pierce the dough and let the fat escape.','Divide the dough in two and roll each half very thin — 3 mm — on an oiled tray.','Cover the first with a single layer of charcuterie, without overlapping, lay the second sheet on and seal all round by folding.','Prick the surface with a fork, glaze with egg and bake for 45 minutes at 190°C. Let it cool before cutting into squares.']::text[]),
('morcela-de-arganil','en','Arganil morcela','Arganil, Beira Serra, Portugal','A Beira blood sausage with cumin and wine, dried then slowly smoked over oak. Unlike northern black puddings it holds neither rice nor onion: only blood, fat and spice, which makes it tight and almost dry when cut.',ARRAY['Collect the blood, whisking it at once with a little salt to stop it setting, then pass it through a sieve.','Dice the fat small and render it only halfway: fully rendered, it would give nothing to chew on.','Pound the garlic with the cumin, paprika, cloves and salt, and slake it all in the red wine.','Combine blood, fat and spices, fill the casings loosely and tie them into horseshoes — the mixture swells as it cooks and an overfull sausage bursts.','Poach for 40 minutes at 80°C, never boiling, then dry for three days and smoke over oak for a week. Grill for a few minutes on the coals to serve.']::text[]),
('bucho-de-arganil','en','Arganil bucho','Arganil, Beira Serra, Portugal','A pig''s stomach stuffed with meats and bread, sewn shut and boiled for three hours, the dish of slaughter day at Christmas. It is served in thick slices with the cozido vegetables, and a share of the filling always goes to the neighbours who came to help.',ARRAY['Turn the stomach inside out and scrape it in vinegared water, then soak it for 12 hours, changing the water three times.','Cut the meats into one-centimetre dice and marinate them overnight with garlic, paprika, cumin and white wine.','Add the crumbled stale bread and beaten eggs: the bread soaks up the juices which, without it, would burst the stomach.','Fill the stomach only two-thirds full and sew it up with kitchen thread in close stitches.','Poach for 3 hours in barely simmering water, pricking the stomach with a needle after 20 minutes to release the air. Add the greens and potatoes to the broth for the last half hour.']::text[]),
('queijada-da-graciosa','en','Graciosa queijadas','Santa Cruz da Graciosa, Azores, Portugal','Small cakes of egg yolk and milk, with no cheese despite the name, baked in pleated paper cases. Graciosa has no fresh cheese to spare: its queijadas are made with butter and starch, and rise into a split dome.',ARRAY['Warm the milk with the butter, zest and a pinch of salt, without letting it boil.','Whisk the yolks with the sugar until pale and ribboning.','Add the sifted cornstarch and flour, then the warm milk in a stream, whisking constantly.','Rest the batter for 30 minutes: the bubbles must rise, otherwise the queijadas sink as they cool.','Fill the cases three-quarters full and bake for 25 minutes at 190°C. The tops must split: that is the Graciosa mark.']::text[]),
('alcatra-terceirense','en','Alcatra','Angra do Heroísmo, Terceira, Azores, Portugal','Beef stewed four hours in a Vila Franca clay pot, with vinho de cheiro and allspice. The pot is not a detail: its unglazed interior lets moisture escape slowly and concentrates the sauce without ever catching.',ARRAY['Rub the inside of the clay pot with bacon, then line the base with thickly sliced onions.','Lay in the meat in large pieces, the garlic, the whole allspice and the bay, and cover with a second layer of onions.','Pour in red wine to cover, add the butter, salt, and on no account add water: the onions will give enough.','Bake covered for 4 hours at 150°C, never stirring or lifting the lid before the third hour.','Uncover for the last half hour to let the sauce reduce and the surface colour. Serve with the island''s sweet bread.']::text[]),
('torresmos-transmontanos','en','Torresmos','Macedo de Cavaleiros, Trás-os-Montes, Portugal','Pieces of pork belly confited in their own fat then finished over high heat until the rind blisters. They are the by-product of slaughter day: the lard was rendered for the year, and what stayed in the cauldron was that evening''s meal.',ARRAY['Cut the belly into 4 cm cubes with the rind on, and marinate them 12 hours with crushed garlic, paprika, white wine and salt.','Drain and pat them dry: marinade left on the meat would make the fat spit dangerously.','Confit them for 1½ hours in lard at 90°C, uncovered, never simmering — this is gentle cooking, not frying.','They are ready when a skewer passes through the rind without resistance. Lift them out and let them cool for 20 minutes.','Bring the fat to 190°C and return them for 4 minutes to blister the rind. Serve with cornmeal porridge and orange quarters, whose acidity cuts the fat.']::text[]),
('arroz-de-lingueirao','en','Razor clam rice','Tavira, Algarve, Portugal','A wet rice with razor clams, gathered from the sand of the Ria Formosa by pouring salt into their hole. The salt mimics the rising tide and the clam comes up by itself: the only way to take it whole.',ARRAY['Purge the razor clams for 2 hours in salted water, then steam them open in 90 seconds and keep every drop of liquor.','Take out the flesh, remove the dark sac and cut the foot into lengths; keep a few shells for serving.','Make a long refogado of onion, garlic and pepper in olive oil, then add grated tomato and let it melt down.','Pearl the rice, moisten with white wine then the strained clam liquor made up with water — three parts to one of rice: this rice must stay soupy.','Cook for 14 minutes, add the clams and coriander off the heat, and serve at once in the pan.']::text[]),
('bacalhau-a-minhota','en','Bacalhau à minhota','Braga, Minho, Portugal','A thick cod loin floured and fried, laid on rounds of fried potato and onion, all flooded with olive oil and finished in the oven. The Minho never sets its cod on boiled potatoes: they must have been fried, to hold up under the oil.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod for 36 hours in the fridge, skin up, changing the water four times — thick loins need longer than flakes.','Pat them dry, flour them and fry 4 minutes a side in hot oil, to a pale gold crust.','Fried separately in thick rounds, the potatoes must stay firm and the onion turn translucent without browning.','Arrange potatoes and onions in the base of an earthenware dish, set the cod on top and cover with sliced garlic and bay.','Douse generously with olive oil and give it 15 minutes at 200°C. The oil is not thrown away: it is mopped up with bread.']::text[]),
('stargazy-pie','en','Stargazy pie','Mousehole, Cornwall, England','A pilchard pie with the heads breaking through the crust to face the sky. Mousehole makes it on 23 December, for Tom Bawcock who braved the storm and saved the village from famine.',ARRAY['Gut and bone the sardines keeping head and tail attached: the whole staging of the dish depends on it.','Fry the bacon and onion, and hard-boil three eggs.','Line a round dish with pastry and fill with bacon, onion and quartered eggs.','Arrange the sardines like the spokes of a wheel, heads outward, and pour over the cream beaten with the remaining eggs and the wine.','Cover with pastry, pushing each head through it, glaze and bake for 40 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('laverbread-gallois','en','Laverbread','Swansea, Wales','Laver seaweed boiled at length, rolled in oatmeal and fried in bacon fat. Richard Burton called it Welsh caviar; it takes eight hours of boiling to become edible.',ARRAY['Wash the seaweed in several changes of water to remove every grain of sand: some always remains, and you taste it.','Boil it for 6 to 8 hours in unsalted water, until it becomes a smooth black purée.','Drain and press it: it should have the consistency of a thick jam.','Grill the bacon and keep the rendered fat in the pan.','Shape patties of the laver rolled in oatmeal and fry them 3 minutes a side. Serve with bacon, cockles and lemon.']::text[]),
('hachee-amsterdam','en','Hachee','Amsterdam, Netherlands','A beef and onion stew with vinegar and cloves, simmered so long it falls apart at the spoon. Served with red cabbage and apple: the Dutch cannot conceive of one without the other.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into cubes and sear it in butter in batches, without crowding the pot.','Lift it out and melt the sliced onions for 25 minutes: you need as much onion as meat.','Dust with flour, add the vinegar and reduce it, then moisten with the stock.','Add cloves, bay, juniper and sugar, return the meat and simmer covered for 2 hours.','Serve with braised red cabbage and apple, and boiled potatoes.']::text[]),
('waterzooi-de-poisson','en','Fish waterzooi','Ghent, East Flanders, Belgium','River fish poached in a vegetable broth thickened with egg yolks. Ghent waterzooi was fish long before it was chicken: pollution in the Scheldt tipped the recipe over in the 20th century.',ARRAY['Cut all the vegetables into julienne of the same thickness: in a waterzooi they must cook together.','Sweat them in butter without colouring, then moisten with the stock and cook for 15 minutes.','Cut the heat and slide in the fish in large lengths: they poach in the residual warmth, 8 minutes.','Beat the yolks and cream, temper with a ladle of broth, then stir into the soup off the heat.','Never let it boil again: the liaison would split. Lemon and parsley as you serve.']::text[]),
('malakoff-vaudois','en','Vaud malakoff','Vinzel, Vaud, Switzerland','A fritter of grated gruyère on a slice of bread, deep-fried and served scalding with white wine. Vaud mercenaries are said to have brought it back from the siege of Malakoff in 1855; the village of Vinzel still lives on it.',ARRAY['Coarsely grate the gruyère and let it come to room temperature: cold, it will not bind.','Mix it with the eggs, flour, white wine, kirsch, pounded garlic and nutmeg, into a dense paste.','Rest it for 2 hours in the cold: the paste should hold its shape without slumping.','Cut 7 cm rounds of bread and set a flattened ball of paste on each, barely overhanging.','Lower them bread side up into oil at 175°C for 4 minutes, until the cheese puffs and colours. Serve immediately.']::text[]),
('arroz-caldoso-de-marisco','en','Soupy seafood rice','Cádiz, Andalusia, Spain','A shellfish rice deliberately left soupy, eaten with a spoon. Unlike paella, arroz caldoso never dries: it must run when the plate is tilted, and is eaten within twenty minutes.',ARRAY['Peel the prawns and make a strong stock with the heads and shells, 25 minutes; strain it.','Make a sofrito of onion, garlic, pepper and tomato in olive oil, 20 minutes, until it darkens.','Add the paprika off the heat, then the rice, and let it turn pearly for a minute.','Pour in four parts scalding stock to one of rice — twice a paella — with the infused saffron.','Cook uncovered for 16 minutes, add the shellfish and fish for the last 6, and serve at once.']::text[]),
('kalops','en','Kalops','Borås, Västergötland, Sweden','A beef stew with allspice, served with pickled beetroot. The name comes from English ''collops'', slices of meat: Swedish sailors brought it back from Scotland in the 18th century.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into large cubes, flour them lightly and sear them in butter in batches.','Lift out the meat and soften the thickly sliced onions in the same fat until golden.','Return the beef, add the whole allspice and the bay, and moisten with the stock.','Simmer covered for 2 hours over the gentlest heat: the stew should never come to a rolling boil.','Add the carrot for the last 30 minutes. Serve with steamed potatoes and pickled beetroot.']::text[]),
('fiskesuppe-bergen','en','Bergen fish soup','Bergen, Norway','A white fish soup, thickened with cream and sharpened with vinegar and sugar. That sweet-sour edge, inherited from the Hanseatic League, surprises at first and is sorely missed when removed.',ARRAY['Cut all the vegetables into fine julienne and sweat them in butter without colouring.','Moisten with the stock and cook for 12 minutes: the vegetables should stay crisp.','Beat the yolks and cream, temper with a ladle of hot broth, then thicken the soup off the heat.','Sharpen with vinegar and sweeten: taste and adjust, the balance should be felt without being named.','Slide in the cubed fish and poach for 4 minutes off the heat. Add the prawns and chives at the last moment.']::text[]),
('ploughmans-lunch','en','Ploughman’s lunch','Cheddar, Somerset, England','Bread, cheddar, pickle and a pickled onion, laid on a board with not a single hot dish. The name smells of ploughed fields but dates from the 1950s: the Cheese Bureau invented it to revive pub cheese sales after rationing.',ARRAY['Take the cheddar out two hours ahead: cold, it is brittle and tasteless, and it is the centrepiece.','Choose a farmhouse cheddar aged at least twelve months, with a paste that crumbles rather than cuts.','Break it into large uneven pieces, as much by hand as by knife — tidy slicing has no place here.','Set out on a board the bread, butter, cheese, ham, pickled onions, gherkins and quartered apple.','Assemble nothing in advance: the bread dries and the salad wilts. A brown pickle and a pint of ale finish the plate.']::text[]),
('steak-and-kidney-pie','en','Steak and kidney pie','London, England','A stew of chuck and beef kidney sealed under a crust, moistened with stout. The kidney is not there to bulk it out: it gives the gravy an iron depth the beef alone never reaches, and it needs to be a quarter of the total weight.',ARRAY['Soak the kidney for 30 minutes in milk or vinegared water, then trim the white core fat, which would give a urinous taste.','Flour and sear the diced beef in small batches in a scalding pot: crowded, it would weep instead of browning.','Soften the onion, return the meats, deglaze with stout and moisten with broth, Worcestershire and herbs.','Simmer covered for 2½ hours, then cool completely: a hot filling would melt the puff pastry before it could bake.','Fill the dish, cover with pastry, seal, glaze, cut a vent and bake 35 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('beef-wellington','en','Beef Wellington','London, England','A seared beef fillet lined with mushroom duxelles and cured ham, then sealed in puff pastry. The whole problem is moisture: the duxelles must be dried until it releases nothing more, or the pastry underneath stays raw.',ARRAY['Sear the tied fillet on all sides over fierce heat, 4 minutes in all, brush it with mustard and let it cool.','Chop the mushrooms very finely by knife and dry them out in a dry pan for 25 minutes, stirring: they must lose three-quarters of their volume and stop sizzling.','Lay the cured ham in a rectangle on cling film, cover with duxelles, set the fillet on it and roll very tightly. Chill for an hour.','Wrap this roll in puff pastry, seam underneath, glaze twice twenty minutes apart and chill again for 30 minutes.','Bake 35 minutes at 200°C to 52°C at the centre, then rest 15 minutes before carving. Without that rest, all the juice escapes and soaks the pastry.']::text[]),
('melton-mowbray-pork-pie','en','Melton Mowbray pork pie','Melton Mowbray, Leicestershire, England','A pie of coarsely chopped pork, raised by hand without a tin, its jelly poured through the top hole after baking. The meat is not cured with nitrate: which is why it stays grey rather than pink, unlike every other English pie.',ARRAY['Boil the water with the lard and pour it onto the salted flour: this is hot water crust, and it only works while warm.','Keep a quarter of the dough warm for the lid and raise the rest by hand around a floured jar, pinching the walls upward.','Chop the pork by knife into pea-sized pieces — a mincer would give a paste, and the slice would lose its grain.','Season heavily with salt, white pepper, sage and nutmeg, pack it into the crust, set the lid on and pierce a hole in the centre.','Bake 30 minutes at 200°C then 1½ hours at 160°C. Pour the warm bone jelly through the hole, and set for 24 hours in the cold.']::text[]),
('toad-in-the-hole','en','Toad in the hole','York, Yorkshire, England','Sausages baked in Yorkshire pudding batter that puffs up around them. The name comes from the sausages breaking the surface like toads in a pond; the original 18th-century version used up leftover meat.',ARRAY['Beat the flour, eggs, milk and salt, and rest the batter at least an hour — cold and rested, it rises far more.','Heat the beef dripping in the dish at 220°C until it smokes: this is the decisive point of the recipe.','Brown the sausages separately, then set them in the smoking fat and pour in the batter all at once.','Bake 30 minutes without once opening the door: the least draught of cold air collapses the batter for good.','Meanwhile make a gravy of softened onions, broth and mustard. Serve straight from the oven.']::text[]),
('bangers-and-mash','en','Bangers and mash','London, England','Grilled sausages on mashed potato, flooded with onion gravy. The word banger dates from the Great War: sausages were then so padded out with water that they burst in the pan with a bang.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes whole in their skins in salted water: cut up, they take on water and the mash turns gluey.','Peel them scalding, pass them through a ricer — never a blender, which breaks the starch into glue — and beat in the butter then the hot milk.','Cook the sliced onions gently for 25 minutes until brown and sweet.','Dust with flour, moisten with broth, add the Worcestershire and mustard, and reduce for 10 minutes.','Cook the sausages slowly for 20 minutes, turning often, without pricking them. Set them on the mash and flood with gravy.']::text[]),
('eton-mess','en','Eton mess','Eton, Berkshire, England','Strawberries, whipped cream and broken meringue, tumbled together without order. Served at Eton College during the cricket match against Harrow; a persistent legend says a labrador sat on the pudding, which would explain both the mess and the name.',ARRAY['Whisk the whites to peaks and firm them with the sugar rained in, until the meringue is smooth and glossy.','Bake it 90 minutes at 100°C, then let it dry in the switched-off oven with the door ajar: it must be brittle right through.','Crush a third of the strawberries to a purée with a little icing sugar and the lemon juice, and cut the rest into pieces.','Whip the cream without tightening it: it must stay soft, a stiff cream would make the pudding heavy.','Break the meringue by hand into uneven pieces and fold everything once, barely. Serve within ten minutes, or the meringue dissolves.']::text[]),
('eccles-cake','en','Eccles cake','Eccles, Greater Manchester, England','A small puff-pastry parcel packed with currants, so tightly that the fruit shows through. They are nicknamed dead fly pies; the Puritans banned them in the 17th century as too tied to religious feasts, which only made them more popular.',ARRAY['Melt the butter with the brown sugar, add the currants, orange peel and spices, and cool completely.','A warm filling would melt the pastry as you assemble: that is the one absolute rule of the recipe.','Cut 10 cm discs, set a good spoonful in the centre and gather the edges to the middle like a purse.','Turn the parcel seam down and flatten it gently by hand until the currants show through.','Cut three slashes on top, brush with egg white, dust with sugar and bake 20 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('trifle','en','Trifle','Bristol, England','A pudding built in layers in a glass bowl: sponge soaked in sherry, fruit, custard and whipped cream. The first recipe with sponge dates from 1751; before that a trifle was merely a flavoured cream, and it was the sherry that changed everything.',ARRAY['Make a custard thickened with cornstarch, and cover it in contact so it does not skin as it cools.','Line the base of the bowl with sponge and douse it with sherry: it must be soaked but not sodden, or the layer collapses.','Cover with roughly crushed fruit and a little sugar, without reducing it to a coulis.','Pour on the completely cold custard: warm, it would bleed into the layers and the trifle would lose its clean bands.','Finish with whipped cream and toasted almonds, and set for 6 hours in the cold before serving with a spoon, digging right to the bottom.']::text[]),
('spotted-dick','en','Spotted dick','Cambridge, England','A suet pudding studded with currants, steamed in a cloth and served drowned in custard. The spots are the fruit; as for dick, the word is said to come from dough said in a northern accent — the etymology is unsettled and has raised laughs for two centuries.',ARRAY['Combine the flour, grated suet, sugar, baking powder, salt and zest, then add the currants.','Pour in the milk and bring the dough together without working it: an overworked pudding turns rubbery and heavy.','Roll it into a cylinder in buttered greaseproof paper then in a cloth, leaving a pleat down the middle — the pudding swells by a good third.','Tie both ends and the centre, and steam for 2 hours, topping up with boiling water and never cold.','Turn out, cut thick slices and serve under a hot, pourable custard.']::text[]),
('scotch-pie','en','Scotch pie','Edinburgh, Scotland','A small mutton pie in hot water crust, with straight walls standing proud of a sunken lid. That rim is no ornament: hollow, it holds the mash, beans or gravy, which is what makes it edible standing up in a football ground.',ARRAY['Boil the water and lard and pour them at once onto the salted flour; work with a spoon then by hand as soon as you can.','Raise the warm dough around a floured glass, with straight walls standing 1.5 cm proud: once cold it cracks instead of shaping.','Mix the minced mutton with breadcrumbs, grated onion, broth, nutmeg, mace and plenty of white pepper.','White pepper and mace are what separate a Scotch pie from an English one: do not swap them for black pepper.','Fill, set a lid sunk 1 cm below the rim, pierce it in the centre and bake 45 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('shortbread-ecossais','en','Shortbread','Dundee, Scotland','Three ingredients in the proportion one-two-three: one part sugar, two butter, three flour. Mary Stuart is said to have loved it; the petals of the round mould recall the Twelve Apostles'' skirts, and the biscuit descends from yesterday''s bread dried in the oven.',ARRAY['Rub the cold butter and sugar together with your fingertips, without creaming: aerated butter would make the biscuit rise, which is not wanted.','Add the flours and salt and bring the dough together in only a few movements — the more it is worked, the harder it sets.','The rice flour is not optional: it is what gives the sandy grain that snaps under the tooth.','Press the dough 1.5 cm thick into a tin, prick it with a fork and chill for 30 minutes.','Bake 45 minutes at 150°C: shortbread must stay pale, barely golden at the edges. Sugar and cut it as it comes out, while still soft.']::text[]),
('stornoway-black-pudding','en','Stornoway black pudding','Stornoway, Isle of Lewis, Scotland','A Hebridean pudding with coarse oatmeal and beef suet, protected by a geographical indication since 2013. It stands apart for its higher proportion of oatmeal, which gives it a crumbly rather than a yielding texture.',ARRAY['Whisk the blood as soon as it is collected with a little salt to stop it setting, then pass it through a fine sieve.','Chop the onion very finely and dice the suet small — large pieces would melt away and leave holes.','Mix everything with the coarse oatmeal, salt, add white pepper and let it swell for 30 minutes: the oatmeal drinks part of the blood.','Fill the casings loosely, only half full, because the oatmeal swells further in cooking and would burst the skin.','Poach 1½ hours at 80°C, never boiling, pricking with a needle after 10 minutes. Cut thick and fry in butter.']::text[]),
('stovies','en','Stovies','Aberdeen, Scotland','Potatoes stewed slowly in the fat and juices of yesterday''s roast, until half of them collapse. The word comes from the French étuver: it is the Monday dish, and every Scottish family maintains that theirs is the only true one.',ARRAY['Melt the dripping and soften the thickly sliced onion in it for 15 minutes.','Cut the potatoes into uneven pieces: the small ones will collapse into purée, the large ones hold, and that mixture is what you want.','Add them with yesterday''s meat, diced, season, and pour in only a shallow layer of broth.','Cover and stew for an hour over very low heat, stirring no more than twice: stovies steam, they do not boil.','They are ready when a golden crust forms on the base. Serve with oatcakes and pickled beetroot.']::text[]),
('arbroath-smokie','en','Arbroath smokie','Arbroath, Angus, Scotland','Haddock salted, tied in pairs by the tail and hot-smoked in a buried barrel covered with hessian. The name is protected across Europe: only smoking within eight kilometres of Arbroath may carry it.',ARRAY['Gut and behead the haddock but keep them whole, leaving the tails joined.','Dry-salt them for only 2 hours: long salting would give a hard fish, and a smokie must stay tender.','Rinse and dry overnight in the cold until the skin is dry to the touch — without that pellicle the smoke will not take.','Tie them in pairs by the tail, hang them over a beech fire in a covered barrel, and hot-smoke for 45 minutes: the flesh must cook as it smokes.','Serve warm, opened with a thumb along the backbone, with butter, lemon and bread. There is nothing else to do.']::text[]),
('scottish-tablet','en','Scottish tablet','Stirling, Scotland','A milk sugar that snaps then melts, more crumbly than fudge and softer than hard toffee. The whole difference lies in the beating: the mass is stirred off the heat until it grains, producing minute crystals instead of a smooth paste.',ARRAY['Melt the butter in the milk, add the sugar and stir until fully dissolved before it boils — one undissolved grain crystallises the lot.','Bring to the boil and cook 20 minutes to 118°C, washing the sides down with a wet brush.','Add the condensed milk and continue 5 minutes, stirring constantly: the base catches very quickly.','Off the heat, beat hard with a wooden spoon for 8 minutes, until the mass thickens, loses its shine and sounds like sand.','Pour at once into a buttered tin and score the squares with a blade after 10 minutes, before it sets hard.']::text[]),
('clootie-dumpling','en','Clootie dumpling','Inverness, Scotland','A dried-fruit pudding boiled for four hours in a cloth — the cloot — which gives it its characteristic brown skin. A coin and a thimble were once hidden in it to tell the children''s fortunes; it is eaten hot at Christmas, and cold, sliced and fried, for breakfast.',ARRAY['Scald the cloth, wring it out and flour it generously: that flour is what will form the pudding''s skin as it cooks.','Mix all the dry ingredients, add the grated suet, the fruit, treacle, egg and buttermilk for a soft batter.','Set it in the centre of the cloth, gather it into a purse leaving slack, and tie: the pudding swells by a quarter.','Boil for 4 hours in a large pot, on an upturned saucer so the cloth does not touch the base, topping up with boiling water.','Turn out and dry for 20 minutes in a 150°C oven to firm the skin. Serve with custard.']::text[]),
('rumbledethumps','en','Rumbledethumps','Melrose, Scottish Borders, Scotland','A gratin of mashed potato, cabbage and onion under cheddar. The name says the action: rumble, to stir, and thump, to bash — the Borders have their version of what Ireland calls colcannon and England bubble and squeak.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them hot and crush them roughly with a fork.','Fry the finely shredded cabbage and onion in butter for 12 minutes: they must stay crisp, boiled cabbage would weep into the gratin.','Combine everything with the remaining butter, the milk, chives, salt and plenty of pepper, without aiming for smoothness.','Turn into a buttered dish and rough up the surface with a fork, leaving peaks — they are what will brown.','Cover with grated cheddar and bake 30 minutes at 190°C, until the top is brown and blistered.']::text[]),
('aberdeen-rowie','en','Aberdeen rowie','Aberdeen, Scotland','A flat, salty, greasy lard-laminated roll, the croissant''s brutal cousin. It was devised for North Sea fishermen: its fat and salt content keeps it for weeks at sea without going rancid or stale.',ARRAY['Knead a plain bread dough with the flour, yeast, water, sugar and salt, and prove it for an hour.','Cream the lard and butter together and divide into three parts.','Roll out the dough, dot it with a third of the fat in small nuggets, fold in three and rest 40 minutes in the cold.','Repeat twice: a rowie is not laminated with a sheet of butter like a croissant, but with fat dabbed on, which gives its irregular flaking.','Cut squares, flatten them by hand without rolling, prove 45 minutes and bake 20 minutes at 200°C. They should be swimming in their own fat halfway through.']::text[]),
('glamorgan-sausage','en','Glamorgan sausage','Cardiff, Wales','A meatless sausage of Caerphilly cheese, leek and breadcrumbs, crumbed and fried. It is recorded from 1850, long before vegetarianism was an idea: it was simply the sausage for days when there was no pig.',ARRAY['Slice the leek very finely and sweat it for 8 minutes without colour, then cool it completely.','Mix it with the grated cheese, 150 g of the breadcrumbs, the herbs, mustard, two egg yolks and the milk.','Salt lightly: Caerphilly already is, and an oversalted sausage cannot be rescued.','Shape finger-sized rolls and chill them for an hour — warm, they collapse in the pan.','Dip them in beaten egg white then in the remaining crumbs, and fry 8 minutes in oil, turning on every side.']::text[]),
('welsh-cakes','en','Welsh cakes','Pontypridd, Wales','Currant cakes cooked on a cast-iron bakestone, neither baked nor fried. Miners'' wives slipped them into lunch tins: they are eaten cold, survive a day in a pocket, and need no oven — which mattered in the terraces.',ARRAY['Rub the cold butter into the flour, baking powder, salt and nutmeg, to coarse crumbs.','Add the sugar and currants, then the egg and just enough milk to bring the dough together.','Roll to 8 mm and cut 6 cm rounds: thicker, the centre stays raw; thinner, the cake dries out.','Heat a cast-iron bakestone with no fat and cook 3 minutes a side over medium-low heat.','They should be light brown and still soft in the middle. Sugar them warm and cool on a rack.']::text[]),
('faggots-gallois','en','Faggots','Merthyr Tydfil, Wales','Balls of pork liver and belly wrapped in caul fat, baked in their own gravy. The dish of the Welsh mining valleys: the caul melts away entirely and is there only to hold the ball together for the first half hour.',ARRAY['Soak the caul for 20 minutes in warm vinegared water to relax it: dry, it tears at the slightest handling.','Chop the liver and belly together, neither too fine nor too coarse, and mix with the sweated onion, breadcrumbs, sage and mace.','Shape balls the size of a tennis ball and wrap each in a square of caul, tucking it underneath.','Pack them tightly in a dish — they hold each other up — and bake 20 minutes at 200°C to colour them.','Pour over the flour-thickened broth, drop to 170°C and give them 40 minutes more. Serve with mushy peas.']::text[]),
('teisen-lap','en','Teisen lap','Aberdare, Wales','A flat, moist, dense currant cake baked on a plate rather than in a tin. Teisen lap means hand cake: flat, it fitted in a miner''s hand and his tin, and its shallowness kept it from being crushed.',ARRAY['Rub the butter into the flour, baking powder, salt and nutmeg, then add the sugar and currants.','Beat the eggs with the milk and pour them in at once: the batter must be far looser than a cake batter, almost pourable.','That wetness is what makes a teisen lap: too firm and it becomes an ordinary fruit cake and dries out in a day.','Pour into a buttered pie plate, only 3 cm deep, and smooth the top.','Bake 35 minutes at 180°C until the top is golden and the centre still yielding. Eaten warm or cold, with nothing.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('ulster-fry','en','Ulster fry','Belfast, Northern Ireland','The Ulster fried breakfast, set apart from the English one by its two griddle breads: the soda farl and the potato bread. No beans, no mushrooms — the breads take their place, and that is what makes it Northern Irish.',ARRAY['For the potato bread, mash hot potatoes with 60 g of the flour and melted butter, roll to 1 cm and cut triangles.','For the soda farl, mix the remaining flour, buttermilk, bicarbonate and salt without kneading, flatten into a round and quarter it.','Cook both breads on a dry griddle, 5 minutes a side: they are griddled and never baked, and that is the whole difference.','Fry the bacon, sausages and black pudding, and keep the rendered fat in the pan.','Fry the breads in that fat until they crisp, then fry the eggs and serve everything together, without delay.']::text[]),
('champ','en','Champ','Antrim, Northern Ireland','Mashed potato with spring onion and milk, hollowed into a well where a knob of butter melts. You eat from the outside in, dipping each spoonful in the butter at the centre; unlike colcannon, no cabbage or kale goes in.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes whole in their skins, in generously salted water.','Slice the spring onions, whites and greens apart, and infuse them 10 minutes in hot milk without boiling.','That infusion is the whole dish: thrown in raw, the onions stay sharp and sit apart instead of scenting it.','Peel the potatoes scalding, rice them and beat in the infused milk with its onions, then half the butter.','Mound it up, hollow a well in the centre with a spoon and drop in the remaining butter. Serve before it melts away.']::text[]),
('fifteens','en','Fifteens','Lisburn, Northern Ireland','A no-bake roll whose recipe is its name: fifteen digestive biscuits, fifteen marshmallows, fifteen glacé cherries. Condensed milk binds it, coconut coats it, and the oven is never lit.',ARRAY['Crush the biscuits in a bag with a rolling pin, to coarse crumbs with a few larger shards.','Quarter the marshmallows and cherries; dust the knife with coconut so the marshmallows do not stick.','Mix everything with the condensed milk: the mass should be sticky but hold together in a ball.','Roll it into a log in cling film, tightly, then roll the log in coconut until entirely coated.','Chill at least 4 hours, then cut one-centimetre slices, wiping the knife between cuts.']::text[]),
('yellowman','en','Yellowman','Ballycastle, County Antrim, Northern Ireland','A brittle, honeycombed toffee sold at Ballycastle''s Ould Lammas Fair since 1606, eaten there with dried dulse. Bicarbonate thrown into the boiling sugar makes it foam at once: that surge is what makes the honeycomb.',ARRAY['Butter a deep tray very generously and keep it within reach: what follows leaves you no more than three seconds.','Melt the butter, syrup, sugar, water and vinegar over low heat, without stirring once the sugar has dissolved.','Take it to 149°C, hard crack: lower and the yellowman stays tacky; higher and it turns bitter.','Off the heat, throw in the bicarbonate all at once and whisk for two seconds only — the mass triples instantly and overflows a small pan.','Pour without spreading and let it cool untouched: smoothing it would collapse the bubbles. Break it with a hammer when cold.']::text[]),
('dublin-bay-prawns','en','Dublin Bay prawns','Howth, Dublin, Ireland','Langoustines grilled under garlic and parsley butter, dredged from the mud of the Irish Sea. The name comes from the trawlers that landed them at Dublin; elsewhere they are scampi, but it is the same Nephrops norvegicus.',ARRAY['Buy them alive and cook them the same day: a dead langoustine degrades within hours and turns woolly.','Split them lengthwise along the back, and lift out the dark gut with a knife point.','Cream the butter with the crushed garlic, parsley, lemon zest, a pinch of cayenne and the white wine.','Set a hazelnut of it in each half and give them 5 minutes under a fierce grill, flesh upward.','They are done the moment the flesh turns opaque: a minute more and it shrinks. Serve with bread and the butter from the dish.']::text[]),
('barmbrack','en','Barmbrack','Dublin, Ireland','A yeasted fruit loaf whose fruit is steeped in tea, served at Halloween with objects hidden in the crumb. A ring meant marriage within the year, a pea poverty, a rag a bad match: the custom survives, with the ring alone.',ARRAY['Brew a very strong black tea and pour it scalding over the dried fruit; let it swell overnight.','Drain them the next day keeping the liquid, warm it and dissolve the yeast in it.','Knead the flour, sugar, spices, salt, butter and egg with that yeasted tea for 12 minutes.','Fold in the fruit at the end: added too early, its sugar would hinder the rise.','Prove 1½ hours, shape a round loaf, prove 45 minutes and bake 50 minutes at 180°C. Sliced thin and buttered.']::text[]),
('drisheen','en','Drisheen','Cork, Ireland','A Cork pudding of sheep''s blood and milk, with no cereal, whose texture is nearer a custard than a sausage. It was sold by the cup at the English Market; it is eaten with tripe, and the flesh stays almost pale under a grey skin.',ARRAY['Whisk the blood as it comes with salt and pass it twice through a fine sieve: the least fibre would spoil the smooth set.','Mix it with warm milk at a ratio of one to two, season, and scent with tansy.','Fill the casings very loosely, or simply pour into ramekins: drisheen needs no skin, it sets by itself.','Poach 45 minutes at 75°C — any hotter and the blood splits irrecoverably and weeps.','Cool, cut thick slices and fry in butter. In Cork it is served with tripe stewed in milk and onion.']::text[]),
('waterford-blaa','en','Waterford blaa','Waterford, Ireland','A very floury white roll with a soft, pale crust, protected by a geographical indication since 2013. It is said to come from French Huguenots settled in Waterford in the 17th century; the name from blanc, and it is eaten within four hours.',ARRAY['Knead a soft, fairly wet dough for 12 minutes, and prove it for 1½ hours.','Divide into 80 g pieces and shape them loosely: a blaa must stay slack to keep its cottony crumb.','Set them on the tray almost touching: that contact is what stops a crust forming on the sides.','Dust them generously through a sieve, twice, before and after the second 45-minute prove.','Bake 18 minutes at 220°C. They must stay white: a browned blaa is no longer a blaa, and it is eaten the same day.']::text[]),
('irish-apple-cake','en','Irish apple cake','Limerick, Ireland','A dense cake of sharp apples under a crumble top, served hot with custard. It was once made in a cast-iron pot hung over the turf fire, with embers set on the lid to cook the top.',ARRAY['Take 80 g of the flour, 60 g of the butter and 60 g of the sugar and rub them to crumbs for the topping; chill.','Cream the remaining butter and sugar, add the eggs one at a time, then the flour, baking powder, cinnamon and milk.','Peel the apples and cut them into large wedges, never slices: big pieces keep their sharpness at the heart of the cake.','Fold them into the batter, turn into a tin, cover with the crumble and scatter sugar.','Bake 50 minutes at 180°C. Serve hot, under a custard made with the milk, yolks and vanilla.']::text[]),
('irish-coffee','en','Irish coffee','Foynes, County Limerick, Ireland','Coffee, whiskey, sugar and a cream that floats without mixing. Joe Sheridan invented it in 1943 to warm flying-boat passengers stranded at Foynes; asked whether it was Brazilian coffee, he answered that it was Irish coffee.',ARRAY['Whip the cream very lightly, just until it coats the spoon: whipped stiff, it collapses in lumps instead of floating.','Scald the stemmed glasses and empty them: a cold glass cracks and drops the cream.','Pour the whiskey and sugar into the base and stir until fully dissolved — it is the sugar that, by making the coffee denser, lets the cream hold.','Add the scalding coffee and stir once more.','Pour the cream gently over the back of a spoon held at the surface. Do not stir again: the hot coffee is drunk through the cold cream.']::text[]),
('hakarl','en','Hákarl','Bjarnarhöfn, Snæfellsnes, Iceland','Greenland shark fermented for three months then dried for four, served in cubes with a glass of brennivín. The fresh flesh is toxic — the shark has no kidneys and excretes urea through its skin — and fermentation alone makes it edible.',ARRAY['Cut the flesh into large blocks and bury it in a pit of gravel and sand, covered with stones.','The weight of the stones presses the fluids out of the flesh: it is the expulsion of urea and trimethylamine oxide, not cold or salt, that detoxifies the shark.','Ferment for six to twelve weeks depending on the season, then dig out the blocks and rinse them.','Hang them for four months in a shed open to the wind, until a brown crust forms.','Trim the crust, cut the pale flesh into one-centimetre cubes and serve on cocktail sticks, with rye bread. It is not cooked: it is prepared over seven months and eaten as it is.']::text[]),
('rugbraud','en','Rúgbrauð','Laugarvatn, Iceland','A black, sweet rye bread baked for twenty-four hours buried in the hot sand of a geothermal spring. It is called hverabrauð, spring bread; with no oven to watch, you mark the spot with a stake and come back the next day.',ARRAY['Mix the flours, raising agents and salt, then add the milk, syrup and brown sugar: the batter should pour, almost like a cake mix.','Pour into a greased loaf tin or metal can, filling only two-thirds.','Seal it airtight: it is steaming in a closed vessel, with no evaporation, that gives the moist crumb and the black colour.','Bury the container 30 cm down in geothermal sand at 90°C — or failing that, bake covered in a bain-marie for 12 hours at 100°C.','Turn out the next day and let it cool. Sliced very thin, with salted butter and herring or smoked trout.']::text[]),
('hardfiskur','en','Harðfiskur','Bolungarvík, Westfjords, Iceland','Haddock dried in the wind, without salt or smoke, torn by hand and buttered. It was medieval Iceland''s currency: rents and tithes were paid in dried fish, and a man lived on it all winter.',ARRAY['Gut and behead the fish, then split them along the backbone leaving the two fillets joined at the tail.','Do not salt: harðfiskur is one of the few dried fish in the world that is not, and salt would stop the wind doing its work.','Hang the pairs astride poles in a shed open to every wind, between 0 and 5°C.','Dry for four to six weeks, sheltered from rain and sun: above 8°C the fish spoils before it dries.','It is ready when it snaps cleanly and tears into strips. Eaten as it is, spread with butter — never rehydrated.']::text[]),
('svid','en','Svið','Reykjavik, Iceland','Half a sheep''s head singed, boiled and served whole, eye and cheek included. A dish of the þorrablót, the midwinter feast: on an island without timber or grain, no one could afford to throw away an animal''s head.',ARRAY['Singe the heads with a blowtorch to burn off the wool, then scrape them with a knife and rinse in cold water.','Split them lengthwise, remove the brain, and soak them 4 hours in cold salted water.','Poach them 1½ hours at a bare tremble with the salt and bay, skimming for the first few minutes.','A boiling broth would split the skin and loosen the cheek, which is the best part.','Serve hot with mashed swede and potatoes. What is left is pressed into a terrine and eaten cold, as sviðasulta.']::text[]),
('slatur','en','Slátur','Akureyri, Iceland','Two puddings sewn into sheep''s stomach: blóðmör with blood and lifrarpylsa with liver. Families make them together in autumn at slaughter time; what is not eaten at once goes into sour whey, where it keeps a year.',ARRAY['Cut the stomach into squares, fold them into pouches and sew two sides with linen thread.','For blóðmör, mix the sieved blood, diced suet, rye, oats and salt; for lifrarpylsa, replace the blood with liver blended with milk.','Fill the pouches only two-thirds — the cereals swell a great deal and would burst the seam.','Sew the last side and poach 2½ hours at 85°C, without boiling, pricking each pudding after 20 minutes.','Serve hot with potatoes and swede. The rest keeps in sour whey, where it becomes súrsað.']::text[]),
('sursadir-hrutspungar','en','Soured ram’s testicles','Vestmannaeyjar, Iceland','Ram''s testicles boiled, pressed into a terrine then kept in sour whey. One of the pieces on the þorramatur platter; the whey, the only preservative on an island without salt or spice, acidifies without ever salting.',ARRAY['Remove the outer membrane and soak the pieces 6 hours in cold water, changing it three times.','Poach them 2 hours at a simmer with the bay, until a needle passes through without resistance.','Pack them tightly into a terrine and press under a weight for 24 hours: pressing is what welds them into a sliceable block.','Submerge the block completely in sour whey and let it acidify two to three months in the cold.','The whey must always cover it: a piece that breaks the surface moulds. Serve in thin slices on buttered rye bread.']::text[]),
('laufabraud','en','Laufabrauð','Akureyri, Iceland','Discs of dough as thin as a leaf, cut with geometric patterns and fried, served at Christmas in northern Iceland. Flour was so scarce that it was stretched to the limit: the thinness was not a feat, it was a necessity.',ARRAY['Bring the milk to a tremble with the butter and pour it scalding onto the flour, sugar, baking powder, cardamom and salt.','Knead hard for 10 minutes: the dough must be very firm, almost hard — a soft dough will not roll thin enough.','Divide into balls and roll each until you can see the worktop grain through it, then cut a 20 cm disc.','Cut the patterns with a knife or a laufabrauðsjárn, lifting each tab to one side: that lifting is what makes the raised design.','Fry 20 seconds a side in mutton fat at 190°C, then press flat under a board as they cool.']::text[]),
('vinarterta','en','Vínarterta','Reykjavik, Iceland','A cake of five to seven layers of dry biscuit separated by cardamom prune compote. It has nearly vanished from Iceland but survives untouched in Manitoba, where emigrants took it around 1875: Canadian Icelanders still make it as they did in the 19th century.',ARRAY['Simmer the stoned prunes in water for 30 minutes, crush them with 100 g of the sugar, the cardamom and cinnamon, and cool: the compote must be thick, almost a paste.','Cream the butter and remaining sugar, add the eggs, milk, then the flour and baking powder for a firm dough.','Divide into five equal parts, roll each into a 22 cm disc and bake 10 minutes at 180°C. They must stay pale and dry.','Stack the discs, spreading compote between them and pressing lightly at each layer.','Wrap the cake and let it rest a week before slicing: the dry layers must drink the moisture from the compote, or the cake shatters under the knife.']::text[]),
('kleinur','en','Kleinur','Reykjavik, Iceland','Cardamom dough diamonds, twisted by passing one tip through a slit, and fried in mutton fat. They exist across Scandinavia, but Iceland makes them with soured milk and fries them in tallow, which gives a flavour butter does not replace.',ARRAY['Rub the butter into the flour, sugar, raising agents, cardamom and salt, then bind with the eggs and buttermilk.','Rest the dough an hour in the cold: that rest is what stops kleinur puffing into round doughnuts.','Roll to 5 mm, cut 8 cm diamonds and slit each in the centre for two centimetres.','Pass one tip of the diamond through the slit and pull gently: the twist must hold by itself, without being tight.','Fry 90 seconds a side at 180°C in mutton fat. They should be dark brown — pale, they are raw inside.']::text[]),
('pylsur','en','Pylsur','Reykjavik, Iceland','The Icelandic hot dog, made with lamb sausage, served with fried onion under the sausage and raw onion on top. You order it eina með öllu, one with everything; the order of the toppings is not negotiable, and the crisp onion goes at the bottom of the bun.',ARRAY['Slice one onion thinly, flour it and fry at 170°C until brown; drain on paper.','Chop the other two onions raw into very small dice and keep them cold.','Poach the sausages 7 minutes in barely simmering water — never boiling, which would burst the skin — and steam the buns above them.','Spread the crisp onion in the base of the bun first, then lay the sausage on it: that way it stays crisp instead of going soggy.','Finish with sweet ketchup, sweet brown mustard, gherkin remoulade and raw onion. Eaten standing, within the minute.']::text[]),
('escudella-andorrana','en','Andorran escudella','Andorra la Vella, Andorra','The pot-au-feu of the Catalan Pyrenees, served in two acts: first the broth with its pasta, then the meats and vegetables on a platter. At the centre sits the pilota, a large meatball each family seasons its own way and which is only divided at the last moment.',ARRAY['Soak the chickpeas overnight in warm salted water, and cook them in a separate net: otherwise they would be lost in the pot.','Start the meats in cold water and bring them up slowly, skimming without pause for the first fifteen minutes — that is when, and only when, the broth turns clear.','Let it tremble for 2 hours with the carrot and celery, never boiling: an agitated broth clouds and cannot be recovered.','Shape the pilota from the minced pork, eggs, breadcrumbs, garlic, parsley and a pinch of cinnamon; roll it into a large oval, flour it and lower it gently into the broth for the last hour.','Add the cabbage and potatoes at the end. Serve the soup first, with the pasta cooked in it, then the carn d''olla on a platter, the pilota sliced at the centre.']::text[]),
('trinxat-de-la-cerdanya','en','Cerdanya trinxat','Pas de la Casa, Encamp, Andorra','A crush of winter cabbage and potato, fried in bacon fat until it takes a crust. Trinxar means to chop: only curly kale picked after the first frosts is used, which loses its bitterness and turns sweet.',ARRAY['Boil the roughly cut cabbage and the potatoes in large pieces together in salted water for 25 minutes.','Drain them at length and press them against the colander: trinxat always fails for the same reason, too much water.','Crush them with a fork along with the pounded garlic — roughly, recognisable pieces must remain, never a smooth purée.','Fry the diced bacon in the lard until crisp, lift it out and tip the crush into the hot fat.','Press it into a cake and let it set for 8 minutes untouched, then flip it in one movement using a plate. Crown it with the crisp bacon.']::text[]),
('conill-amb-tomaquet','en','Rabbit with tomato','Sant Julià de Lòria, Andorra','Hutch rabbit stewed in a long-cooked tomato sofregit, with an almond and liver picada to bind the sauce. The picada is the Catalan gesture par excellence: it thickens not by flour but by pounding, and always goes in during the last ten minutes.',ARRAY['Joint the rabbit, keep the liver aside, and brown it all over in olive oil before lifting it out.','Make the sofregit in the same oil: grated onion for 20 minutes, then grated tomato for 25 more, until no water is left and the mass is brick-red and glossy.','A hurried sofregit stays acidic: it is the one step that cannot be shortened.','Return the rabbit, deglaze with white wine, add the herbs and moisten to cover; simmer covered for an hour.','Pound the garlic, almonds, hazelnuts, fried bread, sautéed liver and chocolate in a mortar, slake with a little sauce and stir it in for the last ten minutes.']::text[]),
('xai-rostat-de-muntanya','en','Mountain roast lamb','Arinsal, La Massana, Andorra','A quarter of valley lamb roasted slowly on a bed of potatoes, with white wine and thyme from the Coma Pedrosa. Andorran lamb summers at fifteen hundred metres on aromatic plants: its fat keeps the taste, which is why so little seasoning goes on it.',ARRAY['Rub the lamb with lard, salt and crushed garlic twelve hours ahead, and take it out of the cold an hour before it goes in the oven.','Line an earthenware dish with onion and thickly sliced potato, moisten with oil and set the meat on top.','Roast 20 minutes at 230°C to seize the crust, then drop to 160°C.','Baste every twenty minutes with the juices and the white wine poured down the side of the dish, never over the meat — direct basting softens the crust you have just made.','Allow 2 hours in all and rest it 15 minutes out of the oven before carving. The potatoes will have drunk every drop.']::text[]),
('truites-a-l-andorrana','en','Andorran-style trout','Ordino, Andorra','Valira trout stuffed with bacon and pan-fried, browned in nut butter and garlic. The river comes down from the Ordino cirques in freezing water: trout grow slowly there, and the firm flesh needs neither marinade nor sauce.',ARRAY['Gut the trout without scaling them — river trout barely have scales — and wipe the cavity without rinsing it.','Salt the inside, slip in two slices of bacon and a sprig of parsley, and close with a wooden pick.','Flour them lightly, tapping off the excess: thick flour makes a shell instead of a crisp skin.','Fry 4 minutes a side in hot oil, then lift them out and discard the oil.','Foam the butter with sliced garlic and flaked almonds until it smells of hazelnuts, pour over the trout and squeeze lemon over.']::text[]),
('canelons-a-l-andorrana','en','Andorran cannelloni','Santa Coloma, Andorra la Vella, Andorra','Cannelloni filled with the leftovers of the Christmas roast, covered in béchamel and gratinated. They are eaten on 26 December, Sant Esteve: the filling is not made for them, it is what is left from the day before, and that is the whole logic of the dish.',ARRAY['Mince yesterday''s cooked meats with the sautéed livers, twice through the mincer: the filling must be fine and even.','Sweat the onion, add the filling, flame with brandy and moisten with a ladle of béchamel to bind it.','Cook the sheets or tubes in salted water keeping them very firm, and spread them on a damp cloth without overlapping.','Fill and roll tightly, then lay the canelons side by side, seam down, in a buttered dish.','Coat with béchamel until they are entirely covered — a cannelloni that breaks the surface dries and hardens — scatter cheese and gratinate 25 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('coca-massegada','en','Coca massegada','Andorra la Vella, Andorra','A flat coca with lard and anise, kneaded at length then stretched by hand and covered with sugar and pine nuts. Massegar means to knead without end: it is the working of the dough, not a leaven, that gives it its tight crumb and long keeping.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm water and let it foam, then knead it with the flour, two eggs, 80 g of the sugar, the zest, anisette and salt.','Add the soft lard in small pieces and knead a good twenty minutes, until the dough turns smooth and elastic under the hand.','That long kneading is what makes a coca: skimp it and you get a crumbly cake instead of a dough that tears into strands.','Prove for an hour, then stretch by hand into a rectangle barely a centimetre thick, with no rolling pin, pulling from the centre.','Glaze with egg, cover with sugar and pine nuts, prove 30 minutes and bake 25 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('bringuera-andorrana','en','Bringuera','Canillo, Andorra','An Andorran sausage of pork liver and lung, dried a few weeks then poached or grilled. It is born of the matança, the December killing: offal does not keep, so it is cased at once, and the bringuera is eaten before the noble charcuterie.',ARRAY['Poach the liver and lung 20 minutes in salted water to firm them: raw, they clog the mincer and give a mush.','Mince them with the belly on the medium plate, and mix with crushed garlic, paprika, pepper, salt and white wine.','Rest the mixture 12 hours in the cold so the salt penetrates, and taste a fried patty before casing.','Fill the casings loosely, tie into horseshoes and prick out the air bubbles with a needle.','Dry for three weeks at 12°C, then poach 40 minutes at 80°C or grill over coals. Serve with cabbage and potatoes.']::text[]),
('donja-llonganissa-dolca','en','Donja','Escaldes-Engordany, Andorra','A sweet Andorran longaniza made with mountain honey, dried two months and eaten in thin slices. The sugar is no whim: in a valley without sea salt, honey helped preserve as much as it flavoured, and donja is served at dessert as readily as before a meal.',ARRAY['Cut the meat and fat by knife into half-centimetre dice, never by mincer: a donja is known by the visible grain of its slice.','Mix them with salt, cracked pepper, cinnamon, crushed garlic, white wine and lightly warmed honey.','Macerate 24 hours in the cold, stirring twice: the honey must be entirely absorbed, or it weeps during drying.','Fill thin casings firmly, tie every twenty centimetres and prick out every air pocket carefully.','Dry two months at 12°C and 75% humidity, until it has lost a third of its weight. White bloom on the surface is normal; green mould is brushed off with vinegar.']::text[]),
('bull-negre-andorra','en','Bull negre','Anyós, La Massana, Andorra','A fat blood pudding cased in the large intestine, cut in wide slices and fried. Bull comes from boiled: unlike dry puddings it does not keep, and is eaten within the week of the matança, with mountain beans.',ARRAY['Poach the pig''s head 2 hours, bone it while hot and dice the meat and rinds small.','Melt the onion in the rendered fat for 40 minutes over very low heat, until pale and dry.','Combine the sieved blood, the meat, the onion and the spices; the mass should be loose enough to fall slowly from the ladle.','Case it in the large intestine, filling only two-thirds, and tie both ends firmly.','Poach 1½ hours at 80°C, pricking with a needle after 15 minutes; a prick that runs clear means it is done. Cut thick and fry.']::text[]),
('bisbe-blanc','en','Bisbe blanc','Llorts, Ordino, Andorra','The great white pudding of the Andorran matança, filled with tongue, rinds and lean meat, without a drop of blood. It is called a bishop for its size and whiteness; it was kept for the meal that closed the killing, and is cut in centimetre slices.',ARRAY['Poach the tongues for an hour, peel them hot — the skin comes away in one piece while they are scalding — and cut them into large dice.','Cook the rinds an hour until tender, and chop them coarsely with the shoulder and fat.','Mix everything with the salt, cracked pepper, nutmeg, garlic and white wine, and rest overnight.','Fill the stomach three-quarters full and sew it with close stitches, expelling the air as you go.','Poach 3 hours at 80°C, then press under a board for 24 hours in the cold: pressing is what makes the slice compact and even.']::text[]),
('formatge-de-tupi','en','Tupí cheese','Vall de Sorteny, Ordino, Andorra','Leftover ewe''s cheeses crushed, doused with brandy and left to ferment for months in an earthenware pot. The tupí is the pot itself: it is never emptied, more cheese goes on top, and some family pots have been running for generations.',ARRAY['Take dry, hard ewe''s cheeses, the ones nobody wants any more: tupí is made precisely for them.','Cut off the rind, grate them coarsely and pound them with the garlic and pepper to a lumpy paste.','Pack into a stoneware pot, expelling air at each layer, and cover with a finger of brandy.','The brandy must always sit on top: it is what keeps mould off while fermentation works underneath.','Cover with a cloth and a loose lid, and leave in the cellar two to four months. Stir weekly. Spread on toast, in very small quantities.']::text[]),
('borra-andorrana','en','Borra','Soldeu, Canillo, Andorra','A thick soup of toasted flour and milk, with an egg broken into it at the last moment. It was the shepherds'' breakfast before going up to the summer pastures: it costs nothing, takes a quarter of an hour and holds a body until evening.',ARRAY['Melt the lard and throw in the flour; stir constantly over low heat until it turns hazelnut brown and smells of toast.','That toasting takes a good ten minutes: it is where all the flavour of borra comes from, white flour gives only a bland paste.','Pour in the hot milk in a stream, whisking hard, so that not a single lump forms.','Let it thicken for 20 minutes over very low heat, stirring often — the base catches in an instant.','Break the eggs into the simmering soup and let them set for 3 minutes without stirring. Sugar and cinnamon, or salt, depending on the hour.']::text[]),
('cargols-a-la-llauna','en','Cargols a la llauna','Aixirivall, Sant Julià de Lòria, Andorra','Snails grilled dry on a sheet of tin, mouth upward, and dipped in aioli. The llauna is the tin itself: set over coals, it heats fiercely enough for the snail to cook in its own juice without a drop of fat.',ARRAY['Fast the snails a week in an airy box, then purge them 2 hours in coarse salt and rinse until the water runs clear.','Set them on the tin mouth upward, packed tight, and give each a pinch of coarse salt.','Put the tin over the coals: they froth for a few minutes, then the froth dries and the snail retracts — the sign they are half done.','Allow 20 minutes in all and baste with nothing: any fat would drown the flavour and make the tin smoke.','Meanwhile make an aioli with pounded garlic and oil trickled in. Serve the snails scalding, on picks, with the aioli and bread.']::text[]),
('torro-d-andorra','en','Andorran torró','Andorra la Vella, Andorra','A nougat of mountain honey and hazelnuts, darker and rougher than the Alicante kind. Andorran pedlars sold it across Catalonia at Christmas; the heather honey of the high valleys gives it a bitterness sugar alone never reaches.',ARRAY['Roast the hazelnuts 12 minutes at 160°C and rub them in a cloth to remove the skins: one skin left makes the nougat bitter in patches.','Cook the honey alone for 20 minutes over low heat to drive off its water, then add the sugar and take it to 140°C.','Whisk the whites to firm peaks and pour in the scalding honey in a thin stream, whisking without pause: poured at once it would scramble them.','Keep beating 15 minutes over a bain-marie, until a drop thrown into cold water hardens immediately.','Fold in the warm nuts, pour between two sheets of rice paper and press under a weight for 24 hours before cutting.']::text[]),
('mato-amb-mel','en','Mató with honey','Encamp, Andorra','A fresh unsalted curd cheese, served drowned in mountain honey and walnuts. Mató is never salted and never aged: it is eaten within two days, and its complete lack of salt is what leaves all the room to the honey.',ARRAY['Heat the raw milk to exactly 35°C: any hotter and the rennet works too fast and the curd turns rubbery.','Add the rennet, stir for ten seconds then leave it absolutely still for 45 minutes, never touching the vessel — the least jolt breaks the forming curd.','Cut the curd in a 2 cm grid and let it firm up for 15 minutes.','Lift it out with a skimmer into moulds; drain for 2 hours, without pressing.','Turn out, pour honey over generously and scatter walnut halves. Do not salt under any circumstances.']::text[]),
('coques-d-oli-i-anis','en','Oil and anise coques','Pal, La Massana, Andorra','Thin sweet biscuits of olive oil and anise, baked without leaven and crisp as glass. They were made for the village patron saints'' days: they travel unbroken in a cloth and keep for a whole month.',ARRAY['Warm the olive oil with the anise seeds and zest, and infuse for an hour before straining.','Mix it with the flour, 80 g of the sugar, the anisette, white wine and salt, and bring the dough together without kneading.','Rest it for 30 minutes: relaxed, it rolls twice as thin.','Roll to 2 mm and cut ovals by hand rather than with a cutter, to keep uneven edges.','Sprinkle with the remaining sugar and bake 12 minutes at 190°C. Take them out as soon as the edges go gold: a browned coca is bitter.']::text[]),
('civet-d-isard','en','Pyrenean chamois civet','Vall d’Incles, Canillo, Andorra','Pyrenean chamois marinated three days in red wine then long stewed, the sauce bound with blood. The animal lives above two thousand metres and its flesh is lean and sinewy: without the marinade and the bacon that feeds it, it would stay dry.',ARRAY['Marinate the meat in large pieces for three days in the cold, in the wine, vegetables, juniper and herbs, turning it daily.','Drain and pat the meat thoroughly dry: wet meat boils instead of searing, and the civet loses its dark colour.','Sear it in small batches, dust with flour, return the strained marinade and the vegetables, and simmer covered for 3 hours.','Collect the blood mixed with vinegar — the vinegar stops it setting — and add it only at the very last moment, off the heat.','The blood must never boil, or the sauce grains irrecoverably. Add the chocolate, stir and serve at once.']::text[]),
('truita-de-riu-amb-pernil','en','Trout with mountain ham','Sornàs, Ordino, Andorra','A trout wrapped in a slice of dry-cured ham and pan-fried, with no other seasoning. The ham does the work of fat, salt and crust all at once: the dish of valleys where fish and pig were the only two reliable resources of the year.',ARRAY['Gut the trout and wipe them; on no account salt them, the ham does that and a double salting would be inedible.','Slip a crushed garlic clove and a sprig of parsley into each belly.','Wind two slices of ham in a spiral around each fish, overlapping slightly.','Fry in hot oil 5 minutes a side, starting on the seam of the ham so that it seals as it cooks.','The ham should be crisp and the flesh just opaque at the bone. Pepper, squeeze lemon and serve at once.']::text[]),
('bunyols-de-quaresma','en','Lenten bunyols','Escaldes-Engordany, Andorra','Small yeasted fritters with anise and zest, fried and rolled in sugar, eaten through Lent. They are lean in the religious sense: no butter, no lard, only oil — and that is precisely why they were permitted.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm milk and let it foam for a quarter of an hour before adding anything else.','Knead with the flour, eggs, 60 g of the sugar, the zests, anisette, olive oil and salt for 12 minutes.','Prove for 1½ hours: the dough should be soft and sticky, worked with oiled hands and never floured ones.','Form rings by piercing each ball with an oiled finger and let them rise 30 minutes on a cloth.','Fry 2 minutes a side at 170°C — no hotter, or they brown before the centre cooks. Roll them warm in the remaining sugar.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('vi-calent-de-muntanya','en','Mountain mulled wine','El Tarter, Canillo, Andorra','Red wine warmed with honey, cinnamon and orange peel, served in the refuges after the descent. The whole difficulty is temperature: above 78°C the alcohol evaporates and nothing is left but a warm, characterless syrup.',ARRAY['Pare the orange and lemon zest with a peeler, avoiding the white pith which would bring bitterness.','Stud a whole orange with cloves and put it in the cold wine with the zests, cinnamon and star anise.','Bring slowly to 70°C and hold it there for 20 minutes without ever letting it tremble: this is an infusion, not cooking.','Add the honey and sugar off the heat and stir until dissolved, then the brandy last.','Strain and serve scalding in thick glasses scalded beforehand, otherwise they crack.']::text[]),
('pescaito-frito','en','Pescaíto frito','El Palo, Málaga, Andalusia, Spain','A fry of small fish coated in durum flour and dropped into scalding olive oil. Andalusian frying uses neither egg nor breadcrumb: flour alone makes a film so thin the fish''s skin shows through it.',ARRAY['Salt the whole fish half an hour ahead, then blot them: salt draws out the surface water, and it is that water that makes oil spit.','Flour them in a sieve of semolina flour and shake them hard — excess flour falls off into the oil and burns it.','Bring the olive oil to 190°C: cooler, the fish drinks it; hotter, the flour blackens before the flesh cooks.','Fry in small handfuls, 90 seconds only, never filling more than a third of the pan.','Drain upright in a colander rather than flat on paper, so steam does not soften the underside. Salt and lemon at the table.']::text[]),
('tortillitas-de-camarones','en','Shrimp fritters','San Fernando, Cádiz, Andalusia, Spain','Lace-edged fritters of minute shrimps, made from a batter of chickpea flour and seawater. The camarones are eaten whole, shell and all; the batter must be so thin that it spreads by itself in the oil.',ARRAY['Mix the two flours, the very finely chopped spring onion and parsley, and the paprika.','Whisk in cold water to the consistency of pouring cream — far thinner than pancake batter.','Add the raw whole shrimps and rest for an hour in the cold: the batter must relax, and the shrimps give up a little of their salty water.','Salt lightly, or not at all, if you have used seawater as they do in San Fernando.','Pour a ladleful into oil at 190°C without spreading it: it should run out by itself into a lacy disc. 90 seconds a side, and serve at once.']::text[]),
('ajo-blanco','en','Ajo blanco','Almáchar, Málaga, Andalusia, Spain','A cold soup of almonds, garlic and bread, served with muscat grapes. It predates gazpacho by centuries: it is the soup of al-Andalus, made before the tomato arrived from America, and garlic stands in for the pepper.',ARRAY['Blanch the almonds for a minute and pinch off the skins: one skin left specks the soup brown and bitter.','Soak the stale crumb in a little water, then barely press it — it must stay wet.','Blend the almonds, bread, garlic and salt at length with a third of the water, at least 4 minutes, until perfectly smooth.','Trickle in the olive oil with the motor running, as for mayonnaise: that emulsion is what gives the whiteness and the body.','Loosen with the rest of the iced water, sharpen with sherry vinegar and chill for 3 hours. Serve with peeled muscat grapes.']::text[]),
('flamenquin-cordobes','en','Flamenquín','Andújar, Córdoba, Andalusia, Spain','A roll of pork escalope wrapped around serrano ham, crumbed and fried. The name is said to come from the golden colour, recalling the fair hair of the Flemings at Charles V''s court; it is eaten cold as a tapa as readily as hot on a plate.',ARRAY['Cut the loin into escalopes and beat them between two films to 3 mm, striking with the flat and not the edge of the mallet.','Pepper but do not salt: the serrano ham brings all the salt needed.','Lay a slice of ham on each escalope and roll very tightly, expelling the air; chill 30 minutes so the roll holds.','Flour, dip in beaten egg then breadcrumbs, and repeat egg and crumb a second time — that double coating is what stops the roll opening.','Fry 5 minutes at 170°C, rolling them in the oil, and drain upright. Cut on the bias.']::text[]),
('espetos-de-sardinas','en','Sardine espetos','Pedregalejo, Málaga, Andalusia, Spain','Six sardines threaded on a cane and planted at an angle before a wood fire, in an old boat filled with sand. They are made only in the months without an R, May to August, when sardines are fat: lean, they dry out before they are done.',ARRAY['Neither gut nor scale the sardines: the viscera scent the flesh as it cooks and the scales protect the skin from the fire.','Thread them through the flank, under the backbone, six to a cane, all heads facing the same way.','Salt generously with coarse salt half an hour ahead, working it into the skin, and leave them in the open air.','Plant the cane at an angle in the sand, thirty centimetres from the coals, belly side to the fire first.','Give them 4 minutes on that side then turn the whole cane: a sardine is done when the eye whitens and the skin splits. Never turn it twice.']::text[]),
('cazon-en-adobo','en','Cazón en adobo','Cádiz, Andalusia, Spain','Cubes of dogfish marinated in vinegar, garlic and cumin, then floured and fried. The adobo is not a seasoning but a preservation: vinegar kept the fish two days before refrigeration, and the cumin is a direct inheritance from al-Andalus.',ARRAY['Cut the cazón into 3 cm cubes, removing the central cartilage, which comes away in one piece.','Pound the garlic with the cumin, oregano, paprika and salt, then slake with the vinegar and water.','Marinate the fish at least 4 hours, ideally 12: less and the adobo does not penetrate; longer and the vinegar cooks the flesh and turns it mealy.','Drain without rinsing, blot, and flour with fine semolina just before frying.','Fry 3 minutes at 180°C, in small batches. Serve scalding, without lemon: the acidity is already in the fish.']::text[]),
('polea-andaluza','en','Andalusian poleá','Seville, Andalusia, Spain','A porridge of flour, milk and anise, covered with fried croutons and cinnamon. It is the pudding of the hungry years, when there were neither eggs nor sugar to spare; Sevillians still eat it out of nostalgia and serve it at All Saints.',ARRAY['Infuse the milk with the anise seeds, zest and a cinnamon stick for 15 minutes without boiling, then strain.','Slake the flour in a glass of cold milk before anything hot goes in: the only sure way to avoid lumps.','Pour the hot infused milk onto the slaked flour, whisking, then return to low heat with the sugar.','Stir without pause for 20 minutes, scraping the corners of the pan: poleá catches the instant you stop.','Fry bread dice in olive oil, drain them, and set them on the warm porridge with cinnamon. They must stay crisp.']::text[]),
('pringa','en','Pringá','Seville, Andalusia, Spain','The meats from a cocido crushed with a fork and packed into a small round roll. Pringar means to dip: a leftovers dish that has become Seville''s most-ordered tapa, and it is never crushed in a machine.',ARRAY['Take the meats from an already-cooked cocido, still warm: cold, the fat sets and crushing becomes impossible.','Skin the chorizo and the black pudding, and remove any bone and gristle from the belly.','Crush everything with a fork on a board, working each meat separately before combining: they do not yield alike.','The texture must stay stringy and lumpy — that is the difference between a pringá and a potted meat.','Pepper, add a touch of crushed garlic, and pack it generously into a barely warm roll. Eaten with no cutlery.']::text[]),
('pa-amb-tomaquet','en','Pa amb tomàquet','Barcelona, Catalonia, Spain','Toasted bread rubbed with ripe tomato, dressed with olive oil and salted. The tomato is never laid on in slices: it is halved and rubbed until only the skin is left in your hand, so the pulp goes into the crumb.',ARRAY['Choose a country loaf with a tight crumb and a thick crust: too airy a bread tears under the rubbing.','Toast the slices on both sides until golden but still soft in the middle.','Rub very lightly with a cut garlic clove — the garlic is optional, and many in Barcelona go without.','Halve the tomato and rub the cut flesh onto the hot crumb, pressing, until the slice is red and damp.','Trickle over olive oil and salt with coarse salt. Eaten within five minutes, or the bread goes soggy.']::text[]),
('botifarra-amb-mongetes','en','Botifarra with beans','Vic, Catalonia, Spain','A Catalan sausage grilled with white beans sautéed in its own fat. Mongetes del ganxet, whose skin is so fine it vanishes under the tooth, grow only within fifty kilometres of Vic and Barcelona.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight and cook them 1½ hours at a bare simmer with the bay, unsalted — salt hardens the skin.','Salt only at the end, and let them cool in their liquid: that is how they keep their shape.','Prick the botifarres just twice with a needle and cook them 15 minutes in a pan over low heat, turning often.','Lift them out, keep every drop of rendered fat and turn the sliced garlic pale gold in it without burning.','Tip the drained beans into that fat and sauté 5 minutes, moving them by shaking the pan, never with a spoon which would break them. Add parsley.']::text[]),
('canelons-de-sant-esteve','en','Sant Esteve cannelloni','Barcelona, Catalonia, Spain','The cannelloni of 26 December, filled with the remains of the previous day''s escudella and gratinated under béchamel. Italian cooks who came to work in Barcelona in the 19th century brought the pasta; Catalans filled it with what they had, and the tradition was born of a household need.',ARRAY['Make a sofregit of onion and grated tomato, 40 minutes over low heat, until only a brick-red paste remains.','Mince yesterday''s cooked meats and the sautéed livers twice, add them to the sofregit and flame with brandy.','Bind the filling with a ladle of béchamel and cool it completely: warm, it tears the pasta as you roll.','Cook the sheets al dente, spread them on a damp cloth, fill and roll tightly, seam down.','Coat with nutmegged béchamel until covered, scatter cheese and pine nuts, and gratinate 25 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('gilda','en','Gilda','San Sebastián, Basque Country, Spain','An olive, a pickled green chilli and an anchovy on a cocktail stick, dressed with oil. Created at Casa Vallés in 1946, it is named after the Rita Hayworth film: verde, salada y un poco picante — green, salty and a little spicy, like the heroine.',ARRAY['Choose pickled Ibarra guindilla peppers, long and pale: they are barely hot, only sour.','Drain and blot them, and the anchovies too, or vinegar runs down your fingers at the first bite.','Pull off the pepper stalks and fold each in two or three depending on its length.','Thread onto the stick in this order: olive, folded pepper, coiled anchovy, folded pepper, olive. The order matters: the anchovy must sit in the centre, shielded by the vinegar.','Trickle over olive oil and serve at once, at room temperature. A cold gilda has no flavour at all.']::text[]),
('talo-con-chistorra','en','Talo con chistorra','Tolosa, Basque Country, Spain','A maize flatbread cooked on a griddle, rolled around a grilled chistorra. Talo was the daily bread of the Basque Country before wheat became common there; it survives only at village festivals, made in front of the crowd.',ARRAY['Mix the maize flour, salt and very hot water, working with a spoon then by hand as soon as you can: the water must be boiling, warm water will not take the flour.','Knead 5 minutes into a supple ball and rest it 15 minutes under a damp cloth.','Flatten balls between two films into 2 mm discs: without gluten the dough tears, and you patch it with your fingertips.','Cook on a very hot dry griddle, 2 minutes a side — the flatbread puffs a little and blisters brown.','Grill the chistorra, lay it on the scalding talo with a shaving of ewe''s cheese, roll it and eat with your hands at once.']::text[]),
('bocadillo-de-calamares','en','Calamari sandwich','Plaza Mayor, Madrid, Spain','Fried squid rings piled into a roll, with no sauce and no garnish. Madrid is three hundred kilometres from the sea: the bocadillo was born at the freight station where fish arrived overnight, and the city made it its most improbable emblem.',ARRAY['Clean the squid and cut it into rings exactly a centimetre wide: thinner they dry out, thicker they stay raw in the middle.','Soak them 2 hours in milk — the only tenderising Madrid practises, and it transforms frozen squid.','Drain, blot, then flour them in a shaken bag, and sieve off the excess.','Fry at 190°C for 90 seconds, in small handfuls: beyond two minutes the flesh shrinks and turns elastic.','Split the roll without buttering it and pile in the scalding rings. A wedge of lemon on the side, never a sauce.']::text[]),
('callos-a-la-madrilena','en','Madrid-style tripe','Madrid, Spain','Beef tripe stewed with chorizo, black pudding and snout, in a red paprika sauce. The gelatine from the calf''s foot binds the sauce without flour: that is how good callos are known, they stick to the lips.',ARRAY['Scrub the tripe with coarse salt and vinegar, rinsing seven or eight times, until the water runs perfectly clear — it takes a while and it is essential.','Blanch 15 minutes, throw the water away, then cook 3 hours with the calf''s foot, snout, bay and cloves.','The calf''s foot is not there for flavour: it brings the gelatine that will bind the sauce, and without it callos stay thin.','Make a sofrito of onion and garlic, add the ham, and the sweet and hot paprika off the heat — burnt, paprika turns bitter in three seconds.','Combine tripe, sofrito, chorizo and black pudding, and simmer an hour more. Rest overnight and reheat: they are better the next day.']::text[]),
('huevos-estrellados','en','Broken eggs and chips','Madrid, Spain','Fried eggs broken over a bed of chips, crushed with a fork at the table so the yolk runs everywhere. Estrellar means to smash: the gesture is part of the dish, and it belongs to the customer, never the cook.',ARRAY['Cut the potatoes into thick batons and first confit them 12 minutes at 140°C in olive oil, without letting them colour.','Lift them out, let them cool for twenty minutes, then return them at 190°C to brown — the two baths are not negotiable.','Fried once only, they would be soft inside and would collapse under the yolk.','Fry the eggs in the same very hot oil, spooning it over the white, until the edges are lacy and the yolk still liquid.','Set them on the chips, add the ham in strips, salt with coarse salt and smash everything with a fork in front of your guests.']::text[]),
('torreznos-de-soria','en','Soria torreznos','Soria, Castile and León, Spain','Belly pork with rind blown into even bubbles, crisp as glass. The Soria torrezno is the only one in Spain with a guarantee mark; the secret is not in the frying but in the three days of drying that precede it.',ARRAY['Dry-salt the belly, rind included, and leave it 24 hours in the cold; rinse then dry it for three days in cool air.','That drying of the rind is the whole secret: damp, it will never bubble and stays leathery.','Cut 3 cm strips and lay them rind down in a cold pan with a finger of water.','Heat gently: the water evaporates, the fat renders and replaces the liquid without the rind burning. Allow 45 minutes over very low heat, rind always down.','Finally take the oil to 190°C: the rind bursts into bubbles in about thirty seconds. Lift them out at once and salt.']::text[]),
('all-i-pebre','en','All i pebre','Albufera, Valencia, Spain','Lagoon eels stewed with garlic, paprika and almond in an earthenware pot. The dish of the Albufera fishermen: no tomato, no onion, only garlic — the name, garlic and pepper, states the whole recipe.',ARRAY['Have the eels cut into sections by the fishmonger and rinse them at length: all their mucus must go, or the sauce stays slimy.','In the earthenware pot, turn the whole unpeeled garlic, the almonds and the slice of bread golden, then lift them out.','Pound them in a mortar with the parsley and a little water to make the picada.','Off the heat, throw the paprika into the hot oil and stir for two seconds before adding the water immediately: this is the critical gesture, burnt paprika makes the dish bitter.','Cook sliced potatoes 15 minutes in that broth, add the eels and the picada, and simmer 12 minutes without ever stirring — you shake the pot instead.']::text[]),
('esgarraet','en','Esgarraet','Valencia, Spain','Roasted red peppers torn by hand and mixed with raw desalted cod, garlic and oil. Esgarrar means to tear: the knife is banned, because a blade cuts the fibres instead of separating them and the pepper then weeps.',ARRAY['Roast the whole peppers 40 minutes at 200°C, turning them, until the skin blisters and blackens in patches.','Shut them in a sealed bag for 20 minutes: the steam lifts the skin, which then comes away in one movement.','Peel them without running them under water — water would carry off all the juice the roasting concentrated — and tear them into strips by hand.','Desalt the cod for 24 hours, changing the water four times, blot it and shred it raw into coarse strands.','Mix everything with finely sliced garlic and olive oil, and macerate 6 hours in the cold. Served cold, with bread.']::text[]),
('arroz-al-horno','en','Baked rice','Xàtiva, Valencia, Spain','Rice baked in an earthenware cazuela, crowned with a whole head of garlic and slices of potato. It was made the day after the cocido with its broth: the head of garlic planted in the centre is no ornament, it is squeezed into the rice at the table.',ARRAY['Brown the pork ribs in the cazuela with the oil, then the potato slices and the halved tomatoes; set everything aside.','Tip the rice into the scented oil and stir 2 minutes to pearl it, then add the paprika off the heat.','Pour in boiling broth in the exact ratio of two and a quarter parts to one of rice: this rice is not stirred again and cannot be corrected.','Distribute the meats, potatoes, chickpeas and black pudding, and plant the whole head of garlic in the centre.','Bake 45 minutes at 200°C without once touching the dish. The rice must be dry, every grain separate, with a light crust formed on top.']::text[]),
('horchata-de-chufa','en','Tiger nut horchata','Alboraya, Valencia, Spain','A tiger nut milk, white and sweet, drunk iced with fartons dipped in it. The chufa is a tuber the Arabs brought to Valencia; in Europe it grows on barely four hundred hectares north of the city, and industrial horchata has nothing to do with the fresh kind.',ARRAY['Wash the tiger nuts under plenty of water, scrubbing, then soak them 12 hours: dry, they are hard as gravel and no blender touches them.','Rinse them again and blend with a third of the water, at length, to a gritty purée.','Add the rest of the water, the zest and cinnamon, and macerate 3 hours in the cold.','Strain through a fine cloth, twisting hard: it is that pressing, not the blending, that extracts the fat and gives the velvet.','Sweeten, stir until dissolved and serve very cold, almost frosted. It keeps two days at most and separates: shake before pouring.']::text[]),
('agua-de-valencia','en','Agua de Valencia','Valencia, Spain','Orange juice, cava, vodka and gin, served by the jug. Created at the Café Madrid in 1959 for a group of Basques who wanted something other than the usual cava; the owner served them this saying it was water from Valencia, and the name stuck.',ARRAY['Squeeze the oranges the same day: yesterday''s juice loses its scent and takes on a bitter peel taste.','Strain it through a wide sieve to hold back the pips but let the pulp through, which gives the body.','Dissolve the sugar in the cold juice, stirring at length, before any alcohol goes in.','Add the vodka and gin, stir, then chill for an hour: everything must be freezing before the next step.','Pour the cava in last, gently, down the side of the jug, and stir only once, slowly. Serve without ice, which would water it down.']::text[]),
('tarta-de-santiago','en','Tarta de Santiago','Santiago de Compostela, Galicia, Spain','An almond cake with neither flour nor leaven, marked with the cross of Saint James in icing sugar. Only three ingredients go in: it is the quantity of egg, and the air whisked into it, that gives all the lightness to an otherwise dense cake.',ARRAY['Whisk the whole eggs with the sugar for at least 10 minutes, until the mixture triples and ribbons for three seconds.','With no flour and no leaven, that aeration is the only thing keeping the cake from being a dense disc.','Fold in the ground almonds, zest and cinnamon with a spatula, lifting, in some twenty movements.','Pour into a buttered 24 cm tin and bake 40 minutes at 175°C: a skewer should come out just dry.','Cool, set a stencil of the cross of Santiago on top and dust with icing sugar before lifting it straight up.']::text[]),
('caldo-gallego','en','Caldo gallego','Lugo, Galicia, Spain','A soup of turnip tops, white beans and potato, enriched with unto — aged rancid lard. Unto is what separates a real caldo from a vegetable soup: its slight rancidity, deliberately sought, gives a depth neither bacon nor oil replaces.',ARRAY['Soak the beans and the salt pork separately, overnight each, changing the pork''s water twice.','Start the beans, bones and pork in cold water and simmer for an hour, skimming at the beginning.','Add the lard and the potatoes broken with a spoon rather than cut — breaking releases the starch and thickens the broth by itself.','Wash the turnip tops and blanch them 3 minutes separately if the season is late: after flowering they turn bitter and would spoil the whole pot.','Bring everything together and cook 40 minutes more. Salt only at the end, and rest an hour before serving.']::text[]),
('pimientos-de-padron','en','Padrón peppers','Padrón, Galicia, Spain','Small green peppers fried whole in olive oil and salted with coarse salt. The local saying goes uns pican e outros non, some are hot and some are not — about one in ten, and nothing tells you before you bite.',ARRAY['Choose small peppers, four to five centimetres: larger ones are older, more fibrous and far more often hot.','Wash and dry them carefully: the least drop of water makes the scalding oil explode.','Do not remove the stalks — they are the handle for eating — but pierce each one with a knife point so it does not burst.','Toss them over high heat in hot oil for 5 minutes, shaking the pan constantly, until the skin blisters and browns in patches.','Drain on paper and salt with coarse salt while scalding: salt does not stick to a cooled pepper.']::text[]),
('cachopo','en','Cachopo','Oviedo, Asturias, Spain','Two veal escalopes sealed around ham and cheese, crumbed and fried, as wide as a plate. It is ordered for two and shared: a single-portion cachopo does not exist on Asturian menus.',ARRAY['Have the butcher beat the escalopes to 4 mm, in two pieces of identical size.','Pepper the inside but do not salt: ham and cheese already bring all the salt.','Lay the ham then the cheese leaving two centimetres clear all round, and set the second escalope on top.','Seal the edges pressing firmly with your fingertips, then pinching: a cachopo that opens empties its cheese into the oil.','Flour, egg and crumb twice, then fry 6 minutes at 170°C — low and long, so the centre melts before the crust burns.']::text[]),
('sobao-pasiego','en','Sobao pasiego','Vega de Pas, Cantabria, Spain','A small butter cake baked in a paper case, dense and moist, in which butter is a third of the weight. It grew out of leftover sweet bread that the Pas valley women reworked with butter; sobar means to knead long by hand.',ARRAY['Take the butter out three hours ahead: it must be perfectly soft, neither melted nor firm, or nothing emulsifies.','Beat it with the sugar for at least 8 minutes, until it pales and nearly doubles.','Add the eggs one at a time, waiting for each to be fully absorbed: poured in together they split the mixture.','Fold in the sifted flour and baking powder, the zest, honey, rum and salt, without working it.','Fill the paper cases three-quarters full and bake 22 minutes at 200°C. The top should be dark brown and the centre still moist.']::text[]),
('quesada-pasiega','en','Quesada pasiega','Pas valley, Cantabria, Spain','A custard of curdled milk, butter and cinnamon, baked shallow until the top browns. It was made from milk that had turned: a thrift pudding, and fresh milk never gives the same acidity in the background.',ARRAY['Drain the fresh cheese 2 hours in a colander: too wet, the quesada does not set and stays liquid in the middle.','Crush it with a fork with the sugar, then add the eggs one at a time and the warm melted butter.','Fold in the sifted flour, the milk, cinnamon, zest and salt; the batter should ribbon thickly off the whisk.','Pour into a buttered rectangular tin, 3 cm deep at most — this is a quesada, not a cake, and its shallowness makes its texture.','Bake 45 minutes at 180°C until the top is dark brown. Cool completely before cutting into squares.']::text[]),
('morcilla-de-burgos','en','Burgos morcilla','Burgos, Castile and León, Spain','A blood pudding with rice and onion, in which rice is nearly a quarter of the whole. It is bound with neither crumb nor flour: the grains swell in the blood and show distinctly in the slice, which sets Burgos apart from every other Spanish pudding.',ARRAY['Confit the very finely sliced onion in the fat for an hour over low heat, until translucent and entirely dry.','Cook the rice only halfway, 8 minutes, and refresh it: fully cooked, it would burst inside the pudding.','Combine the warm onion, the rice, the spices and salt, then pour in the sieved blood, stirring.','Fill the casings only half full — the rice doubles again while poaching, and a full skin bursts without fail.','Poach 45 minutes at 80°C, never boiling, pricking with a needle after 10 minutes. Cut thick and fry or grill.']::text[]),
('botillo-del-bierzo','en','Botillo del Bierzo','Bembibre, León, Spain','A large casing stuffed with pork ribs, tail and bones, smoked then boiled for three hours. It is served whole in the middle of the table and opened with a single cut: the pieces keep their bones, and they are eaten with the fingers.',ARRAY['Cut the ribs and tail into sections with their bones: the bone gives a botillo its flavour and its gelatine, and a boned botillo is no longer one.','Rub every piece with crushed garlic, paprika, oregano and salt, and macerate 24 hours in the cold.','Pack the casing by hand, pressing firmly, tie it and hang it to dry for three days.','Cold-smoke over oak for a week, then prick it in five or six places before poaching — without those pricks it bursts in the pot.','Poach 3 hours at a bare simmer, changing the water once, and cook the cabbage, chorizo and potatoes in the second broth.']::text[]),
('patatas-revolconas','en','Patatas revolconas','Ávila, Castile and León, Spain','A mash of potato crushed with paprika, bright orange, crowned with crisp torreznos. Revolcar means to wallow: the mash is not whipped, it is rolled in the paprika fat until it is soaked through.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes whole in their skins with bay and salt, and keep a glass of the cooking water.','Fry the belly lardons until crisp, lift them out and keep every drop of fat.','Turn the garlic golden in that fat, then take the pan off the heat before adding the paprikas and cumin: over the flame they burn in two seconds and turn bitter.','Peel the potatoes hot and crush them straight into the pan with a fork, rolling them in the red fat.','Loosen with a little cooking water, salt and crown with the crisp lardons at the moment of serving.']::text[]),
('migas-manchegas','en','Migas manchegas','Albacete, Castile-La Mancha, Spain','Stale bread crumbled and dampened, fried at length with oil and garlic until every crumb separates. It was the breakfast of La Mancha shepherds: they set out with the week''s bread, and the previous night''s rain served to dampen it.',ARRAY['Crumble two-day-old bread with a knife into pieces the size of a maize grain — never in a processor, which gives powder.','Sprinkle it with salted water, mix by hand, wrap it in a damp cloth and leave it overnight.','The bread must be supple but not sodden: too much water and the migas clag into mush, too little and they stay hard.','Fry the unpeeled garlic, chorizo and belly in the oil, lift them out and add the paprika off the heat.','Tip in the crumbs and stir constantly for 30 minutes over medium heat, lifting with a spatula: they must end golden, dry and perfectly separate. Serve with grapes.']::text[]),
('caldereta-extremena','en','Extremaduran caldereta','Trujillo, Extremadura, Spain','A milk-lamb stew bound with a picada of liver, bread and almonds, cooked in a cauldron over the fire. Transhumance shepherds made it with whichever beast had injured itself on the road: nothing goes in that a mule cannot carry.',ARRAY['Sear the lamb in large bone-in pieces in scalding oil, in small batches, until well coloured.','Fry the onion, garlic and pepper in the same oil, add the paprika off the heat and return the meat.','Moisten with white wine and water to cover, add the bay and cloves, and simmer covered for 2 hours.','Sauté the lamb''s liver and fry the bread, then pound them in a mortar with the almonds, raw garlic and a little sauce.','Add this picada 20 minutes before the end: it is what binds and what gives the flavour, and it must on no account boil long, or the liver turns grainy.']::text[]),
('zorongollo','en','Zorongollo','Cáceres, Extremadura, Spain','A warm salad of roasted red peppers, tomato and hard-boiled egg, with Extremaduran oil. The pepper is peeled without ever going under water: the juice collected during roasting is the dressing, and rinsing it would be throwing the dish away.',ARRAY['Roast the whole peppers and tomatoes 45 minutes at 200°C, turning them halfway, until the skin blisters and lifts.','Shut them under a cloth or in a sealed bag for 20 minutes, while the steam frees the skins.','Peel them over a bowl to catch every drop of juice, and tear the flesh into strips by hand.','Strain the juice and mix it with the oil, vinegar, crushed garlic, cumin and salt: that is the whole dressing.','Add the chopped hard-boiled eggs and macerate 2 hours at room temperature — never in the fridge, cold kills the flavour of the pepper.']::text[]),
('migas-aragonesas','en','Aragonese migas','Teruel, Aragon, Spain','Bread crumbs fried with Teruel ham and grapes, fattier and coarser than the La Mancha kind. In Aragon they are cut into dice rather than crumbs, and served with hot chocolate at breakfast — the custom surprises, but it is old.',ARRAY['Cut the stale bread into half-centimetre dice rather than crumbling it: that is the Aragonese cut, and it gives a firmer mouthful.','Dampen with salted water sprinkled by hand, mix, and rest under a cloth for six to twelve hours.','Fry the unpeeled garlic, the belly and the diced ham in the oil, and lift them out.','Tip the bread dice into the fat and stir without pause for 25 minutes over medium heat — this is an arm exercise, there is no shortcut.','Add the paprika off the heat, return the meats and the fresh grapes, and serve scalding. The grapes bring the acidity that cuts the fat.']::text[]),
('bacalao-ajoarriero','en','Bacalao al ajoarriero','Pamplona, Navarre, Spain','Shredded salt cod stewed with garlic, choricero pepper and tomato. The name comes from the arrieros, the muleteers who carried salt cod across Spain: they cooked it on the road, with the garlic they always carried.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod 36 hours in the fridge, changing the water five times, then shred it raw by hand into strands.','Soak the choricero peppers 2 hours in hot water and scrape the pulp with a spoon: it, not paprika, gives the colour and the flavour.','Confit the onion and green pepper 30 minutes over low heat, then add the sliced garlic without letting it brown.','Add the grated tomato and the choricero pulp, and reduce for 20 minutes to a thick paste.','Fold in the cod and move it by shaking the pan rather than with a spoon: the gelatine from the skin binds the sauce by itself. 10 minutes, and not one more.']::text[]),
('tumbet-mallorqui','en','Mallorcan tumbet','Palma, Mallorca, Balearics, Spain','Layers of potato, aubergine and pepper fried separately, flooded with tomato sauce. Each vegetable is fried alone because none shares the same cooking time or the same way of drinking oil: it is slow, and it is the very definition of the dish.',ARRAY['Slice the aubergines, salt them and let them drain 45 minutes in a colander, then blot them thoroughly.','This step is not optional: an undrained aubergine drinks three times the oil and weeps into the dish.','Fry each vegetable separately in the same oil: potatoes first, then peppers, aubergines last — from the most neutral to the most scented.','Make a long tomato sauce, 40 minutes, with the garlic, bay and a pinch of sugar, and pass it through a sieve.','Build the layers in an earthenware dish, salting each, coat with sauce and bake 20 minutes at 180°C. Eaten warm or cold, never scalding.']::text[]),
('gofio-escaldado','en','Gofio escaldado','La Gomera, Canary Islands, Spain','Toasted grain flour scalded with fish broth, beaten to a thick purée and eaten with a spoon. Gofio is the inheritance of the Guanches, the Canaries'' people before the conquest: they toasted barley before grinding it, and the practice has never stopped.',ARRAY['Make a broth from fish heads and bones, strained and full-flavoured: gofio is made with water only in years of hardship.','Pound the garlic, mint, coriander and a little salt in a mortar to a green paste.','Pour the boiling broth onto the gofio all at once, whisking hard from the moment they meet: poured slowly it lumps beyond rescue.','Work it 5 minutes with a wooden spoon: the paste must come away from the sides and fall in a heavy ribbon.','Fold in the herb paste, mound it up and hollow the top with a spoon to hold a trickle of oil and some raw sliced onion.']::text[]),
('mojo-picon','en','Mojo picón','La Palma, Canary Islands, Spain','A red sauce of garlic, palmero chilli, cumin and paprika, mounted with oil and vinegar. There is a mojo for everything: red for meat and potatoes, green with coriander for fish, and they are not swapped.',ARRAY['Soak the dried chillies 30 minutes in hot water, open them and scrape the pulp with a spoon, discarding seeds and skins.','Pound the garlic with the coarse salt in a mortar: the salt acts as an abrasive, and the garlic turns to cream instead of leaping into the corners.','Add the cumin, paprika and chilli pulp, and keep pounding to an even paste.','Pour in the vinegar then the oil in a thin stream, turning the pestle always the same way — this is an emulsion, and it splits if you hurry.','Add a soaked crumb of bread if the mojo stays thin. Rest it 24 hours: it is poor on the first day and excellent on the second.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('bienmesabe-canario','en','Canarian bienmesabe','Tenerife, Canary Islands, Spain','A thick cream of almonds, egg yolks and syrup, scented with lemon and cinnamon. The name means it tastes good to me: it came from al-Andalus by way of Antequera, and Canarian convents adopted it in the 17th century, making it denser.',ARRAY['Bring the water and sugar to 105°C with the zest and cinnamon, then take it off and let it fall to 50°C.','Stir the ground almonds into the warm syrup and return to very low heat for 10 minutes, stirring.','Beat the yolks separately, pour a ladle of the hot mixture onto them whisking, then return everything to the pan.','This tempering is no idle precaution: yolks poured straight into hot syrup scramble into lumps beyond recovery.','Cook 10 minutes below 80°C, never boiling, until the cream coats the spoon. Scent with rum and serve cold over sponge.']::text[]),
('tajarin-al-tartufo','en','Tajarin with white truffle','Alba, Piedmont, Italy','Very fine tagliolini made with thirty egg yolks to the kilo, served in butter and shaved with white Alba truffle. The dough is almost orange: it contains not a single white, and that richness is what lets it be cut so fine without breaking.',ARRAY['Knead the flour and yolks for 15 minutes: the dough is very dry at first and seems impossible, it smooths out with work.','Wrap it and rest it an hour — without rest it shrinks back as you roll and never goes thin enough.','Roll until you can see your hand through it, dust with semolina, roll the sheet on itself and cut one-millimetre ribbons with a knife.','Cook 90 seconds only in generously salted water: these noodles are done almost before they reach the bottom.','Melt the butter without colouring it, bind the tajarin with a spoonful of cooking water, and shave the truffle at the table, never before.']::text[]),
('agnolotti-del-plin','en','Agnolotti del plin','Langhe, Piedmont, Italy','Tiny ravioli closed with a pinch of thumb and forefinger — the plin — filled with three roasted meats. They are traditionally served on a napkin, with no sauce at all, so that no moisture masks the taste of the filling.',ARRAY['Roast the three meats together in butter and rosemary, deglaze with white wine and let them cool in their juices.','Mince them twice on the fine plate with blanched, pressed spinach, and bind with parmesan, yolks and nutmeg.','Roll the dough very thin and set hazelnuts of filling every two centimetres along a single strip.','Fold the dough over, expel the air, then pinch between each mound: that pinch is the plin, and it seals without dampening.','Cut with a wheel and cook for 2 minutes. Serve in sage butter, or on a napkin with a few drops of the roasting juices, as in the Langhe.']::text[]),
('gianduiotto','en','Gianduiotto','Turin, Piedmont, Italy','An elongated chocolate ingot made with Piedmont hazelnuts, born of the continental blockade of 1806. With cocoa scarce and dear, Turin chocolatiers cut it with hazelnut paste: a shortage produced the most copied chocolate in the world.',ARRAY['Roast the hazelnuts 15 minutes at 160°C and rub them in a cloth: not one skin must remain, or the paste turns bitter and gritty.','Blend them at length, ten minutes at least, scraping the sides: they pass through powder, then paste, then oil — that last stage is the one to reach.','Melt the chocolate and cocoa butter to 45°C, then bring it down to 27°C stirring on marble before raising it to 31°C.','This tempering is not optional: without it the gianduiotto blooms within days and melts between the fingers.','Combine hazelnut paste, tempered chocolate and icing sugar, then mould into three-sided ingots or pipe them. Crystallise 12 hours at 16°C.']::text[]),
('fonduta-valdostana','en','Valle d’Aosta fonduta','Aosta, Aosta Valley, Italy','Fontina melted in milk and bound with egg yolks, served as a cream and not as a stringy mass. Unlike the Swiss and Savoyard fondues it holds neither wine nor starch: it is an egg custard, nearer to a sauce than to a pot.',ARRAY['Slice the fontina thinly and soak it in the milk for 12 hours in the fridge: without that soaking it never melts evenly.','The next day, melt the butter in a bain-marie and add the cheese with its milk.','Whisk without pause until the mixture is even; never go above 70°C, beyond which the cheese separates into strings and oil.','Add the yolks one at a time, off the heat, whisking briskly after each.','Return to the bain-marie for 3 minutes until the cream coats the spoon, pepper and serve over toast or polenta, with shaved white truffle.']::text[]),
('focaccia-genovese','en','Genoese focaccia','Genoa, Liguria, Italy','A low, oily focaccia dimpled with deep holes where oil and brine pool. In Genoa it is dipped in cappuccino at breakfast; the holes are not decorative, they hold the brine that keeps the dough from drying in the oven.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, warm water, yeast, malt, 40 ml of oil and salt for 12 minutes: the dough should be supple and slightly tacky.','Prove for an hour, then spread it by hand in an oiled tray to 1.5 cm and let it relax for 45 minutes.','Push your fingers straight down to the bottom of the tray, hand held vertical: the holes must go through the dough, not merely mark it.','Beat the remaining water, coarse salt and remaining oil into a salamoia, and pour it over the whole surface.','Prove 45 minutes more — the brine will be half absorbed — and bake 18 minutes at 230°C. It must stay soft and glossy.']::text[]),
('cotoletta-alla-milanese','en','Cotoletta alla milanese','Milan, Lombardy, Italy','A veal cutlet on the bone, crumbed and fried in clarified butter, a finger thick. It is not flattened: the true milanese keeps the thickness of the chop, and it is the later Viennese version that is beaten with a mallet.',ARRAY['Ask for two-centimetre veal chops with the bone, and on no account flatten them.','Clarify the butter: melt it gently, skim the foam and pour it off leaving the whey behind. Whole butter would burn long before the cooking ends.','Beat the eggs without salting them — salt thins the egg and the crumb slides off — and dip the whole chop, bone included.','Press firmly into fine breadcrumbs on both sides, pushing with the flat of your hand so they grip.','Cook 6 minutes a side in the foaming clarified butter over medium heat, spooning it over. Salt on coming out, lemon at the table.']::text[]),
('panettone','en','Panettone','Milan, Lombardy, Italy','A Christmas bread raised on natural leaven, with raisins and candied peel, proved over thirty-six hours. It cools hanging upside down on two skewers: its crumb is so airy and so fragile that it would collapse under its own weight.',ARRAY['Refresh the starter three times in a day, four hours apart: it must triple in three hours and smell of milk, never of vinegar.','Make a first dough in the evening with half the flour, sugar, butter and yolks, and prove 12 hours at 26°C until tripled.','Next day add the remaining ingredients and knead 25 minutes; work in the butter last, little by little, then the fruit by hand.','Prove 6 hours in the paper mould, until the dough reaches the rim — never sooner, on pain of a tight crumb.','Cut a cross, set a knob of butter in the centre and bake 55 minutes at 160°C. Skewer it at the base and hang it upside down for 12 hours.']::text[]),
('gorgonzola-e-polenta','en','Polenta with gorgonzola','Gorgonzola, Lombardy, Italy','Yellow polenta covered with sweet gorgonzola melting over it, the dish of the Po plain towns. Sweet gorgonzola is not cooked: laid on scalding polenta it runs by itself, and an oven would turn it to oil.',ARRAY['Bring the salted water to the boil and rain in the maize meal very finely with one hand, whisking hard with the other.','Then switch to a wooden spoon and stir for 45 minutes over low heat, always the same way, scraping the sides.','Polenta cooked for less than forty minutes keeps a raw taste that neither butter nor cheese hides.','It is ready when it comes away from the pot in one block and a crust forms on the base; then beat in the butter and parmesan.','Pour it scalding into the plates, set the gorgonzola on top in large pieces and scatter walnuts. Do not stir: the cheese must run by itself.']::text[]),
('baccala-mantecato','en','Baccalà mantecato','Venice, Veneto, Italy','Stockfish beaten with oil into a white foam, served on toast. Venetians use air-dried stockfish, not salt cod: the confusion of terms is five centuries old and dates from a shipwreck in the Lofoten islands in 1432.',ARRAY['Soak the stockfish for three full days under running water or changing the water four times a day: it is dry wood, and nothing shortens that.','Poach it 30 minutes in milk and water with the bay, without boiling, then let it cool in its liquid.','Remove skin and bones carefully, working by hand: a single bone ruins the texture of a mantecato.','Put the flesh in a bowl and beat with a wooden spoon while trickling in the oil, as for mayonnaise, for a full 20 minutes.','The flesh must whiten and triple in volume. Add a little of the poaching liquid if it tightens too much. Serve on grilled polenta croutons.']::text[]),
('spritz-veneziano','en','Venetian spritz','Venice, Veneto, Italy','White wine, a bitter and soda, served with an olive and a slice of orange. The name comes from German spritzen, to spray: 19th-century Austrian soldiers in the Veneto watered down the local wines, which they found too strong.',ARRAY['Chill the stemmed glasses in the freezer for ten minutes: a warm glass melts the ice and unbalances everything within two minutes.','Fill them with ice to the top — plenty of ice melts more slowly than a little, contrary to what people think.','Keep the Venetian proportion of three, two, one: three parts prosecco, two of bitter, one of soda.','Pour the prosecco first then the bitter, which is heavier and falls through the glass in an orange trail; stir just once.','Finish with the soda, half a slice of orange on the rim and a green olive on a stick. In Venice the olive is compulsory.']::text[]),
('bigoli-in-salsa','en','Bigoli in salsa','Venice, Veneto, Italy','Thick wholemeal spaghetti served with a sauce of melted onion and anchovy, without a scrap of cheese. It is a fast-day dish: no meat, no egg, no parmesan, and Venice still eats it on Good Friday and Christmas Eve.',ARRAY['Knead the wholemeal flour and warm water for 15 minutes into a very firm dough, and rest it an hour.','Extrude it through a bigolaro, or failing that the widest die of a pasta machine: bigoli must be rough, that is what holds the sauce.','Slice the onions very finely and melt them in the oil for 45 minutes over very low heat, adding a little water whenever they catch.','Desalt the anchovies, remove the bone, and crush them into the hot onions: they must dissolve entirely, not one piece should remain visible.','Deglaze with white wine, pepper, and bind the bigoli, cooked 8 minutes, with a little of their water. Never cheese, under any circumstances.']::text[]),
('tagliatelle-al-ragu','en','Tagliatelle al ragù','Bologna, Emilia-Romagna, Italy','The ragù registered in 1982 with the Bologna chamber of commerce, served on tagliatelle and never on spaghetti. The official recipe prescribes eight millimetres of width for the raw pasta and forbids garlic, herbs and any tomato beyond a little purée.',ARRAY['Render the chopped pancetta then the soffritto of very finely cut onion, carrot and celery, 25 minutes over low heat.','Add the coarsely minced beef and let it colour without stirring too much, until no water remains in the base.','Deglaze with white wine, add the purée and simmer 3 hours uncovered, moistening with broth as it goes.','Pour in the milk during the last hour: it is what tenderises the meat and rounds the acidity, and its absence is noticed at once.','For the pasta, knead flour and eggs 15 minutes, roll very thin, roll up the sheet and cut 8 mm ribbons. Cook 2 minutes and bind with butter and parmesan.']::text[]),
('lasagne-alla-bolognese','en','Lasagne alla bolognese','Bologna, Emilia-Romagna, Italy','Green spinach pasta sheets alternated with ragù and béchamel, in at least eight layers. Bologna''s pasta is green, not yellow: the spinach adds almost no flavour, but it colours and makes the sheet more supple to roll.',ARRAY['Make the ragù the day before: it is better rested, and overnight lets the fat rise so it can be lifted off.','Blanch the spinach, press it thoroughly dry and chop it by knife before kneading it with the flour and eggs.','Roll very thin, cut rectangles and blanch them 45 seconds; refresh and lay them on cloths without overlapping.','Build eight layers beginning and ending with béchamel, with very little filling per layer: a lasagne is judged by its number of sheets, not the thickness of its ragù.','Bake 35 minutes at 190°C, then rest 10 minutes out of the oven before cutting: hot, a lasagne runs across the plate.']::text[]),
('panzanella','en','Panzanella','Florence, Tuscany, Italy','A salad of soaked stale Tuscan bread, tomatoes and red onion, with oil and vinegar. Tuscan bread is made without salt: which is why it goes stale fast without moulding, and why so many dishes were invented to use it up.',ARRAY['Use unsalted bread at least two days old: fresh bread turns to glue and salted bread unbalances the seasoning.','Cut it into large pieces and soak them 10 minutes in the water with a little vinegar added.','Then press them hard between your hands and crumble them: they must be damp but not sodden, and on no account puréed.','Soak the sliced red onion 20 minutes in cold water to take off its bite, and salt the tomatoes separately to collect their juice.','Combine everything with the tomato juice, oil, vinegar and torn basil, and rest an hour at room temperature before serving.']::text[]),
('cantucci-di-prato','en','Prato cantucci','Prato, Tuscany, Italy','Almond biscuits baked twice, so hard that they are dipped in vin santo. Biscotto means twice cooked: the second baking dries the crumb entirely, and that is what lets them keep for months in a tin.',ARRAY['Do not blanch or toast the almonds: Prato cantucci use them raw and whole, skins on.','Beat the eggs and sugar, add the flour, baking powder, zest and salt, and fold in the almonds last by hand.','The dough is very sticky: flour the worktop generously and shape flat logs 5 cm wide, without trying to smooth them.','Glaze with yolk and bake 25 minutes at 180°C, then take them out and let them cool for exactly ten minutes.','Slice on the bias at 1.5 cm while still warm — cold, they shatter — and bake the slices flat for another 12 minutes at 150°C.']::text[]),
('lampredotto','en','Lampredotto','Florence, Tuscany, Italy','Beef abomasum stewed then served in a roll dipped in its own broth, with two sauces, green and hot. Florence''s lampredottai have kept their carts since the 15th century; the name comes from the lamprey, which the tripe resembles in shape.',ARRAY['Rinse the abomasum at length in cold water and blanch it 10 minutes, then throw that first water away.','Cook it again for 3 hours with onion, carrot, celery, tomato and bay, at a bare simmer — an agitated broth toughens tripe.','It is ready when a blade passes through without effort. Cool it in its broth, then slice it thinly.','Make the salsa verde by pounding parsley, garlic, capers, anchovies and oil, and a salsa piccante of chilli and oil.','Split the roll, dip the top in the scalding broth — dipped, never dry — fill with lampredotto and both sauces.']::text[]),
('suppli','en','Supplì','Rome, Lazio, Italy','Croquettes of tomato risotto with a mozzarella centre, fried, broken in two to see the cheese pull into a thread. They are called supplì al telefono because of that thread, like a handset cord; without it the croquette has failed.',ARRAY['Make a tomato and meat risotto, but moisten it a little less than usual: it must be tight, almost dry.','Spread it on a tray and cool it completely, at least 4 hours — warm risotto cannot be shaped.','Cut the mozzarella into batons and drain them an hour on paper: it is the mozzarella''s water that bursts supplì.','Shape ovals around each baton, making sure the cheese is entirely enclosed in rice.','Egg, then crumb, and fry 4 minutes at 175°C. Rest two minutes before breaking, or the cheese runs instead of pulling.']::text[]),
('carciofi-alla-giudia','en','Carciofi alla giudia','Roman Ghetto, Lazio, Italy','A whole artichoke fried twice until it opens like a flower, the outer leaves as crisp as crisps. It is the dish of the Roman Ghetto where Roman Jews were confined for three centuries; the romanesco artichoke, choke-free, is found only from February to April.',ARRAY['Turn the artichokes in a spiral with a knife, taking off the tough leaves down to the pale ones, and trim the stem leaving it long.','Drop them into lemon water as you go: a cut artichoke blackens within three minutes.','Press them head down on the worktop to open the leaves, and season between them.','First frying at 130°C, 10 minutes, head down: it cooks the heart without colouring. Lift them out and drain head down for 20 minutes.','Second frying at 180°C, 3 minutes, spooning oil over: the leaves open and brown. Flick a little cold water in at the end, and the steam makes them bloom at once.']::text[]),
('pastiera-napoletana','en','Neapolitan pastiera','Naples, Campania, Italy','An Easter tart of cooked wheat, ricotta and orange blossom, latticed with seven strips of pastry. It is made on Maundy Thursday and eaten on Sunday: it needs three days for the scents to marry, and it is frankly better on the third.',ARRAY['Cook the wheat with the milk, a little butter and the zest for 30 minutes over low heat, until creamy: it is that wheat cream, not whole grains, that makes a pastiera.','Make a rich shortcrust with lard or butter, without working it, and rest it 2 hours in the cold.','Sieve the ricotta and beat it at length with the sugar, then add the yolks one at a time, the candied fruit, orange blossom water and cinnamon.','Finally fold in the whites whisked to soft peaks: they lighten an otherwise dense filling.','Fill the tin, lay seven pastry strips in a lattice and bake 1 hour 20 at 170°C. Cool in the switched-off oven and wait two days.']::text[]),
('baba-al-rum','en','Rum babà','Naples, Campania, Italy','A mushroom-shaped brioche soaked in rum syrup until it drips. It came to Naples with the French cooks of the Bourbons in the 18th century; Neapolitans adopted it so completely that sei nu babà, you are a babà, became a compliment.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, yeast and three eggs for 10 minutes, then add the other eggs one at a time, waiting for full absorption.','Work in the softened butter last and knead 15 minutes more: the dough must be so elastic it stretches to a veil without tearing.','Prove 2 hours, fill the moulds only a third full and let them rise to the rim, about an hour.','Bake 25 minutes at 180°C then let the babàs dry overnight in the open air: a fresh babà will not drink, a stale one drinks everything.','Boil the water, sugar and zests, let it fall to 60°C, add the rum and submerge the babàs. Turn them and press gently with a spatula.']::text[]),
('ragu-napoletano','en','Neapolitan ragù','Naples, Campania, Italy','Large pieces of meat stewed six hours in tomato, the sauce dressing the pasta and the meat serving as a second course. Nothing to do with Bolognese ragù: the meat is not minced, and Neapolitans say the sauce must pippiare, barely burble, throughout.',ARRAY['Make braciole: flatten the chuck, fill with pancetta, garlic, parsley, pine nuts and raisins, roll and tie.','Brown them with the pork ribs in oil, then lift them out and melt thickly sliced onion for 40 minutes.','Return the meats, deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate entirely before adding the purée, then the tomato passata.','Let it pippiare for 6 hours over minimal heat, half covered, stirring with a wooden spoon every half hour so nothing catches.','The sauce must turn from bright red to dark brown: that is the only reliable indicator. Serve on hand-broken ziti, the meat afterwards.']::text[]),
('frittura-di-paranza','en','Frittura di paranza','Naples, Campania, Italy','A fry of the gulf''s smallest fish, floured and thrown whole into the oil. The paranza is the trawler: you do not choose what you fry, you fry what came up, and the mix changes daily.',ARRAY['Gut only the fish over ten centimetres: the smallest are eaten whole, and gutting would make them fall apart.','Blot everything carefully then flour with fine semolina in a sieve, shaking hard.','Keep the species apart: squid needs two minutes, anchovy forty seconds, and frying them together ruins one or the other.','Take the oil to 190°C and fry in small handfuls, never more than one at a time, so the temperature does not fall.','Drain in a colander, never on paper laid flat, and salt as they come out. Eaten within five minutes, from a paper cone.']::text[]),
('focaccia-barese','en','Focaccia barese','Bari, Apulia, Italy','A potato focaccia covered with crushed cherry tomatoes and black olives. The mashed potato in the dough is no eccentricity: it holds water and keeps the focaccia soft for two days, which flour alone cannot do.',ARRAY['Boil the potato whole, peel it hot and rice it: cold, it would lump in the dough.','Knead it with both flours, the yeast, warm water, 40 ml of oil and salt for 12 minutes. The dough must stay very soft, almost pourable.','Prove 1½ hours, tip into a generously oiled round tin and spread with your fingertips without working it.','Press the halved cherry tomatoes into the dough cut side up, sinking them halfway, and scatter whole unstoned olives.','Salt with coarse salt, moisten with oil, prove 45 minutes and bake 25 minutes at 230°C. The base should be fried in oil, not merely baked.']::text[]),
('tiella-barese','en','Tiella barese','Bari, Apulia, Italy','Layers of potato, rice and raw mussels baked together, with no broth made first. The rice cooks in the water released by the mussels and the potatoes: it is the only possible method, and it forbids opening the oven before the end.',ARRAY['Open the mussels raw with a knife, keeping each on its half shell and collecting every drop of their liquor: on no account cook them open.','Strain that liquor through fine cloth: it is the dish''s main seasoning, and it already holds all the salt needed.','Oil the tiella, line the base with onion then a layer of 3 mm potato slices.','Arrange the mussels on their shells, scatter the dry raw rice over by hand, then the tomatoes, garlic, parsley and pecorino.','Finish with a second layer of potato, pour in the mussel liquor made up with clear water to just cover, and bake 55 minutes at 190°C without ever opening the door.']::text[]),
('panzerotto-barese','en','Panzerotto','Bari, Apulia, Italy','A pizza-dough turnover filled with tomato and mozzarella, fried rather than baked. It is eaten standing and scalding, held point down: the first bite releases a jet of steam that has scalded generations of Bari children.',ARRAY['Knead a supple pizza dough and prove it slowly for 4 hours, or overnight in the cold: too young a dough drinks oil.','Drain the diced mozzarella at least 3 hours on paper, and reduce the tomato purée until thick.','Moisture is the only enemy: a wet filling bursts the panzerotto in the boiling oil.','Cut 15 cm discs, fill one half leaving a two-centimetre border, fold and seal with a fork, pressing hard.','Fry at 175°C for 3 minutes, turning once, until they puff and brown. Drain upright and eat at once.']::text[]),
('peperoni-cruschi','en','Peperoni cruschi','Senise, Basilicata, Italy','Sweet peppers sun-dried then fried for three seconds, crisp as crisps and sweet. Crusco means crunchy in Lucanian dialect; the Senise pepper has skin so thin that it dries whole in a fortnight without ever moulding.',ARRAY['Wipe the dried peppers with a dry cloth — never water — and shake out the stalk and seeds.','Heat the oil to exactly 160°C, and test with a small piece: it should swell and change colour in two seconds.','Plunge the peppers for three seconds, no longer: at the fourth they blacken and turn bitter, beyond rescue.','Lift them out with a skimmer and stand them on paper: they harden as they cool, and it is in cooling that they crisp.','Salt and serve as they are, or crumble them over salt cod and garlic-fried bread, as they do in Basilicata.']::text[]),
('fileja-alla-nduja','en','Fileja with nduja','Vibo Valentia, Calabria, Italy','Pasta rolled by hand around an iron rod, served with nduja melted into tomato. Nduja dissolves entirely in the heat: it is not a piece of charcuterie added to the dish, it becomes the sauce itself.',ARRAY['Knead the semolina and warm water for 15 minutes into a very firm dough, and rest it 30 minutes under a bowl.','Roll pencil-thick ropes, cut 8 cm lengths and wind each around an iron rod or knitting needle.','Withdraw the rod by rolling the pasta on the table: what remains should be a hollow, irregular tube, never smooth.','Fry the onion in oil, add the nduja and crush it with a spoon until it melts entirely and turns the oil red.','Add the tomato and cook 20 minutes. Bind the fileja, cooked 8 minutes, with a little of their water and serve with pecorino.']::text[]),
('cannoli-siciliani','en','Sicilian cannoli','Caltanissetta, Sicily, Italy','Fried pastry tubes filled with sweetened sheep''s ricotta, filled only to order. A shell filled in advance goes soft within twenty minutes: in Palermo a pastry cook who fills his cannoli ahead loses his name in a season.',ARRAY['Drain the ricotta 24 hours in a cloth under a weight: it is the one step that decides success, and it cannot be shortened.','Knead the flour, 40 g of the sugar, the lard, cocoa, marsala and salt into a very firm dough, and rest it 2 hours.','Roll it to 1 mm — you should be able to read through it — cut ovals and wrap them around metal tubes, sealing with beaten egg.','Fry 2 minutes at 180°C: the shell must blister into small bubbles, the sign the dough was thin enough. Cool before sliding off the tubes.','Sieve the ricotta with the remaining sugar, without whipping it, and pipe it in at the last moment, filling from both ends.']::text[]),
('granita-al-limone','en','Lemon granita','Messina, Sicily, Italy','A scraped lemon ice, eaten at breakfast with a brioche col tuppo. Sicilians eat it in the morning, dipping the brioche in; the texture must be creamy and not gritty, despite the name, and regular scraping is what gives it.',ARRAY['Make a syrup with the water, sugar and zest pared without pith, bring to a tremble and cool completely with the zest in it.','Strain, add freshly squeezed lemon juice and a minute pinch of salt, which lifts the acidity without being tasted.','Keep the proportion: one part juice to four of syrup. More juice and the granita freezes into a hard block.','Pour 3 cm deep into a flat tray and freeze; scrape with a fork every twenty minutes, working the edges which set first.','Allow five or six scrapings over three hours. Serve at once with a warm brioche — a granita that waits becomes a block again.']::text[]),
('cassata-siciliana','en','Sicilian cassata','Palermo, Sicily, Italy','A ricotta cake lined with green marzipan and covered with candied fruit, iced with sugar. It comes from Palermo''s convents; the green marzipan imitates the nuns'' habit, and the candied fruit laid in a crown recalls Norman mosaics.',ARRAY['Drain the ricotta 24 hours under a weight, then pass it twice through a fine sieve without ever whipping it: whipped, it weeps and the cassata runs.','Mix it with the sugar, chocolate chips and a little diced candied fruit, and chill.','Make the marzipan with ground almonds, icing sugar, water and green colour from blended pistachios; roll it to 5 mm.','Line a sloping-sided mould alternating strips of marzipan and marsala-soaked sponge, pressed tight against each other.','Fill with the ricotta, close with a layer of sponge, press and chill 12 hours. Turn out, ice with icing sugar and water, and crown with whole candied fruit.']::text[]),
('panelle','en','Panelle','Palermo, Sicily, Italy','Fried chickpea-flour wafers, served in a sesame roll. They are a direct Arab inheritance: ground chickpea reached Sicily in the 9th century, and the panella remains Palermo''s cheapest street food.',ARRAY['Whisk the chickpea flour into cold water, then pass it through a fine sieve: a single lump becomes a hard bump in the panella.','Set it over medium heat and stir without pause for 20 minutes with a wooden spoon, always the same way.','The batter is ready when it comes away from the sides in one block and a spoon stands up in it.','Pour it scalding onto an oiled marble and spread it with a spatula to 3 mm, as fast as you can: it sets within a minute.','Cut rectangles once cold and fry 3 minutes at 180°C, until they blister. Serve in a sesame roll, with lemon.']::text[]),
('pasta-con-le-sarde','en','Pasta con le sarde','Palermo, Sicily, Italy','Bucatini with fresh sardines, wild fennel, raisins and pine nuts. The sweet-and-savoury is Arab, the wild fennel Sicilian: legend gives the dish to the cook of general Euphemios, landed in 827, who had only fish and herbs to hand.',ARRAY['Boil the wild fennel 15 minutes in plenty of salted water, drain it keeping the water, and chop it.','That cooking water will cook the pasta: it carries the fennel through the whole dish, and throwing it away would lose half the recipe.','Fillet the sardines, soften the onion, add the desalted anchovies and crush them until they dissolve entirely.','Add the sardines, plumped raisins, pine nuts and saffron, and cook 10 minutes without stirring much, to keep pieces whole.','Cook the bucatini in the fennel water, combine, and cover with dry-toasted breadcrumbs — never cheese, Sicily does not allow it on fish.']::text[]),
('porceddu-sardo','en','Porceddu','Barbagia, Sardinia, Italy','A suckling pig spitted and roasted upright before a fire of myrtle and mastic, seasoned with nothing but salt. It is traditionally served buried in fresh myrtle branches for half an hour: the myrtle steam scents it better than any marinade.',ARRAY['Split the pig, spit it on a green wood pole and plant the spit at an angle in the ground, in front of the fire and never over it.','Cooking is by radiant heat, never direct flame: fat falling into the fire would set the skin alight.','Allow 3 hours, turning the spit a quarter turn every twenty minutes, and salt only halfway through.','Baste the skin with a rind of pork fat on the end of a stick, which feeds it and browns it evenly.','Carve it and bury the pieces under a bed of fresh myrtle in a closed box for half an hour, before serving warm.']::text[]),
('pane-carasau','en','Pane carasau','Barbagia, Sardinia, Italy','Sheets of semolina bread so thin they are called music paper, baked twice. The first baking blows them into a balloon; they are then split into two discs and baked again separately, and they keep for a year — the bread of shepherds gone six months.',ARRAY['Knead the fine semolina, warm water, yeast and salt for 20 minutes: the dough must be very firm and perfectly smooth.','Prove 2 hours, divide into 100 g balls and roll each into a 1 mm disc, dusting with semolina.','Bake at 300°C at least, on a scalding stone: the disc must balloon within two minutes. In a cooler oven it dries without separating.','Take it out at once and cut round the edge with a knife to get two discs, protecting yourself from the steam.','Stack the discs under a board to flatten them, then bake each again for 2 minutes until golden and brittle.']::text[]),
('malloreddus-alla-campidanese','en','Malloreddus alla campidanese','Campidano, Sardinia, Italy','Small saffron semolina gnocchi, ridged on a wicker basket, with a sauce of fresh sausage and tomato. Malloreddus means little calves in Sardinian: women shaped them like beasts for feast days, on baskets called ciuliris.',ARRAY['Infuse the saffron in the warm water for 20 minutes: it must dye the water deep yellow before it goes into the dough.','Knead with the semolina and salt for 15 minutes into a firm dough, and rest it 30 minutes.','Roll thin ropes, cut one-centimetre pieces and press each against the wicker of the basket with your thumb, rolling it.','The ridges are not decorative: they hold the sauce, and a smooth malloreddu lets it slide into the plate.','Make the sauce by crumbling the raw sausage into the onion, without chopping it, and simmer 45 minutes with the tomato. Bind the pasta, cooked 12 minutes, and cover with pecorino.']::text[]),
('gnocco-fritto','en','Gnocco fritto','Modena, Emilia-Romagna, Italy','Diamonds of bread dough fried in lard, which puff into hollow cushions, served with charcuterie. Lard cannot be replaced by oil: it is what gives the flavour, and in Modena it goes into the dough as well.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, warm milk, yeast, 50 g of lard and salt for 12 minutes, and prove 1½ hours.','Roll the dough to exactly 3 mm: thicker it stays raw inside, thinner it does not puff.','Cut 8 cm diamonds with a wheel and rest them uncovered for 15 minutes, so the surface dries.','Melt the remaining lard and take it to 180°C; drop in two or three diamonds at a time, no more.','They puff within ten seconds: ladle fat over them so they fill with air, turn them and lift them out at 30 seconds. Serve scalding with the charcuterie.']::text[]),
('torta-al-testo','en','Torta al testo','Perugia, Umbria, Italy','An unleavened flatbread cooked on a stone or cast-iron disc, split and filled with charcuterie or sautéed greens. The testo is the stone itself: heated in the hearth, it cooks the bread by contact and mass, with no oven — a household bread needing no baker.',ARRAY['Mix the flour, bicarbonate and salt, add the water and oil and knead 8 minutes into a supple but non-sticky dough.','Rest it 30 minutes under a cloth: without rest it shrinks back as you roll and bakes into a hard disc.','Divide in two and roll one-centimetre discs, the exact diameter of your testo or cast-iron pan.','Heat the testo dry and very hot, and cook 8 minutes a side, pricking the blisters with a fork as they appear.','Split the scalding torta horizontally and fill with charcuterie, pecorino or chard sautéed with garlic. Eaten within the half hour.']::text[]),
('gricia','en','Gricia','Grisciano, Lazio, Italy','Pasta with guanciale and pecorino, no tomato and no egg: the mother from which amatriciana and carbonara derive. It is sometimes called white amatriciana; in truth it is older, born before the tomato reached the Apennines.',ARRAY['Cut the guanciale into half-centimetre lardons, keeping the rind on to remove it afterwards.','Render it dry in a cold pan over low heat for 10 minutes: it must give up its fat slowly and crisp without burning.','Crack the pepper in a mortar and toast it 30 seconds in the rendered fat, off the heat: that is what wakes its scent.','Cook the pasta very al dente and transfer it to the pan with two ladles of its water.','Off the heat, add the grated pecorino in three goes, stirring briskly: tipped in at once onto a hot pan it strings and forms lumps beyond rescue.']::text[]),
('spaghetti-alla-chitarra','en','Spaghetti alla chitarra','Chieti, Abruzzo, Italy','Square-sectioned spaghetti cut by pressing the dough onto a wooden frame strung with wires, the chitarra. The square section is no detail: the sharp edges grip sauce as no round spaghetti does, and the instrument dates from the early 19th century.',ARRAY['Knead the flours and eggs for 15 minutes into a firm dough, and rest it an hour under a bowl.','Roll sheets to exactly the thickness of the wire spacing — two millimetres — because that thickness is what gives the square section.','Lay the sheet on the chitarra and run the pin over it, pressing: the wires cut, and the spaghetti fall into the frame.','Make pallottine, minute meatballs of the three minced meats, hazelnut-sized, and brown them before they go into the sauce.','Simmer the sauce for an hour, cook the pasta 3 minutes and combine. Serve with Abruzzo pecorino.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('barbajuans','en','Barbajuans','Monaco-Ville, Monaco','Fried ravioli filled with chard, ricotta and parmesan, the Principality''s national dish. They are eaten on 27 January for Saint Dévote; the name is said to come from uncle John, barba Juan in Ligurian, credited by legend with the idea of frying leftover ravioli filling.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, one egg, the olive oil, a pinch of salt and a little warm water for 10 minutes, and rest the dough an hour under a cloth.','Separate the stems from the chard leaves: only the leaves go into the filling, the stems will serve elsewhere.','Wilt them in a pan with the sliced onion and leek, then press them thoroughly dry between your hands — a wet filling bursts the barbajuan in the oil.','Chop them by knife and bind with the drained ricotta, parmesan, remaining eggs and nutmeg.','Roll the dough to 1 mm, set hazelnuts of filling on it, close into squares cut with a fluted wheel and fry 3 minutes at 175°C. Eaten warm, with the fingers.']::text[]),
('stocafi-monegasque','en','Monegasque stocafi','La Condamine, Monaco','Stockfish stewed three hours in tomato, Nice olives and brandy, the dish of the harbour fishermen. Stockfish is not salt cod but wind-dried cod, hard as wood: it needs five days of soaking before it can be cut at all.',ARRAY['Soak the stockfish for five days under running water or changing the water morning and evening: it takes time, and no shortcut works.','Cut it into large pieces keeping the skin, which brings the gelatine that binds the sauce.','Make a sofrito of onion and garlic in olive oil, add the crushed tomato and reduce for 30 minutes to a thick paste.','Flame with marc, moisten with white wine, add the fish, herbs and olives, and simmer 2½ hours without ever stirring with a spoon.','Shake the pot instead of stirring: stockfish shreds at the first touch of a spoon. Add the potatoes for the last half hour.']::text[]),
('fougasse-monegasque','en','Monegasque fougasse','Monaco-Ville, Monaco','A flat brioche with anise and orange blossom, strewn with walnuts, almonds and red and white aniseed comfits. It closes the thirteen desserts of the Monegasque Christmas Eve; the coloured comfits echo the flag, and it is not cut — it is broken.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm milk with a spoonful of sugar and let it foam for a quarter of an hour.','Knead with the flour, two eggs, sugar, salt, anise, orange blossom water and rum for 15 minutes, then work in the softened butter.','Prove 1½ hours, then flatten the dough by hand into a 2 cm oval — never with a pin, which would drive out the air.','Cut three fan-shaped slashes on top, glaze with egg and cover with chopped walnuts and almonds.','Prove 45 minutes and bake 30 minutes at 180°C. Scatter the aniseed comfits as it comes out, while the surface is still tacky.']::text[]),
('brandamincium','en','Brandamincium','Monaco-Ville, Monaco','The Monegasque ancestor of brandade, cod pounded in a mortar with milk and oil into an ointment. The Latinate name is recorded on the Rock from the Middle Ages; unlike the Nîmes brandade it contains neither potato nor cream.',ARRAY['Desalt the cod 36 hours in the fridge, skin up, changing the water five times.','Poach it 10 minutes in the milk with the bay, never boiling, then drain it keeping the milk.','Remove the skin and every bone by hand, flaking the flesh: a single bone ruins the texture.','Pound in a mortar with the garlic, then mount it by pouring in the warm oil and hot milk alternately, in thin streams, as for a mayonnaise.','That alternation is the whole method: poured too fast, either one splits the ointment. Season with white pepper and serve warm, on toast.']::text[]),
('bouillabaisse-monegasque','en','Monegasque bouillabaisse','Fontvieille, Monaco','A rockfish soup from the Larvotto, lighter than the Marseille version and without potato. Monaco adds neither excess saffron nor orange: the broth stays clear, and the mussels added to it are a liberty Marseille would never permit.',ARRAY['Gut and scale the rockfish, keep the heads, and fry them 10 minutes in oil with the onion, fennel and garlic.','Moisten with boiling water — never cold, which would seize the flesh and cloud the broth — and cook 25 minutes at a bare simmer.','Pass through a food mill pressing the bones, then through a fine chinois: the broth must be velvety and free of a single splinter.','Make the rouille by pounding garlic, an egg yolk, a pinch of cayenne and bread crumb soaked in the broth, mounted with oil.','Poach the whole fish 8 minutes in the broth, add the mussels for the last minute, and serve the broth first, the fish after.']::text[]),
('salade-monegasque','en','Monegasque salad','Monte-Carlo, Monaco','A cold rice salad with anchovies, green beans and hard-boiled egg, born in the kitchens of the Hôtel de Paris in the 1920s. The rice is what separates it from the niçoise: Monaco introduced it to make a buffet dish that holds a whole evening.',ARRAY['Cook the rice in plenty of salted water, drain it and refresh it at once under cold water to stop the cooking and rinse off the starch.','Spread it on a tray and trickle oil over while it is still warm: that is the only moment it absorbs any.','Blanch the green beans for 4 minutes then plunge them into iced water: they must stay bright green and snap.','Salt the quartered tomatoes and let them drain for twenty minutes in a colander, or their water washes out the salad.','Combine everything at the last moment with the lemon juice and oil, and tear the basil by hand. Serve cool but not chilled.']::text[]),
('beignets-de-fleurs-de-courgettes','en','Courgette flower fritters','Fontvieille, Monaco','Courgette flowers filled with herbed ricotta, dipped in beer batter and fried. Only the male flowers are picked, those on a bare stalk: the female ones carry the forming courgette, and taking them would sacrifice the crop.',ARRAY['Pick or buy the flowers the same day: after a night they close and wilt beyond rescue.','Open each flower gently and remove the pistil, which is bitter, without tearing the petals.','Work the well-drained ricotta with the parmesan and chopped basil and mint, and pipe it in without filling more than two-thirds.','Make the batter at the last minute with the flour, egg and very cold beer, deliberately leaving it lumpy — a smooth, rested batter gives a heavy crust.','Dip the flowers holding them by the stalk and fry 2 minutes at 180°C, turning them once only. Salt and squeeze lemon as they come out.']::text[]),
('omelette-plate-aux-herbes','en','Flat herb omelette','Monte-Carlo, Monaco','An omelette cooked flat, never folded, scattered with fresh herbs and parmesan. It is the croupiers'' omelette, served between night shifts: it is eaten warm or cold, cut in wedges, and stands waiting better than a runny one.',ARRAY['Chop the herbs just before beating the eggs: chopped in advance they blacken and lose their scent within a quarter of an hour.','Beat the eggs with a fork rather than a whisk, some thirty strokes only: overbeaten, they rise and then collapse.','Add the herbs, parmesan, salt and pepper, and rest five minutes while the bubbles rise.','Heat the butter and oil in a 24 cm pan until the butter foams without colouring, and pour in all at once.','Cook 5 minutes over low heat without stirring, then flip it in one movement using a plate and give it 2 minutes on the other side. Served warm, with salad.']::text[]),
('poulpe-a-la-monegasque','en','Monegasque octopus','Fontvieille, Monaco','Octopus stewed two hours in red wine, tomato and violet olives, the dish of the fishing families of the breakwater. Octopus contracts violently on meeting heat: it is dipped three times in boiling water before cooking, so the tentacles curl instead of toughening.',ARRAY['Freeze the octopus for 48 hours then thaw it: freezing breaks the fibres and stands in for the beating on the rocks the fishermen used to give it.','Bring a large pot to the boil and dip the octopus by the head three times, five seconds each, before leaving it in.','Blanch it 20 minutes, drain it and cut the tentacles into three-centimetre lengths.','Make a sofrito of onion, garlic and tomato, add the octopus, red wine, orange zest and herbs, and simmer 1½ hours covered.','Salt only at the end: octopus releases a great deal of seawater, and salted too early the dish becomes inedible. Add olives and potatoes for the last half hour.']::text[]),
('rougets-a-la-monegasque','en','Monegasque red mullet','La Condamine, Monaco','Red mullet flamed with pastis and cooked with wild fennel gathered on the Tête de Chien. Mullet is the one fish cooked ungutted: its liver, left inside, melts into the juices and scents them — which is why it is called the woodcock of the sea.',ARRAY['Scale the mullet without gutting them, working against the grain from tail to head under running water.','On no account remove the liver: it is what makes the dish, and a gutted mullet loses all its point.','Slice the fennel very finely and melt it in olive oil with the garlic for 15 minutes, without colour.','Flour the mullet barely and sear them 3 minutes a side over high heat, then flame with pastis, tilting the pan.','Add the fennel, cherry tomatoes and a splash of water, cover and give them 4 minutes. Crush the liver into the juices as you serve.']::text[]),
('epaule-agneau-monegasque','en','Monegasque stuffed lamb shoulder','Monaco-Ville, Monaco','A boned shoulder stuffed with chard and rice, roasted and served with white beans. It is the Rock''s Easter dish, as served at the Palace; the rice stuffing is Monegasque rather than Provençal, and it swells as it cooks, so it must not be packed too tight.',ARRAY['Soak the beans the day before and cook them 1½ hours with the bay, unsalted, in water that never comes to a rolling boil.','Cook the rice only halfway, 7 minutes, and refresh it: fully cooked, it would burst inside the stuffing.','Wilt the chopped chard with the onion, press it thoroughly dry and bind it with the rice, eggs and parmesan.','Lay the boned shoulder out flat, fill without packing — the stuffing swells by a good quarter — roll and tie with string every three centimetres.','Sear 20 minutes at 220°C, then 2 hours at 160°C basting with the white wine and juices. Rest 15 minutes before carving, string still on.']::text[]),
('oignons-farcis-monegasques','en','Monegasque stuffed onions','Monaco-Ville, Monaco','Large sweet onions hollowed out and filled with sausage meat and chard, gratinated. Cebe farcie are the Rock''s thrift dish: the flesh scooped from the onion goes back into the filling, and nothing is wasted — not even yesterday''s bread as crumbs.',ARRAY['Poach the whole unpeeled onions 20 minutes in salted water: cooked, they hollow out without tearing; raw, they break into layers.','Cut a lid, scoop them out with a melon baller leaving two layers of wall, and chop the flesh removed.','Fry that flesh with the garlic and chopped chard, press it dry, and mix with the sausage meat, egg, parmesan and breadcrumbs.','Fill the onions in a dome, pack them tightly in an oiled dish — tight, they hold each other up and do not collapse.','Pour broth to a third of their height and bake 55 minutes at 180°C, basting twice. Eaten warm.']::text[]),
('escargots-a-la-monegasque','en','Monegasque snails','Monte-Carlo, Monaco','Small grey snails returned to their shells under a butter of walnut, parsley and white wine. The walnut is the Monegasque signature: it replaces part of the garlic and gives the butter a grain the smoother Burgundian version lacks.',ARRAY['If starting from live snails, fast them for eight days, purge them in coarse salt and poach them 2 hours in a thyme court-bouillon.','Crush the walnuts with a knife rather than a blender: you want shards that crunch, not powder.','Cream the butter with the walnuts, parsley, garlic, shallot and the white wine reduced to nothing.','Slip a hazelnut of butter into each shell, the snail on top, then plug generously and dust with breadcrumbs.','Bake 12 minutes at 220°C, openings up: the butter should sizzle and the crumbs go gold, without the butter running over.']::text[]),
('girella-monegasque','en','Girella','La Condamine, Monaco','A bread rolled in a spiral around anchovies, onions and green olives, cut into slices. Street sellers cried girella cauda! in the lanes of the Condamine; the spiral is not decorative, it spreads the salty filling through every mouthful.',ARRAY['Knead a soft olive-oil bread dough and prove it 1½ hours covered.','Confit the sliced onions 30 minutes over very low heat, until pale and dry, and cool them completely.','A warm filling melts the dough as you roll and the spiral unwinds: that is the only real difficulty.','Roll the dough into a 5 mm rectangle and spread the onions, chopped anchovies, olives and oregano, leaving a two-centimetre border.','Roll very tightly lengthwise, seal, prove 40 minutes and bake 25 minutes at 210°C. Cut into one-centimetre slices, warm.']::text[]),
('tarte-citron-menton-monaco','en','Menton lemon tart','Monte-Carlo, Monaco','A tart of Menton lemon cream under a torched meringue, served in the palaces of Monte-Carlo. The Menton lemon has almost no biting acidity and a thick, highly scented rind: it is the zest, more than the juice, that makes the difference.',ARRAY['Line the tin, prick the base, line with paper and beans and blind-bake 15 minutes at 180°C, then 5 minutes uncovered.','Dust the still-hot base with ground almonds: they will drink the cream''s moisture and keep the pastry crisp.','Infuse the zest in the juice for an hour before cooking anything — that is where the scent is decided, not in the cooking.','Cook the zested juice, 150 g of the sugar, the eggs and cornstarch over a bain-marie to 82°C, then blend in the cold diced butter off the heat with a stick blender.','Whisk the whites with the remaining sugar to a firm meringue, pipe it onto the cold tart and torch it. Do not chill afterwards: the meringue would weep.']::text[]),
('biscuit-chataigne-miel','en','Chestnut and honey biscuit','Fontvieille, Monaco','A rustic biscuit of chestnut flour and acacia honey from the Jardin Exotique. It was made for the workers on the Fontvieille site, reclaimed from the sea in the seventies: dense and honey-sweet, it kept a man going to the end of the shift.',ARRAY['Sift the chestnut flour twice: it clumps into hard nuggets no beater will later break up.','Warm the honey with the butter without going above 50°C, beyond which acacia loses its floral scent.','Beat the eggs, add the warm mixture, then the flours, baking powder, cinnamon and salt, and finally the milk.','The batter is heavier than an ordinary biscuit dough: chestnut has no gluten and barely rises, which is normal.','Drop well-spaced spoonfuls on the tray, scatter chopped walnuts and bake 22 minutes at 170°C. They harden as they cool and keep a fortnight.']::text[]),
('endives-au-jambon','en','Belgian endive gratin','Brussels, Belgium','Braised endives wrapped in ham and gratinated under béchamel. The chicon was discovered by accident around 1830: a Schaerbeek grower had abandoned chicory roots in his cellar, and they had produced white shoots.',ARRAY['Cut out the bitter cone at the base of each endive with a pointed knife: it, not the leaf, carries the bitterness.','Braise them covered in butter for 30 minutes with a pinch of sugar and a squeeze of lemon, adding no water.','Then press them firmly between your hands: a waterlogged endive thins the béchamel and the gratin swims.','Wrap each endive in a slice of ham and pack them tightly, seam down, in a buttered dish.','Coat with a nutmegged béchamel, scatter cheese and gratinate 25 minutes at 200°C, until the top blisters.']::text[]),
('appeltaart','en','Appeltaart','Amsterdam, Netherlands','A very tall apple tart with a thick shortcrust and a lattice top, served with whipped cream. The Dutch recipe is recorded from 1514; unlike the French tart it is baked in a deep springform tin and eaten in upright wedges.',ARRAY['Rub the cold butter into the flour with half the sugar, bind with one egg and bring together without kneading; chill an hour.','Plump the raisins in warm rum and cut the apples into large wedges, never slices.','Line a 24 cm springform tin, taking the pastry 5 cm up the sides: an appeltaart is tall, that is its signature.','Scatter breadcrumbs over the base before filling — they will drink the apple juices and keep the pastry crisp.','Cover with a lattice, glaze and bake 1 hour 20 at 175°C. Cool completely in the tin, or the tart collapses when released.']::text[]),
('kibbeling','en','Kibbeling','Amsterdam, Netherlands','Chunks of cod in beer batter, fried and sold in a cone with tartare sauce. The word once meant cod cheeks, an offcut fishmongers sold cheap; today it is diced fillet, but the name has stuck.',ARRAY['Cut the cod into 4 cm cubes, salt them and chill 20 minutes to firm the flesh.','Blot them thoroughly: wet flesh makes the batter slide, and it peels off in sheets in the oil.','Make the batter at the last minute with very cold beer, leaving it lumpy — a rested batter turns heavy and soft.','Flour the cubes, dip them and lower them into oil at 180°C, six at a time, for 3 minutes.','Drain upright in a colander, salt and serve with a tartare of mayonnaise, chopped gherkin and capers.']::text[]),
('oliebollen','en','Oliebollen','Amsterdam, Netherlands','Balls of yeasted dough with raisins and apple, fried and buried in icing sugar, eaten on 31 December. The custom is said to go back to the Germanic tribes, who threw them into the fields on Yule nights so that if the goddess Perchta slit a belly, her blade would slide off the fat.',ARRAY['Plump the raisins for an hour in warm water, then drain and blot them — wet, they make the oil spit.','Beat a soft, almost pourable yeasted batter: an oliebol is spooned out and never shaped by hand.','Fold in the raisins and diced apple, and prove for an hour covered until the batter bubbles on the surface.','Scoop with two oiled spoons and drop into oil at 175°C: the balls turn themselves if they are round enough.','Allow 7 minutes in all, turning if needed — hotter, and they brown before the centre cooks. Dust with icing sugar while warm.']::text[]),
('gromperekichelcher','en','Gromperekichelcher','Luxembourg City, Luxembourg','Cakes of grated potato with parsley and onion, fried and sold at every fairground in the Grand Duchy. They are eaten with apple sauce; the word gromper comes from grumpire, a Lorraine corruption of pomme de terre.',ARRAY['Grate the potatoes coarsely and press them at once in a cloth: a large glassful of water should come out.','Let that water settle for five minutes, pour off the clear liquid and recover the white starch at the bottom, which goes back into the gratings.','That recovered starch is what binds the cake: without it you need three times the flour and the result turns pasty.','Mix with grated onion, sliced leek, eggs, parsley and salt, and work fast — the gratings darken within minutes.','Flatten spoonfuls into a centimetre of oil at 180°C and let them set 4 minutes a side, untouched. Serve with apple sauce.']::text[]),
('bouneschlupp','en','Bouneschlupp','Luxembourg City, Luxembourg','A soup of green beans, potato and bacon bound with cream, the Luxembourgish national dish. It is made with the stringy late-summer beans, snapped by hand rather than cut: the break follows the fibre and takes the string with it.',ARRAY['Snap the beans by hand into three-centimetre lengths, pulling away the string that comes loose at each break.','Render the diced bacon, add the onion and let it turn golden without burning.','Moisten with water, add the potatoes in large pieces and the savory — it is the bean herb, and without it the soup lacks depth.','Cook 35 minutes, then crush a few potatoes roughly against the side of the pot to thicken it.','Bind with the cream mixed with a spoonful of flour, off the boil, and rest a quarter of an hour before serving.']::text[]),
('quetschentaart','en','Quetschentaart','Luxembourg City, Luxembourg','A tart of damsons stood upright in the pastry, made in September when the purple plum ripens. The plums are set vertically, skin down: they release their juice as they bake and it must stay in the fruit and not in the pastry.',ARRAY['Make a shortcrust with cold butter bound with an egg, and rest it an hour in the fridge.','Stone the damsons by opening them out without separating the halves, so that they will stand up.','Scatter ground almonds and semolina over the pastry base: the mixture absorbs the juice, which damsons give in quantity.','Stand the plums upright and tightly packed, skin down, overlapping slightly — they shrink by a third.','Sugar and cinnamon, and bake 45 minutes at 190°C. Sugar again as it comes out, when the juice rises.']::text[]),
('frell-am-reisleck','en','Trout in Riesling','Moselle valley, Luxembourg','Trout poached in a Riesling from the Luxembourg Moselle, the sauce bound with cream. The wine must tremble rather than boil: brought to the boil, a Riesling loses its fine acidity and leaves a bitterness cream cannot rescue.',ARRAY['Gut the trout, salt them inside and rest them twenty minutes before cooking.','Sweat the chopped shallot in butter without colour, then lay the trout on top, head to tail so they sit flat.','Pour in the Riesling and a little water to half their depth, cover and poach 12 minutes at a tremble — never at a rolling boil.','Lift out the fish, take off the fillets and keep them warm under buttered paper.','Reduce the liquor by half, add the cream and mushrooms, thicken 5 minutes and whisk in cold butter off the heat.']::text[]),
('kachkeis','en','Kachkéis','Luxembourg City, Luxembourg','A cooked, melted cheese, runny and yellow, spread on black bread with mustard. It was born of the need to rescue a failed curd: it was left to ripen until translucent, then melted — a cheese never eaten in slices.',ARRAY['Drain fresh curd 12 hours in a cloth, then crumble it and let it ripen two days at room temperature under a tea towel.','It is ready when it begins to smell strongly and the grains turn translucent at the edges: that ripening is everything.','Dust with bicarbonate, mix and wait an hour: the curd begins to melt of its own accord under the alkalinity.','Melt it over very low heat with the butter and milk, stirring without pause to a smooth, stringy mass.','Bind with a beaten egg off the heat, salt and scent with caraway. Pour it hot into a pot and serve warm on black bread, with mustard.']::text[]),
('mielzepudder','en','Mielzepudder','Luxembourg City, Luxembourg','A semolina and milk pudding with raisins, baked and served warm. Mielz means semolina in Luxembourgish; it was the everyday pudding, made from what was always in the house — flour, milk and eggs.',ARRAY['Plump the raisins in warm rum for half an hour and drain them.','Bring the milk to a tremble with the zest, salt and half the sugar, then rain in the semolina, whisking without pause.','Cook 8 minutes over low heat, stirring: the semolina must thicken until a spoon stands up in it.','Let it cool to 50°C before adding the yolks — poured into scalding semolina they set into lumps.','Fold in the whites whisked to soft peaks and the raisins, turn into a buttered dish and bake 45 minutes at 180°C. Serve warm, never cold.']::text[]),
('obatzda','en','Obatzda','Munich, Bavaria, Germany','An overripe camembert crushed with butter and paprika, served in beer gardens with a pretzel. Katharina Eisenreich is said to have put it on the menu in 1920 to use up her runny cheeses; obatzt means mashed in Bavarian, and the gesture is in the name.',ARRAY['Choose a frankly ripe camembert, runny under the rind: a young cheese will not mash and stays in rubbery lumps.','Crush it with a fork, rind and all — the rind carries the flavour, and removing it makes a bland spread.','Cream the butter separately, then work it into the cheese by crushing, never by whisking.','Add the paprika, caraway, grated onion and a splash of beer to loosen it: the beer is the Bavarian element, bringing the bitterness that cuts the fat.','Rest it 2 hours in the cold, take it out half an hour ahead, and serve with a pretzel and radishes.']::text[]),
('dampfnudeln','en','Dampfnudeln','Munich, Bavaria, Germany','Balls of yeasted dough cooked covered in a shallow layer of buttered milk, soft on top and caramelised beneath. It all hangs on one rule: the lid is never lifted, or the steam escapes and the dumplings collapse for good.',ARRAY['Knead a soft yeasted dough with the flour, yeast, 150 ml of warm milk, one egg, 30 g of sugar and the softened butter, and prove for an hour.','Shape 70 g balls and prove them 30 minutes on a floured cloth, well spaced.','In a wide pot, melt the remaining butter with the milk and sugar: the liquid should come up barely a centimetre, no more.','Set the balls in without touching, cover with a heavy lid and give them 30 minutes over low heat — never lifting the lid.','You will hear the liquid evaporate then the base sizzle: the sign the crust is forming. Serve with a vanilla custard.']::text[]),
('halve-hahn','en','Halve Hahn','Cologne, Rhineland, Germany','A buttered rye roll with a thick slice of aged Gouda, mustard and onion. The name means half a cockerel and contains no chicken at all: legend has a Cologne joker order it that way to catch out a guest, and the name stuck.',ARRAY['Use a roggenbrötchen, a caraway rye roll: wheat bread cannot stand up to an aged Gouda.','Choose a Gouda of at least eighteen months, crystalline and brittle, and cut a slice a good centimetre thick.','The slice must be thicker than the butter and overhang the bread: in Cologne a thin halve Hahn is an offence.','Butter both faces generously, lay on the cheese, then the mustard on the side of the plate — never spread on it.','Add raw onion rings and a gherkin. Eaten by hand, with a Kölsch served in a narrow two-hundred-millilitre glass.']::text[]),
('leipziger-allerlei','en','Leipziger Allerlei','Leipzig, Saxony, Germany','A stew of spring vegetables with crayfish and marrow dumplings, bound with crayfish butter. Legend has it that the people of Leipzig served this meatless dish to beggars and tax collectors to look poor, while feasting on what it concealed.',ARRAY['Cook each vegetable separately in boiling salted water and refresh it at once: none shares a cooking time or a colour to preserve.','The carrots are glazed in butter and sugar, the asparagus poached three minutes, the peas two, the cauliflower five.','Shell the crayfish and pound the shells in a mortar with butter, then melt that butter and strain it: this is crayfish butter, and it gives the taste and the colour.','Make a velouté with the crayfish butter, flour and the vegetable cooking liquid, then bind with cream.','Combine everything off the heat, warm through without boiling, and serve at once: the vegetables lose their colour within ten minutes.']::text[]),
('quarkkeulchen','en','Quarkkeulchen','Dresden, Saxony, Germany','Small cakes of quark and yesterday''s potato, pan-fried and dusted with cinnamon sugar. It is a leftovers dish: the potatoes must have been boiled the day before, cooled and grated, as fresh potato would make the batter sticky and grey.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes in their skins the day before and cool them twelve hours: the starch retrogrades and firms up.','Peel and grate them coarsely, then drain the quark in a cloth for an hour.','Mix everything with your fingertips without working the batter: overworked, it turns elastic and the cakes shrink in the pan.','Shape cakes 8 cm across and a centimetre thick, lightly floured.','Fry in butter 4 minutes a side over medium heat, and dust with cinnamon sugar while scalding. Serve with apple sauce.']::text[]),
('braunkohl-mit-kassler','en','Braunkohl mit Kassler','Oldenburg, Lower Saxony, Germany','Curly kale long stewed with smoked pork loin and caramelised potatoes. It is made only after the first frosts: cold turns the kale''s starch to sugar, and a leaf picked before November stays tough and bitter.',ARRAY['Strip the kale from its central ribs, blanch it 5 minutes and chop it roughly by knife.','Render the bacon and onion in lard, add the kale and let it stew down 20 minutes before adding liquid.','Set the smoked loin on top and simmer 2 hours covered over low heat, never stirring the base.','Thicken with the oats in the last half hour: it is the northern thickener, and flour will not replace it.','Sauté the potatoes separately in lard with a spoonful of sugar, until they caramelise. Serve everything with strong mustard.']::text[]),
('frankfurter-grune-sosse','en','Frankfurt green sauce','Frankfurt, Hesse, Germany','A cold sauce of seven compulsory herbs, served over hard-boiled eggs and potatoes. The composition is protected: borage, chervil, cress, parsley, salad burnet, sorrel and chives, no more and no fewer — Goethe, born in Frankfurt, was said to love it.',ARRAY['Wash the seven herbs and dry them thoroughly: a single drop of water left thins the sauce and turns it within two hours.','Chop them very finely by knife, all together — a blender oxidises herbs and the sauce turns olive brown.','Crush four hard-boiled yolks with the mustard, vinegar and oil to make an emulsified base.','Fold in the smetana and yoghurt, then the herbs, and dice the remaining whites to add.','Rest 3 hours in the cold, no longer: beyond that the herbs blacken. Serve over hard-boiled eggs and hot potatoes.']::text[]),
('ahle-worscht','en','Ahle Worscht','Kassel, Hesse, Germany','A dry sausage from northern Hesse, cased with meat still warm from the slaughter. That is its whole peculiarity: the meat must be worked within two hours, before the pH falls, otherwise it no longer binds and the sausage crumbles.',ARRAY['Work the meat within two hours of slaughter, still warm: that is the imperative condition of Ahle Worscht.','Mince the shoulder and belly on the medium plate, keeping visible fat, and salt at 25 g per kilo.','Pepper very generously and knead for five minutes only: overworked, the mass warms and the fat melts.','Fill the casings firmly, expelling air, and tie into horseshoes or straight lengths according to the village''s tradition.','Dry three weeks at 15°C, then age three to six months in a cellar. It must lose a third of its weight and just hold together when sliced.']::text[]),
('birchermuesli','en','Birchermüesli','Zurich, Switzerland','Oat flakes soaked overnight, mixed with grated apple, nuts and condensed milk. Maximilian Bircher-Brenner devised it around 1900 in his Zurich sanatorium: it was not a breakfast but a starter, served before every meal, and apple far outweighed oats.',ARRAY['Soak the oats in milk overnight in the fridge: soaked for an hour they stay dry and harsh at the centre.','The next day, grate the unpeeled apples at the last moment, straight over the bowl.','Douse them at once with lemon juice: without it grated apple browns within three minutes and the müesli turns grey.','The original recipe holds three times more apple than oats — the reverse of what modern packets sell.','Fold in the condensed milk, cream, honey and chopped nuts, and serve immediately, still cold.']::text[]),
('luxemburgerli','en','Luxemburgerli','Zurich, Switzerland','Tiny macarons, smaller and lighter than the Parisian kind, filled with an aerated ganache. A Luxembourgish apprentice at Sprüngli brought them back around 1957; his Zurich colleagues named them after him, and the name has been trademarked since.',ARRAY['Sift the ground almonds and icing sugar together twice, then a third time through a fine sieve: a single grain makes a crater in the shell.','Whisk an Italian meringue by pouring syrup cooked to 118°C onto the whites, and beat until the bowl cools.','Macaronner by pressing the mass against the side until it ribbons and settles within thirty seconds — no more, no less.','Pipe shells only 2 cm across, rap the tray and let them skin for 30 minutes: you should be able to touch the surface without it sticking.','Bake 12 minutes at 150°C, fill with a whipped ganache and mature 24 hours in the cold before serving.']::text[]),
('engadiner-nusstorte','en','Engadine nut tart','Scuol, Engadine, Switzerland','A closed shortcrust tart filled with caramelised walnuts in cream. Engadine emigrants who worked as confectioners in Italy and France brought it back in the early 20th century; it keeps three weeks without loss, which made it the cake of mountain passes and long journeys.',ARRAY['Make a rich shortcrust with butter and one egg, and rest it 2 hours: it is fragile and worked cold.','Make a dry caramel with the sugar, with no water and no stirring — stirred, it crystallises and you must start again.','Stop the cooking with hot cream poured in three additions, standing back: it spits violently and seizes for a moment before smoothing.','Add the roughly chopped walnuts and the honey, and cool completely before filling.','Line, fill, cover with a disc, seal, glaze and prick the top. Bake 45 minutes at 180°C and wait two days before cutting.']::text[]),
('capuns','en','Capuns','Chur, Grisons, Switzerland','Chard leaves wrapped around a spätzle batter and cured meats, poached then baked in cream. Every Grisons valley has its version and nobody agrees on the size: some make them thumb-sized, others like a cigar, and the argument passes for conversation.',ARRAY['Blanch the chard leaves 20 seconds and refresh them: any longer and they tear when folded.','Flatten the central rib with a knife laid flat, without cutting it — it must bend and not snap.','Beat a thick spätzle batter with the flour, eggs, milk and salt, and fold in the diced bacon and salami.','Set a spoonful on each leaf and roll it up, folding the sides in like a small parcel, seam underneath.','Poach 12 minutes in simmering broth, pack them tightly in a dish, coat with cream and cheese and bake 15 minutes at 200°C.']::text[]),
('kiachln','en','Tyrolean Kiachln','Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria','Rounds of yeasted dough stretched by hand with a thick rim, fried in lard. The rim is left thick and the centre almost translucent: it is filled with lingonberry jam or, in the savoury version, sauerkraut — the hollow is deliberate.',ARRAY['Knead a soft yeasted dough with warm milk, eggs, sugar, rum and butter, and prove it 1½ hours.','Divide into 80 g balls and prove them 30 minutes under a cloth.','Stretch each ball by hand over the backs of your fists, turning: the centre must go almost transparent, the rim stay thick.','A rolling pin would flatten the rim and the Kiachl would have no hollow: the shaping is done by hand, without exception.','Lower them flat into lard at 175°C, 90 seconds a side. Fill the hollow with lingonberry jam or hot sauerkraut.']::text[]),
('salzburger-rindfleisch','en','Salzburg beef with mushrooms','Salzburg, Austria','Beef braised with mushrooms in a cream and red wine sauce, served with Spätzle. This is the bourgeois cooking of alpine Austria: the sauce is thickened with neither flour nor starch but by reduction, which means never covering the pot at the end.',ARRAY['Sear the beef in large cubes in butter in small batches, until frankly coloured on every face.','Lift it out, soften the onion, deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate entirely before adding the broth.','Braise 1½ hours covered with the bay and juniper, then uncover for the last half hour.','Sauté the mushrooms separately over high heat in a very hot pan: added raw to the pot they would weep and thin the sauce.','Add them with the cream and reduce uncovered until the sauce coats. Serve with Spätzle.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('aebleskiver','en','Æbleskiver','Copenhagen, Denmark','Small balls of batter cooked in a dimpled pan, served with icing sugar and jam through Advent. The name means apple slices: one was once slipped into the centre, a habit long abandoned, but the name has stayed and so has the hollow.',ARRAY['Separate the eggs: the yolks go into the batter, the whites whisked to peaks are folded in last.','The whites are what give the lightness: without them an æbleskiv is a dense, dull ball.','Heat the dimpled pan and butter every hollow generously before each batch, never once for all.','Fill the hollows three-quarters full and wait for the edge to set, then turn a quarter turn with a knitting needle.','Repeat the quarter turn four or five times: the still-raw batter runs downward at each rotation and closes the sphere. Dust with sugar and serve with jam.']::text[]),
('rugbrod','en','Rugbrød','Copenhagen, Denmark','A dense, almost black rye bread studded with seeds, the compulsory base of smørrebrød. It is baked in a rectangular tin rather than as a round: it must slice thin and even to carry a topping, and the average Dane eats several slices a day.',ARRAY['Soak the seeds overnight in the beer and a little water: dry, they would draw water out of the dough and the bread would be dry.','Mix the starter, warm water, flours, treacle and salt — rye dough is sticky and is not kneaded, it is stirred with a spoon.','Do not try to develop gluten: rye has almost none, and working the dough only makes it gummy.','Pour into a buttered loaf tin, smooth with a wet spatula and prove 4 to 6 hours, until cracks appear on the surface.','Bake 1 hour 40 at 180°C, turn out and cool 24 hours before slicing: cut warm it clings to the knife and seems raw.']::text[]),
('remoulade-danoise','en','Danish remoulade','Copenhagen, Denmark','A pale yellow sauce with chopped vegetables and turmeric, served on hot dogs and breaded fish. Little remains of the French remoulade: the Danes added cauliflower, sugar and turmeric, and get through over two kilos a head each year.',ARRAY['Blanch the cauliflower in very small florets for 4 minutes and refresh it: raw, it stays hard and acrid in the sauce.','Chop the gherkins, cauliflower, capers and onion by knife into one- or two-millimetre dice — long work with no substitute.','Steep the onion ten minutes in the sweetened vinegar, then drain it keeping the vinegar.','Mix everything into the mayonnaise with the mustard, turmeric, sugar and the reserved vinegar.','Rest 24 hours in the cold: the sauce is bland on the first day and right on the second. The turmeric keeps colouring overnight.']::text[]),
('koldskal','en','Koldskål','Copenhagen, Denmark','A cold buttermilk soup with vanilla and lemon, eaten with a spoon in summer with small biscuits. It appears in Danish kitchens when the thermometer passes twenty degrees and vanishes in September: supermarkets sell pallets of it for three months and none the rest of the year.',ARRAY['Whisk the yolks with the sugar and scraped vanilla until pale and ribboning.','Koldskål is never cooked: the yolks stay raw, which requires very fresh eggs and serving the same day.','Pour in the very cold buttermilk in a stream, whisking without pause, so the mixture does not split.','Add the yoghurt, cream and lemon zest, then the juice, drop by drop and last — poured too early it curdles the buttermilk.','Chill 2 hours and serve iced, with cut strawberries and crumbled kammerjunker on top.']::text[]),
('braendende-kaerlighed','en','Brændende kærlighed','Copenhagen, Denmark','Mashed potato hollowed into a crater and filled with fried bacon and onion. The name means burning love: the crater of golden fat in the white suggested a volcano, and it was the end-of-the-month meal in Danish households.',ARRAY['Boil the potatoes whole in their skins and peel them scalding: cut before cooking they take on water.','Rice them and beat in the butter then the hot milk, never using a blender.','Fry the bacon gently so it renders before it crisps, then soften the onion in that fat for twenty minutes.','The fat is not discarded: it is the heart of the dish, and it is what fills the crater.','Mound the mash, hollow it with a spoon, fill it with the scalding mixture and serve with pickled beetroot.']::text[]),
('golabki','en','Gołąbki','Warsaw, Poland','Cabbage leaves stuffed with meat, rice and mushrooms, stewed in tomato sauce. The name means little pigeons: Poland shares the practice with Russia, but almost always adds dried mushrooms, giving the filling a woodland depth golubtsy lack.',ARRAY['Soak the dried mushrooms an hour in warm water and keep the strained liquid: it is worth a broth.','Freeze the whole cabbage for two days then thaw it: the leaves then come away by themselves, without blanching, and do not tear.','Cook the rice halfway, 7 minutes, and mix it with the meats, chopped mushrooms, onion and marjoram.','Roll tightly, folding the sides in, and line the base of the pot with damaged cabbage leaves so nothing catches.','Pack the rolls in, pour over the tomato and mushroom liquor, and simmer 1½ hours covered without ever stirring. Serve with smetana.']::text[]),
('placki-ziemniaczane','en','Placki ziemniaczane','Kraków, Poland','Fried grated potato cakes, served with sour cream or sugar. In Silesia they come under a goulash, elsewhere with apple sauce: one of the few Polish dishes eaten equally savoury or sweet, and the argument is unresolved.',ARRAY['Grate the potatoes on the fine holes, not the coarse: the Polish placki is finer and smoother than a latke or a rösti.','Grate the onion at the same time straight into the gratings: its acidity slows oxidation and delays the darkening.','Press only lightly — unlike the Luxembourg cake, a placki keeps some of its water, which makes it soft in the middle.','Add the eggs, flour, salt and plenty of pepper, and cook without delay: the batter breaks down within a quarter of an hour.','Fry in half a centimetre of hot oil, 3 minutes a side. Serve with sour cream, or sweetened with apple sauce.']::text[]),
('sernik','en','Sernik','Poznań, Poland','A cake of twaróg, the Polish fresh cheese passed three times through a sieve, denser and less sweet than a cheesecake. Twaróg is not cream cheese: it is lean and grainy, and it is the repeated sieving, not the fat, that gives the smooth texture.',ARRAY['Pass the twaróg three times through a fine sieve: twice is not enough, and a blender makes it elastic instead of smooth.','Cream the softened butter with the sugar for 8 minutes, then add the yolks one at a time, waiting for full absorption.','Fold in the cheese, the potato starch — never cornstarch, which leaves a taste — the zest and vanilla.','Whisk the whites to soft peaks and fold them in three times with a spatula: they are what stop a sernik being solid.','Bake 1 hour 20 at 160°C, then cool for an hour with the door ajar: taken out suddenly, it sinks in the centre and cracks.']::text[]),
('kielbasa-grillee','en','Grilled kiełbasa','Warsaw, Poland','A smoked pork and beef sausage, split and grilled, served in bread with mustard. Kiełbasa wiejska, the country sausage, is sold by weight in a coil; splitting it before grilling is no detail, it is what stops it bursting and losing its juices.',ARRAY['Choose a smoked but uncooked kiełbasa, yielding under the finger: pre-cooked sausages dry on the grill and turn mealy.','Split it lengthwise without cutting through the skin underneath, so it opens flat like a book.','Grill it cut side down first over grey, flameless coals for 6 minutes, then turn it onto the skin.','The skin should swell and brown without splitting: if it hisses and spurts, your fire is too fierce and the juice is going.','Grill the onion rings in the fat that has fallen on the plate, and serve the sausage in rye bread with strong mustard.']::text[]),
('suya','en','Suya','Abuja, Nigeria','Strips of beef coated in yaji, a groundnut and spice powder, grilled over coals. Hausa mallams sell them at night from wooden racks; the yaji goes on again after grilling, never only before.',ARRAY['Dry-roast the peanuts, rub off the skins between your hands, then grind them.','Press the powder in a cloth to squeeze out the oil: too oily, it will not cling to the meat.','Mix it with the paprika, cayenne, ginger, garlic and onion powder and the crumbled stock cube: this is yaji.','Cut the beef into very thin strips, thread them concertina-style on skewers, oil and roll in the yaji.','Grill for 6 minutes, turning, then dust with yaji again as it comes off. Serve with raw onion, tomato and cabbage.']::text[]),
('attieke-poisson','en','Attiéké with grilled fish','Abidjan, Ivory Coast','Fermented cassava semolina, served with grilled fish and a sharp onion relish. Attiéké is known by its sourness: that is the cassava fermenting, and it answers the oily fish.',ARRAY['Scale and gut the fish, score it three times each side and rub it with lemon and salt.','Pound the garlic, ginger, one onion, the chilli and the stock cube, and push the paste into the cuts and the belly.','Marinate for an hour, then grill over moderate coals 15 minutes a side, basting with oil.','Steam the attiéké for 15 minutes and fluff it with a fork to separate the grains.','Make the raw relish: sliced onion, tomato, chilli and lemon, uncooked. Serve everything together.']::text[]),
('poulet-moambe','en','Chicken moambe','Kinshasa, DR Congo','Chicken stewed in palm nut pulp, with cassava leaves alongside. It is the national dish of both Congos; eaten with the fingers and chikwangue, the cassava paste in its wrapper.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken for 2 hours in pounded garlic and ginger with lemon.','Slake the palm nut pulp in a litre of hot water and pass it through a sieve to remove the fibres.','Colour the chicken in palm oil, add the onion and tomato, then pour in the palm juice.','Simmer uncovered for 50 minutes: the orange oil must rise clearly to the surface.','Separately cook the pounded cassava leaves for 1 hour with the groundnut. Serve the two side by side, with rice.']::text[]),
('djenkoume','en','Djenkoumé','Lomé, Togo','A maize flour cake cooked in tomato sauce, cut into squares and served with chicken. It turns out like a cake and is eaten by hand: Togo makes it the dish of Sundays and christenings.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken in garlic, ginger and stock cube, then fry it until golden.','In the same oil, fry the onion and tomato paste, then add the blended tomato and chilli.','Reduce for 25 minutes: the sauce should be very concentrated, almost a paste.','Take out two thirds of the sauce, let it down with boiling water and rain in the maize flour.','Work for 20 minutes until the paste leaves the sides, turn into an oiled tin, let it set and cut into squares.']::text[]),
('riz-gras-guineen','en','Guinean riz gras','Conakry, Guinea','Rice cooked in a broth of smoked fish, tomato and sweet potato leaves. Guinea smokes its fish on wooden racks: it is that smoke, more than the tomato, that flavours the rice.',ARRAY['Soak the smoked fish for 20 minutes in hot water, then flake it, removing the bones.','Fry the tomato paste in the oil for 8 minutes, until the oil turns red.','Add the onion, garlic, fresh tomato and smoked fish, and reduce for 20 minutes.','Add two litres of water, the leaves, okra and whole chilli, and cook for 25 minutes.','Lift out the vegetables, pour the rice into the remaining broth and let it absorb for 25 minutes, covered.']::text[]),
('cachupa-rica','en','Cachupa rica','Praia, Santiago, Cape Verde','A stew of cracked maize, beans and every available meat, simmered half a day. The next day it is fried with an egg: cachupa guisada, and many prefer it that way.',ARRAY['Soak the maize and beans separately overnight: cracked maize needs twelve hours.','Cook the maize for 1½ hours in a large pot, then add the beans for 45 minutes.','Add the meats in large pieces, the onion, garlic and bay, and simmer for 1 hour.','Add the roots and squash in order of firmness, then the cabbage for the last 20 minutes.','Crush a little squash against the side to thicken. Next day, fry it in a pan with a fried egg.']::text[]),
('thiebou-yapp','en','Thiébou yapp','Nouakchott, Mauritania','The meat rice of the Senegal River, browned with tomato and long-cooked onion, without fish. It is served in a single dish set on the ground, each person eating from the quarter facing them.',ARRAY['Colour the lamb in large pieces in the oil, then lift it out.','Melt the sliced onions in the same oil for 40 minutes, until they brown: they give the colour.','Add the tomato paste and let it darken, then the fresh tomato, garlic and two litres of water.','Return the meat and simmer for 50 minutes, then add the vegetables in order of firmness and remove each when done.','Pour the washed rice into the remaining broth, cover and let it absorb for 25 minutes over very low heat.']::text[]),
('riz-au-gras-burkinabe','en','Burkinabè riz gras','Ouagadougou, Burkina Faso','Rice simmered in a tomato and meat broth, cooked in a single pot. It is the dish of the maquis bars and of ceremonies; the rice must stay firm and each grain keep its colour.',ARRAY['Marinate the chicken in garlic, ginger and crumbled stock cube for an hour.','Colour it in the oil, lift it out, and fry the tomato paste for 8 minutes in the same oil.','Add the onion, blended tomato and whole chilli, and reduce for 20 minutes.','Add three litres of water, return the chicken and cook for 30 minutes, then add the carrot and cabbage.','Lift out the meat and vegetables, pour in the washed rice and let it absorb the broth for 25 minutes, covered.']::text[]),
('kilishi','en','Kilishi','Niamey, Niger','Sheets of beef sun-dried, coated in spiced groundnut paste, then grilled. The meat is sliced so thin you can read through it: a kilo of beef yields under three hundred grams of kilishi.',ARRAY['Cut the beef into 2 mm sheets along the grain: the knife must be long and perfectly sharp.','Spread them on racks and sun-dry for 6 hours, turning, until they stiffen.','Grind the roasted peanuts and mix them with water, ginger, garlic, cayenne and stock cube into a thin paste.','Dip each sheet in the paste and return it to dry for 3 hours, until the coating sets.','Grill for 4 minutes over gentle coals, watching closely: groundnut burns in seconds.']::text[]),
('kanyah-liberien','en','Kanyah','Monrovia, Liberia','A bar of toasted rice, groundnut and sugar, pressed by hand and cut into squares. It is the Liberian travelling ration: no cooking, keeps a week, and sees a man through to evening.',ARRAY['Dry-toast the raw rice in a heavy pan, stirring constantly, until it browns and pops.','Roast the peanuts separately and rub off the skins between your hands.','Grind the toasted rice very finely, then the peanuts, into an oily powder.','Mix the two with the sugar, nutmeg and salt, and add the oil a spoonful at a time until the mass holds together.','Press firmly into a flat mould, let it firm for an hour and cut into squares.']::text[]),
('gozo-sauce-koko','en','Gozo with koko sauce','Bangui, Central African Republic','A cassava paste with a sauce of koko leaves, smoked meat and groundnut. The koko leaf is a forest vine that is never cultivated: it is gathered, and slicing it is an art.',ARRAY['Slice the koko leaves into hair-fine ribbons: it takes time, and it makes the texture of the sauce.','Soak the smoked fish, flake it and remove every bone.','Heat the palm oil, add the onion, garlic and chilli, then the leaves and half a litre of water.','Simmer for 40 minutes: the leaves should lose their bitterness without turning black.','Slake the groundnut and stir it in with the fish for 20 minutes. Serve with gozo, the cassava paste worked with boiling water.']::text[]),
('haricots-bananes-vertes','en','Beans with green banana','Bujumbura, Burundi','Red beans stewed with green plantains and palm oil. It is the daily meal of the Burundian hills: the bean for strength, the banana to last until evening.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then cook them for 1 hour in unsalted water.','Peel the green plantains under water — their sap stains the hands lastingly — and cut them into lengths.','Fry the onion, garlic, leek and celery in palm oil, and add the tomato.','Pour this sofrito over the beans with their liquid, add the plantains and the whole chilli.','Simmer for 40 minutes: the plantains should turn meltingly soft and the broth thicken by itself.']::text[]),
('matapa','en','Matapa','Maputo, Mozambique','Cassava leaves pounded with coconut milk, with crab and prawns, served over rice. Mozambique pounds the leaf and the groundnut in the same mortar: the two merge and the sauce turns green and rich.',ARRAY['Pound the cassava leaves and peanuts together, at length, into a coarse paste.','Blanch them for 20 minutes in plenty of water and squeeze them dry: the raw leaf is toxic, and long cooking is compulsory.','Fry the onion and garlic in oil, add the pressed paste and half a litre of water.','Simmer for 30 minutes, then pour in the coconut milk and continue for 15 minutes without boiling.','Add the crab and prawns for the last 8 minutes only. Serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('potjie-de-gibier','en','Game potjie','Windhoek, Namibia','An oryx or kudu stew cooked in layers in a three-legged pot over embers. Namibia eats game as others eat beef: the meat is lean, and the bacon added to it is not a luxury.',ARRAY['Marinate the game for 12 hours in red wine with juniper and bay: the marinade tenderises and softens the venison note.','Render the bacon in the pot set over moderate embers, then sear the drained meat.','Add the onion, strained marinade and stock, and simmer covered for 2 hours.','Arrange the vegetables in distinct layers, longest-cooking first, and never stir again.','Give it another hour. Serve by digging in from the edge, to keep the tiers visible.']::text[]),
('papa-ea-poone','en','Papa ea poone','Maseru, Lesotho','A paste of freshly grated maize, cooked slowly, served with moroho, the greens from the garden. Lesotho makes it with harvest maize, still milky: it is sweet without anything being added.',ARRAY['Grate the fresh maize kernels on a coarse grater, catching the milk that runs from them.','Cook them gently with the collected milk for 25 minutes, stirring: the mass thickens by itself.','Rain in the maize flour to firm it up, and work for another 15 minutes with a stick.','For the moroho, wilt the greens with the onion and tomato, covered, for 20 minutes.','Serve the paste as a dome, hollowed into a well of butter, with the greens alongside.']::text[]),
('sishwala','en','Sishwala with chicken','Mbabane, Eswatini','A maize porridge thicker than pap, served with a stewed farmyard chicken. Sishwala can be cut with a knife once cool: that is what sets it apart from the region''s other pastes.',ARRAY['Colour the jointed chicken in the oil, then add the onion and garlic.','Add the crushed tomato and crumbled stock cube, barely moisten and simmer covered for 50 minutes.','Bring a litre and a half of salted water to the boil and rain in the maize flour, whisking.','Work it for 30 minutes over low heat with a wooden stick: sishwala should be firmer than an ordinary pap.','Serve the paste moulded in a wetted bowl, with the chicken and pumpkin leaves in groundnut.']::text[]),
('cari-poisson-seychellois','en','Coconut fish curry','Victoria, Mahé, Seychelles','A reef fish curry with fresh coconut milk, turmeric and curry leaves. Seychelles grate the nut on a bladed stool, the grajeur: hand-pressed milk tastes nothing like the tin.',ARRAY['Cut the fish into thick steaks and rub them with turmeric, salt and lime; leave 20 minutes.','Fry the pounded onion, garlic and ginger in oil with the curry leaves and cinnamon.','Add the tomato and the remaining turmeric, and reduce for 10 minutes.','Pour in the coconut milk and bring to a bare simmer, never boiling: it would split.','Slide in the steaks and poach for 12 minutes uncovered, basting. Lime at the last moment.']::text[]),
('vindaye-de-poisson','en','Vindaye fish','Port Louis, Mauritius','Fried fish marinated in turmeric, mustard and vinegar, eaten cold. Vindaye descends from Goan vindaloo; in Mauritius it keeps a week and improves daily.',ARRAY['Cut the tuna into thick slices, rub them with turmeric and salt, and fry them until coloured.','Crush the mustard seeds in a mortar with the garlic, ginger and a little vinegar.','Fry this paste in the oil for 8 minutes, without letting it burn: the mustard would turn bitter.','Add the sliced onion and split chillies, and barely wilt them: they should stay crunchy.','Pour in the rest of the vinegar, let it cool, then submerge the fish in it. Wait 24 hours and serve cold.']::text[]),
('langouste-a-la-vanille','en','Lobster with vanilla','Moroni, Comoros','Lobster in coconut milk scented with Comoran vanilla. The islands grow ylang-ylang and vanilla; pairing the pod with shellfish is an island idea, and it works.',ARRAY['Split the live lobsters lengthways in one clean stroke, and remove the grit sac.','Sear them flesh down in foaming butter for 4 minutes, then lift them out.','Sweat the shallot, garlic and ginger, deglaze with white wine and reduce by half.','Add the coconut milk and the split, scraped vanilla pods, and infuse for 10 minutes at a simmer.','Return the lobsters shell down and cook for 8 minutes, basting. Lime and coriander at the end.']::text[]),
('oladii','en','Oladii','Moscow, Russia','Small thick pancakes made with kefir, taller and softer than blini, served at breakfast with jam. The kefir must be frankly sour: its acidity reacts with the bicarbonate and lifts the batter, with no yeast and no waiting.',ARRAY['Take the kefir out an hour ahead: cold, it does not react with the bicarbonate and the oladii stay flat.','Beat the egg, sugar and salt into the kefir, then tip in the sifted flour all at once and mix in twenty turns only.','Add the bicarbonate last and stop stirring altogether: the bubbles form at once, and stirring drives them out.','Rest ten minutes untouched — the surface should crater — then spoon it out without disturbing the bottom.','Cook in a centimetre of hot oil, 2 minutes a side. Serve with apricot jam and smetana.']::text[]),
('pirog-au-chou','en','Cabbage pirog','Suzdal, Russia','A large brioche pie of melted cabbage and hard-boiled egg, cut into squares and served at feasts. In the north a coin was hidden inside to mark who would be lucky that year; the cabbage must be long melted, or it weeps and soaks the dough.',ARRAY['Knead a brioche dough with the flour, warm milk, yeast, one egg, the butter and salt, and prove for 1½ hours.','Slice the cabbage very finely and melt it in a pan for 40 minutes with the onion, covered at first then uncovered.','It must have lost three-quarters of its volume and stopped weeping altogether: that is the whole success of a pirog.','Cool it completely, then add three chopped hard-boiled eggs and the dill.','Line a tin, fill, cover with a second sheet, seal by pinching, glaze and bake 50 minutes at 180°C. Eaten warm, cut into squares.']::text[]),
('koulibiak-royal','en','Royal koulibiak','Saint Petersburg, Russia','A layered pie: salmon, rice, mushrooms and egg, separated by thin pancakes. The pancakes are no ornament: they absorb the moisture of each layer and stop the base pastry staying raw, and Escoffier kept them when he took it to Paris.',ARRAY['Make three very thin pancakes with the flour, milk and one egg, and cool them flat on a cloth.','Dry out the chopped mushrooms in a pan for 20 minutes with the shallot, until they stop sizzling.','Cook the rice, hard-boil two eggs, cut the salmon into slabs and let everything cool completely.','Build on the pastry: pancake, rice, salmon, mushrooms, chopped egg, pancake, pressing each layer and salting at every stage.','Close, seal, glaze, cut two chimneys and bake 20 minutes at 210°C then 35 minutes at 170°C. Rest 15 minutes before slicing.']::text[]),
('chebureki','en','Chebureki','Grozny, Chechnya, Russia','Large lamb turnovers fried in boiling oil, their dough blistered with bubbles. The filling holds set broth that melts as it cooks: the first bite releases scalding juice, and a chebureki is always held tilted downward.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, iced water, salt and a spoonful of vodka into a firm dough: the vodka boils off in the fryer and is what makes the blisters.','Rest the dough for an hour, wrapped, so it stretches without shrinking back.','Chop the onion very finely — almost to a purée — and mix it with the lamb and the cold broth set to a jelly.','Roll 20 cm discs to 1 mm, spread a thin layer of filling on one half only, and seal with a fork, expelling all the air.','Fry 3 minutes at 190°C, ladling oil over the top: the dough must blister into large brown bubbles. Eat scalding, tilted over the plate.']::text[]),
('belyashi','en','Belyashi','Kazan, Tatarstan, Russia','Small yeasted buns open at the centre, filled with meat and pan-fried, hole down first. The hole is not decorative: it lets steam escape instead of bursting the dough, and it half closes as it cooks.',ARRAY['Knead a soft yeasted dough with the flour, warm milk, yeast, egg, sugar and salt, and prove for 1½ hours.','Mix the minced meats with grated onion and two spoonfuls of iced water: that water becomes the juice, and without it the filling stays dry.','Do not pre-cook the filling: it goes in raw and cooks inside the bun, which is what separates a belyash from an ordinary filled fritter.','Flatten 60 g balls, fill them, gather the edges to the centre in pleats and leave a hole a finger wide.','Fry in two centimetres of oil at 165°C, hole down for 4 minutes, then turn for 4 more. Low heat: the raw meat must cook before the dough browns.']::text[]),
('echpochmak','en','Echpochmak','Kazan, Tatarstan, Russia','A triangle of yeasted dough filled with raw beef and potato, pierced at the top so broth can be poured in. The name means three corners in Tatar; meat and potato go in raw and cook together sealed, which needs a full hour of oven and no less.',ARRAY['Knead a yeasted dough with milk, butter and one egg, and prove it for 1½ hours.','Cut the beef and potato by knife into 5 mm dice — minced they would turn to mush; larger, they would not cook.','Mix them with very finely chopped onion, salt heavily and pepper: it is the only seasoning, and it must be forthright.','Cut 15 cm discs, fill the centre and raise three sides, sealing them into a triangle and leaving a one-centimetre hole at the top.','Bake 20 minutes at 200°C, then pour a spoonful of hot broth through the hole and continue 40 minutes at 170°C. Repeat once halfway through.']::text[]),
('pryanik-de-toula','en','Tula pryanik','Tula, Russia','A flat spice bread filled with jam and stamped from a carved wooden mould. Tula was capital both of gunsmithing and of the pryanik; it was presented to tsars, and it keeps three months, growing softer rather than drying out.',ARRAY['Heat the honey and sugar to 110°C, then pour them scalding onto the flours mixed with the spices, and work with a spoon.','This scalded dough is the key: it gelatinises the starch and gives the pryanik its lasting suppleness.','Cool it completely, then work in the eggs, butter and bicarbonate, and rest it 12 hours in the cold.','Roll two 5 mm sheets, spread apple jam on one leaving a two-centimetre border, and cover with the second, sealing.','Stamp the pattern with a floured wooden mould, pressing once cleanly and never twice, and bake 22 minutes at 190°C. Glaze with sugar as it comes out.']::text[]),
('ptichye-moloko','en','Ptichye moloko','Moscow, Russia','A cake of agar mousse under a dark chocolate shell, created at the Praga restaurant in 1968. It is the only Soviet cake to have been patented; its name comes from bird''s milk, the unobtainable food of ancient myth, and people queued two hours to buy one.',ARRAY['Soak the agar for an hour in cold water, then bring it to the boil with the sugar and hold it 2 minutes at 110°C.','Agar is not gelatine: it must boil to activate, and it sets at 40°C, which leaves very little time to work.','Whisk the whites with the lemon juice, then pour in the boiling syrup in a stream, whisking without pause, and beat until it cools.','Fold in the softened butter creamed with the condensed milk, in three additions, with a spatula and without delay.','Pour onto a sponge base baked separately, smooth it and set 3 hours in the cold, then coat with melted chocolate cooled to 30°C.']::text[]),
('smetannik','en','Smetannik','Ufa, Bashkortostan, Russia','A Bashkir soured-cream cake, soft and tangy, built in thin layers. The smetana does everything: its fat replaces butter in the batter and its acidity activates the bicarbonate, so no leaven is needed.',ARRAY['Beat 400 g of the smetana with the eggs, 150 g of the sugar and the salt, then add the flour and bicarbonate.','The bicarbonate foams on meeting the smetana: pour into the tins at once, a batter that waits collapses.','Divide the batter in two and add cocoa to one half, then bake four thin 20 cm discs, 10 minutes each at 180°C.','Whisk the remaining smetana with the remaining sugar without ever whipping it like cream: overbeaten, it splits and weeps whey.','Build alternating pale and dark discs, coat the top and sides, scatter walnuts and let it soak 6 hours in the cold.']::text[]),
('vatrushka','en','Vatrushka','Moscow, Russia','A yeasted bun hollowed into a well of sweetened tvorog, round as the sun. It was a pagan offering to the hearth god before becoming the pastry of every Soviet canteen; the tvorog must be well drained, or it swells and overflows the well.',ARRAY['Drain the tvorog 3 hours in a cloth: it is the only precaution that matters, and it decides everything else.','Knead a yeasted dough with the flour, warm milk, yeast, one egg, the butter and 40 g of the sugar, and prove 1½ hours.','Sieve the tvorog with a yolk, the remaining sugar, the vanilla and a spoonful of semolina, which will take up any residual moisture.','Shape 70 g balls, flatten them and press the well with the floured base of a glass, leaving a two-centimetre rim.','Fill, glaze the rim only, prove 30 minutes and bake 22 minutes at 190°C. The tvorog should barely colour.']::text[]),
('koulitch','en','Easter kulich','Moscow, Russia','A tall narrow raisin brioche, iced white and sprinkled with coloured strands, blessed at church on Holy Saturday. It is taller than it is wide because it imitates the prosphoron, the liturgical bread; a dried piece is kept all year.',ARRAY['Make a starter with the yeast, warm milk and 150 g of the flour, and let it double for an hour.','Add the yolks beaten with the sugar, the saffron infused in a spoonful of hot milk, the vanilla and salt, then the remaining flour.','Knead 20 minutes before working in the softened butter little by little: this dough is very rich and takes a long time to come smooth.','Prove 3 hours, fold in the rum-soaked fruit, fill the tall tins only halfway and prove 2 hours more.','Bake 50 minutes at 170°C, covering with paper as soon as the top colours. Cool it lying on its side, turning regularly, then ice with egg white and sugar.']::text[]),
('kasha-grechka','en','Buckwheat kasha','Moscow, Russia','Toasted buckwheat cooked by absorption, served with butter, mushrooms and onion. The proverb says kasha is our mother: it has been the base of the Russian army''s diet since Suvorov, and it is never stirred while it cooks.',ARRAY['Toast the buckwheat dry in the pan for 4 minutes, stirring, until it smells of hazelnuts — untoasted buckwheat gives a soft, bland kasha.','Pour in exactly two parts boiling salted water to one of grain, and do not measure by eye.','Bring to the boil, cover tightly and drop to the lowest heat: from that point the lid is not lifted and nothing is stirred.','Give it 20 minutes, then kill the heat and let it swell 10 minutes more, still covered.','Meanwhile fry the onion with the fresh mushrooms and the rehydrated dried ones. Add the butter and mushrooms with a fork, fluffing the grains.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('golubtsy','en','Golubtsy','Krasnodar, Russia','Cabbage leaves stuffed with meat and rice, stewed in a tomato and smetana sauce. Golub means pigeon: the plump rolls reminded Russians of roast pigeon, a rich man''s dish they imitated with cabbage.',ARRAY['Cut out the cabbage core and plunge the whole head into boiling water for 10 minutes, peeling off leaves as they soften.','Flatten the central rib of each leaf with a rolling pin or knife: without that it snaps as you fold and the filling escapes.','Cook the rice only halfway, 7 minutes, and mix it with the meat and the grated onion and carrot, salting heavily.','Roll them folding the sides inward like a parcel, and pack the rolls tightly seam down in the pot.','Coat with tomato and smetana let down with water, cover and simmer 1¼ hours over very low heat, never stirring.']::text[]),
('vinegret','en','Vinegret','Saint Petersburg, Russia','A bright pink winter salad of beetroot, gherkins and sauerkraut, without a trace of meat. It was the Lenten salad, the one permitted on fast days; the unrefined sunflower oil, which smells of toasted seed, cannot be swapped for a neutral one.',ARRAY['Boil the beetroot, potatoes and carrots whole in their skins, separately: cooked together, everything takes the colour of the beetroot.','Cool them completely then peel and cut them into even one-centimetre dice.','Coat the beetroot dice alone with a spoonful of oil before mixing them with the rest: the oil seals them and stops the salad turning uniformly red.','Add the gherkins, pressed sauerkraut, onion and beans, then the remaining oil, salt and pepper.','Rest 2 hours in the cold before serving, and toss only at the last moment. Never add mayonnaise: it is no longer a vinegret.']::text[]),
('salade-mimosa','en','Mimosa salad','Saint Petersburg, Russia','A layered salad of tinned salmon, egg and cheese, crowned with crumbled yolk. It is served on 8 March, women''s day, when mimosa arrived from Georgia and Abkhazia: the grated yolk on top imitates the flower, and that is where the name comes from.',ARRAY['Hard-boil the eggs 9 minutes, refresh them and separate the whites from the yolks carefully: the salad depends on that separation.','Freeze the butter for an hour: grated frozen it forms threads that melt in the mouth, softened it would make a greasy layer.','Mash the salmon with a fork removing skin and bones, and soak the chopped onion ten minutes in cold water.','Build inside a ring: grated potato, salmon, onion, grated white, grated butter, cheese, carrot, with a thin layer of mayonnaise between each.','Finish with the yolks pushed through a fine sieve, rained on lightly and not pressed down. Set 4 hours in the cold before lifting the ring.']::text[]),
('seledka-pod-shuboy','en','Herring under a fur coat','Murmansk, Russia','Salted herring buried under layers of grated vegetable and mayonnaise, topped with violet beetroot. A 1918 legend says the colours spell out the revolution — red for the Bolsheviks, white for bourgeois mayonnaise; historians disbelieve it, and the dish is served every New Year regardless.',ARRAY['Fillet the herring, pull every bone with tweezers and cut it into one-centimetre dice.','Soak the chopped onion for twenty minutes in vinegared water: raw, it dominates every layer above it.','Boil the beetroot, potatoes and carrots whole and cool them completely — grated warm, they soak the mayonnaise.','Build in a dish: herring, onion, potato, carrot, egg, beetroot, with a thin layer of mayonnaise between each, without pressing.','Rest 12 hours in the cold: that rest is what carries the herring''s salt into the vegetables and pulls the dish together.']::text[]),
('khychin-balkar','en','Balkar khychin','Nalchik, Kabardino-Balkaria, Russia','A very thin flatbread filled with cheese and potato, cooked dry then buttered. The Balkars, a people of the high Caucasus valleys, stack ten of them and cut them in wedges like a cake; the kefir dough must be so thin the filling shows through.',ARRAY['Mix the kefir and bicarbonate and wait for them to foam before adding the flour and salt: that reaction stands in for yeast.','Knead briefly, without force, and rest the dough 30 minutes under a bowl — overworked, it shrinks back and will not roll.','Mash the hot potato with the fresh cheese and spring onion, or replace the potato with blanched nettles in spring.','Enclose a ball of filling in a ball of dough, seal it, then roll very gently out to 25 cm across.','Cook on a dry griddle 3 minutes a side, prick the blisters, and butter generously on both sides as it comes off.']::text[]),
('shurpa-daguestan','en','Dagestani shurpa','Makhachkala, Dagestan, Russia','A lamb soup with vegetables left whole, cooked in a kazan over the fire. The vegetables are not cut: they cook keeping their shape and are served apart from the meat, and the broth is drunk on its own from a bowl, before the rest.',ARRAY['Soak the chickpeas overnight, then start the meat and bones in cold water and bring them up slowly.','Skim without pause for the first fifteen minutes: in Dagestan a shurpa is judged by the clarity of its broth.','Add the chickpeas and simmer 1½ hours without ever boiling, uncovered, topping up the evaporated water.','Then add the whole vegetables — onions, carrots, potatoes — and give them 40 minutes more.','Serve in three acts: the broth first, in a bowl, then the vegetables, then the meat on a platter, with coriander and ground cumin.']::text[]),
('veau-orloff','en','Veal Orloff','Moscow, Russia','A roasted veal loin, sliced then reassembled with duxelles and soubise between each slice, coated in Mornay and gratinated. Created in Paris by the French chef of Prince Orlov, the tsar''s ambassador: a Russian dish never cooked in Russia before it came back there.',ARRAY['Roast the loin 40 minutes at 180°C to 58°C at the centre, and let it cool: veal is never sliced hot, it crumbles.','Make the soubise by cooking the sliced onion and rice in the milk for 30 minutes, then blend and dry it out to a thick purée.','Make the duxelles by drying the chopped mushrooms in a pan for 20 minutes, until they release nothing more.','Slice the veal 1 cm thick, spread each slice with soubise and duxelles, and rebuild the loin, tying it lightly.','Coat with a gruyère Mornay, scatter cheese and gratinate 25 minutes at 200°C. Remove the string before serving.']::text[]),
('kournik','en','Kurnik','Kuban, Russia','A tall domed pie of seven layers — chicken, rice, mushrooms, egg — separated by thin pancakes. It is the Cossack wedding pie: it was cut before the couple, and the number of layers said how many children were wished on them.',ARRAY['Poach the chicken 45 minutes, bone it hot and shred the meat; keep the broth to cook the rice.','Make four thin pancakes and cool them flat: they divide the layers and absorb the moisture of each.','Prepare the fillings separately — rice in broth with dill, mushrooms dried out with onion, chopped hard-boiled egg, chicken bound with a little broth — and cool everything.','Line with shortcrust and stack into a dome: chicken, pancake, rice, pancake, mushrooms, pancake, egg, narrowing toward the top.','Cover with puff pastry, seal, glaze, cut a chimney and bake 20 minutes at 200°C then 50 minutes at 170°C.']::text[]),
('kalatch-de-mourom','en','Murom kalach','Murom, Russia','A wheat bread shaped like a padlock, with a handle to hold it by without soiling it. The handle was not eaten: it was given to the poor or to dogs, and from that comes the Russian phrase to have reached the handle, to have hit rock bottom.',ARRAY['Refresh the starter twice during the day: kalach is made on leaven alone, and a weak starter gives a flat bread.','Knead a very firm dough, then work it at length on the table, slapping and folding it, for a good half hour.','That prolonged beating drives out the large bubbles and gives the tight, satiny crumb that separates a kalach from ordinary bread.','Prove overnight in the cold, then shape a thick ring, drawing one end out into a slim handle.','Bake 30 minutes at 240°C with steam for the first ten. The belly should be golden and the handle almost white.']::text[]),
('kharcho','en','Kharcho','Sochi, Russia','A Georgian beef soup with rice, walnuts and sour tkemali plum, heavily spiced. The acidity comes from neither lemon nor vinegar but from fermented green plum: it is what makes a kharcho, and without it you have an ordinary beef soup.',ARRAY['Start the beef in cold water and bring it up slowly, skimming, then simmer 1½ hours with the bay.','Pound the walnuts in a mortar until they release their oil: it is that oil, not the fragments, that binds the soup.','Melt the very finely chopped onion for 20 minutes, add the tomato purée and let it darken slightly.','Bring everything together with the rice and cook 20 minutes, then add the tkemali and khmeli-suneli off the heat.','The spice mix must never boil: cooked, it loses its fenugreek and dill scent. Add the pounded garlic and coriander at the last moment.']::text[]),
('pyshki-petersbourg','en','Petersburg pyshki','Saint Petersburg, Russia','Rings of yeasted dough fried and buried in icing sugar, an institution on Bolshaya Konyushennaya street since 1958. In Petersburg they are never called doughnuts: a pyshka has no filling, is eaten standing and comes with milky coffee served in a glass.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm milk with a spoonful of sugar and wait until it foams properly.','Knead with the flour, eggs, sugar, salt and melted butter for 12 minutes: the dough must stay soft and slightly tacky.','Prove for an hour, then roll to 1.5 cm and cut rings with a cutter, re-rolling the trimmings once only.','Prove them 25 minutes on a floured cloth, uncovered: a light skin forms and keeps the pyshka from drinking oil.','Fry 90 seconds a side at 180°C, drain and roll them in icing sugar while they are still scalding.']::text[]),
('plombir-sovietique','en','Soviet plombir','Moscow, Russia','An ice cream of very rich cream, with no stabiliser and no flavouring, as the GOST standard defined it. Mikoyan, commissar for food, brought the machines back from the United States in 1936 and imposed fifteen per cent fat; it was sold outdoors, even at minus twenty.',ARRAY['Infuse the split vanilla pod in the hot milk for half an hour off the heat, then lift it out, scraping the seeds in.','Whiten the yolks with the sugar, pour the warm milk over and cook to the nappe at 83°C, never above.','At 85°C the yolks coagulate and the custard grains: that is the only real difficulty, and a thermometer beats an eye.','Strain, cool fast in an iced water bath, add the cold cream and mature 4 hours in the fridge.','Churn, then harden 3 hours in the freezer. With no stabiliser it crystallises within two days: it is eaten the same day.']::text[]),
('sbiten','en','Sbiten','Veliky Novgorod, Russia','A hot honey and spice drink, sold in Russian streets before tea arrived from China in 1638. Street sellers carried it in copper samovars on their backs crying sbiten!; the name comes from sbivat, to beat together.',ARRAY['Bring the water and sugar to the boil with sliced fresh ginger, cinnamon, cloves, star anise and a few peppercorns.','Infuse 20 minutes covered over very low heat, never at a rolling boil.','Take it off and let it fall to 60°C before stirring in the honey: above that it loses its aromas and leaves only its sugar.','Add the mint and lemon zest and infuse ten minutes more, off the heat and covered.','Strain and serve scalding in thick glasses scalded beforehand. Wine was once added, which remains optional.']::text[]),
('kvass-blanc','en','Bread kvass','Suzdal, Russia','A fermented drink of toasted rye bread, barely alcoholic, drunk iced in summer. It appears in the chronicle of 996, when Vladimir baptised the Rus'' and had kvass distributed to the people; it was sold from street tankers into the 1990s.',ARRAY['Cut the rye bread into slices and brown them in the oven 20 minutes at 180°C, until the edges are nearly black.','That deep toasting gives the colour and the flavour: merely dried bread makes a pale, bland kvass.','Pour boiling water over the bread and infuse 8 hours, then strain through a cloth without pressing.','Add the sugar and the slaked yeast, cover with a cloth — never an airtight lid — and ferment 24 hours at room temperature.','Bottle with three raisins per bottle, close and leave 24 hours more in the cold. Open over the sink: the pressure is real.']::text[]),
('tchakhokhbili','en','Chakhokhbili','Krasnodar, Russia','A poultry stew with tomatoes and herbs, cooked without a drop of water. The name comes from khokhobi, Georgian for pheasant, replaced by chicken in Soviet times; the meat is seared dry in the pot first, and its own juices make the sauce.',ARRAY['Joint the chicken and colour the pieces in a dry pot, with no fat: the skin releases enough for what follows.','Lift them out and melt thickly sliced onion in that fat for 25 minutes, until pale and yielding.','Peel the tomatoes after thirty seconds in boiling water, and crush them roughly by hand.','Return the chicken with the tomato, cover and simmer 40 minutes without adding water: the tomato and onion give enough.','Add the pounded garlic, khmeli-suneli and chopped herbs only in the last five minutes, off the boil.']::text[]),
('balyk-de-saumon','en','Salmon balyk','Vladivostok, Russia','The back of a sockeye salmon salted then air-dried in the cold, sliced as thin as possible. Balyk uses only the dorsal part, the fattest and densest; the belly, too thin, is set aside and smoked separately under the name teysha.',ARRAY['Take the back of the salmon off in one piece, without the belly, and pull every bone with tweezers.','Mix the salt, sugar, cracked pepper and crumbled bay, and bury the fish in it, flesh to flesh if there are two pieces.','Cure 48 hours in the cold, turning morning and evening, and pouring off the brine each time.','Rinse, blot, then hang in cold moving air — between 2 and 6°C — for five to seven days, wrapped in muslin against flies.','It is ready when it has lost a third of its weight and the flesh is translucent and firm. Slice with a long knife, almost flat, and serve on buttered rye bread.']::text[]),
('pelmeni-au-renne','en','Reindeer pelmeni','Yakutsk, Yakutia, Russia','Reindeer dumplings shaped by the hundred and frozen outdoors, in the Yakutian cold. Reindeer meat is so lean it would stay dry: marrow or pork fat is added, and the pelmeni keep all winter in a sack hung under the eaves.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, egg, iced water and salt for 15 minutes into a very firm dough, and rest it an hour wrapped.','Chop the meat and fat by knife, not in a processor, and add grated onion with its juice and two spoonfuls of iced water.','That water is the pelmen''s juice: without it the reindeer cooks dry and the dumpling has no point.','Roll to 1 mm, cut 6 cm rounds, fill, fold into half-moons then join the two points into a ring.','Freeze them flat for an hour before cooking: they hold better. Poach 6 minutes in simmering broth until they rise, and serve with smetana.']::text[]),
('omoul-fume-du-baikal','en','Smoked Baikal omul','Listvyanka, Lake Baikal, Russia','A whitefish found only in Lake Baikal, salted then hot-smoked over alder on the shore. Omul lives nowhere else and its fishing has been restricted since 2017; its flesh is so fatty that it needs no added fat to stay moist.',ARRAY['Gut the fish without scaling them and remove the gills, which would bring bitterness to the smoking.','Dry-salt them, rubbing inside and out, and leave them 12 hours in the cold under a light weight.','Rinse, then hang them to dry for 3 hours in the air: the skin must be dry to the touch, otherwise the smoke slides off without taking.','Lay dampened alder chips in the smoker and hold 80°C: this is hot smoking, the fish cooks as it takes the smoke.','Allow 2½ hours until the skin is golden brown and the flesh lifts from the bone. Eaten warm, with the fingers, and black bread.']::text[]),
('chou-a-la-sakhaline','en','Sakhalin fermented cabbage','Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk, Sakhalin, Russia','Chinese cabbage fermented with chilli, garlic and kelp, a Russian cousin of kimchi. The island was Japanese until 1945 and holds a deported Korean community: this cabbage was born of that meeting, and the kombu in it is the local addition.',ARRAY['Quarter the cabbages lengthwise, salt between every leaf and let them drain 4 hours, turning twice.','Rinse three times in clear water and press: just enough salt must remain for the leaf to fold without snapping.','Soak the kombu 30 minutes, then blend it with the garlic, ginger, sugar and fish sauce into a paste.','Mix that paste with the Korean chilli to a bright red purée, and coat every leaf, one by one, with a gloved hand.','Pack into a jar expelling the air, ferment 2 days at room temperature then move to the cold. It is eaten from a week on and keeps all winter.']::text[]),
('pelmeni-de-sakhaline','en','Sakhalin seafood pelmeni','Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk, Sakhalin, Russia','Dumplings of crab, scallop and prawn, born in the canteens of the island''s oil platforms. The filling is not minced: the flesh stays in recognisable pieces, and the dumpling poaches for less time than its meat cousin.',ARRAY['Knead the pelmeni dough as usual and rest it an hour: it must be firm and thin, the sea does not forgive thick pastry.','Cut the scallop and prawn by knife into half-centimetre pieces, and shred the crab by hand.','Blend nothing: a seafood filling reduced to a paste loses its texture and takes on a tinned taste.','Add the spring onion, grated ginger, a pinch of salt and nothing more — certainly no egg, which would mask everything.','Shape them and poach 4 minutes only in simmering salted water. Serve with melted butter and soy, and a few drops of sesame oil.']::text[]),
('manty-siberiens','en','Siberian manty','Novosibirsk, Russia','Large steamed dumplings of a hundred grams, filled with beef, onion and pumpkin. The pumpkin is no cheap filler: it melts and releases its sweet water as it cooks, feeding a lean meat that nothing else would moisten.',ARRAY['Knead a firm dough with warm water and rest it an hour: manty are folded a great deal and a brittle dough tears.','Cut the meat, fat and pumpkin by knife into 5 mm dice, and chop the onion very finely.','The proportion matters: as much onion as half the weight of meat, otherwise the filling stays dry.','Cut 12 cm squares, fill them and bring the four corners to the centre, then seal the four openings in pairs.','Oil the steamer trays — manty stick and tear otherwise — and steam 40 minutes. Serve with melted butter and smetana.']::text[]),
('langue-de-renne-dolgane','en','Dolgan reindeer tongue','Norilsk, Taymyr, Russia','Reindeer tongues poached with juniper berries, pressed and served cold in thin slices. The Dolgans, nomadic herders of Taymyr, keep them for honoured guests; the skin comes off in one piece while the tongue is scalding, and never afterwards.',ARRAY['Soak the tongues 6 hours in cold water, changing it twice, to draw out the blood.','Poach them 2½ hours with the onion, carrot, juniper, bay and pepper, at a bare tremble and never boiling.','They are ready when a needle passes through the base without resistance: the tip is always done first, the base decides.','Lift them out and peel them immediately, under cold running water — once cool the skin clings and must be cut away.','Press them 12 hours in the cold under a weighted board, then slice very thinly on the bias. Serve with horseradish smetana and black bread.']::text[]),
('shangi','en','Shangi','Arkhangelsk, Russia','Open rye cakes topped with mashed potato and brushed with smetana. They are the ancestors of the vatrushka, baked in the Russian ovens of the far north: the topping is not set in a well but spread over the whole surface, right to the edge.',ARRAY['Knead both flours, the warm milk, yeast and salt: rye dough is sticky and heavy, and there is no point trying to make it elastic.','Prove it 1½ hours; it will not double — rye rises little, which is normal.','Make a firm mash, beaten with butter and an egg yolk but no milk: it must sit on the cake without running.','Flatten 12 cm discs, leave a one-centimetre rim and spread the mash over the whole surface.','Brush generously with smetana and bake 22 minutes at 220°C. Butter them as they come out, and stack them under a cloth to stay soft.']::text[]),
('tchoudou-daghestanais','en','Chudu','Makhachkala, Dagestan, Russia','A very thin flatbread filled with meat, nettles or pumpkin, cooked dry then buttered. Dagestan has fourteen official languages and as many chudu: every people has its own, and a family is known by the thickness it gives its dough.',ARRAY['Mix the kefir, bicarbonate and salt, wait for the foam, then add the flour and knead briefly.','Rest it 30 minutes: it is that rest, not the kneading, that makes the dough supple enough to stretch without tearing.','For the meat filling, mix the raw lamb with grated onion and two spoonfuls of water; for the green one, blanch the nettles thirty seconds and press them thoroughly dry.','Enclose the filling in a ball of dough, seal it, then roll very gently out to 28 cm — the filling should show faintly through.','Cook on a scalding dry griddle, 3 minutes a side, pricking the blisters. Butter generously on both sides and stack under a cloth.']::text[]),
('pian-se','en','Pyan-se','Vladivostok, Russia','A large steamed Korean bun filled with spiced cabbage and pork, sold on the streets of Vladivostok. Primorye Koreans brought it in the 1920s; it is eaten standing in the cold, three hundred grams apiece, held in both hands for the warmth.',ARRAY['Knead a soft yeasted dough with the flour, warm water, yeast and sugar, and prove 1½ hours.','Salt the shredded cabbage and let it drain 40 minutes, then press it hard: its water is what would sog the bun.','Mix it with the raw pork, onion, garlic, Korean chilli, soy and sesame oil, and taste a fried patty before filling.','Flatten 100 g balls, fill generously and close by gathering the edges to the centre, seam firmly pinched underneath.','Prove 30 minutes on paper squares, then steam 25 minutes without once lifting the lid — a draught of cold air collapses the dough.']::text[]),
('kovrizhka-de-siberie','en','Taiga honey kovrizhka','Irkutsk, Siberia, Russia','A Siberian spice loaf of rye and taiga honey, rougher and denser than the pryanik. It kept six months in an unheated izba; taiga honey, gathered from the flowers of the vast boreal forest, is nearly black and faintly bitter.',ARRAY['Warm the honey with the water without going above 50°C, then let it cool: heated further, taiga honey loses what sets it apart.','Crush the juniper berries in a mortar with the cinnamon and cloves — the juniper is what gives a kovrizhka its resinous, Siberian taste.','Mix the flours, spices, salt and bicarbonate, then pour in the honey, oil and beaten eggs.','The batter is thick and sluggish: do not beat it to lighten it, a kovrizhka must stay dense.','Pour into a lined tin 4 cm deep, scatter walnuts and bake 45 minutes at 165°C. Wrap it warm and wait three days.']::text[]),
('pastilla-de-fes','en','Fes pastilla','Fes, Morocco','A pie of warqa leaves filled with pigeon, almonds and cinnamon, dusted with icing sugar. The sweet-savoury comes from al-Andalus, whose exiles peopled Fes in the 15th century: pastilla is the Andalusian dish that survived in Morocco after vanishing from Spain.',ARRAY['Braise the pigeons 1 hour with grated onion, saffron, ginger and herbs, then bone them and shred the meat.','Reduce the cooking juices until almost no liquid remains, then pour in the beaten eggs, stirring without pause.','The eggs must set into fine, dry curds: that layer absorbs the moisture and stops the warqa going soggy.','Fry the almonds, chop them roughly and mix them with the icing sugar and cinnamon.','Build three layers — eggs, meat, almonds — between buttered sheets, bake 30 minutes at 200°C and dust in a lattice as it comes out.']::text[]),
('harira','en','Harira','Marrakesh, Morocco','A soup of tomato, lentils and chickpeas thickened with tadouira, a fermented flour-and-water starter. It is the fast-breaking soup of Ramadan; the tadouira is made the day before and must sour slightly, or the harira stays without depth.',ARRAY['Make the tadouira the day before: slake the flour in two glasses of water and ferment it covered at room temperature for 24 hours.','Fry the diced lamb with the onion, smen and spices, add the soaked chickpeas and cover with water; allow 1 hour.','Blend the tomato with the coriander and parsley and pour it in: never crushed, it must be liquid and smooth to bind with the tadouira.','Add the lentils and give them 30 minutes, then pour in the tadouira in a thin stream, whisking without pause.','Poured in at once it lumps beyond rescue. Thicken 20 minutes, add the vermicelli and serve with dates and lemon.']::text[]),
('briouates','en','Briouates','Casablanca, Morocco','Small warqa triangles filled with minced meat or seafood, fried. They are folded like a flag, the strip turned over itself in successive triangles; the last flap is sealed with flour paste, never egg, which would blister in the oil.',ARRAY['Cook the filling until it releases nothing more, and cool it completely: a warm filling softens the warqa as you fold.','Cut the sheets into 8 cm strips and keep them under a damp cloth — warqa dries and cracks within three minutes in the air.','Set a spoonful at one end and fold into a triangle, then keep turning the triangle over itself to the end of the strip.','Seal the last flap with a paste of flour and water, not with egg.','Fry at 170°C, 2 minutes a side, six at a time. Drain upright: laid flat they go soft underneath.']::text[]),
('zaalouk','en','Zaalouk','Marrakesh, Morocco','A cooked salad of aubergine and tomato crushed with garlic, cumin and olive oil, served cold. It is one of the Moroccan salads set out in small bowls before the main dish; the aubergines are crushed with a fork and never blended, which makes them foamy.',ARRAY['Char the whole aubergines over a flame until the skin blackens and the flesh slumps.','That direct charring is what gives the smoky taste: boiled or oven-baked, aubergines have no character at all.','Peel them and let them drain half an hour in a colander — their bitter water would wash out the whole dish.','Cook the grated tomato down with the garlic for 25 minutes, until only a brick-red paste remains.','Crush the aubergines into the tomato with a fork, add the spices and oil, and cool. Eaten cold, preferably the next day.']::text[]),
('msemen','en','Msemen','Casablanca, Morocco','A square layered pancake, hand-stretched then folded with semolina and butter, cooked on a griddle. The layers come not from turning but from stretching: the dough is pulled until transparent, which is only possible with a heavily oiled dough.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, fine semolina, warm water and salt for fifteen minutes: the dough must become very supple, almost pourable.','Divide into balls, oil them generously and rest them 30 minutes under the oil — it is what will allow the stretching.','Spread each ball flat on an oiled surface, pulling from the centre outward, until you can see your hand through it.','Scatter melted butter and semolina, then fold in three one way and three the other to make a square of nine layers.','Flatten slightly and cook on a dry griddle 3 minutes a side, pressing with a spatula. Serve with honey and butter.']::text[]),
('the-a-la-menthe','en','Mint tea','Marrakesh, Morocco','Gunpowder green tea infused with mint and heavily sweetened, poured from a height into small glasses. The height is no flourish: it aerates the tea, raises the expected foam and cools the liquid enough for the glass to be held.',ARRAY['Scald the tea once and throw the water away after thirty seconds: that rinse removes the bitterness and dust from the rolled leaves.','Keep that first liquor in a separate glass — Moroccans set it aside and return it at the end, as it holds the essence of the tea.','Pour boiling water over the rinsed leaves and infuse three minutes only, without mint: the tea infuses alone.','Then add the whole mint, stems and all, and the sugar, and give it two minutes more — boiled mint turns bitter.','Pour back and forth three times between pot and glass to mix, then serve holding the pot fifty centimetres up.']::text[]),
('chorba-frik','en','Chorba frik','Algiers, Algeria','A lamb soup with cracked green wheat, tomato and coriander, served every evening of Ramadan. Frik is durum wheat harvested green then burned over straw: that scorching gives it a smoky taste ripe wheat lacks, and it thickens without disintegrating.',ARRAY['Fry the small-diced lamb in smen with grated onion and cinnamon for ten minutes, without deep colour.','Add the tomato purée and grated tomato and let them cook down fifteen minutes before adding liquid: what Algerian cooks call sweating the sauce.','Moisten with hot water, add the soaked chickpeas and cook 45 minutes.','Rinse the freekeh in plenty of water and rub it between your hands: it carries a dust of burnt chaff that would make the soup harsh.','Add it for 25 minutes only, stirring often as it catches. Finish with coriander, mint and a squeeze of lemon.']::text[]),
('rechta','en','Rechta','Tlemcen, Algeria','Fresh noodles steamed and served in a white chicken broth with turnips and chickpeas. It is the dish of great occasions in Algiers and Tlemcen; the broth is deliberately pale, with no tomato and no chilli, and cinnamon takes the place of paprika.',ARRAY['Knead a firm dough, rest it an hour, then roll it very thin and roll up the sheet before cutting one-millimetre noodles.','Separate them by hand with flour, then oil them lightly and steam them three times for fifteen minutes.','Between each steaming, take them out, sprinkle with salted water and separate them with a fork — without those three cookings they stick in a block.','Make the broth with the chicken, onion, cinnamon and smen, with no tomato: rechta is known by its pale colour.','Add turnips, courgettes and chickpeas for the last half hour. Serve the noodles in a mound, the broth poured over at the table.']::text[]),
('mhadjeb','en','Mhadjeb','Constantine, Algeria','A semolina pancake stretched until transparent, filled with a tomato and onion compote and folded into a square. It is Algerian street food par excellence; the stretching is done flat on an oiled table, and the dough must be so thin it tears at the least jerk.',ARRAY['Knead the fine semolina and warm salted water for twenty minutes: it is long, but semolina becomes extensible only at that price.','Oil the balls and rest them an hour under oil, never under a cloth — the oil prevents a skin and allows the stretching.','Cook the onion and tomato down for 40 minutes to a thick paste, and cool it completely.','Stretch each ball flat on the oiled table, from centre to edge, until you can see the wood through it.','Set the filling in the centre, fold the four sides into a square and cook on a dry griddle 4 minutes a side, pressing down.']::text[]),
('mtewem','en','Mtewem','Tizi Ouzou, Kabylia, Algeria','Garlic lamb meatballs simmered in a white sauce, with two whole heads of garlic in the pot. Mtewem means with garlic: half goes into the meatballs, half into the sauce, and it is a dish served only to people one knows well.',ARRAY['Pound a whole head of garlic with salt in a mortar to a cream — chopped by knife it would stay in hard pieces inside the meatball.','Mix it with the minced lamb, eggs, breadcrumbs, cinnamon and parsley, and rest an hour in the cold.','Fry the meat pieces with grated onion and smen, add the second head of garlic in whole cloves and the chickpeas.','Moisten to cover and simmer 45 minutes before lowering the meatballs in, one by one and without stirring.','They cook 25 minutes and must never boil hard: agitated, they fall apart. The sauce stays white, with not a trace of tomato.']::text[]),
('kalbelouz','en','Kalb el louz','Constantine, Algeria','A semolina and almond cake drowned in orange blossom syrup, served during Ramadan. The name means heart of almond: the semolina is not worked but left to rest, and cold syrup is poured over a scalding cake — the reverse does not work.',ARRAY['Mix the coarse semolina, melted butter, a little sugar and orange blossom water with your fingertips, without kneading.','Rest it 2 hours: the semolina must swell of its own accord, and a worked dough gives a rubbery cake.','Press half into a dish, spread the almonds blended with sugar, cover with the rest and cut the diamonds before baking.','Bake 45 minutes at 180°C, then pour cold syrup over the scalding cake as it leaves the oven.','Hot syrup on a hot cake stays on the surface: it is the difference in temperature that drives it in. Wait six hours before serving.']::text[]),
('makroud','en','Makroud','Kairouan, Tunisia','Semolina diamonds filled with date paste, fried then dipped in honey. Kairouan made them its speciality and sells them by weight on the Great Mosque square; the dough holds no water but only olive oil, which makes it short rather than elastic.',ARRAY['Rub the semolina and warm olive oil between your hands for ten minutes, until every grain is coated, and rest it an hour.','Add water only at the very end, and as little as possible: an over-hydrated dough turns elastic and the makroud hardens.','Stone the dates, blend them with the cinnamon, zest and orange blossom water to a supple paste.','Form a rope of dough, hollow it into a channel, fill with dates and close it, then flatten and cut diamonds.','Fry at 170°C until golden, drain, then plunge them scalding into warm honey for three minutes. Drain on a rack.']::text[]),
('ojja-merguez','en','Ojja with merguez','Tunis, Tunisia','Eggs poached in a harissa tomato sauce with merguez, served in the cooking pan. It is eaten with a spoon and bread, straight from the pan; the eggs are not beaten but broken in whole, and the yolk must stay runny.',ARRAY['Char the green peppers over a flame, peel them and cut them into strips: raw, they would stay crunchy and unbalance the sauce.','Fry the merguez, lift them out and keep the red fat — it, not the tomato purée, is what colours the sauce.','Slake the harissa and purée in that fat and fry them for a minute before adding the grated tomato.','Reduce for 20 minutes until the oil rises to the surface: the sign the sauce is ready, exactly as for a tagine.','Make wells, break the eggs into them, cover and give them three minutes only. Serve in the pan, with bread and nothing else.']::text[]),
('kamounia','en','Kamounia','Djerba, Tunisia','A stew of liver or beef dominated by cumin, cooked long into an almost black sauce. The name comes from kamoun, cumin: three spoonfuls go in, which seems excessive until you understand that long cooking removes its bitterness.',ARRAY['Sear the diced meat in olive oil until frankly brown, in small batches.','Pound the garlic with the cumin and salt in a mortar: ground dry, cumin does not release its oils and stays dusty.','Fry that paste for thirty seconds in the hot oil before adding the purée and harissa — that brief pass removes the raw taste.','Moisten to cover and simmer 2 hours covered, until the sauce darkens and thickens.','Add the diced liver in the last ten minutes only: cooked longer it turns grainy and bitter.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('tajine-tunisien','en','Tunisian tajine','La Goulette, Tunis, Tunisia','A thick baked omelette bound with cheese and eggs, cut into squares like a frittata. Here the word does not mean the Moroccan earthenware dish but its exact opposite: the Tunisian tajine is oven-baked, eaten cold, and cut with a knife.',ARRAY['Cook the chicken with the onion and turmeric until not a drop of liquid remains, then shred it.','Any residual moisture makes the tajine collapse and stops it cutting cleanly: the only real difficulty.','Fry the diced potato separately, until tender and golden.','Beat the eggs, add the grated cheese, breadcrumbs, parsley and harissa, then all the cooled fillings.','Pour into an oiled dish 4 cm deep and bake 40 minutes at 180°C. Cool completely before cutting into squares.']::text[]),
('bambalouni','en','Bambalouni','Sidi Bou Saïd, Tunisia','A ring doughnut fried and rolled in sugar, sold scalding as it leaves the oil. It is eaten standing at the stall: it exists only at that moment, and a ten-minute-old bambalouni has nothing left of what you came for.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm water and let it foam, then beat it into the flour and salt — no sugar in the dough, only on it.','The dough must be very soft and sticky, nearly liquid: it is worked with a wetted hand, never a floured one.','Beat it hard for ten minutes, lifting it and letting it fall back, then prove 1½ hours.','Take a handful, pierce it with a finger and stretch the ring by hand directly over the oil — a bambalouni is never shaped in advance.','Fry at 180°C, one minute a side, and roll in sugar immediately. Eaten within three minutes.']::text[]),
('mrouzia','en','Mrouzia','Tunis, Tunisia','A lamb tagine with honey, raisins and ras el hanout, deeply sweet and long confited. It is made for Eid, when the sacrificial meat must keep without refrigeration: honey and salt turn it into a savoury preserve that lasts weeks.',ARRAY['Rub the lamb with ras el hanout, saffron and salt twelve hours ahead — the mixture must penetrate before any cooking.','Fry it in smen with the grated onion, then moisten to cover and simmer 2 hours covered.','Plump the raisins separately and add the honey only in the last half hour: poured in at the start it would burn on the base.','Uncover and reduce until the sauce becomes a thick syrup that coats the meat and shines.','Fry the almonds separately and scatter them as you serve, with toasted sesame seeds.']::text[]),
('shorba-libyenne','en','Libyan shorba','Tripoli, Libya','A lamb soup with tomato and orzo pasta, heavily scented with dried mint. The mint is crushed between the palms over the pot just before serving: added during cooking it loses everything and the soup becomes an ordinary tomato soup.',ARRAY['Fry the small-diced lamb with the onion and dry spices for ten minutes, before the tomato goes in.','Add the tomato purée and fry it for a minute: that frying removes the metallic acidity no sugar would later correct.','Moisten with hot water, add the chickpeas and cook 1 hour at a bare simmer.','Add the orzo for the last twelve minutes only — any earlier and it drinks the broth and the soup becomes a porridge.','Crush the dried mint between your palms over the pot, off the heat, and serve with plenty of lemon.']::text[]),
('asida-libyenne','en','Asida','Tripoli, Libya','A cooked flour paste mounded into a dome and hollowed into a well of honey and melted butter. It is made for the birth of a child and for the Mawlid; it is eaten by hand, pinching a piece from the edge of the dome and dipping it in the well.',ARRAY['Bring salted water to the boil and tip the flour into the centre all at once, without stirring, then leave it three minutes.','That wait without stirring is the Libyan method: the flour hydrates by itself and forms fewer lumps than any whisking.','Then work it with a wooden spoon for twenty minutes, pressing against the sides: the paste must turn smooth, elastic and come away.','It is ready when it forms a block that follows the spoon out of the pan without sticking.','Mound it on a dish, hollow a wide well in the centre with your fist and fill it with warm honey and melted butter.']::text[]),
('mbatan','en','Mbatan','Tripoli, Libya','Two slices of potato enclosing a spiced meat filling, crumbed and fried. The potato is cut like a wallet, without going through: it needs a thin blade and a steady hand, and a slice cut all the way through spills its filling into the oil.',ARRAY['Cut the potatoes into 1 cm slices, then split each slice through its thickness keeping one side attached.','Blanch them three minutes in salted water and cool them: raw, they would not cook through during frying.','Cook the filling until dry, then cool it completely — warm, it would make the coating slide off.','Fill each wallet without overloading, close it and press with the flat of your hand to seal.','Flour, egg then crumb, and fry at 175°C, 4 minutes a side. Serve with harissa and lemon.']::text[]),
('ghoriba-libyenne','en','Ghoriba','Tripoli, Libya','A cracked shortbread biscuit, with almonds or semolina, so short it dissolves without chewing. The cracks are not decorative: they appear by themselves when the fat is in the right proportion, and a smooth top signals an over-hydrated dough.',ARRAY['Mix the ground almonds, icing sugar and oil by hand, without whisking: a ghoriba is worked with the fingertips.','Add one egg, the orange blossom water, then the flour and baking powder, and stop the moment the dough comes together.','Rest it an hour in the cold: without rest the oil weeps out during baking and the biscuits spread.','Shape balls and roll them in a thick layer of icing sugar — it is that sugar that cracks, not the dough itself.','Press an almond into the centre and bake 18 minutes at 160°C. They must stay pale: browned, they are dry.']::text[]),
('mhalbiya','en','Mhalbiya','Tripoli, Libya','A rice-flour milk cream scented with rose water, served cold and scattered with pistachios. It holds neither egg nor gelatine: rice starch alone sets it, which demands slow cooking and constant stirring so it does not catch.',ARRAY['Slake the rice flour and cornstarch in a quarter of the cold milk until not a lump remains, then strain.','Bring the rest of the milk to a tremble with the sugar and pour in the cold mixture, whisking without pause.','Stir without stopping for twenty minutes over low heat: rice flour catches instantly and a burnt note carries through the whole dish.','The cream is ready when it coats the spoon and a furrow drawn with a finger holds for three seconds.','Add the rose water off the heat — heated, it evaporates and leaves nothing. Pour into cups and set 4 hours in the cold.']::text[]),
('ruz-mufalfal','en','Ruz mufalfal','Tripoli, Libya','A spiced rice in which every grain stays separate, served with lamb and pine nuts. Mufalfal means peppered, but really names the texture: the rice must be grain by grain, achieved by rinsing it seven times and pearling it in fat before any liquid.',ARRAY['Rinse the rice seven times until the water runs perfectly clear, then soak it 30 minutes and drain it for an hour.','Fry the diced lamb with the onion and whole spices in smen, then moisten and cook 45 minutes.','Pearl the drained rice in the fat for two minutes before any liquid: that coating isolates each grain and stops it sticking.','Pour in broth at exactly one and a half volumes to one of rice, bring to the boil, cover and drop to the lowest heat.','Twelve minutes, then ten minutes off the heat without uncovering. Fluff with a fork and crown with fried pine nuts and almonds.']::text[]),
('tamiya','en','Ta‘miya','Cairo, Egypt','The Egyptian falafel, made from dried broad beans rather than chickpeas, green inside and coated in sesame. Broad beans give a far lighter, moister texture than chickpeas; Egypt claims the older version, and Copts were making it during Lent.',ARRAY['Soak the skinned broad beans 12 hours and on no account cook them: falafel is made with raw pulses, and cooked beans give a mush that falls apart in the oil.','Mince them with the herbs and garlic twice, keeping some grain — a blender would reduce them to a smooth, dense paste.','Salt and add the bicarbonate only as you fry: added earlier, it makes the mixture weep.','Shape flat 5 cm patties and press one face into the sesame, not both.','Fry at 175°C, three minutes a side. The inside must stay bright green and moist; a brown centre means the mixture waited too long.']::text[]),
('mahshi','en','Mahshi','Cairo, Egypt','Hollowed vegetables and vine leaves stuffed with herbed rice, packed tight in a pot. The rice goes in raw and swells by half: which is why they are never filled more than two-thirds, and an overfilled mahshi bursts and empties into the broth.',ARRAY['Hollow the courgettes and aubergines with a corer leaving five millimetres of wall: any thinner and the vegetable collapses.','Rinse the rice and mix it raw with grated tomato, onion, chopped herbs and melted smen, without cooking it.','Fill only two-thirds and press barely — the rice must have room to swell.','Line the base of the pot with tomato slices and pack the vegetables upright and tight, so they hold each other up.','Set a plate on top to keep them down, add liquid to half their height and simmer 1¼ hours without ever stirring. Lemon on serving.']::text[]),
('fattah','en','Fattah','Cairo, Egypt','Layers of fried bread, rice and meat doused with a garlic and vinegar broth. It is the dish of Eid al-Adha, made with the sacrificial meat; the stale bread at the bottom must stay crisp in places and soaked in others, and that contrast is the point.',ARRAY['Poach the lamb 2 hours with the onion, cardamom and bay, skimming, and keep the broth.','Cut the stale bread into squares and fry or toast them until brown and hard: soft bread would turn to paste.','Make a rice with vermicelli toasted in smen, moistened with the lamb broth.','Make the sauce by frying pounded garlic in smen until golden, then add the vinegar and tomato purée, and take it off the heat at once.','Build: bread at the bottom, broth poured over, rice, meat, then the garlic sauce. Serve immediately, before all the bread has softened.']::text[]),
('rozz-bil-laban','en','Rozz bil laban','Cairo, Egypt','A very creamy rice pudding, browned in the oven until a skin forms. The rice is first cooked in water then long in milk: cooked straight in milk it would stay hard at the centre, because milk sugars and calcium toughen the starch.',ARRAY['First cook the rice in water for 15 minutes, until tender but whole, and drain it.','Add the hot milk and cook 40 minutes over very low heat, stirring every five minutes.','Sweeten only halfway through: sugar added too early stops the starch swelling and the rice stays firm.','Thicken with cornstarch slaked in a little cold milk, add the cream and vanilla, and scent with rose water off the heat.','Pour into a dish and give it five minutes under the grill to form the brown skin. Eaten warm or cold, never scalding.']::text[]),
('umm-ali','en','Umm Ali','Cairo, Egypt','A gratin of crumbled puff pastry, milk, nuts and raisins, browned in the oven. Legend attributes it to Shajar al-Durr, a 13th-century sultana, who is said to have had it distributed to celebrate a rival''s death; the story is doubtful, the pudding is not.',ARRAY['Bake the puff pastry flat until deeply golden and dry, then cool it and crumble it by hand.','Pale or soft pastry would turn to glue: it must be frankly baked before it meets the milk.','Heat the milk with the sugar and vanilla without boiling, and plump the raisins in it for ten minutes.','Mix the crumbled pastry with the chopped nuts in a dish, pour the hot milk over and let it absorb for five minutes.','Cover with unsweetened whipped cream and give it 25 minutes at 200°C, until the top is brown and blistered. Serve scalding.']::text[]),
('mhadjeb-au-fromage','en','Cheese mhadjeb','Constantine, Algeria','The melted cheese version of the Constantine mhadjeb, more recent and now the biggest street seller. The cheese replaces the tomato compote but brings a new constraint: it melts and tries to escape, which means sealing all four sides with a firm pinch.',ARRAY['Knead the fine semolina and warm salted water for twenty minutes, until the dough turns smooth and very supple.','Divide into balls, cover them with oil and rest an hour: without that immersion the stretching is impossible.','Melt the onion for 30 minutes with the paprika and harissa, cool it, then mix it with the diced cheese.','Stretch the dough flat on an oiled table until transparent, fill the centre and fold the four sides in, pinching each seam.','Cook on a dry griddle 4 minutes a side, pressing down: the cheese must melt entirely without ever escaping.']::text[]),
('or-lam','en','Or lam','Xiangkhouang, Laos','A buffalo and vegetable stew with sakhan wood, which numbs the tongue like Sichuan pepper. A stick of sakhan goes into the pot and comes back out: it is not eaten, it flavours.',ARRAY['Grill the meat over coals before it goes in the pot: that smoke is what separates or lam from an ordinary stew.','Bruise the sakhan wood with a mallet to release its sap, and drop it into a litre of boiling water.','Add the meat, bruised lemongrass and shallot, and simmer for 1 hour.','Take the sakhan out before it dominates everything, then add the aubergines and long beans.','Finish with the wood ear mushrooms, dill and chilli. Serve with sticky rice, eaten by hand.']::text[]),
('amok-trey','en','Amok trey','Siem Reap, Cambodia','A fish mousse with kroeung paste and coconut milk, steamed in a banana leaf cup. It must set like a custard: if it stays runny, either the egg or the patience was short.',ARRAY['Pound lemongrass, galangal, turmeric, kaffir lime leaves, shallot, garlic and shrimp paste in a mortar: this is kroeung, and it takes twenty minutes of arm work.','Slice the fish thinly and work it with the kroeung in a bowl, always stirring the same way.','Add the coconut milk a spoonful at a time, then the beaten eggs: the mixture should thicken and turn silky.','Make cups from banana leaf softened over a flame, pinned at the corners.','Line them with noni leaves, fill and steam gently for 25 minutes. Decorate with coconut cream and pepper.']::text[]),
('banh-xeo','en','Bánh xèo','Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam','A crisp turmeric rice pancake filled with prawns and bean sprouts. The name imitates the sound of batter hitting the scorching pan: xèo, the sizzle, is the only measure of the right heat.',ARRAY['Mix the rice flour, coconut milk, turmeric, water and spring onion, and rest for 2 hours.','The batter must be as thin as milk: any thicker and the pancake goes soft instead of crisp.','Steam the mung beans and fry the sliced pork with the prawns.','Heat a pan until a drop of water dances on it, oil it and pour in a thin ladleful, swirling.','Fill one half, cover for 2 minutes, then uncover so the edge crisps. Fold, and eat rolled in a lettuce leaf, dipped in nuoc cham.']::text[]),
('gudeg','en','Gudeg','Yogyakarta, Java, Indonesia','Green jackfruit stewed five hours in coconut milk and palm sugar, with teak leaves that stain it mahogany. Yogyakarta calls itself the gudeg city; it is eaten at breakfast as at dinner.',ARRAY['Boil the green jackfruit for 20 minutes and discard that water: it removes the latex.','Pound the shallot, garlic, candlenuts, coriander and galangal into a fine paste.','Line the base of the pot with teak leaves — they give the colour, without which gudeg stays pale.','Pack in the jackfruit, hard-boiled eggs and chicken, and pour over the paste, coconut milk and palm sugar.','Simmer for 5 hours over very low heat without stirring, until almost dry. Serve with rice and cracklings.']::text[]),
('ayam-betutu','en','Ayam betutu','Gianyar, Bali, Indonesia','A whole chicken stuffed with spice paste, wrapped in banana bark and cooked four hours under embers. Betutu is ordered a day ahead in the warungs of Gianyar: nobody makes it to order.',ARRAY['Pound all the aromatics into a thick paste — the Balinese base genep — and fry it in oil for 20 minutes.','Rub the chicken inside and out, working under the skin, and marinate for 6 hours.','Stuff it with blanched cassava leaves mixed with a third of the paste, and sew the opening.','Wrap it in several thicknesses of flame-softened banana leaf, and tie it firmly.','Bury under embers or cook for 4 hours at 150°C: the flesh should leave the bone by itself.']::text[]),
('nasi-kandar','en','Nasi kandar','Butterworth, Penang, Malaysia','Plain rice drowned under several curries poured together in one ladle. Kandar was the pole of the Tamil hawkers who sold this rice door to door; the mixing of sauces is called banjir, the flood.',ARRAY['Cook the rice plain, without spices: it is the white canvas on which everything will mix.','Make a chicken curry with coconut milk, spiced with cinnamon sticks and star anise.','Make a second curry, of okra and aubergine with tamarind, sharper and thinner.','Fry hard-boiled eggs until the skins crack, and keep a fish curry alongside.','Serve the rice and pour the sauces one over another: the banjir is not an accident, it is the point.']::text[]),
('kaeng-som','en','Southern kaeng som','Krabi, Thailand','A sour yellow curry, without coconut milk, of a strength northern Thailand considers unreasonable. Tamarind and turmeric give it body; the chilli is present in quantities that startle even regulars.',ARRAY['Poach a piece of the fish and pound it in a mortar with the chillies, shallot, fresh turmeric and shrimp paste.','This fish-based paste is the southern signature: it gives the broth a body the central curry lacks.','Slake it in a litre of boiling water and simmer for 10 minutes.','Season with tamarind, fish sauce and palm sugar: the first taste should be frankly sour.','Add the vegetables in order of firmness, then the fish and prawns for 5 minutes only.']::text[]),
('sinigang-na-baboy','en','Sinigang na baboy','Quezon City, Philippines','A pork soup soured with green tamarind, with vegetables and kangkong leaves. The sourness may come from tamarind, guava or green mango: each family defends its acid and scorns the others.',ARRAY['Blanch the ribs for 10 minutes and discard that water: the broth must stay clear.','Return them to two litres of water with the onion and tomato, and simmer for 1 hour, skimming.','Add the diced taro: it breaks down and thickens the broth naturally, which is the point.','Stir in the tamarind and taste: the soup should make you salivate, without catching the throat.','Add the vegetables in order of firmness, the water spinach last of all, and serve with rice and fish sauce.']::text[]),
('la-paz-batchoy','en','La Paz batchoy','Iloilo, Panay, Philippines','A noodle soup in a bone and offal broth, crowned with crushed chicharrón. La Paz market claims its invention in the 1930s; the broth there simmers from opening to closing.',ARRAY['Make a broth from pork and beef bones, 3 hours at a simmer, skimming relentlessly.','Soak the kidney for 2 hours in vinegared water, then poach it and slice it thinly.','Do the same with the liver, poaching it barely: overcooked it turns grainy.','Season the broth with a spoonful of shrimp paste and soy sauce, without making it salty.','Assemble the noodles, offal, pork and a poached egg, and crown with fried garlic and crushed chicharrón at the last moment.']::text[]),
('bun-cha-ca','en','Bún chả cá','Nha Trang, Vietnam','A clear fish broth with cakes of pounded flesh, fried then poached. The fish is pounded until springy: it is that bounce under the tooth, not the taste, that judges the cake.',ARRAY['Fillet the fish, skin it and pound the flesh in a mortar with garlic, sugar, fish sauce and plenty of pepper.','Pound for 15 minutes: the paste should turn sticky and bounce when thrown against the side.','Fold in chopped dill and spring onion, shape flat cakes and fry them 3 minutes a side.','Make a clear broth from the heads and bones with pineapple and tomato, and strain it carefully.','Poach the cakes for 5 minutes in that broth and serve over vermicelli, with sprouts, herbs and lime.']::text[]),
('lu-rou-fan','en','Lu rou fan','Taipei, Taiwan','Fatty minced pork stewed in soy sauce and rice wine, spooned over white rice. Taiwan calls it the national dish; north and south argue over the size of the dice, to the millimetre.',ARRAY['Cut the belly by knife into 5 mm dice, fat included: the fat makes the texture, and removing it makes another dish.','Fry the sliced shallots in oil to a deep gold, then lift them out and keep the oil.','Render the pork dry in a wok until it colours and the fat runs.','Add the garlic, fried shallots, both soy sauces, rice wine, rock sugar and spices.','Cover with water and simmer uncovered for 1¼ hours: the sauce should turn syrupy and coat every dice.']::text[]),
('o-a-tsian','en','Oyster omelette','Tainan, Taiwan','An omelette bound with sweet potato starch, translucent and elastic, with small oysters. The starch is what matters: without it, it is merely an oyster omelette, and the night markets would not forgive.',ARRAY['Slake the sweet potato starch in twice its volume of cold water and let it stand: it should stay thin and milky.','Make the sauce by thickening ketchup, miso, sugar and a little water with the cornstarch.','Heat the wok fiercely, oil it, and toss the oysters for 30 seconds.','Pour the starch batter over them: it turns translucent and gelatinous at once. Do not stir.','Break the eggs over the top, add the greens, flip in one movement and serve under the red sauce.']::text[]),
('thenthuk','en','Thenthuk','Boudhanath, Kathmandu, Nepal','A soup of dough torn by hand straight into the broth, with mutton and vegetables. Then means to pull, thuk soup: the pieces are irregular on principle, and a rolling pin would miss the point.',ARRAY['Knead the flour, water and salt into a firm dough, and rest it for an hour under a damp cloth.','Fry the diced mutton with the onion, garlic, ginger and tomato, then add two litres of water.','Simmer for 40 minutes, then add the daikon and carrot.','Roll the dough into 3 cm strips, oil them, then stretch and tear pieces straight over the simmering broth.','They rise within 3 minutes when cooked. Add the spinach and herbs, and serve scalding.']::text[]),
('biryani-sindhi','en','Sindhi biryani','Hyderabad, Sindh, Pakistan','A biryani with potatoes, prunes and yogurt, sharper and hotter than its cousins. Sindh adds dried plum: that sweet-sour counterpoint surprises, then quickly becomes indispensable.',ARRAY['Fry the sliced onions to a deep brown and set half aside: they perfume and colour.','Marinate the chicken for 3 hours in the yogurt, ginger-garlic paste, masala and half the fried onions.','Cook the marinade for 30 minutes with the tomato and potatoes, until the oil separates.','Par-boil the soaked rice for 6 minutes in salted water with whole spices, and drain it three-quarters cooked.','Layer meat, prunes, rice, herbs, fried onions and saffron; seal and leave 25 minutes over very low heat.']::text[]),
('kuku-sabzi','en','Kuku sabzi','Isfahan, Iran','An omelette so green with herbs that the egg barely shows, served at Nowruz. Green is the colour of renewal: eaten on the first day of spring so the year will be fertile.',ARRAY['Wash the herbs, spin them thoroughly dry and chop them very finely: you need an enormous volume, nearly two packed litres.','Blot them in a cloth: the least moisture would leave the kuku soft in the middle.','Beat the eggs without aerating them, add the flour, baking powder, turmeric and salt.','Fold in the herbs, crushed walnuts and barberries: the mixture should be more green than yellow.','Pour into a buttered pan and cook covered for 20 minutes over low heat, then flip for 15 more. Serve warm with yogurt.']::text[]),
('khash','en','Khash','Gyumri, Armenia','A cow''s-foot broth cooked all night and eaten at dawn with raw garlic and lavash. Served from December to March, before sunrise, and custom requires vodka with it, never wine.',ARRAY['Soak the trotters for 24 hours under cold running water, changing it six times: this removes the smell.','Scrape them thoroughly and blanch them for 15 minutes; discard that water.','Return them to a large pot of clean water, without salt, vegetable or any spice.','Simmer for 7 to 8 hours, skimming: the broth should turn milky and set to a jelly when cold.','Serve scalding and unsalted. Each diner adds salt, pounded garlic and crumbled lavash to their own bowl.']::text[]),
('shah-plov','en','Shah plov','Sheki, Azerbaijan','Saffron rice and lamb sealed in a crown of lavash bread, baked golden like a crown. It comes whole to the table and is opened in front of the guests: the crisp bread is half the dish.',ARRAY['Rinse the rice seven times and soak it for 2 hours in salted water: this gives long, separate grains.','Par-boil it for 6 minutes in plenty of water and drain it half cooked.','Fry the diced lamb with the onion, apricots, chestnuts and raisins for 30 minutes.','Line a buttered pot with lavash brushed with ghee and egg yolk, leaving the edges hanging well over.','Fill with layers of rice and meat, fold the bread over, and bake for 50 minutes at 180°C. Turn out in one movement and cut like a cake.']::text[]),
('soup-curry-sapporo','en','Sapporo soup curry','Sapporo, Hokkaidō, Japan','A thin, spiced curry in which a chicken leg and whole vegetables sit, with rice served separately. Invented in Sapporo in the 1970s, it is the exact opposite of thick Japanese curry: the broth is spooned, and the vegetables stand proud of the bowl.',ARRAY['Confit the very finely sliced onion for 40 minutes over low heat, until brown and melting: it is the base of the broth.','Add the grated garlic and ginger, then the spices off the heat — thrown into hot oil they burn in three seconds.','Add the grated tomato, reduce, then moisten with the broth and simmer 40 minutes.','On no account thicken it: a soup curry must stay as thin as consommé, and the least flour would make it an ordinary curry.','Fry the vegetables whole and separately so they hold their shape, and stand them up in the bowl with the leg. Rice on the side.']::text[]),
('ishikari-nabe','en','Ishikari nabe','Ishikari, Hokkaidō, Japan','A pot of salmon and vegetables in miso, simmered at the table over a burner. It comes from the mouth of the Ishikari, where fishermen cooked the unsellable trimmings; head and skin go in, for the gelatine, rather than the handsome fillets.',ARRAY['Soak the kombu an hour in cold water and lift it out as soon as the water trembles: boiled, it turns slimy and bitter.','Blanch the salmon pieces for ten seconds then rinse them cold — this removes scum and odour, and clears the broth.','Stand the vegetables upright in the pot, the slowest to cook at the bottom, and set the salmon on top.','Slake the miso in a ladle of hot broth before stirring it in, and never let it boil: above 80°C it loses its scent.','Finish with a knob of butter, the Hokkaidō signature, and set the pot in the middle of the table over its burner.']::text[]),
('kaisendon','en','Kaisendon','Otaru, Hokkaidō, Japan','A bowl of vinegared rice covered with raw fish, sea urchin and salmon roe. It is eaten at the harbour market from seven in the morning; unlike sushi the fish is not worked but simply sliced, which demands a freshness only a port allows.',ARRAY['Season the still-warm rice with sweetened vinegar, cutting it in with a spatula and never stirring, and fan it to make it shine.','Let it cool to body temperature: ice-cold rice numbs the fish, scalding rice cooks it.','Slice each fish according to its grain: tuna across the fibres, salmon on the bias and thinner, scallop split through its thickness.','Wipe the blade between each fish so as not to carry the taste of one onto another.','Arrange without overlapping, leaving the rice visible in places, and set the sea urchin and roe last — they crush under anything else.']::text[]),
('gyutan-sendai','en','Sendai gyūtan','Sendai, Tōhoku, Japan','Slices of beef tongue salted then charcoal-grilled, served with barley rice and oxtail soup. A Sendai cook put them on his menu in 1948: the occupying Americans ate neither tongue nor tail, and he took both.',ARRAY['Peel the tongue raw by slitting it with a point: at this stage the skin comes away with a knife, whereas after cooking it must be torn.','Cut it into 6 mm slices across, and score each slice in a shallow lattice on both sides.','Those cuts are not decorative: they stop the slice curling into a ball over the coals.','Dry-salt it and rest 48 hours in the cold: that long cure tenderises the tongue, which is a very firm muscle.','Grill over fierce coals 90 seconds a side, no longer. Serve with barley rice, oxtail soup and lemon.']::text[]),
('wanko-soba','en','Wanko soba','Morioka, Iwate, Japan','Tiny portions of buckwheat noodles served in small bowls that a server refills without pause. You stop only by closing the lid, and custom demands you count: fifteen bowls make one normal portion, and regulars pass a hundred.',ARRAY['Knead the buckwheat and wheat flours with warm water, working fast: buckwheat dough dries and cracks within minutes.','It has almost no gluten and is not kneaded like bread dough: you press it together, never stretching it.','Roll to 1.5 mm, fold the sheet like a concertina and cut noodles the same width as their thickness.','Cook them 90 seconds only in plenty of boiling water, then rinse in iced water to stop the cooking and wash off the starch.','Serve in small handfuls in a dashi and soy broth, with the garnishes apart. The bowls are refilled the moment they are empty.']::text[]),
('kiritanpo','en','Kiritanpo','Akita, Tōhoku, Japan','Rice mashed and moulded around a cedar stick, grilled then simmered in chicken broth. Akita woodsmen carried their rice into the forest this way and grilled it over the fire; the name comes from the shape, like a cut spear-tip wrapping.',ARRAY['Cook the rice wetter than usual, then pound it until half the grains are broken — not all, or it becomes a paste.','Mould it while still hot around wooden sticks in a one-centimetre layer, wetting your hands in salted water.','Grill over coals, turning, until the surface is golden and dry: that crust is what will stop the rice falling apart in the broth.','Twist out the sticks, cut into 5 cm lengths and add them to the broth for the last five minutes only.','Beyond that they collapse. Serve with the chicken, burdock shaved into slivers and maitake mushrooms.']::text[]),
('ika-no-shiokara','en','Ika no shiokara','Ishinomaki, Tōhoku, Japan','Strips of raw squid fermented in their own salted viscera, eaten on the tips of chopsticks with sake. The fermentation is driven by enzymes from the squid''s liver: a preservation method from before refrigeration, and the taste is powerfully briny.',ARRAY['Gut the squid keeping the brown-orange liver sac intact: it drives the fermentation, and a burst sac ruins the dish.','Salt the liver alone and let it drain 24 hours in the cold, then sieve it to keep only the cream.','Skin the bodies, cut them into 5 mm strips and salt them separately for two hours, then blot them.','Mix liver and squid with the sake and mirin, and ferment three to seven days at 8°C, stirring daily.','The mixture must smell of the sea and not of ammonia: at the least sharp odour it must be thrown away. Serve very cold, in very small quantities.']::text[]),
('tempura','en','Tempura','Tokyo, Japan','Prawns and vegetables in a barely mixed batter, fried to a pale, lacy crust. The Portuguese brought it in the 16th century with their ember-days fritters — tempora — but the Japanese made it the opposite of a coating: the batter must be nearly invisible.',ARRAY['Score the belly of the prawns in three places and stretch them: without this they curl into rings in the oil.','Beat the egg into iced water with ice cubes, then tip in the flour all at once and mix barely, eight or ten strokes with chopsticks.','The batter must stay lumpy and full of dry flour: overmixed, it develops gluten and gives a thick, soft crust.','Keep the bowl on a bed of ice throughout the frying — a batter that warms loses its lightness within two minutes.','Fry at 180°C, two or three pieces at a time. Serve with a dashi and soy broth and grated daikon.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('monjayaki','en','Monjayaki','Tsukishima, Tokyo, Japan','A very thin batter cooked on the griddle until sticky, scraped up with small spatulas straight off the iron. Unlike okonomiyaki it never sets: it stays semi-liquid and is eaten in small amounts, scalding, directly from the plate.',ARRAY['Whisk the flour into cold dashi to a liquid barely thicker than water: that is the whole difference from okonomiyaki.','Strain it: a single lump stays hard in the middle of a batter that itself will never set.','First fry the shredded cabbage and the fillings on the oiled griddle, then gather them into a ring with a hole in the centre.','Pour the liquid into the hole and let it bubble for a minute before breaking the dam and mixing everything.','Scrape and press constantly with two small spatulas for five minutes, until the batter catches. Eat straight off the griddle, in small scrapes.']::text[]),
('chanko-nabe','en','Chanko nabe','Ryōgoku, Tokyo, Japan','The sumo wrestlers'' pot, loaded with chicken, meatballs, tofu and vegetables. Chicken goes in rather than beef for a superstitious reason: a chicken stands on two legs, like a wrestler on his feet, whereas a four-legged animal touches the ground with its hands.',ARRAY['Make a broth from chicken carcass and dashi, and skim it relentlessly for the first fifteen minutes.','Shape minced chicken balls with grated ginger and a little miso, and poach them first: they scent the whole broth.','Arrange the ingredients in sectors rather than mixed: each cooks at its own pace and is lifted out when ready.','Add the cabbage last, packing it down: it releases a great deal of water and would thin the broth if added at the start.','Eat as you go, adding more ingredients to the broth as it concentrates. Finish with udon noodles in what remains.']::text[]),
('miso-katsu','en','Miso katsu','Nagoya, Chūbu, Japan','A breaded pork cutlet under a thick sauce of Nagoya red miso. Hatchō miso, fermented two years in barrels under stacked stones, is almost black and far saltier than white miso: it is used sparingly, cut with sugar and dashi.',ARRAY['Score the fat of the cutlet every two centimetres so it does not curl, and flatten it slightly.','Coat in flour, egg then panko, pressing barely: the panko must stay in standing flakes, not crushed.','Fry 6 minutes at 170°C, low and long — breaded pork over fierce heat browns before the centre cooks.','Make the sauce by slaking red miso in hot dashi with the sugar, mirin and sake, and reduce for 10 minutes.','It must coat the spoon and stay glossy: over-reduced it turns bitter. Pour it at the last moment, over a sliced katsu.']::text[]),
('hoto-nabe','en','Hōtō','Kōfu, Yamanashi, Japan','Wide flat noodles simmered directly in a miso and pumpkin soup, never boiled separately. That is exactly what sets it apart from udon: the noodle starch thickens the broth, and rinsing them first would ruin the dish.',ARRAY['Knead the flour and salted water for 10 minutes into a firm dough and rest it 30 minutes under a cloth.','Roll it to 3 mm and cut strips a centimetre and a half wide: hōtō is far wider and thicker than udon.','On no account rinse them or precook them: their flour is what will thicken the soup, and that is the whole recipe.','Cook the pumpkin, daikon and taro in the dashi for 20 minutes, until the pumpkin begins to collapse.','Drop the raw noodles in and simmer 15 minutes, then slake the miso off the heat. The soup should be thick and yellow.']::text[]),
('kishimen','en','Kishimen','Nagoya, Chūbu, Japan','Wheat noodles as flat as ribbons, served in a clear dashi broth. They are so thin they cook in three minutes against twelve for udon; they are sold on the platforms of Nagoya station, eaten standing between two trains.',ARRAY['Knead a firm dough, then tread it wrapped in a bag, as in Japan: the foot develops the gluten without warming the dough.','Rest it 2 hours: without rest it shrinks back and never reaches the required thinness.','Roll to 1.5 mm — far thinner than udon — and cut 8 mm ribbons.','Cook them 3 minutes in plenty of boiling water and rinse cold to remove the starch, then reheat them in the broth.','Nagoya broth is clear and made with light soy: a dark broth would mask the wheat flavour of the noodle, which is the whole point.']::text[]),
('kitsune-udon','en','Kitsune udon','Osaka, Kansai, Japan','Udon in a clear broth, crowned with a sheet of fried tofu simmered in soy syrup. The name means fox: legend has Inari, the harvest deity, travelling with foxes, and foxes are said to love the fried tofu left at her shrines.',ARRAY['Blanch the fried tofu sheets for thirty seconds and press them between two plates: this degreasing is essential, or the broth turns oily.','Simmer them 15 minutes in a syrup of dashi, soy, mirin and sugar, until they have drunk it entirely.','Let them cool in that syrup: they keep absorbing as they cool, and that is when they take their sweet-salt flavour.','Make a kombu and bonito dashi, and season it with light Kansai soy — Osaka broth is nearly transparent, unlike Tokyo''s.','Cook the udon, rinse them, reheat them in the broth and lay the tofu sheet on top, whole and uncut.']::text[]),
('yudofu','en','Yudōfu','Nanzen-ji, Kyoto, Kansai, Japan','Tofu warmed in barely trembling kombu water and dipped in a soy sauce. It is the cooking of Kyoto''s Zen temples: nothing is added to the tofu, and the exercise is precisely to do nothing — overheated, it turns spongy and riddled with holes.',ARRAY['Use a Kyoto tofu, firm but silky, made with spring water: it is the only ingredient in the dish and nothing will hide its quality.','Soak the kombu an hour in cold water in the earthenware pot, and do not remove it — here it stays in the bottom throughout the meal.','Cut the tofu into 5 cm cubes and lower them gently into the cold water, before heating.','Bring it very slowly to 70°C and hold: the water must never tremble, only steam. At a boil, tofu holes and toughens.','Lift the cubes out with a skimmer as they rise, and dip them in a sauce of soy, mirin and bonito, with grated ginger.']::text[]),
('kaiseki-ryori','en','Kaiseki ryōri','Kyoto, Kansai, Japan','A meal of some ten courses following a fixed order of techniques: raw, grilled, simmered, steamed, fried. It descends from the light meal served before the tea ceremony; its first rule is seasonal — an ingredient outside its fortnight cannot appear.',ARRAY['Choose every ingredient within its fortnight of perfection: kaiseki has no fixed recipe, only a calendar.','Make a first-extraction dashi — kombu lifted out at 60°C, bonito infused thirty seconds — and never let a drop of it boil.','Serve in the order of techniques: raw sakizuke, wanmono in clear broth, grilled yakimono, simmered takiawase, fried agemono.','Each course is three or four mouthfuls and comes in a different vessel, chosen for the season and the colour of the food.','Finish with rice, miso soup and pickles served together: that is the signal of the end, and nothing follows.']::text[]),
('hiroshima-tsukemen','en','Hiroshima tsukemen','Hiroshima, Chūgoku, Japan','Cold noodles dipped in a chilli sauce, with raw cabbage and cucumber. Unlike Tokyo tsukemen, served with a scalding broth, the Hiroshima version is eaten entirely cold: the sauce is sour, fiercely hot, and ordered by strength level.',ARRAY['Make the sauce the day before: soy, vinegar, sugar, grated garlic, sesame oil and Korean chilli, rested overnight in the cold.','The chilli must infuse rather than be added at the last moment: a same-day sauce is aggressive and unbalanced.','Cook the noodles, rinse them at length in iced water and drain thoroughly — residual water would thin the sauce.','Shred the cabbage and cucumber very finely and leave them ten minutes in iced water so they snap.','Serve noodles, vegetables and sauce in three separate vessels. You dip mouthful by mouthful, and the sauce is never poured over the noodles.']::text[]),
('kaki-no-dotenabe','en','Oyster dote-nabe','Hiroshima, Chūgoku, Japan','Oysters simmered in a pot whose rim has been lined with a wall of miso. Dote means dyke: the miso is smeared as a bank around the inner wall, and pushed into the broth little by little to adjust the seasoning as the meal goes on.',ARRAY['Wash the shucked oysters in salted water with a little starch, which traps the grit without damaging them, then rinse and blot.','Mix the miso with the sugar, mirin and sake to a supple paste, and smear it as a bank around the inner wall of the pot, above the liquid line.','Pour in the dashi and arrange the vegetables, without the liquid reaching the miso.','Bring to a tremble and push a little miso into the broth with chopsticks: the seasoning is adjusted gradually, on demand.','Add the oysters last and count three minutes: beyond that they shrink and toughen beyond recovery.']::text[]),
('miyajima-manju','en','Momiji manjū','Miyajima, Chūgoku, Japan','Small maple-leaf-shaped cakes filled with red bean paste. They were created around 1906 for pilgrims to the Itsukushima shrine; the shape recalls the maples of the Momijidani valley, and the island sells several million a year.',ARRAY['Whisk the eggs and sugar for 8 minutes to the ribbon, then add the warm honey, milk and flour sifted with the baking powder.','Rest the batter an hour in the cold: it must relax, and a rested batter flows better into the mould''s cavities.','Heat the mould over low heat and brush it with butter for every batch, without exception.','Fill each cavity halfway, set a ball of bean paste in the centre and cover with the remaining batter.','Close the mould and cook 3 minutes a side, turning once. The cakes must stay pale and soft, never browned like a biscuit.']::text[]),
('sanuki-udon','en','Sanuki udon','Takamatsu, Kagawa, Shikoku, Japan','Thick, square-sectioned udon famous for their elastic chew, served with very little dressing. Kagawa eats three times the Japanese average and has eight hundred shops for a million people; the dough there is kneaded underfoot, wrapped in a bag.',ARRAY['Dissolve the salt in the water before adding it: the proportion varies with the season, higher in winter, and it governs the elasticity.','Knead, then wrap the dough in a bag and tread it for twenty minutes, folding as you go: the foot develops gluten without warming the dough as a hand would.','Rest 3 hours covered, then tread it again briefly before rolling.','Roll to 3 mm and cut noodles 3 mm wide: the section must be square, the sanuki signature.','Cook 12 minutes in a great deal of unsalted water, rinse cold while rubbing, and serve with a plain dashi, a raw egg or grated ginger.']::text[]),
('katsuo-no-tataki','en','Katsuo no tataki','Kōchi, Shikoku, Japan','A loin of bonito seared for seconds over a rice-straw flame, raw at the centre, cut in thick slices. Straw burns very fast and very hot: it seizes the surface without cooking the flesh, and leaves a smokiness charcoal does not give.',ARRAY['Fillet the fish keeping the skin: it takes the flame and shields the flesh during the operation.','Salt the surface generously and leave it ten minutes — the salt forms a crust that helps the searing.','Skewer the loin on a fan of chopsticks and pass it through the straw flame, ten seconds a side, turning constantly.','Plunge it immediately into iced water to stop the cooking dead, then blot it thoroughly: without that shock the heat travels on toward the centre.','Cut thick slices, a centimetre, and serve with plenty of raw sliced garlic, ginger, spring onion and ponzu.']::text[]),
('tokushima-ramen','en','Tokushima ramen','Tokushima, Shikoku, Japan','A ramen in a dark, sweet pork broth, crowned with simmered belly and a raw egg cracked on top. The egg is not soft-boiled but entirely raw: it is broken and stirred into the scalding broth, which thickens it and softens the soy, very forward here.',ARRAY['Blanch the pork bones five minutes, throw the water away and rinse them: that is what gives a clean broth rather than a grey, scummy one.','Return them to cold water with the garlic and ginger, and simmer 3 hours without ever boiling hard.','Simmer the belly separately in a syrup of soy, sugar, mirin and sake for 90 minutes, then cut it into strips.','Season the broth with that cooking syrup: it gives Tokushima ramen its brown colour and its sweetness.','Assemble the noodles, belly and bean sprouts, and crack a raw egg in the centre. It blends into the broth from the first mouthful.']::text[]),
('mentaiko','en','Mentaiko','Fukuoka, Kyūshū, Japan','Pollock roe sacs salted then marinated in chilli, eaten with rice or tossed through pasta. A Japanese repatriate from Korea developed them in Fukuoka in 1949, adapting Korean myeongnan-jeot to local taste, less salty and less fiercely hot.',ARRAY['Choose intact, firm sacs: a pierced membrane lets the eggs escape during salting and the whole batch is lost.','Dry-salt them between two layers of salt and leave 12 hours in the cold, tilting the dish so the brine runs off.','Rinse with sake and let them soak in it for two hours: the alcohol removes bitterness and firms the membrane.','Make the marinade with mirin, Korean chilli, kombu and garlic, and rest it overnight before laying the sacs in.','Marinate five days in the fridge, turning daily. Slice and serve on hot rice, or mix with butter for pasta.']::text[]),
('champon','en','Champon','Nagasaki, Kyūshū, Japan','Noodles in a white pork and seafood broth, under a mountain of stir-fried vegetables. Chen Ping-shun, a Chinese restaurateur in Nagasaki, invented it around 1899 to feed Chinese students cheaply: the noodles cook in the broth itself, which turns it milky.',ARRAY['Boil the pork bones and chicken hard for 3 hours — unlike other Japanese broths this one must boil vigorously to emulsify the fat and turn white.','Stir-fry the meats, seafood and vegetables over fierce heat in sesame oil, in that order, within three minutes.','Pour the scalding broth over the vegetables in the wok and bring it to the boil.','Tip the raw noodles straight in: their starch finishes thickening and whitening the broth, and that is the whole difference from ramen.','Give it three minutes, adjust the salt and serve at once, vegetables on top. A champon that waits has its noodles drink the broth.']::text[]),
('castella','en','Castella','Nagasaki, Kyūshū, Japan','A dense, moist honey cake with no fat, baked in a wooden frame. Portuguese traders brought it to Nagasaki in the 16th century as pão de Castela; it holds neither butter nor leaven, and all its lightness comes from beating the eggs.',ARRAY['Use a high-gluten flour and sift it three times: with no fat, the structure holds only by gluten and air.','Whisk whole eggs and sugar over a warm bain-marie for 12 minutes, until the mixture ribbons and takes five seconds to settle.','Add the honey slaked in warm milk, then the flour in three additions, folding with a spatula without breaking the foam.','Pour into a paper-lined frame, rap the tin to bring the large bubbles up, and drag a chopstick across the surface to burst them.','Bake 50 minutes at 160°C, turn out warm and wrap in film at once: that overnight rest in its own moisture gives castella its texture.']::text[]),
('rafute','en','Rafute','Naha, Okinawa, Japan','Pork belly simmered three hours in awamori, black sugar and soy, until it melts. It was a dish of the Ryūkyū royal court; Okinawa eats the whole pig, from snout to trotters, and says only the squeal is wasted.',ARRAY['Poach the whole belly 1 hour in water with the ginger, then throw that water away: it carries off most of the fat and the strong smell.','Let the meat cool before cutting it into 5 cm cubes: hot, it collapses under the knife.','Simmer it in awamori and dashi for 1 hour, with no soy or sugar — salt added too early firms the fibres and blocks the tenderising.','Then add the black sugar, and twenty minutes later the soy: sugar penetrates before salt, the Japanese order, and it is not arbitrary.','Reduce for another hour uncovered, basting, until the sauce coats and shines. Serve with rice and a touch of mustard.']::text[]),
('sata-andagi','en','Sātā andagī','Naha, Okinawa, Japan','Okinawan doughnuts that split into three as they fry, like a flower opening. The name means fried sugar in Okinawan; the split is the sign of good frying, and a ball left smooth means the oil was too hot.',ARRAY['Beat the eggs and sugar without aerating them: an airy batter would swell too fast and burst rather than split cleanly.','Add the flour sifted with the baking powder and mix as little as possible; the batter should be thick and sticky, almost impossible to shape.','Rest it 30 minutes then scoop balls with an oiled hand, without smoothing them.','Lower them into oil at 160°C only — no hotter: it is the low temperature that lets the ball swell slowly and split in three.','Allow 8 minutes, turning them: they should be brown outside and dry within. At 180°C they would burn before opening.']::text[]),
('okinawa-soba','en','Okinawa soba','Nago, Okinawa, Japan','Thick wheat noodles in a pork and bonito broth, with a pork rib and pickled ginger. Despite the name there is not a gram of buckwheat in it: the Japanese soba federation objected in 1976, and Okinawa won a dispensation to keep the word.',ARRAY['Dissolve the bicarbonate in the water before kneading: it gives the noodles their yellow colour and their chew in place of kansui.','Knead firmly, rest 2 hours, then roll to 2 mm and cut noodles 3 mm wide.','Make a pork and bonito broth, skimmed relentlessly: in Okinawa it stays clear, unlike the tonkotsu of Kyūshū.','Braise the pork ribs 2 hours in a syrup of soy, brown sugar and awamori until the meat leaves the bone.','Cook the noodles, rinse them, oil them lightly and rest them an hour — in Okinawa they are cooked ahead, and they are the better for waiting.']::text[]),
('jiaozi','en','Jiaozi','Beijing, Northern China','Pork and cabbage dumplings, poached or pan-fried, made as a family on New Year''s Eve. Their shape recalls imperial silver ingots, and they are eaten at midnight to call in prosperity; one is sometimes hidden with a coin inside.',ARRAY['Salt the chopped cabbage and let it drain 30 minutes, then press it thoroughly: its water is the enemy of a jiaozi, piercing the wrapper from inside.','Work the filling always in the same direction for five minutes, adding ginger water a spoonful at a time until it turns sticky.','That emulsion is what gives the juice: a filling merely mixed stays dry and solid.','Roll discs thicker at the centre than at the edge, fill them and pleat only the upper edge against the smooth lower one.','Poach three times, adding a glass of cold water at each return to the boil — the northern method, and it stops the wrappers bursting.']::text[]),
('zhajiangmian','en','Zhajiangmian','Beijing, Northern China','Wheat noodles under a very thick sauce of pork and fermented soybean paste, ringed with raw vegetables cut into batons. The sauce is nearly black and so salty that only a spoonful goes in: it is the raw vegetables, in quantity, that balance the bowl.',ARRAY['Slake both soybean pastes in a little water and rest them ten minutes: dry, they burn instantly in the wok.','Fry the minced pork letting it render all its fat and begin to crisp — that fat will carry the sauce.','Tip in the slaked pastes and fry them slowly for 20 minutes over low heat, stirring: they must darken and the oil rise to the surface.','That separation of oil is the sign the sauce is ready; before it, the sauce keeps a raw fermented taste.','Cut every raw vegetable into fine batons and ring them around the noodles. Each person mixes their own bowl.']::text[]),
('guo-bao-rou','en','Guo bao rou','Harbin, Northeastern China','Slices of pork loin fried twice and lacquered with a vinegar caramel, crisp and sharp. A Harbin cook created them around 1907 for Russian diplomats who found Chinese food too salty: he sweetened and soured it, and the dish stayed.',ARRAY['Cut the loin into 3 mm slices across the grain and marinate them twenty minutes in sake and salt.','Soak the potato starch in water for an hour, then pour off the clear water and keep the paste at the bottom: it is that wet starch, not dry powder, that gives the blistered crust.','Coat each slice in the paste and fry once at 160°C for 2 minutes, until they whiten.','Raise the oil to 190°C and fry them again for 40 seconds: the second bath gives the crispness that survives the sauce.','Make a pale caramel with sugar and vinegar, tip in the slices and toss for ten seconds only, off the heat. Serve immediately.']::text[]),
('clay-pot-rice','en','Clay pot rice','Guangzhou, Guangdong, China','Rice cooked in an earthenware pot with Chinese sausage, served straight from the pot. The whole point is at the bottom: a crust of toasted rice scraped up at the end, which a cook achieves by tilting the pot over the flame in the last minutes.',ARRAY['Soak the rice 30 minutes and drain it: soaked, it cooks faster and the base crusts before the top burns.','Oil the inside of the cold pot generously, sides included — without that the crust sticks and tears.','Add the rice and water at one and a quarter volumes, bring to the boil uncovered then cover and drop to the lowest heat.','After ten minutes, lay the sausages and marinated chicken on the rice, close it and give it twelve minutes.','Tilt the pot over the flame and turn it a quarter turn every thirty seconds for three minutes: that is how the crust forms. Pour over the sauce and mix at the table.']::text[]),
('wonton-noodles','en','Wonton noodles','Guangzhou, Guangdong, China','Fine egg noodles and shrimp dumplings in a clear broth of dried shrimp. Cantonese noodles are kneaded with a long bamboo pole the cook straddles and rides: the only way to compress so dry a dough.',ARRAY['Make a broth from pork bones and dry-toasted dried shrimp: they, not fish, give a Cantonese broth its flavour.','Salt the raw prawns for twenty minutes, rinse them and cut them into large pieces — never minced, they must snap.','Mix them with the pork and sesame oil, working in one direction only, and rest an hour in the cold.','Fold the wontons without seeking perfection: the floating tail of wrapper is part of the dish, and it gives the cloud texture.','Cook the noodles 20 seconds only, rinse them cold then return them to boiling water for five seconds: that double pass gives the Cantonese chew.']::text[]),
('shuang-pi-nai','en','Double skin milk','Shunde, Guangdong, China','A buffalo milk custard with two skins, steamed in its own bowl. The first skin forms as the milk cools, the milk is poured out from under it, then a second forms during steaming: hence the name, and the operation fails with milk below eight per cent fat.',ARRAY['Bring the buffalo milk to a tremble then pour it into the bowls and let it cool twenty minutes: a thick skin forms on the surface.','Pierce that skin gently at the edge and pour the milk out from beneath into another vessel, leaving the skin stuck to the bottom of the bowl.','This is the decisive gesture: a torn skin, or one carried off with the milk, and the dish has one skin instead of two.','Mix that milk with the beaten whites and sugar, strain, then pour it back very gently down the side so the skin lifts and rises.','Steam 12 minutes over gentle heat, covered with film: condensation falling on it would pierce the second skin.']::text[]),
('hong-shao-rou','en','Hong shao rou','Shanghai, Eastern China','Cubes of pork belly braised in a caramel of cane sugar, soy and rice wine, lacquered and glossy. Red braising is a technique rather than a recipe: it is the caramel, made dry before any liquid, that gives the colour, not the soy sauce.',ARRAY['Poach the whole belly 10 minutes with ginger, throw the water away and rinse: that blanching removes scum and odour.','Cut it into 4 cm cubes keeping the rind — without it the piece falls apart and the sauce gains no gelatine.','Melt the rock sugar dry in a little oil to a dark amber, then tip in the pork cubes and coat them.','That caramel is the heart of the technique: too pale and the colour is missing; burnt and the whole dish turns bitter and must be started again.','Deglaze with Shaoxing wine, add the soys, spices and water to cover, and simmer 1½ hours then reduce uncovered for twenty minutes.']::text[]),
('chou-doufu','en','Stinky tofu','Shanghai, Eastern China','Tofu fermented in a brine of vegetables and shrimp, fried and served with a chilli sauce. The smell is legendary and carries fifty metres; the taste is mild and milky, which disconcerts anyone who knew only the smell.',ARRAY['The brine is made weeks ahead by fermenting vegetables, dried shrimp and salt in water, until it smells frankly.','Press the tofu between two boards for two hours to drive out the water — waterlogged tofu will not take up brine.','Submerge it completely and ferment it six to forty-eight hours according to the strength wanted, in the cold.','Rinse it quickly and blot it, then fry at 180°C for four minutes: the outside must be brown and crackling, the inside stay creamy.','Serve scalding, split at the centre, with fermented cabbage and a sauce of chilli, garlic, soy and vinegar poured into the cut.']::text[]),
('nian-gao','en','Nian gao','Shanghai, Eastern China','A glutinous rice cake with brown sugar, steamed then sliced and fried. Its name is a pun: nian gao means year cake, but sounds like higher year, and it is eaten at New Year so that the coming one may rise above the last.',ARRAY['Dissolve the brown sugar in hot water and let it cool: poured boiling onto glutinous rice flour it cooks it into unrecoverable lumps.','Pour the warm syrup onto the flour, whisking, to a smooth batter the consistency of thick pancake batter.','Strain it: glutinous rice flour forms lumps that stay hard after cooking and never break down.','Pour into an oiled tin, set the red dates on top and steam 1 hour, covered with film against condensation.','Cool twelve hours — a warm nian gao is impossible to slice. Cut slices, dip in beaten egg and fry until golden.']::text[]),
('zui-ji','en','Drunken chicken','Shanghai, Eastern China','Chicken poached then marinated overnight in Shaoxing wine, served cold as a starter. The flesh must stay barely pink at the bone: in Shanghai a chicken white right through is held to be overcooked, and the marinade never rescues dry meat.',ARRAY['Rub the chicken with salt and leave it an hour, then lower it into simmering water with the ginger and spring onion.','Kill the heat as soon as it returns to a tremble, cover, and let the chicken poach in the cooling water for 40 minutes.','That carry-over cooking is what keeps the flesh juicy: water held at a boil would dry it out within twenty minutes.','Plunge it at once into iced water: the shock contracts the skin and gives it its characteristic gelatinous texture.','Joint it, lay the pieces in the Shaoxing wine with the goji berries and star anise, and marinate 24 hours in the cold. Served chilled.']::text[]),
('dandan-mian','en','Dandan noodles','Chengdu, Sichuan, China','Noodles over a broth-free sauce of crisp pork, sesame and Sichuan pepper. The name comes from the dan dan, the carrying pole the seller balanced on his shoulder with his baskets: the original has no broth precisely because it would have had to be carried.',ARRAY['Fry the minced pork dry until it crisps and separates into grains — a good ten minutes, until it stops sizzling.','Add the chopped preserved greens and stir-fry two minutes: their acidity is what stops the dish being merely fatty and hot.','Toast the Sichuan pepper dry then grind it in a mortar: ground in advance, it loses its numbing effect within days.','Build the sauce in the bottom of the bowl — sesame paste, chilli oil, soy, vinegar, garlic, pepper — without a drop of broth.','Set the scalding noodles on top with two spoonfuls of their cooking water, and mix at the table. Crown with the pork and crushed peanuts.']::text[]),
('gong-bao-ji-ding','en','Kung Pao chicken','Chengdu, Sichuan, China','Diced chicken stir-fried with dried chillies, peanuts and a sweet-sour black vinegar sauce. It carries the title of Ding Baozhen, governor of Sichuan in the 19th century; it was renamed during the Cultural Revolution to erase the imperial reference, then took its name back.',ARRAY['Cut the chicken into 1.5 cm dice and marinate them twenty minutes in Shaoxing wine, soy and starch: that coating holds the water in the flesh.','Make the sauce entirely in advance in a bowl — black vinegar, sugar, soy, starch: the stir-fry takes ninety seconds and nothing can be improvised.','Toast the dried chillies and Sichuan pepper in warm oil until they darken, and lift them out if you prefer it mild.','Sear the chicken over maximum heat without stirring for the first thirty seconds: that is how it colours instead of boiling.','Pour in the sauce and stir ten seconds only: it thickens instantly. Add the peanuts off the heat, so they stay crisp.']::text[]),
('tang-yuan','en','Tang yuan','Chengdu, Sichuan, China','Glutinous rice balls filled with black sesame, served in their sweetened cooking water. They are eaten at the Lantern Festival that closes New Year: their round shape and their name, close to tuányuán, reunion, make them the symbol of the family gathered.',ARRAY['Toast the black sesame dry until it crackles, then grind it while still warm: cold, it will not release its oil.','Mix it with the sugar and lard, and chill until firm before shaping balls — a soft filling pierces the dough as you work.','Pour boiling water onto a fifth of the flour to make a cooked paste, then mix it with the rest and warm water: that blend of cooked and raw dough is what makes the ball extensible.','Enclose each ball in a disc of dough, drawing it up by hand, and roll it smooth with not the least crack left.','Poach in trembling — never boiling — water until they rise, then two minutes more. Serve in a syrup of ginger and rock sugar.']::text[]),
('poutine-rapee','en','Poutine râpée','Bouctouche, New Brunswick, Canada','A ball of grated potato stuffed with salt pork, boiled for three hours. Nothing to do with Quebec poutine; in Acadia it is the Christmas dish, and grating twenty pounds of potatoes is a family affair.',ARRAY['Grate two thirds of the potatoes very finely and press them in a cloth until nothing more runs out.','Collect the pressing liquid and let it settle: the starch falls to the bottom; discard the clear water and keep the starch.','Boil the remaining third, mash it, and mix it with the pressed gratings and the recovered starch.','Shape balls around a cube of salt pork and onion, sealing them well: one leak and the ball falls apart.','Poach for 3 hours in barely simmering salted water, never at a hard boil. Serve with butter, brown sugar or molasses.']::text[]),
('bannock-des-prairies','en','Bannock','Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, Canada','A flat unleavened bread cooked in a pan or on a stick over the fire. First Nations adopted it in the 19th century with trading-post flour; it has become an ambiguous symbol, both food and memory.',ARRAY['Mix the flour, baking powder and salt, then rub in the lard with your fingertips.','Pour in the warm water all at once and bring the dough together in a few movements: overworked, it turns elastic and hard.','Fold in the saskatoon berries without crushing them, if you have them.','Flatten the dough to 2 cm and cook it in a pan in a little fat, 8 minutes a side over medium heat.','It is ready when it sounds hollow. Serve hot, broken by hand, with maple syrup.']::text[]),
('hangtown-fry','en','Hangtown fry','Placerville, California, United States','An omelette of fried oysters and bacon, born in 1849 in the gold camps. A newly rich miner is said to have asked for the most expensive dish on the menu: oysters came by mule from San Francisco, and eggs cost a fortune.',ARRAY['Shuck the oysters and pat them thoroughly dry: a wet oyster refuses the crumb.','Flour them, dip in beaten egg then breadcrumbs, and fry 90 seconds a side in butter.','Grill the bacon until crisp, and keep the fat in the pan.','Beat the remaining eggs with the cream, without aerating them, and pour them onto the hot fat.','Arrange the oysters and bacon over the top, let it set for 4 minutes over low heat, and serve with lemon.']::text[]),
('green-chile-stew','en','Green chile stew','Albuquerque, New Mexico, United States','A pork stew with roasted Hatch green chiles, thick and green. Every autumn New Mexico roasts its chiles in rotating drums in car parks: the smell announces the season better than a calendar.',ARRAY['Roast the green chiles until the skins blister and blacken, then shut them in a bag for 15 minutes.','Peel, seed and chop them roughly: they should stay in visible pieces.','Sear the cubed pork in lard, dust with flour and let it turn golden.','Add the onion, garlic, cumin and oregano, then the stock, and simmer for 45 minutes.','Add the chiles, tomato and potatoes for 30 minutes. Serve with flour tortillas.']::text[]),
('frogmore-stew','en','Frogmore stew','Beaufort, South Carolina, United States','Shrimp, smoked sausage, corn and potatoes boiled together and tipped onto a table covered in newspaper. You eat standing, without plate or fork, and throw the shells into the middle.',ARRAY['Bring a very large pot of water to the boil with the seasoning, cut lemons and whole garlic.','The broth should taste frankly too salty and too spiced: the ingredients will absorb it.','Add the potatoes for 12 minutes, then the sausage in lengths for 5 minutes.','Add the corn in lengths for 5 minutes, then the whole shrimp for 3 minutes only.','Drain in one movement and tip the pot into the middle of the table. Melted butter and lemon to taste.']::text[]),
('kalua-pig','en','Kālua pig','Hilo, Hawaii, United States','A whole pig cooked seven hours in an imu, an earth oven lined with lava stones and banana leaves. Hawaiian red sea salt is the only seasoning; the smoke does the rest.',ARRAY['Dig a pit and heat lava stones in it on a hardwood fire for three hours.','Score the pork deeply and rub it with red salt, working into the cuts.','Slip hot stones into the cavity: the animal then cooks from within as well as without.','Wrap it in ti leaves then banana leaves, set it on the stones and cover with damp earth.','Leave for 7 hours without opening. The flesh shreds at a fork; toss it with a little steamed cabbage.']::text[]),
('burgoo-kentucky','en','Burgoo','Lexington, Kentucky, United States','A stew of several meats and every vegetable, simmered half a day in a cauldron. It is made for horse races and political rallies; the rule is that the spoon must stand up in it.',ARRAY['Cook the meats together for 2 hours in the beef stock, until they leave the bone.','Lift them out, bone them and shred them by hand; return the meat to the broth.','Add the tomato, onion and potatoes, and simmer for 1 hour, stirring regularly.','Add the corn, lima beans, okra and cabbage, and continue for 2 hours over very low heat.','Stir the bottom often: at this stage burgoo catches in an instant. Finish with Worcestershire and bourbon.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('haluski-pittsburgh','en','Pittsburgh haluski','Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States','Noodles with butter-melted cabbage, the dish of Slovak steelworkers. The cabbage must cook forty minutes until sweet and brown: rushed, it stays a vegetable and misses the point.',ARRAY['Slice the cabbage very finely, salt it and let it drain for 30 minutes, then squeeze it.','Fry the bacon, lift it out, and melt the onion in the rendered fat and butter.','Add the cabbage and cook it for 40 minutes over medium heat, stirring now and then.','It should go from white to golden brown and turn frankly sweet: that is the only moment that matters.','Toss in the cooked noodles, caraway and bacon, season generously with pepper, and serve with soured cream.']::text[]),
('rice-and-beans-belize','en','Belizean rice and beans','Belize City, Belize','Rice cooked in coconut milk with red beans, served with chicken stewed in recado. Belize distinguishes rice and beans, cooked together, from beans and rice, cooked apart: they are not the same dish.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then cook them for 1 hour with the garlic, thyme and whole chilli.','Marinate the chicken in recado rojo, vinegar and garlic for 3 hours.','Caramelise the brown sugar in the oil until it smokes, and drop in the chicken: it takes a mahogany colour.','Barely moisten and simmer covered for 45 minutes.','Pour the rice into the beans with the coconut milk — two parts liquid to one of rice — and cook covered for 25 minutes.']::text[]),
('nacatamal','en','Nacatamal','Managua, Nicaragua','A very large maize tamal filled with pork, rice, potato and olive, cooked five hours. Eaten on Sunday morning at breakfast, with coffee and bread: one is ample for two.',ARRAY['Marinate the pork for 12 hours in sour orange, garlic and annatto.','Work the masa with melted lard and a little broth, at length, until a small ball floats in water.','Soften the banana leaves over a flame and lay them crosswise, two thicknesses deep.','Spread a large ladle of masa, then the pork, raw rice, sliced potato, olive, prune and mint.','Fold into a large square parcel, tie it, and steam for 5 hours. It is opened on the plate, never before.']::text[]),
('sancocho-de-gallina-panama','en','Sancocho de gallina','Chitré, Herrera, Panama','A hen broth with yam and culantro, drunk scalding even in great heat. In Panama it is served at daybreak after a night out, and it is sworn to set a man right.',ARRAY['Marinate the jointed hen in pounded garlic, oregano and lime for an hour.','Put it in three litres of cold water and bring slowly to a simmer, skimming relentlessly.','Cook for 1 hour: a farm hen needs that long, a battery chicken far less.','Add the yam in large pieces and the corn, and continue for 30 minutes.','Add the culantro only at the end, whole, and lift it out before serving. White rice alongside.']::text[]),
('gallo-en-chicha','en','Gallo en chicha','Suchitoto, El Salvador','A cockerel stewed in chicha, fermented maize beer, with dried fruit and panela. It is made for the patron saint''s day; the chicha needs eight days to ferment, so nobody starts the day before.',ARRAY['Marinate the jointed cockerel for 12 hours in the chicha with garlic, bay and spices.','Drain it keeping the marinade, pat it dry and colour it in the oil.','Dry-toast the relajo spices and grind them: this Salvadoran blend is what perfumes the dish.','Add onion, tomato, marinade, panela and dried fruit, and simmer covered for 2½ hours.','The sauce should turn dark and syrupy, frankly sweet-sour. Serve with rice and bread.']::text[]),
('run-down','en','Run down','Port Antonio, Jamaica','Fish stewed in coconut milk reduced until the oil separates. The name describes the method: the coconut milk is ''run down'' until it breaks into custard, before the fish even goes in.',ARRAY['Boil the coconut milk uncovered for 25 minutes, stirring, until it thickens and the oil beads: that is the run down.','Add nothing before this step: put in too early, aromatics stop the milk separating.','Add the onion, garlic, spring onion, thyme and crushed allspice.','Add the tomato and the whole scotch bonnet — unsplit — and give it 10 minutes.','Slide in the fish in lengths and poach for 12 minutes without stirring. Serve with boiled green plantain.']::text[]),
('conch-fritters','en','Conch fritters','Nassau, Bahamas','Fritters of chopped conch, served with a pink lime sauce. The conch is beaten with a mallet before chopping: without that it stays rubbery whatever the cooking.',ARRAY['Beat the conch with a mallet to tenderise it, then chop it finely by knife.','Marinate it for 20 minutes in lime: the acid continues what the mallet began.','Mix the flour, baking powder, eggs and milk into a thick batter, denser than a pancake batter.','Fold in the drained conch, the finely diced vegetables and the chilli.','Drop spoonfuls into oil at 175°C and fry for 4 minutes, turning. Serve with mayonnaise, ketchup and lime.']::text[]),
('fungee-and-pepperpot','en','Fungee and pepperpot','St. John’s, Antigua and Barbuda','A maize polenta with okra alongside a stew of greens and salted meats. Antigua''s national dish is eaten on Wednesdays, the day schools historically served pepperpot.',ARRAY['Soak the salted meats for 8 hours, blanch them, then cook them for 1 hour in clean water.','Add the squash, aubergine and okra, and give them 25 minutes: the vegetables should collapse and thicken the stew.','Add the spinach and the whole chilli for the last 10 minutes.','For the fungee, cook the sliced okra in salted water, lift it out, and rain in the maize flour.','Work it for 20 minutes with a flat stick, return the okra, beat in butter, and mould it in a wetted bowl.']::text[]),
('callaloo-chicken-dominique','en','Callaloo chicken','Roseau, Dominica','Chicken stewed in a purée of dasheen leaves with coconut milk, thick and dark green. The dasheen leaf scratches the throat if undercooked: forty minutes is a minimum, not a suggestion.',ARRAY['Wash the chicken with lime as Caribbean custom requires, then marinate it in garlic, thyme and spring onion.','Strip the ribs from the dasheen leaves and chop them roughly: the rib stays tough and irritant.','Colour the chicken in coconut oil, then add the onion and garlic.','Add the leaves, okra and half a litre of water, cover and cook for 40 minutes.','Pour in the coconut milk, break down the leaves with a whisk to thicken, and give it 15 minutes without boiling. Serve with rice.']::text[]),
('pepperpot-guyana','en','Pepperpot','Georgetown, Guyana','A black stew with cassareep, the bitter cassava syrup that preserves meat without refrigeration. Some family pepperpots have been reheated for generations: cassareep is a natural antiseptic, and the dish does not spoil.',ARRAY['Blanch all the meats for 15 minutes and discard that water: pepperpot must stay clean despite its colour.','Return them to the pot with the cassareep, cinnamon, cloves and orange peel.','Add water only to the level of the meat: cassareep must not be diluted, it is the sauce.','Simmer for 3 to 4 hours over very low heat, until the meat falls away and the sauce coats.','Add the whole wiri wiri peppers at the end. Serve with plait bread; reheat every day.']::text[]),
('bouillon-d-awara','en','Awara broth','Cayenne, French Guiana','A stew of awara palm paste, smoked meats and fish, made at Easter. Guianese belief holds that whoever eats it will return to Guiana: it takes two days and gathers the whole family.',ARRAY['Slake the awara paste in two litres of water and reduce it for 4 hours uncovered, stirring regularly.','It should go from bright orange to dark brown and halve in volume: that long cooking is everything.','Soak and blanch the smoked meats, and cook them separately for 40 minutes.','Add them to the paste with the smoked fish and the vegetables, in order of firmness.','Simmer for another hour, add the prawns at the end, and serve with white rice.']::text[]),
('pato-no-tucupi','en','Pato no tucupi','Belém, Pará, Brazil','Duck poached in tucupi with jambu, served at the Círio de Nazaré. The duck is first roasted then boiled: that double treatment, contrary to all European culinary logic, is what makes the dish.',ARRAY['Rub the duck with pounded garlic, lime and salt, and leave it 12 hours in the cold.','Roast it for 1 hour at 200°C, until the skin is well coloured and the fat has rendered.','Boil the tucupi for 2 hours with the garlic, chicory and basil, uncovered.','Joint the roast duck and lower the pieces into the boiling tucupi for 40 minutes.','Blanch the jambu and add it at the end. Serve with rice and farofa.']::text[]),
('guatita','en','Guatita','Guayaquil, Ecuador','Tripe stewed in a peanut and potato sauce, yellow with annatto. The name means ''little belly''; it is Guayaquil''s street dish, served at all hours and never on a smart restaurant menu.',ARRAY['Scrub the tripe with coarse salt and lemon, rinse it at length, then blanch it for 15 minutes.','Cook it for 2 hours at a bare simmer until a fork slides in easily, and cut it into strips.','Make a refrito of onion, garlic, tomato, cumin and annatto for 20 minutes.','Slake the peanut butter in warm milk and stir it into the refrito, with the diced potatoes.','Add the tripe and simmer for 30 minutes: the potatoes collapse and thicken the sauce. Coriander at the end.']::text[]),
('chairo-paceno','en','Chairo paceño','La Paz, Bolivia','A soup of chuño, potato freeze-dried by Andean nights, with lamb and wheat. Chuño is made by letting tubers freeze overnight and treading them by day: the technique is a thousand years old.',ARRAY['Soak the chuño overnight, then rub it between your hands to remove the skins and rinse it in several changes of water.','Soak the wheat overnight too, and cook it separately for 1 hour: it does not soften at the same rate.','Cook the meats for 1 hour in three litres of water with the onion and garlic.','Make a refrito of ají amarillo, cumin and oregano, and stir it into the broth.','Add the roughly crushed chuño, the wheat and the vegetables, and give them 45 minutes. Huacatay off the heat.']::text[]),
('silpancho','en','Silpancho','Cochabamba, Bolivia','A breaded steak flattened until it covers the whole plate, over rice and potatoes. Cochabamba judges a silpancho by its size: if it does not overhang the plate, it was not beaten enough.',ARRAY['Beat the beef slices between two sheets into a 3 mm disc, 25 cm across.','Season, and press them firmly into the breadcrumbs on both sides, without egg wash: that is the local peculiarity.','Cook the rice, and fry the sliced potatoes until golden.','Fry the meat for 90 seconds a side in very hot oil: any longer and it dries out.','Plate rice, potatoes and meat, then two fried eggs, and crown with a raw salad of tomato, onion and locoto.']::text[]),
('mbeju','en','Mbejú','Ciudad del Este, Paraguay','A cake of cassava starch and cheese cooked in a pan, without flour or leavening. It is not kneaded: the mixture stays as dry crumbs and welds together in the heat, which disconcerts the first time.',ARRAY['Cream the lard with the salt, then work in the grated cheese and the eggs.','Add the cassava starch and rub it in with your fingertips: the mixture must stay crumbly, never form a ball.','Add the milk a spoonful at a time, just enough that a crumb holds when squeezed in the hand.','Tip a 1 cm layer into a hot pan and press it only lightly with the back of a spoon.','Let it weld for 8 minutes over medium heat, flip it in one movement and cook 6 minutes. Serve scalding.']::text[]),
('boston-baked-beans','en','Boston baked beans','Boston, Massachusetts, United States','White beans baked seven hours in a stoneware pot with molasses and salt pork. Boston was nicknamed Beantown because of them: the city refined Caribbean sugar, and molasses, a worthless by-product, cost less than sugar.',ARRAY['Soak the beans overnight, then simmer them 40 minutes until the skins split when you blow on them.','Never salt the soaking or parboiling water: salt hardens the skin and seven hours of oven will not undo it.','Score the salt pork in a lattice without cutting through the rind, and bury it in the centre of the pot with the whole onion.','Mix the molasses, brown sugar and dry mustard into a litre of hot water, and pour to cover the beans by a centimetre.','Bake covered 6 hours at 130°C, topping up with hot water if the level drops, then uncover for the last hour to let the top crust.']::text[]),
('blueberry-pie-du-maine','en','Maine blueberry pie','Machias, Maine, United States','A wild blueberry pie, the state''s official dessert since 2011. Maine grows nearly all of North America''s wild blueberries: half the size of cultivated ones, twice as sharp, and gathered only with a hand rake.',ARRAY['Mix the blueberries with the sugar, cornstarch, zest and lemon juice, and macerate 30 minutes so they give up their juice.','Drain that juice, reduce it by half in a pan and pour it back over the fruit: without this the pie floods its base.','Line the tin, fill it in a dome and dot with butter.','Cover with a second disc, seal, glaze and cut five wide vents — the steam must escape fast, one chimney is not enough.','Bake 15 minutes at 220°C then 40 minutes at 190°C, until juice bubbles through the vents. Cool 4 hours before cutting.']::text[]),
('whoopie-pie','en','Whoopie pie','Lewiston, Maine, United States','Two cocoa cake domes enclosing a marshmallow cream, neither pie nor cake. Maine, the Pennsylvania Amish and Massachusetts all claim the invention; the name is said to come from the cry of farmers finding one in their lunch pail.',ARRAY['Cream 60 g of the butter with the brown sugar, add the egg, then alternate the cocoa flour and buttermilk in three additions.','The sour buttermilk activates the bicarbonate: the batter rises at once and must be piped without delay.','Drop generous spoonfuls onto the tray, well spaced — they spread by half as they bake.','Bake 11 minutes at 180°C: they must stay soft and matt, an overbaked whoopie crisps and is no longer one.','Whisk the remaining butter with the icing sugar, marshmallow and vanilla, fill generously and press without squashing.']::text[]),
('louis-lunch-hamburger','en','Louis’ Lunch hamburger','New Haven, Connecticut, United States','A beef patty cooked upright in a vertical cast-iron broiler, served between two slices of toasted white bread. Louis Lassen improvised it in 1900 for a customer in a hurry; the shop still uses the same 1898 broilers, and flatly refuses ketchup.',ARRAY['Choose mince at twenty per cent fat and do not work it: shape the patties with your fingertips, without packing.','Compressed meat turns dense as it cooks; it is the one gesture that separates a good burger from a bad one.','Salt and pepper the surface only, just before cooking: salted ahead, mince weeps and welds together.','Cook upright in a vertical broiler, or failing that on a scalding cast-iron pan, 4 minutes a side, never pressing with a spatula.','Toast the bread, set the patty on it, a slice of cheese melted by the meat''s heat, onion and tomato. Nothing else — certainly not ketchup.']::text[]),
('toll-house-cookies','en','Toll House cookies','Whitman, Massachusetts, United States','The first chocolate chip cookies, invented in 1938 by Ruth Wakefield at the Toll House Inn. Out of nuts, she chopped up a Nestlé bar expecting it to melt: it did not, and she sold the recipe to Nestlé for a dollar and a lifetime supply of chocolate.',ARRAY['Cream the softened butter with both sugars for 5 minutes, until it pales: that aeration gives the crisp edge.','Add the eggs one at a time then the vanilla, and finally the flour and bicarbonate, mixing as little as possible.','Chop the chocolate with a knife rather than using chips: irregular shards make seams, chips stay beads.','Chill the dough 24 hours: the flour hydrates, the sugar caramelises better, and the cookie keeps its thickness in the oven.','Bake 11 minutes at 190°C. Take them out while the centre still looks raw — they finish setting on the hot tray.']::text[]),
('fluffernutter','en','Fluffernutter','Somerville, Massachusetts, United States','A peanut butter and marshmallow cream sandwich, on white bread. Marshmallow Fluff was invented in Somerville in 1917; a Massachusetts senator proposed making it the official state sandwich, which the legislature is still debating.',ARRAY['Take the peanut butter out half an hour ahead: cold, it tears the crumb instead of spreading.','Spread it on one slice in a thick, even layer, right to the edges.','Spread the marshmallow cream on the other slice, never on the same one: layered together they merge and the contrast of textures is lost.','Close the sandwich without pressing — marshmallow escapes under the least pressure.','Cut on the diagonal with a knife run under hot water and wiped. Eaten within the hour, before the bread goes damp.']::text[]),
('pancakes-au-sirop-derable','en','Buttermilk pancakes with maple syrup','Stowe, Vermont, United States','Thick buttermilk pancakes drowned in Vermont maple syrup. The Abenaki were tapping maples before the settlers arrived; forty litres of sap make one of syrup, which explains the price and rules out flavoured imitations.',ARRAY['Mix the dry ingredients on one side, the wet on the other, then combine in ten turns only.','The batter must stay lumpy: a smooth batter has developed its gluten and gives flat, rubbery pancakes.','Rest it 15 minutes, while the bicarbonate reacts with the buttermilk and bubbles rise to the surface.','Heat a pan over medium heat and grease it with a buttered cloth. Pour a ladleful and wait for the bubbles to burst and the edges to dry before turning — once only.','Warm the maple syrup before serving: cold, it sets the butter and slides off instead of soaking in.']::text[]),
('apple-pie-nouvelle-angleterre','en','New England apple pie','Burlington, Vermont, United States','A double-crust apple pie, served warm with a slice of sharp Vermont cheddar. The pairing startles outside New England; a local saying has it that apple pie without cheese is like a kiss without a squeeze.',ARRAY['Use two varieties: one that holds, one that collapses. The first gives the pieces, the second the purée that binds them.','Cut the apples into thick wedges, squeeze lemon over and mix with the sugar, flour and spices.','Let them drain 45 minutes in a colander and reduce the collected juice to a syrup before pouring it back: that is what stops the gap forming under the crust.','Fill in a high dome, dot with butter, cover, seal by pinching and cut four vents.','Bake 20 minutes at 220°C then 40 minutes at 190°C. Serve warm, with aged cheddar cut in thick slices.']::text[]),
('bagel-lox-schmear','en','Bagel with lox and schmear','Lower East Side, New York, United States','A split bagel spread with cream cheese and covered with cured salmon, red onion and capers. The bagel came from Poland with Ashkenazi Jews around 1880; lox is not smoked but brined, and the word comes from Yiddish laks, salmon.',ARRAY['Choose a bagel baked that day, with a glossy crust: a bagel boiled before baking shines, one merely baked does not.','Split it and toast it only if it is a day old — toasting a fresh bagel destroys its characteristic chew.','Take the cream cheese out twenty minutes ahead and work it with a spatula until smooth.','Spread it thickly, almost as thick as the crumb: the schmear is not a thin layer, it is one of the two main ingredients.','Lay the salmon in loose folds, add very thin onion rings, capers, dill and pepper. Close and eat at once.']::text[]),
('dirty-water-hot-dog','en','New York cart hot dog','New York, United States','A beef frankfurter kept hot in the simmering water of a cart, served with sauerkraut and onion sauce. New Yorkers call it a dirty water dog: the water in the tank is never changed all day, and that is reckoned to make the flavour.',ARRAY['Make the onion sauce: sliced onions melted for 25 minutes, then tomato purée, vinegar, sugar and a pinch of cinnamon, let down with water.','Reduce it 20 minutes until it coats: it is what separates a New York hot dog from every other.','Heat water to 85°C and hold the frankfurters in it at least 15 minutes, never boiling — a boil splits the skin.','Warm the buns in the steam above the tank, never in a toaster: the bread must be soft and damp.','Dress in this order: yellow mustard, frankfurter, pressed sauerkraut, onion sauce. Eaten standing, within two minutes.']::text[]),
('black-and-white-cookie','en','Black and white cookie','Yorkville, New York, United States','A large soft cookie iced half vanilla, half chocolate, on its flat side. Born around 1902 in a German bakery in Yorkville, it became a New York emblem of coexistence — and is always eaten starting from the dark side.',ARRAY['Cream the butter and sugar, add the eggs then alternate flour and milk: this is a cake batter, not a biscuit dough.','Drop large spoonfuls well apart and smooth them into 10 cm discs with the back of a wet spoon.','Bake 14 minutes at 190°C until the edges barely colour, and cool completely upside down, flat side up.','It is the flat side that is iced, never the domed one: that is the New York rule and it admits no exception.','Make a thick icing of icing sugar, hot water and lemon; ice one half white, add cocoa to the rest and ice the other, drawing the border with a straight edge.']::text[]),
('pork-roll-taylor-ham','en','Pork roll, egg and cheese','Trenton, New Jersey, United States','Slices of cured pork griddled with an egg and cheese in an English muffin. New Jersey is split over the name: Taylor ham in the north, pork roll in the south, with the border crossing the state at Trenton — the argument has never been settled.',ARRAY['Cut the pork roll into 6 mm slices and make four cuts in each from the edge toward the centre.','Those cuts are not decorative: without them the slice domes as it cooks and touches the griddle only at the edges.','Griddle them 3 minutes a side on a dry plate, until the edges brown and crisp.','Cook the eggs on the griddle in the rendered fat, yolk firm or runny to taste, and lay the cheese on so it melts in the heat.','Toast the muffin, stack pork roll, egg and cheese, and close. Ketchup, salt, pepper — the only combination New Jersey allows.']::text[]),
('biscuits-and-gravy','en','Biscuits and gravy','Louisville, Kentucky, United States','Savoury buttermilk scones drowned in a white sausage gravy. It has been the Appalachian breakfast since the early 19th century: two cheap ingredients, flour and milk, kept a man going until evening in the mines.',ARRAY['Freeze the butter for half an hour and grate it into the flour: the frozen shreds melt in the oven and create the leaves that lift the biscuit.','Pour in the cold buttermilk and bring the dough together in a few movements, without kneading — a worked biscuit turns to stone.','Fold the dough in three twice, like puff pastry, then cut with a cutter pressed straight down in one clean movement.','A cutter that is twisted seals the layers and the biscuit rises lopsided. Bake 14 minutes at 220°C with the biscuits touching.','Fry the crumbled sausage meat, dust with two spoonfuls of flour, moisten with hot milk and pepper very generously: pepper is the gravy''s third ingredient, not a seasoning.']::text[]),
('pulled-pork-caroline-du-nord','en','North Carolina pulled pork','Lexington, North Carolina, United States','A pork shoulder smoked for ten hours then pulled, dressed with a cider vinegar sauce. North Carolina divides into two schools: the East smokes the whole hog with pure vinegar sauce, the West takes only the shoulder and tolerates a touch of tomato.',ARRAY['Rub the shoulder with a mix of salt, pepper, paprika and brown sugar, and leave it twelve hours in the cold, uncovered.','Uncovered drying forms a tacky film on the surface: that film holds the smoke, and wet meat takes none.','Smoke at 110°C over hickory or oak for ten hours, to 95°C at the centre — temperature, not the clock, decides.','Around 70°C the temperature stops rising for two hours: that is the stall, collagen melting. Do not raise the heat, wait.','Rest it an hour wrapped, then pull it by hand with two forks. Dress with the vinegar sauce and serve in a bun with coleslaw.']::text[]),
('southern-mac-and-cheese','en','Southern mac and cheese','Atlanta, Georgia, United States','Macaroni bound with egg and evaporated milk, baked until it can be cut in squares. Thomas Jefferson brought the recipe back from France in 1790, but it was James Hemings, his enslaved cook trained in Paris, who worked it out in America.',ARRAY['Cook the macaroni two minutes less than the packet says: it finishes in the oven and drinks the custard.','Grate the cheese yourself: pre-grated cheese is coated in anti-caking starch, which makes the bake grainy.','Beat the eggs with the evaporated milk, mustard and cayenne — this is a custard, not a béchamel, and it is what lets you cut it.','Layer pasta and cheese in three tiers in a buttered dish, keeping a third of the cheddar for the top.','Pour over the custard, rest ten minutes so it seeps through, and bake 45 minutes at 180°C. Cool 20 minutes before cutting.']::text[]),
('fried-green-tomatoes','en','Fried green tomatoes','Savannah, Georgia, United States','Slices of unripe tomato crumbed in cornmeal and fried, served with a remoulade. The recipe appears in northern books as early as 1877; the South adopted it during the Depression, when waiting for a tomato to ripen was a luxury.',ARRAY['Choose frankly green, hard tomatoes, never blushing: one that has begun to ripen collapses in the fryer.','Cut them into 8 mm rounds, salt them and drain them 20 minutes on paper, then blot them.','Flour, dip in buttermilk beaten with egg, then cornmeal: cornmeal alone, without flour beneath, will not cling to a wet tomato.','Fry 3 minutes a side in two centimetres of oil at 180°C, without crowding the pan.','Drain upright and salt at once. Serve with a remoulade of mayonnaise, chopped gherkin, paprika and cayenne.']::text[]),
('pimento-cheese','en','Pimento cheese','Athens, Georgia, United States','A spread of orange cheddar, sweet peppers and mayonnaise, called the caviar of the South. It was born in New York around 1910, from newly made cream cheese meeting tinned Spanish sweet peppers, but the South has claimed it beyond recall.',ARRAY['Grate the cheddar on the coarse holes, not in a processor: the shreds must stay distinct, a cheese purée is no longer pimento cheese.','Choose a well-aged orange cheddar: the annatto colouring changes nothing in the taste but gives the expected colour.','Drain the sweet peppers and chop them by knife, keeping visible pieces.','Mix with a fork with the mayonnaise, cream cheese, grated onion, cayenne and plenty of pepper.','Rest it overnight in the fridge: the cheese absorbs the mayonnaise and the spread firms up. Take it out an hour before serving.']::text[]),
('skillet-cornbread','en','Skillet cornbread','Savannah, Georgia, United States','A cornbread baked in a scalding cast-iron skillet, with a dark, crackling crust. The South puts no sugar in it and says so firmly: a sweetened cornbread is cake, and a sure sign of a northern recipe.',ARRAY['Put the cast-iron skillet with the lard in a 230°C oven twenty minutes ahead: it must be smoking when the batter arrives.','It is the shock of cold batter on scalding iron that forms the crust: in a warm pan you get a pale, soft cake.','Mix the cornmeal, flour, bicarbonate, baking powder and salt, then the buttermilk and eggs, in twenty turns only.','Take out the skillet, swirl the smoking fat up the sides, and pour the batter in at once: it must sizzle immediately.','Bake 22 minutes. Turn out onto a board and serve warm with butter — or crumbled into a glass of buttermilk, as in the South.']::text[]),
('etouffee-aux-ecrevisses','en','Crawfish étouffée','Breaux Bridge, Louisiana, United States','Crawfish tails smothered in a blond roux and served over rice. Étouffer, in Acadian French, means to cook covered in little liquid; Breaux Bridge has proclaimed itself the crawfish capital of the world since 1959 and holds a festival for it.',ARRAY['Make a butter and flour roux and cook it to the colour of peanut butter, stirring without pause for 10 minutes.','An étouffée roux is blond, not brown: the dark roux belongs to gumbo, and confusing the two changes the dish entirely.','Throw in the trinity — onion, celery, green pepper — all at once into the scalding roux: the shock stops the roux cooking dead.','Add the garlic, broth and cayenne, and let it thicken 15 minutes uncovered.','Fold in the crawfish tails and cover: they cook in 5 minutes and turn rubbery beyond eight. Add parsley and serve over rice.']::text[]),
('muffuletta','en','Muffuletta','New Orleans, Louisiana, United States','A round sesame loaf filled with cured meats, provolone and a marinated olive salad. Salvatore Lupo invented it in 1906 for the Sicilian dockers of the French Market; it is eaten cold and is better for being pressed for hours before cutting.',ARRAY['Make the olive salad the day before: green and black olives chopped, giardiniera, garlic, capers, oregano, drowned in olive oil.','It must macerate at least twenty-four hours — it is the dish, the cured meats are only a support.','Split the round loaf horizontally and hollow the crumb of the lid slightly to make room.','Moisten both faces with oil from the salad, then build: olives, provolone, meats, provolone, olives.','Close, wrap tightly and press under a weight for at least 3 hours. Cut in quarters at the moment of serving, never before.']::text[]),
('boudin-cajun','en','Cajun boudin','Lafayette, Louisiana, United States','A sausage of pork, liver and cooked rice, inherited from French boudin blanc by the deported Acadians. It is bought at the butcher''s and eaten standing in the car park, squeezing the casing between the teeth to take only the filling — the casing is thrown away.',ARRAY['Poach the pork, liver and trinity 1 hour in salted water, and keep the broth carefully.','Mince the meat and liver on the medium plate, keeping some grain: a Cajun boudin is not a mousse.','Mix with the rice cooked in that broth, the spring onion, parsley, cayenne and plenty of black pepper.','The proportion is roughly half rice, half meat: more rice and the boudin turns pasty, less and it falls apart.','Fill the casings loosely and poach 20 minutes at 75°C, never boiling — beyond that the casing splits and all the rice escapes.']::text[]),
('king-cake','en','King cake','New Orleans, Louisiana, United States','A cinnamon brioche crown iced in the three Mardi Gras colours — purple, gold and green. The French bean has become a plastic baby hidden underneath: whoever finds it must provide the next king cake, which keeps the custom going until Lent.',ARRAY['Knead a brioche dough with the flour, warm milk, yeast, eggs, sugar and salt, then work in the softened butter.','Prove 1½ hours, roll into a large rectangle and cover with butter, brown sugar, cinnamon and beaten cream cheese.','Roll it up tightly lengthwise, then join the two ends into a crown, pinching the join firmly.','Cut the top of the crown on the bias every five centimetres, to let the spiral of filling show.','Prove for an hour and bake 30 minutes at 180°C. Ice with sugar and lemon, and sprinkle in three bands of purple, gold and green.']::text[]),
('chili-con-carne-texas','en','Texas chili con carne','San Antonio, Texas, United States','A stew of beef cubes in a purée of dried chillies, without a single bean. The Chili Queens sold it on San Antonio''s Plaza from 1880; in Texas, putting beans in a chili is an offence no competition admits.',ARRAY['Seed the dried chillies, toast them 30 seconds a side in a dry pan, then soak them 30 minutes in hot water.','That brief toasting wakes the oils; burnt a second too long, they make the whole chili bitter and nothing rescues it.','Blend them with the soaking water into a smooth purée and pass it through a sieve to hold back the skins.','Cut the beef into 2 cm cubes — never minced — and sear them in lard in small batches.','Add the chilli purée, garlic, cumin, oregano and broth, and simmer 2½ hours. Thicken with slaked masa harina in the last twenty minutes.']::text[]),
('breakfast-tacos','en','Breakfast tacos','Austin, Texas, United States','Flour tortillas filled with scrambled egg, chorizo and potato, eaten at the wheel. Austin and San Antonio dispute the invention with equal bad faith; the one point of agreement is that the tortilla must be flour and made that morning.',ARRAY['Dice the potatoes small and fry them 12 minutes, until golden and tender.','Fry the crumbled Mexican chorizo separately and keep its red fat: it is what will colour the eggs.','Beat the eggs with no milk or cream and cook them in that fat over low heat, stirring without pause — they must stay creamy and underdone.','Warm the tortillas thirty seconds a side on a dry griddle: microwaved, they turn rubbery.','Fill, add cheese to melt in the heat, the tomato and jalapeño salsa, coriander and a squeeze of lime. Fold and eat at once.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('kolaches-texan','en','West kolaches','West, Texas, United States','A soft brioche wrapped around a smoked sausage, sold on the highway between Dallas and Austin. Moravian Czechs settled in West in 1880; a true koláč is sweet and open, and the savoury sausage version is properly called a klobasnek.',ARRAY['Make a starter with the yeast, warm milk and a little flour, and wait for it to double before going further.','Knead with the rest, then work in the softened butter: Czech dough is richer than bread dough and lighter than brioche.','Prove 1½ hours, then flatten 60 g pieces into rectangles.','Blot the sausages carefully: a wet sausage makes the dough lift as it bakes and the klobasnek opens.','Roll the sausage up with a strip of cheese and jalapeño, seam underneath, prove 40 minutes and bake 18 minutes at 190°C.']::text[]),
('chicken-fried-steak','en','Chicken fried steak','Lamesa, Texas, United States','A tenderised steak, crumbed and fried like chicken, drowned in a white pepper gravy. It is the Viennese schnitzel filtered through Texas''s German and Czech immigrants; Lamesa claims the invention in 1911 and holds an annual festival for it.',ARRAY['Tenderise the steaks with a toothed mallet to 8 mm: the connective tissue must be broken, or the meat shrinks in the oil.','Flour them, dip in egg beaten with milk, then in flour mixed with baking powder and paprika.','The baking powder in the coating is the Texan trick: it puffs the crust and gives it its blistered look.','Rest the crumbed steaks 15 minutes on a rack: the coating hydrates and grips, instead of peeling off in sheets.','Fry 4 minutes a side at 175°C, set aside, and make the gravy in three spoonfuls of the frying fat with flour, hot milk and plenty of black pepper.']::text[]),
('chile-con-queso','en','Chile con queso','Austin, Texas, United States','A melted cheese dip with chillies and tomato, served scalding with corn chips. Born in San Antonio in the 1920s, it owes its fluidity to the emulsifying salts of processed cheese: a farmhouse cheddar would split into oil and strings.',ARRAY['Roast the green chillies until the skin blackens, shut them in a bag for 15 minutes, peel and chop them.','Fry the onion and crumbled chorizo, then add the crushed tomato and reduce for 10 minutes.','Dice the cheese and melt it over a bain-marie with the milk, never over direct heat: above 70°C it splits.','Fold the garnish into the melted cheese off the heat, and add the cumin and coriander.','Serve at once in a warmed dish: queso sets within five minutes, and reheating it a second time turns it grainy.']::text[]),
('kansas-city-ribs','en','Kansas City ribs','Kansas City, Missouri, United States','Pork ribs smoked five hours and lacquered with a thick molasses sauce. Henry Perry, born in Tennessee, opened his first stand in 1908 and founded the Kansas City school; the city now counts over a hundred barbecue houses.',ARRAY['Remove the white membrane from the back of the rack by lifting it with a knife then pulling with a cloth: it never melts and blocks the smoke.','Rub with a mix of brown sugar, paprika, salt and pepper, and leave twelve hours in the cold.','Smoke at 110°C over applewood, three hours uncovered, spraying apple juice every hour.','Wrap in foil with a little juice and continue two hours: that stage tenderises without drying.','Unwrap, brush with sauce and return for thirty minutes to lacquer it. The meat should give under the tooth, but not fall off the bone.']::text[]),
('cincinnati-chili','en','Cincinnati chili','Cincinnati, Ohio, United States','A beef sauce with cinnamon and cocoa served over spaghetti, under a mountain of grated cheddar. The Kiradjieff brothers, Macedonian immigrants, created it in 1922 by adapting a Balkan stew; you order it three, four or five ways depending on the toppings.',ARRAY['Never brown the meat: whisk the raw minced beef into cold water with a fork, until not a lump remains.','That is what sets Cincinnati chili apart from every other: boiled meat stays in fine grains and the sauce is not greasy.','Bring to the boil, add the grated onion, tomato purée and every spice, and simmer 2½ hours uncovered.','Rest it overnight in the cold and lift off the set fat: it is the only way to reach the expected texture.','Serve over spaghetti, with grated cheddar piled high, kidney beans and raw onion according to how many ways you want it.']::text[]),
('toasted-ravioli','en','Toasted ravioli','The Hill, St. Louis, Missouri, United States','Breaded, fried ravioli dipped in tomato sauce, the speciality of St. Louis''s Italian quarter. Legend has a cook dropping ravioli into the fryer instead of the pot around 1940; they are called toasted although they are fried, which nobody explains.',ARRAY['Use fresh uncooked ravioli: pre-poached ones tear during crumbing and let the filling escape.','Beat the eggs with the milk and pass the ravioli first through that, then through breadcrumbs mixed with parmesan.','Let them dry 15 minutes on a rack before frying: the coating grips and does not peel off in the oil.','Fry 2 minutes a side at 180°C, six at a time, so the temperature does not fall.','Drain, salt and scatter parmesan while they are scalding. Serve with hot tomato sauce, for dipping.']::text[]),
('corn-dog','en','Corn dog','Springfield, Illinois, United States','A sausage on a stick, dipped in cornbread batter and fried. Patented in 1929 in Springfield as the crusty cur, it found its public at Midwest state fairs; it is the stick, more than the batter, that made it the first American meal eaten walking.',ARRAY['Blot the sausages and roll them in a film of dry flour: without it the batter slides off and bares the sausage in the oil.','Skewer them on wooden sticks, pushing them in only two-thirds of the way.','Mix the batter and pour it into a tall narrow glass: the only way to dip a corn dog in one vertical movement.','The batter should be as thick as waffle batter — too thin it runs, too thick it stays raw in the centre.','Dip, drain for two seconds and lower into oil at 180°C, holding the stick for ten seconds before letting go, so the crust sets.']::text[]),
('brownies-palmer-house','en','Palmer House brownies','Chicago, Illinois, United States','The first known brownie, created in 1893 at the Palmer House hotel for the World''s Fair. Bertha Palmer wanted a dessert ladies could carry in a lunch box: smaller than a slice of cake, sturdier than a tart, apricot-glazed to keep.',ARRAY['Melt the chocolate and butter over a bain-marie, never above 45°C, and let it cool before the eggs go in.','Whisk the eggs and sugar separately for 5 minutes to the ribbon: that foam gives the thin shiny crust on top.','Fold in the warm chocolate, then the flour and salt in ten turns — overmixed, the batter gives a cake and not a brownie.','Pour into a lined tin and cover with whole walnut halves, generously, as at the Palmer House.','Bake 35 minutes at 175°C: the centre should still tremble. Coat with apricot glaze let down with hot water as it leaves the oven.']::text[]),
('navajo-taco','en','Navajo taco','Window Rock, Arizona, United States','A round of fried bread topped with beans, meat and raw vegetables. Fry bread was born in 1864 during the Long Walk, when the army issued deported Navajos flour and lard in place of their crops: a dish born of a deportation, now both emblem and subject of argument.',ARRAY['Mix the flour, baking powder and salt, pour in the warm water and bring the dough together without kneading: fry bread needs no developed gluten.','Rest it 30 minutes under a cloth — it is the rest, not the working, that makes it supple to stretch.','Stretch each ball by hand to 20 cm and pierce a hole at the centre with a fingertip: without it the round balloons and cooks unevenly.','Fry in two centimetres of lard at 190°C, 90 seconds a side, until it blisters and browns.','Drain and top it scalding with crushed pinto beans, spiced meat, lettuce, tomato, cheese and sour cream. Eaten by hand, folded in two.']::text[]),
('chimichanga','en','Chimichanga','Tucson, Arizona, United States','A burrito fried until crisp, coated with sauce and melted cheese. Monica Flin of El Charro in Tucson is said to have dropped a burrito in the fryer in 1922 and swallowed a curse in front of her nephews: chimichanga is the word she invented instead.',ARRAY['Braise the chuck 3 hours with the onion, cumin and green chillies, then shred it and let it cool.','A warm filling softens the tortilla and the chimichanga opens in the oil: the commonest failure.','Warm the tortillas to make them pliable, fill the lower third, fold the sides in then roll very tightly.','Fix the flap with a wooden pick or a smear of bean paste, which glues as it cooks.','Fry 3 minutes at 190°C, spooning oil over the top, then coat with sauce and cheese and pass under the grill. Remove the pick before serving.']::text[]),
('carne-adovada','en','Carne adovada','Albuquerque, New Mexico, United States','Pork marinated overnight in a purée of New Mexico red chillies, then braised until it shreds. Adovada comes from adobado, marinated: the chilli marinade was first a preservation, before refrigeration, and the dish is traditionally served at Christmas.',ARRAY['Remove the stems and seeds from the dried red chillies, then soak them 30 minutes in boiling water.','Do not toast them as you would for a Texas chili: New Mexico chilli is sweet and fruity, and toasting turns it bitter.','Blend them with the soaking water, garlic, oregano, cumin, vinegar and honey, and pass through a sieve.','Cut the pork into 4 cm cubes and marinate it overnight in that purée, in the cold.','Braise covered 3 hours at 160°C, adding nothing but broth if the sauce tightens too far. Serve with flour tortillas.']::text[]),
('salsa-verde-du-desert','en','Desert salsa verde','Santa Fe, New Mexico, United States','A green sauce of roasted tomatillos, jalapeños and coriander. The tomatillo is not a green tomato but a husked fruit, grown by the Aztecs long before it; its acidity, not the lime''s, is what gives the sauce its bite.',ARRAY['Remove the papery husks from the tomatillos and wash them in hot water: they are covered in a sticky, bitter film.','Roast them whole on a dry scalding griddle with the onion, unpeeled garlic and jalapeños, turning them.','They are ready when the skin blackens in patches and the flesh slumps — allow 12 minutes, no more.','Blend barely, in pulses: a salsa verde blended too long turns foamy and pale instead of staying dark green.','Add the coriander, lime juice and salt outside the blender, with a fork. Rest an hour before serving.']::text[]),
('california-roll','en','California roll','Little Tokyo, Los Angeles, California, United States','An inside-out maki, rice outside, filled with crab, avocado and cucumber. Ichiro Mashita invented it in the sixties at the Tokyo Kaikan for Americans put off by raw fish and by visible seaweed: rice on the outside hid the nori.',ARRAY['Wash the rice seven times until the water runs clear, cook it and season it with sweetened vinegar, cutting it in with a spatula and never stirring.','Spread the cold rice over the whole nori sheet, then turn the lot over: the nori ends up underneath and the rice against the mat.','Cover the mat with cling film before turning, otherwise the rice sticks to it and the roll tears.','Line up the crab, avocado and cucumber along the lower third, and roll pressing evenly, without crushing.','Roll the log in sesame seeds and cut into eight with a wetted knife, wiping the blade between each slice.']::text[]),
('avocado-toast','en','Avocado toast','Venice, Los Angeles, California, United States','A slice of sourdough covered with crushed avocado, lemon and chilli flakes. Popularised in Sydney in the 1990s, it exploded in Venice around 2010; California grows nearly all American avocados, and crushing rather than blending is the one rule that matters.',ARRAY['Choose a Hass avocado that yields under the thumb near the stem but stays firm in the middle: overripe, it turns stringy and grey.','Toast the sourdough until frankly brown: pale bread goes soggy under avocado within two minutes.','Crush the avocado with a fork in a bowl, with lemon juice and coarse salt, leaving pieces — never in a blender.','The lemon is not there for flavour but against oxidation: without it the avocado browns before it reaches the table.','Spread on the still-warm bread, trickle olive oil, pepper, add the chilli flakes and, if you like, a poached egg.']::text[]),
('sourdough-san-francisco','en','San Francisco sourdough','Fisherman’s Wharf, San Francisco, California, United States','A wild-leavened bread with a thick crust and a frankly sour crumb. The starter arrived with the gold rushers of 1849; a bacterium peculiar to the bay, named Lactobacillus sanfranciscensis, gives it its acidity, and the same starters have run since the 1906 earthquake.',ARRAY['Refresh the starter twelve hours ahead: it must triple and just begin to fall, the sign it is at peak activity.','Mix the flours and water alone and leave them an hour to autolyse — hydration before salt makes the dough extensible without kneading.','Add the starter then the salt, and give it six sets of folds thirty minutes apart instead of kneading.','Ferment 4 hours at 24°C, shape into a tight round and chill overnight: it is the cold that develops the acidity.','Bake in a pot preheated to 250°C, 20 minutes covered then 25 uncovered at 230°C. Wait two hours before cutting.']::text[]),
('in-n-out-double-double','en','Double-Double','Baldwin Park, California, United States','Two thin patties and two slices of cheese in a soft bun, with a pink sauce. Harry and Esther Snyder opened California''s first drive-through in Baldwin Park in 1948; animal style, with caramelised onions and a mustard-cooked patty, has never appeared on a menu in sixty years.',ARRAY['Make the sauce: mayonnaise, ketchup, very finely chopped gherkins and a spoonful of their vinegar, mixed and chilled an hour.','Shape two 60 g patties and press them between films to 12 cm across: they must be wider than the bun, because they shrink by a quarter.','Griddle the cut faces of the bun in butter until brown: that toasted layer is what keeps it from going soggy.','Sear the patties on a scalding griddle, 90 seconds a side, brushing the raw face with mustard before turning them.','Stack: sauce, lettuce, tomato, onion, patty, cheese, patty, cheese. Serve within the minute.']::text[]),
('mission-burrito','en','Mission burrito','Mission District, San Francisco, California, United States','A giant burrito holding rice, beans, meat, cheese, guacamole and sour cream, wrapped in foil. It was born in 1961 in the Mission District: the innovation was the steamed tortilla, supple enough to wrap a whole meal in one piece.',ARRAY['Marinate the steak in lime, garlic and cumin for an hour, then grill it over high heat and rest it before dicing.','Steam the large tortilla for ten seconds, or thirty in a damp cloth in the microwave: it must go soft and elastic.','A dry tortilla cracks when folded, and a burrito that cracks loses everything; it is simple mechanics.','Fill it in a thick band down the centre, rice and beans at the bottom to absorb the juices, meat and wet items above.','Fold both sides inward, then roll, tightening firmly at every turn. Wrap in foil immediately: the foil is what holds it together.']::text[]),
('cobb-salad','en','Cobb salad','Hollywood, Los Angeles, California, United States','A salad composed in parallel stripes of chicken, bacon, egg, avocado and Roquefort. Bob Cobb, who ran the Brown Derby, improvised it one night in 1937 from what was left in the fridge; the next day Sid Grauman ordered one, and it stayed on the menu for thirty years.',ARRAY['Chop all the greens very finely — this is a chopped salad, not a leaf salad, and that is what lets it be eaten with a spoon.','Grill the bacon until it snaps, drain it on paper and chop it once cold, otherwise it squashes.','Boil the eggs 9 minutes, refresh them in iced water and chop them separately.','Lay each element in a parallel stripe over the bed of greens, without mixing: the arrangement in rows is the definition of the dish.','Whisk a red wine vinegar and mustard dressing, and pour it at the table, in front of your guests, before tossing.']::text[]),
('french-dip','en','French dip','Los Angeles, California, United States','A roast beef sandwich in a bun dipped in the roasting juices, served with a bowl of jus on the side. Two Los Angeles houses have disputed the invention since 1908; one says a roll fell into the dripping pan, the other that a customer with sore gums asked for it that way.',ARRAY['Sear the joint on every face, then braise it 2 hours with the onion, garlic and herbs in the broth.','Cool it completely in its juices: the only way to slice it thinly enough, a hot joint shreds under the blade.','Strain the juices, skim them and keep them scalding: they are the dish, and lukewarm jus ruins everything.','Slice the beef with a long knife, almost translucent, and warm it thirty seconds in the jus before filling.','Dip the cut face of the roll in the jus — the top stays dry so the sandwich can be held — fill it and serve a bowl of jus alongside.']::text[]),
('donuts-glazed-la','en','Los Angeles glazed donut','Inglewood, Los Angeles, California, United States','A ring of yeasted dough fried and sugar-glazed, still warm. Los Angeles has more donut shops than any other American city, largely run by Cambodian refugees who arrived after 1975; the glaze must go onto a hot donut, so that it turns transparent.',ARRAY['Knead a soft yeasted dough with warm milk, yeast, eggs, sugar and salt, then work in the softened butter.','Prove for an hour, roll to 1 cm and cut the rings; re-roll the trimmings once only.','Prove 45 minutes on squares of oiled paper, uncovered: an over-proved donut collapses in the oil.','Fry at 175°C, 60 seconds a side, sliding them into the oil off their paper so as not to distort them.','Dip them in a glaze of icing sugar and milk as they come out, still scalding: on a cold donut the glaze stays white and opaque.']::text[]),
('cedar-plank-salmon','en','Cedar plank salmon','Pike Place, Seattle, Washington, United States','A sockeye salmon fillet cooked on a red cedar plank set over coals. The peoples of Puget Sound have cooked this way for a thousand years: the wet plank smoulders without burning and the wood gives a resin no marinade or smoker reproduces.',ARRAY['Soak the cedar plank at least 2 hours in water, weighted with a plate: a dry plank catches fire within three minutes.','Use only untreated red cedar sold for cooking — hardware-store boards are impregnated with chemicals.','Salt the salmon half an hour ahead, blot it, then brush it with mustard and brown sugar.','Set the plank alone on the coals until it crackles and smokes, then turn it over and lay the salmon on the hot face.','Cover and allow 20 minutes without ever turning the fish: it is ready when the flesh parts into flakes under a point. Serve on the plank.']::text[]),
('dungeness-crab','en','Dungeness crab','Seattle, Washington, United States','A whole crab boiled in seawater, served with clarified butter and lemon. It takes its name from a hamlet in Washington State where it was fished from 1848; it became the state crustacean in 2009, and its season runs November to June.',ARRAY['Buy them alive and lively: a crab dead more than an hour develops a bitterness cooking does not remove.','Bring a large pot of water salted to 35 g per litre — the salinity of the sea — to a rolling boil with the bay.','Drop the crabs in head first and allow 12 minutes for eight hundred grams, from the return to the boil.','Chill them at once in iced water: without that shock they keep cooking inside the shell and the meat turns stringy.','Pull off the apron, lift the carapace, remove the gills and crack the claws with the back of a knife. Garlic clarified butter and lemon, nothing else.']::text[]),
('smoked-salmon-chowder','en','Smoked salmon chowder','Seattle, Washington, United States','A creamy soup of alder-smoked salmon, potato and corn. It is the Pacific Northwest version of the New England chowder: salmon replaces the clam, and alder replaces southern hickory, which gives a gentler smoke.',ARRAY['Render the diced bacon, lift it out and sweat the onion and celery in its fat, without colour.','Dust with flour, cook it two minutes then whisk in the hot fish broth.','Add the diced potatoes and cook 15 minutes, until they crush against the side of the pot under a spoon.','Crush a quarter of the potatoes into the soup to bind it: a chowder is thickened with neither starch nor cream alone.','Add the cream, corn and flaked smoked salmon off the heat, and do not boil again — smoked salmon shrinks and dries within a minute.']::text[]),
('marionberry-pie','en','Marionberry pie','Portland, Oregon, United States','A pie of marionberry, a blackberry bred in 1956 in Marion County. It is Oregon''s unofficial berry, and the state grows nearly the world''s entire crop: juicier and sharper than a common blackberry, it needs more thickener than any other.',ARRAY['Mix the berries with the sugar, tapioca and lemon juice, and macerate 30 minutes so the tapioca hydrates.','Tapioca beats cornstarch for this berry: it survives long acidic cooking without breaking down, which cornstarch does not.','Fill the pastry case in a dome, dot with butter and cover with a lattice of strips rather than a solid disc.','The lattice is not decorative: this berry gives up so much juice that it must be allowed to evaporate as it bakes.','Bake 15 minutes at 220°C then 40 minutes at 190°C, until the juice bubbles at the centre and not only at the edges. Cool 4 hours.']::text[]),
('seattle-teriyaki','en','Seattle teriyaki','Seattle, Washington, United States','Grilled, lacquered chicken thighs served on rice with a cabbage salad. Toshihiro Nakae opened Toshi''s in 1976 and created the format: the city now has several hundred teriyaki shops, a Seattle dish more than a Japanese one.',ARRAY['Use boneless thighs with the skin: breast dries out and Seattle teriyaki is not made otherwise.','Reduce the soy, sugar, mirin, garlic and ginger by half: the sauce must coat the back of a spoon, or it slides off the meat.','Marinate the chicken an hour in a third of that sauce, and keep the rest for lacquering.','Grill skin down first over medium heat for ten minutes, without basting: the sugar would burn before the flesh cooked.','Lacquer only in the last three minutes, in two coats. Serve on rice, with cabbage shredded and dressed with rice vinegar.']::text[]),
('spam-musubi','en','Spam musubi','Honolulu, Hawaii, United States','A lacquered slice of Spam on a block of rice, belted with nori. Spam arrived with army rations during the Pacific war and never left: Hawaii eats more of it per head than any other state, and Barbara Funamura settled this format around 1980.',ARRAY['Cook the sushi rice and let it cool without vinegar: in Hawaii the musubi rice is plain, only the Spam is seasoned.','Cut the Spam into eight one-centimetre slices and griddle them dry until the edges brown and crisp.','Deglaze the pan with soy, sugar and mirin, and let the syrup coat each slice 2 minutes a side.','Use the empty Spam tin as a mould: press the rice into the bottom, set the slice on top, and push it out in one movement.','Belt it with a strip of nori, damping the end to stick it. Eaten by hand, warm or cold, within six hours.']::text[]),
('loco-moco','en','Loco moco','Hilo, Hawaii, United States','A bowl of rice topped with a beef patty, a fried egg and brown onion gravy. Invented in 1949 at the Lincoln Grill in Hilo for hungry teenagers coming off the surf, who wanted something other than a sandwich and could not afford a proper meal.',ARRAY['Cook a short, sticky white rice, wetter than a pilaf: it must hold a dome in the bowl and drink the gravy.','Shape thick 150 g patties without packing, and press a hollow in the centre with your thumb so they do not dome as they cook.','Sear them 4 minutes a side, lift them out and soften the onion in the rendered fat for 15 minutes.','Dust with flour, moisten with broth, add soy and Worcestershire, and let it thicken to a coating consistency.','Build: rice, patty, gravy, then the fried egg at the last moment, yolk runny. It is the yolk that binds everything in the bowl.']::text[]),
('shave-ice-hawaienne','en','Hawaiian shave ice','Haleiwa, Oahu, Hawaii, United States','Ice shaved into flakes, doused with tropical fruit syrups, over a scoop of ice cream and azuki beans. Japanese plantation workers brought it around 1900 as kakigori; the ice is shaved, never crushed, and that is the whole difference.',ARRAY['Make a simple syrup of equal sugar and water, divide it in three and flavour each part with a different fruit, blended and strained.','Cool the syrups completely: poured warm over the ice they melt it before it reaches the table.','Freeze a block of water rather than cubes: it is a block that is shaved, and cubes give shards instead of flakes.','Shave the ice in very thin layers and pile it without pressing, into a high dome: the snow must stay airy and melt on the tongue.','Set the scoop of ice cream and the azuki beans at the bottom of the cup, under the snow, and pour the syrups in three sectors. Condensed milk over the top.']::text[]),
('malasada-hawaienne','en','Hawaiian malasada','Honolulu, Hawaii, United States','A Portuguese doughnut with no hole and no filling, rolled in sugar. Madeiran and Azorean workers brought it to the plantations from 1878; it was eaten on Shrove Tuesday to use up the lard, and Honolulu still calls that day Malasada Day.',ARRAY['Dissolve the yeast in warm milk with a spoonful of sugar and wait until it foams properly.','Knead with the flour, eggs, 60 g of the sugar, the zest and salt for 15 minutes, then work in the softened butter.','The dough must stay very soft and sticky: that is what gives the stringy crumb, and it is handled with oiled hands.','Prove 1½ hours, scoop out balls with an oiled spoon without shaping them — a malasada is irregular by definition.','Fry 90 seconds a side at 175°C and roll them in the remaining sugar while scalding. Eaten within the hour.']::text[]),
('huli-huli-chicken','en','Huli huli chicken','Honolulu, Hawaii, United States','A chicken marinated in soy, pineapple and ginger, grilled between two racks turned over and over. Ernest Morgado created it in 1955 for a farm fundraiser; huli means turn in Hawaiian, and the cook shouted it so the helpers flipped the racks together.',ARRAY['Spatchcock the chicken by removing the backbone: laid flat, it cooks evenly between two racks.','Mix the soy, ketchup, brown sugar, pineapple juice, grated ginger and garlic, and set aside a third for glazing.','Marinate three hours at most: pineapple holds an enzyme that dissolves protein, and a whole night would turn the flesh to mush.','Grill over indirect heat, bone side down first, turning every five minutes for forty minutes.','Baste only in the last ten minutes, in three coats, turning each time: applied earlier, the sugar carbonises.']::text[]),
('saimin','en','Saimin','Honolulu, Hawaii, United States','A noodle soup in dashi and pork broth, topped with char siu, egg and kamaboko. It was born in the plantation camps where Japanese, Chinese, Filipinos and Portuguese shared kitchens: nobody can claim its origin, which is precisely what it says.',ARRAY['Blanch the pork bones five minutes, throw the water away and rinse them: that is what gives a clear broth instead of a grey one.','Return them to cold water with the ginger and simmer 2½ hours without ever boiling.','Meanwhile soak the kombu and dried shrimp cold, and heat them only to 60°C at the end.','Kombu brought to the boil turns slimy and bitter: it is lifted out the moment the water trembles, the rule of dashi.','Combine both broths, season with soy, and pour over noodles cooked separately. Top with char siu, egg, kamaboko and spring onion.']::text[]),
('japadog','en','Japadog','Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada','A hot dog dressed the Japanese way: Japanese mayonnaise, teriyaki sauce, grated daikon and shredded nori. A Japanese couple opened their cart on Burrard Street in 2005; the queue they created has spawned half a dozen imitators in the city.',ARRAY['Reduce the soy, sugar and mirin by half to a teriyaki that coats: too thin and it runs to the bottom of the bun.','Grate the daikon at the last moment and press it lightly — undrained, it soaks the bread within two minutes.','Griddle the sausages rather than boiling them: a japadog is known by its crisp skin, unlike the classic North American hot dog.','Steam the buns, then dress in this order: mayonnaise, sausage, teriyaki, daikon.','Finish with nori cut into fine threads and sesame seeds. The seaweed must stay dry: put under the sauce, it goes limp.']::text[]),
('bison-burger','en','Bison burger','Calgary, Alberta, Canada','A burger of prairie bison, a lean and faintly sweet meat. Bison fed the First Nations for millennia before being wiped out in the late 19th century; Alberta''s rebuilt herds now supply it, and its leanness demands short cooking.',ARRAY['Bison holds only two or three per cent fat: mix a spoonful of cold melted butter into each patty, or it will be dry.','Shape the patties without packing and press a hollow in the centre so they do not dome.','Sear them on a scalding griddle 3 minutes a side, no longer: medium, bison is still good; well done, it is beyond saving.','Never press with a spatula — the little juice it holds would go at once.','Rest 3 minutes before assembling, while the juices redistribute, and build the burger on a butter-toasted bun.']::text[]),
('green-onion-cake','en','Green onion cake','Edmonton, Alberta, Canada','A layered spring onion pancake, pan-fried and cut into wedges. Siu To introduced it to Edmonton in 1979; the city adopted it so completely that it became its unofficial dish, and it has been sold at every Alberta festival for forty years.',ARRAY['Pour boiling water onto the flour and work it with a spoon: this is a scalded dough, which turns extensible and will not tear when stretched.','Knead five minutes once cool enough, then rest an hour under a damp cloth.','Roll very thin, brush with oil and cover with sliced spring onion, then roll it up tightly lengthwise.','Coil the rope into a spiral and press it gently flat: that spiral, not butter lamination, is what creates the layers.','Fry in oil 4 minutes a side over medium heat, then crush the cake between two spatulas to separate the leaves. Serve with a soy and vinegar dip.']::text[]),
('fat-boy-winnipeg','en','Winnipeg fat boy','Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada','A burger drowned in a chilli meat sauce, with shredded cabbage and mayonnaise. It came out of Winnipeg''s Greek cafés in the sixties; the sauce, thick and cumin-scented, is ladled on and the sandwich wrapped in paper at once so it holds together.',ARRAY['For the sauce, whisk half the raw minced beef into cold broth before heating: the meat stays in fine grains and the sauce is not greasy.','Add the grated onion, tomato purée, chilli and cumin, and simmer 45 minutes until it coats the ladle.','Shape flat patties from the remaining beef and sear them on a scalding griddle, 3 minutes a side.','Shred the cabbage very finely and mix it with the mayonnaise just before serving — made ahead, it weeps.','Build: toasted bun, mayonnaise, patty, cheese, a good ladle of sauce, cabbage. Wrap in paper and wait a minute before biting.']::text[]),
('honey-dill-sauce','en','Honey dill sauce','Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada','A sauce of mayonnaise, honey and dill, served with chicken fingers and chips. It is so particular to Manitoba as to be unknown two provinces away: Manitobans often discover while travelling that it exists nowhere else.',ARRAY['Use fresh dill and not dried: dried, it brings only a grassy bitterness and no freshness at all.','Chop it very finely by knife, without the tough stems, and leave it a minute on the board to release its oil.','Warm the honey barely so it blends without lumps — heated above 40°C it loses its scent.','Mix into the mayonnaise with the garlic powder, a touch of vinegar and the lemon juice, whisking with a fork.','Rest 4 hours in the cold: the sauce is flat for the first quarter of an hour and right afterwards. It keeps a week.']::text[]),
('pickered-fish','en','Manitoba pickled fish','Gimli, Manitoba, Canada','Walleye or pike from Lake Winnipeg pickled in sweetened vinegar with onions and juniper. Gimli was founded in 1875 by Icelanders fleeing a volcanic eruption; they recreated their fish curing there, and the settlement remains the largest Icelandic community outside Iceland.',ARRAY['Fillet the fish and pull every pin bone with tweezers: pike has whole rows of them, and one spoils the dish.','Dry-salt them 12 hours in the cold, then rinse and soak them 2 hours in clear water.','Boil the vinegar, water, sugar and spices, then cool completely — a warm marinade would cook the fish white and leave it woolly.','Cut the fillets into 4 cm pieces and layer them in a jar with onion rings.','Cover with the cold marinade and refrigerate at least five days. It keeps a month and goes with rye bread.']::text[]),
('tarte-au-sucre','en','Sugar pie','Quebec City, Quebec, Canada','A tart of brown sugar and cream, set like a soft caramel. It descends from the sugar tarts of northern France, adapted first to maple sugar then to imported brown sugar; it must not be baked until firm, it sets as it cools.',ARRAY['Line the tin and blind-bake the pastry 12 minutes: without that pre-baking the very liquid filling would soak the base.','Mix the brown sugar and flour dry before adding any liquid — the only way to avoid lumps.','Add the cream, melted butter, egg and vanilla, and whisk just enough to combine, without aerating.','Pour into the pre-baked case and bake 30 minutes at 180°C: the centre must still tremble frankly when it comes out.','A tart baked until firm turns grainy as it cools. Let it set 4 hours at room temperature.']::text[]),
('cretons','en','Cretons','Montreal, Quebec, Canada','A pork spread cooked in milk with cinnamon and clove, eaten on toast at breakfast. Despite the name they are nothing like French cretons, which are fried fat residues: the milk and the sweet spices make this a Quebec pâté of its own.',ARRAY['Sweat the very finely chopped onion and garlic, then add the minced pork, crushing it with a fork so it does not clump.','Moisten with milk and simmer 1½ hours uncovered over very low heat, stirring every ten minutes.','The milk must evaporate almost entirely and the fat separate: the sign the cretons are ready.','Add the breadcrumbs in the last ten minutes — they absorb the remaining fat and give the spreadable texture.','Pot them, press down and set 12 hours in the cold. The spices only come through on the second day.']::text[]),
('pouding-chomeur','en','Pouding chômeur','Montreal, Quebec, Canada','A plain cake over which hot syrup is poured before baking: the cake rises, the syrup sinks, and a sauce forms beneath the sponge. It was created by Montreal factory women during the 1929 slump, with brown sugar — the maple syrup came later.',ARRAY['Cream the butter and sugar, add the eggs then alternate flour and milk, and pour the batter into a deep dish.','The dish must be tall: the syrup nearly doubles as it boils and overflows a tart tin.','Bring the maple syrup, cream and brown sugar to the boil and let them bubble for two minutes.','Pour it scalding over the raw batter, without mixing: that difference in temperature is what sends the syrup to the bottom.','Bake 40 minutes at 180°C without opening the door. Serve warm, digging down to the sauce beneath.']::text[])
insert into public.atlas_dish_texts (dish_id,lang,name,place,description,steps) values
('cipate','en','Cipâte','Abitibi, Quebec, Canada','A layered pie of game, pork and potato separated by sheets of pastry, baked for six hours. The name comes from sea pie, the English sailors'' dish, reshaped by French ears; in Abitibi it is made with hare and moose, elsewhere with chicken.',ARRAY['Cut all the meats into 3 cm cubes and marinate them overnight with the onion, savory and spices.','Line a cast-iron pot with a thick sheet of pastry taken up the sides — a cipâte bakes in its dish and is never turned out.','Alternate meat, potato and pastry sheets in three tiers, salting each layer.','Pour in hot broth to cover the top tier, cover with a sheet of pastry and cut a wide chimney.','Bake 45 minutes at 200°C then 5 hours at 140°C. Top up with broth through the chimney if the level drops: a dry cipâte is a failure.']::text[]),
('oreilles-de-crisse','en','Oreilles de crisse','Quebec City, Quebec, Canada','Strips of salt pork belly fried until they curl into crisp horns. They accompany the sugar shack meal in spring; the name, a disguised oath, comes from the shape they take in the fryer — an ear folded on itself.',ARRAY['Soak the salt pork belly 2 hours in cold water, changing it twice, or the oreilles will be inedible.','Cut it into 5 mm strips across the grain, keeping the rind: it is the rind that makes them curl.','Poach them 10 minutes in simmering water and blot them thoroughly — that pre-cooking renders some fat and stops them burning.','Fry them in their own fat over medium heat for 8 minutes, adding nothing: they fold themselves.','Drain on paper, pepper and trickle over maple syrup. Eaten scalding, they soften within a quarter of an hour.']::text[]),
('grands-peres-au-sirop','en','Grands-pères in maple syrup','Quebec City, Quebec, Canada','Balls of dough poached straight in boiling maple syrup, swollen and soaked through. They are never fried: they steam under the lid, above the syrup as much as in it, which is why the pan must not be opened.',ARRAY['Bring the maple syrup, water and sugar to the boil in a wide, deep pan: the liquid should be three centimetres deep.','Rub the cold butter into the flour, baking powder and salt, then bind with the milk without working the dough.','Scoop balls with a spoon and set them on the surface of the boiling syrup, without pushing them under and without touching.','Cover immediately and give them 20 minutes over low heat, never lifting the lid: the trapped steam doubles them in size.','They are done when a needle comes out dry. Serve scalding, in the syrup, with cold cream.']::text[]),
('partridgeberry-pie','en','Partridgeberry pie','St. John’s, Newfoundland and Labrador, Canada','A pie of tundra lingonberries, very sharp, sweetened and thickened with starch. Newfoundlanders call them partridgeberries and pick them in September on the wind-scoured barrens; the berries freeze on the plant and keep all winter.',ARRAY['Sort the berries by hand and pick out leaves and stems — washing them makes them burst and lose their juice.','Mix them with the sugar and cornstarch, but taste first: lingonberry is far sharper than blueberry and takes twice the sugar.','Heat a third of them in a pan for five minutes to burst them, then mix with the raw ones: the first give the sauce, the second hold their shape.','Fill, dot with butter and cover with a solid disc — this berry gives little juice, and a lattice would dry it out.','Glaze, cut three vents and bake 50 minutes at 190°C. Cool completely: warm, the filling runs.']::text[]),
('malpeque-oysters','en','Malpeque oysters','Malpeque, Prince Edward Island, Canada','Oysters from Malpeque Bay, served raw on ice with lemon. They grow four to seven years in colder water than farther south, which slows growth and concentrates flavour; they took the grand prize at the St. Louis Exposition in 1900.',ARRAY['Keep the oysters flat, cupped side down, in a damp cloth: laid on their side they lose their liquor.','Open them with a short knife going in at the hinge and not the side: the hinge gives with a quarter turn, the side needs force.','Discard the first liquor and wait two minutes: the oyster makes a second, clearer and far better.','Free the oyster from the lower muscle so nobody has to do it, and return it to its shell.','Serve on crushed ice with a shallot vinegar and lemon. A few may go two minutes under the grill, buttered with garlic.']::text[]),
('hodgepodge','en','Hodgepodge','Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada','A creamy stew of the season''s first garden vegetables, barely cooked and bound with butter. It is not a winter dish but a late July one: awaited all year, made once or twice when the new potatoes and beans arrive together, and then the season is over.',ARRAY['Use new potatoes with thin skins, unpeeled but scrubbed: at this season the skin is the best of the vegetable.','Cook each vegetable in the same pot but one after another, longest first, lifting each out as it is done.','Keep very little water in the base — two centimetres — because with the cream it will make the sauce.','Return everything to the pot, add the cream and butter and let it tremble for three minutes only, never boiling.','The vegetables must stay crisp: an overcooked hodgepodge becomes a soup and loses what made it worth waiting for.']::text[]),
('rapure-acadienne','en','Acadian rappie pie','Baie Sainte-Marie, Nova Scotia, Canada','A bake of grated, pressed potato in which the extracted water is replaced by chicken broth. It is an operation of substitution: every drop of liquid is removed from the potato and exactly the same volume given back as broth, producing a translucent, gelatinous texture.',ARRAY['Poach the chicken 1½ hours with the onion and bay, bone it and keep all the broth, strained and scalding.','Grate the potatoes very finely and press them by handfuls in a cloth, until not another drop comes out.','Measure the extracted water: exactly that volume of boiling broth must go back into the gratings, no more and no less.','Pour the very hot broth in, whisking: the gratings whiten, thicken and turn translucent within seconds.','Build in two layers with the chicken in the middle, scatter bacon and bake 2½ hours at 200°C to a brown, crackling crust.']::text[]),
('blueberry-grunt','en','Blueberry grunt','Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada','Wild blueberries stewed under steamed dumplings, all in one pan. The name comes from the sound: the fruit grunts as it bubbles under the lid. It dates from when kitchens had no oven and everything was done over the fire.',ARRAY['Stew the blueberries with the sugar, water, zest and cinnamon for 8 minutes, until they burst and give up their juice.','The liquid must stay generous: it is the steam rising from it that cooks the dumplings, and a dry base leaves them raw.','Rub the butter into the flour, baking powder and salt, then bind with the milk without working the dough.','Drop spoonfuls of dough onto the bubbling fruit, without pushing them under and well spaced — they double.','Cover and give them 15 minutes without ever lifting the lid. Serve scalding, with cold cream poured over.']::text[]),
('caribou-steak','en','Caribou steak','Yellowknife, Northwest Territories, Canada','A caribou steak seared in butter, served rare with lingonberries. Caribou has been hunted by the Dene and Inuvialuit for millennia; the meat holds almost no fat and the animal feeds on lichen in winter, which gives it a taste of moss and resin.',ARRAY['Take the meat out two hours ahead: a steak frozen at the centre would cook outside before the middle was even warm.','Crush the juniper in a mortar with the pepper and rub it into the meat, but salt only as it goes into the pan.','Sear over fierce heat in foaming butter, 2 minutes a side, spooning it over with the garlic and thyme.','Caribou has no fat to protect it: past rare it turns dry and livery, with no way back.','Rest it as long as it cooked, then deglaze with red wine and lingonberry jam. Serve with potatoes.']::text[]),
('muktuk','en','Muktuk','Iqaluit, Nunavut, Canada','Skin and blubber of narwhal or bowhead whale, eaten raw in cubes. It is one of the few sources of vitamin C in the Arctic: the skin holds as much as an orange, and without it scurvy would have been unavoidable in a diet with no plants.',ARRAY['Muktuk is taken from a freshly hunted animal and eaten within hours, or frozen immediately.','It is not cooked: heating destroys the vitamin C that justifies its use, and turns the skin rubbery.','Cut it into two-centimetre cubes with an ulu, keeping the black skin and white blubber together: those two layers are what make muktuk.','The skin should crack under the tooth and the blubber melt: if the skin is soft, the animal was old or the keeping too long.','Serve raw, with salt, raw onion or a touch of soy. It is also eaten lightly fermented under moss, but that preparation needs local knowledge.']::text[]),
('whitebait-fritters','en','Whitebait fritters','Hokitika, West Coast, New Zealand','Whole whitebait held in a plain egg batter, barely bound. On the West Coast neither flour nor onion goes in: that would hide a fish that sells for the price of white gold.',ARRAY['Rinse the whitebait in iced water and drain them thoroughly, without pressing: they are fragile.','Beat the eggs barely, with a fork, with salt and pepper — nothing else.','Fold in the whitebait gently: the mixture should hold far more fish than egg.','Foam butter in a pan and pour a ladleful per fritter, without spreading it.','Give them 90 seconds a side, no more: overcooked, whitebait falls apart and loses its sweetness. Serve with lemon, on buttered bread.']::text[]),
('rewena-paraoa','en','Rēwena parāoa','Taupō, New Zealand','A Māori bread raised on a potato starter, dense and faintly sour. The starter, the bug, is handed down through generations and fed on potato and sugar: some are over a hundred years old.',ARRAY['Three days ahead, boil the potatoes and mash them with their water, a spoonful of flour and one of sugar.','Ferment covered in a glass jar, stirring daily: the starter should bubble and smell sweet-sour.','If it smells frankly of vinegar it has gone too far; if it does nothing by the third day, start again.','Knead the flour, salt, starter and warm water for 12 minutes, and prove for 2 hours.','Add the bicarbonate when shaping, prove for 40 minutes, and bake for 45 minutes at 190°C. Always keep back a spoonful of starter for next time.']::text[]),
('barramundi-au-paperbark','en','Paperbark barramundi','Darwin, Northern Territory, Australia','A whole barramundi wrapped in melaleuca bark and cooked over coals. Top End peoples have used this bark for millennia: soaked, it does not burn and lends the fish a eucalypt note.',ARRAY['Soak the melaleuca bark for 30 minutes in water: dry, it catches fire within seconds.','Scale and gut the fish, and score it three times on each side.','Rub it with lemon myrtle, ground bush tomato and salt, and fill the belly with lemon and spring onion.','Wrap it in two thicknesses of wet bark and tie it with fibre string.','Set the parcel on moderate coals for 15 minutes a side. The bark blackens on the surface without the fish burning.']::text[]),
('lamingtons','en','Lamingtons','Brisbane, Queensland, Australia','Cubes of sponge dipped in chocolate icing then rolled in coconut. Named after Lord Lamington, governor of Queensland, who reportedly called them ''those bloody poofy woolly biscuits''.',ARRAY['Make a butter sponge, bake it in a tray for 30 minutes at 180°C, and let it cool completely.','Wrap it and rest it overnight: fresh sponge crumbles when dipped, day-old sponge holds.','Cut 4 cm cubes, wiping the blade between cuts.','Make a fluid icing with the cocoa, icing sugar, melted butter and a little hot milk.','Dip each cube on a fork, let it drip, then roll it at once in the coconut. Dry for 2 hours on a rack.']::text[]),
('lu-sipi','en','Lū sipi','Neiafu, Vavaʻu, Tonga','Lamb and taro leaves cooked in coconut milk, in parcels, under the earth. Tonga has bought frozen mutton flaps from New Zealand since the 1970s: the dish is old, the ingredient recent.',ARRAY['Cut the lamb into large dice with its fat: lean, it dries out under the earth.','Strip the ribs from the taro leaves and stack them into cups, five or six per parcel.','Fill with meat, onion and coconut cream, salt, and close them carefully.','Wrap each cup in a banana leaf and tie it with fibre.','Cook for 2½ hours in the umu on hot stones, or covered in a 160°C oven. Serve with boiled taro.']::text[]),
('coconut-crab-vanuatu','en','Coconut crab in coconut milk','Luganville, Espiritu Santo, Vanuatu','The largest land crustacean in the world, grilled then stewed in its own coconut milk. It feeds on coconuts and its flesh keeps that taste; its scarcity now makes it an occasional dish.',ARRAY['Boil the crab for 15 minutes in salted water, then split it and detach the claws.','Save the yellow fat from the abdomen: it is the prized part, and it flavours the sauce.','Grill the pieces briefly over coals to fix the aroma.','Fry the onion, garlic and ginger, add the coconut cream and the reserved fat.','Simmer for 15 minutes without boiling, return the crab, and serve with taro and island cabbage.']::text[]),
('sup-sup','en','Sup sup','Port Moresby, Papua New Guinea','A soup of root vegetables and greens in coconut milk, thick and mild. The name is Tok Pisin: sup sup, the soup of soups, made with whatever the garden gives that day.',ARRAY['Peel the roots and cut them into large even cubes: they must cook together.','Boil them for 20 minutes in a litre of water with the onion, garlic and flaked smoked fish.','Add the squash and green plantain, and give them another 15 minutes.','Pour in the coconut milk and bring to a bare simmer, never boiling.','Add the aibika leaves last, for 5 minutes only: overcooked, they turn grey.']::text[]),
('poulet-au-lait-de-coco-salomon','en','Chicken with coconut and green pawpaw','Honiara, Solomon Islands','Chicken stewed in coconut milk with green pawpaw, which behaves here as a vegetable. Green pawpaw holds an enzyme that tenderises meat: it does a marinade''s work without being one.',ARRAY['Peel the green pawpaw and remove the seeds; cut it into large cubes.','Colour the jointed chicken in the oil, then add the onion, garlic and ginger.','Add a glass of water and the pawpaw, and simmer covered for 30 minutes.','Pour in the coconut milk and continue for 20 minutes without boiling: the pawpaw turns translucent and soft.','Add the island cabbage leaves at the end. Serve with boiled sweet potato and lime.']::text[]),
('fafaru','en','Raw fish in coconut milk','Bora Bora, French Polynesia','Raw tuna marinated in lime and drowned in coconut milk, the everyday dish of Polynesia. It is made at the lagoon''s edge with the morning''s fish: beyond a few hours it loses all interest.',ARRAY['Choose tuna caught the same day and keep it on ice until the last moment.','Cut it into 2 cm cubes, salt with coarse salt for 5 minutes, then rinse and pat dry.','Pour over the lime juice and leave 10 minutes only: the flesh must stay red at the centre.','Drain and discard the juice, which would curdle the coconut milk.','Add the fresh coconut milk, julienned cucumber and carrot, tomato and spring onion. Serve immediately.']::text[]),
('rukau','en','Rukau','Avarua, Rarotonga, Cook Islands','Taro leaves long-melted in coconut milk and onion, served as an accompaniment. In the Cooks a cook is judged on her rukau: undercooked it scratches the throat, overcooked it is a characterless purée.',ARRAY['Strip the ribs from the taro leaves: they carry most of the irritant crystals.','Boil them for 30 minutes in plenty of water, then drain and discard that water.','Squeeze them between your hands and chop them roughly.','Soften the onion in butter, add the leaves and cook covered for 15 minutes.','Pour in the coconut milk and continue for 15 minutes over low heat. Serve with boiled taro or a roast.']::text[]),
('ika-mata-niue','en','Takihi','Alofi, Niue','A gratin of thinly sliced green pawpaw and taro, drowned in coconut cream. Niue serves it at village feasts; the pairing of root and fruit, both barely sweet, surprises then quickly convinces.',ARRAY['Peel the green pawpaw and taro, and cut them into 3 mm slices, as even as you can.','Rinse the taro in cold water to remove surface starch, which would make the gratin gluey.','Line a dish with banana leaves and alternate layers of taro and pawpaw.','Salt lightly between the layers and pour in coconut cream to just below the surface.','Cover with leaves and bake for 1 hour at 180°C. Rest for 15 minutes before cutting into portions.']::text[]),
('palolo','en','Faʻapapa','Apia, Upolu, Samoa','A dense coconut bread, baked in leaves, somewhere between cake and flatbread. Coconut cream keeps it soft for days, and it is cut into thick slices for travelling.',ARRAY['Grate the fresh coconut and press half of it for cream; keep the pulp.','Mix the flour, baking powder, sugar, salt and grated pulp.','Add the coconut cream and melted butter, and work just enough to bring it together: overworked, the dough goes hard.','Spread it 2 cm thick on softened banana leaves, and fold into a flat parcel.','Bake for 30 minutes at 190°C or on hot stones. The top should brown and the centre stay soft.']::text[]),
('maori-boil-up-pork','en','Toheroa soup','Dunedin, Otago, New Zealand','A green soup of giant clams, thickened with cream, once served at state banquets. Toheroa has been protected since 1971: its green colour comes from the phytoplankton it feeds on, and nothing replaces it.',ARRAY['Purge the clams for 4 hours in seawater or heavily salted water, changing it twice.','Steam them open and strain their liquor carefully through a cloth: sand always hides in it.','Chop the flesh finely by knife — blended, it turns elastic and loses its grain.','Make a pale roux in butter, moisten with the strained liquor and the milk, and cook for 15 minutes.','Add the flesh and the cream and simmer for 5 minutes without boiling. Lemon and parsley at the last moment.']::text[]),
('anzac-biscuits','en','ANZAC biscuits','Adelaide, South Australia, Australia','Oat biscuits with golden syrup, eggless, posted to soldiers in the Great War. The absence of egg is no accident: without it the biscuit survives two months at sea without spoiling.',ARRAY['Combine the oats, flour, coconut, sugar and salt dry.','Melt the butter with the golden syrup, without letting them boil.','Slake the bicarbonate in the boiling water and pour it into the butter: the mixture foams at once and doubles.','Pour over the dry ingredients and work quickly, then shape flattened balls, well spaced.','Bake 15 minutes at 160°C for a chewy biscuit, 20 for a crisp one. Both camps have their partisans.']::text[])
